// Know-before-you-go basics for each country: getting around, money, beds, food, phone data.
// Written for budget travellers in October 2026. Shown on country pages and the static destination pages.
window.GUIDES = {
  "vietnam": {"around": "Sleeper buses are the cheap backbone of the country, and the Reunification Railway running Hanoi to Saigon is slower but far more comfortable if you grab a soft sleeper berth. For long hops, VietJet and Bamboo fares are often barely more than the bus. In cities, Grab, Be and Xanh SM sort you out with motorbike taxis and cars at fixed prices, so you skip the meter games. Book train berths a few days ahead, and well ahead around Tet when the whole country heads home.", "money": "Vietnam runs on the dong, and the huge numbers take a day to get used to, so double check whether that note is 10,000 or 100,000 before you hand it over. Cash rules at street stalls, markets and family guesthouses, while bigger hotels, cafes and chain shops take cards and QR payments. Most ATMs charge a fee per withdrawal, so pull out bigger amounts less often and look for banks like TPBank or VPBank that often skip the fee. Tipping isn't expected, though rounding up for a good tour guide goes down well, and gentle haggling is normal at markets and with xe om drivers.", "sleep": "Hostel dorms are cheap almost everywhere, often with free breakfast or free beer hours thrown in, and family guesthouses give you a private room for not much more. Hanoi's Old Quarter, Hoi An and District 1 in Saigon are the classic backpacker bases. Up in Ha Giang, Sapa and the northern mountains, homestays with a home-cooked dinner are the way to go.", "eat": ["Pho: beef or chicken noodle soup with fresh herbs", "Banh mi: baguette sandwich with pork, pate and pickled vegetables", "Bun cha: grilled pork with rice noodles, Hanoi style", "Ca phe sua da: strong iced coffee with condensed milk"], "data": "Viettel has the best coverage, with Vinaphone and Mobifone close behind, and SIMs are cheap but need passport registration. An eSIM saves you the airport queue, and wifi is fast and free in almost every cafe and hostel.", "heads_up": "Riding a motorbike without a valid licence for that class of bike usually voids your travel insurance, so check your cover before you rent one.", "food": [{"name": "Pho", "wiki": "Pho", "desc": "Beef or chicken noodle soup with a fragrant broth and a pile of fresh herbs, eaten for breakfast at plastic-stool stalls. A bowl usually costs a couple of dollars.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Breakfast, at street stalls", "order": "Pho bo tai for rare beef slices"}, {"name": "Banh mi", "wiki": "Bánh mì", "desc": "A crackly baguette stuffed with pork, pate, pickles, chili and coriander, the best cheap lunch going. Hoi An has some famous stands, but every street corner sells one.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast or a snack, from carts", "order": "Banh mi thit is the classic mixed pork"}, {"name": "Bun cha", "wiki": "Bun cha", "desc": "Hanoi's lunchtime favourite: smoky grilled pork patties in a sweet fishy dipping broth with cold rice noodles and herbs.", "price": "$2 to $3.50", "when": "Lunch in Hanoi, around noon", "order": "Add a nem ran (fried spring roll) on the side"}, {"name": "Banh xeo", "wiki": "Bánh xèo", "desc": "A crispy turmeric pancake stuffed with pork, shrimp and bean sprouts that you wrap in lettuce and herbs and dunk in fish sauce.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Afternoons and dinner, in the south", "order": "Wrap pieces in rice paper and herbs, then dip"}, {"name": "Cao lau", "wiki": "Cao lầu", "desc": "Chewy, smoky noodles with pork and greens in just a splash of broth, a Hoi An speciality you won't find done right anywhere else.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Lunch or dinner in Hoi An", "order": "Stir it well and add the chili jam"}, {"name": "Ca phe sua da", "wiki": "Vietnamese iced coffee", "desc": "Strong drip coffee over ice with sweet condensed milk, the fuel of the whole country. Try egg coffee in Hanoi too.", "price": "$0.80 to $1.50", "when": "Morning, at pavement cafes", "order": "Say ca phe den da if you want it black"}], "food_tip": "Eat where the plastic stools are full of locals and you'll rarely pay more than a couple of dollars a meal. Com binh dan (canteen rice) joints let you point at dishes for a cheap, filling plate.", "culture": ["Take your shoes off before entering homes and many guesthouses, and cover shoulders and knees at temples and pagodas.", "Losing your temper or shouting is a sure way to lose any argument, since keeping calm and saving face matters a lot.", "Use both hands or your right hand when passing money or a business card, especially to older people.", "Ancestor altars are everywhere, in homes, shops and even hostels, so don't touch the offerings or treat them as props for photos.", "Ask before photographing people, particularly ethnic minority communities in the north who often get treated like a tourist attraction.", "Pointing chopsticks at people or leaving them standing upright in rice is bad form, since it echoes funeral incense."], "safety": "Vietnam is very safe for travellers, and the main hassle is petty stuff like overcharging, fake taxis and phone snatching by passing motorbikes in Saigon, so hold your phone away from the road. Use Grab or a reputable taxi firm like Mai Linh or Vinasun rather than random cabs at the airport. Traffic is the real danger, so cross slowly and steadily and let the bikes flow around you. Be wary of rented bikes \"breaking down\" or getting \"damaged\" and costing you a fortune at return, and photograph any rental before you ride off.", "eat_beats": ["Locals eat early, so breakfast pho runs from about 6am and many street stalls pack up after the lunch rush.", "Street stalls often sell one dish only, so sit down, hold up fingers for how many, and pay when you leave.", "Don't drink tap water, but ice in city cafes is factory made and generally fine.", "Look for signs saying com chay or quan chay for vegetarian food, especially near pagodas on the 1st and 15th of the lunar month.", "Tipping isn't expected at local places, though rounding up or leaving small change at sit-down restaurants is appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Bia hoi", "desc": "Light draft beer brewed daily and poured on street corners into small glasses, about $0.50 to $1 a glass. Hanoi's Old Quarter is the classic place to try it."}, {"name": "Ca phe trung", "desc": "Hanoi's egg coffee, with a thick whipped yolk and sugar foam floating on strong coffee. Expect $1.50 to $3 a cup."}], "greet": "A smile and a slight nod work well, and men often shake hands. Greet older people first, and Vietnamese use family titles like anh, chi and em rather than first names.", "dress": "Light, casual clothes are fine in cities and beach towns. Cover shoulders and knees at pagodas, temples and Ho Chi Minh's mausoleum, where guards check.", "table": "Dishes are shared, so take a little at a time onto your rice bowl and lift the bowl close to your mouth. Let the eldest start eating first, and offer to pour drinks for others.", "faith": "Most Vietnamese mix Buddhism, ancestor worship and folk religion, and there are large Catholic and Cao Dai communities. At pagodas, take shoes off where others do, speak softly and never point your feet at altars.", "phrases": [["Xin chào", "Hello"], ["Cảm ơn", "Thank you"], ["Bao nhiêu?", "How much?"], ["Không cay", "Not spicy"], ["Một, hai, ba, dzô!", "One, two, three, cheers!"]]},
  "laos": {"around": "The Laos-China Railway has changed everything, linking Vientiane, Vang Vieng and Luang Prabang in a couple of hours, but tickets often sell out, so book a few days ahead through the LCR Ticket app or an agent. Elsewhere it's minivans and buses on winding mountain roads, and the classic two-day slow boat down the Mekong from Huay Xai to Luang Prabang is a backpacker rite of passage. Tuk-tuks handle short trips around town, and the LOCA app works in Vientiane if you want a fixed price.", "money": "The Lao kip is the currency, and Thai baht and US dollars are often accepted in tourist areas, though usually at a poor rate. ATMs are in all the main towns but have low withdrawal limits and per-transaction fees, so you may need a few goes to get a decent stack. Cards only work at some hotels, tour offices and fancier restaurants, so carry plenty of cash, especially before heading to remote spots. Tipping isn't expected, and gentle, smiley haggling is fine at markets and with tuk-tuk drivers.", "sleep": "Family-run guesthouses are the standard cheap bed, and Luang Prabang and Vang Vieng have plenty of social hostels too. In Nong Khiaw, Muang Ngoi and around the Thakhek and Bolaven loops, simple riverside bungalows and village homestays cost very little.", "eat": ["Laap: minced meat salad with herbs, lime and toasted rice", "Khao piak sen: thick rice noodle soup, a common breakfast", "Tam mak hoong: spicy green papaya salad", "Beerlao: the light national lager, sold everywhere"], "data": "Unitel and Lao Telecom SIMs are cheap and need your passport, and coverage is decent in towns but drops out in the mountains and along the river. Guesthouse wifi exists almost everywhere but can be slow.", "heads_up": "Unexploded bombs from the Vietnam War still lie in rural areas, so stick to marked paths when hiking and never pick up anything metal you find.", "food": [{"name": "Laap", "wiki": "Larb", "desc": "Minced meat or fish tossed with lime, chili, mint and toasted rice powder, often called the national dish. Eat it with a ball of sticky rice.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Dinner, shared with sticky rice", "order": "Laap gai (chicken) is the mild, safe pick"}, {"name": "Khao piak sen", "wiki": "Khao piak sen", "desc": "Thick, slippery rice noodles in a rich chicken or pork broth, the go-to breakfast at roadside stalls for a dollar or two.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Breakfast, from noodle stalls", "order": "Add a fried dough stick to dunk"}, {"name": "Tam mak hoong", "wiki": "Green papaya salad", "desc": "Pounded green papaya with chili, lime and fermented fish sauce. The Lao version is funkier and fierier than the Thai one, so ask for it mild if you need to.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Afternoon snack at roadside stalls", "order": "Ask for 'phet noi' if you want less chilli"}, {"name": "Sticky rice", "wiki": "Glutinous rice", "desc": "Khao niao comes in a little woven basket with almost every meal. Roll a ball with your right hand and use it to scoop up dips and salads.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "With every meal, from a basket", "order": "Roll a small ball and dip it"}, {"name": "Or lam", "wiki": "Or lam", "desc": "A thick, peppery stew from Luang Prabang made with meat, aubergine, herbs and chewy bits of sakhan wood that make your tongue tingle.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch or dinner in Luang Prabang", "order": "Chew the sakhan wood, but don't swallow it"}, {"name": "Beerlao", "wiki": "Beerlao", "desc": "The light, easy national lager, sold everywhere in big bottles and drunk over ice when it's hot.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Evenings by the Mekong", "order": "The dark version is the local favourite upgrade"}], "food_tip": "Night markets and street stalls are where you eat cheapest, and Luang Prabang's vegetarian buffet lane lets you pile up a plate for a couple of dollars. Baguette sandwiches from street carts make a cheap breakfast.", "culture": ["Laos is gentle and laid-back, and raising your voice or showing anger is seen as embarrassing for everyone involved.", "Cover shoulders and knees in temples, take your shoes off, and never point your feet at a Buddha image or a monk.", "Women shouldn't touch monks or hand things directly to them; place the item down or pass it through a man.", "If you watch the morning alms giving in Luang Prabang, do it quietly from a distance, skip the flash, and only take part if you actually mean it.", "The head is considered sacred, so don't pat anyone on the head, even kids.", "The traditional greeting is the nop, palms together with a slight bow, and a smile and \"sabaidee\" go a long way."], "safety": "Laos is relaxed and low in crime, though bag snatching from motorbikes happens in Vientiane and Luang Prabang occasionally. Vang Vieng tubing is far tamer than it used to be, but mixing booze and fast rivers is still how people get hurt, and drug busts there can be set up. Rental bikes on the loops are fun but roads are rough, so ride slowly and check brakes and tyres first. Bring a decent mosquito repellent, since dengue is common in the wet season.", "eat_beats": ["Locals eat early, with noodle soup from about 6am and dinner around 6 to 7pm.", "Don't drink tap water; buy big bottles or use the refill stations many guesthouses have.", "Vegetarians can say 'bo kin sin' (I don't eat meat), but fish sauce and padaek turn up in most dishes.", "Tipping isn't expected at local places, though rounding up in tourist restaurants is appreciated.", "Morning markets are busiest before 8am and sell grilled skewers, sticky rice and fruit for small change."], "drinks": [{"name": "Lao coffee", "desc": "Strong robusta from the Bolaven Plateau, served with sweet condensed milk over ice. A glass costs about $1."}, {"name": "Lao Lao", "desc": "Rice whisky, often homemade and poured at village celebrations. A bottle runs $1 to $3, and a shot is usually free if you're a guest."}], "greet": "People greet with the nop, palms pressed together at chest height with a small bow, and a soft 'sabaidee'. Handshakes are fine between men in business settings.", "dress": "Light, loose clothing works, but cover shoulders and knees in towns as well as temples. Skimpy beachwear away from the river or pool is frowned upon.", "table": "Dishes are shared, and sticky rice is eaten with the right hand from a common basket. Use a spoon and fork for rice plates and chopsticks for noodle soup.", "faith": "Most Lao are Theravada Buddhists, mixed with older beliefs in spirits called phi. In temple halls, sit with your feet tucked behind you, and keep your head lower than monks and Buddha images where you can.", "phrases": [["Sabaidee (ສະບາຍດີ)", "Hello"], ["Khop jai (ຂອບໃຈ)", "Thank you"], ["Tao dai? (ເທົ່າໃດ)", "How much?"], ["Bo phet (ບໍ່ເຜັດ)", "Not spicy"], ["Saep lai (ແຊບຫຼາຍ)", "Very tasty"]]},
  "thailand": {"around": "Trains, government buses, private coaches and minivans reach almost everywhere, and ferries and speedboats link the islands. Overnight sleeper trains from Bangkok to Chiang Mai or the south are a cheap and fun way to save a night's accommodation, but book berths ahead in the cool high season. Grab and Bolt work in Bangkok, Chiang Mai, Phuket and most big towns, and the BTS Skytrain and MRT are the quickest way across Bangkok. AirAsia, Nok Air and Thai Lion often fly domestic routes cheaply if you book early.", "money": "Thailand uses the baht, and while cards and contactless work in malls, 7-Elevens and chain restaurants, street food, markets, songthaews and many guesthouses are cash only. Thai ATMs slap a hefty flat fee on foreign cards on every withdrawal, so take out larger sums at once. Tipping is optional, though leaving small change in restaurants and tipping massage therapists is appreciated. Haggling is expected at markets and with tuk-tuks, but keep it friendly and don't bother in shops with price tags.", "sleep": "Thailand might have the best hostel scene in Asia, with cheap, clean dorms in Bangkok, Chiang Mai, Pai and the islands, from party spots to quiet ones. On the beaches and islands, simple fan bungalows and guesthouses are still around for low prices. Book ahead on the Andaman islands and Koh Phangan around Full Moon parties and from December to March.", "eat": ["Pad kra pao: stir-fried meat with holy basil, chili and rice", "Som tam: pounded green papaya salad, spicy and sour", "Khao soi: northern curry noodle soup topped with crispy noodles", "Mango sticky rice: sweet coconut sticky rice with ripe mango"], "data": "Tourist SIMs from AIS, True or dtac are sold at airport arrivals and every 7-Eleven, need your passport, and come with plenty of data. Coverage is excellent even on the islands, and eSIMs work well too.", "heads_up": "If a friendly stranger tells you a temple or palace is closed today and offers a cheap tuk-tuk tour instead, it almost always ends at a gem or tailor shop.", "food": [{"name": "Pad kra pao", "wiki": "Phat kaphrao", "desc": "Minced pork or chicken stir-fried with holy basil and a lot of chili, served over rice with a crispy fried egg. The Thai office worker's lunch, cheap at any street stall.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Lunch, at street stalls and office canteens", "order": "Say \"kai dao\" to add the fried egg on top"}, {"name": "Som tam", "wiki": "Green papaya salad", "desc": "Shredded green papaya pounded with lime, chili, fish sauce and peanuts. Order it \"phet nit noi\" if you want it only a little spicy.", "price": "$1 to $2.50", "when": "Afternoon snack, from Isan carts and stalls", "order": "Som tam Thai is milder; som tam poo has salted crab"}, {"name": "Khao soi", "wiki": "Khao soi", "desc": "Northern Thailand's curry noodle soup, rich with coconut and topped with crunchy fried noodles. Chiang Mai is the place to try it.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Lunch, in Chiang Mai noodle shops", "order": "Chicken drumstick is the classic; add lime and pickled greens"}, {"name": "Pad thai", "wiki": "Pad thai", "desc": "Stir-fried rice noodles with egg, tofu, shrimp, peanuts and lime, sold from street carts everywhere for a couple of dollars.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Evening, from carts and night markets", "order": "Ask for \"goong sod\" if you want fresh prawns"}, {"name": "Tom yum", "wiki": "Tom yum", "desc": "Hot and sour soup loaded with lemongrass, galangal, lime leaves and prawns. Ask for the creamy \"nam khon\" version for something richer.", "price": "$2 to $6", "when": "Dinner, shared at local restaurants", "order": "Nam sai is the clear version; both come with prawns"}, {"name": "Mango sticky rice", "wiki": "Mango sticky rice", "desc": "Sweet coconut sticky rice with fresh ripe mango, best in the hot season from roughly March to May when mangoes are at their peak.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Afternoon dessert, from market stalls", "order": "Ask for extra coconut sauce on the side"}], "food_tip": "Street stalls and night markets feed you well for a couple of dollars a dish, and food courts in malls are cheap, clean and good for air-con. 7-Eleven toasties are a legit backpacker breakfast.", "culture": ["The Thai royal family is deeply respected and protected by strict lese-majeste laws, so never joke about or criticise the king, and don't step on banknotes, which carry his face.", "Dress modestly at temples, covering shoulders and knees and taking shoes off, and the Grand Palace in Bangkok is strict about it.", "The wai, palms together with a slight bow, is the traditional greeting, but as a visitor a smile and \"sawasdee\" works fine.", "Feet are considered low and dirty, so don't point them at people or Buddha images, and don't put them up on tables.", "Thais value keeping cool, and getting angry or loud in public makes you lose face rather than win the argument.", "When the national anthem plays at 8am and 6pm in public places, people stop and stand still, so do the same."], "safety": "Thailand is pretty safe, and the classic scams are the main thing: the friendly stranger who says the palace is closed, rigged gem shops, and jet ski operators claiming you damaged the ski. Motorbike accidents are the biggest real risk to backpackers, so wear a helmet, have the right licence and don't ride drunk. At Full Moon and other beach parties, watch your drink, avoid buckets from dodgy stalls and stay out of the sea at night. Drug laws are strict and police on the islands do target tourists.", "eat_beats": ["Thais eat little and often, so street stalls run from early morning to late night and there is no fixed lunch hour.", "Don't drink the tap water; refill stations dispense filtered water for a few baht a litre, and ice in restaurants is factory made and safe.", "Vegetarians should look for yellow flags with red \"jay\" lettering, which mark vegan Chinese Thai food stalls.", "Most dishes contain fish sauce or shrimp paste, so say \"mai sai nam pla\" to ask for no fish sauce.", "Tipping isn't expected at stalls, but leaving the coins from your change at sit-down restaurants is common."], "drinks": [{"name": "Cha yen", "desc": "Strong orange Thai iced tea with sweet condensed milk, sold in plastic bags or cups at street carts for about $1."}, {"name": "Chang and Leo beer", "desc": "The everyday lagers, drunk over ice in a glass, which locals actually do. A big bottle costs around $2 to $3 at a 7-Eleven, but alcohol sales stop between 2pm and 5pm."}], "greet": "The wai, palms pressed together at chest height with a slight bow, is returned to elders but not to children or shop staff. Men say \"sawasdee khrap\" and women \"sawasdee kha\".", "dress": "Light, loose clothes suit the heat, but cover shoulders and knees at temples and government buildings. Going shirtless away from the beach is seen as rude.", "table": "Thais eat with a fork and spoon, pushing food onto the spoon with the fork; chopsticks are only for noodles. Dishes are shared, so take a little at a time.", "faith": "Around 90 percent of Thais are Theravada Buddhist. At temples take shoes off, never touch a monk if you are a woman, and keep your head lower than Buddha images.", "phrases": [["Sawasdee khrap / kha (สวัสดีครับ / ค่ะ)", "Hello"], ["Khob khun (ขอบคุณ)", "Thank you"], ["Tao rai? (เท่าไหร่)", "How much?"], ["Mai phet (ไม่เผ็ด)", "Not spicy"], ["Aroy mak (อร่อยมาก)", "Very delicious"]]},
  "cambodia": {"around": "Buses and minivans from companies like Giant Ibis, Larryta and Mekong Express link Siem Reap, Phnom Penh, Battambang, Kampot and the coast, and you can book a day ahead online or through your guesthouse. Tuk-tuks handle short trips, and Grab and PassApp work in the main cities so you skip the haggling. For Angkor, hire a tuk-tuk for the day or cycle the small circuit if you can handle the heat.", "money": "Cambodia runs on two currencies: US dollars for most things and Cambodian riel handed back as small change, usually at about 4,000 riel to the dollar. ATMs dispense dollars but most charge a few dollars per withdrawal, so take out larger amounts and look for banks that charge less. Shops often refuse torn or worn dollar bills, so don't accept them in your change either. Tipping isn't required but is very welcome given low wages, and haggling is normal at markets and with tuk-tuk drivers.", "sleep": "Hostel dorms and guesthouses are very cheap in Siem Reap and Phnom Penh, often with a pool for not much extra. Kampot and Kep have riverside bungalows and chilled hostels, and Koh Rong and Koh Rong Samloem have beach huts and party hostels.", "eat": ["Fish amok: fish steamed in coconut curry with lemongrass", "Lok lak: stir-fried beef with a lime and pepper dip", "Num banh chok: rice noodles with fish curry, a breakfast staple", "Kuy teav: pork broth noodle soup eaten in the morning"], "data": "SIMs from Smart, Cellcard or Metfone are very cheap and need your passport, and coverage is good in towns and along main roads. Cafes and guesthouses nearly all have free wifi.", "heads_up": "Skip orphanage visits and volunteering with children, which can fuel harm to the very kids they claim to help.", "food": [{"name": "Fish amok", "wiki": "Fish amok", "desc": "Fish steamed in a fragrant coconut curry with lemongrass and kaffir lime, often served in a banana leaf cup. Cambodia's signature dish.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch or dinner at Khmer restaurants", "order": "Chicken amok is common and cheaper than fish"}, {"name": "Lok lak", "wiki": "Shaking beef", "desc": "Stir-fried beef cubes on lettuce and tomato with a tangy lime and black pepper dip, usually with a fried egg and rice.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Dinner, often with a cold beer", "order": "Ask for a fried egg on top"}, {"name": "Num banh chok", "wiki": "Num banhchok", "desc": "Fresh rice noodles with a green fish curry and crunchy herbs, sold by women at markets and street corners in the morning for about a dollar.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Breakfast from women at the market", "order": "Green fish curry gravy is the classic topping"}, {"name": "Kuy teav", "wiki": "Kuyteav", "desc": "Pork broth noodle soup topped with herbs, lime and chili, the standard breakfast everywhere.", "price": "$1 to $2.50", "when": "Early breakfast at noodle stalls", "order": "Pick pork or beef, rice noodles or egg noodles"}, {"name": "Kralan", "wiki": "Sticky rice in bamboo", "desc": "Sticky rice, coconut milk and black beans stuffed into bamboo tubes and roasted over coals, sold by roadside vendors around Battambang and on the way to Angkor sites.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Roadside snack, especially on bus trips", "order": "Peel the bamboo back in strips and eat from inside"}, {"name": "Kampot pepper crab", "wiki": "Kampot pepper", "desc": "In Kep's crab market, fresh crab is stir-fried with green Kampot peppercorns, a bit of a splurge but still cheap by world standards.", "price": "$6 to $12", "when": "Seafront stalls in Kep at sunset", "order": "Ask for fresh green pepper on the vine"}], "food_tip": "Markets and street stalls serve rice and noodle dishes for a dollar or two, and Khmer restaurants away from Pub Street in Siem Reap are much cheaper than the tourist strip.", "culture": ["The traditional greeting is the sampeah, palms together with a slight bow, and returning it shows respect.", "Cover shoulders and knees at Angkor and other temples, as staff can refuse entry, and some areas need more modest dress than others.", "Monks are respected, and women should avoid touching them or handing things directly to them.", "Visiting the Tuol Sleng Genocide Museum and the Choeung Ek Killing Fields is moving and important, so be quiet and respectful there and dress modestly.", "Keeping calm matters, and public displays of anger or affection are frowned upon.", "Use your right hand or both hands to give and receive things, and avoid touching anyone's head."], "safety": "Cambodia is generally friendly and safe, but bag and phone snatching from passing motorbikes happens in Phnom Penh and Siem Reap, so keep your bag on the side away from the road, even in a tuk-tuk. Watch for overcharging and the occasional tuk-tuk driver who takes you to a commission shop. Avoid drugs entirely, as \"happy\" pizzas and strong drugs sold as something milder have caused serious harm. Never wander off marked paths in rural areas, since landmines remain in parts of the country.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast starts early, around 6am, and many noodle stalls sell out by 10am.", "Don't drink tap water, but refill stations and big water jugs at guesthouses make plastic bottles avoidable.", "Vegetarians should say \"ot si sach\" (no meat), and remember fish sauce goes into almost everything.", "Market food courts work on a sit-and-point basis, so grab a stool at a stall and point at what you want.", "Tipping isn't expected at local places, but leaving small change or a dollar at sit-down restaurants is appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Teuk ampoh", "desc": "Fresh sugarcane juice pressed in front of you on a hand-cranked machine, often with a squeeze of lime. A cup costs about 2,000 riel, or 50 cents."}, {"name": "Kafe toek doh ko", "desc": "Strong iced coffee sweetened with condensed milk, sold from carts on every corner. Expect to pay $1 to $1.50."}], "greet": "Press your palms together at chest height and bow slightly in the sampeah, raising your hands higher for elders and monks. Younger Cambodians in cities may also shake hands.", "dress": "Light, loose clothes work in the heat, but cover shoulders and knees at Angkor, the Royal Palace and any pagoda. Swimwear stays on the beach.", "table": "Dishes are shared, and you eat with a fork in the left hand and a spoon in the right, using the fork to push food onto the spoon. Wait for the eldest person to start.", "faith": "Around 95% of Cambodians are Theravada Buddhists, with a Cham Muslim minority. At pagodas take off shoes and hats, sit with your feet pointing away from Buddha images and never touch a monk if you're a woman.", "phrases": [["Suosdei (សួស្តី)", "Hello"], ["Arkun (អរគុណ)", "Thank you"], ["Thlai ponmaan? (ថ្លៃប៉ុន្មាន?)", "How much?"], ["Ot hel (អត់ហឹរ)", "Not spicy"], ["Chhnganh (ឆ្ងាញ់)", "Delicious"]]},
  "indonesia": {"around": "On Java, KAI trains are cheap, comfy and easily booked on the Access by KAI app, while buses and Pelni ferries link the islands. Domestic flights on Lion Air, Citilink and Batik Air save days on long hops between islands like Bali, Lombok, Flores and Sumatra. Grab and Gojek run in most cities and on Bali for motorbike rides, cars and food delivery, though some areas ban them from pickups. Book trains well ahead around Lebaran at the end of Ramadan, when the whole country travels.", "money": "Indonesia uses the rupiah, and with all those zeros you'll feel like a millionaire after one ATM visit. Cash is king outside the cities and Bali's busier areas, and ATMs are common but often cap withdrawals, so stick to machines attached to banks like BCA, Mandiri or BNI. Cards work at nicer restaurants and hotels in Bali and Jakarta, often with a small surcharge. Tipping is optional, and haggling is expected at markets and with drivers, while warungs have fixed prices.", "sleep": "Family homestays and guesthouses are the best value, often with breakfast included, and you'll find them everywhere from Ubud to Yogyakarta to the Gili Islands. Bali, especially Canggu and Uluwatu, has lots of social hostels with pools. On Flores, Sumatra and Sulawesi, simple homestays are cheap and a good way to meet local families.", "eat": ["Nasi goreng: fried rice, often topped with a fried egg", "Gado-gado: vegetables with peanut sauce, tofu and egg", "Rendang: beef slow-cooked in coconut milk and spices", "Sate: grilled meat skewers with peanut sauce"], "data": "Telkomsel has the widest coverage, especially on remote islands, and SIMs are sold at airports with passport registration, though phones bought abroad may need IMEI registration if you stay long. An eSIM is simplest for short trips.", "heads_up": "On Bali, some small money changers short-change you with sleight of hand, so count your cash carefully at the counter or just use a bank ATM.", "food": [{"name": "Nasi goreng", "wiki": "Nasi goreng", "desc": "Sweet, smoky fried rice with kecap manis, usually topped with a fried egg and krupuk crackers. Any warung will do it for a couple of dollars.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Dinner or late night, from carts", "order": "Ask for nasi goreng spesial, with an extra egg"}, {"name": "Mie goreng", "wiki": "Mie goreng", "desc": "The fried noodle twin of nasi goreng, spicy and sweet, sold from carts and warungs late into the night.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Late evening at roadside carts", "order": "Mie goreng Jawa is the smoky, charcoal-cooked version"}, {"name": "Gado-gado", "wiki": "Gado-gado", "desc": "Blanched vegetables, tofu, tempeh and egg drowned in a rich peanut sauce, a great pick for vegetarians.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch, at warungs and street carts", "order": "Say tidak pedas if you can't handle chilli"}, {"name": "Rendang", "wiki": "Rendang", "desc": "Beef slow-cooked for hours in coconut milk and spices until it's dark and tender, from West Sumatra but found at every nasi padang counter.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch, at nasi padang counters", "order": "Point at it in the window and get it over rice"}, {"name": "Sate", "wiki": "Satay", "desc": "Grilled skewers of chicken, goat or beef with peanut or soy sauce, cooked over smoky coals at night stalls.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Evenings, from smoky roadside grills", "order": "Sate ayam is chicken; Balinese sate lilit is minced fish"}, {"name": "Bakso", "wiki": "Bakso", "desc": "Meatball soup with noodles and tofu, sold from carts with a bell or a bowl-tapping sound, a cheap snack all over the country.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Afternoon snack from passing carts", "order": "Add sambal, soy and vinegar from the table jars"}], "food_tip": "Eat at warungs, small local cafes, where a full plate costs a couple of dollars, and at nasi padang or nasi campur places where you pick a few dishes over rice. Prices in Seminyak or Canggu cafes can be ten times higher.", "culture": ["Indonesia is the world's largest Muslim-majority country, though Bali is mostly Hindu, so dress modestly outside beach areas, with shoulders and knees covered.", "At Balinese temples, you need a sarong and sash, often lent or rented at the entrance, and women on their period are traditionally asked not to enter.", "Use your right hand to eat, give and receive things, as the left is considered unclean.", "Don't step on the little canang sari offerings you'll see on Bali's pavements, and step around them where you can.", "Pointing with your index finger is rude; locals point with their thumb instead.", "During Ramadan, avoid eating, drinking or smoking in public in conservative areas like Aceh and much of Java during the day."], "safety": "Indonesia is mostly safe and the people are warm, but petty theft and bag snatching from motorbikes happens in Bali and Jakarta. Motorbike crashes are the biggest danger for travellers on Bali, so wear a helmet, carry a licence and ride sober. Methanol poisoning from cheap or fake spirits has killed travellers, so stick to beer or drinks from sealed, trusted bottles. Drug laws are extremely strict, including possible death sentences for trafficking, so stay well away.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, and many warungs cook in the morning and sell out by mid afternoon, so go before 2pm for the freshest food.", "Don't drink the tap water anywhere; buy big bottles or use refill stations, and ice in busy places is usually factory made and fine.", "Vegetarians do well with tempeh, tofu, gado-gado and sayur (vegetable) dishes, but ask about terasi, a shrimp paste hidden in many sambals.", "At nasi padang places, plates may be set out on your table and you pay only for what you touch, so check before digging in.", "Tipping isn't expected at warungs, while tourist restaurants often add tax and service, shown as ++ next to menu prices."], "drinks": [{"name": "Es teh manis", "desc": "Sweet iced tea that comes with almost every warung meal. It costs around 30 to 60 cents."}, {"name": "Jamu", "desc": "A herbal tonic of turmeric, ginger and tamarind, sold from bottles carried by street sellers or at small stalls for under $1."}], "greet": "A light handshake followed by touching your hand to your chest is common, and younger people may press an elder's hand to their forehead. Say selamat pagi in the morning.", "dress": "Cover shoulders and knees in towns, mosques and temples, and keep swimwear for the beach. Aceh follows local sharia rules, so dress very conservatively there and women should carry a headscarf.", "table": "Locals usually eat with a spoon in the right hand and a fork to push food onto it, or with the right hand at casual places. Wait for your host to invite you to start.", "faith": "About 87 percent of Indonesians are Muslim, with Hindus in Bali and large Christian communities in Flores, North Sulawesi and Papua. Remove shoes at mosques, wear a sarong at temples and never climb on shrines.", "phrases": [["Selamat pagi", "Good morning"], ["Terima kasih", "Thank you"], ["Berapa harganya?", "How much is it?"], ["Tidak pedas", "Not spicy"], ["Enak sekali", "Very tasty"]]},
  "nepal": {"around": "Tourist buses run between Kathmandu, Pokhara and Chitwan and are worth the small extra over local buses, which are cramped and slow. Shared jeeps and local buses reach most trailheads, and domestic flights to Lukla or Jomsom save days but are often delayed by weather, so build in buffer days. Pathao and inDrive work in Kathmandu and Pokhara and are much cheaper than haggling with taxis. Book tourist buses a day ahead.", "money": "Nepal uses the Nepalese rupee and is still mostly a cash country, though QR payments and cards are spreading in Kathmandu and Pokhara. ATMs give small amounts per withdrawal with a fee, and there are almost none on trekking routes, so withdraw everything you need in Kathmandu or Pokhara before setting off. Tip trekking guides and porters at the end of a trek, round up in restaurants, and expect to haggle in Thamel shops.", "sleep": "Thamel in Kathmandu and Lakeside in Pokhara are packed with cheap guesthouses and hostels. On treks you stay in teahouses, simple lodges where the room is very cheap or even free on the understanding that you eat your meals there. Bhaktapur and Bandipur have atmospheric old guesthouses if you want something calmer.", "eat": ["Dal bhat: rice, lentil soup and curry, with free refills", "Momos: steamed dumplings filled with meat or vegetables", "Thukpa: Tibetan-style noodle soup, good at altitude", "Masala chiya: sweet milky tea with spices"], "data": "SIMs from Ncell or Nepal Telecom need a passport and photo, and coverage is good in cities but patchy on trails. Many teahouses sell wifi by the hour, often slow, and eSIMs are now an option for simpler setup.", "heads_up": "Altitude sickness is a real risk on the Annapurna and Everest routes, so climb slowly, add rest days and descend if symptoms get worse.", "food": [{"name": "Dal bhat", "wiki": "Dal bhat", "desc": "Rice, lentil soup, vegetable curry and pickle, often with free refills. It's what powers porters up mountains, hence the saying \"dal bhat power, 24 hour\".", "price": "$1.50 to $4", "when": "Late morning and evening, twice daily", "order": "Ask for refills of rice and dal, they're free"}, {"name": "Momos", "wiki": "Momo (food)", "desc": "Steamed or fried dumplings filled with buffalo, chicken or vegetables, served with a spicy tomato dip. A plate is cheap at any momo shop.", "price": "$1 to $2.50 a plate", "when": "Afternoon snack at momo shops", "order": "Try jhol momo, served in a spicy soupy sauce"}, {"name": "Thukpa", "wiki": "Thukpa", "desc": "Tibetan-style noodle soup, warming and great at altitude after a long day on the trail.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Dinner on treks and in Thamel"}, {"name": "Sel roti", "wiki": "Sel roti", "desc": "A sweet, ring-shaped fried rice bread, especially popular around the Dashain and Tihar festivals.", "price": "$0.30 to $0.80", "when": "Festival mornings with tea"}, {"name": "Newari feast", "wiki": "Newar cuisine", "desc": "Kathmandu Valley Newari food: beaten rice, spiced buffalo, lentil pancakes and pickles. Try it in Patan or Bhaktapur.", "price": "$3 to $7", "when": "Festivals and Newari eateries in Patan", "order": "Ask for a set with bara and choila"}, {"name": "Masala chiya", "wiki": "Masala chai", "desc": "Sweet, milky spiced tea sold for pennies everywhere, the perfect warmer on a cold trekking morning.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.60", "when": "Morning and afternoon, from tea stalls", "order": "Say chini kam for less sugar"}], "food_tip": "Local dal bhat joints are cheap and filling, and the refills mean you won't go hungry. On treks, food prices rise the higher you go, so stick to dal bhat for value.", "culture": ["Greet people with \"namaste\", palms together, which is used everywhere from cities to mountain villages.", "Walk clockwise around Buddhist stupas, prayer wheels and mani stones, and take shoes off before entering temples and homes.", "Many Hindu temples, including the inner area of Pashupatinath, don't allow non-Hindus inside, so respect the signs.", "Cows are sacred in Hindu culture, and beef is rarely on the menu, so be thoughtful about where you ask for it.", "Use your right hand for eating and giving, and don't touch food or people with your feet.", "Dress modestly, especially outside tourist areas, and keep public affection low key."], "safety": "Nepal is very safe for travellers in terms of crime, and the real risks are altitude, roads and weather. Never trek beyond your limits, and travel insurance must cover helicopter rescue at your altitude. Solo trekking is now restricted in many national parks, which means hiring a licensed guide. Watch for overcharging in Thamel and the occasional fake charity or sadhu asking for money for a photo.", "eat_beats": ["Nepalis eat dal bhat around 10am and again at 7pm, with snacks like chow mein or momos in the afternoon.", "Don't drink tap water, so refill from filtered water stations in towns and teahouses to cut plastic.", "Vegetarian food is easy, as many Hindu and Newari families eat no meat on certain days anyway.", "Eating with your right hand is normal, and cheap local spots often have a sink for washing up first.", "A 10 percent service charge and 13 percent VAT are often added at tourist restaurants, so check before tipping extra."], "drinks": [{"name": "Tongba", "desc": "Warm fermented millet in a wooden mug, topped up with hot water and sipped through a straw. A mug costs about $1.50 to $3 in the hills."}, {"name": "Lassi", "desc": "Thick sweet yoghurt drink, best from small shops in Kathmandu and Bhaktapur, for $0.70 to $1.50."}], "greet": "Say namaste with your palms pressed together, which works for anyone at any time. Handshakes are fine between men in cities.", "dress": "Cover shoulders and knees, especially at temples and in villages. Trekking gear is fine on trails, but avoid tiny shorts.", "table": "Eat with your right hand and don't touch food others will eat once it's touched your lips, as it becomes jutho, or ritually unclean. Don't offer anyone food from your plate.", "faith": "Most people are Hindu with a large Buddhist minority, and the two blend closely. Remove shoes at temples, ask before entering, and avoid leather in some Hindu shrines.", "phrases": [["Namaste (नमस्ते)", "Hello"], ["Dhanyabad (धन्यवाद)", "Thank you"], ["Kati ho? (कति हो?)", "How much?"], ["Piro nahaalnus (पिरो नहाल्नुस्)", "No chilli please"], ["Mitho chha (मिठो छ)", "It's tasty"]]},
  "india": {"around": "Trains are the best value for long distances, and sleeper or 3AC classes overnight save on a bed while giving you a real slice of Indian life. Book through IRCTC, or apps like ConfirmTkt and Cleartrip, weeks ahead if you can, as popular routes sell out, and foreign tourist quota tickets can help. Overnight sleeper buses and cheap domestic flights on IndiGo or Air India Express fill the gaps. Uber, Ola and Rapido work in most cities and avoid haggling with drivers.", "money": "India uses the rupee, and UPI QR payments are everywhere, though foreigners can only use them through special tourist wallets. Cards work in cities, malls and better hotels, but you'll need cash for street food, small shops, rickshaws and rural areas, and ATMs charge fees on foreign cards. Haggling is normal at markets and with auto-rickshaws, often starting at half the first price. Small tips of around 10 percent are appreciated in restaurants, and a few rupees for porters and helpers goes a long way.", "sleep": "Hostel chains like Zostel, goStops and The Hosteller are cheap, social and found in most backpacker spots, from Rishikesh to Goa to Kerala. Simple guesthouses are dirt cheap, and Rajasthan has heritage havelis at fair prices. Hill stations and Goa get pricier in peak season and holidays.", "eat": ["Masala dosa: crispy rice crepe filled with spiced potato", "Thali: a platter of curries, dal, rice and bread", "Chole bhature: spicy chickpeas with puffy fried bread", "Masala chai: milky spiced tea sold on every corner"], "data": "Airtel and Jio SIMs need your passport, visa and a photo, and activation can take a day, so an eSIM is simpler for many travellers. Coverage is good in most areas, and data is very cheap.", "heads_up": "Drink bottled or filtered water, and be wary of station touts who tell you your booked hotel or train has been cancelled.", "food": [{"name": "Masala dosa", "wiki": "Masala dosa", "desc": "A huge, crispy fermented rice crepe stuffed with spiced potato and served with sambar and chutneys, a South Indian breakfast that costs pennies.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Breakfast, in South Indian tiffin places", "order": "Try a ghee roast or Mysore masala dosa"}, {"name": "Thali", "wiki": "Thali", "desc": "A platter of several curries, dal, rice, bread and pickle, often with unlimited refills, the best-value meal in the country.", "price": "$1 to $4", "when": "Lunch, at busy thali restaurants", "order": "Ask if it's an unlimited thali with free refills"}, {"name": "Chole bhature", "wiki": "Chole bhature", "desc": "Spicy chickpea curry with giant puffy fried bread, a heavy and glorious Delhi and Punjab favourite.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Late breakfast or lunch, mostly up north", "order": "One plate is huge, so consider sharing"}, {"name": "Pani puri", "wiki": "Panipuri", "desc": "Crispy hollow balls filled with spiced potato and tangy water, eaten in one bite from street stalls. Go for busy stalls with fresh water.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Evening snack, at street carts", "order": "Eat each one in a single bite before it goes soggy"}, {"name": "Biryani", "wiki": "Biryani", "desc": "Layered spiced rice with meat or vegetables cooked slowly in a sealed pot. Hyderabad and Lucknow are famous for it.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch and dinner, big on Sundays", "order": "Hyderabadi dum biryani comes with salan and raita"}, {"name": "Masala chai", "wiki": "Masala chai", "desc": "Sweet, milky spiced tea sold in tiny cups or clay pots on every corner and every train.", "price": "$0.10 to $0.40", "when": "All day, from stalls and trains", "order": "Ask for kam cheeni if you want less sugar"}], "food_tip": "Local dhabas, thali places and street food are cheap and delicious, often a dollar or two for a full meal. Eat where the turnover is high and food is cooked fresh in front of you.", "culture": ["Dress modestly, especially in rural areas and religious sites, covering shoulders and knees, and women may find a light scarf useful for covering their head in temples and gurdwaras.", "Take shoes off before entering temples, mosques, gurdwaras and many homes, and some temples don't allow leather items.", "Use your right hand for eating and passing things, as the left is linked to toilet duties.", "Public displays of affection are frowned upon in much of the country, so keep it low key.", "The head wobble can mean yes, maybe or I hear you, so confirm in words when it matters.", "Cows are sacred to many Hindus, and many places are vegetarian or alcohol-free, so be respectful about it."], "safety": "India can be intense, but most travellers have a great time. Scams to watch include touts claiming your hotel or train is cancelled, fake tourist offices, and drivers taking you to commission shops. Women travellers can get unwanted attention, so dressing modestly and using women-only train compartments or ride options helps, and avoid isolated places at night. Drink bottled or filtered water and start slowly with street food to avoid the dreaded Delhi belly.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch runs from about 1 to 3pm and dinner is late, often 8:30 to 10pm in big cities.", "Drink only sealed bottled or filtered water, and skip ice and raw salads at roadside stalls.", "India is the easiest place on earth to be vegetarian, and packaged food carries a green dot for veg and a brown or red one for non-veg.", "Street food is safest at busy stalls where it's cooked in front of you, ideally straight out of hot oil.", "Tip around 10 percent in sit-down restaurants unless a service charge is already on the bill; dhabas and stalls don't expect it."], "drinks": [{"name": "Lassi", "desc": "A thick yoghurt drink, sweet, salty or mango, often served in clay cups. Expect to pay 40 cents to $1."}, {"name": "Nimbu pani", "desc": "Fresh lime water with salt, sugar and spices, sold from carts for 20 to 50 cents. Pick carts that use bottled water."}], "greet": "Namaste, with palms pressed together and a small nod, works everywhere. Men usually shake hands with men, but wait for a woman to offer her hand first.", "dress": "Loose clothes that cover shoulders and knees fit in best, and in Goa and other beach spots swimwear stays on the sand. Carry a scarf to cover your head in gurdwaras and some mosques.", "table": "Eat with your right hand, using your fingertips to scoop rice and curry with torn bread. Don't touch shared dishes with your eating hand or offer anyone food you've already bitten.", "faith": "Around 80 percent of Indians are Hindu, alongside large Muslim, Christian, Sikh, Buddhist and Jain communities. Go barefoot at temples, cover your head in gurdwaras, walk clockwise around shrines and ask before taking photos inside.", "phrases": [["Namaste (नमस्ते)", "Hello"], ["Dhanyavaad (धन्यवाद)", "Thank you"], ["Kitne ka hai? (कितने का है?)", "How much is it?"], ["Kam teekha (कम तीखा)", "Less spicy"], ["Bas (बस)", "Enough, that's all"]]},
  "sri-lanka": {"around": "Trains are slow but cheap, and the hill country line through Kandy, Nuwara Eliya and Ella is one of the world's most scenic rides. Reserved seats sell out weeks ahead, so book early online or take an unreserved ticket and squeeze in. Buses run often between towns and are super cheap but driven hard. PickMe and Uber work in Colombo and other cities for tuk-tuks and cars.", "money": "Sri Lanka uses the rupee, and while cards work at bigger hotels and supermarkets, most daily spending is cash. ATMs are easy to find in towns, but many charge a fee and some limit withdrawals, so Commercial Bank and Sampath ATMs are good picks. Tuk-tuk drivers often quote high, so agree on a fare first or use an app. Small tips of around 10 percent are welcome where no service charge is added, and haggling is normal at markets.", "sleep": "Family guesthouses and homestays are the best value, often with a big homemade rice and curry dinner. Hostels are common in Colombo, Ella, Kandy and the south coast beach towns like Mirissa, Hikkaduwa and Weligama. Arugam Bay on the east coast has surf hostels and cabanas, busiest from roughly May to September.", "eat": ["Rice and curry: rice with several small vegetable and meat curries", "Hoppers: bowl-shaped rice flour pancakes, often with an egg", "Kottu roti: chopped flatbread stir-fried with vegetables and meat", "Ceylon tea: strong local black tea, often served with milk"], "data": "Dialog and Mobitel (now branded SLT-Mobitel) sell tourist SIMs at the airport with passport registration, and coverage is good across most of the island. Wifi in guesthouses is usually fine.", "heads_up": "Posing with your back to a Buddha statue, or showing off Buddha tattoos, is considered deeply disrespectful.", "food": [{"name": "Rice and curry", "wiki": "Sri Lankan cuisine", "desc": "A heap of rice with several small vegetable, dal and meat or fish curries plus sambol. Lunchtime local places serve it cheaply.", "price": "$1.50 to $4", "when": "Lunchtime, the main meal of the day", "order": "Ask for a fish or chicken curry with veg sides"}, {"name": "Hoppers", "wiki": "Appam", "desc": "Bowl-shaped crispy rice flour pancakes, often with an egg cooked into the middle, best eaten in the evening with spicy sambol.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50 each", "when": "Evening street food, cooked to order", "order": "Get an egg hopper with lunu miris"}, {"name": "Kottu roti", "wiki": "Kottu", "desc": "Chopped flatbread stir-fried with vegetables, egg and meat on a hot griddle, listen for the clanging blades at night.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Late evening, listen for the clanging", "order": "Cheese kottu or dolphin kottu with thin roti strips"}, {"name": "Pol sambol", "wiki": "Pol sambol", "desc": "Grated coconut with chili, lime and onion, served as a side with almost everything.", "price": "Free with your curry", "when": "On the side of every rice plate"}, {"name": "Lamprais", "wiki": "Lamprais", "desc": "Rice and curries baked in a banana leaf parcel, a Dutch Burgher dish found in Colombo bakeries.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Sunday lunch or special occasions", "order": "Order a day ahead at some places"}, {"name": "Ceylon tea", "wiki": "Ceylon tea", "desc": "Strong local black tea, often served sweet and milky. Visit a plantation in Ella or Nuwara Eliya to see where it comes from.", "price": "$0.20 to $1", "when": "Morning and 4pm tea break", "order": "Kiri te is milk tea, plain te is black"}], "food_tip": "Local rice and curry joints and short eats bakeries are the cheapest way to eat, with snacks like rolls and patties for a few cents. Tourist restaurants on the coast cost several times more.", "culture": ["Posing with your back to a Buddha statue, or showing Buddha tattoos, is considered disrespectful and can get you deported.", "Cover shoulders and knees at temples, take off shoes and hats, and wear white at places like the Temple of the Tooth in Kandy if you want to blend in with pilgrims.", "Use your right hand for eating, which many locals do with their fingers, and for giving and receiving things.", "Public affection is frowned upon outside the beach resorts, so keep it to holding hands.", "The side-to-side head wobble usually means yes or okay, so don't mistake it for a no.", "Every full moon is a Poya day, a Buddhist public holiday when alcohol sales are banned and many shops close, so stock up the day before if you fancy a sunset beer."], "safety": "Sri Lanka is generally safe and friendly, and the main hassles are overcharging tuk-tuk drivers, touts steering you to their cousin's guesthouse, and gem deals that are too good to be true. Buses are driven like they're in a race, so be careful on the roads and on rented scooters. Rip currents at some south and west coast beaches are deadly, especially in the monsoon, so swim where the locals and lifeguards are. Solo women can get unwanted attention, and dressing modestly and being firm helps, while dengue is common, so use repellent.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the big rice and curry meal, and many small places run out by 2pm.", "Tap water isn't safe to drink, so use filtered refills where hotels offer them or buy bottled water.", "Vegetarians do well, since dhal, jackfruit, beetroot and pumpkin curries are standard sides.", "Short eats are displayed on a plate at your table, and you only pay for what you eat.", "A 10% service charge is often added to bills, and leaving extra small change is a kind gesture."], "drinks": [{"name": "King coconut", "desc": "The orange thambili coconut is hacked open at roadside stalls and is wonderfully refreshing. Expect about 150 to 250 rupees, under $1."}, {"name": "Arrack", "desc": "Coconut palm spirit that locals drink with ginger beer or cola. A bottle of a mid-range brand is about $10 to $15 in shops."}], "greet": "The traditional greeting is \"ayubowan\", said with palms pressed together, meaning \"may you live long\". Handshakes are common between men in cities.", "dress": "Cover shoulders and knees at temples, and in towns go for loose cotton rather than beachwear. Topless sunbathing is not allowed.", "table": "Eat with your right hand, mixing rice and curry with your fingertips, and wash your hands before and after. It's polite to accept a second helping when offered at someone's home.", "faith": "About 70% of people are Buddhist, with Hindu, Muslim and Christian communities. Remove shoes and hats at temples, walk clockwise around stupas and never point your feet at a Buddha statue.", "phrases": [["Ayubowan (ආයුබෝවන්)", "Hello, may you live long"], ["Sthuthiyi (ස්තූතියි)", "Thank you"], ["Kiyada? (කීයද?)", "How much?"], ["Sera nathuwa (සැර නැතුව)", "Not spicy"], ["Hari rasai (හරි රසයි)", "Very tasty"]]},
  "kyrgyzstan": {"around": "Marshrutkas (shared minibuses) and shared taxis link towns cheaply, leaving when full from bus stations. Yandex Go works in Bishkek and Osh and is much cheaper than street taxis. For remote spots like Song-Kul or the Ala-Kul trek, arrange a driver or tour through a guesthouse or Community Based Tourism office. Hitchhiking is common and generally safe, though many drivers expect a small payment, so ask up front.", "money": "Kyrgyzstan uses the som, and cash is king once you leave Bishkek, Osh and Karakol, since cards rarely work and ATMs are scarce in rural areas. Clean, newer US dollar or euro notes are a handy backup and easy to change at exchange booths in Bishkek and Osh, often at better rates than ATMs. Tipping isn't expected but rounding up or leaving 10 percent in nicer restaurants is appreciated. Light haggling is fine at bazaars and with taxis.", "sleep": "Guesthouses and homestays are cheap and common, usually with breakfast and dinner included. Yurt camps run at mountain lakes in summer, and Bishkek and Karakol have good hostels. Community Based Tourism offices can arrange homestays in villages.", "eat": ["Beshbarmak: boiled meat with flat noodles, a festive dish", "Plov: rice cooked with carrots, onions and lamb", "Lagman: hand-pulled noodles with meat and vegetables", "Kymyz: fermented mare's milk, slightly sour and fizzy"], "data": "Beeline, MegaCom and O! SIMs are cheap and need a passport, with good coverage in towns and none in many mountain valleys. Wifi is common in Bishkek and Karakol guesthouses.", "heads_up": "Mountain weather changes fast even in summer, so bring warm layers for treks and yurt stays.", "food": [{"name": "Beshbarmak", "wiki": "Beshbarmak", "desc": "Boiled lamb or horse meat over flat noodles with an onion broth, the festive national dish. The name means five fingers, as it was traditionally eaten by hand.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Weddings, holidays and homestay feasts", "order": "Ask if it's horse or lamb"}, {"name": "Plov", "wiki": "Pilaf", "desc": "Rice slow-cooked with yellow carrots, onions and lamb in a huge kazan pot, a Central Asian staple you'll find at every bazaar canteen for a few dollars.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch at bazaar canteens"}, {"name": "Lagman", "wiki": "Laghman (food)", "desc": "Hand-pulled noodles with meat, peppers and vegetables, either in a soup or fried. Introduced by the Dungan and Uyghur communities and cheap everywhere.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch at canteens and cafes", "order": "Ask for gyuro lagman, the fried version"}, {"name": "Manti", "wiki": "Manti (food)", "desc": "Big steamed dumplings filled with lamb, onion and fat, served with sour cream or vinegar. Cheap, greasy and perfect after a hike.", "price": "$2 to $4 a plate", "when": "Lunch or dinner", "order": "Add sour cream and chilli"}, {"name": "Boorsok", "wiki": "Boortsog", "desc": "Little puffy pieces of fried dough piled on the table with tea, jam and cream at every homestay and yurt camp.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 a plate", "when": "With tea at every meal"}, {"name": "Kymyz", "wiki": "Kumis", "desc": "Fermented mare's milk, sour, fizzy and mildly alcoholic, sold by herders along mountain roads in summer. An acquired taste, so start with a sip.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 a glass", "when": "Summer, from roadside yurts", "order": "Start with a small sip"}], "food_tip": "Cheap canteens (stolovaya) and bazaar food stalls will fill you up for a few dollars, and homestays usually throw in dinner and breakfast, which saves you a lot.", "culture": ["Kyrgyzstan is mostly Muslim but pretty relaxed about it, and vodka flows freely at celebrations, but dress modestly in villages and cover up properly at mosques.", "Hospitality is huge, and you will be poured endless cups of tea, so accept at least a little of whatever is offered, even if you just nibble a piece of bread.", "Remove shoes when entering homes and yurts.", "Bread (nan) is treated as sacred, so never throw it away, step over it or put it upside down on the table.", "Greet men with a handshake, but wait for women to offer their hand.", "Ask before photographing people, especially women and elders."], "safety": "Kyrgyzstan is generally safe and welcoming, but pickpockets work the crowded bazaars like Osh Bazaar in Bishkek, and drunk men can get pushy late at night. Shared taxi drivers sometimes drive scarily fast on mountain roads, so don't be shy about asking them to slow down. Mountain rescue is limited and slow, so tell someone your route, carry proper gear and consider a guide for passes like Ala-Kul. Treat stream water before drinking it, since livestock graze everywhere.", "eat_beats": ["Meals follow a normal pattern, but tea time with bread and jam can happen at any hour.", "Don't drink the tap water, especially in villages, so boil or filter it.", "Vegetarian food is hard as meat is in almost everything, but tell your host and expect bread, salads and potatoes.", "Many cafes add a 10 to 15 percent service charge, so tipping beyond that isn't expected."], "drinks": [{"name": "Maksym", "desc": "A fizzy, slightly sour drink made from fermented grains, sold from street barrels in summer for about $0.30 a glass."}, {"name": "Black tea", "desc": "Tea is poured only half full so the host can keep topping it up warm. At a cafe, a pot costs $0.50 to $1."}], "greet": "Men shake hands with a hand on the heart, and women often nod or hug each other. Say salam aleikum to elders.", "dress": "Casual clothes are fine in Bishkek, but cover shoulders and knees in villages. Bring layers, as mountain nights get freezing.", "table": "Sit where you're told, as the place facing the door is for honoured guests. Pray with a hand-over-face gesture (omin) at the end of a meal if others do.", "faith": "Most Kyrgyz are Sunni Muslim, mixed with older nomadic beliefs, and there's a Russian Orthodox minority. At mosques, cover up, remove shoes, and women should wear a headscarf.", "phrases": [["Salamatsyzby (Саламатсызбы)", "Hello (polite)"], ["Rakhmat (Рахмат)", "Thank you"], ["Kancha turat? (Канча турат?)", "How much?"], ["Daamduu (Даамдуу)", "Delicious"], ["Jakshy (Жакшы)", "Good"]]},
  "georgia": {"around": "Marshrutkas, the shared minibuses, link almost every town for a few lari and leave from bus stations like Didube in Tbilisi when full or on a rough timetable. Slow but comfy trains run from Tbilisi to Batumi, Zugdidi and Kutaisi, and the summer Batumi train sells out, so book on the railway website a few days ahead. Bolt and Yandex Go work well in Tbilisi and Batumi and spare you haggling with taxis. Shared jeeps and minibuses to Mestia or Ushguli are best booked through your guesthouse a day ahead.", "money": "Georgia uses the lari, and cards and contactless work pretty much everywhere in Tbilisi and Batumi, even in small cafes. Carry cash for marshrutkas, family guesthouses, markets and mountain villages like Mestia and Kazbegi. Bank ATMs from TBC and Bank of Georgia are everywhere and usually fee-free or cheap, while standalone machines can sting you. Restaurants often add a 10 to 18 percent service charge, so check the bill before tipping on top, and light haggling is only normal at markets.", "sleep": "Family guesthouses are the classic Georgian stay, cheap and usually with a huge home-cooked breakfast or dinner, often with homemade wine. Tbilisi has loads of hostels in Old Town and around Fabrika, a converted Soviet sewing factory turned hostel and bar hub. Mestia, Kazbegi and other mountain spots fill up in July and August, so book ahead then.", "eat": ["Khachapuri: bread filled with melted cheese, the Adjarian one topped with egg", "Khinkali: big soup dumplings, eaten by hand, leave the knot", "Lobio: stewed kidney beans with herbs, often served in a clay pot", "Churchkhela: walnuts strung and dipped in thickened grape juice"], "data": "A Magti or Silknet SIM is cheap and quick to buy with your passport, with generous data packages. Coverage is good in towns and along main roads, but patchy high in the mountains.", "heads_up": "Supra feasts mean many toasts with homemade wine or chacha, so pace yourself, it's fine to sip rather than drain the glass every time.", "food": [{"name": "Khachapuri", "wiki": "Khachapuri", "desc": "Bread filled with gooey melted cheese. Adjarian khachapuri is boat-shaped and topped with a runny egg and butter you swirl in, while Imeruli is the round everyday version.", "price": "$3 to $8", "when": "Lunch, dinner and bakery breakfasts", "order": "Adjaruli for a feast, Imeruli or Megruli to share"}, {"name": "Khinkali", "wiki": "Khinkali", "desc": "Big soup dumplings filled with spiced meat and broth. Hold the knot, bite a small hole, slurp the juice, then leave the knot on the plate.", "price": "$0.40 to $0.80 each", "when": "Dinner with beer or wine", "order": "Order at least five each, mixed meat or kalakuri herbs"}, {"name": "Lobio", "wiki": "Lobio", "desc": "Kidney beans stewed with herbs and spices, often served bubbling in a clay pot with cornbread and pickles. Cheap, hearty and vegetarian.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Lunch or dinner, any day", "order": "Pair it with mchadi cornbread and pickles"}, {"name": "Badrijani nigvzit", "wiki": "Nigvziani badrijani", "desc": "Fried aubergine rolls filled with a garlicky walnut paste and topped with pomegranate seeds, a staple on every supra table.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Starter at feasts and dinners"}, {"name": "Churchkhela", "wiki": "Churchkhela", "desc": "Walnuts strung together and dipped in thickened grape juice, sold hanging like candles at markets. Great trekking snack.", "price": "$1 to $3 a stick", "when": "Snack from market stalls"}, {"name": "Chacha", "wiki": "Chacha (brandy)", "desc": "Fierce grape brandy, often homemade, that you'll be offered at guesthouses. Sip it, it's strong.", "price": "$1 to $3 a shot", "when": "Toasts with dinner at guesthouses"}], "food_tip": "Bakeries sell fresh khachapuri and lobiani (bean bread) for a lari or two, and cheap local restaurants do big plates of khinkali for very little. Guesthouse dinners are often the best value meal you'll have.", "culture": ["Georgian hospitality is legendary, and guests are seen as a gift from God, so you may be invited in for food and wine by people you just met.", "At a supra feast, the tamada (toastmaster) leads long toasts, and you only drink wine after a toast, so follow the room.", "Women should cover heads, shoulders and knees and wear a skirt in Orthodox churches, and men should take off hats and avoid shorts; many churches lend scarves and wraps at the door.", "Georgia is proud of its own identity, so don't call it part of Russia, and be thoughtful when Russia comes up, as the 2008 war and occupied regions are a raw topic.", "Georgians are direct and warm, and loud conversation is normal rather than a sign of anger."], "safety": "Georgia is one of the safest countries in the region, with low crime and friendly locals. The main risks are crazy driving on mountain roads, so pick sober, careful drivers, and stray dogs, which are mostly friendly but can be territorial in villages. Don't try to enter the Russian-occupied regions of Abkhazia or South Ossetia from Russia, as it's illegal under Georgian law. In Tbilisi, watch for overpriced bar tabs in clubs where you've been lured in by strangers.", "eat_beats": ["Georgians eat late, with long dinners starting from 8pm, and lunch is a flexible affair.", "Tap water is generally safe in Tbilisi and the mountains, though many people drink spring water.", "Vegetarians eat well thanks to Orthodox fasting days, with walnut, bean and aubergine dishes on every menu.", "Restaurants usually add a 10 to 18 percent service charge, so you don't need to tip extra if it's there."], "drinks": [{"name": "Qvevri wine", "desc": "Amber and red wine fermented in clay jars buried underground, an 8,000 year old method. A glass costs $2 to $5."}, {"name": "Lagidze water", "desc": "Sweet sodas made with natural syrups like tarragon and cream, about $1 a glass from Tbilisi soda fountains."}], "greet": "Men shake hands and friends kiss once on the cheek. Gamarjoba works for hello any time.", "dress": "City dress is casual, but in churches women cover hair, shoulders and knees and wear a skirt, and men remove hats.", "table": "Expect toasts and more food than you can eat, and try a bit of everything. Drink wine only after a toast, and don't toast with beer, which is used for enemies.", "faith": "Most Georgians are Georgian Orthodox Christians, with Muslim minorities in Adjara and the east. In churches, keep quiet, don't turn your back to the altar and avoid photographing services.", "phrases": [["Gamarjoba (გამარჯობა)", "Hello"], ["Madloba (მადლობა)", "Thank you"], ["Ra ghirs? (რა ღირს?)", "How much?"], ["Gaumarjos! (გაუმარჯოს!)", "Cheers!"], ["Gemrielia (გემრიელია)", "It's delicious"]]},
  "albania": {"around": "Buses and furgons (minibuses) are the main way around, leaving from street-side stops or bus terminals with schedules that are often only known locally. There are no big ride-hailing apps, so agree a taxi fare before you get in. For Theth, book a seat on a minibus from Shkodër a day ahead, and if you plan the Valbona pass, arrange the Komani Lake ferry back through your guesthouse. Renting a car is popular for the Riviera, but roads are winding and drivers aggressive.", "money": "Albania uses the lek, and cash rules outside Tirana and the bigger hotels, although cards are spreading in cities and beach bars. Many ATMs charge a fixed fee, so take out larger amounts in one go, and Raiffeisen and Credins are good bets. Euros are often accepted along the Riviera, but you'll get better value paying in lek. Rounding up or leaving around 10 percent in restaurants is appreciated, and haggling isn't really a thing outside markets.", "sleep": "Guesthouses and family hotels are cheap and common, and Tirana, Berat and Gjirokastër have good hostels. Theth guesthouses usually include dinner and breakfast, which you'll need after the hike. The Riviera, especially Ksamil and Himarë, fills fast in July and August, so book ahead or go in shoulder season when prices drop.", "eat": ["Byrek: flaky filo pie with cheese, spinach or meat", "Tavë kosi: baked lamb and rice in a tangy yogurt custard", "Fërgesë: peppers, tomatoes and cottage cheese baked into a rich dip", "Qofte: grilled spiced meat patties, usually served with bread"], "data": "A local prepaid SIM is cheap and easy to buy in operator shops with your passport, with good coverage in towns but weak signal in the northern mountains. eSIMs are also handy, and roaming from EU SIMs usually isn't included, so check before you burn through data.", "heads_up": "A nod often means no and a head shake means yes, so double check answers in words.", "food": [{"name": "Byrek", "wiki": "Börek", "desc": "Flaky filo pie with cheese, spinach or meat, sold warm from bakeries for breakfast. Pair it with a glass of ayran.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast, warm from bakeries", "order": "Me spinaq is spinach, me djathë is cheese"}, {"name": "Tavë kosi", "wiki": "Tavë kosi", "desc": "Lamb baked with rice under a tangy yoghurt and egg custard, the national dish and originally from Elbasan. Homely and very filling.", "price": "$5 to $9", "when": "Lunch at traditional restaurants", "order": "Elbasan's version is the most famous"}, {"name": "Fërgesë", "wiki": "Fërgesë", "desc": "Roasted peppers, tomatoes and gjizë (salted curd cheese) baked into a rich, gooey dip, scooped up with bread in Tirana's traditional restaurants.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "As a shared starter, especially in Tirana", "order": "Comes with liver or meat, or as a vegetarian version"}, {"name": "Qofte", "wiki": "Kofta", "desc": "Grilled spiced meat patties served with bread, onions and salad at cheap grill joints, a great quick dinner for a few euros.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch or dinner, at grill houses", "order": "Order by the portion with salad and fries"}, {"name": "Raki", "wiki": "Rakia", "desc": "Strong grape or mulberry brandy, often homemade and poured as a welcome drink at guesthouses. Sip it slowly, it's fierce.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "With meze, any time of day", "order": "Mulberry (mani) raki is smoother than grape"}, {"name": "Trileçe", "wiki": "Tres leches cake", "desc": "A soaked three-milk sponge cake with a caramel top, hugely popular in Albania and sold in every pastry shop.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Dessert, at pastry shops"}], "food_tip": "Bakeries sell byrek for under a euro, and grill joints serve qofte and salad cheaply. Away from the Riviera, restaurant meals are very cheap by European standards.", "culture": ["A nod often means no and a head shake means yes, so confirm answers in words.", "Albanians are very hospitable, and besa, a code of honour around keeping your word and protecting guests, still matters.", "Albania is proudly religiously mixed and tolerant, with Muslims, Orthodox Christians and Catholics living side by side, so dress modestly at mosques and churches and take shoes off in mosques.", "Coffee culture is huge, and sitting for hours over one espresso is totally normal, so don't rush your waiter.", "The evening xhiro, a slow stroll along the main street, is a social ritual worth joining.", "Albanians are proud and generous hosts, and if you're invited in, expect raki, coffee and food, and accept at least a little to be polite."], "safety": "Albania is one of the safest and friendliest countries in Europe, and crime against tourists is low. The biggest risk is the roads, as driving can be chaotic, overtaking reckless and mountain roads narrow, so pick careful drivers and think twice about renting a scooter. Hikers in the Accursed Mountains should start early, carry plenty of water and check the weather, as conditions can turn quickly. Agree taxi fares in advance, especially from Tirana airport, where overcharging is common.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is usually 1 to 3pm and dinner from 8pm, while cafés stay busy all morning with people lingering over coffee.", "Tap water isn't recommended for drinking in most places, but bottled water is cheap everywhere.", "Vegetarians do fine with byrek, fërgesë, grilled vegetables, salads and fasule (bean soup), especially during Orthodox fasting periods.", "Many places are cash only outside Tirana and the Riviera, so carry lek.", "Tipping isn't expected, but rounding up or leaving about 10 percent at restaurants is appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Dhallë", "desc": "A cold, slightly salty yoghurt drink that's the classic partner for byrek. A glass costs 50 cents to $1."}, {"name": "Boza", "desc": "A thick, sweet fermented corn drink sold in summer from pastry shops, about $1 a glass."}], "greet": "A handshake is standard, and friends kiss on both cheeks. Say mirëdita (good day), and expect people to ask after your family.", "dress": "Casual clothes are fine in Tirana and on the coast, but northern villages are more conservative, so cover shoulders and knees there. Swimwear stays at the beach.", "table": "As a guest your host will keep refilling your plate and glass, so leave a little to show you're full. Toast with gëzuar and look people in the eye.", "faith": "About half of Albanians are Muslim, mostly Sunni with a Bektashi Sufi minority, alongside Catholics in the north and Orthodox Christians in the south. Remove shoes in mosques and tekkes, and cover shoulders and knees at all religious sites.", "phrases": [["Mirëdita", "Good day"], ["Faleminderit", "Thank you"], ["Sa kushton?", "How much does it cost?"], ["Gëzuar!", "Cheers!"], ["Po / Jo", "Yes / No"]]},
  "poland": {"around": "Trains from PKP Intercity and Polregio cover the country well, from the fast Pendolino express trains to cheap regional ones, and FlixBus fills the gaps cheaply. Book Intercity trains early on the app for the best fares, especially on Friday and Sunday. Bolt, Uber and FreeNow all work in cities, and the Jakdojade app handles local transit tickets. City public transport is cheap and efficient, with trams in most big cities.", "money": "Poland uses the złoty, not the euro, and cards and contactless are accepted almost everywhere, even for tiny amounts at bakeries and tram ticket machines. Avoid Euronet ATMs, the blue and yellow ones in tourist areas, as they charge high fees and push bad exchange rates, and always choose to be charged in złoty rather than your home currency. Kantors (exchange offices) away from the main tourist streets often have good rates. Tip about 10 percent in sit-down restaurants, and be careful saying \"dziękuję\" when handing over money, as it can mean keep the change.", "sleep": "Hostels in Kraków, Warsaw, Gdańsk and Wrocław are plentiful and good value, many with free breakfast or pierogi nights. Private rooms (pokoje) are common in Zakopane and on the coast. Book ahead for the Baltic coast in July and August, and for Kraków on weekends.", "eat": ["Pierogi: dumplings filled with potato and cheese, meat or fruit", "Żurek: sour rye soup with sausage and egg", "Zapiekanka: open baguette with mushrooms and cheese, a cheap street snack", "Oscypek: smoked sheep cheese from the Tatra mountains, often grilled"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Orange, Play, Plus or T-Mobile need registration with your passport in store, so an eSIM is often simpler. EU roaming works for EU SIMs, and coverage is good across the country.", "heads_up": "Milk bars (bar mleczny) serve very cheap home-style meals, so look for them to save money at lunch.", "food": [{"name": "Pierogi", "wiki": "Pierogi", "desc": "Dumplings filled with potato and cheese, meat, sauerkraut or fruit, boiled or fried. Ruskie (potato and cheese) are the classic cheap filler.", "price": "$4 to $8 a plate", "when": "Lunch, at milk bars", "order": "Ruskie for potato and cheese, with fried onions"}, {"name": "Żurek", "wiki": "Sour cereal soup", "desc": "Sour rye soup with sausage and egg, sometimes served in a bread bowl. Perfect on a cold day.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch, especially on cold days", "order": "Ask for it w chlebie, in a bread bowl"}, {"name": "Zapiekanka", "wiki": "Zapiekanka", "desc": "Open baguette topped with mushrooms, cheese and ketchup, a cheap late-night snack. Kraków's Plac Nowy is famous for them.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Late night, in Kraków's Kazimierz"}, {"name": "Bigos", "wiki": "Bigos", "desc": "Hunter's stew of sauerkraut, cabbage, mixed meats and sausage, slow-cooked for days until rich and tangy, perfect on a cold Polish night.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Lunch or dinner, often in winter", "order": "Eat it with rye bread or potatoes"}, {"name": "Oscypek", "wiki": "Oscypek", "desc": "Smoked sheep cheese from the Tatras, grilled and served with cranberry jam at Zakopane stalls.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Snacks at mountain stalls", "order": "Ask for żurawina, the cranberry jam"}, {"name": "Pączki", "wiki": "Pączki", "desc": "Deep-fried doughnuts filled with rose jam or custard, especially on Fat Thursday before Lent.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast or snack, from bakeries", "order": "Try z różą, filled with rose jam"}], "food_tip": "Milk bars (bar mleczny) serve very cheap home-style meals, often just a few euros for soup and a main. Lunch menus (zestaw obiadowy) at restaurants are also great value.", "culture": ["Poland is strongly Catholic, so dress modestly in churches, keep quiet during services and expect many shops to close on Sundays.", "A firm handshake and eye contact is the normal greeting, and Poles can seem reserved at first but warm up fast, especially over food and vodka.", "At Auschwitz-Birkenau and other memorial sites, be respectful, dress modestly and avoid selfies or jokes.", "Saying \"Na zdrowie\" is the standard toast, and it's polite to look people in the eye when you clink.", "Drinking alcohol in public is illegal, and police do hand out fines for drinking in parks and on the street.", "Bring flowers in odd numbers if you're invited to someone's home, as even numbers are for funerals, and take your shoes off at the door."], "safety": "Poland is very safe, and the main things to watch are pickpockets in crowded tourist areas and on trams, and the bar scam in Kraków and Warsaw, where friendly strangers lure you into a club that then hits you with a huge bill. Street taxis can overcharge, so stick to apps. Hiking in the Tatras is great but needs care, as weather changes fast and trails like Orla Perć are steep and dangerous in bad conditions.", "eat_beats": ["Obiad, the main meal, is traditionally eaten mid-afternoon, so lunch deals run roughly 12pm to 4pm.", "Milk bars are self-service, so order and pay at the counter and wait for your number or the dish name to be called.", "Tap water is safe in most cities, though many locals still prefer bottled.", "Vegetarians can live on pierogi ruskie, potato pancakes and naleśniki, and big cities have plenty of vegan places.", "Tip 10% at sit-down restaurants, and be careful saying dziękuję as you hand over cash, as it can mean keep the change."], "drinks": [{"name": "Kompot", "desc": "A homemade drink of fruit boiled with water and sugar, served cool at milk bars. A glass costs $0.50 to $1.50."}, {"name": "Żubrówka", "desc": "Bison grass vodka, often mixed with apple juice into a szarlotka. A shot or mixed drink costs about $2 to $5 in bars."}], "greet": "Shake hands firmly and say dzień dobry, then use Pan or Pani with surnames until invited otherwise. Friends often kiss on the cheek, usually three times.", "dress": "Smart casual is normal in cities. Cover shoulders and knees in churches, and dress modestly and respectfully at memorial sites.", "table": "Say smacznego before eating, and expect hosts to keep refilling your plate. Finishing everything shows you enjoyed it, and toasts happen often with vodka.", "faith": "Around 70% of Poles identify as Roman Catholic, and the church remains important, especially in rural areas. Stay quiet in churches, avoid tours during Mass and don't take photos where signs prohibit it.", "phrases": [["Dzień dobry", "Hello, good day"], ["Dziękuję", "Thank you"], ["Proszę", "Please"], ["Ile to kosztuje?", "How much is it?"], ["Poproszę rachunek", "The bill, please"]]},
  "morocco": {"around": "CTM and Supratours buses link most towns comfortably and cheaply, and you can book on their websites or at the station a day ahead for popular routes like Marrakech to Merzouga or Chefchaouen. ONCF trains cover the north and the coast, including the Al Boraq high-speed line between Tangier, Rabat and Casablanca. Grand taxis are shared between towns and leave when full, while petit taxis work inside cities, so insist on the meter or agree a price first. Ride apps like inDrive work in some big cities, though they sit in a legal grey area and taxis are still the norm.", "money": "Morocco uses the dirham, which is a closed currency, so you'll get it from ATMs once you land rather than at home. Morocco runs on cash: ATMs are easy to find in towns and cities, but cards mostly only work in bigger hotels, supermarkets and upmarket restaurants. Haggling is part of the fun in the souks, so start low, smile, and be ready to walk away. Small tips for guides, porters, cafe waiters and anyone who helps you are expected, so carry a stash of coins and small notes.", "sleep": "Riads and small hostels inside the old medinas are the classic cheap stay, with rooftop terraces and breakfast included, and Marrakech, Fes, Essaouira and Chefchaouen are full of them. Merzouga and the Sahara have simple desert camps and guesthouses, often bundled with a camel ride. In Imlil near Toubkal, rooms are basic gîtes and mountain refuges.", "eat": ["Tagine: slow-cooked stew of meat or vegetables in a clay pot", "Harira: tomato, lentil and chickpea soup, big during Ramadan", "Msemen: flaky square flatbread, eaten with honey or cheese", "Mint tea: sweet green tea with fresh mint, poured from height"], "data": "A local SIM from Maroc Telecom, Orange or inwi is cheap and quick to set up with your passport, often handed out free at the airport. Coverage is good in towns but patchy in the mountains and desert.", "heads_up": "In the medinas, people may tell you a street is closed or offer to show you the way, then lead you to a shop or ask for money, so a polite no and an offline map help.", "food": [{"name": "Tagine", "wiki": "Tagine", "desc": "Slow-cooked stew of meat or vegetables in a cone-shaped clay pot, often with preserved lemon, olives or prunes. Roadside cafes serve cheap ones everywhere.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Lunch, the main meal of the day", "order": "Kefta with egg is the cheap, tasty classic"}, {"name": "Couscous", "wiki": "Couscous", "desc": "Steamed semolina piled with vegetables and meat, traditionally eaten on Fridays after prayers.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Friday lunch after prayers", "order": "Seven vegetable couscous is the standard version"}, {"name": "Harira", "wiki": "Harira", "desc": "Tomato, lentil and chickpea soup, the dish that breaks the fast during Ramadan and costs pennies at stalls.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Breaking the fast in Ramadan, and evenings", "order": "Comes with dates and chebakia sweets"}, {"name": "Msemen", "wiki": "Msemmen", "desc": "Flaky square flatbread fried on a griddle, eaten for breakfast with honey, cheese or amlou.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Breakfast with tea", "order": "Try it with honey, or stuffed with onion and spices"}, {"name": "Pastilla", "wiki": "Pastilla", "desc": "Sweet and savoury pie of pigeon or chicken with almonds and cinnamon, dusted with sugar, a Fes speciality.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Weddings and celebrations, Fes especially", "order": "Seafood pastilla is a savoury alternative"}, {"name": "Mint tea", "wiki": "Maghrebi mint tea", "desc": "Sweet green tea with fresh mint, poured from a height to make foam. You'll be offered it everywhere, so get used to the sugar.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "All day, with every visit", "order": "Say 'shwiya sukkar' for less sugar"}], "food_tip": "Eat where locals eat, at food stalls and small cafes away from the main squares, where a tagine or sandwich costs a few euros. Jemaa el-Fnaa food stalls are fun but agree prices first and watch for unordered extras on the bill.", "culture": ["Morocco is a Muslim country, so dress modestly with shoulders and knees covered, especially in the medinas and rural villages, while beach towns like Taghazout are more relaxed.", "Non-Muslims can't enter most mosques, with the Hassan II Mosque in Casablanca a notable exception that runs tours.", "Use your right hand for eating and giving things, and wash hands before shared meals, often eaten with bread from a communal dish.", "During Ramadan, avoid eating, drinking or smoking in public during daylight, and expect shorter opening hours.", "Always ask before photographing people, as many don't want to be photographed and some will ask for money.", "Public displays of affection are frowned upon, so keep it subtle."], "safety": "Morocco is generally safe, and the hassle is more tiring than dangerous: fake guides, henna ladies grabbing your hand and kids offering directions all want money at the end, so a firm, friendly \"la, shukran\" (no thanks) works wonders. Women travelling alone may get catcalls and unwanted attention, so dressing modestly and walking with confidence helps. Agree prices before any service, from taxis to henna, and watch for pickpockets in crowded squares like Jemaa el-Fnaa. Hashish will be offered constantly in the Rif mountains around Chefchaouen, but it's illegal and some sellers work with police.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch, from about 1 to 3pm, is the biggest meal, and dinner often starts after 8pm.", "Stick to bottled or filtered water, as tap water is treated in cities but can upset stomachs.", "Vegetarians do well with vegetable tagines, lentils, bissara soup and salads, though stock may contain meat.", "Leave about 10 percent in sit-down restaurants and a few dirhams for café service.", "Bread is cheap, sold everywhere, and often doubles as your cutlery."], "drinks": [{"name": "Fresh orange juice", "desc": "Squeezed in front of you at stalls in every medina square, for $0.50 to $1.50 a glass."}, {"name": "Avocado smoothie", "desc": "A thick blend of avocado, milk and sugar, sometimes with dates or almonds. Expect $1 to $2 at juice bars."}], "greet": "People say 'salaam alaikum' with a handshake, then often touch their heart. Friends of the same sex kiss on the cheeks, and men should wait for a woman to offer her hand.", "dress": "Loose, light layers that cover shoulders and knees work best, and a scarf is handy for women. Shorts are fine on surf beaches but stand out in cities.", "table": "Wash your hands before eating, eat from the part of the shared dish in front of you, and use bread to scoop. Hosts will urge you to eat more, and saying 'alhamdulillah' signals you're full.", "faith": "Morocco is almost entirely Sunni Muslim, with a small, long-established Jewish community. Admire mosques from outside unless they welcome visitors, and keep your voice down during the call to prayer.", "phrases": [["Salaam alaikum (السلام عليكم)", "Hello"], ["Shukran (شكرا)", "Thank you"], ["Bshhal? (بشحال؟)", "How much?"], ["La, shukran (لا شكرا)", "No, thank you"], ["Bslama (بالسلامة)", "Goodbye"]]},
  "peru": {"around": "Overnight buses are the backbone of travel, and premium companies like Cruz del Sur, Oltursa and Movil Bus are worth the small extra for safety, reclining seats and fewer stops. Uber, Cabify, DiDi and inDrive work in Lima, Arequipa and Cusco. Book Machu Picchu entry tickets and Inca Trail permits weeks or months ahead in the dry season, and train seats to Aguas Calientes too. Domestic flights on LATAM, Sky or JetSMART save long bus rides if booked early.", "money": "Peru uses the sol, and while cards work in cities, you'll need cash for markets, colectivos, small guesthouses and anywhere rural. Bank ATMs from BCP, Interbank or BBVA usually work best, though many charge a per-withdrawal fee, and some machines also dispense US dollars. Yape is the local payment app everyone uses, but it's tricky for foreigners. Tipping around 10 percent is appreciated in sit-down restaurants, and polite haggling is normal at craft markets.", "sleep": "Hostel dorms and simple family guesthouses are easy to find in Lima's Miraflores and Barranco, Cusco, Arequipa and Huaraz. Huacachina has a handful of small hostels around the oasis, and Lake Titicaca has homestays on the islands. Treks like Salkantay use basic lodges or camping set up by tour operators.", "eat": ["Ceviche: raw fish cured in lime juice with onion and chili", "Lomo saltado: stir-fried beef with onions, tomatoes and fries", "Ají de gallina: shredded chicken in a creamy yellow chili sauce", "Chicha morada: sweet, spiced drink made from purple corn"], "data": "Local SIMs from Claro, Movistar, Entel or Bitel need passport registration at an official store, so an eSIM is often simpler. Claro and Movistar reach furthest into the mountains, but coverage thins out on high-altitude treks.", "heads_up": "Cusco sits at about 3,400 metres, so take your first days slowly and drink water to ease altitude sickness.", "food": [{"name": "Ceviche", "wiki": "Ceviche", "desc": "Raw fish cured in lime juice with red onion, chili and sweet potato. Best at lunch in Lima's cevicherias, as locals say it should never be eaten at night.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Lunch only, at cevicherías", "order": "Try leche de tigre, the citrus marinade, as a shot"}, {"name": "Lomo saltado", "wiki": "Lomo saltado", "desc": "Stir-fried beef with onions, tomatoes and soy sauce, served with fries and rice, a tasty mix of Chinese and Peruvian food.", "price": "$5 to $9", "when": "Lunch or dinner at local diners", "order": "Comes with both rice and fries"}, {"name": "Ají de gallina", "wiki": "Ají de gallina", "desc": "Shredded chicken in a creamy yellow chili and walnut sauce, served with rice and potatoes.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch, often on the menú del día"}, {"name": "Anticuchos", "wiki": "Anticucho", "desc": "Grilled beef heart skewers marinated in spices, sold by street vendors in the evening.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Evenings at street carts", "order": "Beef heart is classic; comes with potato and corn"}, {"name": "Pisco sour", "wiki": "Pisco sour", "desc": "The national cocktail of pisco grape brandy, lime, sugar and egg white, frothy and strong. Happy hours in Cusco and Lima make it cheap.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Evenings, with dinner or at bars", "order": "A chilcano, pisco with ginger ale, is lighter"}, {"name": "Chicha morada", "wiki": "Chicha morada", "desc": "A sweet, spiced drink made from boiled purple corn with pineapple and cinnamon, usually included with cheap set lunches.", "price": "$0.50 to $2", "when": "With lunch, usually from a jug"}], "food_tip": "Look for menú del día set lunches, which give you soup, a main and a drink for a few dollars. Markets like San Pedro in Cusco are cheap for juices and meals.", "culture": ["Say \"buenos días\" or \"buenas tardes\" when you walk into a shop, a small restaurant or a colectivo, as launching straight into a request feels rude.", "Peruvians are hugely proud of their food and Inca heritage, and suggesting pisco is Chilean is a quick way to start a heated debate.", "Dress respectfully in churches like Cusco Cathedral, where photos inside are often not allowed.", "In the Andes many people speak Quechua or Aymara, and learning a word or two like \"sulpayki\" (thank you in Quechua) earns big smiles.", "Women in traditional dress posing with baby alpacas in Cusco expect a small tip for photos, and it's polite to ask anyone else before you snap away.", "Coca leaves are legal and widely chewed or brewed as tea for altitude, but don't carry them home, as they're illegal in most other countries."], "safety": "Peru is fine for most backpackers, but bag theft on buses and in bus stations is the classic loss, so keep your daypack on your lap rather than in the overhead rack. Only use app taxis or ones your hostel calls, especially from Lima airport and at night, since robberies in unlicensed cabs do happen. Counterfeit soles notes circulate, so check larger bills and change money at banks rather than with street changers. Altitude is the bigger health issue, so spend a couple of easy days in Cusco or Arequipa before any trek.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch, from about 1 to 3pm, is the main meal, and seafood places often close by late afternoon.", "Don't drink the tap water; refill big bottles or bring a filter.", "Vegetarians should ask for 'sin carne' and check soups, which often use meat stock; Lima and Cusco have plenty of veggie cafés.", "Tip about 10 percent in sit-down restaurants, but check whether service is already on the bill.", "Chifa, Peruvian Chinese food, is everywhere and serves big, cheap portions of fried rice and noodles."], "drinks": [{"name": "Inca Kola", "desc": "A bright yellow soda that tastes a bit like bubblegum, and a national favourite. About $1 a bottle."}, {"name": "Mate de coca", "desc": "Tea brewed from coca leaves, drunk in the Andes to help with altitude. It's free in many hostels or about $1 in cafés."}], "greet": "Men shake hands, and women greet friends and new acquaintances with a single cheek kiss. Address older people as señor or señora.", "dress": "Pack layers for the Andes, where days are warm and nights get cold, and a light jacket for Lima's grey winter. Cover shoulders and knees in churches.", "table": "Keep your hands visible above the table and wait for the host to say 'buen provecho'. It's polite to say 'buen provecho' to other diners when you leave.", "faith": "Peru is mainly Catholic, often blended with Andean traditions like offerings to Pachamama, Mother Earth. Treat ceremonies, shrines and apachetas (stone cairns on mountain passes) with respect, and don't take stones from them.", "phrases": [["Hola", "Hello"], ["Gracias", "Thank you"], ["¿Cuánto cuesta?", "How much is it?"], ["Sin picante, por favor", "Not spicy, please"], ["Allillanchu? (Quechua)", "How are you?"]]},
  "bolivia": {"around": "Long-distance buses are dirt cheap, and paying a little more for a cama or semi-cama overnight service with a reclining seat is worth it on bumpy routes. La Paz's Mi Teleférico cable car network is a cheap and spectacular way across the city, and inDrive and Uber work in the bigger cities. Domestic flights on BoA save long rides to Rurrenabaque or Santa Cruz but get cancelled a fair bit. Compare several agencies in Uyuni before booking a salt flat jeep tour, and check reviews for the driver and vehicle.", "money": "Bolivia uses the boliviano and is very much a cash country outside upscale hotels and restaurants, so stock up in La Paz, Sucre or Santa Cruz before heading anywhere remote like Uyuni or Rurrenabaque. Bolivia has had serious dollar shortages lately, so clean, undamaged US dollar notes are worth bringing, as they often fetch a much better rate at exchange houses than ATMs or cards. ATM withdrawals can be limited and fee-heavy, so check the current situation with other travellers. Small tips are welcome, and friendly haggling is normal at markets but not in shops with set prices.", "sleep": "Hostel dorms and family-run hostales are cheap in La Paz, Sucre and Copacabana, and Sucre is a favourite place to slow down and take Spanish classes. Uyuni tours use basic shared refuges, some built from salt, with limited heating, so bring a warm sleeping bag. The Amazon around Rurrenabaque has jungle and pampas lodges as part of tours.", "eat": ["Salteña: juicy baked pastry filled with meat stew", "Silpancho: thin breaded beef over rice and potatoes, topped with egg", "Api con pastel: hot purple corn drink with a cheese-filled fried pastry", "Sopa de maní: creamy peanut soup, often topped with fries"], "data": "Entel and Tigo SIMs are cheap but need passport registration, and Entel has the widest coverage. Coverage is good in cities but patchy or absent on the altiplano and salt flats.", "heads_up": "Road blockades (bloqueos) during protests can stop buses for hours or days, so leave slack in your plans and keep an eye on local news.", "food": [{"name": "Salteña", "wiki": "Salteña", "desc": "A juicy baked pastry filled with sweet and spicy meat stew, eaten mid-morning. Bite the top and slurp carefully, as the filling is hot and runny.", "price": "$0.70 to $1.50", "when": "Mid-morning snack, before noon", "order": "Hold it upright and bite the top corner"}, {"name": "Silpancho", "wiki": "Silpancho", "desc": "A thin breaded beef cutlet over rice and potatoes, topped with fried egg and tomato salsa, a big cheap Cochabamba favourite.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch or dinner in Cochabamba"}, {"name": "Sopa de maní", "wiki": "Peanut soup", "desc": "Creamy peanut soup, often topped with crispy fries, served as the first course of cheap set lunches.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "First course of the set lunch"}, {"name": "Pique macho", "wiki": "Pique macho", "desc": "A mountain of beef, sausage, fries, egg and chili meant for sharing, ideal after a big night or a big hike.", "price": "$4 to $10", "when": "Dinner or after drinks, for sharing", "order": "Ask for less locoto (chilli) if you prefer it mild"}, {"name": "Anticuchos", "wiki": "Anticucho", "desc": "Grilled beef heart skewers with potatoes and peanut sauce, sold by street vendors at night.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Evening, from street grills", "order": "Ask for extra peanut sauce (salsa de maní)"}, {"name": "Api", "wiki": "Api (beverage)", "desc": "A thick, hot purple corn drink spiced with cinnamon, drunk at breakfast with a cheese-filled fried pastry.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Early morning, at market stalls", "order": "Pair it with a pastel, a fried cheese pastry"}], "food_tip": "Set lunches (almuerzo) at local spots give you soup, a main and juice for a few dollars, and markets like Mercado Lanza in La Paz or Mercado Central in Sucre are even cheaper.", "culture": ["Bolivia has a large Indigenous population, mainly Aymara and Quechua, and many people are proud of their cultures, so show respect and ask before photographing anyone.", "Cholitas, Indigenous women in bowler hats and wide skirts, are a symbol of national pride, so don't treat them as a photo prop without asking.", "Greetings matter, so say \"buenos días\" before asking a question, and shake hands when meeting people.", "Pachamama is honoured with offerings, and you might see llama fetuses sold at La Paz's Witches' Market for these rituals.", "Dress modestly in churches and rural villages, and avoid political debates during protests."], "safety": "Bolivia is mostly safe for backpackers, but bag theft on buses and fake police scams in La Paz exist, so never get into a taxi with strangers or hand your passport to plain-clothes \"officers\". Use radio taxis or apps at night. Altitude is a real issue in La Paz and Uyuni, so take it slowly. Choose reputable operators for Death Road cycling and Uyuni tours, as cheap ones cut corners on brakes and drivers.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, served from about noon to 2pm, and many kitchens close in the afternoon.", "Don't drink tap water; buy bottled water or use a filter.", "Markets have comedor sections where you share benches with locals and eat set meals cheaply.", "Vegetarian options are limited outside tourist areas, but ask for \"sin carne\" and look for quinoa dishes.", "Tipping isn't expected at cheap places, but around 10 percent is welcome in tourist restaurants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Singani", "desc": "A grape brandy from the southern valleys, most often drunk in a chuflay with ginger ale and lime. Around $2 to $4 a glass in bars."}, {"name": "Mate de coca", "desc": "Coca leaf tea that helps with altitude and is served in most hotels and cafes for under $1."}], "greet": "Shake hands and say \"buenos días\" or \"buenas tardes\", and women friends often greet with a cheek kiss. Using \"señor\" or \"señora\" is polite.", "dress": "Dress in layers, as highland days are warm and nights freezing. Cities are fairly conservative, so skip very short shorts.", "table": "Wait for everyone to be served before eating, and say \"provecho\" to others. Pouring a little of your drink on the ground for Pachamama is a common gesture.", "faith": "Most Bolivians are Catholic, often blended with Andean beliefs in Pachamama and mountain spirits. Be quiet in churches and don't touch offerings or ritual items.", "phrases": [["Buenos días", "Good morning"], ["Gracias", "Thank you"], ["¿Cuánto es?", "How much is it?"], ["Sin carne, por favor", "No meat, please"], ["Kamisaraki", "Hello, how are you? (Aymara)"]]},
  "guatemala": {"around": "Tourist shuttles link Antigua, Lake Atitlán, Semuc Champey and Flores, and any hostel can book you a seat the day before. Chicken buses, old US school buses painted in wild colours, are dirt cheap and an experience in themselves, but they're crowded, driven hard and bags on the roof can wander, so keep valuables on you. Public lanchas zip between the lake villages, and Uber works in Guatemala City and Antigua. For Flores and Tikal, an overnight bus from the capital or a short domestic flight saves a long day.", "money": "Guatemala uses the quetzal, and you'll want cash for chicken buses, lanchas, market stalls and many guesthouses, while cards work in Antigua's nicer cafes and hotels. Card skimming is a known problem, so use ATMs inside banks, malls or supermarkets, with 5B and Banco Industrial machines the most common, and expect a small fee. US dollars are sometimes taken in tourist spots but at rubbish rates. Leave about 10 percent in tourist restaurants if it isn't already on the bill as \"propina\", and haggle with a smile at markets like Chichicastenango.", "sleep": "Antigua is packed with hostels, from party spots with rooftop bars to quiet ones with volcano views, and Lake Atitlán has a village for every mood, with San Pedro for socialising and San Marcos for yoga and calm. Semuc Champey has rustic jungle lodges by the river, and Flores has cheap hostels on its little island, handy for an early start to Tikal. Private rooms in family guesthouses often cost barely more than a dorm bed.", "eat": ["Pepián: rich stew of meat in a roasted seed and chili sauce", "Kak'ik: spicy Mayan turkey soup with a red broth", "Chuchitos: small tamales stuffed with meat and tomato sauce", "Rellenitos: sweet plantain dumplings filled with black beans and chocolate"], "data": "Tigo and Claro SIMs are cheap and quick to set up with a passport, and coverage is good in towns but weaker on the lake trails and in Petén's jungle. Hostel wifi is usually fine but can be flaky around the lake.", "heads_up": "Use bank ATMs in daylight, and don't hike Atitlán's volcanoes or lakeside trails alone, since robberies have happened on isolated paths.", "food": [{"name": "Pepián", "wiki": "Pepián", "desc": "A rich, smoky stew of chicken or beef in a sauce of roasted seeds, tomatoes and chilies, often called the national dish. Look for it at comedores in Antigua.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Lunch, at comedores and festivals", "order": "Comes with rice and tamalitos, ask for both"}, {"name": "Kak'ik", "wiki": "Kak'ik", "desc": "Spicy red turkey soup with deep Mayan roots, from the Cobán area, fragrant with coriander and chilies.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Lunch in Cobán and Alta Verapaz"}, {"name": "Fiambre", "wiki": "Fiambre", "desc": "A giant cold salad of cured meats, sausages, cheeses and pickled vegetables, made by families only around Día de Todos los Santos on 1 November. If you're there then, don't miss it.", "price": "$8 to $15", "when": "Around 1 November, Day of the Dead", "order": "Ask families or markets, it's seasonal"}, {"name": "Rellenitos", "wiki": "Rellenitos de plátano", "desc": "Mashed plantain dumplings filled with sweetened black beans and chocolate, fried and dusted with sugar. Weird on paper, great in real life.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Afternoon snack, from street carts"}, {"name": "Hilachas", "wiki": "Hilachas", "desc": "Shredded beef stewed in a tomato and tomatillo sauce with potatoes and carrots, a homely dish served with rice and tortillas at comedores.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Lunch at comedores", "order": "Comes with rice and corn tortillas"}, {"name": "Atol de elote", "wiki": "Atole", "desc": "A warm, sweet corn drink sold by street vendors in the evening, perfect on chilly highland nights.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Evenings, from street vendors", "order": "Ask for it with corn kernels inside"}], "food_tip": "Comedores, small family eateries, do a menú del día of soup, a main, tortillas and a drink for a few dollars, and markets are even cheaper. Antigua's tourist restaurants cost several times more, so walk a few blocks off the main streets.", "culture": ["More than 40 percent of Guatemalans are Indigenous Maya, and many speak one of over 20 Mayan languages first, so a few local words beyond Spanish go down very well.", "Always ask before photographing people, especially women and children in traditional traje, and accept a no gracefully.", "Say \"buenos días\" when you enter shops and buses, and use the polite \"usted\" with older people.", "At Chichicastenango's Santo Tomás church and other sacred sites, Maya ceremonies with incense and candles take place, so watch quietly and don't take photos inside.", "Dress modestly in highland villages and churches, which are fairly conservative.", "Bargain at markets, but keep it friendly and fair, as the difference is usually tiny for you and big for the seller."], "safety": "Guatemala has a rough reputation, but most backpackers stick to the Antigua, Atitlán, Semuc and Flores circuit and have no problems. Petty theft is the main risk, so keep phones out of sight in Guatemala City, avoid walking alone at night and don't flash cash, and in the capital stick to areas like Zona 4 and Zona 10. Hike volcanoes like Acatenango and Pacaya with a reputable guided group, and use shuttles, tuk-tuks or Uber rather than walking between villages after dark.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch, usually around 1pm, is the main meal, and comedores often run out of the daily special by mid-afternoon.", "Markets have food halls upstairs or at the back where you sit at a counter and point at what's cooking.", "Don't drink tap water; refill from the big garrafón jugs at hostels instead of buying small bottles.", "Vegetarians can rely on black beans, eggs, cheese, tortillas and fried plantains, though many soups use meat stock.", "Restaurants often add a 10% propina to the bill, so check before tipping extra; comedores don't expect tips."], "drinks": [{"name": "Licuado", "desc": "A fresh fruit smoothie made with water or milk at market stalls. Expect $1.50 to $3."}, {"name": "Quetzalteca", "desc": "A cheap sugarcane aguardiente from Quetzaltenango, often flavoured with hibiscus or tamarind. A shot costs around $1 to $2 in local bars."}], "greet": "Shake hands and say buenos días, buenas tardes or buenas noches. Friends and women often greet with a light kiss on the cheek.", "dress": "Bring layers, as highland towns like Antigua and Xela get cold at night. Dress modestly in villages and churches, keeping shorts for the coast and lakeside.", "table": "Say buen provecho to others when you start or when passing a table. Keep your hands above the table, and tortillas double as your spoon.", "faith": "Most Guatemalans are Catholic or Evangelical Christian, and many Maya blend Christianity with traditional Maya spirituality. Keep quiet during ceremonies, don't step into ritual circles and never photograph inside Chichicastenango's church.", "phrases": [["Buenos días", "Good morning"], ["¿Cuánto cuesta?", "How much is it?"], ["Muchas gracias", "Thank you very much"], ["¡Qué chilero!", "How cool! (local slang)"], ["Maltiox", "Thank you (K'iche' Maya)"]]},
  "mexico": {"around": "First-class buses like ADO and ETN are comfy, safe and easy to book on their apps, and overnight routes save you a night's bed. Budget airlines like Volaris and Viva Aerobus are often cheaper than the bus for long hops if you book early and travel light, as bags cost extra. Uber, DiDi and inDrive work in most cities, and the Mexico City Metro costs just a few pesos a ride. The Tren Maya now links Cancún, Tulum, Mérida, Palenque and other Yucatán stops, but check timetables, as services are still bedding in.", "money": "Mexico uses the peso, and cards work in cities, chain shops and most restaurants, but you'll need cash for markets, taco stands, colectivos and small towns. Use ATMs attached to banks like BBVA, Banorte or Santander, which usually charge less, and always decline the machine's own currency conversion. Tip 10 to 15 percent in sit-down restaurants, and a few pesos for the person bagging your groceries or pumping your petrol. Haggling is only really done gently at craft markets.", "sleep": "Hostel dorms are easy to find in Mexico City, Oaxaca, San Cristóbal, Puerto Escondido and along the Yucatán, and many are seriously social with rooftop bars. Small family-run posadas offer good-value private rooms, especially in colonial towns. Tulum and Playa del Carmen are much pricier than the rest of the country, so balance them with cheaper stops like Valladolid or Bacalar.", "eat": ["Tacos al pastor: spit-roasted pork tacos with pineapple and salsa", "Tlayuda: large crisp Oaxacan tortilla with beans, cheese and meat", "Mole negro: dark, complex sauce of chilies, spices and chocolate", "Tamales: steamed corn dough parcels with savory or sweet fillings"], "data": "Telcel has the widest coverage and SIMs are sold in convenience stores like Oxxo, though new lines now need ID registration under the national mobile registry, so an eSIM can save time. Wifi is common in cafes and hostels.", "heads_up": "Drink bottled or filtered water, and use official taxis or apps rather than flagging down cabs on the street at night.", "food": [{"name": "Tacos al pastor", "wiki": "Al pastor", "desc": "Spit-roasted marinated pork shaved onto small tortillas with pineapple, onion and coriander. Mexico City taquerías serve them late into the night for a few pesos each.", "price": "$0.70 to $1.50 per taco", "when": "Late night, at taquerías", "order": "Ask for \"con todo\" to get onion, coriander and pineapple"}, {"name": "Tlayuda", "wiki": "Tlayuda", "desc": "A huge, crisp Oaxacan tortilla spread with beans, lard, stringy quesillo cheese and meat, folded or flat, cooked over coals at night markets.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Evening, at Oaxaca's markets and street grills", "order": "Add tasajo (dried beef) or chorizo on top"}, {"name": "Mole", "wiki": "Mole (sauce)", "desc": "A dark, complex sauce of chilies, spices, nuts and a hint of chocolate, poured over chicken. Oaxaca and Puebla are famous for theirs.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Lunch, often at family celebrations", "order": "Mole negro is the dark Oaxacan classic"}, {"name": "Chilaquiles", "wiki": "Chilaquiles", "desc": "Fried tortilla chips simmered in red or green salsa and topped with cream, cheese and an egg, the ultimate hangover breakfast.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Breakfast, at fondas and cafes", "order": "Choose salsa verde or roja, and add an egg"}, {"name": "Tamales", "wiki": "Tamale", "desc": "Steamed corn dough parcels with savory or sweet fillings, sold from street carts and bicycles in the morning.", "price": "$0.70 to $1.50", "when": "Breakfast, from street carts", "order": "Try a guajolota, a tamal stuffed in a bread roll"}, {"name": "Esquites", "wiki": "Esquites", "desc": "Corn kernels in a cup with mayo, cheese, chili and lime, sold by street vendors in the evening for small change. Also comes on the cob as elote.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Evening, from street carts in plazas", "order": "Say \"sin chile\" if you don't want the heat"}], "food_tip": "Street tacos, market comedores and comida corrida set lunches are the cheapest way to eat well, often a few dollars for a big meal. Busy stalls with high turnover are your best bet for fresh food.", "culture": ["Greet people with \"buenos días\" or \"buenas tardes\", and a handshake or a single cheek kiss among friends is normal.", "Mexico is mostly Catholic, and churches are places of worship, so dress respectfully and keep quiet during services.", "Día de los Muertos, around 1 and 2 November, is a family celebration of the dead rather than a Halloween party, so be respectful at cemeteries and altars.", "Mexicans are polite and indirect, and a soft \"ahorita\" (in a little while) can mean anything from now to never.", "In Indigenous communities like Chamula near San Cristóbal, photography inside churches is strictly forbidden, and taking photos of people without asking can cause real offence."], "safety": "Most backpackers travel Mexico without any trouble, as cartel violence rarely targets tourists, but check your government's advice for specific states before you go. Use app taxis or official ones at night, keep phones out of sight in crowded areas, and watch for card skimming at standalone ATMs. In party towns like Tulum and Playa del Carmen, be careful with drinks and avoid buying drugs, which fund violence and get people into serious trouble.", "eat_beats": ["Mexicans eat a big lunch, the comida, between about 2pm and 4pm, with a lighter dinner later.", "Don't drink tap water; restaurants use purified water and ice, and big refill jugs (garrafones) are cheap.", "Vegetarians can ask for \"sin carne\" and look for quesadillas, chiles rellenos and nopales, but beans may be cooked with lard.", "Street salsas can be fierce, so taste a drop first.", "Tip 10 to 15 percent in sit-down restaurants, while small change is fine at street stalls."], "drinks": [{"name": "Mezcal", "desc": "Smoky agave spirit, mostly from Oaxaca, sipped slowly with orange slices and worm salt. A shot in a mezcalería costs $2 to $5."}, {"name": "Agua fresca", "desc": "Cold drinks made from fruit, flowers or rice, like horchata and jamaica, ladled from big glass jars for about $1."}], "greet": "A handshake is normal for strangers, and women often greet friends with a single cheek kiss. Greet everyone in a room when you arrive.", "dress": "Cities like Mexico City are a bit dressier than beach towns, and locals rarely wear shorts there. Cover shoulders and knees in churches.", "table": "Keep both hands visible on the table, and say \"provecho\" to people eating nearby. Tacos are eaten by hand, never with a knife and fork.", "faith": "Most Mexicans are Catholic, and the Virgin of Guadalupe is a hugely important national symbol. Be quiet in churches, and don't take photos during Mass.", "phrases": [["Buenos días", "Good morning"], ["Gracias", "Thank you"], ["¿Cuánto cuesta?", "How much does it cost?"], ["Sin chile, por favor", "No chilli, please"], ["La cuenta, por favor", "The bill, please"]]},
  "colombia": {"around": "Comfortable long-distance buses link most cities, but the mountains make journeys slow, and domestic flights on Avianca, LATAM or JetSMART are often cheap and save long, twisty drives. Medellín's Metro and cable cars are cheap and easy, and apps like Uber, DiDi, inDrive and Cabify are widely used. Book flights to Cartagena and Santa Marta early for December and January, as prices jump.", "money": "Colombia uses the peso, and with all those zeros a beer can cost thousands, so don't panic at prices. Cards work in cities and most hostels, but carry cash for buses, small shops, markets and the coffee region. ATMs charge a fee per withdrawal and some have low limits, with Bancolombia and Davivienda machines among the most common. Restaurants often add a voluntary 10 percent service charge to the bill, which you can decline, and light haggling is fine at markets and with taxis that don't use meters.", "sleep": "Medellín, Salento, Cartagena, Santa Marta and Bogotá all have plenty of hostel dorms, many of them very social. The coffee region has rooms on working fincas, and Minca and Palomino on the Caribbean coast have jungle and beach hostels. Expect higher prices on the coast in peak season.", "eat": ["Bandeja paisa: big plate of beans, rice, pork, egg and arepa", "Ajiaco: Bogotá chicken and potato soup with corn and capers", "Arepa: grilled corn cake, plain or stuffed with cheese", "Aguapanela: drink of dissolved cane sugar, served hot or cold"], "data": "Claro has the best coverage and prepaid SIMs are easy to buy with a passport, though an eSIM skips the store visit. Wifi is good in cities and hostels.", "heads_up": "Locals say \"no dar papaya\", meaning don't make yourself an easy target, so keep phones out of sight on the street and be careful with drinks and dating-app meetups.", "food": [{"name": "Bandeja paisa", "wiki": "Bandeja paisa", "desc": "A huge platter of beans, rice, minced meat, chorizo, crispy pork belly, fried egg, plantain and arepa. Come hungry in Medellín.", "price": "$5 to $9", "when": "Big lunch in Medellín and the Andes", "order": "Share one, it's a huge plate"}, {"name": "Ajiaco", "wiki": "Ajiaco", "desc": "Bogotá's chicken and potato soup with corn, capers and cream, perfect for the city's chilly weather.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Lunch in Bogotá, especially when chilly", "order": "Add the cream and capers yourself"}, {"name": "Arepa", "wiki": "Arepa", "desc": "A grilled corn cake, plain or stuffed with cheese, eaten with almost every meal and sold on every corner.", "price": "$0.50 to $2", "when": "Breakfast or street snack all day", "order": "Try arepa de huevo on the Caribbean coast"}, {"name": "Empanadas", "wiki": "Empanada", "desc": "Fried corn pastries filled with meat and potato, a cheap snack everywhere with ají sauce.", "price": "$0.30 to $1 each", "when": "Snack from street stands", "order": "Spoon on ají, the fresh chilli salsa"}, {"name": "Sancocho", "wiki": "Sancocho", "desc": "A hearty soup of chicken, fish or beef with plantain, yuca, potato and corn, a Sunday family favourite and often part of cheap set lunches.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Sunday family lunch or a menú soup", "order": "Coast versions use fish, inland ones chicken"}, {"name": "Aguapanela", "wiki": "Aguapanela", "desc": "A simple drink of dissolved panela cane sugar, served hot with a lump of cheese melting into it or cold with lime, and very cheap.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Breakfast, or hot when it's cold", "order": "Have it hot with a chunk of cheese in it"}], "food_tip": "A menú del día or corrientazo lunch with soup, a main and juice costs a few dollars at local restaurants. Fresh fruit juices and street arepas are cheap snacks.", "culture": ["Colombians are warm and very polite, so greet people with \"buenos días\" and a handshake, and expect a bit of small talk before getting down to business.", "Don't joke about Pablo Escobar or narco culture, as many Colombians lost family to the violence and find it offensive.", "People dress fairly smartly in cities, especially Bogotá, so save the beachwear and flip-flops for the coast.", "Colombia is mostly Catholic, so dress respectfully in churches, and avoid making light of religion.", "Tipping and service charges are listed on bills as \"propina voluntaria\", and it really is voluntary, though it's usual to pay it.", "Salsa and dancing are a big part of social life, especially in Cali, so don't be shy about joining in, nobody cares if you're bad."], "safety": "Colombia is much safer than its old reputation suggests, but petty theft is common, so keep phones out of sight on the street and never resist if someone does try to rob you. Use apps rather than hailing taxis, especially at night. Scopolamine drugging happens, often through dating apps or strangers in bars, so watch your drink and be careful with new acquaintances. Some rural and border areas still have armed groups, so check advice before heading far off the main circuit.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, served from noon to 2pm, and dinner is lighter, often an arepa or soup.", "Tap water is safe in Bogotá and Medellín, but stick to bottled or filtered water on the coast and in rural areas.", "Vegetarians can ask for \"sin carne\" and live on arepas, rice, beans, patacones and juices, though beans are sometimes cooked with pork.", "Fruit stalls in markets sell exotic fruits like lulo, guanábana and granadilla, and vendors will often let you taste first.", "Restaurants add a voluntary 10% tip to bills, and you can say no if you prefer."], "drinks": [{"name": "Tinto", "desc": "A small black coffee sold from flasks on street corners, usually sweet. Usually under $0.50."}, {"name": "Aguardiente", "desc": "Anise-flavoured cane spirit, shared from a bottle at parties and drunk in small shots. A bottle costs around $10 to $15."}], "greet": "Shake hands with men, and women usually greet with one cheek kiss. Always say \"buenos días\" or \"buenas tardes\" before asking anything.", "dress": "Cities, especially Bogotá, are dressier and cooler than you expect, so pack jeans and a jacket. On the Caribbean coast shorts are fine, but not in churches.", "table": "Say \"buen provecho\" to people at the table when you start or leave. Keep both hands visible and wait for your host to begin.", "faith": "Most Colombians are Roman Catholic, with growing evangelical churches. Cover shoulders and knees, remove hats and keep quiet during Mass.", "phrases": [["¿Qué más?", "How's it going?"], ["Muchas gracias", "Thank you very much"], ["¿Cuánto vale?", "How much is it?"], ["Sin picante", "Not spicy"], ["Bacano", "Cool, great"]]},
  "argentina": {"around": "Long-distance buses with semi-cama and cama seats cover the country and are comfortable, often with meals, but distances are huge, so budget flights on Aerolíneas Argentinas, Flybondi or JetSMART often make sense for Patagonia. Uber, Cabify and DiDi work in Buenos Aires and other big cities, and the SUBE card covers local buses and the Subte. Book flights and El Chaltén beds early for the December to February peak.", "money": "Argentina uses the peso, and after years of crazy parallel exchange rates things have calmed down: foreign cards now get a fair rate, so paying by card is the easy option almost everywhere. Local ATMs charge stiff fees and have low withdrawal limits, so some travellers use transfer services like Western Union to pick up pesos instead. Clean US dollar notes are a handy backup for exchange houses. Tip about 10 percent in restaurants, ideally in cash.", "sleep": "Hostel dorms are common in Buenos Aires, Mendoza, Bariloche and El Chaltén, and Patagonia has campsites, including free ones in Los Glaciares National Park near El Chaltén. Prices in Patagonia are noticeably higher than in the north. Salta and Jujuy have cheap, charming guesthouses.", "eat": ["Asado: slow-grilled beef and sausages cooked over wood or charcoal", "Empanadas: baked pastries with regional fillings like beef or cheese", "Choripán: grilled chorizo sausage in bread with chimichurri", "Mate: bitter herbal tea shared from a gourd with a metal straw"], "data": "Personal, Claro and Movistar SIMs are sold with a passport, and coverage is good in cities but very limited on Patagonian roads and trails.", "heads_up": "If you're offered mate, drink the whole gourd before passing it back, and only say \"gracias\" when you don't want any more.", "food": [{"name": "Asado", "wiki": "Asado", "desc": "Slow-grilled beef, sausages and offal cooked over wood or charcoal, a social ritual that lasts hours. Hostels often host cheap asados.", "price": "$15 to $30", "when": "Sunday lunch with family and friends", "order": "Start with achuras: chorizo, morcilla and mollejas"}, {"name": "Empanadas", "wiki": "Empanada", "desc": "Baked pastries with regional fillings like beef or cheese, best in Salta and Tucumán.", "price": "$1 to $2.50", "when": "Quick lunch or a Friday night delivery", "order": "Ask for carne cortada a cuchillo, knife-cut beef"}, {"name": "Choripán", "wiki": "Choripán", "desc": "Grilled chorizo sausage in bread with chimichurri, sold at street grills and football matches.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Weekends by the river and at football", "order": "Add chimichurri and salsa criolla"}, {"name": "Milanesa", "wiki": "Milanesa", "desc": "A breaded, fried meat cutlet, often topped with ham, cheese and tomato as a milanesa napolitana.", "price": "$8 to $14", "when": "Lunch at old-school bodegones", "order": "Order it napolitana, or as a sandwich to go"}, {"name": "Dulce de leche", "wiki": "Dulce de leche", "desc": "Thick, sweet milk caramel slathered on everything, from alfajores (sandwich cookies) and pastries to ice cream. Dangerous.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Breakfast and the 5pm merienda", "order": "Try an alfajor from any kiosco"}, {"name": "Mate", "wiki": "Mate (drink)", "desc": "Bitter herbal tea sipped through a metal straw from a shared gourd. You'll see people carrying a thermos of hot water everywhere.", "price": "$3 to $8", "when": "All day, especially in the afternoon", "order": "Buy yerba at a supermarket; the gourd costs extra"}], "food_tip": "Look for menú ejecutivo lunch deals in cities, and pick up empanadas or choripán from street vendors for cheap eats. Supermarkets are good for self-catering steak.", "culture": ["Argentines greet with a single kiss on the right cheek, even between men who are friends, and a handshake can feel a bit cold once you know people.", "Dinner is late, often after 9 or 10pm, and clubs in Buenos Aires don't get going until 2am, so pace yourself and try a siesta.", "The Falklands, called the Malvinas here, are still a sensitive topic, so avoid bringing them up casually, especially if you're British.", "Football is close to a religion, and the Boca Juniors versus River Plate rivalry is fierce, so be careful what shirt you wear and where.", "Mate is shared in a circle with one person pouring, and accepting a round is a nice way to bond with locals.", "Argentines love a long chat and a debate, and turning up 15 to 30 minutes late to social plans is pretty normal."], "safety": "Argentina is one of the safer countries in South America, but pickpockets work busy parts of Buenos Aires like San Telmo, La Boca and the Subte. Motochorros, thieves on motorbikes, snatch phones from people standing near the kerb, so step back from the road when checking maps. Watch for the mustard scam, where someone squirts something on you and a helpful stranger robs you while cleaning it off. Patagonia's wind and weather can turn brutal fast, so carry proper layers and check forecasts before big hikes.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is small, lunch is around 1pm, merienda with pastries comes at 5pm, and dinner rarely starts before 9pm.", "Tap water is safe in Buenos Aires and most cities.", "Vegetarians have plenty of choice in Buenos Aires; elsewhere lean on pizza, provoleta and empanadas de humita (sweetcorn).", "Many restaurants add a cubierto, a per-person table charge, and on top of that a tip of about 10 percent, in cash, is normal.", "Panaderías sell facturas, sweet pastries, by the dozen or half dozen, which makes a cheap breakfast."], "drinks": [{"name": "Fernet con coca", "desc": "Bitter herbal liqueur mixed with cola, the unofficial drink of Córdoba. Expect around $3 to $6 in a bar."}, {"name": "Malbec", "desc": "Argentina's signature red wine from Mendoza. A decent supermarket bottle costs about $5 to $10."}], "greet": "Say hola, ¿todo bien? and expect to kiss everyone in the room hello and goodbye, even at a party. Locals use vos instead of tú.", "dress": "Porteños dress stylishly but casually, so jeans and decent shoes work for most nights out. Cover shoulders in churches, and pack layers for Patagonia, where the weather turns fast.", "table": "Wait until everyone is served before eating, and keep your hands above the table. At an asado, give a round of applause to the asador who grilled the meat.", "faith": "Argentina is mostly Catholic, with growing evangelical and non-religious groups and a large Jewish community in Buenos Aires. Speak quietly in churches and don't wander around during mass.", "phrases": [["Che", "Hey, mate"], ["¿Cuánto sale?", "How much is it?"], ["Dale", "Okay, sure"], ["Boludo", "Dude, among friends only"], ["La cuenta, por favor", "The bill, please"]]},
  "egypt": {"around": "Trains connect Cairo, Luxor and Aswan, and the overnight sleeper train is a fun way to save a night's accommodation, though the cheaper regular trains are also an option and much less hassle to book than they used to be. Go Bus and other coach companies cover Hurghada, Dahab and the rest of Sinai, while Uber, Careem and inDrive work well in Cairo and Alexandria and save you endless haggling. Microbuses are the cheapest way around towns once you figure out the routes. Book the sleeper train a few days ahead in the busy winter months.", "money": "Egypt uses the Egyptian pound, and since the currency was floated in 2024 the old black market has mostly vanished, so ATMs give you a fair rate. Cash is king for street food, taxis, markets and tips, while cards work in bigger hotels, chain stores and many tourist sites, some of which are now card only for tickets. Baksheesh, small tips for almost any service from bathroom attendants to the guy who shows you a tomb, is part of daily life, so keep a wad of small notes handy. Haggle hard in markets and with drivers, often starting at a third of the first price.", "sleep": "Downtown Cairo has loads of cheap hostels and budget hotels in old apartment buildings, and staying near Giza gets you pyramid views from the rooftop. Luxor's West Bank has relaxed guesthouses, Aswan has colourful Nubian guesthouses across the river, and Dahab has seaside camps and dive hostels that are perfect for a long chill. Siwa has small eco-lodges and guesthouses out in the desert.", "eat": ["Koshari: rice, lentils and pasta with tomato sauce and fried onions", "Ful medames: slow-cooked fava beans with oil, cumin and bread", "Taameya: Egyptian falafel made from fava beans", "Karkade: chilled or hot hibiscus tea, deep red and tart"], "data": "SIM cards from Vodafone, Orange, Etisalat (e&) or WE need your passport and are best bought at an official store rather than an airport kiosk with inflated prices. Coverage is good along the Nile and in Sinai towns, and phones bought abroad may need registering if you stay longer than a few months.", "heads_up": "At the pyramids and other big sites, camel and horse rides often get pricier halfway through, so agree the full price and duration before you climb on.", "food": [{"name": "Koshari", "wiki": "Koshary", "desc": "Rice, lentils, macaroni and chickpeas topped with spicy tomato sauce, garlic vinegar and crispy onions. The ultimate cheap Cairo meal at places like Abou Tarek.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch, at busy koshari shops", "order": "Add the garlic vinegar (dakka) and hot sauce yourself"}, {"name": "Ful medames", "wiki": "Ful medames", "desc": "Slow-cooked fava beans mashed with oil, cumin and lemon, eaten for breakfast with bread from street carts for pennies.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Breakfast, from street carts", "order": "Ask for it in baladi bread as a sandwich"}, {"name": "Taameya", "wiki": "Falafel", "desc": "Egyptian falafel, made with fava beans rather than chickpeas, so it's greener and fluffier inside. Grab it in a sandwich with salad and tahini.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.80", "when": "Breakfast, at ful and taameya stalls", "order": "Add pickles and fried aubergine to your sandwich"}, {"name": "Hawawshi", "wiki": "Hawawshi", "desc": "Pita stuffed with spiced minced meat and baked until crisp, a greasy and very satisfying street snack.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Evening snack, at street shops", "order": "Alexandria style uses minced meat with chilli"}, {"name": "Feteer", "wiki": "Feteer meshaltet", "desc": "A flaky, layered pastry eaten sweet with honey and cream or savoury with cheese and meat.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Breakfast or evening, at feteer shops", "order": "Order it sweet with honey and qishta cream"}, {"name": "Karkade", "wiki": "Hibiscus tea", "desc": "Deep red hibiscus tea, served chilled in summer or hot in winter, especially popular in Aswan.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Any time, from juice shops", "order": "Ask for it iced in summer"}], "food_tip": "Street food and local koshari or ful joints feed you for a dollar or two, while tourist restaurants near big sites charge several times more. Ask for the price before ordering anywhere without a menu.", "culture": ["Egypt is a mostly Muslim country with a sizeable Coptic Christian minority, so dress modestly in towns and at religious sites, with shoulders and knees covered.", "Women need to cover their hair when visiting mosques, and everyone should remove shoes, so carry a scarf.", "Use your right hand for eating and giving things, and don't show the soles of your feet when sitting.", "During Ramadan, avoid eating, drinking or smoking in public during daylight hours.", "Egyptians are friendly and chatty, and invitations for tea are common, though some are a lead-in to a shop visit.", "Haggling is expected and done with humour, so smile, take your time and don't get angry."], "safety": "Egypt is generally safe for tourists in the main areas, with a heavy security presence at sites and hotels, but parts of North Sinai and the western desert borders are off limits, so check your government's advice. The bigger daily issue is hassle and scams: touts, fake guides, overpriced perfume and papyrus shops, and drivers changing the price mid-ride. Women often get unwanted attention, so dressing modestly and walking with confidence helps. Drink bottled water and be careful with salads and ice to avoid stomach trouble.", "eat_beats": ["Egyptians eat a late lunch, often around 3pm to 4pm, and dinner can run past 10pm, especially in summer.", "Don't drink tap water; bottled water is cheap, but check the seal is unbroken.", "Vegetarians do well, since koshari, ful, taameya and many stews are naturally meat free, and Coptic fasting periods mean even more vegan options.", "Juice shops sell fresh sugarcane, mango and guava juice for very little.", "Tipping (baksheesh) is part of daily life, so leave 10 percent in restaurants, even when a service charge is added."], "drinks": [{"name": "Asab", "desc": "Fresh sugarcane juice pressed in front of you at juice shops, sweet and frothy, for around $0.25 a glass."}, {"name": "Shai", "desc": "Strong black tea, often with fresh mint and lots of sugar, served in small glasses at street cafes (ahwas) for well under $1."}], "greet": "Men shake hands and friends often kiss on both cheeks. \"As-salamu alaykum\" is the polite greeting, answered with \"wa alaykum as-salam\".", "dress": "Cover shoulders and knees in cities, and loose clothes help both with heat and with attention. Beach resorts like Sharm and Hurghada are more relaxed.", "table": "Eat and pass food with your right hand, and bread (aish) is used as a scoop. Leaving a little food on your plate shows your host was generous.", "faith": "About 90 percent of Egyptians are Sunni Muslim, and most of the rest are Coptic Christian. Remove shoes in mosques, avoid visiting during Friday midday prayers, and women should cover their hair.", "phrases": [["Salam alaykum (السلام عليكم)", "Hello"], ["Shukran (شكرا)", "Thank you"], ["Bikam? (بكام)", "How much?"], ["La, shukran (لا شكرا)", "No, thank you"], ["Inshallah (إن شاء الله)", "God willing"]]},
  "jordan": {"around": "JETT coaches link Amman with Petra, Aqaba and the Dead Sea, and cheap minibuses cover most other routes but leave when full. Careem and Uber work well in Amman and are much cheaper than taxis. Renting a car is popular for the Desert Highway and King's Highway, and it's not too pricey if shared. Book the JETT bus to Petra a day or two ahead, and arrange Wadi Rum camps and jeep tours before you arrive.", "money": "Jordan uses the dinar, one of the strongest currencies in the world, so it's not a cheap country, but you can still travel it on a budget. Cards work in Amman hotels, supermarkets and bigger shops, but carry cash for minibuses, small cafes, Bedouin camps and rural areas, and ATMs are common in towns. Buy the Jordan Pass online before arriving if you're staying three nights or more, as it covers your visa and entry to Petra and dozens of other sites. Haggle in souvenir shops, and leave about 10 percent in sit-down restaurants if no service charge is added.", "sleep": "Amman and Wadi Musa near Petra have hostels and small family hotels with dorms and cheap private rooms. Wadi Rum has Bedouin desert camps, from basic tents to fancy bubble domes, and most budget ones include dinner and a jeep tour. In Dana, simple guesthouses in the old village are the budget option.", "eat": ["Mansaf: lamb cooked in fermented yogurt sauce over rice", "Falafel: fried chickpea balls, usually in flatbread with tahini", "Knafeh: warm cheese pastry soaked in sweet syrup", "Maqluba: upside-down pot of rice, vegetables and chicken or lamb"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Zain, Orange and Umniah are easy to buy at the airport with your passport, and coverage is good except in deep desert and wadis.", "food": [{"name": "Mansaf", "wiki": "Mansaf", "desc": "Jordan's national dish: lamb cooked in a sauce of fermented dried yogurt, served over rice and flatbread with nuts. Traditionally eaten with your right hand.", "price": "$8 to $14", "when": "Lunch at weddings and celebrations", "order": "Eat the traditional way, with your right hand"}, {"name": "Falafel", "wiki": "Falafel", "desc": "Fried chickpea balls, usually in flatbread with tahini and salad. Hashem in downtown Amman is a famous cheap spot.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Breakfast and anytime snack", "order": "Get it in a sandwich with hot sauce"}, {"name": "Hummus", "wiki": "Hummus", "desc": "Creamy chickpea and tahini dip, eaten for breakfast with bread, olive oil and falafel.", "price": "$1.50 to $3 a plate", "when": "Breakfast, with falafel and tea", "order": "Try hummus with ful (fava beans) on top"}, {"name": "Maqluba", "wiki": "Maqluba", "desc": "An upside-down pot of rice, vegetables and chicken or lamb, flipped onto a tray at the table.", "price": "$5 to $9", "when": "Friday lunch with family", "order": "Watch the flip, it's part of the fun"}, {"name": "Musakhan", "wiki": "Musakhan", "desc": "Roast chicken on taboon flatbread smothered in sumac, olive oil and caramelised onions, a Palestinian dish loved across Jordan and meant for sharing.", "price": "$6 to $10", "when": "Lunch, especially in winter", "order": "Tear the bread and eat with your hands"}, {"name": "Knafeh", "wiki": "Knafeh", "desc": "Warm cheese pastry soaked in sweet syrup, best fresh from Habibah in downtown Amman.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Afternoon dessert, from sweet shops", "order": "Try nabulsi (soft) or khishneh (crunchy)"}], "food_tip": "Falafel, hummus and shawarma joints are where you eat cheaply, often for a couple of dinars. Most Wadi Rum camps include meals, often zarb, a Bedouin barbecue slow-cooked in a pit under the sand, which saves you money.", "culture": ["Jordan is a Muslim country, so dress modestly with shoulders and knees covered, especially outside Amman's trendier areas.", "Women need to cover their hair at mosques, and everyone should remove shoes; the King Abdullah I Mosque in Amman lends robes.", "Hospitality is huge, especially among Bedouin, and you'll often be offered tea or coffee, so accept at least a cup.", "The royal family is respected, so avoid criticising them.", "Use your right hand for eating and giving, and don't show the soles of your feet.", "During Ramadan, avoid eating, drinking or smoking in public during daylight."], "safety": "Jordan is one of the safest countries in the Middle East, with low crime and friendly locals, even with tensions in the wider region, so do check your government's latest advice. Avoid the border areas with Syria and Iraq. In Petra, some animal handlers push rides and overcharge, and the animals are often badly treated, so walk instead. Women may get unwanted attention, so dressing modestly helps.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is big and lunch around 2pm is the main meal, while dinner is often light and late.", "Order at the counter in falafel and shawarma shops, then pay after; many places post prices in Arabic only, so ask.", "Tap water is technically treated but most people drink bottled or filtered water, and you should too.", "Vegetarian eating is easy with falafel, hummus, mutabbal, foul and salads, though most main dishes contain meat.", "Leave 10% at sit-down restaurants if no service charge is added, and round up for taxi drivers and guides."], "drinks": [{"name": "Arabic coffee", "desc": "Light, cardamom-spiced coffee served in tiny cups as a sign of welcome, often free. Shake the cup gently when you've had enough."}, {"name": "Limonana", "desc": "Fresh lemonade blended with mint and ice, perfect in the desert heat. A glass costs about $2 to $4."}], "greet": "Say as-salaam alaykum or marhaba, and men shake hands with men. Wait for a woman to offer her hand first, and a hand on the heart is a polite alternative.", "dress": "Cover shoulders and knees in most places, and wear loose clothing outside Amman's trendier districts. Women should bring a scarf for mosques.", "table": "Eat and pass food with your right hand, and accept at least one cup of tea or coffee. Leaving a little food on your plate shows you've had enough.", "faith": "About 97% of Jordanians are Sunni Muslim, with a small Christian community. Remove shoes at mosques, avoid prayer times and don't walk in front of people praying.", "phrases": [["Marhaba (مرحبا)", "Hello"], ["Shukran (شكرا)", "Thank you"], ["Addaysh? (قديش؟)", "How much?"], ["La, shukran (لا، شكرا)", "No, thank you"], ["Sahtain (صحتين)", "Enjoy your meal"]]},
  "tunisia": {"around": "Louages, white shared minibuses that leave from louage stations when full, are the fastest cheap way between towns, and the stripe colour tells you whether it's intercity or local. SNCFT trains run south from Tunis to Sousse, El Jem and Sfax and are cheap and comfy enough, while the TGM light rail from Tunis gets you to Carthage and Sidi Bou Said for pennies. In cities, yellow taxis use meters, so ask the driver to switch it on, and Bolt works in Tunis. For the Sahara around Tozeur and Douz, a shared rental car or a short tour makes life a lot easier.", "money": "Tunisia uses the dinar, which is a closed currency, so you can't legally take it in or out of the country and you'll get it from ATMs or exchange counters once you land. Keep your exchange receipts, as you need them to change leftover dinars back when you leave. ATMs are easy to find in towns, but cards only really work in bigger hotels, supermarkets and some restaurants, so cash rules for louages, cafes and souks. Haggling is expected in the souks, especially in Tunis and Sousse medinas, and rounding up or leaving a few coins is plenty as a tip.", "sleep": "Small hotels and family-run guesthouses are the usual budget beds, with traditional dar houses in the medinas of Tunis, Kairouan and Sousse offering great value, and a handful of hostels in Tunis. Djerba and the coastal resorts have big beach hotels that drop their prices sharply outside summer. In the south, desert camps near Douz and Ksar Ghilane and the troglodyte hotels of Matmata make for memorable cheap nights.", "eat": ["Brik: crisp pastry filled with egg, tuna and parsley", "Lablabi: spicy chickpea soup poured over torn bread", "Couscous: steamed semolina with spicy sauce, fish or lamb", "Makroudh: semolina pastry stuffed with dates, soaked in syrup"], "data": "Ooredoo, Orange and Tunisie Telecom sell SIMs at their shops with your passport, and data is cheap. Coverage is good in towns with gaps in the deep desert.", "heads_up": "Harissa finds its way into a lot of dishes, so ask for less if you don't like it hot.", "food": [{"name": "Brik", "wiki": "Brik", "desc": "Thin, crisp pastry folded around a runny egg, tuna, capers and parsley, then fried. Eat it carefully with your fingers and expect egg on your chin.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Starter, or a Ramadan evening classic", "order": "Eat it with your fingers and catch the egg"}, {"name": "Lablabi", "wiki": "Lablabi", "desc": "Spicy chickpea soup poured over torn stale bread with cumin, harissa, olive oil and often a soft egg, a cheap working-class breakfast.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast in winter, at workers' cafés", "order": "Ask for egg, tuna and lots of harissa"}, {"name": "Couscous", "wiki": "Couscous", "desc": "Steamed semolina topped with a spicy red sauce, vegetables and lamb, chicken or fish, the national dish and a Friday family tradition.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Friday family lunch", "order": "Fish couscous is the coastal speciality"}, {"name": "Bambalouni", "wiki": "Bambalouni", "desc": "A light, crispy fried doughnut dusted with sugar, sold hot from stalls in Sidi Bou Said and seaside towns for small change. Perfect with a mint tea.", "price": "$0.30 to $0.70", "when": "Beach snack in Sidi Bou Said", "order": "Get it dusted with sugar, fresh from the fryer"}, {"name": "Shakshouka", "wiki": "Shakshouka", "desc": "Eggs poached in a spicy tomato and pepper sauce, mopped up with crusty bread.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Breakfast or a quick lunch", "order": "Add merguez sausage for a heartier version"}, {"name": "Makroudh", "wiki": "Makroudh", "desc": "Diamond-shaped semolina pastries stuffed with dates, fried and soaked in honey syrup, famously from Kairouan.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50 each", "when": "With coffee, especially from Kairouan"}], "food_tip": "Street stalls, sandwich shops and small local restaurants serving a plate of couscous or a bowl of lablabi are very cheap, often a few dinars. Look for places full of workers at lunchtime.", "culture": ["Tunisia is a Muslim country but one of the more liberal in the region, so beachwear is fine at resorts, but cover shoulders and knees in towns, medinas and villages.", "Non-Muslims can't enter most mosque prayer halls, but the Great Mosque of Kairouan lets visitors into its courtyard, with modest dress required.", "Use your right hand for eating, shaking hands and giving things.", "During Ramadan, avoid eating, drinking or smoking in public during daylight, and expect cafes to open after sunset.", "French is widely spoken alongside Tunisian Arabic, so a few words of either goes a long way.", "Tunisians are hospitable, and accepting a mint tea or coffee when offered is a nice way to connect."], "safety": "Tunisia is generally safe for travellers, and the main hassles are touts and overly persistent shop owners in tourist medinas, plus taxi drivers who forget to turn on the meter. Avoid the border areas with Libya and parts of the western border with Algeria, which governments advise against visiting. Solo women may get unwanted comments, so dressing modestly and ignoring catcalls helps. Political demonstrations happen occasionally in Tunis, so steer clear of them.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is between 12:30 and 2pm, and dinner from 8pm, later in summer and much later during Ramadan.", "Tap water is treated but tastes strong and upsets some stomachs, so most people drink bottled water.", "Vegetarians can find salade méchouia, lablabi and egg dishes, though many stews use meat stock.", "Fricassé sandwiches and casse-croûte baguettes stuffed with tuna and harissa are the cheapest quick lunches.", "Rounding up the bill or leaving around 10% at restaurants is appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Mint tea with pine nuts", "desc": "Sweet green tea with fresh mint, often topped with pine nuts in cafés. About $0.50 to $1.50 a glass."}, {"name": "Citronnade", "desc": "A fresh lemonade blended with lemon peel and sugar, perfect in summer. About $0.50 to $1."}], "greet": "Shake hands lightly and touch your hand to your chest, and close friends kiss on both cheeks. Men should wait for a woman to offer her hand first.", "dress": "Light clothes are fine in Tunis and resorts, but cover shoulders and knees in medinas, villages and the south. Women should carry a scarf for mosques and shrines.", "table": "Eat with your right hand or bread, and take food from the part of the shared dish nearest you. Accept seconds when offered, as refusing too fast can seem rude.", "faith": "Almost all Tunisians are Sunni Muslim, with a small Jewish community on Djerba. Non-Muslims usually can't enter prayer halls, so stay in courtyards, and dress modestly at shrines like El Ghriba synagogue.", "phrases": [["Aslema (عسلامة)", "Hello"], ["Yaïchek (يعيشك)", "Thank you"], ["Bkadesh? (بقداش؟)", "How much?"], ["Bla harissa (بلا هريسة)", "Without harissa"], ["Barsha (برشا)", "A lot, very"]]},
  "ethiopia": {"around": "Ethiopian Airlines runs a wide domestic network, and fares are much cheaper if you flew into Addis Ababa on the same airline, so buy domestic legs at one of their offices with your international ticket. Selam and Sky Bus coaches and slower minibuses link towns, and the Addis Ababa to Djibouti railway stops at Dire Dawa, close to Harar. In Addis, local apps like Ride and Feres work like Uber, and the light rail and blue and white minibuses are dirt cheap. Roads are long and buses often leave at dawn, so buy tickets the day before.", "money": "Ethiopia uses the birr, and since the currency was floated in 2024 the gap between the official and street rates has shrunk a lot, so ATMs and banks are the sensible way to get cash. Carry plenty of cash, as cards only work in bigger hotels and a few restaurants in Addis Ababa, and ATMs from Commercial Bank of Ethiopia and Dashen Bank are reliable mainly in cities. Haggling is normal for crafts and for minibus fares when you're quoted a foreigner price. A small tip for guides, drivers and scouts is customary, and leaving around 10 percent in restaurants is appreciated.", "sleep": "Budget hotels and simple pensions are common across the country, often very cheap, though quality varies wildly, so check the room and the hot water before paying. Addis has a few hostels, and Harar has some traditional guesthouses inside the old walled city. Camping and community lodges are options in the Simien and Bale Mountains, usually arranged with a local guide.", "eat": ["Injera: spongy sour flatbread made from teff, used as plate and spoon", "Shiro: smooth spiced chickpea stew, a fasting-day staple", "Tibs: pan-fried meat with onion, pepper and rosemary", "Buna: Ethiopian coffee, often served in a home coffee ceremony"], "data": "Ethio Telecom and Safaricom Ethiopia sell SIMs with passport registration, coverage is good in towns, and mobile internet can be cut in some regions during unrest.", "heads_up": "Ethiopia uses its own calendar and counts hours from 6am, so confirm whether a bus time is in Ethiopian or international time.", "food": [{"name": "Injera", "wiki": "Injera", "desc": "Spongy, sour flatbread made from teff that acts as your plate and your cutlery. Tear off a piece with your right hand and use it to scoop up stews.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Every meal, as plate and cutlery", "order": "Beyaynetu, a vegan platter on injera, is a great intro"}, {"name": "Doro wat", "wiki": "Wat (food)", "desc": "A rich, spicy chicken stew with berbere spice and a hard-boiled egg, the national dish and a must at holidays.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Holidays, especially Easter and Christmas", "order": "Comes with a hard boiled egg in the sauce"}, {"name": "Shiro", "wiki": "Shiro (food)", "desc": "A smooth, spiced chickpea stew, cheap, filling and the go-to on fasting days when many Ethiopians skip meat.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch, especially on fasting days", "order": "Try tegabino shiro, bubbling in a clay pot"}, {"name": "Tej", "wiki": "Tej", "desc": "Golden honey wine flavoured with gesho leaves, served in round-bottomed flasks at traditional tej bets. Sweet, cheap and stronger than it tastes.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Evenings in honey wine houses", "order": "Served in a berele, a round glass flask"}, {"name": "Kitfo", "wiki": "Kitfo", "desc": "Minced raw beef with spiced butter and chili, a delicacy you can also order lightly cooked (\"leb leb\") if you're nervous.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Special occasions and Gurage restaurants", "order": "Ask for 'leb leb' if you want it lightly cooked"}, {"name": "Buna", "wiki": "Coffee ceremony of Ethiopia and Eritrea", "desc": "Ethiopia is the birthplace of coffee, and the traditional ceremony of roasting, grinding and brewing it with incense is a ritual worth joining.", "price": "$0.20 to $1", "when": "Afternoons, in a long ceremony", "order": "Expect three rounds: abol, tona and baraka"}], "food_tip": "Local eateries serving shiro or tibs on injera are very cheap, and fasting days (Wednesdays, Fridays and long fasting seasons) mean loads of vegan options. Fruit juice spots blend thick layered juices for pennies.", "culture": ["Ethiopia is deeply religious, mostly Orthodox Christian and Muslim, so dress modestly with shoulders and knees covered, and women should cover their heads in churches.", "Take shoes off before entering churches and mosques, and expect separate areas for men and women in some churches.", "Eat with your right hand from a shared plate of injera, and if someone feeds you a bite by hand, called gursha, it's a sign of friendship.", "Ethiopia uses its own calendar, which runs about seven to eight years behind, and counts hours from dawn, so 1 o'clock Ethiopian time is 7am.", "Ethiopians are proud the country was never colonised, so its history and identity are a source of pride.", "Ask before photographing people, especially in the Omo Valley, where many communities expect payment for photos."], "safety": "Ethiopia is welcoming, but parts of the country, especially Amhara (including routes to Lalibela and Gondar), Tigray and some areas of Oromia, have seen conflict, so check your government's advice before planning your route. In Addis, pickpockets and scams like fake students asking for money are common, so keep valuables close. Altitude can hit in Addis and the highlands, so take it easy at first. Use a reputable guide for treks like the Simien Mountains.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is early, with dishes like firfir or ful, and dinner usually starts around 7pm.", "Don't drink tap water; bottled water is cheap and easy to find.", "On fasting days most restaurants serve 'yetsom', fasting food that is fully vegan.", "Tipping 5 to 10 percent is appreciated in sit-down restaurants, and leaving small change at local cafés is common.", "Raw meat dishes are popular but can upset unused stomachs, so try cooked versions first."], "drinks": [{"name": "Tella", "desc": "Home brewed barley and teff beer, dark and slightly sour. A glass costs under $1 in local tella houses."}, {"name": "Macchiato", "desc": "Espresso with steamed milk in a small glass, an Addis staple since the Italian era. It costs about $0.30 to $0.80."}], "greet": "A handshake while touching your right forearm with your left hand shows respect, and men who know each other often bump shoulders. Friends kiss three times on the cheeks.", "dress": "Long trousers and covered shoulders are the norm, and many people wear white cotton scarves (netela) to church. The highlands, including Addis, get cold at night, so pack a warm layer.", "table": "Wash your hands before the meal, eat with your right hand, and tear injera from the edge closest to you. Don't lick your fingers, and accept gursha gracefully if someone offers it.", "faith": "About 40 percent of Ethiopians are Orthodox Christian, about a third are Muslim and most others are Protestant. Don't walk in front of people praying, and ask before filming services or priests.", "phrases": [["Selam (ሰላም)", "Hello"], ["Ameseginalehu (አመሰግናለሁ)", "Thank you"], ["Sint new? (ስንት ነው?)", "How much is it?"], ["Betam tiru (በጣም ጥሩ)", "Very good"], ["Ishi (እሺ)", "Okay"]]},
  "madagascar": {"around": "Taxi-brousses, shared minibuses crammed with people, luggage and the odd chicken, cover the whole island cheaply but slowly, leaving when full from big stations in each town. Private shuttles like Cotisse cost more but are safer, comfier and actually leave on time, and are worth it for the long RN7 drive south. Roads away from the main highways are rough and many become impassable in the rainy season from roughly December to March. Domestic flights save days but are pricey and often rescheduled, and many travellers split the cost of a car and driver for a week or two.", "money": "Madagascar uses the ariary, and cash is needed for almost everything, as cards rarely work outside a few upmarket hotels and tour agencies. ATMs from BNI, BFV and BOA are found in bigger towns like Antananarivo, Antsirabe, Fianarantsoa and Toliara, but can run dry or reject foreign cards, so stock up before heading into the countryside. Euros are the easiest foreign currency to change, and you'll often see bigger prices quoted in them. Light, good-natured haggling is normal at markets and with taxi drivers, and park guides and drivers expect a tip.", "sleep": "Simple hotels and bungalows are the usual budget beds, often family-run, with rooms rather than dorms, as hostels are rare outside Antananarivo. National parks like Isalo and Andringitra have campsites, and you can rent gear through guides. Places near Andasibe, Isalo and Ranomafana fill up in the July to August high season, so book ahead then.", "eat": ["Romazava: beef and greens stew, often called the national dish", "Ravitoto: pounded cassava leaves cooked with pork", "Mofo gasy: small rice cakes sold hot at breakfast", "Koba: sweet banana, peanut and rice paste wrapped in leaves"], "data": "Telma, Orange and Airtel SIMs are cheap and need your passport, with decent coverage in towns and along main roads but long dead zones in between. Telma tends to reach furthest.", "heads_up": "Hiring a local guide is required in most national parks, and malaria is a risk across much of the island, so ask a doctor about prevention before you go.", "food": [{"name": "Romazava", "wiki": "Romazava", "desc": "A beef and leafy greens stew with a tongue-tingling herb called paracress, often called the national dish.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch, at hotelys", "order": "Add a spoon of sakay, the fiery chilli paste"}, {"name": "Ravitoto", "wiki": "Ravitoto", "desc": "Pounded cassava leaves cooked slowly with pork and garlic, served over a big heap of rice.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch or dinner, at hotelys"}, {"name": "Mofo gasy", "wiki": "Mofo gasy", "desc": "Little sweet rice cakes cooked in round moulds over charcoal, sold hot at street stalls for breakfast.", "price": "$0.05 to $0.20", "when": "Breakfast, from street stalls", "order": "Try mofo akondro, the banana fritter version"}, {"name": "Koba", "wiki": "Koba (sweet)", "desc": "A sweet paste of banana, ground peanuts and rice flour wrapped in leaves and sliced from a big log, a classic roadside snack.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50", "when": "Snack, at roadside stalls", "order": "Ambalavao in the south is famous for it"}, {"name": "Zebu steak", "wiki": "Zebu", "desc": "Humped zebu cattle are everywhere in Madagascar, and grilled zebu with rice or chips is a cheap staple in local restaurants.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch or dinner, at local restaurants", "order": "Comes with rice or chips and a sauce"}, {"name": "Ranon'apango", "wiki": "Malagasy cuisine", "desc": "Hot water poured over the burnt rice at the bottom of the pot, a toasty drink served with meals across the country.", "price": "Free with meals", "when": "With every meal, in place of water"}], "food_tip": "Hotelys, small local eateries, serve a plate of rice with a meat or vegetable stew for about a dollar, and rice is eaten at every meal. Street snacks like mofo gasy and samosas are cheaper still.", "culture": ["Fady, local taboos, vary from village to village and can cover anything from eating pork to pointing at tombs, so ask your guide what applies before you wander around.", "Ancestors are hugely important, and tombs are sacred, so never touch, sit on or photograph them without asking.", "Malagasy people are gentle and polite, and a calm, friendly manner gets you much further than raised voices.", "Greet people with \"salama\" or \"manao ahoana\", and shake hands when meeting.", "Famadihana, the turning of the bones ceremony held in the highlands mostly between July and September, is a family affair, so only attend if invited.", "Dress modestly in villages, as rural areas are fairly conservative."], "safety": "Madagascar is mostly safe for travellers, but petty theft is common in Antananarivo, so keep valuables out of sight and avoid walking at night. Political protests flare up in the capital from time to time, so check the news and stay clear of demonstrations. Taxi-brousse travel at night is best avoided because of road accidents and occasional highway robberies on some routes. Malaria is present across much of the island, so take precautions.", "eat_beats": ["Malagasy people eat rice three times a day, often with lunch around noon and dinner by 7pm.", "Don't drink tap water; bottled water is available in towns, and ranon'apango is boiled so it's safe.", "Vegetarians can ask for \"tsy misy hena\" (no meat) and live on rice, beans and greens.", "Hotelys show what's cooking on a board or simply in the pots, so ask what's ready and point.", "Tipping isn't expected in local eateries, but 5 to 10 percent is appreciated in tourist restaurants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Rhum arrangé", "desc": "Local rum infused with fruit, vanilla or spices in big jars on bar shelves. A shot costs under $1 in local bars."}, {"name": "THB", "desc": "Three Horses Beer, the national lager, sold in big bottles for about $1 to $1.50."}], "greet": "Shake hands gently, often with your left hand touching your right forearm as a sign of respect. Greet people before asking for anything.", "dress": "Cover shoulders and knees in villages and churches, and bring warm layers for the cool highlands. Beachwear is fine only on the beach.", "table": "Eat with a spoon, the usual utensil, and wait for the eldest person to start. Accept food offered to you, even a small taste.", "faith": "Most Malagasy are Christian, but ancestor worship and local beliefs run deep, with some Muslim communities on the coast. Respect fady at sacred sites, and ask your guide what's forbidden before entering.", "phrases": [["Salama", "Hello"], ["Misaotra", "Thank you"], ["Ohatrinona?", "How much?"], ["Veloma", "Goodbye"], ["Tsy misy hena", "No meat"]]},
  "malawi": {"around": "Minibuses and larger coaches run between towns, and shared taxis or pickups cover the last stretch to places like Cape Maclear, though you'll often wait until vehicles fill. AXA coaches are the comfier option between Lilongwe and Blantyre. Fuel shortages can disrupt transport, so check before you set off. The Ilala ferry runs up and down the lake when in service, but its schedule is unreliable, so check locally.", "money": "Malawi uses the kwacha, and you'll need cash for most things, as cards only work at some lodges, hotels and supermarkets in Lilongwe and Blantyre. ATMs from Standard Bank and National Bank are found in cities and larger towns, but often have low withdrawal limits, so take out what you need before heading to the lake. Clean US dollar notes are handy as backup, as foreign currency can be scarce. Haggling is normal at craft stalls and markets, and tips for guides and lodge staff are appreciated.", "sleep": "Lakeside lodges at Cape Maclear, Nkhata Bay and Senga Bay offer dorms, cheap chalets and camping, and most have their own restaurants. Zomba Plateau and the Mulanje Massif have campsites and simple mountain huts, while Liwonde National Park has budget camping options close to the wildlife. Lilongwe has a couple of backpacker hostels.", "eat": ["Nsima: stiff maize porridge, eaten by hand with relish", "Chambo: tilapia-like fish from Lake Malawi, usually grilled or fried", "Usipa: tiny dried lake fish, cooked with tomato and onion", "Mandasi: sweet fried dough, sold at roadside stalls"], "data": "Airtel and TNM SIMs are easy to get with your passport, and data bundles are cheap. Coverage is fair along the main roads and lakeshore, but power cuts are common, so carry a power bank.", "heads_up": "Bilharzia is present in parts of Lake Malawi, and malaria is a risk countrywide, so ask a doctor about both before you go.", "food": [{"name": "Nsima", "wiki": "Ugali", "desc": "Stiff maize porridge, the staple of every meal, rolled into a ball with your right hand and dipped into relish.", "price": "$1 to $3 with a relish", "when": "Lunch and dinner, every day", "order": "Pair it with ndiwo, a relish of greens or beans"}, {"name": "Chambo", "wiki": "Oreochromis karongae", "desc": "Tilapia-like fish from Lake Malawi, usually grilled or fried and eaten with nsima on the lakeshore.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch by the lake", "order": "Ask for it whole and grilled"}, {"name": "Kampango", "wiki": "Kampango", "desc": "A tasty lake catfish, often served grilled or in a stew at lakeside lodges.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Dinner at lakeside lodges"}, {"name": "Mandasi", "wiki": "Mandazi", "desc": "Sweet fried dough, sold at roadside stalls in the morning for pennies.", "price": "$0.10 to $0.30 each", "when": "Breakfast, from roadside stalls"}, {"name": "Chibuku", "wiki": "Chibuku", "desc": "A thick, sour maize beer sold in cartons, which you shake before drinking.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 a carton", "when": "Afternoons and evenings, at bottle stores", "order": "Shake it well before opening"}, {"name": "Thobwa", "wiki": "Thobwa", "desc": "A thick, slightly sweet fermented drink made from maize or millet, sold cheaply in villages and markets and drunk as much as food as a drink.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50", "when": "Afternoon, in villages"}], "food_tip": "Local restaurants serving nsima with chicken, beans or fish are very cheap, often a couple of dollars, while lodge restaurants charge tourist prices. Roadside snacks like grilled corn and mandasi cost pennies, and buying fish straight from the fishermen and having it cooked is a lakeside classic.", "culture": ["Malawi is called the Warm Heart of Africa for good reason, and people are friendly, so greet everyone with \"moni\" before asking anything.", "Malawi is mostly Christian with a Muslim minority, so dress modestly in villages, particularly women, who often wear a chitenje wrap over trousers or shorts.", "Use your right hand for eating and greeting, and shake hands with a slight clap or holding your right forearm with your left hand as a sign of respect.", "Public affection between couples is frowned upon, though you'll see male friends holding hands, which is just friendship.", "Ask before photographing people, and never photograph police, the military or government buildings, which can land you in trouble."], "safety": "Malawi is one of the more relaxed countries in the region, but bag snatching and pickpocketing happen in Lilongwe and Blantyre, so take a taxi rather than walking after dark. Travelling by road at night is risky because of unlit vehicles, pedestrians and livestock, so try to arrive before sunset. On the lakeshore, beach boys selling crafts and boat trips can be pushy but are mostly harmless, and agreeing prices up front avoids arguments. Malaria is serious here, so sleep under a net, use repellent and talk to a doctor about tablets.", "eat_beats": ["Most locals eat nsima at both lunch and dinner, and village restaurants serve a set plate rather than a menu.", "Markets sell roasted maize, sugarcane and fruit cheaply, and you'll be offered snacks through bus windows at every stop.", "Don't drink tap water; use bottled or treated water, especially by the lake.", "Vegetarians can eat nsima with beans, pumpkin leaves or mustard greens almost anywhere.", "Tipping isn't expected at local eateries, but 10% is welcome at lodges and tourist restaurants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Kuche Kuche", "desc": "A light local lager, cheap and cold in most bars. A bottle costs about $1 to $1.50."}, {"name": "Malawi gin", "desc": "The country's well-known local gin, usually drunk with tonic or soda. A bottle from a shop costs a few dollars."}], "greet": "Greetings are slow and polite, starting with moni and muli bwanji (how are you). Shake hands gently, and support your right forearm with your left hand for elders.", "dress": "Women should cover their knees in villages and towns, and a chitenje wrap is handy. Swimwear is fine at lakeside lodges but not walking through villages.", "table": "Wash your hands before and after eating, often from a bowl passed around. Eat with your right hand, and it's polite to take a little of everything offered.", "faith": "About 80% of Malawians are Christian and around 13% Muslim, with traditional beliefs often blended in. Dress modestly at churches and mosques, and ask before watching or photographing ceremonies.", "phrases": [["Moni", "Hello"], ["Muli bwanji?", "How are you?"], ["Ndili bwino", "I'm fine"], ["Zikomo", "Thank you"], ["Ndi ndalama zingati?", "How much is it?"]]},
  "philippines": {"around": "Domestic flights on Cebu Pacific, AirAsia and Philippine Airlines are the quickest way between islands and often super cheap if booked ahead. Ferries and bangkas (outrigger boats) cover shorter hops, while jeepneys, buses and vans handle land travel, and tricycles do short rides. Grab works in Manila, Cebu and Davao, but not on most small islands. Book flights and popular ferries ahead in high season, and keep plans flexible in typhoon season, when boats get cancelled.", "money": "The Philippines uses the peso, and cash is king outside the big cities and resorts, especially on smaller islands like Siquijor, Siargao and parts of Palawan where ATMs are few and can run dry or go offline. Most local ATMs charge a flat fee on foreign cards and have low withdrawal limits, so take out as much as you can when you find one that works. Cards work in malls and nicer hotels, and GCash is used everywhere by locals. Tipping is optional but small tips are appreciated, and light haggling is fine at markets and with tricycle drivers.", "sleep": "Hostels and guesthouses are easy to find in El Nido, Coron, Siargao, Moalboal and Cebu, with plenty of party and surf hostels. Nipa huts, simple bamboo bungalows, are the classic budget beach stay. Book ahead on small islands in the dry season and around Christmas and Holy Week.", "eat": ["Adobo: meat braised in vinegar, soy sauce and garlic", "Sinigang: sour tamarind soup with pork or fish", "Lechon: whole roast pig with crackling skin", "Halo-halo: shaved ice dessert with beans, fruit and milk"], "data": "Globe and Smart SIMs must be registered with your passport under the SIM registration law, and you can do it online after buying the SIM. Coverage is good in cities but weak on remote islands, and eSIMs save the hassle. Resort wifi on small islands is often painfully slow.", "heads_up": "Typhoons between roughly June and November can cancel ferries and flights, so leave spare days in your plans.", "food": [{"name": "Adobo", "wiki": "Adobo (Filipino dish)", "desc": "Chicken or pork braised in vinegar, soy sauce, garlic and bay leaves until tangy and tender, the unofficial national dish. Every family swears theirs is best.", "price": "$1.50 to $4", "when": "Lunch or dinner, with plenty of rice", "order": "Adobong puti skips the soy sauce for a sour, garlicky version"}, {"name": "Sinigang", "wiki": "Sinigang", "desc": "A sour tamarind soup with pork, prawns or fish and vegetables, served with rice and the ultimate rainy-day comfort food.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch, especially on rainy days", "order": "Sinigang na baboy, with pork, is the classic"}, {"name": "Balut", "wiki": "Balut (food)", "desc": "A fertilised duck egg boiled and eaten from the shell with salt and vinegar, sold by street vendors at night. The ultimate dare food, and locals swear by it with beer.", "price": "$0.30 to $0.60", "when": "Night, from vendors calling \"baluuut\"", "order": "Sip the broth first, then add salt and spiced vinegar"}, {"name": "Sisig", "wiki": "Sisig", "desc": "Chopped pork face and ears fried on a sizzling plate with chili and calamansi, a classic beer snack born in Pampanga.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Evening, with beer at grill bars", "order": "Squeeze in calamansi and mix in the raw egg quickly"}, {"name": "Pancit", "wiki": "Pancit", "desc": "Stir-fried noodles with vegetables and meat, cheap at any carinderia and always served at birthdays because long noodles mean long life.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Merienda (afternoon snack) and birthdays", "order": "Pancit canton uses egg noodles; bihon uses thin rice noodles"}, {"name": "Halo-halo", "wiki": "Halo-halo", "desc": "A gloriously messy shaved ice dessert piled with sweet beans, fruit, jelly, purple ube and evaporated milk, the best way to survive the afternoon heat.", "price": "$1.50 to $4", "when": "Hot afternoons, at snack shops", "order": "Look for one topped with leche flan and ube ice cream"}], "food_tip": "Carinderias, small local eateries, serve rice with a dish for a dollar or two, and street food like barbecue skewers and isaw is very cheap. Jollibee is a cultural experience too.", "culture": ["Filipinos are famously warm and hospitable, and adding \"po\" to the end of sentences is a sign of respect when talking to older people.", "The mano, gently pressing an elder's hand to your forehead, is a lovely gesture you'll see in families, and doing it with a host's grandparents goes down well.", "The country is strongly Catholic, so dress modestly in churches and expect lots of things to shut down during Holy Week before Easter.", "Saving face matters a lot, so avoid criticising or embarrassing anyone in public, and keep your cool when things go wrong.", "\"Filipino time\" is real and things often start late, so pack some patience along with your sunscreen.", "Karaoke is a national passion, and being invited to sing is an invitation, not a threat, so grab the mic."], "safety": "The Philippines is generally safe for travellers in the usual tourist spots, but petty theft and pickpocketing happen in Manila, especially on jeepneys and in crowded malls and markets. Most governments advise against travel to parts of Mindanao, especially the Sulu archipelago, so check advice before going south. Boats can be overcrowded and underequipped, so don't get on if the sea looks rough or there are no life jackets. Avoid drugs entirely, as the penalties are severe.", "eat_beats": ["Filipinos eat three meals plus merienda, a mid-afternoon snack, and rice comes with almost everything, even breakfast.", "Drink bottled or filtered water; refill stations are everywhere and cost a few pesos a litre.", "Vegetarian food is hard to find outside big cities, since even vegetable dishes often have pork bits or shrimp paste, so ask \"walang karne?\" (no meat?).", "At carinderias you point at the pots on display, and the server spoons portions onto rice.", "Restaurants often add a 10 percent service charge; if not, leaving 10 percent is appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Tuba", "desc": "Coconut palm wine collected fresh each morning in the Visayas, mildly sour and often reddish from mangrove bark. A bottle in a village costs under $1."}, {"name": "Calamansi juice", "desc": "Fresh juice from tiny local limes, served cold with sugar or hot with honey for a sore throat. About $1 a glass."}], "greet": "A smile and a handshake work for strangers, and people often greet by raising their eyebrows. Add \"po\" when speaking to anyone older.", "dress": "Shorts and T-shirts are fine in most places, but cover shoulders and knees in churches. Muslim areas of Mindanao call for more modest clothing.", "table": "Locals eat with a spoon in the right hand and a fork in the left, and in some places with bare hands (kamayan). Wait for the host to invite you to eat, and accept seconds.", "faith": "About 80 percent of Filipinos are Catholic, with a Muslim population in Mindanao. Keep quiet during Mass and dress modestly at churches and mosques.", "phrases": [["Kumusta?", "How are you?"], ["Salamat po", "Thank you (polite)"], ["Magkano?", "How much?"], ["Masarap!", "Delicious!"], ["Para po", "Stop here, please (on a jeepney)"]]},
  "malaysia": {"around": "Long-distance buses are cheap, comfy and easy to book online, and the ETS trains zip along the west coast linking Kuala Lumpur with Ipoh, Butterworth (for Penang) and beyond. AirAsia and Batik Air fly cheaply to Borneo, Langkawi and Penang if booked early, and flying is the only practical way to cross to Sabah and Sarawak. Grab is everywhere in cities and cheap, and KL's LRT, MRT and monorail are easy with a Touch 'n Go card. Ferries to the Perhentians run from Kuala Besut, and many island businesses close during the northeast monsoon from roughly November to February.", "money": "Malaysia uses the ringgit, and cards, contactless and the Touch 'n Go eWallet work almost everywhere in cities and malls, but hawker stalls, night markets and small shops still prefer cash. ATMs are everywhere and local banks like Maybank and CIMB don't usually add their own fee, though your home bank might. Tipping isn't expected, as many restaurants add a service charge, but rounding up for good service is nice. Haggling is fine at night markets and with souvenir sellers, but not in shops with price tags.", "sleep": "Hostels and guesthouses are cheap in Kuala Lumpur's Bukit Bintang and Chinatown, Penang's George Town, Melaka and the Cameron Highlands, and many are in gorgeous old shophouses. Borneo has jungle lodges near the rainforest and homestays in longhouses. Book island rooms ahead in peak season, especially on the Perhentians and Tioman.", "eat": ["Nasi lemak: coconut rice with sambal, anchovies, peanuts and egg", "Char kway teow: smoky fried flat rice noodles with prawns", "Roti canai: flaky flatbread with curry for dipping", "Teh tarik: strong milky tea poured until frothy"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from CelcomDigi, Maxis (Hotlink), U Mobile or Unifi are sold at airports and convenience stores and need a passport, with cheap data and good coverage in towns. Coverage drops in Borneo's interior, and eSIMs are widely available.", "heads_up": "Dress modestly at mosques, and many of the bigger ones lend robes at the door.", "food": [{"name": "Nasi lemak", "wiki": "Nasi lemak", "desc": "Coconut rice with spicy sambal, crispy anchovies, peanuts, cucumber and egg, often wrapped in a banana leaf. The national breakfast, and dirt cheap from roadside stalls.", "price": "$0.70 to $2.50", "when": "Breakfast, wrapped in banana leaf", "order": "Ask for ayam goreng (fried chicken) on top"}, {"name": "Char kway teow", "wiki": "Char kway teow", "desc": "Smoky, wok-charred flat rice noodles with prawns, cockles, egg and bean sprouts. Penang's hawkers are famous for it.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Dinner at hawker centres", "order": "Ask for extra cockles, or tell them no cockles"}, {"name": "Roti canai", "wiki": "Roti canai", "desc": "Flaky, buttery flatbread flipped and griddled, served with dhal or curry for dipping at mamak stalls, often open 24 hours.", "price": "$0.40 to $1.20", "when": "Breakfast or late night, at mamaks", "order": "Try roti telur (with egg) with mixed curry"}, {"name": "Asam laksa", "wiki": "Laksa", "desc": "Penang's sour, spicy fish-based noodle soup with tamarind, pineapple and mint, a punchy favourite.", "price": "$2 to $3.50", "when": "Lunch or afternoon snack in Penang", "order": "Stir in the shrimp paste for full flavour"}, {"name": "Satay", "wiki": "Satay", "desc": "Charcoal-grilled chicken or beef skewers with peanut sauce, cucumber and rice cakes, best at night markets.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50 per stick", "when": "Evenings, at night markets", "order": "Order by the stick, ten is a normal start"}, {"name": "Teh tarik", "wiki": "Teh tarik", "desc": "Strong milky tea poured back and forth between jugs until frothy, the perfect partner for roti canai.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.20", "when": "Any time, at mamak stalls", "order": "Teh tarik kurang manis means less sweet"}], "food_tip": "Hawker centres, kopitiams and mamak stalls are the cheapest and tastiest way to eat, usually a few ringgit per dish. Penang and Ipoh are serious food towns where you can eat brilliantly for very little.", "culture": ["Malaysia is a mix of Malay, Chinese, Indian and Indigenous cultures, so customs vary, and you'll often see mosques, Chinese temples and Hindu shrines on the same street.", "Islam is the official religion, so dress modestly especially in the more conservative east coast states like Kelantan and Terengganu.", "At mosques, cover arms, legs and, for women, hair; many larger mosques lend robes and scarves at the door, and shoes come off before entering.", "Use your right hand for eating, giving and receiving, and point with your thumb rather than your index finger.", "Public displays of affection are frowned upon, especially in rural areas.", "Take your shoes off when entering homes and some guesthouses."], "safety": "Malaysia is one of the safest countries in Southeast Asia, but bag snatching by motorbike riders and pickpocketing happen in Kuala Lumpur, so carry your bag on the side away from the road. Drug laws are extremely strict, with long prison sentences and even the death penalty for trafficking. On Borneo, some parts of eastern Sabah have government travel advisories, so check before going. Dengue is present, so use repellent.", "eat_beats": ["Mamak stalls run late, many open 24 hours, so a cheap roti and tea is always possible after midnight.", "At hawker centres, grab a table first, order from any stall and they'll bring the food to you; drinks are ordered separately from the drinks seller.", "Tap water is treated in cities, but most people boil or filter it first, so stick to bottled or filtered water.", "Vegetarian food is easy at Indian banana leaf restaurants and Chinese Buddhist vegetarian shops, often marked with a sign saying sayur or su shi.", "Tipping isn't expected, and most restaurants already add a service charge plus 6% to 8% tax."], "drinks": [{"name": "Kopi", "desc": "Malaysian coffee roasted with margarine and sugar, strong and slightly smoky, usually served with condensed milk. A cup costs around $0.50 to $1.20."}, {"name": "Air bandung", "desc": "A bright pink, iced mix of rose syrup and evaporated or condensed milk. Expect $0.50 to $1.20 at hawker stalls."}], "greet": "A light handshake is common, but many Malay Muslims prefer not to shake hands with the opposite sex, so wait for them to offer. A gentle salam, touching your hand to your chest after, is a warm gesture.", "dress": "Cities and beaches are relaxed, but cover shoulders and knees in rural areas and religious sites. Women should carry a scarf for mosques, and in Kelantan and Terengganu dress more modestly overall.", "table": "Many Malays and Indians eat with the right hand, so wash at the sink first. Chopsticks and spoon-and-fork combos are normal at Chinese places, and halal and non-halal food shouldn't be mixed at a shared table.", "faith": "Islam is the majority faith, with sizeable Buddhist, Christian, Hindu and Taoist communities. Remove shoes at mosques and Hindu temples, and avoid visiting mosques during Friday midday prayers.", "phrases": [["Selamat pagi", "Good morning"], ["Terima kasih", "Thank you"], ["Berapa harga?", "How much?"], ["Tak pedas", "Not spicy"], ["Tapau", "Takeaway, please"]]},
  "taiwan": {"around": "The high-speed rail (THSR) zips down the west coast from Taipei to Kaohsiung in about 90 minutes, while the slower TRA trains are much cheaper and cover the gorgeous east coast. Long-distance buses like Kuo-Kuang undercut the trains on many routes, and an EasyCard covers metros, city buses, YouBike rentals and even convenience store snacks. Uber works in the big cities. Book TRA seats to Hualien and Taitung ahead for weekends and holidays, because standing tickets on that line are no fun.", "money": "Taiwan runs on the New Taiwan dollar (TWD, written NT$). Cards are fine in malls, chains and bigger restaurants, but night markets, noodle shops and many hostels want cash, so keep a stack of NT$100 notes on you. ATMs in 7-Eleven and FamilyMart take foreign cards around the clock, and your bank's fees will usually sting more than the local ones. Tipping isn't a thing (sit-down places sometimes add a 10 percent service charge), and haggling is rare outside a few market stalls.", "sleep": "Taipei, Tainan, Taichung and Kaohsiung all have clean, well run hostels, with dorms usually somewhere around NT$500 to 900 a night. Out in the countryside and on the east coast you'll find minsu, family-run guesthouses that are often cheaper and friendlier than hotels. Camping is popular in the mountains, and some trails like Jiaming Lake need permits for their huts and campsites.", "eat": ["Beef noodle soup: braised beef in rich broth with wheat noodles", "Xiaolongbao: soup dumplings with pork filling", "Gua bao: steamed bun with braised pork belly", "Bubble tea: milk tea with chewy tapioca pearls"], "data": "Chunghwa Telecom, Taiwan Mobile and FarEasTone sell cheap unlimited tourist SIMs at the airport with your passport, and eSIMs are easy to set up before you land. Free iTaiwan wifi covers lots of public spots, and coverage is excellent even in the mountains.", "heads_up": "Eating and drinking are banned in MRT stations and on trains, and staff really do hand out fines, so finish that bubble tea before you tap in.", "food": [{"name": "Beef noodle soup", "wiki": "Beef noodle soup", "desc": "Taiwan's unofficial national dish: chewy wheat noodles and braised beef in a dark, spicy broth. A big bowl in a local shop runs around NT$150 to 250.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Lunch or dinner, any noodle shop", "order": "Choose hongshao (red braised) or qingdun (clear broth)"}, {"name": "Xiaolongbao", "wiki": "Xiaolongbao", "desc": "Delicate soup dumplings filled with pork and hot broth. Famous chains charge a premium, but neighbourhood steamers sell a basket for well under NT$150.", "price": "$3 to $6 for 8 to 10", "when": "Lunch or weekend brunch", "order": "Dip in vinegar with ginger shreds"}, {"name": "Lu rou fan", "wiki": "Ló͘-bah-pn̄g", "desc": "Braised pork belly bits in soy and spices spooned over rice, the cheapest comfort meal in Taiwan at around NT$40 to 60 a bowl.", "price": "$1.20 to $2.50", "when": "Any meal, at small rice shops", "order": "Add a braised egg and a side of greens"}, {"name": "Gua bao", "wiki": "Koah-pau", "desc": "A fluffy steamed bun stuffed with braised pork belly, pickled greens, peanut powder and coriander. A classic night market snack.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Snack at night markets", "order": "Ask for no coriander if you're not a fan"}, {"name": "Stinky tofu", "wiki": "Stinky tofu", "desc": "Fermented tofu, usually deep fried and served with pickled cabbage. You'll smell the stall long before you see it, and it tastes far better than it smells.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Evenings at night markets", "order": "Try it steamed or in mala hotpot for extra funk"}, {"name": "Bubble tea", "wiki": "Bubble tea", "desc": "Milk tea with chewy tapioca pearls, invented in Taiwan. Shops are on every corner and you can choose your sugar and ice levels.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Any time, from takeaway shops", "order": "Choose sugar level and ice, half sugar is popular"}], "food_tip": "Night markets and the hot food counters in 7-Eleven and FamilyMart will keep you fed for very little. Look for buffet-style 'zizhu can' shops where you load a tray with rice and dishes and pay by what you pick.", "culture": ["Don't stick your chopsticks upright in a bowl of rice, as it looks like incense at a funeral. Rest them across the bowl or on a holder instead.", "At temples, dress respectfully, step over the raised threshold rather than on it, and don't snap close-ups of people praying without asking.", "Locals queue for everything, from MRT platforms to food stalls, and the dark blue priority seats on the metro are left empty for those who need them even when the train is packed.", "The seventh lunar month (usually August) is Ghost Month, and some locals avoid swimming, moving house or whistling at night. Join in the spirit and don't mock it.", "Gifts and business cards are given and received with both hands. Avoid giving clocks, which are linked to death.", "Taiwan's political status is a sensitive topic, so let locals lead if it comes up. Most are proud of their democracy and happy to talk, but don't assume everyone sees it the same way."], "safety": "Taiwan is one of the safest places in Asia, and petty crime against travellers is rare. The real risks are scooter traffic, summer typhoons and the occasional earthquake, so check forecasts before mountain trips and the east coast. Trails can wash out after storms, and some high-mountain hikes need permits booked weeks ahead.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast shops open early and sell soy milk, egg crepes (dan bing) and fried dough for under $3.", "At night markets, pay at each stall and eat as you walk, and carry cash since many still don't take cards.", "Tap water is safe after boiling, and free hot and cold water dispensers are common in stations and hostels.", "Vegetarian food is easy to find thanks to Buddhist traditions; look for the swastika-like Buddhist symbol or the word 素 (su) on signs.", "Tipping isn't customary, and sit-down restaurants often add a 10% service charge."], "drinks": [{"name": "High mountain oolong", "desc": "Fragrant oolong grown in places like Alishan, sipped from tiny cups at teahouses. A pot at a teahouse runs $5 to $12, and bottled versions cost about $1 at convenience stores."}, {"name": "Taiwan Beer", "desc": "The light local lager, best as the 18 Day fresh version sold chilled. A can costs about $1.50 to $2 at convenience stores."}], "greet": "A smile, nod or light handshake is normal, and people often say ni hao or just hi. Hugging strangers isn't common.", "dress": "Casual, light clothes work everywhere, and Taiwan is relaxed about dress. At temples, avoid very short shorts or beachwear, and bring layers for chilly air-conditioning.", "table": "Dishes are shared, and it's polite to serve others before yourself. Don't stick chopsticks upright in rice, and you can slurp noodles without anyone minding.", "faith": "Most people follow a blend of Buddhism, Taoism and folk religion, with a small Christian minority. In temples, enter by the right door and leave by the left, don't step on thresholds and keep your voice down.", "phrases": [["Nǐ hǎo (你好)", "Hello"], ["Xièxie (謝謝)", "Thank you"], ["Duōshǎo qián? (多少錢?)", "How much?"], ["Bú yào là (不要辣)", "Not spicy"], ["Wài dài (外帶)", "Takeaway"]]},
  "mongolia": {"around": "Buses run from Ulaanbaatar's Dragon bus station to most aimag (province) centres, and the Trans-Mongolian railway links the capital with the north and the Gobi. For the real countryside, almost everyone joins a shared tour in a Russian UAZ van with a driver and guide, which guesthouses in Ulaanbaatar can set up. Domestic flights save days if you're heading to the far west for the Altai. Arrive a few days early in summer so you can find others to split a tour and avoid the Naadam rush in July.", "money": "The currency is the tögrög (MNT). Cards work in Ulaanbaatar cafes, supermarkets and hostels, but once you leave the capital it's cash all the way, and ATMs only turn up in provincial centres. Pull out enough before a countryside tour and carry small notes for snacks and ger stays. Haggling isn't big in shops, though the black market and Narantuul stalls have some wiggle room, and tipping your driver and guide at the end of a tour is expected and much appreciated.", "sleep": "Ulaanbaatar has plenty of backpacker guesthouses and hostels, with dorms often around 10 to 15 US dollars and tours bookable at the front desk. Out on the steppe you'll sleep in tourist ger camps, with nomad families who host travellers, or in a tent wherever you like, since wild camping is broadly accepted. Bring a decent sleeping bag, because nights get cold even in summer.", "eat": ["Buuz: steamed dumplings filled with mutton", "Khuushuur: fried flat pastries stuffed with meat", "Tsuivan: stir-fried noodles with meat and vegetables", "Airag: fermented mare's milk, a summer drink"], "data": "Unitel and Mobicom sell SIMs with your passport at the airport and in the city, and data is cheap. Coverage is solid in towns and along main roads, but expect long stretches with no signal on countryside tours.", "heads_up": "Pickpocketing is common in Ulaanbaatar, especially on buses and at Narantuul market, so keep your phone and wallet zipped away.", "food": [{"name": "Buuz", "wiki": "Buuz", "desc": "Steamed dumplings stuffed with mutton and onion, found in every guanz (canteen) and eaten in huge numbers at Lunar New Year. A plate is cheap and filling.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "At canteens, and during Tsagaan Sar", "order": "Ordered by the piece, usually four to six"}, {"name": "Khuushuur", "wiki": "Khuushuur", "desc": "Deep fried flat pastries filled with minced mutton, the classic Naadam festival snack and a solid roadside lunch.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Naadam festival and roadside stops", "order": "Eat with your hands and mind the hot juices"}, {"name": "Tsuivan", "wiki": "Tsuivan", "desc": "Hand-cut noodles stir fried with mutton and a few vegetables. Basically the default meal in any canteen across the country.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Lunch or dinner at canteens"}, {"name": "Khorkhog", "wiki": "Khorkhog", "desc": "Mutton cooked inside a sealed milk can with hot stones, usually made for guests or special occasions in the countryside. Passing the warm stones between your hands is part of the ritual.", "price": "$10 to $25", "when": "Countryside gatherings and ger camp feasts", "order": "Hold the hot stones; locals say it is healthy"}, {"name": "Airag", "wiki": "Airag", "desc": "Fermented mare's milk, lightly alcoholic and sour, offered in summer by nomad families. Take at least a sip when it's offered.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Summer, July and August", "order": "Start with a small bowl; it can upset your stomach"}, {"name": "Suutei tsai", "wiki": "Suutei tsai", "desc": "Salty milk tea, sometimes with butter or millet, served in every ger and canteen. It's an acquired taste but warms you right up.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "All day, with every meal"}], "food_tip": "Look for a guanz, the simple canteens in towns and along highways, where a plate of tsuivan or buuz and a tea costs very little. Vegetarians should stock up on supplies in Ulaanbaatar, because the countryside menu is mutton, mutton and more mutton.", "culture": ["In a ger, don't step on the doorway threshold, move clockwise around the stove, and don't point your feet at the hearth or the family altar at the back.", "Accept food, drinks and gifts with your right hand, with your left supporting your right elbow, and taste or at least touch what you're offered before putting it down.", "Don't whistle inside a ger, don't lean on the central support poles, and don't throw rubbish into the fire, which is considered sacred.", "If you accidentally kick someone's foot, shake their hand straight after, as it's an old way of saying no offence meant.", "Walk clockwise around ovoo (stone cairns) and Buddhist stupas, and adding a stone to an ovoo for good luck is a nice touch.", "Hospitality is a big deal on the steppe, so bring small gifts like sweets, fruit or photos from home when you visit families."], "safety": "Ulaanbaatar is generally fine, but pickpocketing on crowded buses and around the State Department Store and Narantuul market is common, and drunk men can be a nuisance late at night. In winter the city's air pollution gets genuinely bad, so pack a proper mask if you're there from November to March. Out in the countryside, the bigger risks are long rough drives and remoteness from medical help, so travel insurance with evacuation cover is worth having.", "eat_beats": ["Countryside food is mostly meat, flour and dairy, and fresh vegetables are rare outside Ulaanbaatar.", "Don't drink tap water; buy bottled or boil it, and bring a filter for long trips.", "Vegetarians should carry snacks for road trips, as Ulaanbaatar has the only reliable vegetarian restaurants.", "Tipping isn't traditional, but about 10 percent in Ulaanbaatar restaurants and a tip for drivers and guides are appreciated.", "Stock up at Ulaanbaatar supermarkets and markets before heading out, as countryside shops are small and far apart."], "drinks": [{"name": "Mongolian vodka", "desc": "Drunk at celebrations with lots of toasts, and a bottle costs $5 to $15. Locals dip a ring finger and flick drops to the sky, wind and earth before drinking."}, {"name": "Shimiin arkhi", "desc": "A mild clear spirit distilled from fermented milk by nomad families. It's rarely sold and usually offered free to guests."}], "greet": "The everyday greeting is sain baina uu, and at Tsagaan Sar younger people support their elders' forearms from below. In the city, a normal handshake is fine.", "dress": "Dress in warm layers year round, as nights are cold even in summer. Cover shoulders and knees at monasteries.", "table": "Take a bowl with your right hand, supporting your elbow with your left, and at least sip or nibble what's offered. If you don't want vodka, touching the cup to your lips is enough.", "faith": "Most Mongolians are Tibetan Buddhist, often blended with shamanic traditions, and Kazakhs in the far west are mostly Muslim. At monasteries walk and spin prayer wheels clockwise, and ask before photographing ceremonies.", "phrases": [["Sain baina uu (Сайн байна уу)", "Hello"], ["Bayarlalaa (Баярлалаа)", "Thank you"], ["Hed ve? (Хэд вэ?)", "How much?"], ["Nokhoi khor (Нохой хорь)", "Hold your dog!"], ["Amttai (Амттай)", "Tasty"]]},
  "uzbekistan": {"around": "The fast Afrosiyob trains link Tashkent, Samarkand and Bukhara and are clean, punctual and cheap, while slower Sharq and regional trains run to Khiva and Urgench. Shared taxis and marshrutkas fill the gaps and leave when full, and Yandex Go is the easy ride app in the main cities. Book train seats a few days ahead on the official railway app or website, as the popular routes sell out, especially in spring and autumn.", "money": "The currency is the som (UZS), and you'll handle big stacks of notes. Visa cards are now taken in many city restaurants, hotels and shops, but bazaars, small guesthouses and shared taxis are cash only, so withdraw from bank ATMs that accept foreign cards (Kapitalbank and Hamkorbank machines are usually reliable). Some restaurants add a 10 to 15 percent service charge to the bill, so check before tipping extra. Haggling is expected at bazaars and with souvenir sellers, so start low and keep it friendly.", "sleep": "Family-run guesthouses and B&Bs are the sweet spot, especially inside the old towns of Bukhara and Khiva, and a good breakfast is usually included. Hostels with dorms around 8 to 15 US dollars are easy to find in Tashkent, Samarkand and Bukhara. For something different, yurt camps near Aydarkul Lake and homestays in the Nuratau mountains are cheap and memorable.", "eat": ["Plov: rice cooked with lamb, carrots and spices", "Samsa: baked pastry filled with meat and onion", "Shashlik: grilled skewers of lamb or beef", "Lagman: hand-pulled noodles in a meaty broth"], "data": "Beeline, Ucell, Mobiuz and Uzmobile SIMs are cheap and sold at the airport and official stores with your passport. Coverage is good in cities and along the main train lines, and eSIMs are now an easy option too.", "heads_up": "Cover shoulders and knees when you visit mosques, madrasas and mausoleums.", "food": [{"name": "Plov", "wiki": "Plov", "desc": "Rice cooked in huge cauldrons with lamb, carrots, chickpeas and spices, traditionally a lunchtime dish. Head to a plov centre around midday, before it sells out.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Midday, ideally before 1pm", "order": "Try Tashkent style with quail eggs and horse sausage"}, {"name": "Samsa", "wiki": "Samsa (food)", "desc": "Flaky pastry filled with meat and onion, baked against the walls of a tandir oven. Grab one hot from a street stall for small change.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.20", "when": "Breakfast or a snack", "order": "Look for pumpkin samsa in autumn"}, {"name": "Lagman", "wiki": "Laghman (food)", "desc": "Hand-pulled noodles in a rich meat and vegetable broth, or fried dry. A hearty bowl in a chaikhana is a cheap filling lunch.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch at chaikhanas"}, {"name": "Shashlik", "wiki": "Shashlik", "desc": "Skewers of lamb, beef or minced meat grilled over charcoal and served with raw onion and bread. Every bazaar has a smoky shashlik grill.", "price": "$1 to $3 a skewer", "when": "Evenings at grill stalls", "order": "Try jigar, liver, or lamb with fat"}, {"name": "Manti", "wiki": "Manti (food)", "desc": "Large steamed dumplings stuffed with meat, onion and sometimes pumpkin, served with sour cream or yoghurt.", "price": "$2 to $4 a plate", "when": "Lunch or dinner", "order": "Ask for pumpkin manti for a meat-free option"}, {"name": "Non", "wiki": "Tandyr nan", "desc": "Round, stamped flatbread baked in a tandir oven, eaten with every meal. Samarkand's version is famously thick and heavy.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "With every meal"}], "food_tip": "Eat at chaikhanas (teahouses) and local canteens rather than tourist-facing restaurants in the old towns, and you'll pay a fraction of the price. Bazaars are great for cheap fresh fruit, nuts and bread.", "culture": ["Men greet each other with a handshake and often a hand on the heart, but wait for a woman to offer her hand first. A hand on the heart with a slight nod is a polite greeting for anyone.", "Bread is treated with respect, so never place it upside down, throw it away or put it on the ground.", "Cover shoulders and knees at mosques, madrasas and mausoleums, and women should carry a scarf for active mosques. Take your shoes off where locals do.", "Uzbekistan is mostly Muslim but fairly relaxed, and alcohol is easy to find in cities. Be more discreet during Ramadan and in conservative areas like the Fergana Valley.", "If you're invited to a home, bring a small gift like sweets or fruit, take your shoes off, and expect to be fed far more than you can eat.", "Ask before photographing people, especially women and older folk at bazaars, and avoid photographing police or military sites."], "safety": "Uzbekistan is very safe, and violent crime against travellers is rare. The main hassles are taxi drivers overcharging at airports and stations (use Yandex Go where you can) and the odd friendly guide steering you into a cousin's carpet shop. Summer temperatures in Bukhara and Khiva push past 40 degrees, so sightsee early, drink lots of water and carry rehydration salts for the occasional upset stomach.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch around 1pm is the big meal, and plov centres often run out by early afternoon.", "Don't drink the tap water, and bottled water is cheap everywhere.", "Vegetarians can eat salads, bread, soups and pumpkin manti, but meat stock is common, so ask.", "Many restaurants add a 10 to 20 percent service charge, so check the bill before tipping.", "Bargain at bazaars, but prices at canteens are usually fixed and fair."], "drinks": [{"name": "Green tea (kok choy)", "desc": "Served in bowls without sugar at every meal and offered constantly. A pot is about $0.50 in a chaikhana."}, {"name": "Ayran", "desc": "Salty, chilled yoghurt drink, refreshing in summer and good with fatty food, for about $0.50 to $1."}], "greet": "Men shake hands with a hand on the heart, and say assalomu alaykum. For women, a hand on the heart and a nod is safest.", "dress": "Long trousers and covered shoulders are best, especially at religious sites and in villages. Tashkent is more relaxed.", "table": "Tear bread by hand and don't put it upside down. Hosts pour tea a little at a time, and it's polite to accept a refill.", "faith": "Most Uzbeks are Sunni Muslim, practised in a relaxed way, with small Orthodox and Jewish communities. At mosques and shrines, cover up, remove shoes and stay quiet.", "phrases": [["Assalomu alaykum", "Hello"], ["Rahmat", "Thank you"], ["Qancha turadi?", "How much does it cost?"], ["Mazali", "Delicious"], ["Yaxshi", "Good"]]},
  "tajikistan": {"around": "Shared taxis are the main way between towns and leave from set stands when they're full. For the Pamir Highway, most travellers share a 4x4 with a driver arranged through guesthouses in Dushanbe, Khorog or Osh in Kyrgyzstan, while cyclists and hitchhikers do it the slow way. You'll need the GBAO permit for the Pamirs, which you can add when you apply for the e-visa. Give yourself flexibility, since landslides and road works can close routes for a day or two.", "money": "The currency is the somoni (TJS). ATMs that take foreign cards exist in Dushanbe and Khorog but get scarce or useless elsewhere, so bring a good stash of cash (crisp US dollars or euros are easy to change) before heading to the Pamirs. Cards almost never work outside a few Dushanbe hotels and supermarkets. Tipping isn't expected, though rounding up for drivers and homestay hosts is kind, and haggling is normal for taxis, shared jeeps and at bazaars.", "sleep": "Homestays are the standard all along the Pamir Highway and in the Fann Mountains and Wakhan Valley, and the price usually includes dinner and breakfast. Dushanbe and Khorog have a few hostels and simple guesthouses, while camping is easy and free in the mountains if you have your own gear. Expect basic but warm rooms, shared mats on the floor and outdoor toilets.", "eat": ["Qurutob: flatbread soaked in yogurt sauce with onion", "Osh: rice pilaf with meat and carrots", "Sambusa: baked pastry filled with meat", "Green tea: served everywhere, often with bread and sweets"], "data": "Tcell, Megafon, Babilon-M and ZET-Mobile SIMs need your passport and work fine in towns. Expect long gaps with no signal on the Pamir Highway, and wifi at homestays is rare or very slow.", "heads_up": "Passes on the Pamir Highway climb above 4,000 metres, so take it slow, acclimatise and watch for signs of altitude sickness.", "food": [{"name": "Qurutob", "wiki": "Qurutob", "desc": "The national dish: torn flatbread soaked in a salty yoghurt sauce, topped with fried onions and fresh vegetables. It's eaten with your hands from a shared platter.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch in chaikhanas, shared from one dish", "order": "Eat it with your right hand, the traditional way"}, {"name": "Osh", "wiki": "Plov", "desc": "Tajik plov, rice cooked with mutton, carrots and onion, often served at weddings and big gatherings. Teahouses in Dushanbe serve it at lunchtime.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch, especially Thursdays and Sundays", "order": "Ask if they have the Hisor style with chickpeas"}, {"name": "Sambusa", "wiki": "Samosa", "desc": "Triangular baked or fried pastries stuffed with meat and onion, sold hot from street stalls and tandir ovens.", "price": "$0.30 to $1 each", "when": "Fresh from tandoor ovens at bazaars", "order": "Pick meat and onion or pumpkin"}, {"name": "Laghman", "wiki": "Laghman (food)", "desc": "Thick hand-pulled noodles in a meaty broth with peppers and tomato, a staple in canteens.", "price": "$2 to $3", "when": "Lunch in Dushanbe cafés"}, {"name": "Shashlik", "wiki": "Shashlik", "desc": "Grilled meat skewers, usually mutton, served with raw onion and fresh bread. Easy to find in towns and at roadside stops.", "price": "$1 to $2 a skewer", "when": "Evening grills by the road", "order": "Lamb is classic, with raw onion and bread"}], "food_tip": "Your homestay dinner and breakfast are where most of your eating happens in the Pamirs, so bring snacks like dried fruit and nuts for long drives. In Dushanbe, chaikhanas and bazaar stalls keep meals very cheap.", "culture": ["Hospitality is huge, and you'll be offered tea and bread almost everywhere. Accept at least a cup, and take or offer things with your right hand.", "Take your shoes off before stepping onto carpets or entering a home, and sit cross-legged or with your legs tucked rather than pointing your feet at people or the food.", "Tajikistan is mostly Muslim and fairly conservative, so cover shoulders and knees, and women may want a scarf in villages and at mosques.", "Most Pamiris are Ismaili Muslims with their own traditions, and the Pamiri house with its symbolic pillars and skylight is a special space. Ask your hosts about it, as they're usually proud to explain.", "Bread is sacred, so don't put it upside down or on the floor, and never throw it away in front of people.", "Ask before taking photos of people, and avoid photographing military posts, border guards and bridges."], "safety": "Tajikistan is generally safe and very welcoming, with little crime against travellers. The real risks are altitude (passes on the Pamir Highway top 4,000 metres), remote roads far from medical help and stomach bugs, so acclimatise slowly, purify your water and get insurance that covers evacuation. Stay well away from the Afghan border along the Panj River beyond what the road requires, and check current advice for areas near the Kyrgyz border.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is usually the main meal from noon to 2pm, and in homestays dinner is around 7pm.", "Don't drink tap or stream water without treating it, so bring a filter or purifying tablets for the Pamirs.", "Vegetarian food is hard outside Dushanbe, so ask hosts for eggs, potatoes, bread and soup without meat.", "Bazaars sell bread, dried apricots, mulberries, nuts and dairy, which are cheap snacks for long drives.", "Tipping isn't expected, but rounding up or leaving 5% to 10% in city restaurants is appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Green tea", "desc": "Kuk choy is poured into small bowls, and the host pours little and often to keep you there. A pot in a chaikhana costs about $0.50."}, {"name": "Shir choy", "desc": "A Pamiri tea made with milk, salt and butter, sometimes with bread crumbled in. Usually served free with homestay meals."}], "greet": "Men shake hands, often with the left hand on the heart, and ask after your health and family. Women usually greet each other with a hug, and male visitors should wait for a woman to offer her hand.", "dress": "Wear long trousers and covered shoulders, and women should bring a scarf for villages and mosques. Shorts look strange outside Dushanbe.", "table": "Meals are on the dastarkhan, a cloth laid on the floor, and you shouldn't step on it. Hosts keep pouring tea, so leave a little in your bowl when you're done.", "faith": "Most Tajiks are Sunni Muslim, while Pamiris in Gorno-Badakhshan are Ismaili. Dress modestly at mosques and shrines, take off shoes and ask before taking photos inside.", "phrases": [["Salom (Салом)", "Hello"], ["Rahmat (Раҳмат)", "Thank you"], ["Chand pul? (Чанд пул?)", "How much?"], ["Gusht na (Гӯшт на)", "No meat"], ["Bisyor bomaza (Бисёр бомаза)", "Very tasty"]]},
  "kazakhstan": {"around": "Distances are enormous, so long-distance trains (including fast Talgo services) and overnight sleepers are the classic way across the steppe, with buses filling in shorter routes. Domestic flights on budget carriers like FlyArystan often cost little more than the train and save a full day between Almaty, Astana and Shymkent. Yandex Go and inDrive work in cities. For Charyn Canyon and the Kolsai and Kaindy lakes, join a shared tour from Almaty or split a hired driver.", "money": "The currency is the tenge (KZT). Cards and contactless are accepted almost everywhere in cities, and locals pay for nearly everything with the Kaspi app, but carry cash for rural guesthouses, bazaars and shared taxis. ATMs are everywhere in towns and take foreign cards. Tipping around 10 percent is nice in restaurants, though many already add a service charge, and haggling is fine at bazaars.", "sleep": "Almaty and Astana have plenty of hostels with dorms around 8 to 15 US dollars, and they're good places to meet people for shared tours. In villages near the lakes and canyons, like Saty, guesthouses and homestays are cheap and usually include meals. Camping is easy in the mountains around Almaty in summer.", "eat": ["Beshbarmak: boiled meat on wide flat noodles", "Baursak: fried dough puffs served with tea", "Kazy: horse meat sausage, a festive food", "Kumis: fermented mare's milk, tangy and fizzy"], "data": "Beeline, Kcell, Activ and Tele2 SIMs are cheap, sold with your passport, and coverage is good in cities. Phones on local SIMs may need registering if you stay longer than a month, and signal fades quickly out on the steppe and in the mountains.", "food": [{"name": "Beshbarmak", "wiki": "Beshbarmak", "desc": "The national dish: boiled horse meat or mutton over wide flat noodles with onion broth. It's served at celebrations and in traditional restaurants.", "price": "$6 to $10", "when": "Family celebrations and welcoming guests", "order": "Traditionally eaten by hand; the name means five fingers"}, {"name": "Baursak", "wiki": "Boortsog", "desc": "Puffy fried dough pieces served with tea at almost every meal and gathering.", "price": "$0.50 to $2", "when": "With tea at every gathering"}, {"name": "Kazy", "wiki": "Qazı", "desc": "Rich horse meat sausage, often served sliced on festive platters. Try a bit if you're curious, it's a big part of Kazakh food culture.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Festive tables and Nauryz", "order": "Usually served sliced on a platter of cold meats"}, {"name": "Kumis", "wiki": "Kumis", "desc": "Fermented mare's milk, tangy and slightly fizzy, sold fresh in summer at roadside stands and bazaars.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Summer, from bazaars and roadside stalls", "order": "Ask for a small taste first; it's sour and fizzy"}, {"name": "Manti", "wiki": "Manti (food)", "desc": "Large steamed dumplings stuffed with meat and onion, a cheap and filling canteen classic.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch at canteens and cafés", "order": "Add sour cream, or vinegar and chilli"}, {"name": "Shashlik", "wiki": "Shashlik", "desc": "Charcoal-grilled meat skewers sold at bazaars, parks and roadside cafes, usually with bread and onion.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Evening grills and summer picnics", "order": "Lamb is classic; it's ordered per skewer with onions"}], "food_tip": "Stolovayas (self-service canteens) are the budget hero, where you point at what you want and pay very little. Almaty's Green Bazaar is great for cheap snacks, dried fruit and trying kumis.", "culture": ["Tea is central to hospitality, and hosts often pour only half a cup so they can keep it hot and refill it often. A full cup can hint that it's time to leave.", "Respect elders: stand when they enter, let them start eating first and greet them before others.", "Bread is respected, so never place it upside down or throw it away carelessly.", "Kazakhstan is mostly Muslim but quite secular, and alcohol is common in cities. Dress modestly at mosques and in rural areas.", "Men shake hands, often with both hands as a sign of respect, but let women decide whether to offer theirs.", "Russian is still widely spoken, but learning a couple of Kazakh words like 'rakhmet' (thank you) gets a warm reaction."], "safety": "Kazakhstan is generally safe, though taxi touts at airports and stations overcharge, so stick to Yandex Go or inDrive. Mountain weather near Almaty changes fast, so pack layers and tell someone your plans on hikes. In winter, icy pavements and serious cold are bigger risks than crime.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal around 1pm, and weekday business lunch deals give you soup, a main and tea cheaply.", "Most locals boil or filter their water, so stick to bottled.", "Vegetarians will find salads and vegetable dishes in Almaty and Astana, but outside them meat is in nearly everything.", "Tipping around 10 percent is common in cafés, but many add a service charge, so check the bill first.", "Tea comes before and after almost every meal and is rarely refused."], "drinks": [{"name": "Shubat", "desc": "Fermented camel milk, creamy and tangy, and milder than kumis. About $1 to $2 a glass at bazaars."}, {"name": "Kazakh tea", "desc": "Strong black tea with milk, poured into small bowls called kese. Often free with meals, or $0.50 to $1 in cafés."}], "greet": "Say 'Sälemetsiz be' formally or 'Sälem' to friends, and in cities Russian 'Privet' works too. Greet elders first.", "dress": "Cities are modern and casual, but dress more modestly in the south and in villages, and women should cover their hair in mosques. Winters are brutal, with Astana dropping below minus 30°C, so pack serious layers.", "table": "The guest of honour sits furthest from the door, and you're expected to at least taste what's offered. Toasts are long and frequent, so pace yourself.", "faith": "About 70 percent of Kazakhs are Muslim, mostly Sunni, and around a quarter are Russian Orthodox. Remove shoes at mosques, and be respectful at the Yasawi Mausoleum in Turkistan, an important pilgrimage site.", "phrases": [["Sälemetsiz be (Сәлеметсіз бе)", "Hello (formal)"], ["Rakhmet (Рахмет)", "Thank you"], ["Qansha turady? (Қанша тұрады?)", "How much is it?"], ["Dämdi (Дәмді)", "Tasty"], ["Spasibo (Спасибо)", "Thank you (Russian)"]]},
  "pakistan": {"around": "Daewoo Express and Faisal Movers run comfortable, cheap coaches between the big cities, and NATCO buses head up the Karakoram Highway to Gilgit and Hunza. Flights to Gilgit and Skardu are spectacular but often cancelled for weather, so allow buffer days. Careem, inDrive and Uber (in some cities) work well. Some areas need police escorts or permits for foreigners, so check current rules for each leg before you go.", "money": "The currency is the Pakistani rupee (PKR), and Pakistan is still mostly a cash country. ATMs from big banks like HBL, Meezan and UBL work in cities, but they get sparse up the Karakoram Highway, so withdraw plenty before heading to Hunza, Skardu or Chitral. Cards work in malls and upmarket hotels only. Small tips for drivers, guides and hotel staff are customary, and haggling is normal at bazaars and with rickshaw drivers, so agree on prices first.", "sleep": "Budget hotels and guesthouses are the usual choice, and a growing number of backpacker hostels have popped up in Lahore, Islamabad and Hunza. In the north, family guesthouses in Karimabad, Passu and Skardu are cheap and come with stunning views. Some hotels can't or won't take foreigners, especially in smaller towns, so it's worth messaging ahead.", "eat": ["Biryani: spiced rice layered with meat", "Chapli kebab: flat spiced minced meat patty from Peshawar", "Karahi: meat cooked with tomatoes and chili in a wok", "Doodh patti: strong tea brewed in milk"], "data": "Jazz, Zong and Telenor SIMs need biometric thumbprint registration with your passport at an official shop, and foreign phones can only use local SIMs for a limited time before they need PTA registration. Coverage is great in cities, and in Gilgit-Baltistan, SCOM and Zong usually work best.", "heads_up": "Dress conservatively, and carry copies of your passport and visa for the police checkpoints that are common in the north.", "food": [{"name": "Biryani", "wiki": "Biryani", "desc": "Spiced rice layered with chicken or mutton, with Karachi's fiery Sindhi style the most famous. A plate from a busy local spot costs very little.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch or dinner, at biryani houses", "order": "Ask for raita on the side to cool it down"}, {"name": "Chapli kebab", "wiki": "Chapli kebab", "desc": "A wide, flat minced beef patty packed with spices and fried in fat, a Peshawar classic now found nationwide.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Evening, in Peshawar's grill streets", "order": "Eat in naan with onion and green chutney"}, {"name": "Karahi", "wiki": "Chicken karahi", "desc": "Chicken or mutton cooked fast with tomatoes, green chilli and ginger in a wok-like pan, ordered by the half or full kilo and shared with naan.", "price": "$5 to $12 for half a kilo", "when": "Dinner with friends, at roadside restaurants", "order": "Half a kilo feeds two; Peshawari karahi is less spicy"}, {"name": "Nihari", "wiki": "Nihari", "desc": "Slow-cooked beef or mutton stew with a rich, spicy gravy, traditionally eaten for breakfast in Lahore and Karachi.", "price": "$1.50 to $4", "when": "Breakfast, from 6am until it sells out", "order": "Top with ginger, lemon and green chilli"}, {"name": "Paya", "wiki": "Paya (food)", "desc": "Slow-simmered trotters in a rich, spicy broth, a Lahori breakfast favourite mopped up with naan.", "price": "$1.50 to $4", "when": "Breakfast, especially on winter weekends"}, {"name": "Doodh patti", "wiki": "Masala chai", "desc": "Strong tea brewed directly in milk with lots of sugar. You'll drink a dozen cups a day, often bought for you by strangers.", "price": "$0.15 to $0.50", "when": "All day, at tea stalls", "order": "Ask for \"kam cheeni\" for less sugar"}], "food_tip": "Street food and dhabas (roadside eateries) are incredibly cheap, and you'll often find someone has quietly paid your bill. In Lahore, head to food streets in the old city for the full experience.", "culture": ["Pakistan is a conservative Muslim country, so dress modestly: men in long trousers, and women in loose clothes covering arms and legs with a scarf handy. A shalwar kameez is cheap, comfy and earns big smiles.", "Men shake hands with men, but don't offer your hand to a woman unless she offers first. Use your right hand for eating and passing things.", "Hospitality is legendary, and you'll get invited for tea and meals constantly. It's fine to accept, but don't take advantage of it.", "Ask before photographing people, especially women, and avoid snapping military, police or government sites.", "During Ramadan, avoid eating, drinking or smoking in public in daylight hours.", "Alcohol is illegal for Muslims and hard to find, so don't go looking for it and don't drink in public."], "safety": "Many governments advise against travel to Balochistan, parts of Khyber Pakhtunkhwa and areas near the Afghan border and the Line of Control, so read your own country's advice and check that your insurance covers you. The popular northern routes and the big cities see plenty of backpackers, but checkpoints are common and you should carry copies of your passport and visa. Solo women get a lot of attention, so dressing conservatively and travelling with trusted contacts helps a lot. Road safety on mountain highways is the biggest everyday risk.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is around 1pm to 2pm and dinner often after 9pm, and restaurants stay busy late.", "Don't drink tap water; bottled water is cheap everywhere.", "Vegetarian food is easy, with daal, chana and sabzi on most menus, but always check the gravy.", "Women and families often sit in a separate family section, which is quieter and open to mixed groups.", "Tipping isn't expected at dhabas, but leaving 5 to 10 percent at sit-down restaurants is appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Lassi", "desc": "A thick yoghurt drink served sweet or salty in big steel cups, very refreshing in summer, for under $1."}, {"name": "Kashmiri chai", "desc": "Pink tea brewed with milk, salt and cardamom, topped with nuts, a winter speciality for about $0.50 to $1."}], "greet": "\"Assalam o alaikum\" with a handshake is standard between men, often with a hug among friends. Women greet each other, and men shouldn't initiate contact with women.", "dress": "Wear long, loose clothes everywhere, and women should carry a dupatta (scarf) for mosques and conservative areas. A shalwar kameez is the easiest choice.", "table": "Eat with your right hand, using naan or roti to scoop food. Guests are served first and refused offers of more food are often repeated, so accept a little.", "faith": "About 96 percent of Pakistanis are Muslim, with small Christian, Hindu and Sikh communities. Remove shoes at mosques and Sufi shrines, women should cover their hair, and don't walk in front of people praying.", "phrases": [["Assalam o alaikum (السلام علیکم)", "Hello"], ["Shukriya (شکریہ)", "Thank you"], ["Kitne ka hai? (کتنے کا ہے)", "How much is it?"], ["Mirch kam (مرچ کم)", "Less spicy"], ["Bohat mazay ka (بہت مزے کا)", "Very tasty"]]},
  "armenia": {"around": "Marshrutkas and shared taxis connect Yerevan to most towns, leaving from different bus stations depending on direction, so ask your hostel which one you need. Yandex Go and GG are cheap and handy for day trips to places like Garni and Geghard. Trains are limited, but the summer overnight train to Tbilisi is a nice way to cross into Georgia. Hitchhiking is also common and pretty easy.", "money": "The currency is the dram (AMD). Cards and contactless work in most Yerevan cafes, supermarkets and hostels, but carry cash for marshrutkas, rural guesthouses and markets. ATMs are easy to find and many have low or no fees. Restaurants often add a 10 percent service charge, so check the bill before tipping more, and light haggling is fine at the Vernissage market.", "sleep": "Yerevan is packed with cheap hostels, with dorms often around 8 to 15 US dollars. Family-run guesthouses and B&Bs are the norm in Dilijan, Goris, Yeghegnadzor and near Tatev, and home-cooked meals are often part of the deal. Camping is easy and mostly accepted in the countryside.", "eat": ["Khorovats: Armenian barbecue of pork, lamb or vegetables", "Lavash: thin flatbread baked in a clay tonir oven", "Tolma: grape leaves stuffed with spiced meat and rice", "Ghapama: pumpkin stuffed with rice and dried fruit, a festive dish"], "data": "Team, Viva and Ucom SIMs are cheap and quick to buy with your passport, and an eSIM is easy too. Coverage is good in towns and along major highways, and most cafes and hostels have fast wifi.", "heads_up": "Check current advice before travelling near the border with Azerbaijan, as some roads run very close to it.", "food": [{"name": "Khorovats", "wiki": "Khorovats", "desc": "Armenian barbecue of pork, lamb or chicken, plus grilled vegetables, cooked over coals. It's the centrepiece of any feast or picnic.", "price": "$5 to $9", "when": "Dinners, weekends and celebrations", "order": "Pork khorovats is the most popular choice"}, {"name": "Lavash", "wiki": "Lavash", "desc": "Thin, soft flatbread baked against the walls of a clay tonir oven. It comes with everything and is on UNESCO's heritage list.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 a sheet", "when": "With every meal"}, {"name": "Tolma", "wiki": "Dolma", "desc": "Grape or cabbage leaves stuffed with spiced meat and rice, served with garlicky yoghurt.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Lunch or dinner, in restaurants", "order": "Try summer grape leaves or winter cabbage"}, {"name": "Zhingyalov hats", "wiki": "Zhingyalov hats", "desc": "Flatbread stuffed with up to a dozen chopped wild herbs, a speciality from Karabakh sold at Yerevan street stalls for very little.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Lunch, from street stalls", "order": "Watch it made fresh at Yerevan stalls"}, {"name": "Khash", "wiki": "Khash (dish)", "desc": "A rich broth of boiled cow's feet eaten early on winter mornings with garlic, dried lavash and a shot of vodka. It's a social ritual as much as a meal.", "price": "$5 to $8", "when": "Early winter mornings, often with vodka", "order": "Crumble dry lavash in and add garlic"}, {"name": "Gata", "wiki": "Gata (food)", "desc": "A sweet, buttery bread or pastry, sold at stalls near monasteries like Geghard and Garni.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Snack, near monasteries", "order": "Ask if they have nazook, a rolled version"}], "food_tip": "Bakeries and street stalls selling lahmajo, zhingyalov hats and pastries keep lunch dirt cheap. Shuka markets are great for fruit and dried fruit, and tap water in Yerevan is famously good, so refill your bottle at the little drinking fountains (pulpulaks).", "culture": ["Armenians are big on hospitality and long meals with toasts, so expect to be fed a lot and to raise your glass often.", "At churches and monasteries, dress modestly. Women sometimes cover their heads, and you should keep your voice down during services.", "The 1915 genocide is a deeply sensitive subject. The Tsitsernakaberd memorial in Yerevan is worth visiting, but treat the topic with care.", "Relations with Azerbaijan and Turkey and the loss of Nagorno-Karabakh in 2023 are raw topics, so listen rather than offer opinions.", "Mount Ararat is a national symbol even though it sits across the border in Turkey, so don't be surprised by its emotional weight.", "Greet older people first and respectfully, and a handshake is standard between men."], "safety": "Armenia is very safe, and crime against travellers is rare. Use ride apps over street taxis to avoid overcharging, and watch for slippery paths and sudden weather on mountain hikes. Check current advice before travelling near the Azerbaijan border, as some roads run close to it.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the big meal, and dinners with friends run late with many toasts.", "Yerevan's GUM market is the best place to taste dried fruits, cheese and churchkhela before buying.", "Tap water in Yerevan is excellent and comes from mountain springs, so refill at the pulpulak fountains.", "Vegetarians do well with grilled vegetables, salads, lentil soups, cheeses and zhingyalov hats, especially during Lent.", "Many restaurants add a 10% service charge, so check the bill; otherwise 10% is a kind tip."], "drinks": [{"name": "Tan", "desc": "A salty, chilled yogurt drink that goes well with khorovats. A glass costs about $0.50 to $1."}, {"name": "Armenian brandy", "desc": "Aged brandy, called konyak locally, with a long history in Yerevan. A glass costs around $2 to $5 in bars."}], "greet": "Men shake hands firmly, and friends kiss once on the cheek. Say barev dzez for hello, and greet elders first.", "dress": "Yerevan is stylish and people dress up a little. At churches, wear long trousers or skirts, and women often cover their heads with a scarf.", "table": "Expect a tamada (toastmaster) to lead the toasts, so wait for a toast before drinking. Hosts will keep filling your plate, so leave a bit if you're full.", "faith": "Armenia was the first country to adopt Christianity, in 301, and most people belong to the Armenian Apostolic Church. Light a candle if you like, don't turn your back on the altar when leaving and keep quiet during services.", "phrases": [["Barev dzez (Բարև ձեզ)", "Hello"], ["Shnorhakalutyun (Շնորհակալություն)", "Thank you"], ["Inch arzhe? (Ինչ արժե՞)", "How much?"], ["Ayo (Այո)", "Yes"], ["Kenats! (Կենաց)", "Cheers!"]]},
  "bosnia": {"around": "Buses are the main way to get around, and they're cheap and fairly frequent, though some routes wind slowly through the mountains. The Sarajevo to Mostar train is the scenic highlight, short, cheap and spectacular along the Neretva. Uber and Bolt don't operate, so use metered taxis or ask your host to book one. In summer, buy bus tickets a day ahead for popular routes.", "money": "The currency is the convertible mark (BAM or KM), pegged to the euro, and euros are often accepted at a slightly rough rate. Cards work in cities, but buses, bakeries, small cafes and guesthouses want cash. ATMs are everywhere in towns. Rounding up or leaving around 10 percent in restaurants is normal, and haggling is only really a thing in tourist souvenir shops.", "sleep": "Hostels and family-run guesthouses in Sarajevo and Mostar are cheap, with dorms often around 12 to 20 euros. Private rooms (sobe) and apartments are good value elsewhere. For something special, stay in a village homestay in places like Lukomir, or at a riverside camp near Konjic or the Una River for rafting.", "eat": ["Ćevapi: small grilled sausages in flatbread with raw onion", "Burek: spiral pie filled with minced meat", "Bosnian coffee: strong coffee served in a small copper pot", "Tufahija: poached apple stuffed with walnuts and topped with cream"], "data": "BH Telecom, m:tel and HT Eronet SIMs are cheap at kiosks and shops with your passport. Coverage is good in towns but patchy in the mountains, and Bosnia isn't covered by EU roaming.", "heads_up": "Landmines remain in some rural areas, so stick to marked paths and paved roads when you're hiking.", "food": [{"name": "Ćevapi", "wiki": "Ćevapi", "desc": "Small grilled minced meat sausages stuffed into soft somun flatbread with raw onion and kajmak. A portion in a Sarajevo ćevabdžinica is cheap and essential.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Lunch or dinner, at ćevabdžinicas", "order": "Order a petica (five) or desetka (ten), with kajmak"}, {"name": "Burek", "wiki": "Börek", "desc": "A spiral filo pie, and in Bosnia it only counts as burek if it's filled with meat. Cheese, spinach or potato versions are called pita.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Breakfast, with drinking yoghurt", "order": "Sirnica is cheese and krompiruša is potato"}, {"name": "Bosnian coffee", "wiki": "Turkish coffee", "desc": "Strong coffee served in a small copper džezva with a sugar cube and Turkish delight. Sip it slowly, it's a social ritual.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Morning and afternoon, for hours", "order": "Dip the sugar cube, bite, then sip"}, {"name": "Begova čorba", "wiki": "Bey's soup", "desc": "The Bey's soup, a creamy chicken and okra soup that's a Sarajevo classic.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Starter at traditional restaurants"}, {"name": "Bosanski lonac", "wiki": "Bosnian pot", "desc": "Bosnian pot, a slow-cooked stew of meat and vegetables layered in a clay pot.", "price": "$6 to $10", "when": "Lunch, especially in winter"}, {"name": "Tufahija", "wiki": "Tufahije", "desc": "Poached apple stuffed with walnuts and topped with whipped cream, a simple and sweet Ottoman-era dessert.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Dessert with coffee"}], "food_tip": "Bakeries (pekara) sell burek and pita by weight for very little, and they're the classic cheap breakfast. Grill houses serving ćevapi are great value for a filling meal.", "culture": ["Coffee is never rushed. Expect to linger for an hour with your Bosnian coffee, and don't be in a hurry to leave.", "Bosnia is a mix of Bosniaks, Serbs and Croats, and the 1990s war is still recent memory. Don't ask about people's ethnicity or religion, and let locals bring up the war if they want to.", "Dress modestly when visiting mosques, Orthodox churches and Catholic churches, and women may need a scarf in mosques.", "Smoking is common in cafes and bars, so be prepared if you're sensitive to it.", "In Mostar, bridge divers collect money before jumping, so tip if you watch a dive."], "safety": "Bosnia is generally safe, with low crime against travellers. Watch for pickpockets in crowded spots like Baščaršija and on Sarajevo trams. Landmines still remain in some rural and hilly areas, so stick to marked trails and paved roads, and don't wander into abandoned buildings.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, eaten between noon and 3pm, and many locals have a light dinner.", "Tap water is safe in Sarajevo and most towns, and the public fountains in Sarajevo's old town are drinkable.", "Vegetarians can lean on zeljanica (spinach pie), sirnica, grah (bean stew) and stuffed peppers, but ask, as many stews use meat stock.", "Rounding up or leaving about 10 percent is normal in restaurants, and cafés appreciate small change.", "Pork is rare in Sarajevo's old town, which is largely Muslim, but alcohol is easy to find."], "drinks": [{"name": "Rakija", "desc": "Fruit brandy, often plum šljivovica, poured as a welcome drink. A shot costs around $1.50 to $3."}, {"name": "Boza", "desc": "A cold, sweet and slightly tangy drink made from corn flour, sold in summer for about $1."}], "greet": "Strangers shake hands, and friends kiss three times on the cheeks. Merhaba and selam are common greetings in Sarajevo alongside dobar dan.", "dress": "Casual city clothes are fine, but cover shoulders and knees in old towns and at religious sites. Women should carry a scarf for mosque visits.", "table": "Hosts insist on refills and seconds, so leave a bit on your plate or in your cup when you're done. A polite first refusal is expected to be overruled.", "faith": "About half of Bosnians are Muslim, with Orthodox Serbs and Catholic Croats making up most of the rest. Remove shoes in mosques, avoid Friday midday prayers, and be quiet and respectful at memorials such as Srebrenica-Potočari.", "phrases": [["Dobar dan", "Good day"], ["Hvala", "Thank you"], ["Koliko košta?", "How much does it cost?"], ["Merhaba", "Hello, common in Sarajevo"], ["Polako", "Slowly, take it easy"]]},
  "montenegro": {"around": "Buses connect the coast with Podgorica, Cetinje, Žabljak and the north, and they're cheap but can be slow and crowded in summer. The train from Bar to Podgorica and on to Bijelo Polje is cheap and scenic. Uber and Bolt don't operate, so use local taxi firms or agree on prices before you get in. In July and August, buy coastal bus tickets a day ahead.", "money": "Montenegro uses the euro even though it's not in the EU. Cards are fine in most coastal restaurants and shops, but carry cash for buses, small guesthouses, markets and the north. ATMs are easy to find in towns. Rounding up or leaving about 10 percent in restaurants is common, and haggling isn't really done except for private rooms in shoulder season.", "sleep": "Private rooms (sobe) and apartments are the budget choice on the coast, and Kotor, Budva and Herceg Novi have backpacker hostels with dorms often around 15 to 30 euros in summer. Žabljak in Durmitor has guesthouses and campsites, and camping is the cheapest way to see the mountains. The coast books out in high summer, so reserve ahead.", "eat": ["Njeguški pršut: dry-cured ham from the village of Njeguši", "Kačamak: rich cornmeal and potato porridge with cheese and kajmak", "Black risotto: rice cooked with squid ink and seafood", "Priganice: small fried dough balls with honey or cheese"], "data": "One, Crnogorski Telekom and m:tel sell cheap prepaid SIMs, and an eSIM is handy for short trips. Coverage is good on the coast and spotty in the mountains, and EU roaming doesn't cover Montenegro.", "heads_up": "Montenegro isn't in the EU, so check your roaming plan before you arrive, as EU roaming doesn't cover it.", "food": [{"name": "Njeguški pršut", "wiki": "Njeguški pršut", "desc": "Smoky dry-cured ham from the mountain village of Njeguši, often served with local cheese on the road between Kotor and Cetinje.", "price": "$6 to $12 a plate", "when": "Starter or snack with wine", "order": "Pair it with Njeguški cheese"}, {"name": "Kačamak", "wiki": "Kačamak", "desc": "A thick, heavy porridge of cornmeal and potato with cheese and kajmak, a mountain staple in the north.", "price": "$5 to $8", "when": "Lunch in the mountains"}, {"name": "Burek", "wiki": "Börek", "desc": "Flaky pastry filled with cheese or meat, sold by weight at every bakery and best with a glass of drinking yoghurt for breakfast.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Breakfast with yoghurt", "order": "Order sa sirom for cheese, sa mesom for meat"}, {"name": "Priganice", "wiki": "Uštipci", "desc": "Fried dough balls served with honey, cheese or jam at breakfast or with coffee. You'll also see them called uštipci.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Breakfast or with coffee", "order": "Get them with honey and cheese"}, {"name": "Vranac", "wiki": "Vranac", "desc": "A deep red local wine, cheap and good. Try it in a konoba (tavern) along the coast.", "price": "$3 to $5 a glass", "when": "Evenings at a konoba"}], "food_tip": "Bakeries sell burek and pastries cheaply, and supermarkets are much cheaper than the waterfront restaurants. Eat a little inland or away from the old towns and you'll pay a lot less for the same food.", "culture": ["Montenegrins are famously laid back. 'Polako' (slowly) is practically a national motto, so don't expect service to rush.", "Dress modestly at Orthodox monasteries like Ostrog, covering shoulders and knees, and women may be asked to wear a skirt or scarf.", "National and religious identity, especially the relationship with Serbia, can be a sensitive topic, so let locals lead.", "Coffee culture is big, and people linger for hours in cafes. Smoking indoors is still common in some places.", "Topless sunbathing is fine on some beaches, but walking around towns in swimwear is frowned upon and can get you fined in some places."], "safety": "Montenegro is pretty safe, with petty theft in tourist hotspots the main concern. Mountain roads are narrow and twisty, so take care if you rent a car or scooter, and check weather before hiking in Durmitor or Prokletije. Taxi drivers may overcharge, so agree on the fare first.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal from about 2 to 4pm, and dinner is late and light.", "Tap water is generally safe on the coast and in towns, though some people prefer bottled.", "Vegetarian food is limited to salads, pastries, cheese and grilled vegetables, but you won't go hungry.", "Tip about 10 percent in restaurants or round up, as it's appreciated but not compulsory."], "drinks": [{"name": "Rakija", "desc": "Strong fruit brandy, usually grape (loza) or plum, offered as a welcome. A shot costs $2 to $3."}, {"name": "Turkish coffee", "desc": "Thick coffee boiled in a small pot, sipped slowly for hours. A cup is about $1.50 to $2."}], "greet": "A handshake with eye contact works for anyone, and friends kiss three times on the cheeks. Say dobar dan for good day.", "dress": "Beach clothes stay at the beach, and towns can fine you for walking around in swimwear. Cover shoulders and knees in monasteries.", "table": "Meals are slow and social, and hosts will keep refilling your plate. Look people in the eye when toasting with živjeli.", "faith": "Most Montenegrins are Orthodox Christian, with Muslim and Catholic minorities. Monasteries expect modest dress and silence, and photos may be banned inside.", "phrases": [["Dobar dan", "Good day"], ["Hvala", "Thank you"], ["Koliko košta?", "How much does it cost?"], ["Živjeli!", "Cheers!"], ["Polako", "Slowly, take it easy"]]},
  "north-macedonia": {"around": "Buses link Skopje with Ohrid, Bitola, Struga and most towns, and minibuses cover smaller routes. Trains are slow and limited but cheap if you're not in a hurry. Taxis in Skopje are cheap and metered, though ride apps aren't widespread. Buses to Ohrid fill up on summer weekends, so buy a seat a day ahead.", "money": "The currency is the denar (MKD). Carry cash for buses, bakeries, markets and small guesthouses, though cards work in many restaurants and shops in Skopje and Ohrid. ATMs are easy to find in towns. Rounding up or leaving around 10 percent in restaurants is normal, and light haggling is fine at the Old Bazaar.", "sleep": "Hostels in Skopje and Ohrid are cheap and friendly, with dorms often around 10 to 15 euros. Around Lake Ohrid, private rooms and guesthouses are everywhere, and there are campsites along the lake. Book ahead in Ohrid during July and August.", "eat": ["Tavče gravče: baked white beans in a clay pot", "Ajvar: roasted red pepper spread, often homemade", "Shopska salad: tomatoes, cucumber and onion under grated white cheese", "Pastrmajlija: oval bread topped with diced meat and sometimes egg"], "data": "A1 and Telekom SIMs are cheap and quick to buy with your passport, and coverage is good in towns. North Macedonia isn't in EU roaming, so check your plan.", "heads_up": "As in Albania, a nod can sometimes mean no and a head shake yes, so confirm with words.", "food": [{"name": "Tavče gravče", "wiki": "Tavče gravče", "desc": "Baked white beans in a clay pot with paprika and onions, the national dish and a cheap filling meal.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Lunch, often Friday", "order": "Best in a clay pot from Tetovo"}, {"name": "Ajvar", "wiki": "Ajvar", "desc": "A spread of roasted red peppers, often homemade in autumn, eaten with bread and cheese.", "price": "$2 to $4 a plate", "when": "With bread and cheese at any meal", "order": "Homemade ajvar appears in autumn"}, {"name": "Shopska salad", "wiki": "Shopska salad", "desc": "Tomatoes, cucumbers, onions and peppers under a pile of grated white cheese. It comes with nearly every meal.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Starter with rakija", "order": "Ask for it with sirenje, white cheese"}, {"name": "Pastrmajlija", "wiki": "Pastrmajlija", "desc": "Oval bread topped with diced meat and sometimes egg, a speciality of Štip.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Lunch, especially in Štip", "order": "Add an egg on top"}, {"name": "Burek", "wiki": "Börek", "desc": "Flaky filo pastry with cheese, meat or spinach, sold in bakeries and best with yoghurt for breakfast.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast with yoghurt", "order": "Choose cheese, meat or spinach"}, {"name": "Rakija", "wiki": "Rakia", "desc": "Strong fruit brandy, often homemade, offered as a welcome drink or toast.", "price": "$1 to $3 a shot", "when": "Toasts at meals and celebrations", "order": "Homemade grape rakija is the strongest"}], "food_tip": "Bakeries (pekara) and grill stalls are cheap, and local restaurants (kafana) serve big portions. Markets in Skopje are great for cheap fresh produce.", "culture": ["When toasting with rakija, make eye contact and say 'Na zdravje!', and don't skip the toast.", "The country's name and history are sensitive, and the term 'FYROM' is outdated and unwelcome. Avoid debating Macedonian identity with Greece or Bulgaria.", "Dress modestly at Orthodox churches and mosques, and women may need to cover their heads in some places.", "Coffee is a slow, social affair, so don't expect to rush.", "Some people, especially in Albanian communities, use a nod for no and a head shake for yes, so confirm with words if unsure."], "safety": "North Macedonia is safe, with little crime against travellers. Watch your bag in busy bus stations and the Old Bazaar, and use metered taxis. Air pollution in Skopje is bad in winter, so if you're sensitive, plan accordingly.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is often burek and yoghurt, lunch around 2pm and dinner after 8pm.", "Tap water is safe in Skopje, Ohrid and most towns.", "Vegetarians do well with tavče gravče, salads and ajvar, and Orthodox fasting periods bring more meat-free dishes.", "Skopje's Old Bazaar is full of kebapi grills selling ten small sausages with bread and onions for a few dollars.", "Leaving 10% in restaurants is common and appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Macedonian wine", "desc": "Vranec, a deep red grape from the Tikveš region, is the one to try. A glass costs around $2 to $3."}, {"name": "Boza", "desc": "A thick, slightly fermented malt drink sold in sweet shops in the Old Bazaar. About $0.50 to $1."}], "greet": "Shake hands with direct eye contact, and friends kiss three times on the cheeks. Say \"zdravo\" casually or \"dobar den\" to be polite.", "dress": "Dress is casual and similar to Western Europe. Cover shoulders and knees at monasteries like Sveti Naum, and women should carry a scarf for mosques.", "table": "Hosts will push food and rakija on you, and finishing your plate invites more. Toast before drinking and keep eye contact.", "faith": "About two thirds of people are Macedonian Orthodox and a third Muslim, mostly ethnic Albanians. In churches keep quiet and don't sit with crossed legs, and at mosques remove shoes and avoid prayer times.", "phrases": [["Zdravo (Здраво)", "Hello"], ["Blagodaram (Благодарам)", "Thank you"], ["Kolku chini? (Колку чини?)", "How much?"], ["Na zdravje (На здравје)", "Cheers"], ["Prijatno (Пријатно)", "Enjoy your meal, or goodbye"]]},
  "romania": {"around": "CFR trains are slow but cheap and cover most of the country, and you can book online or in the app. Maxi-taxis (minibuses) and buses fill the gaps, often faster than the train on shorter routes. Bolt and Uber work in Bucharest, Cluj, Brașov and other big cities, and they're much safer than random street taxis. Book sleeper trains to Maramureș or the Danube Delta ahead in summer.", "money": "The currency is the leu (RON), and Romania isn't on the euro yet. Cards and contactless are accepted almost everywhere in cities, but carry some cash for villages, markets and small guesthouses. Skip the standalone Euronet ATMs and always choose to be charged in lei when a card machine or ATM offers a conversion. Tipping around 10 percent in restaurants is normal, and haggling isn't really done outside markets.", "sleep": "Hostels are common in Bucharest, Brașov, Sibiu and Cluj, with dorms often around 12 to 20 euros. In the countryside, pensiune (family guesthouses) are cheap and often serve big homemade breakfasts. Camping is possible in the Carpathians, but read up on bears first.", "eat": ["Sarmale: cabbage rolls stuffed with pork and rice", "Mici: grilled skinless sausages with mustard", "Ciorbă de burtă: sour tripe soup with sour cream and garlic", "Papanași: fried cheese doughnuts with sour cream and jam"], "data": "Orange, Vodafone and Digi SIMs are cheap and easy to buy, and Romania is covered by EU roaming. Coverage is good across most of the country, and wifi is fast and everywhere.", "heads_up": "Stray and shepherd dogs are common in rural areas and on mountain trails, so stay calm, keep your distance and don't run.", "food": [{"name": "Sarmale", "wiki": "Stuffed leaves", "desc": "Cabbage leaves stuffed with minced pork and rice, slow-cooked and served with polenta and sour cream. The ultimate Romanian comfort food.", "price": "$6 to $10", "when": "Christmas, weddings and Sunday lunch", "order": "Served with mămăligă and sour cream"}, {"name": "Mici", "wiki": "Mititei", "desc": "Small grilled skinless sausages of mixed minced meat, served with mustard and bread. Cheap, smoky and found at every grill and festival.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Picnics, May Day and markets", "order": "Eat with mustard; they're often priced per piece"}, {"name": "Ciorbă de burtă", "wiki": "Tripe soup", "desc": "Sour tripe soup with sour cream, garlic and vinegar. Way better than it sounds, and a legendary hangover cure.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Lunch, and as a hangover cure", "order": "Stir in the garlic sauce and bite the fresh chilli"}, {"name": "Mămăligă", "wiki": "Mămăligă", "desc": "Cornmeal porridge, Romania's version of polenta, served with cheese, sour cream or as a side to stews.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Side dish, especially in villages", "order": "Try it with brânză cheese and sour cream"}, {"name": "Papanași", "wiki": "Papanași", "desc": "Fried cheese doughnuts topped with sour cream and jam, the dessert everyone orders.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Dessert after a hearty meal", "order": "Comes with sour cream and jam; often big enough to share"}, {"name": "Cozonac", "wiki": "Cozonac", "desc": "Sweet bread swirled with walnuts or cocoa, baked for Easter and Christmas but sold in bakeries year round.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Easter and Christmas"}], "food_tip": "Covrigi (pretzel-like bread rings) and bakery pastries make a dirt cheap breakfast, and many restaurants offer a 'meniul zilei' (lunch menu) on weekdays. Markets are great for cheap fruit, cheese and bread.", "culture": ["Dress modestly at Orthodox churches and monasteries, especially the painted monasteries of Bucovina. Women may be asked to cover their heads or wear a skirt, and men should take off their hats.", "Give flowers in odd numbers, since even numbers are for funerals.", "Romanians are proud of their Latin heritage and language, and confusing Romanians with Roma people is a common and unwelcome mistake.", "Dracula tourism is fun, but locals see Vlad the Impaler as a national hero rather than a vampire, so keep the jokes light.", "Greet people with 'Bună ziua' (good day), and men often shake hands firmly."], "safety": "Romania is generally safe, though pickpockets work in Bucharest's Gara de Nord and on crowded transport, and street taxis can overcharge. Brown bears are common in the Carpathians and even wander into towns like Brașov, so stick to marked trails, make noise, never leave food outside and don't approach them. Stray and shepherd dogs can be a nuisance on rural trails.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch, between about 1 and 3pm, is the main meal, and dinner usually starts from 7pm.", "Tap water is safe in cities, though many locals prefer bottled.", "Tip around 10 percent in restaurants, ideally in cash.", "Vegetarians can lean on Orthodox fasting food labelled 'de post', which is vegan and easy to find before Easter and Christmas.", "Soup (ciorbă) starts most lunches and usually comes with sour cream and a fresh chilli on the side."], "drinks": [{"name": "Țuică", "desc": "Strong plum brandy, offered as a welcome in homes and called pălincă in the north. A shot costs $1 to $3 in bars."}, {"name": "Socată", "desc": "A lightly fizzy elderflower drink, homemade in early summer. Around $1 to $2 a glass where it's sold."}], "greet": "Friends greet with a kiss on each cheek, and some older men still kiss a woman's hand. Use the formal 'dumneavoastră' with elders.", "dress": "Romanians tend to dress neatly in cities, and winters are cold, so bring layers. Carry a scarf for monasteries, where shorts aren't allowed.", "table": "Wait for the host to say 'Poftă bună' before eating, and expect to be offered seconds and thirds. Say 'Noroc' when toasting and look people in the eye.", "faith": "Most Romanians are Orthodox Christian, with Catholic and Protestant communities in Transylvania. Many people cross themselves passing a church; inside, keep quiet and expect to stand, as Orthodox churches have few seats.", "phrases": [["Bună ziua", "Good day"], ["Mulțumesc", "Thank you"], ["Cât costă?", "How much is it?"], ["Noroc!", "Cheers!"], ["Poftă bună", "Enjoy your meal"]]},
  "bulgaria": {"around": "Buses are usually faster and more frequent than the slow, cheap trains, and they link most towns. Trains are scenic and cheap, especially routes through the mountains. Use metered taxis from known companies or ride apps in Sofia, and check the price sticker on the window. Buses on popular routes rarely sell out, but buy a seat a day ahead in summer.", "money": "Bulgaria switched to the euro on 1 January 2026, so you'll see prices in euros, with some shops still showing the old lev for reference. Cards work in cities and bigger shops, but carry some cash for small shops, markets and village guesthouses. ATMs are easy to find. Tipping around 10 percent in restaurants is normal, and haggling is rare outside markets.", "sleep": "Hostels in Sofia, Plovdiv and Veliko Tarnovo are cheap, friendly and often include breakfast, with dorms often around 12 to 18 euros. Guesthouses are common in villages and mountain towns like Bansko and Koprivshtitsa, and mountain huts are a great budget option for hikers. Book ahead on the Black Sea coast in summer.", "eat": ["Banitsa: flaky filo pastry with egg and white cheese", "Shopska salad: tomatoes, cucumbers and peppers topped with grated sirene cheese", "Tarator: cold yogurt and cucumber soup with dill", "Kebapche: grilled spiced minced meat sausage"], "data": "A1, Yettel and Vivacom SIMs are cheap, need ID registration in store, and EU roaming covers Bulgaria. Coverage is good in towns and along major roads.", "heads_up": "Some Sofia taxis charge silly rates, so check the price sticker on the window before you get in.", "food": [{"name": "Banitsa", "wiki": "Banitsa", "desc": "Flaky filo pastry layered with egg and white cheese, the classic breakfast with a glass of ayran or boza.", "price": "$1 to $2.50", "when": "Breakfast, from bakeries", "order": "Pair it with ayran or boza"}, {"name": "Shopska salad", "wiki": "Shopska salad", "desc": "Tomatoes, cucumbers, peppers and onion under grated sirene cheese. The national salad, eaten with rakia.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Starter at dinner, with rakia"}, {"name": "Lukanka", "wiki": "Lukanka", "desc": "A flat, dry-cured salami spiced with cumin and pepper, sliced thin with bread, cheese and a glass of wine or rakia.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Snack platter with drinks", "order": "Karlovo lukanka is the famous one"}, {"name": "Kebapche", "wiki": "Kebapche", "desc": "Grilled spiced minced meat sausages, served with chips and salad at every mehana (tavern).", "price": "$1 to $2 each", "when": "Dinner, at mehanas and grills", "order": "Order with \"garnitura\" for chips and salad"}, {"name": "Lyutenitsa", "wiki": "Lyutenitsa", "desc": "A spread of roasted peppers and tomatoes, eaten on bread for a cheap snack.", "price": "$2 to $4 a jar", "when": "Snack or side, any time", "order": "Spread it on bread with sirene cheese"}, {"name": "Boza", "wiki": "Boza", "desc": "A thick, slightly sour fermented grain drink, an acquired taste traditionally drunk with banitsa.", "price": "$0.70 to $1.50", "when": "Breakfast, with banitsa"}], "food_tip": "Bakeries selling banitsa and supermarkets keep breakfast cheap, and mehanas serve hearty meals at good prices away from the main squares. Lunch menus in cities are good value.", "culture": ["Bulgarians often nod for no and shake their heads for yes, the opposite of most countries. Confirm with words to avoid confusion.", "Toast with rakia by making eye contact and saying 'Nazdrave!'", "Dress modestly in Orthodox churches and monasteries like Rila, covering shoulders and knees.", "On 1 March, people exchange martenitsi, red and white bracelets, and wear them until they see a stork or blossoming tree.", "Name days are a big deal, often celebrated more than birthdays, so if it's someone's name day, wish them well."], "safety": "Bulgaria is generally safe, but some Sofia taxis charge crazy rates, so check the price sticker or use an app. Watch out for pickpockets in busy areas and on the Black Sea coast in summer, and be wary of overpriced drinks in some Sunny Beach clubs. Mountain weather can change quickly, so check conditions before hiking.", "eat_beats": ["Bulgarians eat lunch around 12 to 2pm and dinner after 7pm, often slowly with salad and rakia first.", "Tap water is safe to drink in most of the country, and mountain springs (cheshma) are everywhere.", "Vegetarians do well with salads, bean soups, stuffed peppers and banitsa.", "Bulgaria adopted the euro in January 2026, so prices are now in euros.", "Tipping 10 percent at restaurants is normal, and you hand it to the waiter rather than leaving it on the table."], "drinks": [{"name": "Rakia", "desc": "Fruit brandy, usually grape or plum, sipped slowly with salad before the main course. A glass costs $2 to $4 in a mehana."}, {"name": "Ayran", "desc": "A cold, salty yoghurt drink that goes with banitsa and grilled meat, about $1."}], "greet": "A firm handshake with eye contact is standard, and friends kiss on both cheeks. Remember the head nod for \"no\" and shake for \"yes\".", "dress": "Casual clothes are fine in cities, but cover shoulders and knees in Orthodox churches and monasteries.", "table": "Wait for the host's toast before drinking, and look people in the eye as you clink. Rakia is sipped slowly, never downed.", "faith": "Most Bulgarians are Eastern Orthodox Christians, with a Muslim minority. In churches keep quiet, light a candle if you like, and don't turn your back on the altar when photographing.", "phrases": [["Zdravei (Здравей)", "Hello"], ["Blagodarya (Благодаря)", "Thank you"], ["Kolko struva? (Колко струва?)", "How much does it cost?"], ["Nazdrave (Наздраве)", "Cheers"], ["Smetkata, molya (Сметката, моля)", "The bill, please"]]},
  "turkey": {"around": "Overnight intercity buses go almost everywhere, with seat-back screens and tea service, and you can compare companies on the Obilet app. YHT high-speed trains link Istanbul, Ankara, Konya and Eskişehir, and cheap domestic flights on Pegasus and AJet are worth it for long jumps east. In Istanbul, use an Istanbulkart for metro, trams, buses and ferries, and BiTaksi or Uber for taxis.", "money": "The currency is the Turkish lira (TRY), and with high inflation, prices change fast, so expect menus to be updated often. Cards are accepted almost everywhere, but carry some lira for dolmuş minibuses, small shops and markets. Use ATMs of big Turkish banks like Ziraat or İş Bankası rather than standalone Euronet machines, and always pay in lira. Haggling is expected in bazaars, and a tip of 5 to 10 percent is customary in restaurants.", "sleep": "Hostels with dorms are easy to find in Istanbul, Cappadocia and along the coast, with Istanbul dorms often around 12 to 25 euros. Family-run pensions are the classic cheap stay elsewhere, often with a big Turkish breakfast. In Cappadocia, cave rooms in Göreme can be surprisingly affordable outside peak season.", "eat": ["Lahmacun: thin flatbread with spiced minced meat, rolled with herbs", "Simit: sesame-crusted bread ring sold from street carts", "Menemen: eggs scrambled with tomatoes and peppers", "Çay: strong black tea served in small tulip glasses"], "data": "An eSIM is the easiest choice, since tourist SIMs from Turkcell, Vodafone or Türk Telekom need passport registration in an official store and are pricey. Foreign phones on a local SIM must be registered (IMEI) after 120 days, and coverage is good in towns and along main roads.", "heads_up": "In Istanbul, be wary of friendly strangers who suggest a drink at a bar they know, as this often ends with a huge bill.", "food": [{"name": "Döner kebab", "wiki": "Doner kebab", "desc": "Meat roasted on a vertical spit, sliced into bread or a wrap. A filling, cheap street meal everywhere.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Any time, often late night", "order": "Ask for it dürüm, wrapped in thin lavash"}, {"name": "Lahmacun", "wiki": "Lahmacun", "desc": "Thin flatbread topped with spiced minced meat, rolled up with parsley, onion and a squeeze of lemon.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Quick lunch, rolled up with parsley", "order": "Squeeze lemon over and roll it"}, {"name": "Simit", "wiki": "Simit", "desc": "A sesame-crusted bread ring sold from street carts for a few lira, the classic breakfast on the go.", "price": "$0.40 to $1", "when": "Morning, from street carts", "order": "Have it with çay and white cheese"}, {"name": "Menemen", "wiki": "Menemen (food)", "desc": "Eggs scrambled with tomatoes, peppers and spices, a cheap and hearty breakfast.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Breakfast cafés", "order": "Choose with or without onions, a fierce debate"}, {"name": "Balık ekmek", "wiki": "Balık ekmek", "desc": "Grilled fish in bread with onions and salad, sold by the boats in Eminönü, Istanbul.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "By the Galata Bridge in Istanbul", "order": "Add pickle juice, sold at nearby stands"}, {"name": "Çay", "wiki": "Tea in Turkey", "desc": "Strong black tea served in small tulip glasses, offered everywhere from shops to buses.", "price": "$0.30 to $1 a glass", "when": "All day, every day", "order": "Say açık for weak, demli for strong"}], "food_tip": "Lokantas (canteens) serve home-style stews and veggie dishes at low prices, and pide and kebab shops are cheap and filling. Avoid eating right next to big sights in Istanbul, where prices jump.", "culture": ["Take your shoes off before entering mosques, and women should cover their heads, shoulders and knees. Avoid visiting during prayer times.", "Tea is offered everywhere, from shops to homes, and accepting a glass is polite. You don't have to buy anything after.", "Insulting Atatürk or the Turkish flag is illegal and deeply offensive.", "During Ramadan, be discreet about eating and drinking in public in conservative areas.", "Public displays of affection are frowned upon outside big cities and tourist areas.", "Haggling is part of the fun in bazaars, so start low and keep it friendly."], "safety": "Turkey is generally safe for travellers, but watch out for scams in Istanbul, like the friendly stranger who suggests a drink at a bar, ending with a massive bill, or shoe shiners who 'accidentally' drop a brush. Pickpockets work in crowded areas like the Grand Bazaar and on trams. Many governments advise against travel near the Syrian border, so check current advice for the far southeast.", "eat_beats": ["Weekend breakfast, called kahvaltı, is a big spread of cheese, olives, eggs and jams that can last two hours.", "Tap water is chlorinated and safe in most cities, but locals drink bottled or filtered water, which is cheap.", "Vegetarians do well with zeytinyağlı (vegetables in olive oil), mercimek soup and gözleme, but check for meat stock.", "At lokantas you point at the trays in the window, and staff bring the plates to your table.", "Leave 5% to 10% in restaurants, often in cash, as card tips don't always reach staff."], "drinks": [{"name": "Ayran", "desc": "A salty yoghurt drink that goes perfectly with kebabs and pide. About $0.50 to $1."}, {"name": "Rakı", "desc": "An aniseed spirit diluted with water until it turns milky, sipped slowly with meze and fish. A glass costs around $5 to $8 in a meyhane."}], "greet": "Shake hands on meeting, and close friends kiss on both cheeks. Kissing an elder's hand then raising it to your forehead is a sign of respect during holidays.", "dress": "Anything goes in Istanbul and the resorts, but dress more modestly in the east and in rural areas. At mosques, cover shoulders and knees, and women need a headscarf, which is lent at big mosques.", "table": "Hosts will keep refilling your plate and tea glass, so leave a little food if you're full. Don't blow your nose at the table.", "faith": "Most Turks are Sunni Muslim, with a large Alevi minority, though the state is secular. At mosques take off your shoes, stay quiet, don't walk in front of people praying and avoid the five daily prayer times.", "phrases": [["Merhaba", "Hello"], ["Teşekkür ederim", "Thank you"], ["Ne kadar?", "How much?"], ["Acısız olsun", "Not spicy, please"], ["Afiyet olsun", "Enjoy your meal"]]},
  "ecuador": {"around": "Buses are cheap and frequent between Quito, Latacunga, Baños and Cuenca, and the Quilotoa Loop is easy by local bus plus hiking. Uber, DiDi and inDrive work in Quito, Guayaquil and Cuenca, and they're safer than hailing taxis on the street. Little needs booking ahead except Galápagos trips and flights there.", "money": "Ecuador uses the US dollar, so there's no exchange to worry about. Bring small bills, since $50 and $100 notes are often refused, and carry cash for buses, markets and small restaurants. ATMs are easy to find in cities but charge fees, and Banco Pichincha and Banco de Guayaquil are reliable. Restaurants may add a 10 percent service charge, and haggling is normal at the Otavalo market.", "sleep": "Hostel dorms and family-run hostales are cheap everywhere, with dorms often around 8 to 15 US dollars. In Quilotoa Loop villages like Isinliví and Chugchilán, hostels often include dinner and breakfast. Baños and Montañita are backpacker hubs with lots of options.", "eat": ["Locro de papa: creamy potato and cheese soup with avocado", "Encebollado: tuna and cassava soup with pickled onions, a coastal favorite", "Llapingacho: fried potato and cheese patties with egg and sausage", "Canelazo: hot cinnamon and fruit drink, often with a splash of liquor"], "data": "Claro and Movistar SIMs are sold with a passport and work well in cities, though coverage drops on remote stretches like the Quilotoa Loop. CNT is the state network.", "heads_up": "On buses, keep your bag on your lap rather than under the seat or overhead, where thefts are common.", "food": [{"name": "Encebollado", "wiki": "Encebollado", "desc": "Tuna and cassava soup with pickled onions, a coastal breakfast and hangover cure.", "price": "$2.50 to $4", "when": "Breakfast on the coast, weekends", "order": "Add lime, chili and chifles (plantain chips)"}, {"name": "Llapingacho", "wiki": "Llapingacho", "desc": "Fried potato and cheese patties with egg, sausage and peanut sauce, a highland favourite.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Lunch in Ambato and Riobamba"}, {"name": "Hornado", "wiki": "Hornado", "desc": "Whole roasted pork served with mote (hominy), potato patties and salad, found at markets in the highlands.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Weekend market lunch in the Sierra", "order": "Ask for the crispy skin, called cuero"}, {"name": "Ceviche", "wiki": "Ceviche", "desc": "Ecuadorian ceviche is soupier than Peru's, often with shrimp, and served with popcorn or plantain chips.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Lunch on the coast", "order": "Ceviche de camarón with popcorn is the classic"}, {"name": "Fanesca", "wiki": "Fanesca", "desc": "A rich soup of grains, beans and salt cod, eaten only during Holy Week.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Easter week only, at home or cafes", "order": "Ask for a small bowl, it's very rich"}, {"name": "Canelazo", "wiki": "Canelazo", "desc": "Hot cinnamon and fruit drink, often spiked with local liquor, perfect for cold Andean nights.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Cold evenings and festivals", "order": "Sin trago means without the alcohol"}], "food_tip": "Set lunches called almuerzos (soup, main and juice) cost a few dollars at local restaurants. Markets are the cheapest places to eat and try local dishes.", "culture": ["Greetings are warm, and women often greet with a kiss on the right cheek. Say 'buenos días' when entering shops.", "Ask before photographing indigenous people, especially at markets, and offer a small tip if they agree.", "Ecuador is mostly Catholic, so dress modestly in churches.", "Bargaining at markets is fine, but keep it friendly and fair.", "Punctuality is relaxed, so 'ahorita' can mean now, later or never."], "safety": "Since 2024, Ecuador has seen a sharp rise in gang violence, particularly in Guayaquil, Esmeraldas and parts of the coast, so check current advice and be extra careful in those areas. The highlands, including Quito, Cuenca and Baños, are generally safer, but use apps rather than street taxis and avoid walking at night. Altitude in Quito (2,850 metres) can hit hard, so take it easy at first.", "eat_beats": ["Almuerzo is served from about noon to 2pm, and the cheapest set meals sell out quickly.", "Market food courts have rows of stalls cooking the same dish; sit at whichever one has the most locals.", "Don't drink tap water, although Quito's is officially treated; most hostels have refill jugs.", "Vegetarian options exist in tourist towns, but in local places ask for sin carne and expect rice, beans, eggs and plantain.", "Restaurants usually add 10% service plus tax to the bill, so extra tipping isn't needed."], "drinks": [{"name": "Jugo de naranjilla", "desc": "Juice from a sour green-orange Andean fruit, tangy and refreshing. About $1 to $2 at markets and with almuerzos."}, {"name": "Colada morada", "desc": "A thick, warm purple drink of black corn and fruit, made around the Day of the Dead on 2 November. Usually served with a guagua de pan bread for $1 to $3."}], "greet": "Shake hands and say buenos días or buenas tardes, and friends kiss once on the right cheek. Use usted with older people and strangers.", "dress": "Bring warm layers for the Andes, where it's chilly at night, and lighter clothes for the coast and Amazon. Cover up for churches and Indigenous communities.", "table": "Wait for the host to say buen provecho before eating, and keep your hands on the table. Locals often eat soup first and the main after, even at breakfast.", "faith": "Most Ecuadorians are Roman Catholic, and Indigenous communities blend Catholic and Andean beliefs. Stay quiet in churches, don't use flash, and treat festivals like Inti Raymi as sacred, not just a party.", "phrases": [["Buenos días", "Good morning"], ["¿Cuánto vale?", "How much is it?"], ["Chévere", "Cool, great"], ["Achachay", "It's cold! (from Kichwa)"], ["Dios le pague", "God repay you, a heartfelt thanks"]]},
  "nicaragua": {"around": "Chicken buses and minivans are super cheap between León, Granada, Managua and the ferry port at San Jorge, while tourist shuttles cost more but save time and hassle. Ferries cross from San Jorge to Ometepe several times a day, and renting a scooter is a popular way to explore the island. Book La Costeña flights to the Corn Islands ahead, since the small planes fill up. Check current entry rules before you go, as Nicaragua requires advance registration and can refuse entry without much explanation.", "money": "The currency is the córdoba (NIO), but US dollars are widely accepted for hostels, shuttles and tours, so carry a mix. Cards work in bigger hotels and restaurants in Granada, León and San Juan del Sur, but cash is king on buses, at markets and on Ometepe. ATMs are in towns (BAC and Banpro are reliable), but fees can be steep. Restaurants often add a 10 percent service charge, and haggling is normal at markets.", "sleep": "León and Granada have plenty of hostel dorms with pools, often around 8 to 15 US dollars. Ometepe has cheap guesthouses and farm stays, and the Corn Islands have simple beach cabins. San Juan del Sur and the surf beaches nearby have lots of surf camps and party hostels.", "eat": ["Gallo pinto: rice and red beans fried together, eaten any time", "Vigorón: cassava topped with pork crackling and cabbage salad", "Nacatamal: large tamale with pork and rice wrapped in banana leaf", "Quesillo: soft cheese in a tortilla with pickled onion and cream"], "data": "Claro and Tigo SIMs are cheap and easy to get with a passport, and coverage is decent in towns but patchy on Ometepe's far side and Little Corn Island.", "heads_up": "Don't photograph police, military or political events, and carry a copy of your passport.", "food": [{"name": "Gallo pinto", "wiki": "Gallo pinto", "desc": "Rice and red beans fried together, eaten any time of day with eggs, cheese or plantain.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Breakfast with eggs, or any meal", "order": "Ask for queso frito and maduro on the side"}, {"name": "Vigorón", "wiki": "Vigorón", "desc": "Boiled cassava topped with pork crackling and tangy cabbage salad, served on a banana leaf. Granada's signature snack.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch or snack, especially in Granada", "order": "Granada's central park stalls are famous for it"}, {"name": "Nacatamal", "wiki": "Nacatamal", "desc": "A big tamale with pork, rice and vegetables wrapped in banana leaf, traditionally eaten on weekend mornings.", "price": "$2 to $3", "when": "Weekend breakfast, often Saturday or Sunday", "order": "Eat it with bread and coffee, like locals"}, {"name": "Quesillo", "wiki": "Quesillo", "desc": "Soft cheese in a warm tortilla with pickled onion and cream, a cheap street snack from the roadside stands around Nagarote.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Snack in Nagarote and La Paz Centro"}, {"name": "Indio viejo", "wiki": "Indio viejo", "desc": "A thick stew of shredded beef and corn dough flavoured with sour orange and mint.", "price": "$4 to $6", "when": "Lunch, at comedores"}, {"name": "Rondón", "wiki": "Run down", "desc": "A Caribbean coast seafood and coconut stew with plantain and cassava, best on the Corn Islands.", "price": "$7 to $12", "when": "Lunch on the Caribbean coast", "order": "Ask what fish came in that day"}], "food_tip": "Comedores (local eateries) and fritangas (street grills) serve cheap, filling meals of meat, rice, beans and plantain. Markets are great for fresh fruit and juice, and set lunches are good value.", "culture": ["People often say 'adiós' when passing someone on the street, as a quick hello rather than goodbye.", "Pointing with your lips is common, so don't be surprised when someone purses their lips at you to show directions.", "Nicaragua is mostly Catholic, so dress modestly in churches, and festivals for patron saints are big, loud and fun.", "Politics is a sensitive and risky topic, so avoid discussing the government, criticising it online or joining protests.", "Greet people with 'buenos días' or 'buenas' when entering shops, and use 'con permiso' when squeezing past."], "safety": "Nicaragua is generally safe for travellers, but petty theft and bag snatching happen, especially in Managua and at bus stations. Avoid photographing police, military or political events, steer clear of protests, and don't bring a drone, as they're banned. Rip currents are strong on Pacific beaches, so swim with care.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is early, and lunch from noon is the main meal; many places close early at night outside cities.", "Fritangas fire up in the late afternoon, and you choose meats and sides from the grill, then pay by item.", "Don't drink tap water; buy bags or big jugs of purified water to save money and plastic.", "Vegetarians can eat well on gallo pinto, cheese, eggs, plantains and fresh fruit, though beans may be fried in lard.", "Restaurants often add a 10% propina voluntaria to the bill, which you can decline, and small places don't expect tips."], "drinks": [{"name": "Pinolillo", "desc": "A traditional drink of toasted cornmeal and cacao mixed with water or milk. A cup costs about $0.50 to $1.50."}, {"name": "Macuá", "desc": "Nicaragua's national cocktail of white rum, guava juice and lemon. Expect $3 to $5 in bars."}], "greet": "Shake hands and say buenos días, buenas tardes or just buenas. Women often greet with a kiss on the cheek.", "dress": "It's hot, so wear light clothes, but avoid beachwear in towns. Dress neatly for churches and government offices.", "table": "Say buen provecho when you start eating or pass people eating. Meals are casual, and it's fine to use tortillas to scoop food.", "faith": "Nicaragua is mostly Catholic, with a growing Evangelical population. At churches, cover up, keep quiet and don't photograph people praying.", "phrases": [["Buenas", "Hi (any time of day)"], ["¿Cuánto vale?", "How much is it?"], ["Tuani", "Cool, nice"], ["Dale pues", "OK then, sure"], ["Chele / chela", "Light-skinned person, what you might be called"]]},
  "honduras": {"around": "Most backpackers stick to the Copán Ruinas, Lake Yojoa, La Ceiba and Utila circuit. Hedman Alas runs comfortable, secure first-class buses on the main routes, and tourist shuttles connect Copán with Antigua in Guatemala and with Lake Yojoa. The Utila Princess ferry runs daily from La Ceiba to Utila, and Uber works in Tegucigalpa and San Pedro Sula. Travel by day where you can and choose direct first-class buses over local chicken buses in the big cities.", "money": "The currency is the lempira (HNL). Carry cash for buses, street food and small guesthouses, since cards are mostly taken at hotels, dive shops and bigger restaurants. US dollars are widely accepted on the Bay Islands, but you'll usually get change in lempiras at a so-so rate. Use ATMs inside banks or malls during the day, tip around 10 percent in sit-down restaurants, and haggle gently at craft markets only.", "sleep": "Copán Ruinas has cheap hostels and guesthouses, with dorms often around 10 to 15 US dollars. On Utila, many dive shops include a dorm bed or discounted room when you book a course, which makes it one of the cheapest places in the world to learn to dive. Lake Yojoa has a famous backpacker hostel with its own microbrewery and good hiking nearby.", "eat": ["Baleada: flour tortilla folded over refried beans, cheese and cream", "Sopa de caracol: conch soup in coconut milk from the north coast", "Plato típico: grilled beef with beans, plantain, cheese and tortillas", "Tajadas: crispy fried green plantain slices, often with cabbage"], "data": "Tigo and Claro SIMs are cheap and easy to buy with a passport, and coverage is good in Copán, La Ceiba and on Utila but weak in remote areas.", "heads_up": "Avoid walking around at night in San Pedro Sula and Tegucigalpa, and pass through them by day where you can.", "food": [{"name": "Baleada", "wiki": "Baleada", "desc": "A thick flour tortilla folded over refried beans, crumbled cheese and cream, with egg, avocado or meat as extras. The go-to cheap meal, from street stands for a dollar or two.", "price": "$0.50 to $2", "when": "Breakfast or dinner, from street stands", "order": "A baleada con todo adds egg, avocado and meat"}, {"name": "Sopa de caracol", "wiki": "Sopa de Caracol", "desc": "Conch soup in coconut milk with plantain and cassava, a north coast and Bay Islands speciality.", "price": "$6 to $12", "when": "Lunch on the north coast"}, {"name": "Nacatamal", "wiki": "Nacatamal", "desc": "A big tamale stuffed with pork, rice and potato, wrapped in banana leaf and often eaten at weekends or holidays.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Weekend breakfast and Christmas"}, {"name": "Pupusa", "wiki": "Pupusa", "desc": "Thick corn tortillas stuffed with cheese, beans or pork, served with pickled cabbage. Common in western Honduras and cheap everywhere.", "price": "$0.70 to $1.50", "when": "Evenings, at pupuserías", "order": "Revuelta mixes cheese, beans and pork"}, {"name": "Tapado", "wiki": "Tapado", "desc": "A rich Garifuna seafood soup with coconut milk, green plantain and cassava, found in coastal towns and on the Bay Islands.", "price": "$8 to $15", "when": "Lunch in Garifuna coastal towns"}], "food_tip": "Street baleadas and comedores serving a plato típico (beef, beans, plantain, cheese and tortillas) are the cheapest way to fill up. On Utila, eat where the divemasters eat rather than on the main strip.", "culture": ["Greet people with 'buenos días' or 'buenas' when entering shops or buses, and say 'con permiso' when squeezing past.", "Honduras is mostly Christian, with both Catholic and evangelical churches, so dress respectfully in churches and small towns.", "The Garifuna people on the Caribbean coast have their own language, music and food. Ask before taking photos, and seek out a punta performance if you can.", "Pointing with your lips is common, and you'll see it when asking for directions.", "Tipping dive instructors and boat crew on Utila is appreciated after a course."], "safety": "Honduras has high crime rates, mostly concentrated in Tegucigalpa and San Pedro Sula, so pass through those by day, use Uber and keep valuables out of sight. The main traveller spots like Copán Ruinas, Lake Yojoa and Utila are generally relaxed, though petty theft happens. On Utila, protect yourself from sandflies and mosquitoes, as dengue is present.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is often big, with eggs, beans, cream and tortillas, and lunch around noon is the main meal.", "Don't drink tap water; buy bottled water or big refillable jugs, and only have ice made from purified water.", "Vegetarians can get by with baleadas sencillas (beans, cheese and cream), pupusas de queso and fried plantains.", "Tip around 10 percent in sit-down restaurants unless a service charge is already on the bill.", "Comedores and street stands are cash only, and small lempira notes make paying easier."], "drinks": [{"name": "Guifiti", "desc": "A Garifuna drink of roots and herbs steeped in rum, said to be medicinal. A shot is around $1 to $3."}, {"name": "Horchata", "desc": "A sweet, cold drink of rice and morro seeds with cinnamon, about 50 cents to $1 a glass."}], "greet": "Men shake hands, and women often greet with one kiss on the right cheek. Use usted with elders and strangers.", "dress": "Dress is casual, but long trousers or a skirt are better in churches and small towns, and swimwear belongs on the beach. Locals rarely wear shorts in Tegucigalpa.", "table": "Say buen provecho to people eating as you pass or leave, and wait for your host to start. Tortillas often double as a spoon.", "faith": "Evangelical Protestants now slightly outnumber Catholics, and Garifuna communities also keep ancestral dügü ceremonies. Ask before photographing services or rituals.", "phrases": [["¿Qué onda, vos?", "What's up?"], ["Catracho", "Honduran, a proud nickname"], ["Pisto", "Money"], ["Cheque", "Okay, cool"], ["¿Cuánto vale?", "How much is it?"]]},
  "myanmar": {"around": "Where travel is still possible, long-distance buses and domestic flights link Yangon with the main tourist areas, and trains are slow but scenic. Large parts of the country are affected by the ongoing conflict, roads can close without warning, and checkpoints are common. Check current conditions for every single leg before booking, and have a plan B.", "money": "Myanmar runs on the kyat (MMK), and since the 2021 coup the economy has been in freefall, with an official exchange rate and a much better (and grey) market rate that shifts constantly. Foreign cards often don't work in ATMs and are rarely accepted for payment, so bring crisp, unmarked, recent US dollars and change them at licensed money changers. Tipping is small and optional, and gentle haggling is normal at markets.", "sleep": "Foreigners may only stay in licensed hotels and guesthouses, which rules out the cheapest local places, but with tourism way down, prices in the areas still visited like Yangon, Bagan and Inle Lake are lower than they used to be. A few backpacker hostels survive in Yangon. Book ahead and confirm the place is licensed for foreigners.", "eat": ["Mohinga: fish and rice noodle soup, the national breakfast", "Laphet thoke: fermented tea leaf salad with nuts and beans", "Shan noodles: rice noodles with chicken or pork in a light sauce", "Lahpet yay: sweet, strong tea with condensed milk"], "data": "MPT, Atom and Mytel sell SIMs with passport registration, but internet shutdowns are common and Facebook and other sites are blocked, so have a VPN installed before arrival and download offline maps.", "heads_up": "Don't photograph military or police sites, convoys or checkpoints.", "food": [{"name": "Mohinga", "wiki": "Mohinga", "desc": "Rice noodles in a lemongrass and fish broth with banana stem, the national breakfast, ladled out by street vendors for very little.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Breakfast from street stalls", "order": "Add a crispy fritter, akyaw"}, {"name": "Lahpet thoke", "wiki": "Lahpet", "desc": "Fermented tea leaf salad with crunchy fried beans, peanuts, garlic and tomato, a unique Myanmar snack you have to try.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Snack or with tea"}, {"name": "Shan tofu", "wiki": "Burmese tofu", "desc": "Yellow tofu made from chickpea flour rather than soy, eaten fried as fritters or cut into a tangy salad, especially in Shan State.", "price": "$0.50 to $2", "when": "Breakfast or snack", "order": "Try tohu nway, the warm melted version"}, {"name": "Ohn no khao swè", "wiki": "Ohn no khao swè", "desc": "Coconut milk chicken noodle soup topped with crispy bits, boiled egg and lime, a rich breakfast or lunch.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast or lunch", "order": "Squeeze on lime and add chilli"}, {"name": "Nan gyi thoke", "wiki": "Nan gyi thoke", "desc": "Thick rice noodles tossed with chicken curry, chickpea flour and crispy toppings, a Mandalay favourite.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast in Mandalay"}], "food_tip": "Teahouses are the cheapest and most social places to eat, serving sweet milky tea, samosas and noodles for very little. Street stalls dish out mohinga and curries for pennies, so just point and smile.", "culture": ["Remove shoes and socks before entering any pagoda or temple compound, and dress modestly, covering shoulders and knees. A longyi (sarong) is cheap to buy and works perfectly.", "Don't touch people's heads, and don't point your feet at people or Buddha images.", "Women should not touch monks or hand things directly to them, so pass items via a man or place them down.", "Walk clockwise around pagodas and stupas.", "Politics is extremely sensitive and can put locals at real risk, so avoid discussing the military, the coup or the conflict in public or tagging people in photos online.", "Thanaka, the pale paste on people's faces, is a traditional sunscreen and cosmetic, and locals are often happy to share a little."], "safety": "Since the 2021 military coup, Myanmar has been in civil war, and many governments advise against all or most travel there. Fighting, airstrikes, landmines and road closures affect large parts of the country, and the March 2025 earthquake caused heavy damage around Mandalay and Sagaing. If you do go, check advice daily, stick to established tourist areas, keep your embassy informed and make sure your insurance actually covers you.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is early, from about 6am, and teahouses fill with locals before work.", "Don't drink tap water, and stick to bottled or filtered water.", "Vegetarians can find many options, especially on Buddhist holy days, so say thet that lo for vegetarian.", "Tipping isn't expected in local places, but small tips for guides and drivers are appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Laphet yay", "desc": "Strong black tea sweetened with condensed milk, served in teahouses for about $0.30 to $0.50. You can order it sweeter or stronger."}, {"name": "Toddy", "desc": "Sap from toddy palms, drunk fresh or lightly fermented in central Myanmar, about $0.50 for a glass."}], "greet": "A smile and mingalaba is the usual greeting, and handshakes are fine with men. Bow slightly to monks and elders.", "dress": "Cover shoulders and knees, especially at pagodas, where shoes and socks come off. Longyis are cheap to buy and fit right in.", "table": "Dishes are shared, and eating with your right hand or a spoon and fork is normal. Elders are served first.", "faith": "Most people are Theravada Buddhist, with Christian, Muslim and Hindu minorities. At pagodas walk clockwise, be quiet, and never touch Buddha images or monks' robes.", "phrases": [["Mingalaba (မင်္ဂလာပါ)", "Hello"], ["Kyay zu tin ba deh (ကျေးဇူးတင်ပါတယ်)", "Thank you"], ["Beh lout leh? (ဘယ်လောက်လဲ)", "How much?"], ["Ma sat ba zay neh (မစပ်ပါစေနဲ့)", "Not spicy please"], ["Sa lo kaung deh (စားလို့ကောင်းတယ်)", "It's delicious"]]},
  "singapore": {"around": "The MRT and buses are clean, fast and cheap, and you can just tap in with a contactless bank card or phone, no separate travel card needed. Grab, Gojek and TADA cover rides when the trains stop around midnight. The city is very walkable, though the heat and humidity make air-conditioned malls your friend. Buses to Malaysia via the Johor causeway get packed on weekends and holidays, so book ahead or travel midweek.", "money": "The currency is the Singapore dollar (SGD). Cards, contactless and phone payments work almost everywhere, and hawker stalls increasingly take PayNow or card, but a few old-school stalls are still cash only, so keep some small notes. ATMs are everywhere. Tipping isn't expected (restaurants add a 10 percent service charge on top of tax), and haggling isn't part of the culture outside a few souvenir shops.", "sleep": "Singapore is pricey, so hostel dorms and capsule-style pods are the budget choice, usually around 30 to 50 Singapore dollars a night. Chinatown, Little India, Kampong Glam and Bugis have the most hostels and are great bases near cheap food. Hotels jump in price around the Formula 1 race in September and big concerts, so book early then.", "eat": ["Hainanese chicken rice: poached chicken with rice cooked in stock", "Laksa: spicy coconut curry noodle soup", "Kaya toast: toast with coconut jam, butter and soft eggs", "Kopi: local coffee, strong and sweet"], "data": "Tourist SIMs from Singtel, StarHub and M1 are sold at the airport and 7-Elevens with your passport, and an eSIM is just as easy. Coverage is excellent, and free Wireless@SG wifi is available in many public places.", "heads_up": "Fines for littering, jaywalking, and eating or drinking on the MRT are real and enforced.", "food": [{"name": "Hainanese chicken rice", "wiki": "Hainanese chicken rice", "desc": "Poached chicken with rice cooked in chicken stock, chilli sauce and ginger. A plate at a hawker centre costs around 4 to 6 dollars.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Lunch, at hawker centres", "order": "Choose steamed (white) or roasted chicken"}, {"name": "Laksa", "wiki": "Laksa", "desc": "Spicy coconut curry noodle soup with prawns, cockles and fish cake. Katong laksa is the local style, eaten with just a spoon.", "price": "$4 to $6", "when": "Lunch or dinner, at hawker stalls", "order": "Katong laksa has cut noodles, eaten with just a spoon"}, {"name": "Char kway teow", "wiki": "Char kway teow", "desc": "Flat rice noodles stir fried with soy, egg, Chinese sausage and cockles over a smoky wok.", "price": "$4 to $6", "when": "Dinner, at hawker centres", "order": "Ask for no cockles if you're wary of shellfish"}, {"name": "Chilli crab", "wiki": "Chilli crab", "desc": "Crab in a sweet, spicy tomato and egg sauce, mopped up with fried mantou buns. It's a splurge, so share it with friends.", "price": "$50 to $90", "when": "Celebration dinners at seafood restaurants", "order": "Priced by weight, so confirm the cost before ordering"}, {"name": "Roti prata", "wiki": "Roti canai", "desc": "Crispy, flaky flatbread with curry for dipping, an all-hours favourite at Indian Muslim stalls for a couple of dollars.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Breakfast or supper, at 24 hour stalls", "order": "Order kosong for plain or telur with egg"}, {"name": "Kaya toast", "wiki": "Kaya toast", "desc": "Toast with coconut jam and butter, served with soft-boiled eggs and kopi (local coffee) for a classic breakfast.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Breakfast, at kopitiams (coffee shops)", "order": "Get the set with two soft eggs and kopi"}], "food_tip": "Hawker centres like Maxwell, Lau Pa Sat, Old Airport Road and Tiong Bahru Market serve world-class food for a few dollars a plate. Michelin-listed hawker stalls are cheap too, but expect a queue.", "culture": ["Locals 'chope' (reserve) tables at hawker centres by leaving a tissue packet on the seat. Don't sit at a choped table.", "Return your tray and crockery to the tray return points at hawker centres, as it's now required and you can be fined for not doing it.", "Singapore is multicultural, with Chinese, Malay, Indian and other communities, so dress modestly at mosques and temples and take your shoes off where asked.", "Chewing gum isn't sold, littering, jaywalking and smoking outside designated areas are fined, and vaping is illegal with heavy penalties.", "Drug laws are among the strictest in the world, including the death penalty for trafficking, so don't even think about it."], "safety": "Singapore is one of the safest cities in the world, with very low crime. The main hazards are the heat, humidity and sudden afternoon thunderstorms, so drink lots of water and carry an umbrella. Dengue is present, so use repellent, especially in parks and green areas.", "eat_beats": ["Hawker centres open for breakfast around 7am and most stalls close by 9 or 10pm, but stalls take random days off, so have a backup.", "Tap water is safe everywhere, though hawker drink stalls charge a little for plain water.", "Queue at the stall, pay when you order, and give your table number if the stall says it will bring the food over.", "Vegetarians can find Indian vegetarian stalls and Chinese Buddhist vegetarian stalls, marked 素, in most hawker centres.", "Tipping isn't expected, and restaurants already add a 10 percent service charge plus 9 percent GST to the bill."], "drinks": [{"name": "Kopi", "desc": "Local coffee roasted with sugar and margarine, served thick and sweet for about $1 to $1.50 at a kopitiam. Kopi-o means black with sugar."}, {"name": "Teh tarik", "desc": "Milk tea pulled between two cups from a height until it's frothy. A glass is about $1 to $2 at Indian Muslim stalls."}], "greet": "A light handshake is normal, but many people simply nod and smile. Some Malay Muslims touch their heart after a handshake, and some Muslim women prefer not to shake hands with men.", "dress": "Light summer clothes are fine almost everywhere, but bring a layer for the icy air conditioning. Cover shoulders and knees at temples and mosques, where robes are often lent at the door.", "table": "Don't stick chopsticks upright in rice, as it looks like a funeral offering. At Malay and Indian stalls eating with your right hand is normal, and spoon and fork is the default for most dishes.", "faith": "About 31 percent of Singaporeans are Buddhist, with Christians, Muslims, Taoists and Hindus forming big communities and a fifth having no religion. Take shoes off at temples and mosques, and step over, not on, the raised thresholds of Chinese temples.", "phrases": [["Can or not?", "Is that possible?"], ["Shiok", "Delicious, amazing"], ["Dabao", "Takeaway, to go"], ["Lah", "Added for emphasis, as in okay lah"], ["Kopi-o kosong", "Black coffee, no sugar"]]},
  "timor-leste": {"around": "Microlets (minibuses) run cheap routes around Dili, while buses and open trucks reach the districts on rough, winding roads. Fast boats and the Berlin Nakroma ferry run to Atauro Island, and the ferry also goes to the Oecusse enclave. There are no ride-hailing apps, so use taxis and agree on fares first. Ferry schedules are limited, so check ahead and plan around the wet season, when roads can wash out.", "money": "Timor-Leste uses the US dollar, with local centavo coins for small change. ATMs are mostly in Dili, and cards are rarely accepted outside a few hotels and restaurants there, so carry enough cash before heading to the districts or Atauro. Tipping isn't expected, and prices are usually fixed, though you can negotiate taxi fares and some market goods.", "sleep": "Dili has guesthouses and a few hostels, with budget rooms often around 20 to 40 US dollars, which is pricier than Indonesia next door. Atauro Island has simple eco-lodges and beach huts, and homestays in mountain towns like Maubisse are basic but welcoming. Camping is possible in some areas, but ask locals first.", "eat": ["Batar da'an: corn, beans and pumpkin stewed together", "Ikan sabuko: grilled Spanish mackerel, common in Dili", "Tukir: meat cooked with spices inside bamboo", "Timorese coffee: locally grown arabica, strong and smooth"], "data": "Telkomcel, Timor Telecom and Telemor sell SIMs with passport registration, with decent coverage in Dili and patchy service in the mountains.", "heads_up": "Malaria and dengue are present, so use repellent and cover up at dawn and dusk.", "food": [{"name": "Ikan pepes", "wiki": "Pepes", "desc": "Fish rubbed with spices, wrapped in banana leaf and grilled or steamed, sold at markets and beachside grills in Dili.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch or dinner at warungs", "order": "Ask for it with rice and sambal"}, {"name": "Feijoada", "wiki": "Feijoada", "desc": "A slow-cooked bean and meat stew left over from the Portuguese era, served with rice at local restaurants.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Family meals and festive days"}, {"name": "Katupa", "wiki": "Ketupat", "desc": "Rice cooked in woven palm-leaf pouches, sold at markets and eaten with grilled meat or fish, especially at festivals.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Festivals and as a snack"}, {"name": "Bibinka", "wiki": "Bebinca", "desc": "A layered coconut cake with Portuguese roots, baked for celebrations and sold at some bakeries.", "price": "$1 to $2 a slice", "when": "Special occasions and holidays"}, {"name": "Timorese coffee", "wiki": "Coffea arabica", "desc": "Organic arabica grown in the hills around Ermera and Maubisse, strong and smooth, and one of the country's biggest exports. A cup in Dili cafes is cheap.", "price": "$0.50 to $2", "when": "Morning, everywhere", "order": "Buy beans in Dili markets to take home"}], "food_tip": "Local warungs (small eateries) and markets serve cheap plates of rice, fish and vegetables, and fresh fruit is plentiful. In the evenings, grab fish grilled on the beach along Dili's waterfront for a cheap feed.", "culture": ["Timor-Leste is mostly Catholic, so dress modestly, especially in churches and rural areas.", "Greet people with a handshake and a smile, and try 'bondia' (good morning) or 'botarde' (good afternoon) in Tetum.", "Ask before taking photos of people, especially in villages.", "The independence struggle is a source of pride, but also deep pain, so listen respectfully if it comes up.", "Sacred houses (uma lulik) are important in local culture, so ask before approaching or photographing them."], "safety": "Timor-Leste is generally safe, though petty theft happens in Dili, especially at night. Roads are rough and winding, so travel by day. Saltwater crocodiles live along the coast and are taken seriously by locals, so ask before swimming. Malaria and dengue are present, so use repellent.", "eat_beats": ["Most locals eat rice with fish, vegetables or meat at lunch, and many warungs close by evening.", "Don't drink tap water, so carry a filter or buy large bottles.", "Vegetarians can live on rice, tempeh, tofu, vegetables and eggs, but ask about fish sauce and meat broth.", "The US dollar is the currency, so carry small notes and Timorese centavo coins for markets.", "Tipping isn't expected, though rounding up in Dili restaurants is a nice gesture."], "drinks": [{"name": "Tua sabu", "desc": "A strong palm spirit brewed in villages and shared at ceremonies. A bottle costs about $2 to $5 at markets."}, {"name": "Young coconut", "desc": "Fresh coconut water sold at beachside stalls. About $1 to $1.50."}], "greet": "Shake hands gently and say \"bondia\", \"botarde\" or \"bonoite\" depending on the time of day. Older people may be called \"avó\" (grandparent) as a sign of respect.", "dress": "Wear clothes that cover your shoulders and knees, especially outside Dili and in churches. Swimwear is fine at the beach, but cover up when you leave it.", "table": "Guests are often served first and given the best portions, so accept graciously. Use your right hand for passing food.", "faith": "Over 95% of Timorese are Catholic, often mixed with traditional animist beliefs. Dress modestly at churches, be quiet during Mass and never touch sacred objects in uma lulik.", "phrases": [["Bondia", "Good morning, in Tetum"], ["Obrigadu / obrigada", "Thank you (male / female speaker)"], ["Folin hira?", "How much?"], ["La ho ai-manas", "Without chilli"], ["Diak ka lae?", "How are you?"]]},
  "japan": {"around": "Load a Suica, Pasmo or ICOCA card on your phone and tap through trains, metros, buses and even konbini checkouts. The shinkansen is fast but pricey, and since the big price hike in 2023 the Japan Rail Pass rarely pays off, so compare it against single tickets or regional passes first. Overnight highway buses like Willer are the backpacker way to save money and a night's accommodation. Book shinkansen seats ahead for Golden Week, Obon in August and New Year, when trains are rammed.", "money": "The currency is the yen (JPY). Cards and phone payments now work in most chains, convenience stores and stations, but small restaurants, shrines, temples and rural guesthouses still like cash, so keep a few thousand yen on you. 7-Eleven and Japan Post ATMs reliably take foreign cards. Tipping isn't done and can even cause confusion, and prices aren't negotiated.", "sleep": "Hostels, capsule hotels and cheap business hotels keep costs down, with dorm beds often around 3,000 to 5,000 yen a night outside the busiest weeks. Guesthouses in old houses and temple stays (shukubo) on Mount Koya are memorable splurges. Book well ahead for cherry blossom season in spring and the autumn leaves in Kyoto, when prices spike.", "eat": ["Ramen: wheat noodles in rich broth, with many regional styles", "Okonomiyaki: savory pancake with cabbage, grilled at your table", "Onigiri: rice ball with filling, sold in convenience stores", "Takoyaki: octopus-filled batter balls, an Osaka street snack"], "data": "Data-only SIMs and eSIMs are the easy choice, since voice SIMs are hard to get on a tourist visa. Coverage is excellent, and pocket wifi rentals are handy if you're travelling in a group.", "heads_up": "Public bins are rare, so carry your rubbish with you, and keep phone calls off on trains.", "food": [{"name": "Ramen", "wiki": "Ramen", "desc": "Wheat noodles in rich broth, with regional styles from tonkotsu pork in Fukuoka to miso in Sapporo. A bowl at a ticket-machine shop runs around 800 to 1,200 yen.", "price": "$6 to $9", "when": "Lunch or late night after drinks", "order": "Ask for kaedama, a cheap extra noodle refill"}, {"name": "Sushi", "wiki": "Sushi", "desc": "Conveyor-belt sushi chains make it cheap to try loads of styles, with plates from around 100 to 200 yen.", "price": "$1 to $2 a plate", "when": "Dinner at conveyor-belt chains", "order": "Order fresh by tablet instead of grabbing passing plates"}, {"name": "Okonomiyaki", "wiki": "Okonomiyaki", "desc": "A savoury cabbage pancake grilled at your table, with Osaka and Hiroshima arguing over whose version is better.", "price": "$7 to $11", "when": "Dinner, often with beer", "order": "Hiroshima style adds noodles and layers the cabbage"}, {"name": "Takoyaki", "wiki": "Takoyaki", "desc": "Octopus-filled batter balls topped with sauce, mayo and bonito flakes, Osaka's favourite street snack.", "price": "$3 to $5 for six to eight", "when": "Street stalls and festivals", "order": "Let them cool, the centres are lava hot"}, {"name": "Gyudon", "wiki": "Gyūdon", "desc": "A bowl of rice topped with sweet simmered beef and onion. Chains like Yoshinoya and Sukiya serve it for a few hundred yen, 24 hours a day.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Quick breakfast or lunch, any hour", "order": "Add a raw egg and miso soup set"}, {"name": "Onigiri", "wiki": "Onigiri", "desc": "Rice balls wrapped in nori with fillings like salmon, tuna mayo or pickled plum, sold at every convenience store for under 200 yen.", "price": "$1 to $1.50", "when": "Breakfast or snacks on the go", "order": "Pull the numbered tabs 1, 2, 3 to unwrap"}], "food_tip": "Convenience stores (konbini) like 7-Eleven, Lawson and FamilyMart are a budget traveller's best friend, and supermarkets discount bento boxes in the evening. Lunch sets (teishoku) are much cheaper than dinner at the same restaurant.", "culture": ["Take your shoes off when entering homes, ryokan, some temples and some restaurants, and swap into the toilet slippers when you go to the loo.", "Keep quiet on trains, don't take phone calls and set your phone to silent.", "Don't stick chopsticks upright in rice or pass food from chopstick to chopstick, as both are linked to funerals.", "At onsen (hot springs), wash thoroughly before getting in, don't put your towel in the water, and be aware that many still refuse people with tattoos, so check first or cover small ones.", "Eating while walking is frowned upon in many places, so eat at the stall or find a bench.", "In Kyoto's Gion district, don't chase or photograph geiko and maiko, and respect the signs banning photos on private lanes."], "safety": "Japan is incredibly safe, with very low crime. The main risks are touts in nightlife districts like Roppongi and Kabukicho who lure you into bars with huge bills, so steer clear. Earthquakes and typhoons happen, so learn the basics and follow local alerts, and the summer heat and humidity can be brutal, so drink lots of water.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch runs from about 11:30 to 1:30 and dinner from 6, and many small places close between the two.", "At many ramen and gyudon shops you buy a ticket from a vending machine by the door and hand it to the cook.", "Tap water is safe everywhere, and free cold water or tea comes with almost every meal.", "Vegetarian food is tricky because fish stock (dashi) is in most dishes, so look for shojin ryori temple food or vegan-labelled spots.", "Tipping is not done and can cause confusion, so just pay the bill at the register on your way out."], "drinks": [{"name": "Sake", "desc": "Rice wine served cold or warm, with a glass at an izakaya costing about $4 to $8. Try a tasting set to compare styles."}, {"name": "Matcha", "desc": "Whisked powdered green tea, bitter and grassy, often served with a small sweet for $5 to $10 in Kyoto tea houses."}], "greet": "People bow rather than shake hands, with a small nod fine for travellers. Add the suffix san to names and avoid hugging.", "dress": "Casual clothes are fine in cities, but keep shoulders and knees covered at temples and shrines. Revealing outfits draw more stares than in Europe.", "table": "Say itadakimasu before eating and gochisosama deshita after. Slurping noodles is fine, but don't pour your own drink in a group, pour for others.", "faith": "Most people mix Shinto and Buddhism without seeing a conflict. At shrines rinse your hands at the water basin, bow twice, clap twice and bow once.", "phrases": [["Konnichiwa (こんにちは)", "Hello"], ["Arigatou gozaimasu (ありがとうございます)", "Thank you"], ["Sumimasen (すみません)", "Excuse me, sorry"], ["Ikura desu ka (いくらですか)", "How much is it?"], ["Oishii (おいしい)", "Delicious"]]},
  "south-korea": {"around": "KTX high-speed trains and express buses link Seoul with Busan, Gyeongju, Jeonju and beyond, and the buses are often half the price of the train and nearly as quick. A T-money card (or a Climate Card for unlimited rides in Seoul) covers subways and city buses, and you can top it up at any convenience store. Kakao T is the go-to taxi app, and Uber also works in Seoul. Book KTX seats and buses ahead around Chuseok and Lunar New Year, when the whole country heads home at once.", "money": "The currency is the won (KRW). Cards and contactless work almost everywhere, even for a 1,500 won coffee, though traditional markets, street food carts and some older guesthouses prefer cash. Look for 'Global ATM' signs at convenience stores and banks like KB, Shinhan and Woori, as not every machine takes foreign cards. Tipping isn't expected and can genuinely confuse people, and haggling is only really a thing at markets.", "sleep": "Hostels and guesthouses are cheap and spotless in Seoul and Busan, with dorm beds often around 20,000 to 35,000 won, and Hongdae, Myeongdong and Haeundae are classic backpacker bases. For a uniquely Korean budget night, crash at a jjimjilbang (24-hour bathhouse), where you soak, sweat and then sleep on a mat for a fraction of a hostel price. Temple stays are a pricier but unforgettable splurge.", "eat": ["Bibimbap: rice bowl with vegetables, egg and chili paste", "Tteokbokki: chewy rice cakes in spicy sauce", "Kimchi jjigae: spicy stew of kimchi, tofu and pork", "Korean fried chicken: double-fried, crispy, often glazed"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs and eSIMs are sold at Incheon Airport and online with your passport, and coverage is excellent even underground. Free wifi is everywhere, from subway stations to cafes.", "heads_up": "Google Maps directions barely work here, so download Naver Map or KakaoMap before you arrive.", "food": [{"name": "Bibimbap", "wiki": "Bibimbap", "desc": "Rice topped with seasoned vegetables, egg, meat and chilli paste, mixed up before eating. Jeonju is the spiritual home of it.", "price": "$8 to $12", "when": "Lunch, especially in Jeonju", "order": "Try dolsot bibimbap in a sizzling stone bowl"}, {"name": "Tteokbokki", "wiki": "Tteokbokki", "desc": "Chewy rice cakes in sweet, spicy gochujang sauce, sold from street carts and market stalls for a few thousand won.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "After school, at street stalls", "order": "Add fish cake skewers and dip fried twigim in sauce"}, {"name": "Kimchi jjigae", "wiki": "Kimchi-jjigae", "desc": "A bubbling stew of aged kimchi, tofu and pork, served with rice and side dishes at cheap lunch spots.", "price": "$7 to $10", "when": "Lunch at small family restaurants", "order": "Comes with rice and free banchan"}, {"name": "Korean fried chicken", "wiki": "Korean fried chicken", "desc": "Double-fried, crackly chicken, plain or glazed in spicy or soy garlic sauce. Pair it with beer for the classic 'chimaek' night out.", "price": "$15 to $22", "when": "Late night with beer, called chimaek", "order": "Order banban: half plain fried, half spicy yangnyeom"}, {"name": "Samgyeopsal", "wiki": "Samgyeopsal", "desc": "Thick slices of pork belly grilled at your table and wrapped in lettuce with garlic and ssamjang, best with friends and a bottle of soju.", "price": "$12 to $16", "when": "Dinner with friends and soju", "order": "Most places need at least two portions per table"}, {"name": "Hotteok", "wiki": "Hotteok", "desc": "Pan-fried pancakes oozing brown sugar, honey and nuts, sold from street stalls especially in winter.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Winter street snack", "order": "Busan's ssiat hotteok comes stuffed with seeds"}], "food_tip": "Kimbap chains, convenience store meals and lunchtime set menus (baekban) keep costs low, and the free refillable side dishes (banchan) stretch every meal. Traditional markets like Gwangjang in Seoul and Jagalchi in Busan are great for cheap street food.", "culture": ["Age and seniority matter a lot. Use two hands (or your right hand supported by your left) when giving or receiving things, especially from older people.", "When drinking, pour for others rather than yourself, hold your glass with two hands when an elder pours, and turn your head slightly away when you drink in front of them.", "Don't write people's names in red ink, as it's associated with the dead.", "Take your shoes off when entering homes, some guesthouses, temples and traditional floor-seating restaurants.", "Don't stick chopsticks upright in your rice, and wait for the eldest person to start eating before you dig in.", "Priority seats on the subway are left empty for elderly and pregnant passengers, even when it's packed, so don't sit there."], "safety": "South Korea is very safe, with low crime and well-lit streets at all hours. Watch for delivery scooters on pavements, and steer clear of big political rallies in central Seoul, which are usually peaceful but crowded. Summer brings monsoon rains and the occasional typhoon, and winters are properly cold, so pack accordingly.", "eat_beats": ["Many restaurants are set up for groups, and barbecue places often won't serve one portion to a solo diner.", "Tap water is safe, and most restaurants give free water from a self-serve dispenser.", "Vegetarian food is tricky, since meat stock and fish sauce hide in many dishes; Buddhist temple food restaurants are the safest bet.", "There's no tipping in Korea, and you usually pay at the counter on the way out.", "Lunch runs from about 12 to 1pm, when office workers flood restaurants, so eat a little earlier or later."], "drinks": [{"name": "Soju", "desc": "A clear spirit of around 16 percent, drunk in shots with food. A bottle costs about $1.50 in a shop and $4 to $6 in restaurants."}, {"name": "Makgeolli", "desc": "Milky, lightly fizzy rice wine, popular on rainy days with pajeon pancakes. Expect $2 to $5 a bottle."}], "greet": "A bow, from a quick nod to a deeper bow for elders, with 'annyeonghaseyo'. Men often add a handshake, touching the right forearm with the left hand as a sign of respect.", "dress": "Seoul is fashion conscious, but casual clothes are fine everywhere. At temples, skip very short shorts and low tops, and keep swimwear for the beach.", "table": "Don't start eating until the eldest picks up their spoon, and don't lift your rice bowl off the table. Rice and soup are eaten with a spoon, while side dishes are shared with chopsticks.", "faith": "Over half of Koreans say they have no religion, and Protestant Christians and Buddhists are the largest faiths. At temples, speak quietly, step over rather than on the door threshold, and don't photograph people praying.", "phrases": [["Annyeonghaseyo (안녕하세요)", "Hello"], ["Gamsahamnida (감사합니다)", "Thank you"], ["Eolmayeyo? (얼마예요?)", "How much is it?"], ["An maepge haejuseyo (안 맵게 해주세요)", "Not spicy, please"], ["Igeo juseyo (이거 주세요)", "This one, please"]]},
  "china": {"around": "China's high-speed rail network is huge, fast and cheap for the distances, and slower overnight sleeper trains save you a night's accommodation. Tickets are tied to your passport, so book on the 12306 app or Trip.com and bring your passport to the station. DiDi has an English interface and works almost everywhere, and city metros are cheap and easy. Avoid travelling during Chinese New Year and the October Golden Week if you can, or book well ahead.", "money": "The currency is the yuan or renminbi (CNY, RMB). Almost everything is paid by scanning a QR code with Alipay or WeChat Pay, and both now let you link a foreign Visa or Mastercard, with a small fee on larger payments. Carry some cash as a backup for the odd small vendor or if your app glitches, and Bank of China and ICBC ATMs usually take foreign cards. Tipping isn't expected, and haggling is normal at markets and with souvenir sellers, where the first price can be wildly inflated.", "sleep": "Youth hostels are cheap and well run in most tourist cities, with dorm beds often around 50 to 120 yuan, and they're great for meeting other travellers and booking tours. Some budget hotels can't legally register foreigners, so check before booking, and stick to places that confirm they accept foreign passports. Guesthouses in old towns like Yangshuo, Lijiang and Dali are cosy and good value.", "eat": ["Jiaozi: boiled dumplings with meat or vegetable filling", "Jianbing: savory crepe with egg, a street breakfast", "Lanzhou lamian: hand-pulled noodles in beef broth", "Mapo tofu: soft tofu in a spicy Sichuan pepper sauce"], "data": "Google, WhatsApp, Instagram and many Western apps are blocked on local SIMs and wifi, so an eSIM from abroad that routes through another country is the easiest way to stay connected. Coverage is excellent.", "heads_up": "Set up Alipay, WeChat and a translation app before you arrive, as they'll make your life much easier.", "food": [{"name": "Jiaozi", "wiki": "Jiaozi", "desc": "Boiled dumplings with pork, cabbage or chive fillings, dipped in vinegar. Order by the jin (500 grams) at cheap dumpling houses.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch and dinner, at dumpling houses", "order": "Order by liang; three liang (about 15 dumplings) feeds one person"}, {"name": "Jianbing", "wiki": "Jianbing", "desc": "A savoury crepe with egg, crispy cracker, herbs and sauces, made fresh at breakfast stalls for a few yuan.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast, from carts near metro stations", "order": "Ask for no coriander with \"bu yao xiangcai\""}, {"name": "Lanzhou beef noodles", "wiki": "Lanzhou beef noodles", "desc": "Hand-pulled noodles in clear beef broth with chilli oil and coriander, served at Muslim noodle shops all over China.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Breakfast or lunch, at noodle shops", "order": "Pick your noodle width, from thin \"xi\" to flat \"kuan\""}, {"name": "Mapo tofu", "wiki": "Mapo tofu", "desc": "Soft tofu in a fiery, numbing Sichuan pepper and bean paste sauce with minced meat, best in Chengdu.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch, at Sichuan restaurants", "order": "Order it with plenty of rice to tame the heat"}, {"name": "Hot pot", "wiki": "Hot pot", "desc": "A simmering pot of broth at your table for cooking meat, veg and noodles. Chongqing's spicy version is legendary, and it's best with a group.", "price": "$10 to $20", "when": "Dinner with friends, often late", "order": "Order a split \"yuanyang\" pot with spicy and mild broths"}, {"name": "Baozi", "wiki": "Baozi", "desc": "Fluffy steamed buns filled with pork or veg, sold at breakfast stalls for a few yuan each.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Breakfast, from steamer stalls", "order": "Xiaolongbao are the smaller soup-filled cousins"}], "food_tip": "Street food stalls, noodle shops and canteens are cheap and delicious. Point at what others are eating or use a translation app to read menus.", "culture": ["Saving face matters, so avoid publicly embarrassing people or losing your temper, as it rarely gets results.", "Politics, especially Taiwan, Tibet, Xinjiang and Tiananmen, are sensitive topics, so avoid them in public and online.", "Don't stick chopsticks upright in rice, as it's linked to funerals, and don't tap your bowl with them.", "Queueing can be chaotic, so hold your ground politely.", "When given a business card or gift, receive it with both hands, and don't open gifts in front of the giver.", "Carry your passport everywhere, as it's needed for trains, hotels, museums and even some tourist sites."], "safety": "China is very safe, with low crime against travellers. Watch out for the tea house scam and the art student scam in Beijing and Shanghai, where friendly strangers lead you to an overpriced tea or art shop, and avoid unofficial taxis. Air pollution can be bad in northern cities in winter, so check the AQI. Traffic is chaotic, especially electric scooters, so be careful crossing roads.", "eat_beats": ["Chinese meal times are early, with lunch around 11:30am to 1pm and dinner from about 5:30pm, and many kitchens close by 9pm outside big cities.", "Don't drink tap water; drink boiled or bottled water, and restaurants often serve hot water or tea for free.", "Payment is almost all by Alipay or WeChat Pay, both of which now accept foreign cards, so set one up before you arrive.", "Vegetarians can look for Buddhist restaurants near temples, and saying \"wo chi su\" (I eat vegetarian) helps elsewhere.", "Tipping is not expected anywhere in China and can even confuse staff."], "drinks": [{"name": "Baijiu", "desc": "A fiery grain spirit, often over 50 percent alcohol, drunk in small shots at banquets with lots of toasting. Cheap bottles start around $3."}, {"name": "Soy milk", "desc": "Warm fresh soy milk is the classic breakfast drink, served sweet or salty with fried dough sticks (youtiao). A cup costs under $1."}], "greet": "A nod, a smile or a light handshake is normal, and \"ni hao\" works for anyone. Address older people first in a group.", "dress": "Dress is casual in cities, and young Chinese follow fashion closely. Cover shoulders and knees at temples, and bring layers, as the north gets very cold.", "table": "Dishes are shared from the centre, often on a lazy Susan, and the host usually orders and pays. Slurping noodles is fine, and leaving a little food shows you were well fed.", "faith": "Most Chinese are not religious, though Buddhism, Taoism and folk traditions are common, and there are Muslim and Christian minorities. At temples be quiet, don't point at statues and walk around the incense burners.", "phrases": [["Ni hao (你好)", "Hello"], ["Xie xie (谢谢)", "Thank you"], ["Duoshao qian? (多少钱)", "How much?"], ["Bu la (不辣)", "Not spicy"], ["Maidan (买单)", "The bill, please"]]},
  "bangladesh": {"around": "Buses and trains link the main cities, with trains being comfier and more relaxed, though traffic and delays are part of the deal. River launches are a slow, cheap and memorable way to travel south to Barisal and the Sundarbans region. Uber and Pathao work in Dhaka and Chittagong, and Pathao motorbike rides are the quickest way through Dhaka traffic. Buy train tickets ahead on the official Rail Sheba app or website, since seats go fast.", "money": "The currency is the taka (BDT). Bangladesh is mostly cash, ATMs from big banks like DBBL, BRAC and City Bank are easy to find in cities, and cards work only in bigger hotels and upmarket restaurants. Agree on rickshaw and CNG (auto-rickshaw) fares before you climb in, and small tips for guides, drivers and hotel staff are welcome. Haggling is normal at markets, though you'll often be charged the local price anyway.", "sleep": "Budget hotels and guesthouses are the norm, with only a handful of hostels in Dhaka. Srimangal has simple eco-cottages among the tea gardens, Cox's Bazar has tons of budget hotels by the beach, and the Sundarbans are visited on boat trips that include beds and meals. Rooms are basic but cheap, often around 15 to 30 US dollars for a double.", "eat": ["Kacchi biryani: mutton and rice slow-cooked together", "Fuchka: crisp shells filled with spiced tamarind water", "Ilish curry: hilsa fish in mustard sauce", "Bhorta: mashed vegetables or fish with mustard oil and chili"], "data": "Grameenphone, Robi and Banglalink SIMs need biometric thumbprint registration with your passport at an official store, and coverage is good in most areas. Data is cheap, and Grameenphone usually has the best rural coverage.", "heads_up": "Dress modestly, especially outside Dhaka, and expect a lot of friendly attention.", "food": [{"name": "Kacchi biryani", "wiki": "Biryani", "desc": "Mutton and rice slow-cooked together with potatoes and saffron, an Old Dhaka speciality at places like Fakhruddin and Haji Biryani.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Weddings, Fridays and Old Dhaka", "order": "Ask for extra potato, a beloved part of the dish"}, {"name": "Fuchka", "wiki": "Panipuri", "desc": "Crisp shells filled with spiced chickpeas and tamarind water, the street snack every Bangladeshi loves. A plate costs pennies.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Evening snack at street stalls", "order": "Comes with tamarind water; eat each one whole"}, {"name": "Shorshe ilish", "wiki": "Shorshe ilish", "desc": "Hilsa, the national fish, cooked in a pungent mustard sauce. Watch for the bones.", "price": "$3 to $7", "when": "Monsoon season, when hilsa is freshest", "order": "Watch for small bones; eat with plain rice"}, {"name": "Bhorta", "wiki": "Bhurta", "desc": "Mashed vegetables, fish or lentils with mustard oil, onion and chilli, served in little piles with rice.", "price": "$0.50 to $2", "when": "Lunch, served with rice and dal", "order": "Try potato, aubergine and dried fish versions"}, {"name": "Haleem", "wiki": "Haleem", "desc": "A thick, slow-cooked stew of meat, lentils and wheat, especially popular during Ramadan.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Ramadan iftar, sold on streets"}, {"name": "Seven-layer tea", "wiki": "Seven-color tea", "desc": "A Srimangal novelty: tea poured in multiple coloured layers, each a different flavour.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Tea stalls around Sreemangal"}], "food_tip": "Street food and local 'hotels' (cheap restaurants) serve filling meals of rice, dal and curry for very little. Tea stalls are everywhere and great for meeting locals over a cup of sweet milky cha.", "culture": ["Bangladesh is a conservative Muslim-majority country, so dress modestly, covering shoulders and knees, especially outside Dhaka. Women will feel more comfortable with a scarf and loose clothing.", "Use your right hand for eating, greeting and passing things.", "Expect a lot of friendly attention and curiosity, including requests for selfies and lots of questions about your country and family.", "During Ramadan, avoid eating, drinking or smoking in public in daylight hours.", "Take your shoes off before entering mosques and homes, and don't walk in front of people praying.", "Men shouldn't initiate handshakes with women, so let them take the lead."], "safety": "Bangladesh is generally safe for travellers, but political protests and strikes (hartals) can flare up, especially around big political events, so follow the news and avoid demonstrations. Road safety is the biggest everyday risk, and overcrowded launches can be dangerous in bad weather. The Chittagong Hill Tracts require a permit for foreigners, and dengue is common in the monsoon, so use repellent.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is often paratha with dal or egg, lunch is the main meal around 2pm, and dinner can be after 9pm.", "Never drink tap water; stick to sealed bottled water.", "Vegetarian options are good thanks to dal, bhorta, vegetable curries and Hindu sweet shops.", "Tipping isn't expected in cheap places, but 5 to 10 percent is welcome in nicer restaurants.", "Most restaurants have a basin for washing hands, since locals eat with their right hand."], "drinks": [{"name": "Borhani", "desc": "A spicy, minty yogurt drink served with biryani at feasts. Around $0.50 to $1 a glass."}, {"name": "Sugarcane juice", "desc": "Pressed fresh on street carts, sweet and cooling, for about $0.20 to $0.50 a glass."}], "greet": "Muslims greet with 'Assalamu alaikum' and men shake right hands, while Hindus say 'Nomoskar' with palms together. Men should wait for women to offer a hand.", "dress": "Men wear long trousers, as shorts look odd outside the beach. Women will be most comfortable in a salwar kameez with a scarf (orna), sold cheaply in local markets.", "table": "Food is eaten with the right hand, mixing rice and curry with your fingers, and hosts will keep refilling your plate. Take a little of everything and let elders start first.", "faith": "About 91 percent of Bangladeshis are Muslim and around 8 percent Hindu, with Buddhists in the Chittagong Hill Tracts. Remove shoes at mosques and temples, women should cover their hair, and avoid visiting mosques during Friday midday prayers.", "phrases": [["Assalamu alaikum (আসসালামু আলাইকুম)", "Hello"], ["Dhonnobad (ধন্যবাদ)", "Thank you"], ["Dam koto? (দাম কত?)", "How much?"], ["Jhal kom (ঝাল কম)", "Less spicy"], ["Bhalo (ভালো)", "Good"]]},
  "bhutan": {"around": "Most visitors get around by hired car with a licensed guide and driver, booked before arrival, since a guide is required for most of the country outside Thimphu and Paro. Public buses link the main towns but are slow and infrequent on twisting mountain roads. There are no ride-hailing apps, though taxis are cheap within Thimphu.", "money": "The currency is the ngultrum (BTN), pegged one to one with the Indian rupee, and Indian rupee notes are widely accepted too. Cash is king for small purchases, and town ATMs don't always like foreign cards, so bring some US dollars to change and don't rely on plastic outside hotels and craft shops. Tipping your guide and driver at the end of a trip is customary, and haggling is uncommon.", "sleep": "Tourist-certified hotels are the main option, and farmstays and homestays offer a homely, good-value alternative with home cooking. Bhutan isn't a cheap backpacker destination, as every visitor pays a Sustainable Development Fee of 100 US dollars per night on top of everything else, so budget for that.", "eat": ["Ema datshi: chilies cooked in cheese, the national dish", "Momos: steamed dumplings with meat, cheese or vegetables", "Red rice: nutty rice grown in the high valleys", "Suja: salty butter tea"], "data": "B-Mobile and TashiCell sell SIMs with a passport, often right at Paro airport, and coverage is good in towns and valleys. Expect patchy signal on treks and high passes.", "heads_up": "Wear long sleeves and a collar in dzongs and temples, and walk clockwise around chortens.", "food": [{"name": "Ema datshi", "wiki": "Ema datshi", "desc": "Chillies cooked in a runny cheese sauce, the national dish and part of nearly every meal. Bhutanese treat chilli as a vegetable, so brace yourself.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Lunch and dinner, with every meal", "order": "Kewa datshi (potato) and shamu datshi (mushroom) are milder"}, {"name": "Momos", "wiki": "Momo (food)", "desc": "Steamed dumplings with beef, pork, cheese or veg, sold in cafes and canteens for a couple of dollars a plate.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Snack or lunch, at cafes", "order": "Cheese momos are a local favourite"}, {"name": "Red rice", "wiki": "Bhutanese red rice", "desc": "Nutty, slightly sticky rice grown in the Paro valley, served with most meals.", "price": "Included with meals", "when": "Lunch and dinner, every day"}, {"name": "Suja", "wiki": "Butter tea", "desc": "Salty butter tea offered to guests and drunk throughout the day in homes and monasteries.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "All day, in homes and monasteries"}, {"name": "Hoentay", "wiki": "Hoentay", "desc": "Buckwheat dumplings stuffed with turnip greens, cheese and chilli, a speciality of the Haa valley, steamed or fried.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Festivals and Losar, in the Haa valley", "order": "Fried hoentay is crispier and great with ezay chilli sauce"}, {"name": "Ara", "wiki": "Ara (drink)", "desc": "A clear homemade spirit distilled from rice, maize or millet, often served warm with butter and egg in villages.", "price": "$1 to $2 a glass", "when": "Festivals and cold evenings in villages", "order": "Try it warm with butter and egg"}], "food_tip": "Local canteens in Thimphu and Paro serve rice, ema datshi and momos for very little, and your tour package usually includes most meals. Ask your guide to skip the bland tourist buffets and take you somewhere local.", "culture": ["Wear long sleeves and a collar in dzongs and temples, keep hats off inside, and walk clockwise around chortens, prayer wheels and temples.", "Selling tobacco is heavily restricted and smoking in public is frowned upon, so bring only your own small personal supply and be discreet.", "Don't point at religious images, people or sacred objects with your finger, use an open hand instead.", "The royal family is deeply loved, so don't criticise or joke about them.", "Take photos inside dzongs and temples only where allowed, which is usually not inside the shrine rooms.", "Bhutan measures success by Gross National Happiness and takes the environment seriously, so leave no trace on treks."], "safety": "Bhutan is very safe, with little crime. Mountain roads are narrow and winding, and landslides can close them in the monsoon, so build in spare time. Altitude can bite on high passes and treks like the Druk Path, so acclimatise and stay hydrated.", "eat_beats": ["Bhutanese meals are simple and filling, usually rice with two or three dishes, with lunch around 1pm and dinner by 7pm.", "Don't drink tap water; bottled and filtered water is available, and many hotels offer refills.", "Vegetarians are well catered for, as many Bhutanese avoid killing animals and most meat is imported from India.", "Tuesdays are traditionally dry days, when bars and alcohol sales close.", "Tipping isn't required in restaurants, but guides and drivers expect a tip at the end of a tour."], "drinks": [{"name": "Druk 11000", "desc": "A strong local lager named after the Druk, the thunder dragon. A bottle costs $1.50 to $3 in shops and bars."}, {"name": "Sweet milk tea", "desc": "Known as ngaja, a milky sweet tea served with snacks across the country, usually under $0.50 a cup."}], "greet": "\"Kuzuzangpo la\" is the greeting, often with a slight bow, and handshakes are common in towns. Adding \"la\" to sentences makes them polite.", "dress": "Locals wear the gho (men) and kira (women) in offices and dzongs. Visitors should wear long sleeves, long trousers or skirts and a collar at religious sites.", "table": "Hosts offer food several times, and it's polite to say \"meshu meshu\" (no thanks) before accepting. Wash your hands before meals and wait for elders.", "faith": "Vajrayana Buddhism shapes daily life, with a Hindu minority in the south. Walk clockwise around sacred sites, don't touch religious objects, and keep silent in shrine rooms.", "phrases": [["Kuzuzangpo la (ཀུ་ཟུ་བཟང་པོ་ལགས།)", "Hello"], ["Kadrinchey la (བཀའ་དྲིན་ཆེ་ལགས།)", "Thank you"], ["Gong ga dem chi mo? (གོང་ག་དེམ་ཅིག་མོ།)", "How much is it?"], ["Tashi delek (བཀྲ་ཤིས་བདེ་ལེགས།)", "Good luck, also used as cheers"]]},
  "maldives": {"around": "Public ferries between local islands cost a few dollars but run only a few times a week on some routes and not on Fridays, so plan your island hops around the timetable. Shared speedboats are faster and cost more, and seaplanes are firmly resort-money territory. In Malé, short taxi rides at fixed fares are the norm, and the bridge bus links Malé, Hulhumalé and the airport cheaply. Your guesthouse can usually book speedboat transfers and check ferry days for you.", "money": "The currency is the rufiyaa (MVR), but US dollars are accepted almost everywhere on local islands, and guesthouses often quote in dollars. Cards work at most guesthouses and dive shops, while ATMs are mostly in Malé, Hulhumalé and a few bigger islands, so bring enough cash for excursions and small cafes. Bills often include a 10 percent service charge plus tax, so extra tipping is optional, and haggling isn't really a thing beyond souvenir shops. Also budget for the nightly green tax that gets added to your accommodation bill.", "sleep": "Since local islands opened to tourism, guesthouses on places like Maafushi, Thoddoo, Dhigurah, Ukulhas and Fulidhoo have made the Maldives doable on a budget, with simple doubles often around 50 to 100 US dollars. There are very few true hostels, so pairing up for a room helps a lot. Book ahead in the dry season from December to April, when the best-value places fill up.", "eat": ["Mas huni: shredded tuna with coconut, chili and onion", "Garudhiya: clear tuna broth with rice and lime", "Hedhikaa: savory short eats served with tea", "Roshi: thin flatbread eaten with tuna or curry"], "data": "Dhiraagu and Ooredoo sell tourist SIMs at the airport with your passport, and coverage is good across inhabited islands. Guesthouse wifi varies from fine to frustrating.", "heads_up": "Alcohol isn't sold on local islands, and swimwear is only for the designated bikini beaches.", "food": [{"name": "Mas huni", "wiki": "Mas huni", "desc": "Shredded smoked tuna mixed with coconut, chilli, onion and lime, eaten with roshi flatbread for breakfast. Cheap at any local cafe.", "price": "$2 to $4 with roshi", "when": "Breakfast, at local cafes", "order": "Ask for it with hot roshi and sweet tea"}, {"name": "Garudhiya", "wiki": "Garudhiya", "desc": "A clear, fragrant tuna broth served with rice, lime, chilli and onion, the backbone of Maldivian home cooking.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch or dinner at home", "order": "Add rihaakuru, lime and chili to taste"}, {"name": "Gulha", "wiki": "Gulha", "desc": "Crispy fried dough balls stuffed with smoked tuna, coconut and chilli, the star of the afternoon hedhikaa (short eats) spread with sweet tea.", "price": "$0.30 to $0.70 each", "when": "Afternoon tea, as a short eat", "order": "Pick from the hedhikaa (snack) tray"}, {"name": "Mas riha", "wiki": "Mas riha", "desc": "Maldivian tuna curry, rich with coconut and spices, served with rice or roshi.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch or dinner, at local cafes", "order": "Ask for it with roshi for scooping"}, {"name": "Rihaakuru", "wiki": "Rihaakuru", "desc": "A thick, salty tuna paste made by boiling down fish stock, eaten in small amounts with rice and roshi. Very Maldivian, very strong.", "price": "Usually free as a side", "when": "Spooned on rice at home", "order": "Use only a little, it's very salty"}], "food_tip": "Eat at the local cafes and teashops where islanders go, rather than in guesthouse restaurants, and you'll pay a few dollars for short eats and tea. Most guesthouses include breakfast, and some offer cheap half-board deals.", "culture": ["The Maldives is a strictly Muslim country, so on local islands cover shoulders and knees away from the designated bikini beaches, and don't swim in swimwear on the town beach.", "Alcohol isn't sold on local islands and can't be brought into the country, so save the drinks for a resort day trip or a liveaboard boat.", "Pork, religious idols and anything seen as offensive to Islam are banned at customs.", "Friday is the day of prayer, so shops close around midday and many ferries don't run.", "During Ramadan, avoid eating or drinking in public in daylight, and expect cafes to open after sunset.", "Public displays of affection are frowned upon, so keep it low key on local islands."], "safety": "The Maldives is very safe, with low crime on local islands. Currents between islands can be strong, so snorkel with a guide or in sheltered spots, and don't touch or stand on coral. The sun is fierce on the equator, so use reef-safe sunscreen and drink loads of water.", "eat_beats": ["Afternoon tea time, around 4pm to 6pm, is when cafes fill up with trays of hedhikaa snacks.", "In local cafes, short eats come to the table on a plate and you pay only for what you eat.", "Tap water on many islands is desalinated or rainwater, so drink bottled or filtered water.", "Vegetarians will find it hard, since tuna is in almost everything; ask for vegetable curry or roshi with dhal.", "Resorts add 10% service and taxes, while local cafes don't expect tips."], "drinks": [{"name": "Kurumba", "desc": "Fresh young coconut water, drunk straight from the nut. About $1 to $2 on local islands."}, {"name": "Raa", "desc": "Sweet sap tapped from coconut palms, drunk fresh in the morning before it ferments. A cup costs $1 to $2 when you can find it."}], "greet": "Say assalaamu alaikum, and men usually shake hands with men. Don't offer a hand to someone of the opposite sex unless they do first.", "dress": "On local islands, cover shoulders and knees, and swim only in a bikini on designated tourist beaches. Resorts follow their own rules.", "table": "Locals often eat with the right hand, mixing rice and curry together. Wait to be served by your host and use your right hand to pass dishes.", "faith": "The Maldives is officially 100% Sunni Muslim, and Islam shapes public life. Visit mosques only with permission, dress modestly, remove shoes and don't visit during prayer times.", "phrases": [["Assalaamu alaikum (އައްސަލާމު ޢަލައިކުމް)", "Hello"], ["Shukuriyaa (ޝުކުރިއްޔާ)", "Thank you"], ["Kihineh? (ކިހިނެއް؟)", "How are you?"], ["Kihaa vareh? (ކިހާވަރެއް؟)", "How much?"], ["Aan (އާން)", "Yes"]]},
  "afghanistan": {"around": "Most visitors travel with a specialist operator who handles transport, permits and the many checkpoints, since road travel carries real risk. Domestic flights link Kabul, Herat, Mazar-i-Sharif and Bamiyan, and shared taxis run on main routes. There are no ride-hailing apps.", "money": "The currency is the afghani (AFN), and US dollars are also widely used for bigger payments. Afghanistan is cash only, foreign cards don't work and international ATMs are basically nonexistent, so bring all the US dollars you need in clean, newer notes and change them at money changers. Tipping drivers and guides is customary, and haggling is normal in bazaars.", "sleep": "Hotels are usually arranged by your tour operator, and the options are mostly simple hotels and guesthouses. Bamiyan has a few basic guesthouses with incredible views. Some hotels can't host foreigners, so book through people who know the current rules.", "eat": ["Kabuli pulao: rice with lamb, carrots and raisins", "Mantu: steamed dumplings topped with yogurt and lentils", "Bolani: thin flatbread stuffed with potato or leeks", "Green tea: served at every meal and meeting"], "data": "Roshan, AWCC, Etisalat and Salaam SIMs are sold with a passport, but mobile internet can be slow and is sometimes cut off entirely, so download offline maps and keep your operator's contacts saved.", "heads_up": "Never photograph people, especially women, or checkpoints without permission.", "food": [{"name": "Kabuli pulao", "wiki": "Kabuli pulao", "desc": "The national dish: rice cooked with lamb, topped with sweet carrots, raisins and nuts.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch, and for guests at home"}, {"name": "Mantu", "wiki": "Manti (food)", "desc": "Steamed dumplings filled with spiced meat, topped with yoghurt and lentil sauce.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Dinner and special occasions"}, {"name": "Bolani", "wiki": "Bolani", "desc": "Thin flatbread stuffed with potato, leeks or pumpkin, fried and sold as street food.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Afternoon snack from street sellers", "order": "Kachaloo is potato, gandana is leek"}, {"name": "Ashak", "wiki": "Aushak", "desc": "Dumplings filled with leeks, topped with yoghurt and meat sauce, often for special occasions.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Eid and family celebrations"}, {"name": "Green tea", "wiki": "Green tea", "desc": "Served at every meal and meeting, often with cardamom, sweets and dried fruit.", "price": "$0.20 to $1", "when": "All day, especially after meals", "order": "Ask for chai sabz, or chai siyah for black"}], "food_tip": "Chaikhanas (teahouses) and kebab stalls serve cheap, hearty meals, and bread from the naan bakeries is a filling snack. Ask your guide where locals eat.", "culture": ["Dress very conservatively: men in long trousers and long sleeves (a shalwar kameez helps you blend in), and women in loose, fully covering clothing with a headscarf, following current rules.", "Never photograph people, especially women, or checkpoints without permission, and be cautious photographing anything that looks official.", "Hospitality is a point of honour, so accept tea and food when offered, and you'll often find it hard to pay.", "Use your right hand for eating and greeting, and men shouldn't initiate any contact with women.", "Music, alcohol and public mixing of genders are heavily restricted under Taliban rule, so follow your guide and local guidance closely."], "safety": "Afghanistan has been under Taliban rule since 2021, and most governments advise against all travel there, with very limited or no consular help available. The risks include terrorism, kidnapping and arbitrary detention of foreigners, and women face severe restrictions on movement and dress. If you go, travel only with an experienced, reputable operator, register with your embassy where possible and check that your insurance actually covers you.", "eat_beats": ["Meals are often eaten sitting on cushions around a dastarkhan, a cloth spread on the floor.", "Drink only bottled or boiled water, and be careful with raw salads and dairy in summer.", "Vegetarian dishes like bolani, sabzi (spinach) and dal exist, but hospitality meals revolve around meat, so tell your guide early.", "Restaurants often seat women and families in a separate section, sometimes behind a curtain.", "Tipping isn't routine in chaikhanas, but rounding up and tipping guides and drivers is appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Doogh", "desc": "A salty yoghurt drink with dried mint, served with kebabs for about 30 to 60 cents."}, {"name": "Qaymaq chai", "desc": "Pink milk tea topped with thick clotted cream, made for weddings and guests. Teahouses that serve it charge around $1."}], "greet": "Men greet with salaam alaikum, a handshake and a hand on the heart, often followed by a hug. Unrelated men and women don't shake hands, so a nod and a hand on the heart is enough.", "dress": "Men should wear long sleeves and long trousers. Taliban rules require women to cover fully, sometimes including the face, so follow your guide on what's enforced locally.", "table": "Wash your hands before eating, eat from the part of the shared dish in front of you using your right hand or bread, and expect hosts to keep adding food. Sit so your feet don't point at people or food.", "faith": "Almost all Afghans are Muslim, mostly Sunni with a Shia minority, many of them Hazara. Ask before entering mosques or shrines, dress very modestly and avoid prayer times.", "phrases": [["Salaam alaikum (السلام علیکم)", "Hello"], ["Tashakor (تشکر)", "Thank you"], ["Chand ast? (چند است؟)", "How much is it?"], ["Khub hasti? (خوب هستی؟)", "How are you?"], ["Mazadar (مزه دار)", "Delicious"]]},
  "turkmenistan": {"around": "Turkmenistan is one of the hardest countries in the world to visit independently. A tourist visa needs a letter of invitation through an agency, and you'll travel with a guide and driver, which also covers the trip out to the Darvaza gas crater. The alternative is a short transit visa, which lets you cross the country on your own in a few days if you have onward visas sorted. Trains and domestic flights are very cheap but run on limited schedules, and shared taxis link the main towns.", "money": "The currency is the manat (TMT), and there's a big gap between the official exchange rate and the black-market rate, which your guide will probably mention quietly. Foreign cards rarely work and ATMs are unreliable at best, so bring all the US dollars you'll need in clean, crisp, recent notes. Tipping guides and drivers at the end of a trip is customary, and polite haggling is fine at bazaars like Altyn Asyr (Tolkuchka) in Ashgabat.", "sleep": "Ashgabat has mostly state-run hotels, some of them grand and nearly empty, and foreigners can only stay in places licensed to register them, so cheap guesthouses are rare. Tours usually camp in tents or yurts beside the Darvaza crater, which is a highlight in itself. In other towns, expect simple Soviet-era hotels.", "eat": ["Palaw: rice pilaf with meat and carrots", "Dograma: bread and meat stew, often for festivals", "Chorek: round flatbread baked in a clay oven", "Ishlekli: meat pie baked in hot sand"], "data": "TMCell is the main network, local SIMs are hard to get on a short visit and the internet is slow and heavily censored, with most VPNs blocked. Plan to be mostly offline and download maps and anything you need before you arrive.", "heads_up": "Photographing government buildings and officials in Ashgabat is not allowed, so keep the camera down unless your guide says it's fine.", "food": [{"name": "Palaw", "wiki": "Plov", "desc": "Rice pilaf with mutton, carrots and onion cooked in a big cauldron, the centrepiece of any celebration and an easy canteen lunch.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch, weddings and holidays"}, {"name": "Chorba", "wiki": "Chorba", "desc": "A hearty mutton and vegetable soup (also called shurpa), slurped with bread at canteens and roadside cafes.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch at canteens and roadside cafes", "order": "Tear bread into the bowl, like locals do"}, {"name": "Chorek", "wiki": "Tandyr nan", "desc": "Round flatbread baked in a tamdyr clay oven, served with every meal. Never put it upside down.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "With every meal"}, {"name": "Ishlekli", "wiki": "Ishlykly", "desc": "A meat and onion pie traditionally baked in hot desert sand, a speciality of nomadic herders.", "price": "$3 to $6 a slice", "when": "Desert camps and family gatherings", "order": "Ask your guide to arrange one at the crater"}, {"name": "Manty", "wiki": "Manti (food)", "desc": "Large steamed dumplings stuffed with meat and onion, served with sour cream or yoghurt.", "price": "$2 to $4 a plate", "when": "Lunch or dinner", "order": "Add yoghurt or sour cream"}, {"name": "Chal", "wiki": "Chal", "desc": "Fermented camel's milk, tangy and refreshing, drunk in the desert in summer.", "price": "$1 to $2 a glass", "when": "Summer, in desert villages", "order": "Start with a small sip"}], "food_tip": "Bazaars and simple canteens are the cheapest places to eat, and a plate of palaw or a few manty costs very little at street rates. Your tour usually includes meals at the crater.", "culture": ["Photographing government buildings, police, soldiers and the presidential palace is not allowed, and in Ashgabat even casual street photos can attract attention from police, so ask your guide.", "The president and his family are treated with great respect in public life, and criticism of the government can get locals into serious trouble, so keep political chat out of conversations.", "Dress modestly in rural areas and at mosques and mausoleums, covering shoulders and knees.", "Bread is sacred, so never throw it away or put it on the ground.", "Hospitality is generous, and you'll be offered green tea constantly. Accept with your right hand."], "safety": "Turkmenistan is safe for travellers in terms of crime, but it's a tightly controlled authoritarian state. Carry your passport and registration papers, expect police checks, and follow your guide's advice about photography and where you can go. Summer heat in the Karakum Desert is extreme, so bring water and sun protection.", "eat_beats": ["Most visitors travel with a required guide, who will often steer you to good local canteens.", "Don't drink tap water, and buy bottled water, which is cheap and easy to find.", "Vegetarians will struggle, as meat is central, but salads, bread, eggs and fruit are always available.", "Tipping isn't standard, but 5 to 10 percent for good service in Ashgabat restaurants is appreciated.", "Bring cash in clean US dollars and change it at official rates, as cards rarely work outside big hotels."], "drinks": [{"name": "Green tea (gok çay)", "desc": "Drunk all day from small bowls, often with sweets or dried fruit. A pot at a cafe costs under $1."}, {"name": "Agaran", "desc": "A tangy fermented milk drink like kefir, sold at bazaars for about $0.50 to $1."}], "greet": "Men shake hands, often with both hands as a sign of respect, and say salam. Wait for women to offer their hand first.", "dress": "Dress conservatively everywhere, with long trousers or skirts and covered shoulders. Women should bring a headscarf for mosques and shrines.", "table": "Meals are often eaten on a cloth (sachak) on the floor, so sit cross-legged and don't step over the food. Break bread by hand and never throw it away.", "faith": "Most Turkmen are Sunni Muslim, with Sufi shrines visited by pilgrims. At mosques and mausoleums, remove shoes, cover up, and follow locals walking around shrines.", "phrases": [["Salam", "Hello"], ["Sag boluň", "Thank you"], ["Näçe?", "How much?"], ["Gaty tagamly", "Very tasty"], ["Bolýar", "Okay"]]},
  "azerbaijan": {"around": "Most people fly into Baku, since the land borders have been closed to regular travelers for years, so check the current rules before planning an overland entry. Buses and marshrutkas run from Baku's main bus terminal to Sheki, Quba, Lahij and other towns for a few manat, and trains run to Ganja and a handful of other cities. In Baku the metro is dirt cheap with a BakiKart card, and Bolt and Uber are the easiest way to avoid haggling with taxi drivers.", "money": "The currency is the manat (AZN), and it has been pretty stable against the dollar for years. Cards work in most Baku cafes, supermarkets and hotels, but once you head out to Sheki, Gabala or the mountain villages you'll want cash. ATMs are everywhere in Baku and in regional towns, and fees are usually small. Restaurants often add a 5 to 10 percent service charge to the bill, so check before you tip, and haggling is normal at bazaars but not in shops.", "sleep": "Baku has a decent spread of hostels, mostly in and around the Old City (Icherisheher) and near Fountain Square, with dorm beds at very fair prices. Outside the capital, family guesthouses are the move, especially in Sheki, Lahij and Xinaliq, where your host will usually feed you too.", "eat": ["Plov: saffron rice with meat, chestnuts and dried fruit", "Dolma: grape leaves stuffed with spiced meat and rice", "Qutab: thin flatbread filled with greens or meat", "Piti: lamb and chickpea stew baked in a clay pot"], "data": "Azercell and Bakcell SIMs are cheap and sold at the airport and city shops with your passport, though a phone may need registering if you stay long. Coverage is solid in Baku and towns, and hostel and cafe wifi is decent.", "heads_up": "Travel to the Karabakh region and close to the Armenian border is restricted and needs special permission, so check the latest rules before you plan anything out that way.", "food": [{"name": "Plov", "wiki": "Pilaf", "desc": "Saffron rice served with lamb or chicken, chestnuts and dried fruit, and the showpiece shah plov is baked inside a crust of lavash. It turns up at every celebration and on most restaurant menus.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Weddings and special family meals", "order": "Shah plov is baked inside a crispy crust"}, {"name": "Dolma", "wiki": "Dolma", "desc": "Vine leaves, cabbage or vegetables stuffed with spiced minced meat and rice, usually with a dollop of garlicky yogurt. A cheap, filling lunch in any local eatery.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch or dinner, as a main", "order": "Try both grape leaf and cabbage versions"}, {"name": "Qutab", "wiki": "Qutab", "desc": "Paper-thin flatbread folded around greens, pumpkin or minced meat and cooked on a griddle. Street stalls and cafes sell them for pocket change, perfect for a quick snack.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 each", "when": "Snack from bakeries and cafés", "order": "Get greens and meat, or pumpkin in autumn"}, {"name": "Piti", "wiki": "Piti (food)", "desc": "Sheki's famous mutton and chickpea stew, slow-cooked in a clay pot. You pour the broth over bread first, then eat the meat and veg as a second course.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Lunch in Sheki, cooked in clay pots", "order": "Pour the broth over bread first, then eat meat"}, {"name": "Lula kebab", "wiki": "Lula kebab", "desc": "Minced lamb pressed onto a skewer and grilled over coals, served with flatbread, raw onion and sumac. Every grill house has it and it's one of the best value meals going.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Dinner, grilled over charcoal", "order": "Comes wrapped in lavash with sumac onions"}, {"name": "Sheki halva", "wiki": "Sheki halva", "desc": "A sticky, lattice-patterned sweet of rice flour, nuts and syrup from the town of Sheki. Buy a slab from the shops near the caravanserai to go with your tea.", "price": "$2 to $4 a piece", "when": "With tea, bought in Sheki"}], "food_tip": "Skip the touristy Old City restaurants and eat at local kebab houses and cafes called yeməkxana, where a big meal with bread and tea costs a fraction of the price. Tea is cheap, endless and served with jam, so it's the default way to rest your feet.", "culture": ["Azerbaijan is a majority Shia Muslim country but very secular day to day, especially in Baku. Dress is relaxed in the capital, but cover shoulders and knees in mosques and conservative rural areas, and women should carry a scarf for mosque visits.", "Tea culture is huge, and being offered tea is a sign of welcome, so accept a glass if you can. It usually comes with lumps of sugar and homemade jam on the side.", "Hospitality is taken seriously, and guests are often fed far more than they can eat. It's polite to try everything, and refusing outright can come across as rude, so take a little and say thank you (sag ol).", "Relations with Armenia are a very sensitive topic, so don't bring it up casually and avoid praising Armenia. Armenian visa stamps aren't usually a problem, but a history of travel to Nagorno-Karabakh without permission can be.", "Men greet each other with a handshake, and close male friends often hug. Wait for a woman to offer her hand first."], "safety": "Azerbaijan is generally very safe, with low street crime and friendly people. The main hassles are taxi drivers overcharging tourists, so use Bolt or Uber, and occasional police checks, so keep your passport or a copy on you. Avoid photographing government buildings, military sites or the oil infrastructure around Baku.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is between 1pm and 3pm, and dinner after 7pm, with tea at every pause in between.", "Tap water in Baku is treated, but most locals and visitors drink bottled water.", "Vegetarians can find qutab with greens, salads, eggplant dishes and lentil soup, though most mains are meat.", "Bazaars like Baku's Yashil Bazaar sell dried fruit, cheese, pickles and fresh bread for cheap picnics.", "Tipping 5% to 10% is common in Baku restaurants, but check if a service charge is already on the bill."], "drinks": [{"name": "Ayran", "desc": "A cooling salted yoghurt drink, also mixed into dovga, a hot yoghurt and herb soup. About $1 a glass."}, {"name": "Sherbet", "desc": "A sweet chilled drink made with fruit, rose water and basil seeds, served at feasts. About $1 to $2."}], "greet": "Say \"salam\" and shake hands, and close male friends often kiss on the cheek. Older people are greeted first.", "dress": "Baku is stylish and modern, so smart casual fits in well. In rural areas and mosques, cover shoulders and knees, and women should wear a headscarf.", "table": "Tea comes before and after the meal, and you may be offered vodka toasts at a feast. Try a bit of everything, and leave something on your plate when full.", "faith": "Most people are Shia Muslim, with a Sunni minority, though society is largely secular. At mosques remove shoes, dress modestly, keep your voice down and avoid prayer times.", "phrases": [["Salam", "Hello"], ["Sağ ol", "Thank you"], ["Neçəyədir?", "How much?"], ["Acı olmasın", "Not spicy"], ["Nuş olsun", "Enjoy your meal"]]},
  "iran": {"around": "Iran's VIP buses are cheap, comfortable and run between every city on the classic Tehran to Kashan to Isfahan to Yazd to Shiraz loop. Overnight trains are a fun alternative and save you a night's accommodation, but berths sell out around Nowruz in March, so get your guesthouse to book. Snapp is the local ride-hailing app and works brilliantly, but you need an Iranian SIM, and foreign apps like Uber don't operate. Domestic flights are cheap for long jumps if you pay in cash through a local agent.", "money": "Iran is fully cut off from international banking, so foreign cards don't work at all and you need to bring every bit of your trip money in cash, usually US dollars or euros. Change it at licensed exchange offices in the cities, where the rate is far better than the official one, and only change what you need as the rial can move fast. Prices are usually quoted in tomans (one toman is 10 rials), so always double-check which one someone means. Some hostels and tour agencies sell prepaid tourist debit cards, handy so you're not carrying a huge wad around, and light haggling is normal in bazaars.", "sleep": "The classic budget stay is a traditional courtyard house turned guesthouse, especially in Yazd, Kashan and Isfahan, where you get a dorm bed or simple room around a fountain for cheap. Tehran, Shiraz and Isfahan also have a few proper backpacker hostels. Couchsurfing has a long tradition here but hosting foreigners can get locals in trouble, so think twice.", "eat": ["Chelo kebab: grilled minced meat kebab with saffron rice", "Ghormeh sabzi: herb and kidney bean stew with lamb", "Fesenjan: chicken stewed in walnut and pomegranate sauce", "Dizi: lamb and chickpea stew, mashed at the table"], "data": "MCI and Irancell tourist SIMs are sold at Tehran's airport with your passport and work well in cities. Instagram, WhatsApp and many foreign sites are blocked and VPNs are widely used, and the government has repeatedly cut the internet entirely during unrest, so tell people at home you may go quiet.", "heads_up": "Women are legally required to wear a headscarf in public, and everyone should avoid photographing government buildings or anything military.", "food": [{"name": "Chelo kebab", "wiki": "Chelow kabab", "desc": "Grilled minced meat kebabs on a mountain of saffron rice with a charred tomato and raw onion. The national lunch, served in simple kebab houses for very little.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch at kebab houses", "order": "Koobideh is the classic; sprinkle sumac on top"}, {"name": "Ghormeh sabzi", "wiki": "Ghormeh sabzi", "desc": "A dark, tangy stew of slow-cooked herbs, kidney beans, dried lime and lamb. Many Iranians swear it's the true national dish.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Home lunches and set menus"}, {"name": "Fesenjan", "wiki": "Fesenjān", "desc": "Chicken or duck simmered in a thick, sweet and sour sauce of ground walnuts and pomegranate molasses. Rich and unforgettable, look for it in traditional restaurants.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Special occasions and winter dinners"}, {"name": "Dizi", "wiki": "Abgoosht", "desc": "Lamb, chickpea and potato stew cooked in a stone pot. You drain the broth over bread, then mash the rest at the table with a pestle, a proper ritual.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Lunch at traditional dizi houses", "order": "Drink the broth with bread first, then mash the rest"}, {"name": "Tahdig", "wiki": "Scorched rice", "desc": "The golden, crispy crust from the bottom of the rice pot, and everyone fights over it. Order it as a side wherever you see it on the menu.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Fought over at every family meal", "order": "Try potato or flatbread tahdig, not just rice"}, {"name": "Ash reshteh", "wiki": "Aush reshteh", "desc": "A thick noodle, bean and herb soup topped with whey, fried onion and mint. Sold cheaply from street stalls and great on a cold day.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Winter evenings and Nowruz", "order": "Comes topped with kashk (whey) and fried mint"}], "food_tip": "Lunch is the main meal, so eat your big meal at a local kebab house or sofrekhaneh at midday when it's cheapest and freshest. Bakeries selling sangak or barbari flatbread straight from the oven are a budget lifesaver.", "culture": ["Women are legally required to cover their hair in public, and both men and women should keep shoulders and knees covered, with long sleeves and loose clothing safest. Enforcement has relaxed in big cities in recent years, but as a visitor you should still follow the law, and expect stricter dress at shrines where women may need a chador.", "Taarof is a complex politeness ritual where people refuse things or offer them insistently out of courtesy, like a taxi driver saying it's free. Always offer to pay again, usually two or three times, before accepting that something really is free.", "The thumbs-up gesture has historically been rude in Iran, so avoid it, especially with older people. Don't blow your nose loudly at the dinner table either.", "Iranians are famously hospitable and curious, and you'll be invited for tea and meals a lot. Be warm, but keep political talk low-key in public, as you never know who's listening.", "Alcohol is illegal, as is public mixing that crosses local norms, so don't seek out booze and avoid public displays of affection. Take shoes off when entering homes and the carpeted areas of shrines."], "safety": "Iranians are very welcoming and day-to-day street crime is low, but the bigger risk is political: many Western governments advise against all travel, foreigners, especially dual nationals, have been detained, and the region has seen open conflict in recent years. Check your government's advisory and your insurance before you go, avoid demonstrations, and never photograph anything military, government or near borders. Watch for motorbike bag snatches in Tehran and keep your cash split up.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is around 1 to 3pm, and dinner is late, often 9pm or after.", "Tap water is safe in most cities, though many travellers stick to bottled.", "Vegetarians can find kuku (herb frittata), mirza ghasemi and ash, but many stews use meat, so ask.", "Tipping around 10 percent is appreciated in restaurants, though some add a service charge.", "Alcohol is banned, so drink doogh, fresh juices and non-alcoholic beer instead."], "drinks": [{"name": "Doogh", "desc": "A salty, fizzy yogurt drink with dried mint, served with kebabs. About $0.50 a bottle."}, {"name": "Chai", "desc": "Black tea drunk all day from small glasses, often sipped through a sugar cube. A pot in a teahouse costs $1 to $2."}], "greet": "'Salaam' with a handshake between men, often with a hand on the heart. Between men and women, wait for them to offer a hand, as many won't.", "dress": "Women should carry a headscarf at all times and wear a long, loose top over trousers, and men should avoid shorts. At big shrines, women are lent a chador at the entrance.", "table": "Meals at home are often served on a cloth (sofreh) on the floor, so sit cross-legged and don't step on it. Hosts will press food on you, so refuse once politely, then accept.", "faith": "Most Iranians are Shia Muslim, with Sunni, Christian, Zoroastrian and Jewish minorities. At shrines, remove shoes, stay quiet, and know that non-Muslims may be kept out of the inner sanctuary.", "phrases": [["Salaam (سلام)", "Hello"], ["Mamnoon (ممنون)", "Thank you"], ["Chand e? (چنده؟)", "How much is it?"], ["Khoshmaze (خوشمزه)", "Delicious"], ["Khasteh nabashid (خسته نباشید)", "Hope you're not tired (polite greeting)"]]},
  "iraq": {"around": "Shared taxis are the main way between cities, leaving from city garages when they fill up, and they're cheap and fairly quick. Minibuses cover shorter hops, and Careem and local apps work in Baghdad and Erbil, which avoids fare arguments. Expect lots of checkpoints, especially crossing between the Kurdistan Region and federal Iraq, so carry your passport and visa paperwork at all times. Iraqi Airways and others fly between Baghdad, Erbil and Basra if you want to skip a long road day.", "money": "Iraq runs on cash, mostly dinars, with US dollars widely accepted for bigger amounts like hotels and drivers. ATMs exist in Erbil, Sulaymaniyah and Baghdad, but foreign cards fail often, so bring enough crisp US dollar notes to cover your trip and change them at exchange shops. Tipping is small and informal, like rounding up for drivers, and light haggling is fine in markets.", "sleep": "Budget hotels in city centers are the standard option, as proper hostels are still rare, though Erbil, Sulaymaniyah and Baghdad each have one or two backpacker-friendly spots. In the Kurdish mountains you can find simple guesthouses, and camping is possible in valleys like Rawanduz if you ask locals first.", "eat": ["Masgouf: butterflied carp slow-grilled beside an open fire", "Kubba: fried or boiled bulgur shells stuffed with spiced meat", "Dolma: vine leaves and vegetables stuffed with rice and meat", "Kahi with geymar: flaky pastry with clotted cream and date syrup"], "data": "Asiacell and Zain cover federal Iraq, while Korek and Asiacell are strongest in Kurdistan, and SIMs need your passport. Data is cheap and fast in cities but weak in remote areas, and some Kurdistan SIMs struggle further south.", "heads_up": "Carry your passport at all times for the many checkpoints, and never photograph soldiers, police or checkpoints.", "food": [{"name": "Masgouf", "wiki": "Masgouf", "desc": "Carp butterflied and slow-grilled upright beside an open fire, served with flatbread, pickles and onion. The Abu Nuwas riverside in Baghdad is the classic place to try it.", "price": "$10 to $20 per fish", "when": "Evening, at riverside grills", "order": "You pick your live carp, then wait an hour"}, {"name": "Kubba", "wiki": "Kibbeh", "desc": "Bulgur or rice shells stuffed with spiced meat, either deep-fried or simmered in soup. Mosul style kubba is flat and huge, and you'll find kubba stalls everywhere.", "price": "$0.50 to $2", "when": "Snack, from street stalls", "order": "Try kubba halab, a rice version from Baghdad"}, {"name": "Dolma", "wiki": "Dolma", "desc": "Iraqi dolma is a whole pot of vine leaves, onions, peppers and zucchini stuffed with rice and meat and cooked in a tangy sauce. Often a family feast, but local restaurants serve it too.", "price": "$3 to $7", "when": "Lunch, at family gatherings and restaurants", "order": "The best bits are the tangy onions at the bottom"}, {"name": "Quzi", "wiki": "Quzi", "desc": "Slow-roasted lamb on a heap of spiced rice with nuts and raisins. Big plates for sharing, served at weddings and in good local restaurants.", "price": "$8 to $15", "when": "Lunch for groups and celebrations", "order": "Order a shared platter for two or more"}, {"name": "Samoon", "wiki": "Samoon", "desc": "Iraq's diamond-shaped bread, baked in stone ovens and sold hot for almost nothing. It gets stuffed with falafel, eggs or kebab for the cheapest meal in the country.", "price": "$0.10 to $0.30", "when": "Breakfast and snacks, from bakeries", "order": "Fill it with fried egg and aubergine for a cheap meal"}, {"name": "Kleicha", "wiki": "Kleicha", "desc": "Cardamom-scented cookies filled with dates or nuts, considered the national biscuit. Great with the strong, sweet tea served in small glasses on every street.", "price": "$1 to $3 a bag", "when": "Eid and with afternoon tea"}], "food_tip": "Street food is the budget move: falafel or egg and eggplant in samoon from a stall costs pennies. Tea houses (chaikhana) are where locals hang out, and a glass of sweet tea costs next to nothing.", "culture": ["Iraq is a conservative Muslim country, with both Shia and Sunni communities plus Christians and Yazidis. Dress modestly everywhere, with long trousers for men, and women should wear loose, long clothing and carry a scarf, which becomes a full abaya at major Shia shrines in Karbala and Najaf.", "Hospitality is intense, and you'll be invited for tea, meals and even to stay over. Accept a cup of tea if offered, and try to reciprocate with small gifts or by paying for a round of tea where you can.", "Iraqi Kurdistan has its own identity and language, so refer to it as Kurdistan or the Kurdistan Region, and be aware that politics between Erbil and Baghdad is a touchy subject.", "Eat and pass things with your right hand, and don't point the soles of your feet at people when sitting on the floor. During Ramadan, don't eat, drink or smoke in public during daylight.", "Men greet each other with handshakes and often cheek kisses, but wait for a woman to offer her hand first."], "safety": "Security has improved a lot, and Kurdistan in particular feels relaxed, but many governments still advise against travel to parts of federal Iraq, so read the advisory and check your insurance. Stick to areas recommended by locals and current travelers, avoid border regions with Iran, Turkey and Syria, and stay away from protests. Petty crime is low, the bigger daily risks are road safety and summer heat, which regularly passes 45C.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, often around 2pm, and dinner can start well after 9pm.", "Don't drink tap water; bottled water is cheap and sold everywhere.", "Vegetarians can live well on falafel, fried aubergine, lentil soup and salads.", "Iraqi Kurdistan uses the same dinar, and cash is king, so carry small notes.", "Tipping isn't expected at stalls, but around 10 percent is appreciated in restaurants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Chai", "desc": "Strong, very sweet black tea in small istikan glasses, often with cardamom, sold at chaikhanas for about $0.25."}, {"name": "Shineena", "desc": "A cold, slightly salty yoghurt drink, served with kebab and rice for under $1."}], "greet": "\"As-salamu alaykum\" with a handshake is standard among men, often followed by a long exchange of how are you. In Kurdistan, \"silaw\" (hello) goes down well.", "dress": "Men should wear long trousers, and women long, loose clothing with a scarf for religious sites. Black abayas are required at the main Shia shrines.", "table": "Hosts pile food on your plate, so take small portions at first. It's polite to accept tea and refuse a few refills before saying you're done by shaking the glass.", "faith": "Iraq is mainly Shia and Sunni Muslim, with Christian, Yazidi and Mandaean minorities. At shrines, remove shoes, women must cover fully, and non-Muslims may be restricted from inner areas.", "phrases": [["Salam alaykum (السلام عليكم)", "Hello"], ["Shukran (شكرا)", "Thank you"], ["Beesh? (بيش)", "How much? (Iraqi Arabic)"], ["Supas (سوپاس)", "Thank you (Kurdish)"], ["Kulish zein (كلش زين)", "Very good"]]},
  "lebanon": {"around": "Shared taxis called service and minibuses are the cheap way around, picking you up on the main roads and leaving from hubs like Charles Helou and Cola in Beirut. Uber and Bolt work in Beirut and the coast, and avoid a lot of haggling. There are no trains, but the country is tiny, so day trips to Byblos, Batroun, Tripoli or Baalbek by minibus are easy. Agree a price with taxi drivers before getting in, and say service if you want the shared fare.", "money": "After the financial crash, Lebanon runs mostly on cash US dollars, and many places quote prices in dollars with change sometimes given in Lebanese pounds. Bring clean dollar notes, as local ATMs are unreliable for foreign cards, though some now dispense dollars. Cards work at bigger hotels and some restaurants in Beirut, often with a surcharge. Tipping about 10 percent is normal in restaurants, and haggling is fine in souks.", "sleep": "Beirut's hostels cluster in Gemmayzeh, Mar Mikhael and Hamra, with dorm beds at mid-range prices for the region. Batroun and Byblos have guesthouses, and up in the mountains and the Qadisha Valley you can stay in family guesthouses or simple monastery lodgings. Camping and guesthouses along the Lebanon Mountain Trail are great for hikers.", "eat": ["Man'oushe: flatbread topped with za'atar and olive oil, a breakfast staple", "Tabbouleh: parsley, bulgur, tomato and lemon salad", "Kibbeh: bulgur and minced meat, fried, baked or raw", "Ka'ak: sesame bread purse sold from street carts"], "data": "Alfa and Touch are the two networks, and prepaid SIMs need your passport, but data is pricey by regional standards, so many travelers just use an eSIM. Power cuts are common, which can knock out wifi, though most places run generators.", "heads_up": "Army checkpoints are common, so carry your passport, and ask before taking photos anywhere near soldiers or military posts.", "food": [{"name": "Man'oushe", "wiki": "Manakish", "desc": "Flatbread baked with za'atar and olive oil, or cheese, from neighborhood bakeries in the morning. It's the cheapest, best breakfast in the country.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Breakfast, from neighbourhood bakeries", "order": "Ask for half za'atar, half cheese"}, {"name": "Tabbouleh", "wiki": "Tabbouleh", "desc": "A bright salad that's mostly finely chopped parsley with tomato, mint, a little bulgur and lots of lemon. Part of any mezze spread.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Lunch or dinner, as part of mezze", "order": "Eat it scooped up with lettuce leaves"}, {"name": "Kibbeh nayyeh", "wiki": "Kibbeh nayyeh", "desc": "Raw minced lamb mixed with bulgur and spices, drizzled with olive oil and eaten with mint and raw onion. A mountain village specialty, best where it's made fresh.", "price": "$8 to $14", "when": "Weekend lunches and family feasts", "order": "Order it only where it's made fresh daily"}, {"name": "Falafel", "wiki": "Falafel", "desc": "Crispy chickpea and fava fritters stuffed into thin bread with tahini, pickles and parsley. Beirut's falafel joints sell sandwiches for a couple of dollars.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch or a quick snack", "order": "Add extra pickles and hot sauce"}, {"name": "Ka'ak", "wiki": "Ka'ak", "desc": "Purse-shaped sesame bread sold from street carts, often filled with za'atar or processed cheese. A classic Beirut snack on the go.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast or snack, from carts", "order": "Ask for it with za'atar, the classic filling"}, {"name": "Knafeh", "wiki": "Knafeh", "desc": "Hot, stretchy sweet cheese under semolina or shredded pastry, soaked in syrup and often served in a sesame bun for breakfast. Tripoli does it best.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Breakfast in a sesame bun, or dessert", "order": "Eat it hot, ka'ak style, in Tripoli"}], "food_tip": "Bakeries, falafel shops and shawarma counters will keep you fed for a few dollars a meal. Mezze at a sit-down restaurant can add up fast, so share plates with others.", "culture": ["Lebanon is a patchwork of 18 official religious communities, mainly Christian, Sunni, Shia and Druze. Beirut feels liberal and fashionable, but dress more conservatively in the south, the Bekaa, Tripoli and at mosques, where women need a headscarf and covered arms and legs.", "Politics, religion and the civil war are deeply personal topics, so listen more than you talk and avoid taking sides. Hezbollah, Israel and refugees are particularly sensitive.", "Lebanese hospitality is generous and coffee or tea will be offered often, so accept it. Greetings often involve three cheek kisses between friends, but stick to a handshake unless it's offered.", "People switch between Arabic, French and English, often in the same sentence. A bonjour or marhaba goes down well.", "Bargaining is expected in souks, but keep it friendly and don't haggle hard over small sums once you've agreed a price."], "safety": "Beirut and the main tourist sites have been generally calm, but the south and parts of the Bekaa have seen Israeli strikes despite the ceasefire, and many governments advise against travel near the southern border. Check the latest advisory before going and avoid protests and areas near Palestinian camps. Petty crime is low, but drivers are wild, so be careful crossing roads.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch from 1pm to 3pm is the main meal, and Sunday lunches with family can last hours.", "Bakeries (furn) are where locals start the day, and you can bring your own toppings to some for baking.", "Don't drink tap water; bottled and filtered water are cheap and everywhere.", "Vegetarians eat very well thanks to mezze like hummus, moutabal, fattoush and lentil dishes such as mujaddara.", "Leave 10% to 15% in restaurants and cafes, as service staff rely on tips; prices are usually in US dollars."], "drinks": [{"name": "Arak", "desc": "An aniseed spirit mixed with water and ice, turning milky white, and drunk with mezze. A glass costs about $3 to $6."}, {"name": "Jallab", "desc": "A sweet drink of grape molasses, date syrup and rose water, served over ice with pine nuts. Expect $2 to $4."}], "greet": "Say marhaba, bonjour or hi, often all at once, and shake hands. Friends kiss three times on the cheeks, alternating sides.", "dress": "Beirut is fashionable and relaxed, but cover shoulders and knees in religious and rural areas, especially in the south and Bekaa. Women should bring a scarf for mosques.", "table": "Mezze comes in waves and is shared, so pace yourself. The host will insist you eat more, so leave a little food on your plate when full.", "faith": "Lebanon has 18 recognised religious communities, mainly Muslim (Sunni and Shia) and Christian (mostly Maronite), plus Druze. Cover up at mosques and churches, remove shoes at mosques and avoid visiting during prayers.", "phrases": [["Marhaba (مرحبا)", "Hello"], ["Shukran (شكرا)", "Thank you"], ["Addeh? (قدّيه؟)", "How much?"], ["Kifak? (كيفك؟)", "How are you? (to a man)"], ["Yalla (يلا)", "Let's go, come on"]]},
  "syria": {"around": "Since the fall of the Assad government in late 2024, more travelers have been visiting independently, but plenty still use a local operator who arranges drivers and handles checkpoints. Intercity buses and microbuses link Damascus, Homs, Hama and Aleppo cheaply, and taxis are inexpensive inside cities, though you'll need to agree fares first. Don't head to rural areas, the north-east, the coast or the south without current, local advice, as the situation changes quickly.", "money": "Foreign cards still basically don't work in Syria, even after many sanctions were eased, so bring all your money as clean, newer US dollar or euro notes. Change it at official exchange offices or with your hotel, and only change small amounts at a time as rates move. The currency has been going through a redenomination, so old and new notes may both be around, and it pays to check which one a price refers to. Small tips for drivers and guides are expected, and haggling is normal in the souks.", "sleep": "Small hotels and a few lovely courtyard houses in old Damascus and Aleppo are the usual stays, and prices are low by regional standards. A handful of hostels and guesthouses have popped up in Damascus as visitor numbers grow. Many travelers book through their operator, which also helps with power and water issues.", "eat": ["Shawarma: spit-roasted chicken or meat wrapped in thin bread", "Fatteh: chickpeas, yogurt and toasted bread with tahini", "Kebab karaz: Aleppo kebab cooked in sour cherry sauce", "Muhammara: roasted red pepper and walnut dip"], "data": "Syriatel and MTN SIMs are sold with your passport, usually from official shops or with help from your guide or hotel. Coverage is fair in cities but slow, and power cuts often take wifi down with them.", "heads_up": "Never photograph checkpoints, soldiers or security buildings, and follow your guide's or host's advice on where it's safe to go.", "food": [{"name": "Shawarma", "wiki": "Shawarma", "desc": "Spit-roasted chicken or meat rolled in thin bread with garlic sauce and pickles. Every street has a stand, and it's one of the cheapest meals going.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Evening and late night, from street stands", "order": "Ask for it arabi, sliced and toasted on a plate"}, {"name": "Fatteh", "wiki": "Fatteh", "desc": "Layers of toasted bread, chickpeas, yogurt and tahini, topped with pine nuts and hot butter. A big Damascus breakfast, sold in specialist shops.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Friday breakfast", "order": "Choose chickpea (hummus) or meat (lahmeh) fatteh"}, {"name": "Cherry kebab", "wiki": "Cherry kebab", "desc": "Aleppo's famous kebab karaz, meatballs cooked in a tart sour cherry sauce over bread. Look for it in Aleppo's traditional restaurants.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Late spring, in sour cherry season"}, {"name": "Muhammara", "wiki": "Muhammara", "desc": "A smoky dip of roasted red peppers, walnuts and pomegranate molasses from Aleppo. A standard on mezze tables.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "As part of a mezze spread"}, {"name": "Ful medames", "wiki": "Ful medames", "desc": "Slow-cooked fava beans mashed with olive oil, lemon and garlic, scooped up with bread. A cheap, filling breakfast all over the country.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast, from ful shops"}, {"name": "Halawet el jibn", "wiki": "Halawet el jibn", "desc": "Sweet cheese rolls filled with clotted cream and topped with syrup and pistachios. Hama and Homs are famous for them.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Dessert, from sweet shops"}], "food_tip": "Street food like shawarma, falafel and ful costs almost nothing, and local restaurants are cheap even for a full mezze. Old city cafes are great value for tea and a nargileh session too.", "culture": ["Syria is mostly Sunni Muslim with Alawite, Druze, Christian and other communities, and sectarian tension is very real after the war. Keep away from political and religious arguments and listen more than you speak.", "Dress modestly: long trousers for men, and loose clothing covering arms and legs for women. Women need a headscarf at mosques like the Umayyad Mosque, where an abaya is often provided at the entrance.", "Syrians are known for warm hospitality, and you'll be offered coffee, tea and meals constantly. Accept graciously and use your right hand when eating or passing things.", "Many people have lost family, homes and livelihoods, so be sensitive about photographing destruction and ask before you photograph people.", "During Ramadan, avoid eating, drinking or smoking in public during daylight hours."], "safety": "Most Western governments still advise against all travel to Syria, which usually means travel insurance won't cover you, so think it through carefully. Violence has flared in some regions since the change of government, and unexploded ordnance is a serious danger in former frontline areas, so stay on roads and paths. Follow local advice closely, check the news daily, and avoid crowds and demonstrations.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is light, lunch around 2 to 4pm is the main meal, and dinner is often late and smaller.", "Don't drink tap water; bottled water is cheap and sold everywhere.", "Vegetarians eat very well on mezze like hummus, baba ghanoush, tabbouleh, fattoush and falafel.", "Foreign cards rarely work, so carry cash for meals, ideally clean dollars or euros changed into Syrian pounds.", "Tipping around 10 percent in restaurants is appreciated, and small change is normal in cafés."], "drinks": [{"name": "Jallab", "desc": "A sweet drink of grape molasses, rose water and dates, served over ice with pine nuts. A glass is about $1."}, {"name": "Erk sous", "desc": "Cold liquorice root drink poured from a big brass jug by street sellers in old Damascus, for well under $1."}], "greet": "Say salaam alaikum or marhaba, and shake hands with people of the same gender. Greetings are long, with questions about health and family.", "dress": "Loose, long clothing works best, and cities are more relaxed than rural areas. Carry a scarf for mosques and shrines.", "table": "Eat with your right hand, tearing bread to scoop mezze, and expect hosts to insist you eat more. Saying sahtein (to your health) before or after eating is polite.", "faith": "Most Syrians are Sunni Muslim, with Alawite, Druze, Ismaili and Christian communities. At mosques and shrines like Sayyida Zaynab remove shoes, cover up (women are often lent a robe) and avoid prayer times.", "phrases": [["Marhaba (مرحبا)", "Hello"], ["Shukran (شكرا)", "Thank you"], ["Addeish? (قديش؟)", "How much?"], ["Sahtein (صحتين)", "Enjoy your meal"], ["Yalla (يلا)", "Let's go"]]},
  "israel": {"around": "Buses and trains are frequent and fairly cheap, and you pay with a Rav-Kav card or by tapping a payment app, with Moovit the go-to app for routes. Almost all public transport stops from Friday afternoon until Saturday evening for Shabbat, though shared sherut minibuses keep running on some main routes. Gett and Yango are the taxi apps, and Uber is limited. The country is small, so most places are day trips from Tel Aviv or Jerusalem.", "money": "The currency is the shekel (ILS or NIS), and cards and phone payments are accepted almost everywhere, even at market stalls. ATMs are easy to find, but avoid the private cash machines in touristy areas with bad rates. Israel is expensive, roughly on par with western Europe. Tipping 10 to 15 percent is expected in restaurants, and bargaining is the norm in markets like Jerusalem's Old City and Carmel Market in Tel Aviv.", "sleep": "Hostels with dorms are spread across Tel Aviv, Jerusalem, Haifa, Tiberias, Eilat and the Galilee, and they're the main way to keep costs down, though even dorms aren't cheap. Some hostels in Jerusalem offer cheaper rooftop beds, and camping is allowed on many beaches and at designated night campsites. Book well ahead for Jewish holidays.", "eat": ["Sabich: pita with fried eggplant, boiled egg and tahini", "Shakshuka: eggs poached in spicy tomato and pepper sauce", "Hummus: chickpea and tahini dip, eaten as a full meal", "Falafel: fried chickpea balls stuffed into pita with salads"], "data": "An eSIM is easiest, and prepaid tourist SIMs from Partner, Cellcom and others are sold at the airport and phone shops. Coverage is excellent and free wifi is everywhere.", "heads_up": "Find out where the nearest shelter is wherever you stay, and install the official Home Front Command alerts app so you get warnings.", "food": [{"name": "Sabich", "wiki": "Sabich", "desc": "Pita stuffed with fried eggplant, hard-boiled egg, tahini, salad and spicy amba mango pickle. Best from a busy street stall in Tel Aviv or Ramat Gan.", "price": "$9 to $13", "when": "Lunch or late night", "order": "Ask for amba if you like tangy flavours"}, {"name": "Shakshuka", "wiki": "Shakshouka", "desc": "Eggs poached in a spicy tomato and pepper sauce, served in the pan with bread for mopping up. A breakfast staple in cafes everywhere.", "price": "$12 to $18", "when": "Breakfast or brunch at cafes", "order": "Ask for it with feta or eggplant"}, {"name": "Hummus", "wiki": "Hummus", "desc": "Served warm and silky as a full meal, with chickpeas, olive oil and pita, in hummusiyas that often close once they sell out. Go early.", "price": "$8 to $12", "when": "Lunch, often before 2pm", "order": "Try it with ful or masabacha"}, {"name": "Falafel", "wiki": "Falafel", "desc": "Fried chickpea balls in pita with salads, pickles and tahini, and most stands let you fill up on extra salad. One of the cheapest meals going.", "price": "$5 to $9", "when": "Lunch or a quick snack", "order": "Add fries inside the pita, a local favourite"}, {"name": "Bourekas", "wiki": "Bourekas", "desc": "Flaky filo or puff pastries filled with cheese, potato or spinach, sold at every bakery. A great budget breakfast or snack.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Breakfast or snack", "order": "Try them with grated tomato and egg"}, {"name": "Jachnun", "wiki": "Jachnun", "desc": "Rolled Yemeni-Jewish pastry baked slowly overnight and eaten on Saturday mornings with grated tomato and a hard-boiled egg. Look for it at weekend markets and Yemeni eateries.", "price": "$8 to $12", "when": "Saturday morning breakfast", "order": "Dip it in the zhug hot sauce"}], "food_tip": "Live on falafel, sabich and hummus, and do a big supermarket shop for breakfast and snacks. Many hostels include breakfast, which helps a lot in an expensive country.", "culture": ["Shabbat runs from Friday sunset to Saturday sunset, when much of Jerusalem shuts down, though Tel Aviv stays lively. Plan your transport and food around it.", "Dress modestly at holy sites: shoulders and knees covered for the Western Wall, churches and mosques, and women may need to cover their hair at some sites. In ultra-Orthodox neighborhoods like Mea Shearim dress conservatively and don't photograph people.", "Israelis are famously direct, which can feel blunt, but it's rarely meant as rude. Queues can be chaotic, so hold your ground politely.", "Politics, the conflict and the war in Gaza are extremely emotive subjects with strong views on all sides, so think before starting those conversations.", "Kosher rules mean many restaurants don't mix meat and dairy, so don't expect cheese on your burger or milk in your coffee in some places."], "safety": "Day-to-day crime is low, but the regional security situation can change fast and rocket or missile alerts have been part of life in recent years, so check your government's advisory and know how to follow alerts. Expect intense security questioning at the airport, especially if you have stamps from Arab countries. Petty theft happens on Tel Aviv beaches, and summer heat is serious in the desert, so carry plenty of water.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is a big deal and lunch is often the main meal, with dinner from 8pm onwards.", "Tap water is safe and good to drink almost everywhere.", "Israel is one of the best places to be vegan or vegetarian, with lots of plant-based options.", "Tip 10 to 15 percent in sit-down restaurants, and check whether service is already included.", "Many shops and restaurants close for Shabbat, so stock up before Friday afternoon."], "drinks": [{"name": "Limonana", "desc": "Frozen lemonade blended with fresh mint, perfect for hot days, for $4 to $7."}, {"name": "Arak", "desc": "An aniseed spirit mixed with water and ice, which turns cloudy. A glass at a bar costs $8 to $12."}], "greet": "Shalom is the all-purpose hello and goodbye, and handshakes are normal. Some Orthodox people don't touch the opposite sex, so wait for them to offer a hand.", "dress": "Tel Aviv is casual and beachy, but cover shoulders and knees in Jerusalem's Old City and religious neighbourhoods.", "table": "Kosher places separate meat and dairy, so don't ask for cheese on meat. Meals are loud, shared and informal.", "faith": "Most Israelis are Jewish, with Muslim, Christian and Druze minorities. At holy sites dress modestly, men cover their heads at the Western Wall, and don't photograph worshippers on Shabbat.", "phrases": [["Shalom (שָׁלוֹם)", "Hello, goodbye"], ["Toda (תּוֹדָה)", "Thank you"], ["Kama ze ole? (כמה זה עולה?)", "How much is it?"], ["Sababa (סבבה)", "Cool, great"], ["Yalla (יאללה)", "Let's go, come on"]]},
  "palestine": {"around": "Arab buses leave from near Damascus Gate in East Jerusalem for Bethlehem and the Ramallah area, and yellow shared minibuses (servees) connect West Bank towns from central stations. Taxis are cheap, but agree the price before you go. Checkpoints can cause long delays, closures can happen with little notice, and Israeli rental cars are often not insured for Area A, so public transport or a local driver is easier.", "money": "The Israeli shekel is used for everyday spending, with Jordanian dinars and US dollars also accepted for larger amounts. Cash is king in markets and small shops, though ATMs are easy to find in Bethlehem, Ramallah and Nablus, and bigger hotels take cards. Prices are much lower than in Israel. Bargaining is part of shopping in the souks, and about 10 percent is a normal tip in restaurants.", "sleep": "Bethlehem is the easiest base, with family guesthouses, a couple of hostels and small hotels, plus the walled-off art scene close by. Ramallah, Nablus and Jericho have budget hotels and some guesthouses, and village homestays can be arranged through community tourism groups and trail organizations.", "eat": ["Musakhan: roast chicken with sumac and onions on taboon bread", "Maqluba: upside-down rice with vegetables and chicken", "Knafeh Nabulsiyeh: Nablus-style sweet cheese pastry in syrup", "Manakish: flatbread topped with za'atar and olive oil"], "data": "Jawwal and Ooredoo sell SIMs with your passport, and Israeli SIMs and eSIMs work in most of the West Bank. Coverage is good in towns, and mobile data on local networks has been upgraded in recent years.", "heads_up": "Dress modestly in towns like Hebron and Nablus, and never photograph soldiers or checkpoints.", "food": [{"name": "Musakhan", "wiki": "Musakhan", "desc": "Roast chicken on taboon bread piled high with onions cooked in olive oil and sumac. Often called the national dish, best during the olive harvest in autumn.", "price": "$6 to $12", "when": "Family meals and olive harvest season", "order": "Eat it with your hands, tearing the bread"}, {"name": "Maqluba", "wiki": "Maqluba", "desc": "A pot of rice, vegetables and chicken or lamb flipped upside down at the table. It's a big family dish, so a homestay or local restaurant is your best bet.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Friday lunch at home", "order": "It's flipped upside down at the table"}, {"name": "Knafeh", "wiki": "Knafeh", "desc": "Nablus is the home of this hot white cheese pastry soaked in syrup with a bright orange top. Sweet shops in Nablus old city sell slices for a couple of shekels.", "price": "$1.50 to $3 a piece", "when": "Afternoon or evening, Nablus is famous", "order": "Get it warm, with the cheese still stretchy"}, {"name": "Ka'ak", "wiki": "Ka'ak", "desc": "Long oval sesame bread sold from carts in Jerusalem and Bethlehem, eaten with za'atar and sometimes a boiled egg. A cheap and classic breakfast.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Breakfast, with za'atar and eggs", "order": "The Jerusalem style is a long sesame ring"}, {"name": "Hummus and falafel", "wiki": "Hummus", "desc": "Hummus joints and falafel stalls are everywhere, with a full plate and bread costing very little. Lunch for locals and backpackers alike.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Breakfast, especially on Fridays", "order": "Ask for msabaha, a warm chunky hummus"}], "food_tip": "Street food and family-run restaurants are cheap and excellent, and you'll often get extra pickles and bread. Buy olive oil, za'atar and dates direct from cooperatives as gifts.", "culture": ["The West Bank is mostly Muslim, with significant Christian communities in Bethlehem and around. Dress modestly, especially in Hebron and Nablus, and women should cover their hair in mosques.", "Palestinians are very hospitable, and you'll likely be offered coffee or tea and invited to talk. Accept if you can, and use your right hand for eating and greeting.", "Life under occupation is the reality here, and people may want to share their stories. Listen with respect and be thoughtful about language and photos.", "Hebron's old city is split, with Israeli settlements in its center and heavy restrictions. A local guide makes the visit far more understandable.", "During Ramadan, don't eat, drink or smoke in public during daylight, and enjoy the evening iftar atmosphere."], "safety": "Gaza is inaccessible to travelers. The West Bank has seen rising violence, raids and settler attacks since 2023, so check your government's advisory, keep up with the news and avoid protests and clashes. Stay in the main towns, use local guides for places like Hebron and the Jordan Valley, and carry your passport everywhere. Many governments advise against some or all travel, so check your insurance.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is often hummus, ful, falafel and bread, lunch is the main meal around 2pm, and dinner is light.", "Tap water quality varies and supply can be cut in summer, so drink bottled or filtered water.", "Vegetarians do very well with mezze like hummus, baba ghanoush, falafel, salads and stuffed vegetables.", "Prices are in Israeli shekels, and markets in old cities like Nablus and Hebron are cheap and lively.", "Leave about 10% in sit-down restaurants, while street stalls don't expect tips."], "drinks": [{"name": "Arabic coffee", "desc": "Strong coffee with cardamom, poured in tiny cups as a sign of welcome. About $1 in a café or free in homes."}, {"name": "Sahlab", "desc": "A thick, hot milk drink thickened with orchid root or starch and topped with cinnamon and nuts, sold in winter. About $1.50 to $3."}], "greet": "Say \"marhaba\" or \"as-salamu alaykum\" and shake hands, and close friends kiss cheeks. Men should wait for a woman to offer her hand.", "dress": "Cover shoulders and knees everywhere, and dress even more conservatively in Hebron, Nablus and villages. Women need a headscarf for mosques.", "table": "Eat with your right hand, and expect hosts to keep offering more, so a little food left means you're full. Shaking your coffee cup gently side to side means no more.", "faith": "Most Palestinians are Sunni Muslim, with a historic Christian minority, mainly around Bethlehem and Ramallah. Dress modestly at the Church of the Nativity and mosques, stay quiet and don't photograph people praying.", "phrases": [["Marhaba (مرحبا)", "Hello"], ["Shukran (شكرا)", "Thank you"], ["Addesh? (قديش؟)", "How much?"], ["Bidun har (بدون حار)", "Not spicy"], ["Sahtein (صحتين)", "Enjoy your meal"]]},
  "oman": {"around": "Renting a car is the best way to see Oman's wadis, forts and desert, as public transport is thin and it splits well between a few people. Mwasalat buses connect Muscat with Nizwa, Sur, Ibri and Salalah cheaply, and in Muscat use the Otaxi app or agree the fare first. You only need a 4x4 for sand dunes and steep mountain roads like Jebel Akhdar, where police check vehicles. Hitchhiking is fairly common and accepted.", "money": "The Omani rial (OMR) is one of the world's strongest currencies, divided into 1000 baisa, so small-looking numbers add up fast. Cards are widely accepted in Muscat and big towns, but carry cash for villages, small cafes and remote petrol stations. ATMs are common in towns. Tipping isn't expected but rounding up is appreciated, and haggling is normal in souks like Mutrah and Nizwa.", "sleep": "Wild camping is widely accepted on beaches, in wadis and in the desert, and it's the cheapest way to sleep and one of Oman's best experiences, so bring a tent and leave no trace. Otherwise expect budget hotels and guesthouses, a few hostels in Muscat, heritage guesthouses in old Nizwa and desert camps in the Wahiba Sands that range from basic to fancy.", "eat": ["Shuwa: marinated lamb slow-cooked underground for a day or more", "Omani halwa: sticky sweet flavored with rosewater and saffron", "Kahwa: cardamom-spiced coffee served with dates", "Mashuai: whole grilled kingfish with lemon rice"], "data": "Omantel, Ooredoo and Vodafone Oman sell tourist SIMs at Muscat airport with your passport. Coverage is good in towns and along main highways but patchy in mountains, wadis and deep desert.", "heads_up": "Wadis can flood fast after rain in the mountains, even miles away, so check the forecast before hiking or swimming and never camp in a wadi bed.", "food": [{"name": "Shuwa", "wiki": "Shuwa", "desc": "Lamb or goat rubbed with spices, wrapped in leaves and slow-cooked in an underground pit for up to two days. Mainly served during Eid, but some restaurants make it year-round.", "price": "$8 to $15", "when": "Eid feasts, slow cooked underground", "order": "Look for it sold by weight around Eid"}, {"name": "Majboos", "wiki": "Kabsa", "desc": "Spiced rice cooked with chicken, lamb or fish, sometimes with dried lime and nuts. A standard lunch at Omani restaurants and cheap Indian-run canteens.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch at local restaurants", "order": "Usually chicken; ask for spicy daqous sauce"}, {"name": "Omani halwa", "wiki": "Halva", "desc": "A dark, sticky sweet made with sugar, ghee, rosewater, saffron and cardamom, served in small bowls. Nizwa souk is the place to buy a tub.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "With kahwa when guests visit", "order": "Rose and saffron versions are the classic"}, {"name": "Kahwa", "wiki": "Arabic coffee", "desc": "Light coffee spiced with cardamom, poured in tiny cups and served with dates. It's offered as a welcome everywhere from homes to hotels.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Offered with dates to every guest", "order": "Shake the cup gently when you've had enough"}, {"name": "Shawarma", "wiki": "Shawarma", "desc": "Spit-roasted chicken in thin bread, sold from roadside cafes for less than a rial. The backbone of a budget road trip.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Evenings, from roadside stands", "order": "Chicken with garlic sauce is the standard"}, {"name": "Harees", "wiki": "Harees", "desc": "Wheat and meat cooked for hours until smooth and porridge-like, topped with ghee. A Ramadan and Eid favorite that local restaurants also serve.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Ramadan iftar, Eid and weddings"}], "food_tip": "Small Indian and Pakistani run cafes serve curry, biryani and paratha for very little, and are way cheaper than the hotel restaurants. Stock up on fruit, bread and water at supermarkets before heading into the mountains or desert.", "culture": ["Oman is a conservative Ibadi Muslim country with a very easy-going feel. Dress modestly: cover shoulders and knees in towns, and women need to cover arms, legs and hair to visit the Sultan Qaboos Grand Mosque, which also only admits tourists in the mornings.", "Swimwear is fine at hotel pools and beaches, but cover up away from the water, and nudity or skimpy swimwear in wadis is offensive to locals.", "Omanis are polite and reserved, so keep your voice down and avoid public displays of affection.", "Alcohol is only available in licensed hotels and some restaurants, and drinking in public or wild-camping with booze in view is a bad idea.", "If you're invited for coffee and dates, accept, and shake the cup gently when you've had enough. Use your right hand to eat and pass things."], "safety": "Oman is one of the safest countries in the region, with very little crime and friendly locals. The real risks are nature: summer heat is brutal, so carry lots of water, and don't drive dunes without experience or another car. Roads are good but driving can be fast, and watch out for camels and goats at night.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch, around 1 to 3pm, is the main meal, and many shops close for an afternoon break until about 4pm.", "Tap water is desalinated and safe in towns, but most people drink bottled.", "Vegetarians eat well at Indian cafés, with dal, chickpea curry and vegetable dishes.", "Tipping isn't expected in cheap places, and around 10 percent is fine in sit-down restaurants.", "During Ramadan, restaurants open at sunset, and iftar meals are a good value way to try local dishes."], "drinks": [{"name": "Laban", "desc": "A thin, salty buttermilk drunk with meals or on its own, about $0.50 a carton."}, {"name": "Karak chai", "desc": "Strong tea boiled with milk, cardamom and sugar, sold at roadside cafés for about $0.30 to $0.50."}], "greet": "Men greet with 'as-salaam alaikum' and a handshake, and close friends may touch noses. Wait for a woman to offer her hand first.", "dress": "Omani men wear the white dishdasha with a kumma cap or turban, and many women wear an abaya. Visitors aren't expected to copy them, but long, loose clothing earns respect in villages and souqs.", "table": "Meals are often eaten on the floor from shared platters, using the right hand only. Accept kahwa and dates with your right hand, and don't show the soles of your feet.", "faith": "Oman is mostly Ibadi Muslim, with Sunni and Shia communities and churches and temples for foreign workers. Remove shoes at mosques, women should cover their hair, and avoid visiting during Friday prayers.", "phrases": [["As-salaam alaikum (السلام عليكم)", "Hello"], ["Shukran (شكرا)", "Thank you"], ["Bikam? (بكم؟)", "How much?"], ["Inshallah (إن شاء الله)", "God willing"], ["Ma'a as-salaama (مع السلامة)", "Goodbye"]]},
  "uae": {"around": "The Dubai Metro, trams and buses are clean, cheap and air-conditioned, and you pay with a Nol card. Intercity buses run from Dubai to Abu Dhabi, Sharjah, Ras al-Khaimah and Al Ain for a few dirhams, and Careem and Uber work across the Emirates. Abra water taxis across Dubai Creek cost almost nothing. A rental car helps for the Hajar Mountains around Ras al-Khaimah and Fujairah.", "money": "The UAE dirham (AED) is pegged to the US dollar, and cards and phone payments work almost everywhere, from malls to small cafeterias. ATMs are everywhere, but always pay in dirhams if a card machine offers currency conversion. Tipping around 10 percent is common in restaurants if no service charge is included, and haggling is expected in the gold, spice and textile souks.", "sleep": "The UAE isn't a budget destination, but a few hostels exist in Dubai and Abu Dhabi, and budget hotels cluster in older areas like Deira, Bur Dubai and Al Karama. Prices drop a lot in the scorching summer months. Camping is possible in some desert and mountain areas, especially in Ras al-Khaimah and Fujairah.", "eat": ["Shawarma: spit-roasted meat wrap, the classic cheap meal", "Machboos: spiced rice with chicken, lamb or fish", "Luqaimat: fried dough balls drizzled with date syrup", "Karak chai: strong tea boiled with milk and cardamom"], "data": "Tourist SIMs from du and e& are sold at the airport with your passport, and eSIMs are easy to set up. Coverage is excellent, but WhatsApp and other voice and video calls are often blocked, so test an alternative app before you arrive.", "heads_up": "Public displays of affection, drunkenness, swearing and rude gestures can lead to fines or arrest, so keep it low-key in public.", "food": [{"name": "Shawarma", "wiki": "Shawarma", "desc": "Spit-roasted chicken or meat in thin bread with garlic sauce, sold at every cafeteria for a handful of dirhams. The classic cheap meal in Dubai.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Late evening, from cafeterias", "order": "Ask for extra garlic sauce and pickles"}, {"name": "Machboos", "wiki": "Kabsa", "desc": "Fragrant spiced rice with chicken, lamb or fish and dried lime, the signature Emirati main. Try it at heritage-area restaurants or Emirati cafes.", "price": "$7 to $14", "when": "Family lunch, especially on Fridays", "order": "Chicken machboos is the most common on menus"}, {"name": "Luqaimat", "wiki": "Lokma", "desc": "Crunchy fried dough balls drizzled with date syrup and sesame seeds, especially popular during Ramadan. Cheap and addictive.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Ramadan iftar and evening snacks", "order": "Eat them hot, straight from the fryer"}, {"name": "Karak chai", "wiki": "Masala chai", "desc": "Strong tea boiled with evaporated milk, sugar and cardamom, sold at roadside cafeterias for very little. A national obsession.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Any time, often brought to your car", "order": "Honk and order through the window at roadside cafeterias"}, {"name": "Harees", "wiki": "Harees", "desc": "Wheat and meat slow-cooked until smooth, a comforting dish served at Ramadan and weddings. Look for it at Emirati restaurants.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Ramadan and weddings, at Emirati restaurants"}], "food_tip": "The cheapest and often best food is in the Indian, Pakistani and Filipino cafeterias of Deira, Karama and Al Satwa, where a full meal costs a fraction of mall prices. Supermarket meal deals help too.", "culture": ["The UAE is cosmopolitan but Islamic law and local norms apply. Dress modestly in malls, souks and old areas, cover shoulders and knees, and wear swimwear only at the beach or pool.", "At the Sheikh Zayed Grand Mosque, women must cover arms, legs and hair, and men need long trousers. Robes are available at the entrance if needed.", "During Ramadan, avoid eating, drinking or smoking in public in daylight, though many places now have screened areas for non-fasters.", "Alcohol is legal only in licensed venues and for adults, and being drunk in public is an offense. Sharjah is completely dry.", "Don't photograph locals, especially women, without permission, and avoid taking photos of government buildings and military sites."], "safety": "The UAE is extremely safe, with very low crime. The bigger risks are legal: strict laws on drugs, some prescription medicines, behavior and online posts, so check any medication you bring. Summer heat over 45C is dangerous, so stay hydrated and avoid hiking in the middle of the day.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is usually 1 to 3pm, and dinner runs late, often after 9pm, especially on weekends.", "Tap water is desalinated and treated, but most people drink bottled or filtered water.", "Vegetarians eat well thanks to the big South Indian community, so look for pure veg restaurants in Bur Dubai and Karama.", "Bills include 5 percent VAT and sometimes a service charge; otherwise tip about 10 percent in restaurants, while cafeterias don't expect it.", "Pork is sold only in separate, labelled sections of some supermarkets, and most restaurants don't serve it."], "drinks": [{"name": "Gahwa", "desc": "Light Arabic coffee spiced with cardamom, poured into tiny cups and served with dates. It's often free in hotels and offices, and around $3 to $5 in cafés."}, {"name": "Laban", "desc": "A chilled, salty yoghurt drink sold in every supermarket for under $1."}], "greet": "Men usually shake hands with men, and Emirati men may touch noses. Wait for a woman to offer her hand first; a hand on your heart is a polite alternative.", "dress": "Swimwear is for beaches and pools only. Cover shoulders and knees in malls, government buildings and the older parts of Dubai and Sharjah.", "table": "Eat and pass food with your right hand. When offered gahwa, gently shake the empty cup side to side to show you've had enough.", "faith": "Islam is the state religion, and there are also large Christian, Hindu and Buddhist expat communities with their own places of worship. Non-Muslims can visit some mosques, such as Sheikh Zayed Grand Mosque, outside prayer times and in covering clothes.", "phrases": [["Marhaba (مرحبا)", "Hello"], ["Shukran (شكرا)", "Thank you"], ["Kam? (كم؟)", "How much?"], ["Yalla (يلا)", "Let's go, come on"], ["Inshallah (إن شاء الله)", "God willing"]]},
  "saudi-arabia": {"around": "The Haramain high-speed train links Jeddah and Medina, other trains run from Riyadh to the north and east, and the new Riyadh Metro makes the capital much easier to get around. SAPTCO buses run between cities cheaply, and Careem, Uber and Jeeny work in the big cities. Domestic flights on flynas and flyadeal are cheap if you book early, and AlUla is easiest by plane, with a car or tour needed once you're there.", "money": "The Saudi riyal (SAR) is pegged to the US dollar, and Saudi Arabia is very cashless, with cards and phone payments accepted almost everywhere, even at tiny shops. ATMs are common, and cash is still useful for rural stalls. Tipping isn't required but rounding up is appreciated, and haggling is fine in traditional souks.", "sleep": "Hostels are still rare, so budget hotels and apartment rentals are the usual cheap options, and they're good value when shared. In AlUla and the desert, camps offer everything from simple tents to luxury, and they fill up in the cooler winter season. Camping in the desert is popular with locals too.", "eat": ["Kabsa: spiced rice with chicken or lamb, the national dish", "Mutabbaq: pan-fried stuffed pastry, a street food favorite", "Saudi coffee: light cardamom coffee served with dates", "Jareesh: cracked wheat cooked slowly with meat and spices"], "data": "STC, Mobily and Zain sell prepaid SIMs with your passport, often at the airport, and registration is quick. Coverage is good in cities and along main highways but drops off deep in the desert.", "heads_up": "Non-Muslims can't enter Mecca or central Medina, and during Ramadan don't eat or drink in public during daylight hours.", "food": [{"name": "Kabsa", "wiki": "Kabsa", "desc": "Spiced rice with chicken or lamb, nuts and raisins, often served on huge shared platters. The national dish, found in every local restaurant.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Lunch, the main meal of the day", "order": "Ask for \"nus\" for a half portion"}, {"name": "Mandi", "wiki": "Mandi (food)", "desc": "Yemeni-style rice and meat slow-cooked in an underground oven for a smoky flavor. Mandi restaurants are everywhere and very cheap for a feast.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Lunch or dinner, in groups", "order": "Choose lamb (lahm) or chicken (dajaj)"}, {"name": "Mutabbaq", "wiki": "Murtabak", "desc": "Pan-fried stuffed pastry filled with meat, egg or banana and cheese, a famous street food in Jeddah. Cheap and filling at night markets.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Late night, at street stalls", "order": "Try the sweet banana and cheese version"}, {"name": "Saudi coffee", "wiki": "Arabic coffee", "desc": "Light coffee spiced with cardamom and saffron, served in small cups with dates. It's offered as a welcome everywhere.", "price": "Free with hospitality", "when": "When welcoming guests, all day", "order": "Shake the cup gently when you've had enough"}, {"name": "Saleeg", "wiki": "Saleeg", "desc": "Creamy rice cooked in chicken broth and milk, a comfort food from the Hejaz region. Try it in Jeddah and Taif.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Lunch or dinner in the Hejaz", "order": "Usually topped with roast chicken"}], "food_tip": "Local shawarma shops, mandi joints and Indian or Pakistani cafes are the budget options. Eating with your hand from a shared platter is common, so wash up first and use your right hand.", "culture": ["Saudi Arabia is a deeply religious Muslim country, but it has opened up a lot in recent years. Foreign women don't need to wear an abaya or headscarf, but loose clothing that covers shoulders and knees is expected for everyone.", "Non-Muslims cannot enter Mecca or central Medina, and the road signs make the boundaries clear. Many businesses close briefly for prayer times, so plan around them.", "Alcohol is illegal and drugs are punished severely, so don't bring either into the country. Public displays of affection are best avoided.", "Saudis are very hospitable, and you may be offered coffee, dates or even dinner. Accept politely and use your right hand to eat and greet.", "Ask before photographing people, especially women, and avoid photographing government buildings, military sites and royal palaces."], "safety": "Saudi Arabia has very low crime and is generally safe for visitors. The main risks are dangerous driving, extreme summer heat and strict laws on drugs, alcohol and behavior. Avoid areas near the Yemeni border, and check your advisory for any regional tensions before you go.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, often after the midday prayer, and dinner can be very late, with streets busy after 10pm.", "Tap water is desalinated and generally safe, but most people drink bottled water.", "Vegetarians can find falafel, hummus and lentil soup, and Indian restaurants are everywhere.", "During Ramadan, restaurants open only after sunset for iftar, and the night food scene is lively.", "Tipping isn't required, but 10 percent in sit-down restaurants is appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Laban", "desc": "A cold, salty buttermilk sold in cartons everywhere and drunk with rice dishes, about $0.50 to $1."}, {"name": "Saudi champagne", "desc": "An alcohol-free mix of apple juice, sparkling water, fruit and mint, served in jugs at cafes for $4 to $8."}], "greet": "Men greet with \"as-salamu alaykum\" and a handshake, and Saudi men often kiss on the cheeks or touch noses. Men should wait for a woman to offer her hand.", "dress": "Everyone should wear loose clothes that cover shoulders and knees, and tight or see-through clothing is frowned upon. Women need an abaya and headscarf at mosques.", "table": "Eat and pass food with your right hand, and sit so your feet don't point at others on floor seating. Coffee is served in small cups; gently shake yours when you've had enough.", "faith": "Islam is the state religion and shapes daily life, and public worship of other faiths is not allowed. Only Muslims may enter Mecca and central Medina, and shops close briefly at prayer times.", "phrases": [["Salam alaykum (السلام عليكم)", "Hello"], ["Shukran (شكرا)", "Thank you"], ["Kam? (كم)", "How much?"], ["Yalla (يلا)", "Let's go"], ["Inshallah (إن شاء الله)", "God willing"]]},
  "yemen": {"around": "Socotra is visited almost only on organized tours that include flights, a driver, a 4x4 and camping gear. Flights to the island are few and the routes have shifted with the politics, so book through an experienced operator well ahead and expect changes. There's no public transport worth relying on, and roads to remote beaches and plateaus are rough. Mainland Yemen remains a war zone and is off limits for travel.", "money": "Bring all your money as US dollars in cash, as cards and ATMs can't be relied on, especially on Socotra. Most of your costs are paid to the tour operator in advance, so you only need cash for extras and tips. The Yemeni rial is used locally, and small tips for drivers, cooks and guides are expected at the end of a trip.", "sleep": "Most tours camp on beaches, dunes and plateaus with basic tents and mats provided, which is half the magic of Socotra. There are a few simple guesthouses and hotels in the main town of Hadibo, mostly used at the start and end of a trip.", "eat": ["Saltah: bubbling meat stew topped with fenugreek froth", "Mandi: slow-cooked rice and meat with smoky spices", "Bint al-sahn: layered honey bread with black seeds", "Fresh fish: grilled catch of the day, often eaten on the beach"], "data": "Don't count on data on Socotra, as coverage is limited to Hadibo and a few roads, so tell people at home you'll be out of contact. Some travelers bring a satellite messenger for remote trips.", "heads_up": "Socotra's plants and animals are protected, so don't take seeds, plants or dragon's blood resin off the island.", "food": [{"name": "Saltah", "wiki": "Saltah", "desc": "A bubbling meat stew topped with frothy whipped fenugreek and eaten with bread. Considered the national dish of Yemen.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch, the main meal of the day", "order": "Scoop it up with hot flatbread called malawah"}, {"name": "Mandi", "wiki": "Mandi (food)", "desc": "Rice and meat slow-cooked in an underground oven, with a smoky flavor. Popular across Yemen and the Gulf.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Lunch, at family gatherings", "order": "Ask for lamb or chicken, both are common"}, {"name": "Bint al-sahn", "wiki": "Bint al-sahn", "desc": "Layered, buttery honey bread sprinkled with black seeds, served at celebrations. A sweet treat if you see it on the menu.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Dessert or with tea", "order": "Pour honey on it while it's hot"}, {"name": "Fahsa", "wiki": "Fahsa", "desc": "A thick meat stew served bubbling in a stone pot, topped with fenugreek froth. Often eaten for lunch with flatbread.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Breakfast or lunch, in a stone pot", "order": "Eat it with bread while it bubbles"}, {"name": "Qishr", "wiki": "Qishr", "desc": "A Yemeni drink brewed from coffee husks with ginger and sometimes cardamom, lighter than coffee and served sweet. You'll be offered it in homes and simple cafes.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50", "when": "Afternoons, with sweets"}], "food_tip": "Meals on Socotra are usually included in the tour price, cooked by your crew. Bring snacks and electrolyte sachets, as fresh food choices are limited outside Hadibo.", "culture": ["Yemen is a conservative Muslim country, and Socotra is traditional. Dress modestly, with long trousers for men and loose clothing that covers arms and legs for women, plus a headscarf in villages.", "Ask before photographing people, especially women, and always respect when the answer is no.", "Chewing qat is a big social ritual in Yemen, often in the afternoon. You'll likely see it, and you might be invited, but there's no pressure to join.", "Alcohol isn't available and shouldn't be brought in.", "Socotra's communities are small, so respect local land and fishing customs, and follow your guide's advice on where to camp and swim."], "safety": "Mainland Yemen is a war zone and most governments advise against all travel there, and insurance is very hard to get. Socotra has been relatively calm, but it's still remote, and the political situation can change, so only go with a reputable operator who handles permits and flights. Bring any medicines you need and enough sun protection, as medical care is minimal.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, eaten early in the afternoon before qat sessions.", "Most visitors only reach Socotra, where cooking is done by your guide's crew and meals are basic but filling.", "Don't drink tap or well water, and bring purification tablets if travelling far from town.", "Vegetarian food is limited, but beans, lentils, bread, rice and vegetable stews are often available if you ask.", "Tipping isn't customary at local eateries, but tipping your guide, driver and cook at the end of a tour is expected."], "drinks": [{"name": "Shai haleeb", "desc": "Sweet, strong tea with milk and sometimes cardamom, served in small glasses all day. Usually less than $0.50 a glass."}, {"name": "Yemeni coffee", "desc": "Coffee from Yemen's mountains, the original mocha, often brewed light with spices. A cup costs under $1 where available."}], "greet": "Say as-salaam alaykum, and men shake hands with men, sometimes for a long time. Don't offer your hand to women unless they do.", "dress": "Dress very conservatively: men in long trousers and women in loose, long clothes with hair covered. On Socotra, follow your guide on where swimwear is acceptable.", "table": "Meals are often eaten seated on the floor, using your right hand and bread. Accept hospitality graciously, and wait for the host to start.", "faith": "Yemen is almost entirely Muslim, mainly Sunni with a large Zaydi Shia minority. Non-Muslims generally can't enter mosques, so admire from outside and be quiet during prayer times.", "phrases": [["As-salaam alaykum (السلام عليكم)", "Peace be upon you, hello"], ["Shukran (شكرا)", "Thank you"], ["Bikam? (بكم؟)", "How much?"], ["Insha'Allah (إن شاء الله)", "God willing"], ["Ma'a as-salama (مع السلامة)", "Goodbye"]]},
  "portugal": {"around": "CP trains link Lisbon, Porto, Coimbra and the Algarve, and the cheaper Intercidades trains are nearly as fast as the Alfa Pendular. Rede Expressos and FlixBus are cheap for longer routes and reach towns the train doesn't. Uber and Bolt work well in the cities and are usually cheaper than taxis. In Lisbon and Porto, load a transit card or tap a contactless card for metro, buses and trams, and skip the queue for the touristy Tram 28 by walking.", "money": "Portugal uses the euro, and cards work almost everywhere, though some small cafes and tascas still prefer cash or have a card minimum. Multibanco ATMs from real banks usually don't charge a local fee, but avoid the blue and yellow Euronet machines, which hit you hard. Tipping isn't a big thing, so leave a few coins or round up for good service, and haggling isn't really done outside flea markets.", "sleep": "Lisbon and Porto have some of the best hostels in Europe, with dorm beds ranging from cheap to mid-priced, often with free walking tours and family dinners. Guesthouses (alojamento local) are common elsewhere, and the Algarve and the Alentejo coast have surf hostels and campsites. Book ahead for summer, especially in the Algarve.", "eat": ["Pastel de nata: custard tart with flaky pastry", "Bacalhau à Brás: shredded salt cod with eggs and fried potatoes", "Francesinha: Porto sandwich of meats under melted cheese and beer sauce", "Bifana: thin pork steak sandwich with garlic"], "data": "MEO, Vodafone and NOS sell prepaid SIMs with your passport, and EU visitors can roam at home rates. Coverage is good across the country, and hostels and cafes have good wifi.", "heads_up": "Bread, olives and cheese put on the table before a meal aren't free, so send them back if you don't want to pay for them.", "food": [{"name": "Pastel de nata", "wiki": "Pastel de nata", "desc": "A flaky custard tart with a scorched top, best eaten warm with cinnamon. Any bakery sells them for around a euro or a bit more.", "price": "$1.30 to $2", "when": "Morning or afternoon coffee break", "order": "Ask for one warm, straight from the oven"}, {"name": "Bacalhau à Brás", "wiki": "Bacalhau à Brás", "desc": "Shredded salt cod mixed with eggs, onions and crispy straw potatoes, topped with olives. A classic lunch in tascas everywhere.", "price": "$9 to $14", "when": "Lunch at a tasca"}, {"name": "Francesinha", "wiki": "Francesinha", "desc": "Porto's monster sandwich of meats and sausage covered in melted cheese and a beer and tomato sauce, often with fries. Share it or skip dinner.", "price": "$12 to $17", "when": "Lunch or dinner in Porto", "order": "Ask for an egg on top, com ovo"}, {"name": "Bifana", "wiki": "Bifana", "desc": "Thin pork steak marinated in garlic and wine, served in a soft roll with mustard. A cheap snack at bars and stalls.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Quick lunch or late night snack", "order": "Add mustard and chilli oil, piri piri"}, {"name": "Caldo verde", "wiki": "Caldo verde", "desc": "A simple potato and kale soup with a slice of chorizo, cheap and warming. On almost every menu, especially in the north.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Starter at lunch and dinner"}, {"name": "Ginjinha", "wiki": "Ginjinha", "desc": "Sour cherry liqueur served in a tiny cup, sometimes chocolate, from hole-in-the-wall bars in Lisbon and Óbidos. A euro or two a shot.", "price": "$1.50 to $2.50 a shot", "when": "Afternoon or evening, standing outside", "order": "Say com elas for the cherries in it"}], "food_tip": "Look for the prato do dia (dish of the day) at local tascas, which gets you a main and often a drink for well under 15 euros. Supermarkets like Pingo Doce sell cheap ready meals and great bread.", "culture": ["Portuguese people are generally reserved and polite, so a bom dia (good morning) or boa tarde (good afternoon) when entering a shop goes a long way.", "Don't assume Portuguese is like Spanish, and try not to speak Spanish at people. English is widely spoken, but learning obrigado (if you're male) or obrigada (if you're female) for thank you is appreciated.", "Lunch and dinner are later than in northern Europe, with dinner often from 8pm. Coffee culture is huge, and a bica in Lisbon or cimbalino in Porto is an espresso.", "Churches are free to visit in most towns, but dress respectfully and keep quiet during services.", "Portugal decriminalized personal drug use, but dealing is still illegal, and the guys whispering offers in Lisbon's Baixa are mostly selling fake stuff."], "safety": "Portugal is one of the safest countries in Europe. Pickpocketing is common on Lisbon's trams and in crowded areas, so keep valuables close. On the beaches, watch for strong currents and only swim where there are lifeguards.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is from about 12:30 to 2:30 and dinner rarely starts before 8, so plan snacks in between.", "The bread, olives and cheese put on your table aren't free, so send them back if you don't want to pay.", "Tap water is safe, though many locals drink bottled out of habit.", "Vegetarian choices are thin in traditional tascas, but cities now have plenty of vegan cafes and soups are often meat-free.", "Tipping isn't expected, so round up or leave 5 to 10 percent for good service."], "drinks": [{"name": "Vinho verde", "desc": "Young, slightly fizzy white wine from the north, light and cheap at $4 to $8 a bottle in supermarkets."}, {"name": "Port", "desc": "Sweet fortified wine from the Douro Valley, poured in the Gaia cellars for tastings from about $10 to $20."}], "greet": "Friends greet with two cheek kisses, starting on the right, while men shake hands. Use bom dia, boa tarde or boa noite.", "dress": "Dress is relaxed and a bit smart in cities. Cover shoulders in churches and keep swimwear for the beach.", "table": "Wait for the host to start, and keep your hands visible on the table. Ask for the bill, a conta, as it won't come until you do.", "faith": "Portugal is mostly Catholic, with Fátima a major pilgrimage site. Be quiet in churches, don't wander during Mass, and dress modestly.", "phrases": [["Olá", "Hello"], ["Obrigado / obrigada", "Thank you (said by a man / woman)"], ["Quanto custa?", "How much is it?"], ["A conta, por favor", "The bill, please"], ["Uma bica", "An espresso (Lisbon)"]]},
  "spain": {"around": "Renfe, Ouigo and Iryo now compete on high-speed routes, so booking early can get you surprisingly cheap fares between Madrid, Barcelona, Valencia, Seville and Málaga. Alsa buses cover smaller towns cheaply, and BlaBlaCar is popular for rides. Uber, Cabify and Bolt work in big cities, and metros are cheap and easy. Domestic flights on Vueling and Ryanair help with the islands.", "money": "Spain uses the euro, and cards and contactless are accepted almost everywhere, even for small amounts. Use bank ATMs, avoid the standalone Euronet ones, and always choose to be charged in euros when a machine offers to convert. Tipping is optional, with small change or rounding up common in bars and about 5 to 10 percent for good service in restaurants. Haggling isn't done outside flea markets like Madrid's El Rastro.", "sleep": "Hostels are everywhere in the big cities and many have private rooms too, while pensiones and hostales are small, cheap family hotels found in every town. Book ahead for summer, festivals like San Fermín and Las Fallas, and the islands. Camping is common along the coasts.", "eat": ["Tortilla española: thick potato and egg omelet", "Patatas bravas: fried potatoes with spicy sauce", "Churros con chocolate: fried dough sticks dipped in thick chocolate", "Gazpacho: cold tomato and pepper soup, popular in summer"], "data": "Movistar, Vodafone and Orange sell SIMs that need passport registration, and EU visitors can roam at home rates. Coverage is good across the country, and free wifi is common in cafes and hostels.", "heads_up": "Pickpocketing is common in Barcelona and Madrid tourist areas, especially on the metro, so keep valuables in a front pocket.", "food": [{"name": "Tortilla española", "wiki": "Spanish omelette", "desc": "A thick potato and egg omelet, often with onion, served in wedges at every bar. A pincho with a drink is cheap.", "price": "$2 to $4 a slice", "when": "Mid-morning or as a bar tapa", "order": "Ask if it comes con cebolla, with onion"}, {"name": "Patatas bravas", "wiki": "Patatas bravas", "desc": "Fried potatoes with a spicy tomato sauce and often aioli, a tapas bar staple. Cheap and perfect with a caña of beer.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Evening tapas with a caña"}, {"name": "Churros con chocolate", "wiki": "Churro", "desc": "Fried dough sticks dipped in thick hot chocolate, a breakfast or late night snack. Madrid's churrerías are the place to go.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Breakfast, or after a big night out", "order": "Porras are the thicker, fluffier churros"}, {"name": "Paella", "wiki": "Paella", "desc": "Saffron rice with seafood or chicken and rabbit, originally from Valencia, usually eaten at lunch. Be wary of cheap paella with photos outside, as it's often frozen.", "price": "$12 to $20 per person", "when": "Sunday lunch, never dinner for locals", "order": "Usually ordered for two or more people"}, {"name": "Jamón ibérico", "wiki": "Jamón ibérico", "desc": "Cured ham from black Iberian pigs, sliced thin and eaten with bread. Good ham is pricey, but a ham sandwich (bocadillo) is cheap.", "price": "$6 to $15 a plate", "when": "Shared tapa or in a bocadillo", "order": "Look for 100% ibérico de bellota labels"}, {"name": "Gazpacho", "wiki": "Gazpacho", "desc": "Cold tomato and pepper soup from Andalusia, refreshing on hot summer days. Often served as a starter in a menu del día.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Summer lunch starter, served cold", "order": "Try salmorejo, its thicker Córdoba cousin"}], "food_tip": "Eat your main meal at lunch with a menu del día, which usually includes a starter, main, dessert and a drink for a fixed price. In Granada and parts of Andalusia, you still get free tapas with every drink.", "culture": ["Spanish meal times are late, with lunch from 2pm and dinner rarely before 9pm, so plan around it or you'll find kitchens closed.", "Many small shops close for a few hours in the afternoon, especially in smaller towns and the south, while bigger stores stay open.", "Regional identity is strong in Catalonia, the Basque Country and Galicia, so avoid calling everything Spanish and be aware that independence is a sensitive topic.", "Greetings among friends often involve two cheek kisses, but a handshake is fine with strangers.", "Cover shoulders and knees in cathedrals and big churches, and don't wander around town in swimwear away from the beach, which can get you fined in some places."], "safety": "Spain is generally safe, but pickpocketing is a big problem in Barcelona, Madrid and other tourist areas. Watch out for distraction scams, like someone spilling something on you, and keep valuables in a zipped front pocket. On busy beaches, don't leave your bag unattended while swimming.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is light, just coffee and toast with tomato, then lunch from 2pm and dinner from 9pm.", "Tap water is safe across Spain, and you can ask for \"agua del grifo\" in restaurants for free.", "Covered food markets in most towns have stalls selling cheap snacks, fruit and ready food.", "Vegetarians can lean on tortilla, pimientos de Padrón, espinacas con garbanzos and salads, but watch for ham in everything.", "Tipping is optional, so rounding up or leaving a euro or two for good service is plenty."], "drinks": [{"name": "Tinto de verano", "desc": "Red wine with lemon soda over ice, what locals actually drink in summer instead of sangria. About $3 to $4 a glass."}, {"name": "Vermut", "desc": "Sweet vermouth on tap with an olive and orange slice, a weekend pre-lunch ritual called \"la hora del vermut\". Around $3 to $5."}], "greet": "Friends and new acquaintances greet with two kisses on the cheek, right then left, while men often shake hands. Always say hola and adiós when entering and leaving small shops.", "dress": "Spaniards dress neatly in cities, so swap beach gear for casual clothes in town. Cover shoulders at cathedrals like Seville and Toledo, where some charge entry and turn people away.", "table": "Keep your hands visible on the table, not in your lap, and don't rush, as the sobremesa chat after a meal can last an hour. The bill only comes when you ask for it.", "faith": "Spain is historically Catholic, though many people are not very religious now. Be quiet in churches, especially during Mass, and step aside for Holy Week processions in spring.", "phrases": [["Una caña, por favor", "A small beer, please"], ["La cuenta, por favor", "The bill, please"], ["¿Cuánto cuesta?", "How much is it?"], ["¿Qué me recomiendas?", "What do you recommend?"], ["Vale", "Okay, sure"]]},
  "france": {"around": "SNCF runs fast TGV trains between cities, with Ouigo selling cheap no-frills high-speed seats if you book early, and slower TER regional trains for smaller towns. FlixBus and BlaBlaCar fill the gaps cheaply. Uber and Bolt work in major cities, and the Paris metro is easy with a contactless card or the Navigo Easy pass. Book high-speed trains early for the best prices.", "money": "France uses the euro, and cards and contactless are widely used, even for a coffee or a baguette. Use bank ATMs (distributeurs) and pay in euros when a card machine offers conversion. Service is included in restaurant bills (service compris), so tipping is optional, though leaving a euro or two for good service is common. Haggling isn't done except at flea markets.", "sleep": "Hostels are common in Paris, Lyon, Marseille, Nice and Bordeaux, with dorms in Paris being among the priciest in Europe. Campsites are cheap and everywhere, especially in the countryside and coasts. Book ahead in summer, especially in Provence, the Riviera and the Alps.", "eat": ["Croissant: buttery layered pastry from any bakery", "Croque-monsieur: grilled ham and cheese sandwich with béchamel", "Crêpe: thin pancake with sweet or savory fillings", "Ratatouille: stewed vegetables from Provence"], "data": "Orange, SFR, Bouygues and Free sell SIMs, and EU visitors can roam at home rates. Coverage is good outside remote mountains, and free wifi is common in cafes and hostels.", "heads_up": "Say bonjour when entering shops and cafes, as skipping it is seen as rude.", "food": [{"name": "Croissant", "wiki": "Croissant", "desc": "Buttery, flaky pastry from any boulangerie, best fresh in the morning. A croissant and coffee at the bar is a cheap breakfast.", "price": "$1.50 to $2.50", "when": "Weekend breakfast from the boulangerie", "order": "Ask for croissant au beurre, made with all butter"}, {"name": "Baguette", "wiki": "Baguette", "desc": "The classic long bread, cheap and baked fresh several times a day. Grab one with cheese and ham for a picnic lunch.", "price": "$1.20 to $1.80", "when": "Bought fresh daily, often twice", "order": "Ask for une tradition, the crustier, better loaf"}, {"name": "Croque monsieur", "wiki": "Croque monsieur", "desc": "Grilled ham and cheese sandwich topped with béchamel. A cafe staple, and the croque madame comes with a fried egg on top.", "price": "$8 to $14", "when": "Lunch at cafés and brasseries", "order": "A croque madame comes topped with a fried egg"}, {"name": "Crêpe", "wiki": "Crêpe", "desc": "Thin pancake with sweet fillings like Nutella or sugar, or savory buckwheat galettes in Brittany. Street stalls sell them for a few euros.", "price": "$4 to $10", "when": "Snacks, or crêperie dinners in Brittany", "order": "Savoury ones are galettes, made with buckwheat"}, {"name": "Steak frites", "wiki": "Steak frites", "desc": "Steak with fries, a bistro classic. Often part of a good value lunch menu.", "price": "$18 to $28", "when": "Brasserie lunch or dinner", "order": "Saignant means rare, à point medium; steaks come pinker here"}, {"name": "Ratatouille", "wiki": "Ratatouille", "desc": "Slow-stewed vegetables from Provence, usually eggplant, zucchini, peppers and tomatoes. A cheap, healthy side or main.", "price": "$12 to $20", "when": "Summer in Provence, often as a side"}], "food_tip": "Lunch menus (formule or menu du jour) are much cheaper than dinner, and picnics with bread, cheese and wine from a supermarket are the ultimate budget meal. Tap water (carafe d'eau) is free in restaurants.", "culture": ["Always say bonjour when entering shops, cafes and even lifts, and au revoir when leaving. Skipping it is seen as rude.", "The French value politeness and formality, so start conversations in French, even just a bonjour, before switching to English.", "Dress modestly in churches and cathedrals, covering shoulders and knees.", "Meals are slow and social, and the waiter won't bring the bill until you ask for it (l'addition, s'il vous plaît).", "Greeting friends with a kiss on each cheek (la bise) is common, but a handshake is fine for strangers."], "safety": "France is generally safe, but pickpocketing is common in Paris, especially on the metro and around tourist sights. Watch out for petition and friendship bracelet scams near Sacré-Coeur and the Eiffel Tower. Strikes can disrupt trains, so check before you travel.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch runs from about 12 to 2pm and dinner from 7:30pm, and many kitchens close in between.", "Covered markets and bakeries are the cheapest way to eat, and most towns hold a market a couple of mornings a week.", "Public fountains in cities are drinkable unless marked 'eau non potable', so refill your bottle.", "Service is included by law (service compris), so tipping is optional; rounding up or leaving a euro or two is plenty.", "Vegetarian choices are growing in cities, but in the countryside you'll often build a meal from cheese, salads and sides."], "drinks": [{"name": "Pastis", "desc": "An anise spirit topped up with cold water, drunk as an aperitif in the south. A glass costs $3 to $5 in a bar."}, {"name": "Cidre", "desc": "Dry or sweet apple cider from Brittany and Normandy, often served in a bowl with crêpes. Expect $4 to $6 in a crêperie."}], "greet": "Strangers shake hands lightly, while friends do la bise, and the number of kisses varies by region from one to four. Use vous with anyone you don't know.", "dress": "Smart casual blends in, especially in Paris, where gym wear stands out. Many public pools require men to wear tight swim trunks rather than board shorts.", "table": "Keep both hands on the table, not in your lap, and put bread straight on the tablecloth beside your plate. Splitting the bill item by item is uncommon, so groups usually divide it evenly.", "faith": "France is officially secular, with Catholicism the largest faith and sizeable Muslim, Protestant and Jewish communities. Speak quietly in churches, avoid wandering around during Mass, and turn off your flash.", "phrases": [["Bonjour", "Hello"], ["Merci", "Thank you"], ["C'est combien ?", "How much is it?"], ["Excusez-moi", "Excuse me"], ["Je suis végétarien(ne)", "I'm vegetarian"]]},
  "italy": {"around": "Trenitalia and Italo run fast trains between cities, and booking early gets cheap fares, while regional trains are cheaper and don't need booking. FlixBus and Itabus offer cheap long routes, and Uber is limited, so taxis or FreeNow are the main options. Ferries link the mainland to Sicily, Sardinia and the islands. In cities, walking is often the best way around.", "money": "Italy uses the euro, and cards are widely accepted now, though small bars, markets and some rural places still prefer cash. Use bank ATMs (bancomat) and avoid the private Euronet ones. Most restaurants add a small cover charge (coperto) per person, so tipping is optional, and leaving a little for good service is enough. Haggling isn't done except at flea markets.", "sleep": "Hostels are common in Rome, Florence, Venice, Milan and Naples, and family-run B&Bs are good value elsewhere. Agriturismi offer farm stays in the countryside, and campsites are common along the coasts and lakes. Book ahead in summer in Cinque Terre, Amalfi and Venice.", "eat": ["Pizza napoletana: soft wood-fired pizza from Naples", "Cacio e pepe: pasta with pecorino and black pepper", "Arancini: fried rice balls stuffed with ragù or cheese", "Gelato: dense Italian ice cream"], "data": "TIM, Vodafone, WindTre and Iliad sell SIMs that need passport registration, and EU visitors can roam at home rates. Coverage is good across the country, and free wifi is common.", "heads_up": "Validate paper regional train tickets in the machines before boarding, or you can be fined.", "food": [{"name": "Pizza napoletana", "wiki": "Neapolitan pizza", "desc": "Soft, wood-fired pizza with a puffy crust from Naples, where a margherita costs just a few euros. The real deal is simple and cheap.", "price": "$6 to $10", "when": "Dinner, at Naples pizzerias", "order": "A margherita or marinara is the true test"}, {"name": "Cacio e pepe", "wiki": "Cacio e pepe", "desc": "Roman pasta with pecorino cheese and black pepper, simple and delicious. A cheap trattoria classic.", "price": "$10 to $14", "when": "Lunch or dinner, in Roman trattorias", "order": "Try its cousins carbonara and amatriciana too"}, {"name": "Arancini", "wiki": "Arancini", "desc": "Fried rice balls stuffed with ragù or cheese, a Sicilian street food. Cheap and filling, from bakeries and street stalls.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Snack, from bakeries and rosticcerie", "order": "In Palermo say arancina; in Catania say arancino"}, {"name": "Pizza al taglio", "wiki": "Pizza al taglio", "desc": "Pizza by the slice, sold by weight in Rome. A cheap lunch on the go.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch on the go in Rome", "order": "Point and show how wide you want the slice"}, {"name": "Gelato", "wiki": "Gelato", "desc": "Dense Italian ice cream, best from places where the colors look natural and it's not piled high. A small cup is a few euros.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Afternoon or evening passeggiata", "order": "Two flavours fit in even the smallest cup"}, {"name": "Spritz", "wiki": "Spritz (cocktail)", "desc": "Prosecco with Aperol or Campari and soda, the classic aperitivo drink from the Veneto. Often comes with free snacks.", "price": "$4 to $9", "when": "Aperitivo hour, roughly 6pm to 8pm", "order": "Venetians often order a Select spritz instead of Aperol"}], "food_tip": "Aperitivo hour in the north often gets you free snacks or even a buffet with a drink. Drinking coffee standing at the bar is much cheaper than sitting down, and supermarkets and bakeries are good for cheap picnics.", "culture": ["Dress modestly in churches, covering shoulders and knees, especially at the Vatican and St Mark's in Venice, where you can be turned away.", "Don't order a cappuccino after lunch, as Italians drink milky coffee only in the morning. An espresso (just ask for un caffè) is fine anytime.", "Meals are slow, and the waiter won't bring the bill until you ask for it (il conto, per favore).", "Greet shopkeepers with buongiorno or buonasera, and learn a few words of Italian, which goes a long way.", "Some cities have rules against sitting on monuments, eating on church steps or swimming in fountains, with fines for breaking them."], "safety": "Italy is generally safe, but pickpocketing is common in Rome, Florence, Venice and Naples, especially on buses and around tourist sights. Watch out for distraction scams, like people offering roses or bracelets, and fake petitions. Train strikes are frequent, so check before you travel.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch runs about 1pm to 2:30pm and dinner rarely starts before 8pm, and many restaurants close in between.", "Tap water is safe, and the free drinking fountains (nasoni in Rome) are great for refilling bottles.", "Vegetarians eat well, with pasta, pizza and vegetable contorni on every menu.", "Many menus add a small coperto, a cover charge for bread and seating, which is legal and normal.", "Tipping isn't expected, though rounding up or leaving a euro or two for good service is kind."], "drinks": [{"name": "Limoncello", "desc": "A sweet, strong lemon liqueur from the Amalfi Coast, served ice cold after dinner. A small glass costs $3 to $5, and some places offer it free."}, {"name": "Caffè", "desc": "A shot of espresso drunk standing at the bar, the way most Italians take it, for about $1.30 to $1.80."}], "greet": "Friends greet with two cheek kisses, starting on the right, while strangers shake hands. Say \"buongiorno\" until mid-afternoon and \"buonasera\" after.", "dress": "Italians dress neatly even for casual outings, so beachwear stays at the beach. Cover shoulders and knees in every church.", "table": "Bread is for mopping sauce, not for dipping in oil, and parmesan never goes on seafood pasta. Twirl spaghetti with a fork only, no spoon.", "faith": "Most Italians are Catholic, though many don't attend Mass regularly. Keep quiet in churches, don't wander around during services and check if photos are allowed.", "phrases": [["Buongiorno", "Good morning"], ["Grazie mille", "Thanks a lot"], ["Quanto costa?", "How much is it?"], ["Il conto, per favore", "The bill, please"], ["Permesso", "Excuse me (to get past)"]]},
  "greece": {"around": "KTEL buses link mainland towns, and ferries connect the islands, with Ferryhopper useful for checking routes and prices. FreeNow and Uber work in Athens, and the Athens metro is cheap and easy. Book ferries ahead in July and August, as they fill up fast. Renting a scooter or ATV on islands is popular, but be careful on the roads.", "money": "Greece uses the euro, and cards work in most places, though carry cash for small tavernas, kiosks and islands. ATMs are common, but avoid the Euronet ones with high fees. Rounding up or leaving about 5 to 10 percent is common in tavernas, and haggling isn't done in shops, though you can try at markets. Some islands charge a small tourist tax on accommodation.", "sleep": "Hostels in Athens and on islands like Ios, Santorini, Mykonos and Corfu are cheap, and rooms for rent (domatia) are common elsewhere. Campsites are found on many islands and along the coasts. Book ahead on islands in high season.", "eat": ["Souvlaki: grilled meat skewers in pita with tzatziki", "Spanakopita: spinach and feta pie in flaky filo", "Moussaka: layered eggplant, minced meat and béchamel", "Loukoumades: fried dough balls drizzled with honey"], "data": "Cosmote, Vodafone and Nova sell SIMs that need ID registration, and EU visitors can roam at home rates. Coverage is good on the mainland and most islands, and free wifi is common.", "heads_up": "Summer heat can be extreme, so carry water and avoid hiking in the middle of the day.", "food": [{"name": "Souvlaki", "wiki": "Souvlaki", "desc": "Grilled meat skewers in pita with tzatziki, tomatoes and onions, a cheap street food staple. A few euros for a wrap.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Lunch or late night, at grill shops", "order": "Ask for kalamaki, the stick, without the pita"}, {"name": "Gyros", "wiki": "Gyros", "desc": "Meat cooked on a vertical spit and wrapped in pita with fries and sauce. Cheap and filling.", "price": "$4 to $6", "when": "Late night, after drinks", "order": "Choose pork or chicken, pork is the classic"}, {"name": "Spanakopita", "wiki": "Savory spinach pie", "desc": "Spinach and feta pie in flaky filo pastry, sold at bakeries. A cheap breakfast or snack.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Breakfast, from bakeries", "order": "Tiropita is the cheese-only version"}, {"name": "Moussaka", "wiki": "Moussaka", "desc": "Layered eggplant, minced meat and béchamel, baked until golden. A taverna classic.", "price": "$10 to $15", "when": "Dinner at tavernas", "order": "Ask if it's made fresh that day"}, {"name": "Greek salad", "wiki": "Greek salad", "desc": "Tomatoes, cucumbers, onions, olives and a slab of feta with olive oil, called horiatiki. On every menu and cheap.", "price": "$8 to $12", "when": "Lunch or dinner, as a shared starter", "order": "One salad usually feeds two"}, {"name": "Loukoumades", "wiki": "Lokma", "desc": "Fried dough balls drizzled with honey and cinnamon. A sweet treat from specialist shops.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Afternoon or evening dessert", "order": "Try them with chocolate or ice cream"}], "food_tip": "Gyros and souvlaki are the budget heroes, and bakeries are great for cheap breakfast pies. Tavernas often bring free dessert or a shot at the end of the meal.", "culture": ["Cover shoulders and knees in churches and monasteries, and women may need to wear a skirt at some monasteries, including at Meteora, where wraps are usually provided.", "Greeks are warm and sociable, and among friends a kiss on each cheek is the usual greeting, while a handshake is fine with strangers.", "The open palm pushed toward someone (moutza) is very rude, so be careful when waving with your palm facing out.", "Don't flush toilet paper in most places, as the plumbing can't handle it, so use the bin provided.", "Meals are long and social, dinner rarely starts before 9pm, and people often share plates, so order a few dishes for the table."], "safety": "Greece is generally safe, but pickpocketing happens in Athens, especially on the metro and around Monastiraki and Omonia. Watch out for scams like being invited to a bar and then given a huge bill. Summer heat and wildfires can be serious, so follow local advice.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is around 2pm to 4pm and dinner from 9pm, so tavernas feel empty if you arrive at 7pm.", "Bakeries are the cheapest breakfast, and weekly street markets (laiki) sell fruit, olives and cheese at low prices.", "Tap water is safe in Athens and most cities, but many islands use desalinated water, so drink bottled there.", "Vegetarians do well with gemista (stuffed vegetables), fava, briam and the many fasting dishes called nistisima.", "Tipping isn't required, but leaving 5% to 10% or rounding up for good service is common."], "drinks": [{"name": "Freddo espresso", "desc": "Espresso shaken with ice and served over more ice, the drink Greeks nurse for hours in summer. Expect $3 to $4.50."}, {"name": "Tsipouro", "desc": "A strong grape spirit served in small bottles with free mezedes in places like Volos. A small carafe costs about $4 to $7."}], "greet": "A handshake is normal with strangers, and friends kiss on both cheeks. Say kalimera in the morning and kalispera in the evening.", "dress": "Beachwear is for the beach, so throw on a shirt in towns. Cover shoulders and knees for churches and monasteries.", "table": "Plates are shared and meals are slow, so don't rush the waiter. The bill usually only comes when you ask for it.", "faith": "Most Greeks are Greek Orthodox, and the church is part of daily life and national identity. In churches, dress modestly, speak quietly and avoid photos during services.", "phrases": [["Yia sas (Γεια σας)", "Hello"], ["Efcharistó (Ευχαριστώ)", "Thank you"], ["Parakaló (Παρακαλώ)", "Please, or you're welcome"], ["Póso káni? (Πόσο κάνει;)", "How much?"], ["Yamas! (Γειά μας!)", "Cheers!"]]},
  "germany": {"around": "Deutsche Bahn covers the whole country, and Sparpreis fares booked a few weeks ahead are much cheaper than walk-up tickets, so plan your long hops. If you're staying a while, the Deutschlandticket (around 63 euros a month in 2026) gives unlimited travel on regional trains, buses, trams and U-Bahns nationwide, which is amazing value for slow travel. FlixBus is often the cheapest way between cities, and BlaBlaCar rides are popular. Expect DB delays and missed connections, and build some slack into your plans.", "money": "Germany uses the euro, and while cards are now accepted in most places, it's still a surprisingly cash-loving country, so carry some notes for bakeries, Spätis, beer gardens and old-school bars. Use bank ATMs (Sparkasse, Volksbank and the like) and steer clear of standalone machines with silly fees. In restaurants, round up or add about 5 to 10 percent, and tell the server the total you want to pay as you hand over the money rather than leaving it on the table. Haggling only happens at flea markets.", "sleep": "Berlin, Hamburg, Munich, Cologne and Leipzig are packed with hostels, and dorm prices are fair by western European standards except in Munich. The official youth hostels (Jugendherbergen) are spread across the country, including in small towns and nature spots, and campsites are common in the countryside. Munich prices go through the roof during Oktoberfest, so book months ahead or stay outside the city.", "eat": ["Currywurst: sliced sausage with curry ketchup", "Brezel: soft pretzel with coarse salt", "Döner kebab: spiced meat in flatbread, a cheap city staple", "Schnitzel: breaded and fried thin pork or veal cutlet"], "data": "Telekom, Vodafone and O2 SIMs need ID verification, which is easiest done in a shop, and EU visitors can roam at home rates. Coverage is good in cities but can be patchy on trains and in rural areas.", "heads_up": "Most shops and supermarkets close on Sundays, so stock up on Saturday or use the shops at big train stations.", "food": [{"name": "Döner kebab", "wiki": "Doner kebab", "desc": "Spiced meat in toasted flatbread with salad and garlic or chili sauce, perfected by Turkish-German cooks in Berlin. Still one of the cheapest full meals in the country.", "price": "$6 to $9", "when": "Late night or a quick lunch", "order": "Say 'mit alles' for everything, 'scharf' for chilli"}, {"name": "Currywurst", "wiki": "Currywurst", "desc": "Sliced pork sausage drowned in curry ketchup with a sprinkle of curry powder, usually with fries. A Berlin and Ruhr street food icon from snack stands.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Snack at Imbiss stands, Berlin especially", "order": "Order mit or ohne Darm, with or without skin"}, {"name": "Brezel", "wiki": "Pretzel", "desc": "Soft, chewy pretzel with coarse salt, sold at every bakery and beer garden, especially in Bavaria. A cheap snack or breakfast with butter.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast, or with beer in Bavaria", "order": "A Butterbrezel, sliced with butter, is a classic breakfast"}, {"name": "Bratwurst", "wiki": "Bratwurst", "desc": "Grilled sausage in a roll with mustard, sold at markets, football matches and Christmas markets. Each region swears by its own version.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Markets, football games and festivals", "order": "Comes in a bread roll with mustard"}, {"name": "Schnitzel", "wiki": "Schnitzel", "desc": "Thin pork cutlet breaded and fried, served with potato salad or fries in pubs and beer halls. Big, filling and fairly cheap at lunch.", "price": "$14 to $22", "when": "Lunch or dinner at a Gasthaus", "order": "Wiener Schnitzel is veal; pork versions are cheaper"}, {"name": "Käsespätzle", "wiki": "Spätzle", "desc": "Soft egg noodles baked with lots of cheese and topped with crispy fried onions, a southern comfort food. Great after a day in the Alps.", "price": "$11 to $16", "when": "Swabia and the Alps, especially in winter", "order": "Look for crispy fried onions on top"}], "food_tip": "Bakeries, döner shops and supermarkets like Aldi and Lidl will keep costs right down, and the Mittagstisch lunch specials at local restaurants are good value. Keep your bottles and cans, as returning them gets you the deposit (Pfand) back.", "culture": ["Rules and order matter here, so wait for the green man before crossing even if the road is empty, especially with kids around, and stay out of bike lanes.", "Sundays are a quiet day: most shops are closed and noisy activities like loud music or recycling glass are frowned upon.", "Recycling is serious business, with separate bins for paper, plastic packaging, organic waste and glass by color, so sort your rubbish properly at hostels.", "Germans tend to be direct and honest, which can feel blunt, but it isn't meant as rude. Use Sie (formal you) with strangers and older people.", "Nudity is normal in saunas, which are usually mixed and swimwear-free, and at some lakes and beaches (FKK areas). Making a Nazi salute or displaying Nazi symbols, even as a joke, is illegal."], "safety": "Germany is very safe, but pickpocketing happens in tourist areas, crowded trains and at festivals like Oktoberfest. Be a bit careful around big train stations at night. Plain-clothes ticket inspectors on public transport hand out fines on the spot if you don't have a valid ticket, so always buy and validate first.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is often the main hot meal, while dinner at home (Abendbrot) can be bread, cheese and cold cuts.", "Tap water is excellent, but restaurants rarely serve it for free, so carry a bottle.", "Round up or add 5 to 10 percent, and tell the server the total as you pay rather than leaving cash on the table.", "Cash is still common in small places, so carry some euros even though cards are spreading.", "Vegetarian and vegan food is easy to find, especially in Berlin, Hamburg and Leipzig."], "drinks": [{"name": "Apfelschorle", "desc": "Apple juice mixed with sparkling water, the go to soft drink everywhere. Expect $3 to $4 in a café."}, {"name": "Radler", "desc": "Half beer, half lemonade, perfect for beer gardens on hot days. A half litre costs about $4 to $6."}], "greet": "A firm handshake with eye contact, plus 'Hallo' or 'Guten Tag'. You'll hear 'Moin' in the north and 'Grüß Gott' in Bavaria, and hugs are for friends.", "dress": "Casual, practical clothing works almost everywhere, with a rain jacket handy most of the year. Cover shoulders in churches, and in Bavaria locals happily wear dirndl and lederhosen to festivals.", "table": "Say 'Guten Appetit' before eating, and look each person in the eye when you clink glasses and say 'Prost'. Keep your hands on the table, and expect to share long tables in beer gardens.", "faith": "Around 45 percent of Germans are Catholic or Protestant, nearly as many have no religion, and there is a sizeable Muslim minority. In churches, keep your voice down, take off hats and don't wander about during services.", "phrases": [["Hallo", "Hello"], ["Danke", "Thank you"], ["Was kostet das?", "How much does it cost?"], ["Zahlen, bitte", "The bill, please"], ["Leitungswasser, bitte", "Tap water, please"]]},
  "uk": {"around": "Trains are fast but expensive, so book Advance tickets early, and get a Railcard if you're 16 to 30, which saves a third on most fares. National Express and Megabus are much cheaper for long routes, and Uber and Bolt work in major cities. In London, tap a contactless card or phone for the tube and buses, which is cheaper than paper tickets and caps your daily spend. Scotland and Wales are great by bus and train, but the Highlands need patience.", "money": "The UK uses the pound sterling, and cards and contactless are accepted almost everywhere, with some places now card-only. ATMs (cash machines) are usually free to use from banks, but avoid the ones in shops that charge a fee. Tipping around 10 to 12.5 percent is normal in sit-down restaurants, but check the bill for a service charge first, and you don't tip at the bar in pubs. Haggling isn't done outside markets.", "sleep": "Hostels are common in cities, especially London, Edinburgh, Manchester and Liverpool, and YHA and Hostelling Scotland hostels cover national parks and rural areas. Wild camping is legal in most of Scotland, which is a big budget saver. Book well ahead in Edinburgh during the August festivals, as prices explode.", "eat": ["Fish and chips: battered fish with thick fries", "Full English breakfast: eggs, bacon, sausage, beans and toast", "Sunday roast: roast meat with potatoes and gravy", "Haggis: Scottish dish of spiced sheep offal and oats"], "data": "EE, Vodafone, O2 and Three sell SIMs without registration, and eSIMs are easy too. Coverage is good in cities but patchy in the Highlands and rural Wales.", "heads_up": "Traffic drives on the left, so look right first when crossing the road.", "food": [{"name": "Fish and chips", "wiki": "Fish and chips", "desc": "Battered fish with thick-cut chips, often with mushy peas, eaten from the paper by the seaside. A chippy portion is cheap and filling.", "price": "$10 to $16", "when": "Friday nights and seaside days", "order": "Try a chip butty or curry sauce on chips"}, {"name": "Full English breakfast", "wiki": "Full breakfast", "desc": "Eggs, bacon, sausage, baked beans, toast, tomato and mushrooms, served in cafes. Keeps you going until dinner.", "price": "$9 to $15", "when": "Weekend brunch at greasy spoon cafes", "order": "Ask for black pudding or a veggie version"}, {"name": "Sunday roast", "wiki": "Sunday roast", "desc": "Roast meat with potatoes, Yorkshire pudding, vegetables and gravy, served in pubs on Sundays. A proper British tradition.", "price": "$18 to $26", "when": "Sunday lunch at the pub", "order": "Book ahead, popular pubs sell out by 2pm"}, {"name": "Chicken tikka masala", "wiki": "Chicken tikka masala", "desc": "Spiced chicken in a creamy tomato sauce, once voted Britain's favorite dish. Curry houses are everywhere, and many are BYOB.", "price": "$13 to $20", "when": "Friday or Saturday night dinner", "order": "Ask for it medium if you don't like heat"}, {"name": "Pasty", "wiki": "Pasty", "desc": "Shortcrust pastry filled with beef, potato, swede and onion, from Cornwall. A cheap, filling lunch.", "price": "$5 to $8", "when": "Lunch on the go"}, {"name": "Haggis", "wiki": "Haggis", "desc": "Scotland's national dish of spiced sheep offal, oats and onion, served with neeps and tatties. Tastier than it sounds.", "price": "$12 to $20", "when": "Burns Night and pub dinners", "order": "Try it in a breakfast or as bon bons"}], "food_tip": "Supermarket meal deals (a sandwich, snack and drink for a few pounds) are a budget lifesaver, and many pubs do cheap weekday food. Greggs is a cheap bakery chain for sausage rolls and pasties.", "culture": ["Queuing is taken very seriously, so wait your turn and never cut in line.", "Pubs are the heart of social life, and you order and pay at the bar. If you're with a group, people often take turns buying rounds.", "British people tend to be polite and indirect, and say sorry a lot. Sarcasm and dry humor are common.", "Stand on the right on escalators in London, as people walk on the left.", "Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland have their own identities, so don't call everyone English."], "safety": "The UK is generally safe, but pickpocketing and phone snatching by cyclists or mopeds happen in London. Be careful at night in city centers when people are drunk. Hiking in the hills can be dangerous in bad weather, so check the forecast.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is around 12 to 2 and dinner from about 6, and pub kitchens often stop serving by 9pm.", "Tap water is safe and restaurants and pubs must give it free if you ask.", "Vegetarian and vegan food is easy to find, and most menus label it clearly.", "Many restaurants add a 12.5 percent service charge, so check the bill, and tip about 10 percent if they don't.", "Street food markets in big cities are a cheap way to eat global food for $8 to $14 a dish."], "drinks": [{"name": "Real ale", "desc": "Cask-conditioned bitter pulled by hand pump, served cool rather than cold. A pint costs about $6 to $9."}, {"name": "Builder's tea", "desc": "Strong black tea with milk and often sugar, the national fuel. A mug in a cafe is $2 to $3."}], "greet": "A handshake or a simple hello works, and hugs are for friends. People often ask \"you alright?\", which just means hello.", "dress": "Dress is casual and layers are wise as the weather changes fast. There's no need to cover up except in some places of worship.", "table": "Keep your fork in your left hand and knife in your right, and say please and thank you a lot. In pubs, offer to buy a round if others buy you one.", "faith": "The UK is largely secular, with Christianity the biggest faith and large Muslim, Hindu and Sikh communities. Be quiet in cathedrals, and remove shoes and cover your head in mosques and gurdwaras.", "phrases": [["Cheers", "Thanks, or bye"], ["Cuppa", "A cup of tea"], ["Skint", "Broke, out of money"], ["Knackered", "Very tired"], ["Wee", "Small (in Scotland)"]]},
  "ireland": {"around": "Bus Éireann and private coaches like Citylink and Dublin Coach link most towns cheaply, while Irish Rail covers the main routes from Dublin to Cork, Galway, Limerick and others. In Dublin, a Leap Card or contactless tap saves money on buses, Luas trams and DART trains. Uber in Dublin only books licensed taxis, so it's not cheaper. To see the wild west coast properly, a rental car or a budget day tour from Dublin or Galway is often the easiest option, and booking ahead helps in summer.", "money": "Ireland uses the euro, and cards and contactless are accepted almost everywhere, pubs and small shops included. Bank ATMs are free to use. Tipping about 10 percent is normal in sit-down restaurants, but nobody expects a tip for drinks at the bar. Ireland is pricey, especially Dublin, so budget accordingly.", "sleep": "Hostels are common in Dublin, Galway, Cork and along the Wild Atlantic Way, though Dublin dorms are some of the priciest in Europe. B&Bs are the classic choice in rural areas, and many hostels in small towns are cozy and full of character. Book ahead in summer and for big events like St Patrick's Day.", "eat": ["Irish stew: lamb or beef stew with potatoes and carrots", "Soda bread: dense bread made without yeast", "Boxty: potato pancake", "Guinness: dark stout"], "data": "Vodafone, Three and Eir sell SIMs, and EU visitors can roam at home rates. Coverage is good in towns but patchy in remote parts of the west.", "heads_up": "The weather changes fast, so bring a rain jacket whatever the season.", "food": [{"name": "Irish stew", "wiki": "Irish stew", "desc": "Lamb or mutton slow-cooked with potatoes, carrots and onions, a hearty pub classic. Perfect after a rainy day.", "price": "$15 to $22", "when": "Pub lunch, especially on cold days", "order": "Lamb is traditional, beef stews often use Guinness"}, {"name": "Full Irish breakfast", "wiki": "Full breakfast", "desc": "Eggs, rashers, sausages, black and white pudding, beans, tomato and toast. Keeps you going until dinner.", "price": "$12 to $18", "when": "Weekend brunch or a hangover cure", "order": "Ask for both black and white pudding"}, {"name": "Soda bread", "wiki": "Soda bread", "desc": "Dense, slightly sour bread made with baking soda and buttermilk instead of yeast. Served with butter alongside soup or stew.", "price": "$3 to $5 a loaf", "when": "With butter, soup or breakfast", "order": "Brown soda bread is the everyday kind"}, {"name": "Boxty", "wiki": "Boxty", "desc": "Potato pancake from the northwest, served with fillings or as a side. Try it in Sligo or Leitrim.", "price": "$10 to $16", "when": "Pubs and cafés in the north midlands"}, {"name": "Spice bag", "wiki": "Spice bag", "desc": "A takeaway bag of chips, crispy chicken, peppers and onions tossed in spicy seasoning. A cheap late-night favorite.", "price": "$10 to $14", "when": "Late night from a Chinese takeaway", "order": "Add curry sauce for dipping"}, {"name": "Guinness", "wiki": "Stout", "desc": "The famous dark stout, which locals swear tastes better in Ireland. Order a pint at any pub and wait for it to settle.", "price": "$6.50 to $8 a pint", "when": "Evenings in the pub, at any age", "order": "Wait for it to settle before you drink"}], "food_tip": "Supermarkets like Aldi, Lidl and Tesco are much cheaper than eating out, and many pubs do decent lunch specials. Chippers (fish and chip shops) and spice bags are the cheap takeaway options.", "culture": ["The Irish are chatty and friendly, and the craic (fun and banter) is a big part of life, so don't be shy about chatting to people in pubs.", "Rounds are a pub tradition, so if someone buys you a drink, you're expected to get the next one in.", "Northern Ireland is part of the UK, and politics, religion and the Troubles can still be sensitive there, so listen more than you talk.", "Don't call the Republic of Ireland British or part of the UK, and skip the fake Irish accent.", "Trad music sessions in pubs are free and often spontaneous, so grab a pint and listen, and keep the chat down during slow songs."], "safety": "Ireland is very safe, but pickpocketing happens in busy parts of Dublin, especially around Temple Bar and O'Connell Street. Weather can change quickly on hikes and coastal walks, so check the forecast and bring layers.", "eat_beats": ["Pubs usually serve food from noon until around 8 or 9pm, and kitchens can close early in rural areas.", "Tap water is safe and free to ask for in any restaurant or pub.", "Vegetarian and vegan options are easy to find in cities, and most menus mark them clearly.", "Tipping 10% to 12% in sit-down restaurants is normal, but you don't tip for drinks at the bar.", "Farmers' markets and food trucks at weekends are good for cheap lunches and local cheese."], "drinks": [{"name": "Irish whiskey", "desc": "Smooth, usually triple distilled whiskey, best tried neat with a drop of water. A measure in a pub costs about $6 to $9."}, {"name": "Red lemonade", "desc": "A sweet, bright red soft drink sold almost only in Ireland, often mixed with whiskey or brandy. About $2 a bottle in shops."}], "greet": "A simple \"how are you?\" or \"what's the story?\" works, and handshakes are normal on meeting. People expect friendly banter rather than formality.", "dress": "Dress is casual everywhere, but pack a waterproof jacket and layers year round. There are no special rules for churches beyond common sense.", "table": "Table manners are relaxed and similar to Britain, with knife and fork. In pubs, order and pay at the bar, and don't skip your round.", "faith": "Most people are Catholic by background, though church attendance has dropped sharply. Visit churches and ancient monastic sites quietly, and don't climb on high crosses or old graves.", "phrases": [["What's the craic?", "What's happening, how are you?"], ["Grand", "Fine, okay"], ["Sláinte", "Cheers, in Irish"], ["Go raibh maith agat", "Thank you, in Irish"], ["Your man", "That guy over there"]]},
  "netherlands": {"around": "NS trains connect every city fast, and you can now just tap in and out with a contactless bank card or phone on trains, trams, metros and buses (OVpay), so no need for paper tickets. Off-peak fares are cheaper, and day trips from Amsterdam to Utrecht, Haarlem, Leiden, Delft or Rotterdam are easy. Renting a bike is the best way to get around towns and the flat countryside. Uber and Bolt work in Amsterdam, but you'll rarely need them.", "money": "The Netherlands uses the euro and is close to cashless, with contactless accepted nearly everywhere and some places refusing cash altogether. Visa and Mastercard work far more widely than they used to, but a few supermarkets and small cafes still only take debit cards, so carry a debit card as backup. Tipping is optional, so round up or leave around 5 to 10 percent for good table service. Haggling isn't a thing outside flea markets.", "sleep": "Amsterdam has loads of hostels but beds aren't cheap, and prices jump on weekends and in summer, so book early or stay in Haarlem, Rotterdam or Utrecht and day-trip in. Campsites around Amsterdam and on the coast are a cheap alternative, and Rotterdam and Utrecht have great hostels. Book ahead for King's Day (late April) and summer weekends.", "eat": ["Stroopwafel: thin waffles with caramel syrup", "Bitterballen: fried breaded meat ragout balls", "Haring: raw herring with onions", "Poffertjes: small fluffy pancakes with powdered sugar"], "data": "KPN, Vodafone and Odido sell SIMs, and EU visitors can roam at home rates. Coverage is excellent across the country, and free wifi is everywhere.", "heads_up": "Don't walk in bike lanes, as cyclists move fast and expect them to be clear.", "food": [{"name": "Stroopwafel", "wiki": "Stroopwafel", "desc": "Two thin waffles stuck together with caramel syrup, best freshly made and warm at markets. Pop a packaged one on top of your coffee to soften it.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Snack at street markets", "order": "Watch it made fresh and eat it warm"}, {"name": "Bitterballen", "wiki": "Bitterballen", "desc": "Crunchy fried balls of meat ragout served with mustard, the classic beer snack in brown cafes. Wait a moment before biting, as the inside is molten.", "price": "$7 to $10", "when": "Late afternoon drinks, the borrel", "order": "A portion of eight comes with mustard for sharing"}, {"name": "Haring", "wiki": "Soused herring", "desc": "Raw brined herring with chopped onions and pickles, eaten whole by the tail or in a bun from fish stands. Best in early summer when the new season catch arrives.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Midday, from fish stands", "order": "Look for \"Hollandse Nieuwe\" signs in June"}, {"name": "Kibbeling", "wiki": "Kibbeling", "desc": "Battered chunks of white fish with garlic sauce, sold at markets and fish stalls. A great option if raw herring isn't your thing.", "price": "$6 to $9", "when": "Lunch at markets and seaside towns", "order": "Ask for remoulade or garlic sauce"}, {"name": "Poffertjes", "wiki": "Poffertjes", "desc": "Tiny fluffy pancakes covered in butter and powdered sugar, served at markets and fairs. Cheap, sweet and very hard to stop eating.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Afternoon treat at markets and fairs", "order": "A portion is usually 10 to 15 pieces"}, {"name": "Patat", "wiki": "French fries", "desc": "Thick fries in a paper cone with mayo, or with mayo, peanut sauce and onions as patatje oorlog. Snack bars sell them everywhere.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Snack, any time, from snack bars", "order": "\"Speciaal\" means mayo, curry ketchup and onions"}], "food_tip": "Supermarkets like Albert Heijn and Jumbo are your budget friends, and snack bars like FEBO sell croquettes and frikandel from coin-op walls. Cheese, bread and fruit from street markets make great cheap picnics.", "culture": ["Dutch people are famously direct and honest, which can come across as blunt, but it's just how conversation works. They also value being on time.", "Bike lanes are sacred, so never walk in them and look both ways before crossing, as bikes come fast and silently.", "Cannabis is tolerated in coffeeshops, but smoking weed in public in Amsterdam's red light district is banned and buying from street dealers is illegal and risky.", "Don't photograph the sex workers in the red light district, as it's disrespectful and can lead to trouble.", "Dutch homes and cafes are casual, but splitting the bill (going Dutch) is normal, so don't be surprised when everyone pays for themselves."], "safety": "The Netherlands is very safe, but bike theft is rampant, so always lock your bike with two locks, and pickpocketing happens around Amsterdam Centraal and on trams. Edibles and strong weed catch a lot of visitors out, so go slow. Never buy drugs from street dealers.", "eat_beats": ["The Dutch eat early, with a simple bread lunch around noon and a warm dinner at about 6pm.", "Tap water is excellent and free to refill, though restaurants rarely bring jugs, so ask for \"kraanwater\".", "Vegetarian and vegan options are easy to find in every city, and supermarkets have big meat-free ranges.", "Many places are card only, and some don't take foreign credit cards, so carry a debit card or contactless phone.", "Service is included in the price, but rounding up or leaving 5 to 10 percent for good table service is common."], "drinks": [{"name": "Jenever", "desc": "Dutch gin, the ancestor of English gin, served in a tulip glass filled to the brim. Bend down and sip the first bit without lifting it, about $3 to $5."}, {"name": "Koffie verkeerd", "desc": "\"Wrong coffee\", a milky coffee like a latte, usually served with a small biscuit for $3 to $4."}], "greet": "A firm handshake and your name is standard with strangers. Friends often give three cheek kisses, starting on the right.", "dress": "Dress is practical and casual, built for cycling and rain. Bring a waterproof jacket, as an umbrella is useless in the wind.", "table": "Expect to be offered one biscuit with your coffee, and it's polite not to reach for a second. Keep your hands on the table, not in your lap.", "faith": "Most Dutch people aren't religious, and Christians and Muslims are the largest faith groups. Churches often charge entry, and you should be quiet during any service.", "phrases": [["Hallo / Goedemorgen", "Hello / Good morning"], ["Dank je wel", "Thank you"], ["Hoeveel kost het?", "How much is it?"], ["Lekker!", "Tasty or great!"], ["Gezellig", "Cosy, fun and sociable"]]},
  "belgium": {"around": "SNCB trains link cities quickly and cheaply, and the country is small enough that Brussels, Ghent, Bruges and Antwerp are all within an hour or so of each other. Weekend return tickets are discounted, and under-26s get cheap youth fares. Uber works in Brussels, and the metro and trams are easy. FlixBus links Belgian cities to Paris, Amsterdam and Cologne cheaply.", "money": "Belgium uses the euro, and cards and contactless are widely accepted, though a few old-school bars and frituur stands may prefer cash. Bank ATMs are easy to find. Service is included in restaurant prices, so tipping is optional, and rounding up is enough for good service. Belgian beer is cheap by European standards, but some strong beers are served in small glasses for a reason.", "sleep": "Hostels are common in Brussels, Ghent, Bruges and Antwerp, and dorm prices are moderate for western Europe. Ghent is a great budget base with lively nightlife and fewer crowds than Bruges. Book ahead for Bruges in summer and Brussels during EU events.", "eat": ["Frites: double-fried fries with mayonnaise", "Moules-frites: mussels with fries", "Belgian waffle: thick waffle with sugar or toppings", "Carbonnade flamande: beef stew cooked in beer"], "data": "Proximus, Orange and Base SIMs need ID registration, and EU visitors can roam at home rates. Coverage is good across the country.", "heads_up": "Pickpocketing is common in Brussels tourist areas, so keep valuables secure.", "food": [{"name": "Frites", "wiki": "French fries", "desc": "Double-fried potato fries served in a paper cone with mayonnaise or other sauces, from frituur stands. Belgians invented them, according to Belgians.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Any time, often as a late snack", "order": "Try andalouse or samurai sauce instead of plain mayo"}, {"name": "Moules-frites", "wiki": "Moules-frites", "desc": "A pot of mussels steamed in white wine, garlic and herbs with a side of fries. Best in the colder months.", "price": "$25 to $35", "when": "Mussel season, roughly July to April", "order": "Marinière, in white wine, is the classic style"}, {"name": "Belgian waffle", "wiki": "Liège waffle", "desc": "Dense, sweet Liège waffles with caramelized sugar are sold hot from stands for a couple of euros. Skip the toppings to taste the real deal.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Afternoon snack from street stands", "order": "Liège waffles are sugary; Brussels ones are light and rectangular"}, {"name": "Carbonnade flamande", "wiki": "Flemish stew", "desc": "Beef slow-cooked in dark Belgian beer with onions and mustard, served with fries. Rich and filling.", "price": "$15 to $25", "when": "Lunch specials and brasseries"}, {"name": "Speculoos", "wiki": "Speculoos", "desc": "Spiced shortcrust biscuits, often served with coffee. Also made into a spread for bread.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "With coffee, any time", "order": "Try the speculoos spread on bread"}, {"name": "Mitraillette", "wiki": "Mitraillette", "desc": "Half a baguette stuffed with meat, fries and sauce, a cheap late-night snack from frituurs in Brussels.", "price": "$7 to $11", "when": "Late night, after drinks"}], "food_tip": "Frituur stands, supermarket picnics and lunch specials (dagschotel or plat du jour) keep costs down. Belgian beer is cheap at bars, but sip slowly, as many are strong.", "culture": ["Belgium is split between Dutch-speaking Flanders in the north and French-speaking Wallonia in the south, with Brussels officially bilingual, so greet in the local language and don't assume everyone speaks French.", "Belgians tend to be polite and low-key, so say hello or bonjour when you walk into a shop and keep your voice down on trains.", "Every beer is served in its own branded glass, and bars take that seriously, so enjoy the ritual and don't be surprised if a strong Trappist comes in a small goblet.", "Among friends, a single kiss on the cheek is the usual greeting, but a handshake is fine with strangers.", "Language and politics are touchy subjects, so avoid jokes about the country splitting up unless you know your company well."], "safety": "Belgium is generally safe, but pickpocketing is common in Brussels tourist areas, especially around Gare du Midi, Brussels-Nord and the Grand Place. Be careful at night around train stations.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is 12 to 2pm and dinner from about 6:30 to 9:30pm, and kitchens in small towns close early.", "Tap water is safe, but restaurants rarely serve it free, so most people order bottled.", "Vegetarians do well in Brussels, Antwerp and especially Ghent, one of Europe's most veggie friendly cities.", "Service is included in the bill, so tipping is optional, though rounding up for good service is common.", "Weekly town markets sell cheese, bread and rotisserie chicken, perfect for a cheap picnic lunch."], "drinks": [{"name": "Jenever", "desc": "A juniper spirit, the ancestor of gin, served in small tulip glasses in Flanders. A glass is around $3 to $5."}, {"name": "Kriek", "desc": "A tart cherry beer brewed from lambic around Brussels. Expect about $4 to $6 a glass in a bar."}], "greet": "Say bonjour or goedendag when entering a shop. In Flanders, start in Dutch or English rather than French.", "dress": "Belgians dress neatly and understated, so smart casual works for dinners. Bring a rain jacket year round.", "table": "Keep both hands on the table and your knife in your right hand. Wait for your host to raise a glass before you drink.", "faith": "Belgium is historically Catholic, though most people today don't practise, and there's a sizeable Muslim minority. Dress respectfully and keep quiet in churches, especially places like the Basilica of the Holy Blood in Bruges.", "phrases": [["Goedendag", "Good day (Dutch)"], ["Bonjour", "Hello (French)"], ["Dank u / Merci", "Thank you (Dutch / French)"], ["Hoeveel kost het?", "How much is it? (Dutch)"], ["Santé! / Schol!", "Cheers! (French / Dutch)"]]},
  "switzerland": {"around": "SBB trains are famously punctual and link the whole country, but they're pricey, so look for Supersaver tickets in the SBB app and consider a Half Fare Card if you're staying more than a few days. The Swiss Travel Pass can be worth it if you're moving a lot and doing mountain trips. Mountain trains and cable cars are very expensive, so check which ones your pass discounts before buying. FlixBus links Zurich, Geneva and Basel to neighboring countries cheaply.", "money": "Switzerland uses the Swiss franc (CHF), not the euro, though some places accept euros at a bad rate with change given in francs. Cards and contactless are accepted almost everywhere, and Twint is the local payment app, but carry a few francs for mountain huts and small farm stalls. Service is included in prices by law, so tipping is optional, and rounding up is plenty. Brace yourself, as this is one of the most expensive countries on the planet.", "sleep": "Hostels are common in cities and mountain resorts, with the Swiss Youth Hostels network covering scenic spots like Interlaken, Zermatt and Lucerne, but even dorms cost a lot. Campsites are a cheaper option in summer, and wild camping is restricted, so stick to official sites. Book ahead in summer and ski season.", "eat": ["Fondue: melted cheese with bread for dipping", "Raclette: melted cheese scraped over potatoes", "Rösti: crispy grated potato pancake", "Älplermagronen: pasta with potatoes, cheese and applesauce"], "data": "Swisscom, Sunrise and Salt SIMs need ID registration, and Switzerland is not covered by EU roaming, so check your plan or get an eSIM. Coverage is good across the country, even in many mountain areas.", "heads_up": "Switzerland is expensive, so shop at supermarkets like Coop or Migros for cheap meals.", "food": [{"name": "Fondue", "wiki": "Fondue", "desc": "Melted cheese with white wine and garlic, eaten by dipping bread on long forks. Best shared in winter at a mountain restaurant.", "price": "$25 to $35 per person", "when": "Winter evenings, shared from one pot", "order": "Moitié-moitié uses Gruyère and Vacherin cheeses"}, {"name": "Raclette", "wiki": "Raclette", "desc": "Melted cheese scraped over boiled potatoes, pickles and onions. A winter favorite from the Valais.", "price": "$10 to $15 a portion", "when": "Winter markets and mountain huts", "order": "Often all you can eat in restaurants"}, {"name": "Rösti", "wiki": "Rösti", "desc": "Crispy grated potato pancake, often topped with cheese or a fried egg. A hearty and relatively cheap dish.", "price": "$15 to $25", "when": "Side to sausage, or a meal itself", "order": "Try it with a fried egg on top"}, {"name": "Älplermagronen", "wiki": "Älplermagronen", "desc": "Alpine mac and cheese with potatoes, onions and a side of applesauce. A cheap and filling hut meal.", "price": "$20 to $28", "when": "Mountain huts after a hike", "order": "Comes with apple sauce on the side"}, {"name": "Zürcher Geschnetzeltes", "wiki": "Zürcher Geschnetzeltes", "desc": "Strips of veal in a creamy mushroom sauce, served with rösti. A Zurich classic.", "price": "$30 to $45", "when": "Zurich restaurants, a classic dinner", "order": "Always served with rösti"}, {"name": "Swiss chocolate", "wiki": "Swiss chocolate", "desc": "Some of the best chocolate in the world, cheap in supermarkets. Grab a bar of Cailler or Frey.", "price": "$2 to $6 a bar", "when": "Any time, from supermarkets", "order": "Supermarket own brands are cheap and very good"}], "food_tip": "Supermarkets like Coop, Migros, Aldi and Lidl are your lifeline, with good value ready meals and discounted items near closing time. Tap water is excellent, and public fountains are safe to drink from.", "culture": ["Punctuality is practically a national value, so if you agree to meet at 9, show up at 9, and remember trains leave on the dot.", "Quiet hours are taken seriously, usually from around 10pm and all day Sunday, when even recycling glass or running a washing machine can earn you a note from the neighbors.", "Recycling rules are strict, and many towns make you buy official rubbish bags, so follow your hostel's system rather than dumping things in any bin.", "There are four national languages, so say grüezi in the German-speaking parts, bonjour around Geneva and Lausanne, and buongiorno in Ticino.", "The Swiss are polite but reserved, so keep your voice down on trains and don't expect instant small talk with strangers."], "safety": "Switzerland is one of the safest places you'll ever travel, with very little crime beyond the odd pickpocket at big stations. The real dangers are in the mountains, where weather turns fast, so check the forecast, stick to marked trails and turn back if it gets sketchy. Make sure your insurance covers mountain rescue, as helicopter call-outs cost a fortune.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is around noon and many kitchens stop serving at 2pm, with dinner from about 6:30pm.", "Restaurant daily lunch specials, called Tagesmenü or plat du jour, are the best value sit-down meals at $20 to $30.", "Supermarket takeaway counters, bakery sandwiches and bratwurst stands are the cheap hot food options.", "Vegetarian food is easy to find, and chains of vegetarian buffets charge by weight in bigger cities.", "Service is included in prices by law, so tipping is just rounding up a few francs if you're happy."], "drinks": [{"name": "Kirsch", "desc": "A clear cherry brandy, splashed into fondue or sipped after it to help digestion. A shot costs about $5 to $8."}, {"name": "Swiss wine", "desc": "Hardly exported, so try it here, like Chasselas from Lavaux vineyards or Merlot from Ticino. A glass costs around $7 to $10."}], "greet": "Shake hands firmly with everyone on meeting and leaving, and friends exchange three cheek kisses. Greet shopkeepers and fellow hikers.", "dress": "Smart casual works in cities, and in the mountains proper boots and layers matter more than style. No special rules for churches beyond being tidy.", "table": "Wait for everyone to be served and for \"en Guete\" or \"bon appétit\" before eating. In fondue, if you drop bread in the pot, tradition says you buy the next round.", "faith": "People are roughly split between Catholic, Protestant and non-religious. Keep quiet in churches and avoid visiting during services.", "phrases": [["Grüezi", "Hello, in Swiss German"], ["Merci vielmal", "Thanks a lot, in Swiss German"], ["Wie viel kostet das?", "How much is it?"], ["En Guete", "Enjoy your meal"], ["Uf Widerluege", "Goodbye"]]},
  "austria": {"around": "ÖBB and Westbahn trains link Vienna, Salzburg, Graz and Innsbruck, and cheap Sparschiene fares appear when you book a few weeks ahead. Postbus regional buses reach Hallstatt and the Alpine valleys, and Uber and Bolt work in Vienna. If you're staying a while, the Klimaticket gives unlimited public transport across the country. Vienna's public transport is excellent and cheap with a 24 or 72-hour pass.", "money": "Austria uses the euro, and cards work almost everywhere, though some mountain huts, bakeries and old-school cafes still prefer cash. Bank ATMs are easy to find. In restaurants, round up or add about 5 to 10 percent, and tell the server the total you want to pay as you hand over the money rather than leaving it on the table. Haggling isn't done.", "sleep": "Hostel dorms are easy to find in Vienna, Salzburg and Innsbruck, while family-run Gasthof and Pension rooms fill the gap elsewhere. Mountain huts in the Alps are a great budget option for hikers, especially with an Alpine Club membership. Hallstatt has very few beds, so stay nearby or book well ahead.", "eat": ["Wiener Schnitzel: thin breaded veal or pork cutlet, fried golden", "Käsekrainer: cheese-filled sausage from a Vienna street stand", "Kaiserschmarrn: torn fluffy pancake with powdered sugar and fruit compote", "Apfelstrudel: thin pastry rolled around spiced apple and raisins"], "data": "Local prepaid SIMs from A1, Magenta or Drei are cheap, EU SIMs roam free, and coverage is strong except in some deep Alpine valleys.", "heads_up": "Most shops close on Sundays, so stock up on groceries on Saturday.", "food": [{"name": "Wiener Schnitzel", "wiki": "Wiener schnitzel", "desc": "Thin breaded veal cutlet fried golden, served with potato salad and lemon. Pork versions are cheaper.", "price": "$16 to $26", "when": "Lunch or dinner at a Gasthaus", "order": "Schnitzel vom Schwein is the cheaper pork version"}, {"name": "Käsekrainer", "wiki": "Käsekrainer", "desc": "Cheese-filled sausage from a Vienna Würstelstand, served with mustard and bread. The ultimate late-night snack.", "price": "$5 to $7", "when": "Late at night from sausage stands", "order": "Ask for it with süßer Senf, sweet mustard"}, {"name": "Kaiserschmarrn", "wiki": "Kaiserschmarrn", "desc": "Torn fluffy pancake with powdered sugar and plum compote. A filling treat at mountain huts.", "price": "$10 to $15", "when": "Lunch at mountain huts", "order": "Easily shared between two"}, {"name": "Apfelstrudel", "wiki": "Apple strudel", "desc": "Thin pastry rolled around spiced apple and raisins, served warm with cream. A classic cafe dessert.", "price": "$5 to $8 a slice", "when": "Afternoon at coffee houses", "order": "Mit Schlag means with whipped cream"}, {"name": "Sachertorte", "wiki": "Sachertorte", "desc": "Rich chocolate cake with apricot jam, the most famous Viennese cake. Try it at a traditional coffee house.", "price": "$6 to $9 a slice", "when": "Afternoon coffee and cake"}], "food_tip": "Würstelstands, bakeries and supermarket deli counters (try Billa or Spar) are cheap, and the weekday Mittagsmenü at local Gasthäuser often gets you soup and a main for a good price. Tap water in Vienna comes straight from the Alps and is free in restaurants if you ask.", "culture": ["Say Grüß Gott or Servus when you walk into a shop, cafe or mountain hut, and a friendly Wiederschauen when you leave.", "Viennese coffee houses are an institution where you can sit for hours over one coffee and a newspaper, and nobody will rush you, so don't rush the waiter either.", "Austrians aren't Germans, so don't lump them together, and they'll appreciate it if you know the difference.", "Jaywalking is frowned upon and can get you a fine, so wait for the green man, and stay out of cycle lanes in Vienna.", "Saunas are usually mixed and swimwear-free, so go with the flow or skip it, but don't wear a swimsuit into a textile-free area."], "safety": "Austria is very safe, with low crime and only occasional pickpocketing around Vienna's busiest spots and stations. The main risks are in the mountains, where weather changes fast, so check forecasts, stick to marked trails and carry layers even in summer. Get insurance that covers mountain rescue if you're hiking or skiing.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is often the main meal from noon to 2, and many village kitchens close by 9pm.", "Cash is still common in small places, so carry some euros alongside your card.", "Vienna has lots of vegetarian and vegan cafes, and dumpling dishes like Käsespätzle are easy veggie options.", "Round up to the nearest euro or add 5 to 10 percent, and tell the server the total when you pay rather than leaving it on the table."], "drinks": [{"name": "Sturm", "desc": "Cloudy, half-fermented young wine sold only in September and October, sweet, fizzy and stronger than it tastes. A glass costs about $3 to $5."}, {"name": "Grüner Veltliner", "desc": "Austria's crisp, peppery white wine. A glass at a Viennese Heuriger wine tavern costs around $4 to $6."}], "greet": "Shake hands firmly with eye contact, and close friends may kiss on both cheeks. Use titles and surnames until invited to switch.", "dress": "Austrians dress neatly, especially for the opera or theatre. Cover shoulders in churches, and you'll see dirndls and lederhosen at festivals.", "table": "Say Mahlzeit or guten Appetit before eating, and clink glasses with eye contact while saying Prost. Keep your hands above the table.", "faith": "Most Austrians are Catholic, though many don't practise. Keep quiet in churches and avoid sightseeing during Sunday Mass.", "phrases": [["Grüß Gott", "Hello"], ["Servus", "Hi or bye (informal)"], ["Danke schön", "Thank you"], ["Was kostet das?", "How much does it cost?"], ["Zahlen, bitte", "The bill, please"]]},
  "czechia": {"around": "Trains and buses are cheap and good, with RegioJet, Leo Express and the national ČD competing on the main routes, so compare prices. RegioJet buses are often the best option for Český Krumlov, and Bolt and Uber work in Prague and Brno. Prague's metro, trams and buses are cheap and excellent, and a 24 or 72-hour ticket is great value. Tickets must be validated or bought in the app before boarding, as inspectors are strict.", "money": "Czechia uses the koruna (CZK), not the euro, though touristy spots in Prague will happily take euros at a terrible rate. Cards and contactless are accepted almost everywhere, even in pubs. Use bank ATMs and always choose to be charged in koruna when a machine or card terminal offers to convert, as the dynamic conversion rates are a rip-off. Leaving about 10 percent in restaurants is normal, usually by telling the server the rounded-up total as you pay.", "sleep": "Prague has tons of hostel dorms, many in the Old Town, New Town and Žižkov, at decent prices for central Europe. Small towns have cheap family pensions (penzion), and Brno and Olomouc are great budget bases with fewer tourists. Český Krumlov fills up on summer weekends, so book ahead.", "eat": ["Svíčková: beef in creamy vegetable sauce with bread dumplings", "Smažený sýr: fried breaded cheese with tartar sauce", "Bramboráky: crisp garlicky potato pancakes", "Kofola: Czech herbal cola, often on tap"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from O2, T-Mobile or Vodafone are easy to buy, EU SIMs roam free, and coverage is good across the country.", "heads_up": "Avoid street money changers and exchange booths with zero commission signs, and order taxis through an app instead of hailing them in central Prague.", "food": [{"name": "Svíčková", "wiki": "Svíčková", "desc": "Beef sirloin in a creamy root vegetable sauce with bread dumplings, cranberries and whipped cream. The quintessential Czech Sunday lunch.", "price": "$8 to $12", "when": "Lunch, as a weekday denní menu", "order": "Expect a dollop of cream and cranberries on top"}, {"name": "Smažený sýr", "wiki": "Smažený sýr", "desc": "Breaded and fried cheese with tartar sauce and fries, a pub classic. Cheap, filling and perfect with beer.", "price": "$6 to $9", "when": "Lunch or dinner at pubs", "order": "Ask for hranolky (fries) or boiled potatoes"}, {"name": "Bramboráky", "wiki": "Potato pancake", "desc": "Crisp, garlicky potato pancakes sold at markets and pubs. A great cheap snack.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Snacks at markets and festivals"}, {"name": "Trdelník", "wiki": "Trdelník", "desc": "Sweet pastry cooked on a spit and rolled in sugar, sold everywhere in Prague. Touristy but tasty.", "price": "$4 to $6", "when": "Afternoon snack in Prague", "order": "Skip the ice cream filling to save money"}, {"name": "Pilsner", "wiki": "Pilsner", "desc": "Czechia invented pilsner, and beer is often cheaper than water in local pubs. Try it unpasteurized from the tank.", "price": "$2 to $3.50 per half litre", "when": "Evenings at local pubs", "order": "Try a mlíko pour, mostly foam, for fun"}, {"name": "Kofola", "wiki": "Kofola", "desc": "Czech herbal cola, often on tap, and a local favorite. Cheap and refreshing.", "price": "$1.50 to $2.50", "when": "With lunch, from the tap", "order": "Ask for Kofola točená, the draft version"}], "food_tip": "Daily lunch menus (denní menu) at local pubs and restaurants are excellent value, often including soup and a main. Avoid the restaurants on Old Town Square, as prices are triple what you'll pay a few streets away.", "culture": ["Czechs can seem reserved or even a bit grumpy at first, but warm up fast over a beer, so say dobrý den when you walk into a shop or pub.", "In a traditional pub (hospoda), the waiter will keep bringing beers and marking them on a slip on your table until you put a beer mat on top of your glass.", "When toasting, look each person in the eye, clink and say na zdraví, as avoiding eye contact is considered bad luck.", "Shoes come off when you enter someone's home, and you'll usually be offered slippers.", "Czechs are often non-religious but proud of their churches, so cover up and keep quiet inside, and don't call the country Eastern Europe, as many locals see themselves as central European."], "safety": "Czechia is very safe, but pickpockets work Prague's Old Town, Charles Bridge and the trams. Avoid street money changers and exchange booths advertising zero commission, which hide awful rates, and get taxis through Bolt or Uber. Pub crawls and stag party zones can get messy late at night, so keep an eye on your drinks and your mates.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the big meal, served from about 11am to 2pm, and the denní menu often sells out early.", "Bread or condiments brought to your table may be added to the bill, so check before you eat them.", "Tap water is safe, but restaurants rarely give it free, so ask for kohoutková voda and expect a small charge.", "Vegetarian options are growing in cities, but in traditional pubs smažený sýr may be the only meat-free main.", "Round up or leave about 10% by telling the server the total you want to pay, rather than leaving cash on the table."], "drinks": [{"name": "Becherovka", "desc": "A herbal liqueur from Karlovy Vary with notes of ginger and cinnamon, drunk as a digestif. A shot costs about $2 to $4."}, {"name": "Svařák", "desc": "Hot mulled wine sold at winter markets and ski areas. A mug costs around $3 to $5."}], "greet": "A firm handshake with eye contact is standard, and use the polite dobrý den until people suggest first names. Friends may kiss on the cheek.", "dress": "Czechs dress casually but neatly, and layers help since weather changes fast. Cover up a little in churches and remove hats.", "table": "Say dobrou chuť before eating and keep both hands visible on the table. Pay at the table, and the server may ask if you're paying together or separately.", "faith": "Czechia is one of the least religious countries in Europe, with a Catholic minority. In churches and synagogues, keep quiet, dress respectfully and check if photos are allowed.", "phrases": [["Dobrý den", "Hello"], ["Děkuji", "Thank you"], ["Prosím", "Please"], ["Kolik to stojí?", "How much is it?"], ["Na zdraví!", "Cheers!"]]},
  "slovakia": {"around": "ZSSK and RegioJet trains connect Bratislava with Košice and Poprad, the base for the High Tatras, and the Tatra Electric Railway links Poprad with mountain resorts. Buses fill the gaps, and Bolt and Uber work in Bratislava. Trains are cheap, and students and under-26s can get discounts. Bratislava is also an easy day trip from Vienna by train or boat.", "money": "Slovakia uses the euro, and cards are common in cities, but bring cash for mountain huts, village shops and small pubs. Bank ATMs are easy to find. Rounding up or leaving about 10 percent in restaurants is normal, and haggling isn't done.", "sleep": "Bratislava has hostels, and the Tatras and Slovenský raj national park have pensions, campsites and mountain huts (chata). Huts in the High Tatras fill fast in summer, so reserve ahead. Accommodation is cheap by western European standards.", "eat": ["Bryndzové halušky: potato dumplings with sheep cheese and bacon", "Kapustnica: sour cabbage soup with sausage", "Lokše: thin potato flatbread with sweet or savory fillings", "Kofola: herbal cola that Slovaks drink everywhere"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Orange, Telekom, O2 or 4ka need ID at purchase, EU SIMs roam free, and coverage is good apart from some mountain valleys.", "heads_up": "Mountain rescue in Slovakia isn't free, so get travel insurance that covers hiking before heading into the Tatras.", "food": [{"name": "Bryndzové halušky", "wiki": "Bryndzové halušky", "desc": "Potato dumplings with sheep cheese and crispy bacon, the national dish. Cheap and filling at any traditional restaurant.", "price": "$7 to $11", "when": "Lunch at a salaš (sheep farm inn)", "order": "Pair it with žinčica, sheep's whey"}, {"name": "Kapustnica", "wiki": "Kapustnitsa", "desc": "Sour cabbage soup with sausage and mushrooms, traditionally eaten at Christmas. Hearty and warming.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Christmas Eve and winter lunches"}, {"name": "Lokše", "wiki": "Lokša", "desc": "Thin potato flatbread with sweet or savory fillings, sold at markets and festivals. A cheap snack.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Markets and festivals", "order": "Try it with goose liver or poppy seeds"}, {"name": "Parenica", "wiki": "Parenica", "desc": "Rolled, smoked sheep cheese, sold at roadside stalls in the mountains. Great for picnics.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Picnics and hiking snacks"}, {"name": "Borovička", "wiki": "Borovička", "desc": "Strong juniper spirit, often offered as a welcome shot. Sip it carefully.", "price": "$2 to $3 a shot", "when": "Welcome drinks and celebrations"}, {"name": "Kofola", "wiki": "Kofola", "desc": "Herbal cola that Slovaks drink everywhere, often on tap. Cheap and refreshing.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Any time, especially on tap", "order": "Ask for a malá (small) or veľká (large)"}], "food_tip": "Daily lunch menus (denné menu) at local restaurants are great value, often including soup and a main. Mountain huts serve cheap, hearty food for hikers.", "culture": ["Slovaks are warm and hospitable, especially in villages, so say dobrý deň in shops and expect to be offered food and drink if you visit someone.", "If someone offers you a shot of borovička or slivovica as a welcome, it's polite to take at least one, and toast with na zdravie while looking them in the eye.", "Shoes come off when you enter a home.", "Slovakia is fairly Catholic, especially in the east, so cover shoulders and knees in churches and keep quiet during services.", "Slovakia has been independent since 1993 and is proud of it, so don't mix it up with Czechia or Slovenia, and avoid calling it Czechoslovakia."], "safety": "Slovakia is very safe, with little crime beyond the occasional pickpocket in Bratislava's old town. The real risks are in the mountains, where weather changes fast, so check forecasts and stick to marked trails. Many High Tatras trails above the huts are closed every year from November 1 to June 15 to protect wildlife, and you can be fined for using them.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal from about 11:30am to 1:30pm, when the denné menu is served.", "Tap water is safe and tastes good across the country.", "Vegetarian dishes like fried cheese (vyprážaný syr) and pierogi are easy to find.", "Round up or tip about 10 percent, and tell the server the total you're paying."], "drinks": [{"name": "Slivovica", "desc": "Strong homemade plum brandy, often offered as a welcome. A shot costs about $2 to $3."}, {"name": "Žinčica", "desc": "Sour whey from sheep's milk, sold at mountain farms with bryndza, about $1 to $2 a glass."}], "greet": "Shake hands and say dobrý deň when you meet. Friends may kiss once or twice on the cheek.", "dress": "Dress is casual and practical, but cover shoulders and knees in churches. Pack proper boots and layers for the mountains.", "table": "Say dobrú chuť before eating and na zdravie when toasting, with eye contact. Keep your hands on the table, not in your lap.", "faith": "Most Slovaks are Roman Catholic, with Lutheran and Greek Catholic communities in the east. Keep quiet in churches, and don't wander around during services.", "phrases": [["Dobrý deň", "Good day"], ["Ďakujem", "Thank you"], ["Koľko to stojí?", "How much is it?"], ["Na zdravie!", "Cheers!"], ["Dobrú chuť", "Enjoy your meal"]]},
  "hungary": {"around": "MÁV trains and Volánbusz coaches run from Budapest to Eger, Pécs and around Lake Balaton at low fares. In Budapest the metro, trams and buses are excellent, and Bolt works through licensed taxis. Validate paper tickets before boarding, or buy them in the BudapestGO app. Trams 2, 4 and 6 are cheap ways to see the city.", "money": "Hungary uses the forint (HUF), not the euro, and cards are widely accepted. Use bank ATMs rather than standalone Euronet ones, and always choose to pay in forint when offered conversion. Check the bill for a service charge before tipping, otherwise leave 10 to 15 percent. Haggling isn't done except at flea markets.", "sleep": "Budapest has a big choice of hostels, often in old apartment buildings in the party district, while Eger, Pécs and Balaton have cheap guesthouses. Balaton is busy in July and August, so book ahead then. Accommodation is cheap by western European standards.", "eat": ["Lángos: deep-fried dough with sour cream and cheese", "Gulyás: paprika beef soup, hearty and warming", "Túró Rudi: chocolate-coated sweet curd cheese bar", "Kürtőskalács: chimney cake rolled in cinnamon sugar"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Telekom, Yettel or One need passport registration, EU SIMs roam free, and coverage is good nationwide.", "heads_up": "In Budapest, be wary of friendly strangers who invite you to a bar, as some lead to bills worth hundreds of euros.", "food": [{"name": "Lángos", "wiki": "Lángos", "desc": "Deep-fried dough with sour cream, cheese and garlic, a cheap street snack at markets and beaches. Greasy and glorious.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch at markets and around Lake Balaton", "order": "The classic is sajtos-tejfölös, cheese and sour cream"}, {"name": "Gulyás", "wiki": "Goulash", "desc": "Paprika beef soup with potatoes and vegetables, hearty and warming. A cheap and filling lunch.", "price": "$5 to $8", "when": "Weekday lunch, often as the soup course", "order": "Bográcsgulyás is the version cooked in a kettle outdoors"}, {"name": "Chicken paprikash", "wiki": "Chicken paprikash", "desc": "Chicken in a creamy paprika sauce, served with nokedli dumplings. A classic home-cooked dish.", "price": "$8 to $13", "when": "Sunday family lunch", "order": "Comes with nokedli, small egg dumplings"}, {"name": "Kürtőskalács", "wiki": "Kürtőskalács", "desc": "Chimney cake cooked on a spit and rolled in cinnamon sugar. Sold at markets and festivals.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Markets and Christmas fairs", "order": "Try walnut or coconut coating instead of cinnamon"}, {"name": "Túró Rudi", "wiki": "Túró Rudi", "desc": "Chocolate-coated sweet curd cheese bar from the supermarket fridge. A cheap local favorite.", "price": "$0.40 to $0.80", "when": "Snack time, from any supermarket fridge", "order": "Look for the red polka dot wrapper"}, {"name": "Pálinka", "wiki": "Pálinka", "desc": "Strong fruit brandy, often homemade, offered as a welcome drink. Sip it carefully.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Before meals and at celebrations", "order": "Szilva (plum) and barack (apricot) are the classics"}], "food_tip": "The weekday napi menü (daily menu) at local restaurants gets you soup and a main for very little, and the upstairs stalls at the Great Market Hall do cheap lángos and stews. Avoid the restaurants on Váci utca and near the river, which charge tourist prices.", "culture": ["Hungarians are polite and a bit formal with strangers, so say jó napot in shops and learn köszönöm (thank you), which takes some practice.", "Some Hungarians still avoid clinking beer glasses, a custom linked to the defeat of the 1848 revolution, though younger people care far less. Wine and pálinka glasses are fine to clink.", "Thermal baths like Széchenyi and Gellért are part of everyday life, so bring flip-flops and a towel, and expect some baths to require a swim cap in the lap pools.", "Shoes come off when you enter someone's home.", "Cover shoulders and knees in churches, including St Stephen's Basilica, and keep quiet during mass."], "safety": "Hungary is generally safe, but pickpocketing happens in Budapest's busy areas and on public transport. Watch out for the bar scam, where friendly strangers invite you to a bar that charges outrageous prices. Use apps for taxis to avoid getting overcharged.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch, from noon to 2pm, is the main meal, and the napi menü is only served on weekday lunchtimes.", "Tap water is safe, but restaurants may not bring it unless you ask for csapvíz.", "Vegetarians should look for főzelék, a thick vegetable stew, and rántott sajt, fried cheese, at canteens and bistros.", "Many restaurants add a 10 to 15 percent service charge (szervizdíj); if not, tip about 10 percent by saying the total you want to pay rather than leaving coins on the table."], "drinks": [{"name": "Fröccs", "desc": "Wine mixed with soda water in set ratios, the summer drink at every bar and wine garden. A glass is around $1.50 to $3."}, {"name": "Unicum", "desc": "A dark, bitter herbal liqueur sold in a round bottle and drunk as a shot. Expect about $2 to $4 in a bar."}], "greet": "Shake hands firmly when meeting, and friends kiss on both cheeks. Use jó napot kívánok with older people and szia with friends.", "dress": "City casual works by day, with smart casual for the opera and nicer restaurants. Bring a proper swimsuit for the thermal baths, not shorts.", "table": "Say jó étvágyat (enjoy your meal) before eating, and keep your hands on the table, not in your lap. When toasting, say egészségedre and look people in the eye.", "faith": "Hungary is mainly Christian, with Catholics the largest group, then Calvinist Protestants, plus a historic Jewish community in Budapest. At the Dohány Street Synagogue men cover their heads and shoulders should be covered.", "phrases": [["Jó napot", "Good day"], ["Köszönöm", "Thank you"], ["Mennyibe kerül?", "How much is it?"], ["Egészségedre!", "Cheers!"], ["Szia", "Hi or bye, informal"]]},
  "slovenia": {"around": "Buses are usually faster and more frequent than trains, with good links from Ljubljana to Bled, Bohinj and Piran. The country is small, so day trips are easy, but the Soča valley is much simpler with a car or a summer shuttle. Ljubljana is very walkable, and its city bikes are cheap. BlaBlaCar is popular for rides too.", "money": "Slovenia uses the euro, and cards are accepted in most places, but carry cash for mountain huts, farm stays and small bakeries. Bank ATMs are easy to find. Rounding up or leaving about 10 percent for good service is enough, and haggling isn't done.", "sleep": "Hostels in Ljubljana and Bled, campsites near the lakes and the Soča, and tourist farms in the countryside are the cheap options. Bled and Bohinj book up in summer, so reserve ahead. Mountain huts are cheap for hikers in the Julian Alps.", "eat": ["Kranjska klobasa: smoked Carniolan pork sausage with mustard", "Idrijski žlikrofi: small potato-filled dumplings from Idrija", "Jota: bean and sauerkraut stew from the west", "Potica: rolled nut cake, served at holidays"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from A1, Telekom Slovenije or Telemach are easy to get, EU SIMs roam free, and coverage is good apart from deep Alpine gorges.", "heads_up": "If you drive, buy the e-vignette online before using motorways, as fines are steep.", "food": [{"name": "Kranjska klobasa", "wiki": "Carniolan sausage", "desc": "Smoked pork sausage, served with mustard and bread. Cheap and found at stands everywhere.", "price": "$5 to $8", "when": "Lunch, with mustard and horseradish", "order": "Ask for it with sauerkraut and bread"}, {"name": "Idrijski žlikrofi", "wiki": "Idrijski žlikrofi", "desc": "Small potato-filled dumplings from Idrija, often served with a meat sauce. A cheap and hearty dish.", "price": "$10 to $15", "when": "Gostilna lunch around Idrija", "order": "Comes with bakalca, a lamb sauce"}, {"name": "Jota", "wiki": "Jota (food)", "desc": "Bean and sauerkraut stew from the west, warming and filling. Common in mountain huts.", "price": "$5 to $8", "when": "Winter lunch in the Karst and Primorska"}, {"name": "Potica", "wiki": "Potica", "desc": "Rolled nut cake, served at holidays. Bakeries sell slices.", "price": "$2 to $4 a slice", "when": "Easter and Christmas, with coffee", "order": "Walnut is classic, tarragon is the twist"}, {"name": "Bled cream cake", "wiki": "Cremeschnitte", "desc": "Vanilla custard and cream between layers of puff pastry, the famous kremšnita of Lake Bled. A slice at a lakeside cafe is a must.", "price": "$5 to $7", "when": "Afternoon at Lake Bled"}, {"name": "Burek", "wiki": "Börek", "desc": "Flaky pastry filled with cheese or meat, a cheap late-night snack. Found at bakeries and kiosks.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Late night snack after bars", "order": "Get it with drinking yoghurt on the side"}], "food_tip": "Look for malica or daily lunch menus at local gostilnas, which often include soup, a main and salad for a good price. Burek from a bakery and supermarket picnics are the cheapest options, and tap water is excellent.", "culture": ["Slovenians are outdoorsy and hiking is close to a national religion, so expect friendly greetings on the trail and say dober dan when you pass people or enter shops.", "Climbing Triglav, the country's highest peak, is a kind of rite of passage, and first-timers traditionally get a playful birch-switch spanking at the top.", "When toasting, look everyone in the eye and say na zdravje.", "Shoes come off when you enter someone's home, and you'll usually be offered slippers.", "Slovenians are proud of their independence since 1991 and are tired of being mixed up with Slovakia, so get the name right."], "safety": "Slovenia is very safe, with low crime. The main risks are in the mountains, where weather can change fast, so check forecasts and stick to marked trails. The Soča river is very cold, so be careful swimming.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal around noon to 2pm, and dinner is from 7pm.", "Tap water is excellent everywhere, and you can drink from mountain springs marked as safe.", "Students get subsidised meals with \"boni\" vouchers, so places near universities have cheap set lunches.", "Ljubljana's Friday food market, Odprta kuhna, runs from spring to autumn and is good for cheap tastings.", "Vegetarians can find štruklji, mushroom soups and salads, and tipping 5% to 10% is normal in restaurants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Cviček", "desc": "A light, slightly sour red blend from the Dolenjska region. About $2 to $3 a glass."}, {"name": "Borovničevec", "desc": "A sweet blueberry liqueur served as a shot after meals, especially in the Alps. About $2 to $4."}], "greet": "Shake hands with eye contact and use \"dober dan\", then switch to \"živjo\" once you're friends. Close friends kiss on the cheek.", "dress": "Casual outdoor clothes are normal, but bring good shoes if you're hiking. Cover shoulders in churches.", "table": "Say \"dober tek\" before eating, and keep your hands on the table. Finishing your plate is a compliment to the cook.", "faith": "Most Slovenians are Catholic, though many are not very religious. Be quiet in churches and keep away from altars during services.", "phrases": [["Dober dan", "Good day"], ["Hvala", "Thank you"], ["Koliko stane?", "How much is it?"], ["Dober tek", "Enjoy your meal"], ["Na zdravje", "Cheers"]]},
  "croatia": {"around": "Buses are how most backpackers get around, with FlixBus, Arriva and a bunch of regional companies linking Zagreb, Plitvice, Zadar, Split and Dubrovnik. Jadrolinija runs the car ferries and catamarans to the islands, alongside private fast boats like Krilo and TP Line, and in July and August it pays to buy tickets online a day or two ahead. Trains exist but are slow and patchy, so they're only worth it on a few inland routes. Uber and Bolt work in Zagreb, Split and Dubrovnik and are usually cheaper than taxis.", "money": "Croatia uses the euro, and cards work in most restaurants, supermarkets and hostels. Keep some cash for island cafés, bakeries, market stalls and the odd ferry kiosk. Skip the bright Euronet machines that charge silly fees and use bank ATMs instead, and always pick euros when a card terminal offers to convert for you. Leaving around 10 percent in sit-down restaurants is normal, and haggling isn't really a thing outside private room rentals in shoulder season.", "sleep": "Hostels are easy to find in Zagreb, Split, Zadar and Dubrovnik, and private rooms and apartments (look for sobe or apartmani signs) are often just as cheap for two people. Summer prices on the coast jump hard, especially in Hvar, Split and Dubrovnik, so book early for July and August. Inland around Plitvice you'll find family guesthouses, and there are plenty of campsites along the coast.", "eat": ["Ćevapi: grilled minced meat rolls in flatbread with onions", "Burek: flaky pastry filled with meat or cheese", "Peka: meat or octopus slow-cooked under an iron bell", "Štrukli: baked dough with fresh cheese, a Zagreb favorite"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Hrvatski Telekom, A1 and Telemach are cheap and easy to buy at kiosks and shops, and EU SIMs roam at no extra cost. Coverage is good on the mainland and most islands, and almost every café and hostel has wifi.", "heads_up": "Split, Hvar and Dubrovnik hand out fines for wandering around town in swimwear or shirtless, so throw something on once you leave the beach.", "food": [{"name": "Ćevapi", "wiki": "Ćevapi", "desc": "Little grilled minced meat sausages stuffed into soft flatbread with raw onion and ajvar. A filling portion from a grill joint costs around 8 to 10 euros.", "price": "$8 to $12", "when": "Lunch or dinner, at grill houses", "order": "Order deset (ten) with ajvar and chopped onion"}, {"name": "Burek", "wiki": "Börek", "desc": "Flaky filo pie with meat, cheese or spinach, sold by the slice at every bakery. It's the cheapest proper breakfast in the country at a couple of euros.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Breakfast, with a cup of drinking yoghurt", "order": "Sir is cheese, meso is meat"}, {"name": "Peka", "wiki": "Peka", "desc": "Lamb, veal or octopus slow-cooked with potatoes under an iron bell covered in hot coals. Usually needs ordering a few hours ahead, so it's a group splurge rather than a quick meal.", "price": "$25 to $40", "when": "Family Sunday lunches and konobas", "order": "Order a day ahead, usually for two or more"}, {"name": "Zagorski štrukli", "wiki": "Zagorski štrukli", "desc": "Thin dough rolled around fresh cheese, then baked or boiled. A Zagreb and Zagorje favourite you'll find at traditional restaurants.", "price": "$8 to $14", "when": "Sunday lunch in Zagreb and Zagorje", "order": "Choose kuhani (boiled) or zapečeni (baked with cream)"}, {"name": "Pašticada", "wiki": "Pašticada", "desc": "Beef marinated in vinegar and slowly stewed with prunes and wine, served with gnocchi. A Dalmatian Sunday classic worth trying at a konoba.", "price": "$15 to $25", "when": "Festive and Sunday lunches in Dalmatia", "order": "Good versions take two days, so ask if it's homemade"}, {"name": "Fritule", "wiki": "Frittella (doughnut)", "desc": "Small fried doughnuts with raisins and citrus zest, dusted with sugar. Especially common around Christmas and at summer street stalls.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Christmas markets and winter festivals", "order": "Get a paper cone topped with chocolate or sugar"}], "food_tip": "Bakeries and supermarket deli counters are your best friends on the coast, where restaurant prices are pitched at tourists. Look for marenda, a cheap late-morning set lunch that local workers eat at konobas.", "culture": ["Greetings are a handshake and a dobar dan, and Croatians appreciate any attempt at a few local words.", "Cover shoulders and knees when visiting churches and cathedrals, which are very much active places of worship in this mostly Catholic country.", "Coffee is a social ritual, not a takeaway habit, and people happily sit for an hour over one cup, so don't feel rushed.", "Avoid lumping Croatia in with the old Yugoslavia or casually bringing up the 1990s war, which is still a sensitive topic for many people.", "Tipping isn't compulsory, but rounding up or leaving about 10 percent for good service is appreciated."], "safety": "Croatia is very safe, and the main risks are sunburn, dehydration and sea urchins on rocky beaches, so pack water shoes. In Dubrovnik and Split watch out for overpriced menus without prices and taxis that don't use the meter. Stay on marked trails in remote inland areas, as a few old landmine zones are still signed and fenced off.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch (ručak) is the main meal, usually 1 to 3pm, and dinner starts around 7 or 8pm, later on the coast in summer.", "Tap water is safe and good everywhere, and you can refill bottles at public fountains in Split and Dubrovnik.", "Open-air markets (tržnica) run roughly 7am to 1pm and are the cheapest place for fruit, cheese and pršut.", "Vegetarians can get by on blitva (chard and potato), štrukli, pizza and grilled vegetables, though most traditional dishes contain meat or fish.", "Leave tips in cash at sit-down restaurants, as tips added on a card don't always reach staff; bakeries and cafés don't expect one."], "drinks": [{"name": "Bevanda and gemišt", "desc": "Bevanda is red wine mixed with water in Dalmatia, and gemišt is white wine with sparkling water in the north. A glass costs about $2 to $4."}, {"name": "Pelinkovac", "desc": "A bitter herbal liqueur made with wormwood, drunk as a digestif. A shot is around $2 to $4."}], "greet": "Friends greet with two kisses on the cheeks, while strangers shake hands. Bok is the casual hi and bye with people your own age.", "dress": "Beachwear belongs on the beach, and walking around shirtless or in a bikini can get you fined in Split, Hvar and Dubrovnik. Cover shoulders and knees in churches.", "table": "Wait for someone to say dobar tek (enjoy your meal) before you start, and keep your hands above the table. Bread often comes automatically and may be charged as a cover.", "faith": "Around 79 percent of Croatians are Catholic, with small Orthodox and Muslim communities. Keep quiet during mass, and dress modestly at pilgrimage sites like Marija Bistrica.", "phrases": [["Dobar dan", "Good day"], ["Hvala", "Thank you"], ["Koliko košta?", "How much does it cost?"], ["Račun, molim", "The bill, please"], ["Živjeli!", "Cheers!"]]},
  "serbia": {"around": "Buses go almost everywhere and are cheap, though many stations add a small platform fee on top of your ticket. The fast Soko train links Belgrade and Novi Sad in under 40 minutes, and the line on to Subotica has also been upgraded, so rail is now handy in the north. The rest of the rail network is slow but scenic. In Belgrade, CarGo, Yandex Go and regular metered taxis are all cheap; just avoid unmarked cabs at the bus and train stations.", "money": "Serbia uses the dinar, and you can't really get it before you arrive, so use an ATM in Belgrade or Novi Sad. Cards work in most city restaurants and shops, but you'll want cash for bakeries, markets, buses and anything rural. Some ATMs charge a fee, so try a few banks. Rounding up or leaving about 10 percent in restaurants is normal, and haggling is only really done at flea markets.", "sleep": "Belgrade has loads of good, cheap hostels, many around Savamala and Dorćol, and Novi Sad has a growing scene too. In the countryside, around Tara, Zlatibor and the Drina, you'll find guesthouses and rural homestays where breakfast is basically a feast. Novi Sad fills up fast during the EXIT festival in July.", "eat": ["Pljeskavica: big spiced grilled patty in a bun", "Ćevapi: small grilled meat rolls with onion and kajmak", "Burek: flaky pie with meat or cheese, eaten with yogurt", "Ajvar: roasted red pepper relish, spread on bread"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Yettel, A1 and mts are cheap and need your passport, and EU roaming does not apply here, so don't rely on your home plan. Coverage is good in cities and along main roads.", "heads_up": "Serbia doesn't treat the Kosovo border as an international entry point, so if you fly into Kosovo and then try to cross into Serbia you can be turned back. Plan your route so you enter Kosovo from Serbia, or go via a third country.", "food": [{"name": "Pljeskavica", "wiki": "Pljeskavica", "desc": "A huge spiced grilled patty stuffed into a flatbread with onion, kajmak and salads. Late-night grill stands in Belgrade sell it for a few euros.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Late night, from grill stands", "order": "The gurmanska version is stuffed with cheese and ham"}, {"name": "Ćevapi", "wiki": "Ćevapi", "desc": "Small grilled minced meat rolls served with raw onion, flatbread and a dollop of kajmak. A plate of ten is a cheap, filling lunch.", "price": "$5 to $9", "when": "Lunch or dinner at a kafana", "order": "Order five or ten, with onions and kajmak"}, {"name": "Burek", "wiki": "Börek", "desc": "Greasy, flaky pie with meat or cheese, traditionally eaten for breakfast with a cup of drinking yogurt. Bakeries sell it by weight for very little.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Breakfast with drinking yogurt", "order": "Ask for sa sirom (cheese) or sa mesom (meat)"}, {"name": "Ajvar", "wiki": "Ajvar", "desc": "Smoky roasted red pepper relish that turns up with almost every meal. Homemade versions in autumn are on another level.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "As a side for grilled meat", "order": "Homemade ajvar from markets beats the jarred kind"}, {"name": "Karađorđeva šnicla", "wiki": "Karađorđeva šnicla", "desc": "A rolled schnitzel stuffed with kajmak, breaded and fried, served with tartar sauce and chips. Heavy, ridiculous and very Serbian.", "price": "$9 to $14", "when": "Dinner at a kafana", "order": "Comes with tartar sauce and fries"}, {"name": "Rakija", "wiki": "Rakia", "desc": "Strong fruit brandy, usually plum (šljivovica), offered as a welcome drink in homes. Sip it, don't shoot it, and say živeli when you clink glasses.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Toasts, guests and celebrations", "order": "Plum rakija (šljivovica) is the classic; sip it slowly"}], "food_tip": "Bakeries (pekara) are open early till late and sell burek, pastries and sandwiches for pocket change. A kafana lunch with grilled meat and salad is still great value compared with most of Europe.", "culture": ["When you clink glasses, look people in the eye and say živeli, as skipping the eye contact is considered bad form.", "Serbians are famously hospitable, and if you're invited to a home expect to be fed far too much; bringing a small gift like coffee, sweets or a drink is appreciated.", "Dress modestly in Orthodox churches and monasteries, covering shoulders and knees, and women may be offered a wrap or skirt at some monasteries.", "The three-finger salute and Kosovo, NATO and the 1990s are political flashpoints, so listen more than you talk on those subjects.", "Smoking is still common in some bars and cafés, so don't be surprised to find a smoky kafana."], "safety": "Serbia is generally safe and laid back. The usual hassle is taxi drivers overcharging at stations and the airport, so use an app or an official taxi. Football derbies and political protests can get rowdy, so give them a wide berth, and keep an eye on your stuff in busy nightlife areas.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is a quick bakery stop, lunch is the big meal from about 2 to 4pm, and dinner is lighter after 8pm.", "Tap water is safe to drink in Belgrade and most towns.", "Tip about 10 percent in restaurants, or round up in cafés.", "Vegetarians can look for 'posno' (fasting) dishes, which are vegan and common before Christmas and Easter.", "Shopska salad, with tomato, cucumber and grated white cheese, comes with almost every meal."], "drinks": [{"name": "Domaća kafa", "desc": "Strong coffee boiled in a small pot and served with the grounds in the cup. About $1 to $2."}, {"name": "Boza", "desc": "A thick, sweet, slightly fermented drink made from corn, sold at bakeries for $0.50 to $1."}], "greet": "A handshake with 'Dobar dan' is standard, and friends kiss three times on the cheeks. Use first names only once you're invited to.", "dress": "Belgrade is stylish and people dress up for nights out, so pack one smarter outfit. Bring a scarf or long layer for monasteries.", "table": "Hosts will keep filling your plate, so leave a little food to show you're full. Wait for the first toast before you start drinking.", "faith": "Most Serbians are Orthodox Christian, with Catholics in Vojvodina and Muslims in the Sandžak region. In churches, people light thin candles and stand, and photos during services aren't welcome.", "phrases": [["Zdravo (Здраво)", "Hello"], ["Hvala (Хвала)", "Thank you"], ["Koliko košta? (Колико кошта?)", "How much is it?"], ["Živeli! (Живели!)", "Cheers!"], ["Prijatno (Пријатно)", "Enjoy your meal"]]},
  "kosovo": {"around": "Buses and minibuses run frequently between Pristina, Prizren, Peja and Gjakova, and you just pay on board or at the station. There are also buses to Tirana, Skopje and Podgorica. Taxis are cheap, but agree the fare or check the meter is on before you set off. For the Rugova Canyon and the mountains, a rental car or a local guide makes life much easier.", "money": "Kosovo uses the euro even though it isn't in the EU, and cash is still king outside the trendier cafés in Pristina. ATMs are easy to find in towns, though some charge a few euros per withdrawal. Rounding up the bill or leaving a euro or two is the normal tip, and prices are low enough that haggling rarely comes up.", "sleep": "Pristina and Prizren both have cheap, friendly hostels, and Peja is a good base for hiking with a couple of budget options. In Rugova and other mountain areas, family-run guesthouses often throw in homemade meals and serious hospitality. Prices are some of the lowest in Europe.", "eat": ["Flija: layered crepe-like pie baked slowly under coals", "Burek: flaky pastry with meat, cheese or spinach", "Macchiato: Kosovo's beloved espresso with foamed milk", "Tavë kosi: baked lamb with rice and a yogurt topping"], "data": "Local SIMs from Vala or IPKO are cheap and coverage is good in towns. EU roaming doesn't apply in Kosovo, so get a local SIM or an eSIM to avoid ugly roaming bills.", "heads_up": "If you're heading on to Serbia afterwards, enter Kosovo from Serbia or route through a third country like North Macedonia or Montenegro, since Serbia may refuse entry if you came into Kosovo another way.", "food": [{"name": "Flija", "wiki": "Flia", "desc": "Thin crepe-like layers brushed with cream and baked slowly under a lid of hot coals, taking hours to make. Often made for family gatherings and served with yogurt or honey.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Weekends and family gatherings", "order": "Best with honey and a dollop of kaymak"}, {"name": "Burek", "wiki": "Börek", "desc": "Flaky pastry filled with meat, cheese or spinach, sold hot in bakeries in the morning. A slice and a yogurt is a dirt cheap breakfast.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast, from bakeries", "order": "Order with a cup of yoghurt"}, {"name": "Tavë kosi", "wiki": "Tavë kosi", "desc": "Lamb baked with rice under a tangy, eggy yogurt topping. Proper Albanian-style comfort food at traditional restaurants.", "price": "$5 to $8", "when": "Lunch, at traditional restaurants"}, {"name": "Macchiato", "wiki": "Caffè macchiato", "desc": "Kosovo is obsessed with its macchiato, an espresso with a cloud of foamed milk, and it's genuinely excellent. Expect to pay around a euro in most cafés.", "price": "$1 to $1.50", "when": "All day, at cafes", "order": "A \"macchiato i madh\" is the bigger version"}, {"name": "Qebapa", "wiki": "Ćevapi", "desc": "Grilled minced meat sausages served with bread, onions and peppers. Grill houses everywhere do them cheaply.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch or dinner, at grill houses", "order": "Order 5 or 10 pieces with bread"}, {"name": "Trileçe", "wiki": "Trileçe", "desc": "A sponge cake soaked in three kinds of milk with a caramel top. Every bakery and café seems to have it.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Afternoon dessert, with coffee"}], "food_tip": "Bakeries and grill joints keep you fed for a few euros a day. Coffee culture is huge and cheap, so café hopping is one of the best budget pastimes in Pristina.", "culture": ["Kosovo is mostly Albanian and mostly Muslim but quite secular in daily life; dress modestly and cover up when visiting mosques and Orthodox monasteries such as Gračanica or Visoki Dečani.", "Locals are often very warm toward foreigners, especially Americans and Brits, thanks to the events of 1999, so don't be surprised by enthusiastic welcomes.", "Independence and Serbia are sensitive subjects, so let people bring politics up and listen rather than argue.", "Saying hello with a handshake is normal, and among friends you'll see cheek kisses; a simple tungjatjeta is a polite greeting.", "Accepting a coffee or tea when offered is good manners, and refusing hospitality can come across as cold."], "safety": "Kosovo is relaxed and safe for travellers, with low crime and friendly people. Stick to marked trails in the mountains, where weather turns quickly, and be careful on roads, where driving can be aggressive. Tensions can still flare in the far north around Mitrovica, so check the news before heading up there.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is around 1pm to 3pm, and dinner after 8pm, and cafes are busy from morning until late.", "Tap water is generally safe in Pristina and Prizren, but many locals still buy bottled water.", "Vegetarians can find byrek with cheese or spinach, stuffed peppers and bean dishes.", "Kosovo uses the euro, and many smaller places are cash only.", "Tipping around 10 percent in restaurants is common, and rounding up at cafes is normal."], "drinks": [{"name": "Peja beer", "desc": "The national lager, brewed in Peja, around $1.50 to $2.50 a bottle in bars."}, {"name": "Rakia", "desc": "Strong fruit brandy, usually grape, offered as a welcome in homes, around $1.50 to $3 a glass."}], "greet": "A handshake is the standard greeting, and friends kiss on the cheeks. \"Tungjatjeta\" means hello, and \"Si je?\" (How are you?) usually follows.", "dress": "Young people dress stylishly in Pristina, and casual clothes are fine. Cover shoulders and knees at mosques and monasteries, and women should cover their hair in mosques.", "table": "Hosts insist on second helpings, so pace yourself. Bread is treated with respect and should never be thrown away carelessly.", "faith": "Most Kosovars are Muslim, with Serbian Orthodox and Catholic minorities. Remove shoes in mosques, and some Orthodox monasteries require passports at the gate.", "phrases": [["Tungjatjeta", "Hello"], ["Faleminderit", "Thank you"], ["Sa kushton?", "How much does it cost?"], ["Gëzuar!", "Cheers!"], ["Mirupafshim", "Goodbye"]]},
  "moldova": {"around": "Minibuses (rutiere) and older buses leave from Chișinău's bus stations for almost every town, including Orheiul Vechi, Soroca and the wineries. Trains are few and slow, though the overnight service to Bucharest and the trains to Romania and Ukraine are handy for border hops. In the capital, trolleybuses cost next to nothing, and taxis are cheap if you book through an app such as Yandex Go or iTaxi rather than flagging one down.", "money": "The currency is the Moldovan leu, and you'll need cash for minibuses, markets and anything outside Chișinău. Cards and contactless work in Chișinău's cafés, supermarkets and many restaurants, and bank ATMs are easy to find there. Leaving around 10 percent in restaurants is common, and market prices are usually fair enough that light haggling is all you need.", "sleep": "Chișinău has a handful of hostels and lots of cheap apartments, so you'll rarely struggle for a bed. Out in the countryside, agritourism guesthouses (pensiuni) near the monasteries, Orheiul Vechi and the wine regions are a great way to see village life, and meals are often included. Prices are among the lowest in Europe.", "eat": ["Plăcinte: thin pastries filled with cheese, potato or cabbage", "Mămăligă: cornmeal porridge served with cheese and sour cream", "Zeamă: sour chicken noodle soup with lovage", "Sarmale: cabbage or vine leaves stuffed with meat and rice"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Orange or Moldcell are very cheap, need a passport, and come with big data allowances. Coverage is good, but EU roaming doesn't apply here, so buy local or use an eSIM.", "heads_up": "Transnistria is a breakaway region with its own currency, its own border checkpoints and no recognised government, so check the latest advice before heading in and keep your passport and migration card on you.", "food": [{"name": "Plăcinte", "wiki": "Plăcintă", "desc": "Thin pastries stuffed with salty cheese, potato, cabbage or cherries, fried or baked. Bakeries and street kiosks sell them for small change.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Breakfast or snack, from kiosks", "order": "Try cu brânză (cheese) or cu varză (cabbage)"}, {"name": "Mămăligă", "wiki": "Mămăligă", "desc": "Thick cornmeal porridge served with sheep's cheese, sour cream and sometimes a fried egg or pork. The backbone of village cooking.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch, as a hearty main", "order": "Get it with brânză, smântână and a fried egg"}, {"name": "Sarmale", "wiki": "Stuffed leaves", "desc": "Cabbage or vine leaves wrapped around rice and minced meat, simmered and served with sour cream. A staple at family meals and holidays.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch, and at weddings and holidays", "order": "Eat with sour cream and mămăligă"}, {"name": "Colțunași", "wiki": "Pierogi (Romania and Moldova)", "desc": "Dumplings filled with cheese, potato or cherries, topped with sour cream. Cheap and filling at canteens and traditional restaurants.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Lunch at canteens", "order": "Cherry colțunași make a good dessert"}, {"name": "Moldovan wine", "wiki": "Moldovan wine", "desc": "Moldova has some of the world's biggest wine cellars, at Cricova and Mileștii Mici, and a glass of good local wine costs very little. Tastings are a highlight even on a tight budget.", "price": "$2 to $4 a glass", "when": "Evenings, and on winery tours", "order": "Try Fetească Neagră, a local red grape"}], "food_tip": "Look for local canteens and plăcinte kiosks for cheap meals, and stock up on cheese, bread and fruit at Chișinău's Central Market. Ask for zeamă, the sour chicken noodle soup, when you're feeling rough.", "culture": ["Moldovans speak Romanian, and many also speak Russian; learning bună ziua (good day) and mulțumesc (thank you) goes down well, and assuming everyone is Russian-speaking does not.", "In Orthodox churches and monasteries, women are expected to cover their heads and wear skirts, and men should remove hats and wear long trousers.", "Hospitality is strong, especially in villages, and turning down homemade wine or food repeatedly can seem rude, so accept at least a little.", "Bring an odd number of flowers if you're invited to someone's home, as even numbers are for funerals.", "Politics around Russia, Romania and the EU is a live topic, so tread carefully and avoid strong opinions with people you've just met."], "safety": "Moldova is generally safe and very low key, with petty theft the main thing to watch in busy places. Taxis without apps sometimes overcharge, so agree a price or use an app. The war in neighbouring Ukraine hasn't spilled over, but check official advice before you go and keep an eye on the news.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch from 12pm to 2pm is the main meal, and many canteens sell set lunches cheaply.", "Chișinău's Central Market (Piața Centrală) has fresh produce, cheese and pickles, and you can taste before buying.", "Don't drink the tap water; bottled water is very cheap, and many villages have wells or springs instead.", "Vegetarians do well with plăcinte, colțunași and mămăligă, and Orthodox fasting periods bring many meat-free dishes.", "Tip about 10% in sit-down restaurants, and round up in cafes."], "drinks": [{"name": "Divin", "desc": "Moldovan aged brandy, often given as a gift. A glass costs about $2 to $5 in bars."}, {"name": "Compot", "desc": "Homemade fruit juice boiled with sugar and served cold, common in canteens. Usually $0.50 to $1 a glass."}], "greet": "Shake hands and say bună ziua, with a kiss on the cheek between friends. Russian is widely understood, but start in Romanian.", "dress": "Smart casual is common in Chișinău. In monasteries, women cover hair and wear skirts, and men wear long trousers without hats.", "table": "Expect lots of toasts, so wait before drinking and say noroc. Hosts will keep refilling your glass and plate, so leave a bit if you've had enough.", "faith": "Over 90% of Moldovans are Eastern Orthodox Christian. In churches, stand quietly, avoid photos during services and don't stand with your back to the altar.", "phrases": [["Bună ziua", "Hello, good day"], ["Mulțumesc", "Thank you"], ["Cât costă?", "How much is it?"], ["Vă rog", "Please"], ["Noroc!", "Cheers!"]]},
  "ukraine": {"around": "Ukrainian airspace has been closed to commercial flights since 2022, so you'll arrive by train or bus from Poland, Slovakia, Hungary, Romania or Moldova. Ukrzaliznytsia trains are the main way to travel inside the country, they're comfortable and cheap, and they sell out fast, so buy tickets early in the official app. Buses fill the gaps, and Uber and Bolt work in the big cities. Respect curfews, which vary by region and can affect late trains and taxis.", "money": "The currency is the hryvnia, and Ukraine is impressively card-friendly, with contactless working almost everywhere in cities, including public transport. ATMs are common, but carry some cash for power cuts, when card machines and ATMs can go down. Leave around 10 percent in restaurants and cafés, and don't expect much haggling outside markets.", "sleep": "Lviv and Kyiv still have hostels and plenty of cheap apartments, and prices are low by European standards. Pick a place with a shelter or basement nearby, and check whether your accommodation lists its nearest shelter. Many hostels are run by people who can give you the best on-the-ground advice.", "eat": ["Borscht: beet soup with sour cream, often with pampushky", "Varenyky: dumplings filled with potato, cheese or cherries", "Salo: cured pork fat eaten on rye bread", "Holubtsi: cabbage rolls stuffed with rice and meat"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Kyivstar, Vodafone and lifecell are cheap and easy to buy with your passport, and data is plentiful. Coverage is good, though outages can happen after strikes on infrastructure, so download offline maps.", "heads_up": "Install an air raid alert app, follow alerts and curfews, and never photograph military sites, checkpoints or the aftermath of strikes.", "food": [{"name": "Borscht", "wiki": "Borscht", "desc": "Beetroot soup with cabbage and meat, served with sour cream and garlicky pampushky buns. A bowl costs a couple of euros in a canteen.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch, as the soup course", "order": "Ask for pampushky with garlic on the side"}, {"name": "Varenyky", "wiki": "Varenyky", "desc": "Dumplings filled with potato, cheese, cabbage or sour cherries, topped with fried onion or sour cream. Cheap, filling and everywhere.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch or dinner, at canteens", "order": "Sour cherry varenyky come with sour cream"}, {"name": "Salo", "wiki": "Salo (food)", "desc": "Cured pork fat, sliced thin and eaten on rye bread with garlic. Locals love it, and trying it is a rite of passage.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Snack with horilka and rye bread"}, {"name": "Chicken Kyiv", "wiki": "Chicken Kiev", "desc": "Breaded chicken breast stuffed with garlic butter that squirts when you cut it. A classic in Kyiv restaurants.", "price": "$5 to $9", "when": "Dinner, at Ukrainian restaurants"}, {"name": "Syrnyky", "wiki": "Syrniki", "desc": "Fried cottage cheese pancakes served with sour cream or jam. A great cheap breakfast in cafés.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Breakfast, at cafés"}, {"name": "Holubtsi", "wiki": "Cabbage roll", "desc": "Cabbage leaves stuffed with rice and meat, simmered in tomato sauce. Home cooking you'll find in canteens and family restaurants.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Family dinners and holidays"}], "food_tip": "Self-service canteens such as the Puzata Hata chain serve full meals for a few euros. Markets and bakeries are cheap, and coffee culture is strong, especially in Lviv.", "culture": ["Learn a few words of Ukrainian, as many people have switched from Russian since 2022 and appreciate the effort; dyakuyu means thank you.", "Cover shoulders and knees in Orthodox and Greek Catholic churches, and women may want a headscarf in more traditional ones.", "The war affects everyone, so be sensitive in conversations, don't treat damaged sites as photo props, and follow local lead on memorials.", "A minute of silence is held every morning at 9am to remember the fallen, and people pause in the street, so stop with them.", "Bring a small gift like sweets or wine if invited to a home, and take your shoes off at the door."], "safety": "Ukraine is at war, and most governments advise against all or most travel, so check your government's advice and make sure your insurance covers you, as most standard policies don't. Install an air raid alert app such as Air Alert, take every alert seriously and know where your nearest shelter is. Stay well away from the east and the front line, and never photograph military sites, checkpoints or strike damage.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, from about noon to 3pm, and many cafés offer a cheap weekday lunch set.", "Locals boil or filter tap water, so buy bottled or use a filter.", "Vegetarians do fine with potato and cabbage varenyky, deruny (potato pancakes) and pickled vegetables, and Orthodox Lent menus are meat free.", "Tip around 10 percent in cafés and restaurants, in cash or added on the card machine.", "Night curfews mean kitchens can close earlier than you'd expect, so check opening hours and keep an air alert app handy."], "drinks": [{"name": "Horilka", "desc": "Ukrainian vodka, sometimes infused with honey and chilli as horilka z pertsem. A shot costs around $1.50 to $3."}, {"name": "Uzvar", "desc": "A smoky compote of dried fruit, served on Christmas Eve and in canteens for about 50 cents to $1."}], "greet": "Men shake hands firmly, while women often nod or kiss friends on the cheek. Say dobryi den, and don't shake hands across a doorway, which is seen as bad luck.", "dress": "People dress neatly in cities, so smart casual clothes fit in. Women should bring a headscarf for monasteries such as Kyiv Pechersk Lavra.", "table": "Toasts come often, so wait for one before drinking at a dinner, and it's polite to finish your plate. Never put an empty bottle back on the table.", "faith": "Most Ukrainians are Orthodox Christians, with Greek Catholics strong in the west and smaller Roman Catholic, Muslim and Jewish communities. Stand quietly during services and ask before photographing priests.", "phrases": [["Dobryi den (Добрий день)", "Good day"], ["Dyakuyu (Дякую)", "Thank you"], ["Skilky koshtuye? (Скільки коштує?)", "How much does it cost?"], ["Smachnoho (Смачного)", "Enjoy your meal"], ["Budmo! (Будьмо!)", "Cheers!"]]},
  "russia": {"around": "Russian Railways (RZD) trains are the best way to cover big distances, from the fast Sapsan between Moscow and St Petersburg to the Trans-Siberian, where cheap open-plan platskart berths are a classic experience. Book long-distance trains ahead in summer. Domestic flights are cheap but you may struggle to pay with a foreign card. In cities the metro is excellent, and Yandex Go is the main ride app, though you'll likely need to pay drivers in cash.", "money": "Western-issued Visa and Mastercard cards don't work in Russia because of sanctions, so you'll need to bring all your money as cash, usually euros or dollars in clean notes, and change it at banks. The currency is the ruble. Some travellers use workarounds like Russian bank cards issued to foreigners, but these change often, so don't count on them. Around 10 percent is a normal restaurant tip, and haggling is only for markets.", "sleep": "Hostels are common in Moscow, St Petersburg and along the Trans-Siberian route, and they're cheap by European standards. Your accommodation must register you with the authorities, so keep the registration slip they give you. Booking can be awkward with foreign cards, so many travellers pay in cash on arrival.", "eat": ["Pelmeni: small meat dumplings with sour cream", "Blini: thin pancakes with sweet or savory fillings", "Shchi: cabbage soup, a staple of Russian home cooking", "Kvass: lightly fermented drink made from rye bread"], "data": "Buying a local SIM needs your passport and registration, and many foreign eSIMs don't work. Mobile internet can be throttled or cut in some regions, many Western apps and sites are blocked, and using a VPN is in a legal grey zone, so download offline maps before you go.", "heads_up": "Foreigners have been detained on vague charges, so avoid photographing military or government sites and stay out of political conversations and posts.", "food": [{"name": "Pelmeni", "wiki": "Pelmeni", "desc": "Small meat dumplings boiled and served with sour cream or butter. Canteens and frozen supermarket packs make them a cheap staple.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch or dinner at canteens", "order": "Siberian pelmeni are the famous kind"}, {"name": "Blini", "wiki": "Blini", "desc": "Thin pancakes rolled around savoury or sweet fillings, from minced meat to condensed milk. Chains like Teremok sell them cheaply.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Snack or quick lunch", "order": "Try them with red caviar during Maslenitsa"}, {"name": "Shchi", "wiki": "Shchi", "desc": "Cabbage soup, sometimes sour, that has been part of Russian home cooking for centuries. Comforting in winter and cheap in canteens.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch at canteens", "order": "Add a spoon of sour cream"}, {"name": "Pirozhki", "wiki": "Pirozhki", "desc": "Little baked or fried buns stuffed with potato, cabbage, meat or egg. Bakeries and station kiosks sell them for small change.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Snack on the go"}, {"name": "Olivier salad", "wiki": "Olivier salad", "desc": "Potato, peas, pickles, egg and meat in mayonnaise, a New Year's Eve must and a fixture in every canteen.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "New Year and canteen lunches"}, {"name": "Kvass", "wiki": "Kvass", "desc": "A lightly fermented, slightly sour drink made from rye bread, sold cold in summer. Refreshing and very low in alcohol.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Hot summer afternoons"}], "food_tip": "Stolovaya canteens serve proper home-style food for very little, and you just point at what you want. Supermarkets and bakeries are good for train picnic supplies on long rail journeys.", "culture": ["Take your shoes off when entering someone's home, and bring a small gift like sweets or flowers in odd numbers, as even numbers are for funerals.", "In Orthodox churches, women should cover their heads and wear skirts, men should remove hats, and both should keep shoulders covered.", "Russians can seem stern in public, but smiling at strangers just isn't the habit; warmth comes once you get to know people.", "Avoid shaking hands across a threshold, which is considered bad luck.", "Politics, the war in Ukraine and criticism of the government can get you into real trouble, so keep opinions to yourself in public and online."], "safety": "Most Western governments advise against all travel to Russia because of the war in Ukraine and the risk of arbitrary detention of foreigners, and your travel insurance may not cover you. Petty crime is the usual city stuff, but the bigger risk is legal: avoid photographing military, police or government sites, stay away from protests and keep political posts off social media. Running out of cash is a real problem with no card backup, so plan your budget carefully.", "eat_beats": ["Western sanctions mean foreign bank cards don't work, so you'll need to bring cash to exchange.", "Don't drink tap water in most cities, and use bottled or boiled water.", "Vegetarian food is easy during Orthodox fasting periods, when many places offer post (lenten) menus.", "Tip about 10 percent in restaurants, but it isn't expected in canteens."], "drinks": [{"name": "Mors", "desc": "Sweet and sour berry juice, often cranberry or lingonberry, for about $1 to $2 a glass."}, {"name": "Kompot", "desc": "A homemade drink of stewed fruit, served cold in canteens for under $1."}], "greet": "Men shake hands firmly, but don't shake across a doorway. Women often greet each other with a kiss or hug.", "dress": "Russians dress neatly in cities, and shorts are rare outside summer. In Orthodox churches, women cover hair and wear skirts.", "table": "Toasts are frequent and long, so wait before sipping. Finish your plate, but leave a little to show you've had enough.", "faith": "Most Russians are Orthodox Christian, with big Muslim communities and some Buddhists. In churches, stand quietly, don't sit with legs crossed, and avoid photos during services.", "phrases": [["Privet (Привет)", "Hi"], ["Spasibo (Спасибо)", "Thank you"], ["Skolko stoit? (Сколько стоит?)", "How much is it?"], ["Za zdorovye (За здоровье)", "To health"], ["Vkusno (Вкусно)", "Tasty"]]},
  "belarus": {"around": "Trains and buses connect Minsk, Brest, Grodno and the towns near the Mir and Nesvizh castles cheaply, and marshrutka minibuses fill in the gaps. Belarusian Railways sells tickets online and at stations. In Minsk the metro is clean, simple and cheap, and Yandex Go is the usual taxi app.", "money": "The currency is the Belarusian ruble. Many foreign-issued cards don't work reliably because of sanctions, so bring enough euros or dollars in cash to exchange at banks. Locals pay by card and contactless all the time, but don't count on yours working. Rounding up or leaving around 10 percent in restaurants is fine, and haggling isn't really done.", "sleep": "Minsk has a few hostels and cheap hotels, and the other cities have simple hotels at low prices. In the countryside, agro-estates (agrousadby) offer rooms in farmhouses and are a nice way to see rural life. Your accommodation will normally handle your registration.", "eat": ["Draniki: crisp potato pancakes with sour cream", "Machanka: pork stew in a creamy sauce for dipping pancakes", "Kalduny: dumplings stuffed with meat or potato", "Kvass: sour, slightly fizzy drink made from bread"], "data": "A local SIM from A1, MTS or life:) requires your passport, coverage is decent, and data is cheap. The internet is monitored and some sites and apps are blocked, so be careful what you post.", "heads_up": "Don't photograph government buildings, police or military sites, and stay well away from any political gathering.", "food": [{"name": "Draniki", "wiki": "Potato pancake", "desc": "Crispy potato pancakes served with sour cream, the unofficial national dish. Cheap in every café and canteen.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch or dinner, any day", "order": "Ask for smetana (sour cream) on the side"}, {"name": "Kalduny", "wiki": "Kalduny", "desc": "Dumplings stuffed with meat or potato, often with a potato dough. Hearty and filling in traditional restaurants.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Lunch at cafés and canteens", "order": "Filled with meat or mushrooms"}, {"name": "Babka", "wiki": "Potato babka", "desc": "A baked potato casserole with bacon and onion, dense and comforting. Classic home cooking.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Winter dinner, baked in a pot"}, {"name": "Khaladnik", "wiki": "Cold beet soup", "desc": "Bright pink cold beetroot soup with kefir, cucumber and egg, perfect in summer.", "price": "$2 to $3", "when": "Hot summer days, as a starter", "order": "Served with a boiled potato on the side"}, {"name": "Kvass", "wiki": "Kvass", "desc": "Sour, slightly fizzy drink made from bread, sold from street tanks in summer. Very low alcohol and refreshing.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Summer, from street barrels or shops"}], "food_tip": "Canteens (stalovaya) are the cheapest way to eat well, and supermarkets are good value for picnic supplies.", "culture": ["Belarusians tend to be reserved with strangers but warm once you get to know them, and hospitality in homes is generous.", "Take off your shoes inside homes and bring a small gift, with flowers in odd numbers only.", "In Orthodox churches women should cover their heads and men remove hats, and both should dress modestly.", "Politics is a dangerous subject; criticising the government or wearing the white-red-white opposition colours can attract police attention."], "safety": "Many governments advise against travel to Belarus because of its role in the war in Ukraine and the risk of arbitrary detention, and insurance may not cover you. Crime is low, but the main risk is getting caught up with the authorities, so stay away from protests and keep your documents and registration with you. Check the latest rules on visa-free entry before you go, as they change and generally only apply when entering at certain points.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is between noon and 3pm, and many cafés offer cheap business lunch sets during those hours.", "Tap water is treated, but most locals boil it or drink bottled water, so do the same.", "Vegetarians can live on draniki, mushroom dishes, salads and soups, but ask about meat stock and lard.", "Markets like Minsk's Komarovsky sell pickles, smoked fish, honey and berries, and you can usually taste before buying.", "Tipping around 10% is normal in restaurants, and card payment is widely accepted, though foreign cards may not work."], "drinks": [{"name": "Mors", "desc": "A sweet berry drink made from cranberries or lingonberries, served cold. About $1 a glass."}, {"name": "Krambambula", "desc": "A traditional spiced honey liqueur, often served warm in winter. About $2 to $3 a shot in a bar."}], "greet": "Men shake hands firmly, but not across a doorway, which is seen as bad luck. Women greet friends with a kiss on the cheek.", "dress": "People dress neatly in Minsk, so tidy casual clothes are best. In Orthodox churches women cover their heads and wear skirts, and men remove hats.", "table": "Toasts are frequent at home meals, so wait for the host's toast before drinking. Refusing food can offend, so take small portions at first.", "faith": "Most Belarusians are Orthodox Christian, with a Catholic minority, especially in the west. In churches speak quietly, don't stand with your back to the altar and avoid photos during services.", "phrases": [["Pryvitannie (Прывітанне)", "Hello, in Belarusian"], ["Dziakuj (Дзякуй)", "Thank you, in Belarusian"], ["Skolko stoit? (Сколько стоит?)", "How much is it? in Russian"], ["Spasibo (Спасибо)", "Thank you, in Russian"], ["Smachna (Смачна)", "Tasty"]]},
  "lithuania": {"around": "LTG Link trains and frequent buses link Vilnius, Kaunas and Klaipėda, and a short train takes you to Trakai Castle. Reaching the Curonian Spit means a ferry from Klaipėda and then a bus to Nida. In cities, Bolt is cheap and reliable, and Vilnius old town is walkable. FlixBus and Lux Express run cheap international routes to Riga, Warsaw and Tallinn.", "money": "Lithuania uses the euro, and cards and contactless work almost everywhere, even for small purchases. You'll barely need cash, though a bit is handy for markets and rural guesthouses. Tipping is optional, around 10 percent is generous in restaurants, and haggling isn't part of the culture.", "sleep": "Vilnius has plenty of good hostels near the old town, and Kaunas and Klaipėda have a few too. The Curonian Spit has guesthouses and campsites that fill quickly in July and August, so book ahead. Prices are lower than Western Europe, but not as cheap as the Balkans.", "eat": ["Cepelinai: large potato dumplings stuffed with meat", "Šaltibarščiai: bright pink cold beet soup with potatoes", "Kepta duona: fried rye bread strips with garlic", "Kibinai: Karaim pastries filled with meat, a Trakai specialty"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Telia, Bitė or Tele2 are cheap and easy to buy at shops and kiosks, EU SIMs roam free, and coverage is excellent. Free wifi is common in cafés and public places.", "heads_up": "Pedestrians are legally required to wear a reflector when walking along unlit roads at night.", "food": [{"name": "Cepelinai", "wiki": "Cepelinai", "desc": "Huge potato dumplings stuffed with meat or curd, smothered in sour cream and bacon bits. One portion will keep you going all day.", "price": "$6 to $10", "when": "Lunch, hearty winter food", "order": "Comes with bacon bits and sour cream sauce"}, {"name": "Šaltibarščiai", "wiki": "Cold beet soup", "desc": "Shockingly pink cold beet soup made with kefir, served with hot boiled potatoes. A summer must.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Summer, with hot boiled potatoes", "order": "Always eat it with the side of potatoes"}, {"name": "Kepta duona", "wiki": "Rye bread", "desc": "Fried strips of dark rye bread rubbed with garlic, the classic beer snack. Often served with a cheesy dipping sauce.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Bar snack with beer", "order": "Comes with garlic and a cheese dip"}, {"name": "Kibinai", "wiki": "Kibinai", "desc": "Crescent-shaped pastries with meat filling from the Karaim community, best eaten in Trakai.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Trakai, as a snack or lunch", "order": "Mutton is traditional; chicken and veggie versions exist"}, {"name": "Kugelis", "wiki": "Kugelis", "desc": "Baked potato pudding with bacon, served with sour cream. Rich, cheap and comforting.", "price": "$5 to $8", "when": "Sunday lunch at home", "order": "Eat it with sour cream"}, {"name": "Šakotis", "wiki": "Šakotis", "desc": "A spiky tree-shaped cake baked on a rotating spit, made for celebrations and sold in bakeries.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Weddings and big celebrations"}], "food_tip": "Look for business lunch deals (dienos pietūs) on weekdays, where a soup and main cost a lot less than evening prices.", "culture": ["Lithuanians can seem reserved, but they're friendly once the ice is broken; a firm handshake and eye contact is the standard greeting.", "Don't call Lithuania a former Soviet country or lump it in with Russia, as the occupation is still a painful memory.", "Bring an odd number of flowers if invited to a home, since even numbers are for funerals.", "Basketball is basically a religion, and chatting about it is a quick way to make friends.", "Dress modestly in churches, which are active places of worship in this mainly Catholic country."], "safety": "Lithuania is very safe, with petty theft in nightlife areas being the main risk. Watch for overpriced drinks in bars where strangers invite you in. Ticks in forests can carry disease, so check yourself after hikes.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch runs from about noon to 2pm and dinner from 6pm.", "Tap water is safe and tastes good.", "Tip 5 to 10 percent in restaurants if the service was good.", "Vilnius has plenty of vegetarian and vegan options, while rural menus lean heavily on potatoes, pork and dairy.", "Covered markets sell smoked meats, curd cheese and dark rye bread cheaply."], "drinks": [{"name": "Gira", "desc": "Lithuanian kvass, a fizzy, slightly sweet drink made from rye bread, sold for about $1 to $2."}, {"name": "Midus", "desc": "Traditional mead, a honey drink that can be very strong. A shot or small glass costs $3 to $5."}], "greet": "Say 'Labas' to friends or 'Laba diena' more formally. Many people avoid shaking hands across a doorway, which is seen as bad luck.", "dress": "Dress is casual and practical, with warm, waterproof layers outside summer. Cover shoulders and take off hats in churches.", "table": "Say 'Skanaus' before eating, and try to finish your plate. When toasting 'į sveikatą', look people in the eye.", "faith": "About three quarters of Lithuanians are Catholic, and old pagan customs still colour festivals like Joninės at midsummer. At churches and the Gate of Dawn chapel in Vilnius, stay quiet and don't photograph people praying.", "phrases": [["Labas", "Hi"], ["Ačiū", "Thank you"], ["Kiek kainuoja?", "How much is it?"], ["Į sveikatą!", "Cheers!"], ["Skanaus", "Enjoy your meal"]]},
  "latvia": {"around": "Cheap electric trains run from Riga to the beaches of Jūrmala and to Sigulda, the gateway to Gauja National Park. Buses cover most other routes and are frequent and comfortable, and Lux Express and FlixBus run cheap international routes to Vilnius and Tallinn. Bolt works well in Riga, and the old town is easy to walk.", "money": "Latvia uses the euro, and cards and contactless are accepted almost everywhere, from supermarkets to bus ticket machines. Keep a little cash for markets and small rural guesthouses. Tipping isn't expected, but around 10 percent is appreciated for good service, and haggling isn't really done outside flea markets.", "sleep": "Riga has heaps of hostels in and around the old town, from party spots to quiet places. Around Gauja, Kuldīga and the coast you'll find guesthouses and campsites, and free campsites in state forests are a bonus for hikers. Summer weekends and the midsummer Jāņi holiday get busy.", "eat": ["Pelēkie zirņi: grey peas with fried bacon and onion", "Rupjmaize: dense dark rye bread", "Sklandrausis: rye tart filled with potato and carrot", "Riga Black Balsam: herbal liqueur, often added to coffee"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from LMT, Tele2 and Bite are cheap and easy to buy, EU SIMs roam free, and coverage is excellent. Free wifi is everywhere, including on many buses.", "heads_up": "In Riga's old town, avoid bars where strangers, often friendly women, invite you in, as some hit tourists with huge bills and aggressive bouncers.", "food": [{"name": "Grey peas with bacon", "wiki": "Grey peas", "desc": "Big grey peas fried with bacon and onion, eaten with kefir. Cheap, filling and a Latvian classic.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Lunch, at buffets and around Christmas", "order": "Eat with a glass of kefir"}, {"name": "Rupjmaize", "wiki": "Rupjmaize", "desc": "Dense, dark, slightly sweet rye bread served with almost everything. A loaf from the market is a great snack.", "price": "$2 to $4 a loaf", "when": "With every meal", "order": "Try it with cottage cheese and honey"}, {"name": "Sklandrausis", "wiki": "Sklandrausis", "desc": "A small rye tart filled with mashed potato and carrot, sweet and savoury at once. Find it in bakeries and markets.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Snack, from bakeries"}, {"name": "Pīrāgi", "wiki": "Speķrauši", "desc": "Little crescent-shaped buns filled with bacon and onion. Cheap at bakeries and perfect for a bus snack.", "price": "$0.70 to $1.50 each", "when": "Snack, at bakeries, Christmas and Jāņi"}, {"name": "Riga Black Balsam", "wiki": "Riga Black Balsam", "desc": "A bitter, inky herbal liqueur that locals mix into coffee, blackcurrant juice or cocktails. An acquired taste.", "price": "$3 to $6 a shot", "when": "After dinner, or in coffee", "order": "Try it mixed with blackcurrant juice"}], "food_tip": "Lido is a local chain of buffet restaurants serving hearty Latvian food at low prices, and the Riga Central Market in old Zeppelin hangars is great for cheap snacks and produce.", "culture": ["Latvians are reserved with strangers, so don't take quietness as rudeness; a handshake is the normal greeting.", "Midsummer (Jāņi) in late June is the biggest celebration of the year, with bonfires, singing, flower crowns and plenty of beer in the countryside.", "Bring an odd number of flowers when invited to someone's home, as even numbers are for funerals.", "Don't call Latvia a former Soviet state or assume people speak Russian by default, as language and history are sensitive subjects."], "safety": "Latvia is safe for travellers, but Riga's old town has a known scam where friendly strangers lure you into bars that charge enormous bills, so pick your own bars. Drink spiking and pickpocketing can happen in nightlife areas. Check for ticks after forest walks.", "eat_beats": ["Latvians eat lunch around noon to 2pm, and many cafes offer a cheap \"dienas piedāvājums\" (daily special).", "Tap water is safe to drink, though some people prefer bottled water for taste.", "Vegetarians can find plenty of potato, mushroom and cottage cheese dishes, and Riga has many veggie cafes.", "Foraging is a national hobby, so look for wild mushrooms and berries in markets in late summer.", "Tipping around 10 percent is common in restaurants if service is good."], "drinks": [{"name": "Kvass", "desc": "A fizzy, slightly sweet drink made from fermented rye bread, sold in bottles for about $1 to $2."}, {"name": "Birch sap", "desc": "Sweet, watery sap tapped from birch trees in early spring, drunk fresh or fermented, about $2 a bottle."}], "greet": "A firm handshake is standard, and men often take off gloves before shaking. Latvians may seem reserved, but are warm once you know them.", "dress": "Dress is neat and practical, and Riga is fairly stylish. Winters are cold and wet, so pack waterproofs and warm layers.", "table": "Wait for your host to start, and keep your hands visible on the table. Toasts are common with \"Priekā!\", and you should look people in the eye.", "faith": "Latvia is mainly Lutheran, Catholic and Orthodox, with many people not religious. Keep quiet in churches, and some Orthodox churches ask women to cover their heads.", "phrases": [["Sveiki", "Hello"], ["Paldies", "Thank you"], ["Cik maksā?", "How much does it cost?"], ["Priekā!", "Cheers!"], ["Uz redzēšanos", "Goodbye"]]},
  "estonia": {"around": "Elron trains and frequent intercity buses link Tallinn with Tartu, Pärnu and the coast, and tickets are cheap, especially if you book online. Lahemaa National Park is easier with a car or a tour. Ferries to Saaremaa and Hiiumaa are quick but busy in summer, so book ahead if you're bringing a car. Bolt started here and works everywhere, and Tallinn's public transport is cheap.", "money": "Estonia uses the euro, and it's one of the most digital countries on earth, so cards and contactless work almost everywhere, even for small amounts. You'll rarely need cash. Tipping isn't expected, though rounding up or 10 percent for good service is welcome, and haggling isn't really done.", "sleep": "Tallinn has many hostels in and around the old town, and Tartu has a few good ones too. The state forest agency RMK runs free campsites and simple huts all over the countryside, which are a brilliant budget option in summer. Guesthouses on the islands fill up in July.", "eat": ["Kiluvõileib: rye bread with spiced sprats, egg and butter", "Rukkileib: dark rye bread served with almost every meal", "Kama: roasted grain flour mixed with kefir or buttermilk", "Verivorst: blood sausage, traditionally eaten at Christmas"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Telia, Elisa and Tele2 are easy to get, EU SIMs roam free, and coverage is excellent. Free wifi is in most public places, cafés and even forests.", "heads_up": "Pedestrians must wear a reflector in the dark outside lit areas, and police can fine you for not having one.", "food": [{"name": "Kama", "wiki": "Kama (food)", "desc": "A flour made from roasted barley, rye, oats and peas, mixed with kefir or buttermilk for breakfast or a snack. Very Estonian, very cheap.", "price": "$2 to $4 a pack", "when": "Breakfast or snack, at home", "order": "Mix with kefir and berries"}, {"name": "Mulgipuder", "wiki": "Mulgipuder", "desc": "Mashed potato and barley porridge served with fried pork and onions. Simple southern comfort food.", "price": "$8 to $12", "when": "Lunch in southern Estonia"}, {"name": "Verivorst", "wiki": "Blood sausage", "desc": "Blood sausage, traditionally eaten at Christmas with lingonberry jam and sauerkraut.", "price": "$8 to $14", "when": "Christmas season, at home or restaurants", "order": "Eat it with lingonberry jam and sauerkraut"}, {"name": "Kohuke", "wiki": "Curd snack", "desc": "A sweet curd cheese bar covered in chocolate, sold in every supermarket fridge. Addictive and cheap.", "price": "$0.70 to $1.50", "when": "Snack, from supermarket fridges", "order": "Try the vanilla or berry versions"}, {"name": "Vana Tallinn", "wiki": "Vana Tallinn", "desc": "A sweet, spiced rum-based liqueur, sipped neat or added to coffee. A popular souvenir.", "price": "$4 to $6 a shot", "when": "Evenings, or in coffee", "order": "Try it in hot coffee in winter"}], "food_tip": "Look for weekday lunch deals (päevapakkumine) where a soup and main cost a fraction of the dinner price, and fill up on dark rye bread and smoked fish from the markets.", "culture": ["Estonians value personal space and silence, so small talk with strangers is rare; don't take quietness for unfriendliness.", "Sauna is a big deal and often done naked, so follow the lead of your hosts.", "Don't call Estonia Russian or part of the former Soviet Union, as the occupation is still a raw memory.", "Punctuality matters, so show up on time for tours and meetings.", "Song festivals and Midsummer's Eve (Jaanipäev) are huge, with bonfires and singing all over the countryside."], "safety": "Estonia is safe. In Tallinn's old town, watch your drink and your wallet on busy nights, especially around stag parties. Ticks in forests carry disease, so check yourself after hiking.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch deals usually run from 11am to 3pm on weekdays, and dinner starts around 6pm.", "Tallinn's Balti Jaam market has a food hall with cheap stalls and fresh local produce.", "Tap water is safe and good to drink across the country.", "Vegetarians are well catered for in Tallinn and Tartu, though rural menus lean heavily on pork and potatoes.", "Tipping isn't required, but 5% to 10% for good service is appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Kali", "desc": "A slightly sweet, fermented bread drink, a bit like kvass. A bottle costs about $1.50 to $3."}, {"name": "Kasemahl", "desc": "Fresh birch sap collected in spring, light and slightly sweet. Expect $3 to $5 a bottle in April and May."}], "greet": "Shake hands firmly and say tere, with direct eye contact. Estonians are reserved, so hugging is for close friends.", "dress": "Casual and practical clothes work, with layers and a waterproof since the weather changes fast. Churches expect modest dress.", "table": "Say head isu (good appetite) before eating, and keep hands on the table. Clinking glasses with eye contact and saying terviseks is the norm.", "faith": "Estonia is one of the least religious countries in the world, with small Lutheran and Orthodox communities. In churches, keep quiet and respect services.", "phrases": [["Tere", "Hello"], ["Aitäh", "Thank you"], ["Palun", "Please"], ["Kui palju see maksab?", "How much is it?"], ["Terviseks!", "Cheers!"]]},
  "finland": {"around": "VR trains and cheap Onnibus coaches connect Helsinki with Tampere, Turku, the Lakeland and the north. The night trains to Rovaniemi and Lapland sell out, especially in winter, so book early. Buses are usually cheaper than trains, and Uber and Bolt work in Helsinki and other big cities. Ferries to Tallinn and Stockholm are cheap ways to hop between countries.", "money": "Finland uses the euro and is close to cashless, so cards and contactless work almost everywhere, even in saunas and at market stalls. Some places don't take cash at all. Tipping isn't expected, and haggling is not a thing. It's an expensive country, so budget carefully.", "sleep": "Hostels in Helsinki and bigger cities are the cheapest beds, and campsites with cabins are common in summer. Everyman's right lets you camp a night on most uncultivated land, and wilderness huts in Lapland are free for hikers. Prices are high, so book early in summer and around Christmas in Lapland.", "eat": ["Karjalanpiirakka: rye pastry filled with rice porridge and egg butter", "Lohikeitto: creamy salmon soup with potatoes and dill", "Korvapuusti: cardamom cinnamon bun", "Salmiakki: salty licorice that Finns love"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from DNA, Elisa and Telia are cheap with big data allowances, EU SIMs roam free, and coverage is good except in remote parts of Lapland.", "heads_up": "Saunas are usually enjoyed naked and in quiet conversation, and shared ones are often split by gender.", "food": [{"name": "Karjalanpiirakka", "wiki": "Karelian pasty", "desc": "Thin rye pastry filled with rice porridge and topped with egg butter. Cheap and sold in every bakery and supermarket.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Breakfast or snack, from bakeries", "order": "Spread munavoi (egg butter) on top while it's warm"}, {"name": "Lohikeitto", "wiki": "Lohikeitto", "desc": "Creamy salmon soup with potatoes and dill, served with rye bread. Market halls do great versions.", "price": "$12 to $18", "when": "Lunch at market halls"}, {"name": "Korvapuusti", "wiki": "Cinnamon roll", "desc": "Cardamom-spiced cinnamon bun, best eaten with coffee in a café. Finns drink more coffee per person than almost anyone.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "The afternoon coffee break"}, {"name": "Salmiakki", "wiki": "Salty liquorice", "desc": "Salty black liquorice that Finns adore and most visitors find shocking. Grab a bag from any supermarket.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Snack, from any shop"}, {"name": "Leipäjuusto", "wiki": "Bread cheese", "desc": "A squeaky baked cheese served warm with cloudberry jam, especially in the north.", "price": "$6 to $12", "when": "Dessert in Lapland and the north"}, {"name": "Sautéed reindeer", "wiki": "Sautéed reindeer", "desc": "Thin strips of reindeer fried with butter and served with mashed potato and lingonberries. A Lapland classic.", "price": "$20 to $35", "when": "Dinner in Lapland", "order": "It's called poronkäristys on menus"}], "food_tip": "Lunch buffets on weekdays are the best deal in Finland, with all-you-can-eat soup, mains and salad at a fixed price. Supermarkets like Lidl and K-Market are where budget travellers eat the rest of the time.", "culture": ["Finns value personal space and comfortable silence, and small talk is rare, so quietness isn't rudeness; watch how people spread out at a bus stop.", "Sauna is central to life, usually taken naked and with quiet chat, and shared saunas are often separated by gender.", "Take your shoes off when entering someone's home.", "Being on time matters, whether it's a dinner invite or a tour pickup, and turning up late is quietly noted.", "Respect everyman's right by leaving no trace, staying away from homes and not lighting fires where it's banned."], "safety": "Finland is one of the safest countries in the world. The real risks are the weather, so dress properly for winter cold and plan hikes carefully in Lapland. Mosquitoes in summer are brutal, so bring repellent.", "eat_beats": ["Finns eat lunch early, from about 11am, and dinner around 5 to 6pm, so lunch buffets usually end by 2pm.", "Tap water is excellent everywhere and served free in restaurants.", "Vegetarian and vegan dishes are standard, and menus mark allergens with letters like L (lactose free) and G (gluten free).", "Tipping isn't expected, as service is included in prices.", "Supermarkets only sell drinks up to 8 percent alcohol, so anything stronger comes from the state-run Alko shops."], "drinks": [{"name": "Lonkero", "desc": "The long drink, gin mixed with grapefruit soda, sold in cans for around $3 to $4 at supermarkets."}, {"name": "Sima", "desc": "A lightly fermented lemon and brown sugar drink made for Vappu, the May Day festival. A bottle costs about $3."}], "greet": "A firm handshake with eye contact and a short moi (hi) is all it takes. Hugs are for close friends.", "dress": "Dress for the weather in layers, and in winter add warm boots, a hat and wool base layers. Smart casual works almost everywhere.", "table": "At buffets take only what you'll eat and finish your plate. Wait for the host to say kippis before you drink.", "faith": "About 62 percent of Finns belong to the Lutheran Church, though few attend weekly. Keep your voice low in churches, and treat Sámi sacred sites in Lapland, like Ukonkivi on Lake Inari, with respect.", "phrases": [["Moi", "Hi"], ["Kiitos", "Thank you"], ["Paljonko maksaa?", "How much is it?"], ["Kippis!", "Cheers!"], ["Anteeksi", "Excuse me, sorry"]]},
  "sweden": {"around": "SJ trains, regional trains and FlixBus connect Stockholm, Gothenburg and Malmö, and booking early gets much cheaper fares. Night trains run north to Abisko and Kiruna for the Kungsleden trail. Uber and Bolt work in major cities, and public transport passes in Stockholm are good value if you're staying a few days.", "money": "Sweden uses the krona and is almost cashless, with many places only taking cards. Contactless works everywhere, and you may never see a banknote. Tipping isn't expected, though rounding up in restaurants is common, and there's no haggling.", "sleep": "STF hostels and huts are the classic budget option, including mountain huts along the Kungsleden. The right of public access (allemansrätten) lets you camp a night on most open land, which is a huge money saver. Cities are expensive, so book hostels ahead in summer.", "eat": ["Köttbullar: meatballs with cream sauce, lingonberries and potatoes", "Kanelbulle: cardamom-scented cinnamon bun, best with fika coffee", "Räksmörgås: open shrimp sandwich with egg and mayonnaise", "Pyttipanna: fried diced potatoes, meat and onion with egg"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Telia, Tele2, Telenor or Comviq are easy to buy, EU SIMs roam free, and coverage is good except in remote mountains.", "heads_up": "Alcohol stronger than 3.5 percent is only sold in state-run Systembolaget shops, which close early on Saturdays and all day Sunday.", "food": [{"name": "Köttbullar", "wiki": "Meatball", "desc": "Swedish meatballs with cream sauce, lingonberries and mashed potatoes. A lunch special classic.", "price": "$13 to $18", "when": "Weekday lunch, dagens lunch"}, {"name": "Kanelbulle", "wiki": "Cinnamon roll", "desc": "Cardamom-scented cinnamon bun, eaten during fika with coffee. Bakeries everywhere.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Fika time, mid-morning or afternoon", "order": "Try a kardemummabulle, the cardamom version"}, {"name": "Kladdkaka", "wiki": "Kladdkaka", "desc": "A gooey chocolate cake, almost like a brownie, served with cream. Cafés love it.", "price": "$4 to $6 a slice", "when": "Fika at cafes"}, {"name": "Semla", "wiki": "Semla", "desc": "Cardamom bun filled with almond paste and whipped cream, eaten around Lent. Look out for it from January to March.", "price": "$4 to $6", "when": "January to Shrove Tuesday"}, {"name": "Pyttipanna", "wiki": "Pyttipanna", "desc": "Fried diced potatoes, meat and onion topped with a fried egg and pickled beetroot. Cheap comfort food.", "price": "$11 to $16", "when": "Lunch or easy dinner"}, {"name": "Surströmming", "wiki": "Surströmming", "desc": "Fermented Baltic herring with a legendary smell, eaten in late summer. Try it outdoors.", "price": "$15 to $25 a tin", "when": "Late August parties", "order": "Eat it in tunnbröd with potatoes and onion"}], "food_tip": "Look for dagens lunch (lunch of the day) on weekdays, a cheap set meal with salad, bread and coffee included. Supermarkets like Lidl, ICA and Willys keep things affordable.", "culture": ["Fika, a coffee break with something sweet, is sacred, and joining one is a great way to meet locals.", "Swedes take queuing seriously, so look for the ticket machine (nummerlapp) in pharmacies, bakeries and shops and wait for your number.", "Take your shoes off when entering someone's home, it's close to universal.", "Lagom, not too much and not too little, sums up the culture: modesty is valued and bragging goes down badly.", "Respect allemansrätten by leaving no trace and keeping away from people's homes when camping."], "safety": "Sweden is very safe for travellers. Pickpockets operate in busy Stockholm spots, so keep an eye on your phone. In the mountains, weather turns fast, so plan hikes properly and carry layers.", "eat_beats": ["Swedes eat early, with lunch from 11am and dinner from 6pm.", "Tap water is excellent and free, so fill up anywhere.", "Vegetarian and vegan food is everywhere, and most lunch menus include a veggie option.", "Tipping isn't required as wages are fair, but rounding up or adding 5 to 10 percent is welcome.", "Most places are card only, so don't bother carrying cash."], "drinks": [{"name": "Snaps", "desc": "A small shot of flavoured aquavit, drunk with a song at midsummer and crayfish parties. Around $5 to $8 at a bar."}, {"name": "Julmust", "desc": "A sweet, spiced soft drink sold only at Christmas, outselling Coca-Cola then, for about $2 a bottle."}], "greet": "A firm handshake with eye contact is standard, and hugs come with closer friends. Say hej for hello.", "dress": "Swedes dress simply and practically, with neutral colours. There are few rules, but wear layers, as weather changes fast.", "table": "Wait for the host to say skål before drinking, and make eye contact before and after the toast. Don't help yourself to seconds before everyone has had firsts.", "faith": "Sweden is one of the least religious countries in the world, though the Lutheran Church of Sweden is still part of life. Be quiet in churches and respect services.", "phrases": [["Hej", "Hello"], ["Tack", "Thank you"], ["Vad kostar det?", "How much does it cost?"], ["Skål!", "Cheers!"], ["Ska vi fika?", "Shall we have a coffee break?"]]},
  "norway": {"around": "Vy trains run the scenic Bergen and Nordland lines, and cheap minipris fares appear when you book early on Vy or Entur. Ferries and express boats are part of the public transport network in the fjords, and buses serve the Lofoten Islands. Hitchhiking is fairly common in rural areas, and FlixBus and Nor-Way Bussekspress are cheaper than trains on some routes.", "money": "Norway uses the krone and is close to cashless, with cards working everywhere from tiny kiosks to ferries. It's one of the most expensive countries on the planet, so budget carefully. Tipping isn't expected, though rounding up for good service is welcome, and there's no haggling.", "sleep": "Hostels, campsites with cabins and DNT (Norwegian Trekking Association) huts keep costs down. The right to roam allows free wild camping on most uncultivated land, which can save you a fortune. Lofoten rorbuer and fjord campsites book up in summer.", "eat": ["Brunost: sweet brown caramel-like cheese, sliced onto bread", "Fiskesuppe: creamy fish soup, a Bergen classic", "Pølse i lompe: hot dog wrapped in a soft potato flatbread", "Skillingsboller: large cinnamon buns from Bergen bakeries"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Telia, Telenor or Ice are easy to buy, EU SIMs roam free as Norway is in the EEA, and coverage is strong even in many fjords.", "heads_up": "Mountain weather changes quickly, so check the forecast and the hiking advice before longer hikes, even in summer.", "food": [{"name": "Brunost", "wiki": "Brunost", "desc": "Sweet caramel-flavoured brown cheese, sliced thin onto bread or waffles. A supermarket staple.", "price": "$5 to $8", "when": "Breakfast on bread, packed lunches", "order": "Use a cheese slicer (ostehøvel) for thin slices"}, {"name": "Fårikål", "wiki": "Fårikål", "desc": "Mutton and cabbage stewed with whole peppercorns, the national dish, eaten in autumn.", "price": "$20 to $30", "when": "Autumn dinners, the last Thursday in September", "order": "Eat with boiled potatoes and flatbread"}, {"name": "Lefse", "wiki": "Lefse", "desc": "A soft potato flatbread, spread with butter, sugar and cinnamon as a snack, or as lompe wrapped around a petrol station hot dog.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Snacks, cafes and Christmas", "order": "Try a hot dog wrapped in lompe at a petrol station"}, {"name": "Kvikk Lunsj", "wiki": "Kvikk Lunsj", "desc": "A chocolate wafer bar that Norwegians always carry on hikes. Cheap trail fuel.", "price": "$1.50 to $2.50", "when": "On every hike, especially at Easter"}, {"name": "Rakfisk", "wiki": "Rakfisk", "desc": "Fermented trout eaten in winter, a strong-tasting delicacy you'll either love or hate.", "price": "$25 to $40", "when": "Winter, around Christmas", "order": "Eat in lefse with onion and sour cream"}, {"name": "Skillingsboller", "wiki": "Cinnamon roll", "desc": "Big cinnamon buns from Bergen bakeries, perfect with coffee.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Coffee break, at bakeries", "order": "Bergen's version is bigger and less sweet"}], "food_tip": "Supermarkets like Rema 1000, Kiwi and Coop Extra are where budget travellers eat, and hot dogs at petrol stations are the cheapest hot meal around. Bring a reusable bottle, as tap water is excellent.", "culture": ["Norwegians are reserved with strangers and small talk is rare, but they warm up fast out on a hiking trail.", "Take your shoes off when you go into someone's home, and expect hosts to offer slippers or a coffee straight away.", "The right to roam (allemannsretten) comes with responsibility, so leave no trace, camp at least 150 metres from homes and don't light fires in summer bans.", "17 May, Constitution Day, is the big national party, with parades, bunads (folk costumes) and everyone out on the streets; it's a great day to be in town.", "Norway is very egalitarian, so showing off wealth or status is frowned upon."], "safety": "Norway is very safe. Mountain weather changes quickly, so check yr.no forecasts and stick to marked trails. Popular hikes like Trolltunga and Preikestolen see rescues every year from underprepared hikers.", "eat_beats": ["Norwegians eat lunch early, often a packed open sandwich (matpakke) around 11:30am, and dinner from about 4pm to 6pm.", "Tap water is safe and delicious, and asking for it in restaurants is free and normal.", "Supermarkets close on Sundays, apart from small convenience stores, so shop on Saturday.", "Alcohol over 4.7 percent is sold only at Vinmonopolet state shops, and supermarket beer sales stop at 8pm on weekdays and 6pm on Saturdays.", "Service is included, so tipping isn't expected, though rounding up for good service is fine."], "drinks": [{"name": "Akevitt", "desc": "A caraway-flavoured spirit drunk with Christmas food and fish, often aged in barrels at sea. A shot in a bar costs around $12 to $15."}, {"name": "Solo", "desc": "A classic orange soda that Norwegians are proud of, about $3 for a bottle from a supermarket."}], "greet": "A firm handshake and eye contact is standard, and friends may hug. Norwegians use first names quickly, even with bosses.", "dress": "Dress is casual and outdoorsy, so good layers and waterproofs matter more than style. Bring wool, as weather changes fast in the mountains.", "table": "Wait for the host to say \"vær så god\" before you start eating. Saying \"takk for maten\" (thanks for the food) at the end is expected.", "faith": "Most Norwegians belong to the Lutheran Church of Norway but few attend services. Stave churches are protected heritage sites, so follow signs and don't touch the carvings.", "phrases": [["Hei", "Hello"], ["Takk", "Thank you"], ["Hvor mye koster det?", "How much does it cost?"], ["Skål!", "Cheers!"], ["Takk for maten", "Thanks for the meal"]]},
  "denmark": {"around": "DSB trains and regional buses connect Copenhagen with Odense, Aarhus, Møn and the rest of the country, and the cheap Orange tickets released in advance can halve the fare. FlixBus is often the cheapest option for long hauls and international hops to Germany and Sweden. In Copenhagen the metro runs around the clock, but a bike is the real local way to get around, either rented from your hostel or on an app like Donkey Republic.", "money": "Denmark uses the Danish krone, not the euro, and it's practically cashless, with cards and contactless taken everywhere from hot dog wagons to public toilets. Service is built into prices, so tipping isn't expected, though rounding up for great service is a nice touch. There's no haggling, and it's an expensive country, so a daily budget needs a bit of discipline.", "sleep": "Copenhagen has big, well-run hostels that are as nice as many hotels, though dorm beds still cost more than most of Europe. Around the country there are Danhostels, plenty of campsites, and hundreds of free or very cheap basic wooden shelters for hikers and cyclists, bookable on the Udinaturen site. Book ahead for summer and festival weeks like Roskilde.", "eat": ["Smørrebrød: open rye bread sandwiches with many toppings", "Rød pølse: bright red hot dog from a street wagon", "Wienerbrød: buttery laminated pastries, what Americans call Danish", "Stegt flæsk: crispy pork belly with parsley sauce and potatoes"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Lebara, Telia or 3 are easy to buy in supermarkets and kiosks, EU SIMs roam free, and coverage is excellent. Wifi is everywhere, including on trains.", "heads_up": "Never walk in the bike lanes, as cyclists move fast and expect them to be clear.", "food": [{"name": "Smørrebrød", "wiki": "Smørrebrød", "desc": "Open sandwiches on dense rye bread, topped with things like pickled herring, roast pork, egg and shrimp or liver pâté. Bakeries and lunch counters are much cheaper than the fancy smørrebrød restaurants.", "price": "$8 to $14 a piece", "when": "Lunch, usually two or three pieces", "order": "Start with herring, then move to meat"}, {"name": "Rød pølse", "wiki": "Rød pølse", "desc": "A bright red sausage from a pølsevogn street wagon, loaded with mustard, ketchup, remoulade and crispy onions. One of the cheapest hot meals in the country.", "price": "$4 to $6", "when": "Quick snack from a pølsevogn", "order": "Get it with remoulade and crispy onions"}, {"name": "Wienerbrød", "wiki": "Danish pastry", "desc": "The buttery, flaky pastries the rest of the world calls Danishes. Grab one from any bakery for breakfast.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Morning, with coffee", "order": "Try a kanelsnegl, a cinnamon swirl"}, {"name": "Stegt flæsk", "wiki": "Stegt flæsk", "desc": "Crispy fried pork belly with boiled potatoes and parsley sauce, voted Denmark's national dish. Old-school lunch places serve it all you can eat.", "price": "$15 to $22", "when": "Lunch in old-fashioned cafés", "order": "Comes with potatoes and parsley sauce"}, {"name": "Æbleskiver", "wiki": "Æbleskiver", "desc": "Fluffy round pancake balls dusted with icing sugar and dipped in jam, mostly around Christmas with a glass of gløgg.", "price": "$5 to $7 for three", "when": "Christmas markets in December", "order": "Dip them in jam and powdered sugar"}, {"name": "Flødebolle", "wiki": "Chocolate-coated marshmallow treats", "desc": "A chocolate-covered marshmallow puff on a wafer base, sold everywhere and often popped on top of ice cream.", "price": "$1 to $2 each", "when": "Treat, with coffee or kids' parties"}], "food_tip": "Supermarkets like Netto, Lidl and Rema 1000 will save your budget, and their bakery sections are great for breakfast. Street food halls such as Reffen in Copenhagen are cheaper than sit-down restaurants and a good place to hang out.", "culture": ["Hygge, the Danish knack for cosiness, is real: candles, long dinners and unhurried chats are a way of life, especially in winter.", "Danes are friendly but reserved with strangers and quite direct, so a blunt answer isn't meant as rude.", "Never stroll in the bike lanes, as cyclists move fast and will ring their bells or worse; look both ways before stepping off the kerb.", "Society is very informal and egalitarian, so first names are normal with everyone and showing off is frowned upon.", "Take your shoes off when you go into someone's home, and if you're invited for dinner, turning up on time matters."], "safety": "Denmark is about as safe as travel gets. The main issues are bike theft, so always lock your bike to something, and pickpockets in busy spots like Nyhavn, Strøget and Copenhagen Central Station. Christiania has its own rules, notably no photos on Pusher Street.", "eat_beats": ["Danes eat early, lunch from 11:30am and dinner around 6pm, so kitchens can be quiet by 9pm.", "Tap water is excellent and free to ask for, though some restaurants charge a small fee for it.", "Vegetarian and vegan options are easy to find, especially in Copenhagen and Aarhus.", "Supermarket bakeries have cheap fresh bread, and sold-out deals are listed on food waste apps.", "Service is included in prices, so tipping isn't expected, though rounding up is fine."], "drinks": [{"name": "Akvavit", "desc": "A spirit flavoured with caraway or dill, drunk in ice-cold shots with herring at lunch. About $6 to $9 a shot."}, {"name": "Gløgg", "desc": "Hot spiced wine with raisins and almonds, sold at Christmas markets. About $5 to $8 a cup."}], "greet": "A firm handshake and eye contact on meeting, and hugs between friends. Small talk is minimal, so get to the point.", "dress": "Danes dress smart but casual, often in black and muted colours. Bring rain gear and layers, as the weather changes fast.", "table": "Wait for the host to say \"skål\" before drinking, and look around the table while toasting. Don't leave the table early at a dinner party.", "faith": "Most Danes belong to the Lutheran Church of Denmark, though few attend regularly. Churches are open to visitors, so keep quiet and avoid services.", "phrases": [["Hej", "Hi"], ["Tak", "Thanks"], ["Hvad koster det?", "How much is it?"], ["Skål", "Cheers"], ["Hyggeligt", "Cosy, nice"]]},
  "iceland": {"around": "Strætó public buses cover parts of the Ring Road but are infrequent and some routes only run in summer, so most people rent a car or campervan or join tours. Rentals sell out in summer, so book ahead, and get gravel protection insurance. There's no Uber, and taxis are expensive, so use the Flybus or Airport Direct from Keflavík airport. Hitchhiking is fairly common and generally safe.", "money": "Iceland uses the Icelandic króna, but cards are accepted everywhere, even for a coffee or a campsite shower, so you rarely need cash. You'll often need a PIN for card payments at petrol stations, so make sure you know yours. Tipping isn't expected, and there's no haggling. Iceland is brutally expensive, so plan for groceries and camping to keep costs down.", "sleep": "Hostels, guesthouses and campsites are the budget options, and campervans are hugely popular in summer. Wild camping in tents is heavily restricted, so use official campsites, and campervans must stay in campsites. Book beds along the south coast and in Reykjavik well ahead in high season.", "eat": ["Pylsa: Icelandic lamb-based hot dog with crispy onions", "Skyr: thick, protein-rich cultured dairy, like strained yogurt", "Kjötsúpa: hearty lamb and root vegetable soup", "Plokkfiskur: mashed fish and potato stew, served with rye bread"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Síminn, Nova or Vodafone are easy to buy, EU SIMs roam free as Iceland is in the EEA, and coverage is good along the Ring Road but patchy in the highlands.", "heads_up": "Check road.is and safetravel.is before driving or hiking, and never drive an ordinary rental car on F-roads, which need a 4x4 and are closed most of the year.", "food": [{"name": "Pylsa", "wiki": "Bæjarins Beztu Pylsur", "desc": "Iceland's famous hot dog made mostly from lamb, topped with raw and crispy onions, ketchup, sweet mustard and remoulade. Bæjarins Beztu in Reykjavik is the classic stand, and it's one of the cheapest meals in the country.", "price": "$5 to $7", "when": "Any time, from stands and petrol stations", "order": "Ask for 'eina með öllu', one with everything"}, {"name": "Skyr", "wiki": "Skyr", "desc": "Thick, protein-rich cultured dairy, like a super strained yogurt. Cheap from supermarkets and a staple for hikers.", "price": "$2 to $3", "when": "Breakfast or a snack", "order": "Plain skyr with berries and cream is the traditional way"}, {"name": "Rúgbrauð", "wiki": "Rúgbrauð", "desc": "Dense, sweet rye bread sometimes baked in hot springs, eaten with butter and smoked fish.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "With butter, smoked trout or lamb", "order": "Look for hverabrauð, baked by geothermal heat"}, {"name": "Hákarl", "wiki": "Hákarl", "desc": "Fermented shark with an ammonia smell, usually eaten in tiny cubes with a shot of Brennivín. More a dare than a meal.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Þorrablót midwinter feasts", "order": "Chase it with a shot of brennivín"}, {"name": "Kleina", "wiki": "Klenät", "desc": "Twisted fried doughnuts sold in supermarkets and bakeries, great with coffee.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Coffee breaks, from bakeries"}, {"name": "Brennivín", "wiki": "Brennivín", "desc": "Caraway-flavoured schnapps nicknamed Black Death, the traditional chaser for hákarl.", "price": "$10 to $14", "when": "Þorri feasts and celebrations", "order": "Served ice cold as a shot"}], "food_tip": "Bónus and Krónan supermarkets are where everyone eats on a budget, and many hostels have kitchens. Happy hours in Reykjavik bars make drinking a little less painful, and tap water is excellent, so never buy bottled.", "culture": ["Shower naked before entering public pools and hot springs, it's a strict hygiene rule and attendants will remind you.", "Icelanders are laid back and egalitarian, and first names are used for everyone, even the president.", "Respect nature: stay on marked paths, don't build cairns or drive off road, which is illegal and fined heavily.", "Don't pick wildflowers or moss, which takes decades to recover once trampled or torn up.", "Icelanders mostly use patronymic surnames ending in -son or -dóttir, so people go by first names and families rarely share a surname."], "safety": "Iceland's crime rate is tiny, but nature is dangerous: sneaker waves at Reynisfjara beach have killed tourists, so stay well back from the water. Weather changes fast, so check road.is and safetravel.is daily and leave your travel plans with SafeTravel. Don't walk on glaciers without a guide.", "eat_beats": ["Restaurant meals are expensive, so lunch specials are the best time to eat out.", "Petrol stations double as diners on the Ring Road, serving hot dogs, burgers and soup.", "Tipping isn't expected, as service is included in prices.", "Vegetarian and vegan options are easy in Reykjavik but thinner in rural towns, so stock up before long drives.", "Hot tap water smells of sulphur, so fill your bottle from the cold tap."], "drinks": [{"name": "Malt og appelsín", "desc": "A Christmas favourite mixing malt drink with orange soda, sold in shops for about $3."}, {"name": "Coffee", "desc": "Icelanders are among the world's biggest coffee drinkers, and some cafés offer refills. A cup costs $4 to $6."}], "greet": "A handshake with 'Hæ' or 'Góðan daginn' is standard. Icelanders can seem reserved at first, but they warm up quickly, and friends hug.", "dress": "Layers, waterproofs and good boots matter far more than style, even in summer. There are no strict dress rules, but pack a swimsuit for pools and hot springs.", "table": "Take your shoes off at the door of a home, and wait for the host to say 'gjörið svo vel' before starting. Meals are relaxed and informal.", "faith": "Most Icelanders belong to the Lutheran Church of Iceland, though few attend regularly, and the old Norse faith Ásatrú is growing. Visit churches quietly and don't wander in during services.", "phrases": [["Halló / Hæ", "Hello"], ["Takk fyrir", "Thank you"], ["Hvað kostar þetta?", "How much is this?"], ["Skál!", "Cheers!"], ["Bless", "Bye"]]},
  "malta": {"around": "Malta Public Transport buses reach almost every corner of Malta and Gozo, and a Tallinja card with a discounted bundle of journeys makes them very cheap, though buses can be slow and packed in summer. The Gozo Channel car ferry from Ċirkewwa and the fast ferry from Valletta link to Gozo, and the little Valletta to Sliema ferry beats the traffic. Bolt, Uber and eCabs are widely used and fairly priced. Renting a scooter is popular but the driving is chaotic, so only do it if you're confident.", "money": "Malta uses the euro, and cards work in most restaurants, supermarkets and hostels. Carry cash for pastizzi shops, village bars, kiosks and the odd taxi, as plenty of small places still prefer it. Bank ATMs are easy to find, though some independent machines charge a fee. Leave 5 to 10 percent in restaurants if service isn't already on the bill, and don't expect to haggle.", "sleep": "Hostels cluster around St Julian's, Paceville and Sliema, which are lively and close to the nightlife. Gozo has farmhouse rooms and guesthouses that feel much more relaxed. Summer prices rise sharply and the island gets packed, so book early for June to September.", "eat": ["Pastizzi: flaky pastries filled with ricotta or mushy peas", "Ftira: Maltese bread with tuna, tomato, capers and olives", "Stuffat tal-fenek: rabbit stewed in wine and garlic", "Kinnie: bittersweet orange and herb soft drink"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Epic, GO or Melita need ID, EU SIMs roam free, and coverage is excellent on Malta and Gozo.", "heads_up": "Traffic drives on the left, so look right first when crossing the road.", "food": [{"name": "Pastizzi", "wiki": "Pastizz", "desc": "Flaky diamond-shaped pastries stuffed with ricotta or mushy peas, sold hot from hole-in-the-wall shops for well under a euro each. The ultimate cheap Maltese snack.", "price": "$0.60 to $1", "when": "Breakfast or late night snack", "order": "Choose ricotta (tal-irkotta) or mushy pea (tal-piżelli)"}, {"name": "Ftira", "wiki": "Ftira", "desc": "Ring-shaped Maltese bread stuffed with tuna, tomato paste, capers, olives and onions, especially good on Gozo. A filling beach lunch for a few euros.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Lunch, especially on Gozo", "order": "The classic filling is tuna, capers, olives and tomato"}, {"name": "Stuffat tal-fenek", "wiki": "Rabbit stew", "desc": "Rabbit slowly stewed in wine and garlic, Malta's national dish. A fenkata, a rabbit feast at a village restaurant, is a proper local night out.", "price": "$14 to $22", "when": "Sunday lunch and family feasts"}, {"name": "Imqaret", "wiki": "Imqaret", "desc": "Fried date-filled pastries flavoured with aniseed, sold from street stalls at festas and markets.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Festas and street stalls", "order": "Best hot, fresh from the fryer"}, {"name": "Bigilla", "wiki": "Bigilla", "desc": "A garlicky bean dip served with Maltese bread and crackers, often as a bar snack.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Starter with galletti crackers"}, {"name": "Kinnie", "wiki": "Kinnie", "desc": "Malta's own bitter orange and herb soft drink, a bit like a lighter, more bitter cola. Try it with a Cisk lager by its side.", "price": "$1.50 to $2.50", "when": "Any time, with pastizzi"}], "food_tip": "Pastizzi shops and ftira kiosks keep you fed for a few euros, and supermarkets like Lidl are good for self-catering. Village festa stalls in summer are great for cheap snacks.", "culture": ["Malta is strongly Catholic, so cover shoulders and knees in churches, including St John's Co-Cathedral in Valletta, where you may be turned away otherwise.", "Summer village festas honour each parish's patron saint with fireworks, brass bands and processions, and visitors are welcome to join in.", "English is an official language alongside Maltese, so communication is easy, but a grazzi (thank you) is always appreciated.", "Swimwear is for the beach; walking around towns shirtless or in bikinis is frowned upon and sometimes fined.", "Maltese people are proud of their history and very sociable, so expect chats with strangers in bars and buses."], "safety": "Malta is very safe, with the main risks being sunstroke, slippery rocks at swimming spots, and the occasional jellyfish bloom. Paceville nightlife can get rowdy late at night, so watch your drinks and belongings. Traffic is heavy and drivers can be impatient, so take care crossing roads.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is around 12:30 to 2pm and dinner from 7:30pm, with Sunday lunch a big family event.", "Tap water is safe but desalinated and tastes odd, so most locals drink bottled.", "Tipping 5 to 10 percent is customary in restaurants.", "Vegetarians can enjoy pastizzi, bigilla, ġbejniet cheese and salads.", "A Maltese platter, with sausage, ġbejniet, bigilla and olives, makes a good shared starter."], "drinks": [{"name": "Bajtra", "desc": "A sweet liqueur made from prickly pears, served chilled. A shot costs $3 to $5."}, {"name": "Te fit-tazza", "desc": "Tea in a glass, usually with sweet condensed milk, served at village bars for $1 to $2."}], "greet": "A handshake with 'Bonġu' (good morning) or 'Bonswa' (good evening), and friends kiss on both cheeks.", "dress": "Summer is hot, so light clothes are fine, but bring a cover up for churches. People dress up a little for village festas and nights out.", "table": "Meals are long and social, especially Sunday lunch, and it's polite to accept a second helping. Mopping up sauce with bread is normal.", "faith": "Over 80 percent of Maltese are Catholic, and the islands have more than 350 churches. Keep quiet during Mass, and during Holy Week processions stand respectfully as the statues pass.", "phrases": [["Bonġu", "Good morning"], ["Grazzi", "Thank you"], ["Kemm jiswa?", "How much is it?"], ["Saħħa!", "Cheers / Goodbye"], ["Iva / Le", "Yes / No"]]},
  "cyprus": {"around": "There are no trains, but cheap intercity buses run by Intercity Buses link Nicosia, Limassol, Larnaca and Paphos, and local buses serve the beaches and airport. Buses to the Troodos mountains and the more remote corners are limited, so many people rent a car for a few days; Cypriots drive on the left. In the north, dolmuş minibuses connect towns cheaply. Bolt operates in the main cities in the south.", "money": "The Republic of Cyprus in the south uses the euro, and cards work widely in towns, though village tavernas and kiosks often want cash. Northern Cyprus uses the Turkish lira, though euros are often accepted at a worse rate. ATMs are easy to find on both sides. Leaving about 10 percent is the norm in restaurants unless service is already included, and haggling is only for markets in the north.", "sleep": "Paphos, Larnaca and Nicosia have a few hostels and plenty of cheap apartments, especially outside July and August. Troodos villages have agrotourism guesthouses in restored stone houses. In the north, Kyrenia has budget hotels and guesthouses.", "eat": ["Halloumi: squeaky grilled cheese, often from goat and sheep milk", "Souvlaki: grilled pork skewers in pita with salad", "Kleftiko: lamb slow-baked in a sealed clay oven", "Koupepia: vine leaves stuffed with rice and minced meat"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Cyta, Epic or PrimeTel are easy to buy and EU SIMs roam free in the south, but northern Cyprus uses Turkish networks where EU roaming does not apply.", "heads_up": "Crossing between the south and the north is easy at official checkpoints in Nicosia, but EU roaming and car rental insurance usually stop at the line, so check before you cross.", "food": [{"name": "Halloumi", "wiki": "Halloumi", "desc": "Squeaky grilled cheese made from goat and sheep milk, served everywhere from breakfasts to sandwiches. Grab a block at the supermarket for picnics.", "price": "$5 to $9 as a starter", "when": "Breakfast, meze and sandwiches", "order": "Try it grilled with watermelon in summer"}, {"name": "Souvlaki", "wiki": "Souvlaki", "desc": "Grilled pork or chicken skewers stuffed into pita with salad and tahini. Cheap and filling at any souvlaki shop.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Lunch or late night, at grill shops", "order": "Ask for half pork, half sheftalia"}, {"name": "Sheftalia", "wiki": "Sheftalia", "desc": "Grilled sausages wrapped in caul fat, usually served in pita alongside souvlaki.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Dinner, at grill shops and meze"}, {"name": "Kleftiko", "wiki": "Kleftiko", "desc": "Lamb slow-baked in a sealed clay oven until it falls off the bone, a village taverna classic.", "price": "$12 to $18", "when": "Sunday lunch, at village tavernas", "order": "Order ahead, as it takes hours"}, {"name": "Loukoumades", "wiki": "Lokma", "desc": "Little fried dough balls soaked in honey syrup, sold hot from street stalls.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Evening, at festivals and stalls", "order": "Get them hot with cinnamon and honey"}, {"name": "Commandaria", "wiki": "Commandaria", "desc": "Sweet dessert wine from the Troodos foothills, said to be one of the oldest named wines in the world.", "price": "$3 to $6 a glass", "when": "After dinner, with dessert"}], "food_tip": "A meze in a village taverna is great value if you're hungry and in a group, as the plates just keep coming. Bakeries sell cheap halloumi pies and sandwiches for breakfast.", "culture": ["Cyprus is divided, and the Cyprus problem is a sensitive subject on both sides, so listen rather than offer opinions.", "Greek Cypriots are mostly Orthodox and Turkish Cypriots mostly Muslim; cover shoulders and knees in churches, monasteries and mosques.", "Cypriots are hospitable and family oriented, and being offered coffee or fruit by a stranger in a village is common.", "Place names are political here: the north is only recognised as a state by Turkey, so simply saying the north is the neutral choice in conversation.", "Afternoons can be slow, with some village shops closing for a siesta in summer, and dinner tends to start late, around 8 or 9pm."], "safety": "Cyprus is safe, with the main risks being the summer heat, sunburn and careless driving on mountain roads. Ayia Napa's party scene can get messy, so look after your drinks and your mates. Don't photograph military zones near the Green Line, which are clearly signed.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is around 1pm to 3pm, and dinner rarely before 8pm, especially in summer.", "Tap water is safe but often tastes of minerals, so many people drink bottled water.", "Vegetarians do well with salads, halloumi, bean dishes and vegetable meze.", "Both sides of the island have their own food, and the north uses the Turkish lira.", "Tipping 5 to 10 percent is normal in restaurants, unless service is already included."], "drinks": [{"name": "Zivania", "desc": "A strong, clear grape spirit, drunk ice cold with meze, about $2 to $4 a shot."}, {"name": "Frappé", "desc": "Iced instant coffee whipped to a foam, the summer drink at every cafe, about $3 to $4."}], "greet": "A handshake is standard, and friends kiss on both cheeks. Greek Cypriots say \"yia sou\" and Turkish Cypriots \"merhaba\".", "dress": "Beachwear is fine at the beach, but cover up in towns and villages. Cover shoulders and knees at churches, monasteries and mosques.", "table": "Meals are shared and long, and hosts keep bringing food, so pace yourself. Saying no to food is often taken as politeness, so expect a second offer.", "faith": "Greek Cypriots are mainly Greek Orthodox and Turkish Cypriots mainly Muslim. Remove shoes in mosques, and some monasteries don't allow women inside.", "phrases": [["Yia sou (Γεια σου)", "Hello"], ["Efcharistó (Ευχαριστώ)", "Thank you"], ["Póso kánei? (Πόσο κάνει;)", "How much?"], ["Merhaba", "Hello (Turkish)"], ["Teşekkürler", "Thank you (Turkish)"]]},
  "algeria": {"around": "Comfortable long-distance buses and SNTF trains link the northern cities, including Algiers, Oran and Constantine, and shared taxis leave when full for shorter routes. The Algiers metro and tram are cheap and easy. Air Algérie flies to the far south, including Djanet and Tamanrasset, which saves days of travel. In Algiers and other big cities, the local app Yassir works for rides.", "money": "The currency is the Algerian dinar, and Algeria is mostly a cash economy where foreign cards rarely work outside a few big hotels. Most travellers bring euros and change them; the official bank rate is much worse than the widely used informal market rate, so ask your hotel or agency what's normal and be careful about where you change. Haggling is light outside souks, and rounding up the bill or leaving a little extra is plenty for a tip.", "sleep": "Mid-range and simple hotels are the main option, as backpacker hostels are rare, though a few guesthouses have popped up in Algiers and the oases. In the desert around Djanet and the Tassili n'Ajjer, trips are run by local agencies and you sleep in tents or under the stars. Couchsurfing and local hospitality fill the gaps for some travellers.", "eat": ["Couscous: steamed semolina with vegetables and meat, served on Fridays", "Chorba frik: soup of cracked green wheat with lamb", "Mhadjeb: thin folded flatbread stuffed with spicy tomato and onion", "Garantita: baked chickpea flan sold as street food"], "data": "Mobilis, Djezzy and Ooredoo sell SIMs with your passport at official shops, data is cheap, and coverage is good in the north but thin in the Sahara.", "heads_up": "Trips into the deep south are usually arranged through a licensed local agency, which also sorts the invitation letter often needed for your visa, so start planning early.", "food": [{"name": "Couscous", "wiki": "Couscous", "desc": "Steamed semolina piled with vegetables and lamb or chicken, traditionally a Friday family lunch. Algerians consider it their national dish.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Friday lunch, with family", "order": "Try it with chicken or with fish on the coast"}, {"name": "Chorba frik", "wiki": "Chorba", "desc": "Soup made with cracked green wheat, lamb, tomato and herbs, especially popular during Ramadan.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Evenings, especially during Ramadan", "order": "Add lemon and a pinch of chili"}, {"name": "Mhadjeb", "wiki": "Mahdjouba", "desc": "Thin folded flatbread stuffed with spicy tomato and onion, sold hot by street vendors for very little.", "price": "$0.30 to $0.70", "when": "Snack, from street vendors"}, {"name": "Garantita", "wiki": "Karantika", "desc": "A baked chickpea flan sold in squares in a sandwich with harissa and cumin, Oran's signature street food.", "price": "$0.30 to $0.70", "when": "Snack, mostly in Oran", "order": "Order it in bread with harissa and cumin"}, {"name": "Chakhchoukha", "wiki": "Chakhchoukha", "desc": "Torn pieces of flatbread soaked in a spicy lamb and vegetable stew, a speciality from the east around Biskra and Constantine.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch in Biskra and Batna"}, {"name": "Makroudh", "wiki": "Makroudh", "desc": "Semolina pastries filled with dates, fried or baked and soaked in honey, usually served with mint tea.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50 each", "when": "With coffee or tea"}], "food_tip": "Street food like mhadjeb and garantita costs pocket change, and cafés serve cheap coffee and pastries. Lunch menus in small restaurants are filling and very cheap.", "culture": ["Algeria is a Muslim country, so dress modestly, covering shoulders and knees, and women may want a headscarf for mosques.", "Ramadan changes daily life, with restaurants closed by day and lively evenings after sunset; avoid eating or drinking in public during fasting hours.", "Algerians are famously welcoming, and accepting an invitation for tea or a meal is common and polite.", "Use your right hand for eating and handing things over.", "The independence war against France is a source of national pride, so be respectful when discussing history."], "safety": "Algeria is generally safe in the north, but police checkpoints are common, and in some areas police may want to escort foreigners. Areas near the borders with Libya, Mali and Niger are off-limits, so follow official advice. Petty theft is the main city concern.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch from 12pm to 2pm is the main meal, and many restaurants close early in the evening outside the big cities.", "Markets (souks) sell dates, olives and bread cheaply, and street vendors sell hot snacks all day.", "Drink bottled water, which is cheap and everywhere.", "Vegetarians can find salads, vegetable stews and chickpea dishes, but ask about meat stock.", "Tipping isn't expected in small places, but leaving 10% or rounding up in restaurants is appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Mint tea", "desc": "Green tea brewed with fresh mint and lots of sugar, poured from a height. A glass costs about $0.30 to $1."}, {"name": "Lben", "desc": "A sour fermented milk drink, often served with couscous or dates. Expect $0.50 to $1 a bottle."}], "greet": "Say salam alaykum or bonjour, and men shake hands with men, often touching their heart after. Friends of the same sex kiss on both cheeks.", "dress": "Cover shoulders and knees, and women should wear loose clothing, especially away from big cities and in the south. Women bring a scarf for mosques.", "table": "Eat with your right hand, and if sharing a dish, eat from the section in front of you. Accepting food and tea is polite.", "faith": "Almost all Algerians are Sunni Muslim. Mosques are often closed to non-Muslims, so ask first, dress modestly and remove shoes if you go in.", "phrases": [["Salam alaykum (السلام عليكم)", "Hello"], ["Saha (صحة)", "Thanks (Darija)"], ["Bechhal? (بشحال؟)", "How much?"], ["Labas? (لاباس؟)", "How are you?"], ["Azul (ⴰⵣⵓⵍ)", "Hello (in Tamazight, Berber)"]]},
  "libya": {"around": "Travel in Libya is only practical through a specialist tour operator, who arranges drivers, guides, permits and your visa. Domestic flights link Tripoli, Benghazi and Sabha, and roads between towns have frequent checkpoints. Independent travel isn't realistic at present.", "money": "The currency is the Libyan dinar, and Libya is a cash economy where foreign cards basically don't work. Operators usually advise bringing enough clean US dollars or euros for the whole trip, and there's a big gap between official and street exchange rates, so follow your operator's advice. Haggling is normal in markets, and tips for drivers and guides are appreciated.", "sleep": "Hotels are arranged by your operator, with decent options in Tripoli and Benghazi and limited choices elsewhere. Desert trips to places like the Akakus or Ghadames use simple guesthouses or camping.", "eat": ["Bazeen: barley dough dome served with a spicy lamb stew", "Shorba libiya: tomato soup with lamb, orzo and mint", "Couscous: steamed semolina with lamb and vegetables", "Asida: sweet wheat porridge eaten with date syrup and butter"], "data": "Local SIMs from Libyana and Al Madar need a passport and are best arranged through your operator; coverage is decent in cities and thin in the desert.", "heads_up": "Check your government's latest advice before planning anything, and follow your operator's guidance at checkpoints.", "food": [{"name": "Bazeen", "wiki": "Bazin (bread)", "desc": "A dome of barley dough served with a spicy lamb and tomato stew, eaten with your right hand from a shared plate.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Family lunch, especially Fridays", "order": "Pinch the dough with your right hand and dip"}, {"name": "Sharba libiya", "wiki": "Chorba", "desc": "Spicy tomato soup with lamb, orzo and mint, the dish that breaks the fast every night in Ramadan.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Ramadan iftar and winter lunches"}, {"name": "Couscous", "wiki": "Couscous", "desc": "Steamed semolina with lamb and vegetables, a staple for family meals.", "price": "$3 to $7", "when": "Friday lunch"}, {"name": "Usban", "wiki": "Usban", "desc": "A sausage made with rice, herbs and meat stuffed into tripe, served for festivals and special occasions.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Eid al-Adha and weddings"}, {"name": "Asida", "wiki": "Asida", "desc": "Sweet wheat porridge eaten with date syrup and butter, especially at Mawlid and when a baby is born.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Mawlid mornings and after a birth"}, {"name": "Libyan tea", "wiki": "Libyan tea", "desc": "Strong, sweet tea poured from a height to create a thick foam, often with mint or peanuts.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Evenings with friends, after meals", "order": "Later rounds often come with roasted peanuts"}], "food_tip": "Street sandwiches and cafés in Tripoli are cheap, and your operator will often include meals. Local restaurants serve big portions of grilled meat and rice for little money.", "culture": ["Libya is conservative and Muslim, so dress modestly; women should cover arms and legs, and a headscarf is useful.", "Alcohol is illegal, so don't bring any.", "Use your right hand for eating and greeting.", "Libyans are hospitable and welcoming, so expect plenty of tea invitations.", "Ask before photographing people, especially women."], "safety": "Most governments advise against all travel to Libya due to armed conflict, kidnapping risk and political instability, and normal travel insurance won't cover you. If you go, use a reputable specialist operator and follow their advice exactly, especially at checkpoints. Keep a low profile, avoid demonstrations and check official advice regularly.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch, around 2 to 4pm, is the main meal, and many shops and restaurants close in the afternoon.", "Drink bottled water only.", "Vegetarian options are limited, so ask for salads, vegetable couscous, bread and lentil dishes.", "During Ramadan most restaurants close by day and open for iftar at sunset.", "Tipping isn't expected, but rounding up and tipping guides and drivers is appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Laban", "desc": "Tangy fermented milk drunk with couscous and bazeen, under $1 a glass."}, {"name": "Qahwa", "desc": "Strong Arabic coffee spiced with cardamom, served in tiny cups for about 50 cents to $1 in cafés."}], "greet": "Men greet with salaam alaikum and a handshake, often followed by a hand on the heart. Men shouldn't offer a hand to a woman unless she offers first.", "dress": "Men should wear long trousers in towns, and women loose clothing with a headscarf in conservative areas and at mosques.", "table": "Meals are often shared from one big dish, so eat from the part closest to you. If offered tea, accept a few rounds rather than leaving after the first.", "faith": "Almost all Libyans are Sunni Muslim, mostly of the Maliki school. Ask before entering mosques, remove your shoes and avoid prayer times.", "phrases": [["Salaam alaikum (السلام عليكم)", "Hello"], ["Shukran (شكرا)", "Thank you"], ["Bikam? (بكم؟)", "How much?"], ["Bahi (باهي)", "Good, okay"], ["Shin halek? (شن حالك؟)", "How are you?"]]},
  "sudan": {"around": "Sudan has been in a civil war since April 2023, and normal travel is not possible. Khartoum airport has been heavily damaged, and transport across the country is badly disrupted. Before the war, buses linked Khartoum with the north, minibuses covered smaller routes, and travellers visited the pyramids of Meroë by road.", "money": "Before the war, Sudan ran almost entirely on cash, foreign cards did not work, and travellers brought US dollars or euros to change. The Sudanese pound has since collapsed in value and banking is badly disrupted, with many people relying on mobile banking apps like Bankak. Haggling was normal in markets, and Sudanese hospitality meant tips were appreciated but not expected.", "sleep": "Before the war, budget stays meant simple hotels and lodges in Khartoum and the north, with wild camping possible near Meroë and in the desert. Many places have since closed or been damaged.", "eat": ["Ful: stewed fava beans with oil and spices, the daily breakfast", "Kisra: thin fermented sorghum flatbread, eaten with stews", "Gurasa: thick wheat pancake served with stew", "Karkade: hibiscus drink served cold or hot"], "data": "Zain, MTN and Sudani were the main networks, and coverage and internet have been heavily disrupted by the conflict, with long blackouts in many areas.", "heads_up": "Most governments advise against all travel to Sudan, so treat this guide as background for when conditions change.", "food": [{"name": "Ful", "wiki": "Ful medames", "desc": "Stewed fava beans with oil, cumin and cheese, eaten with bread for breakfast across the country. Usually cost very little.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 (pre-war)", "when": "Breakfast, shared from one bowl", "order": "Ask for it with feta and oil"}, {"name": "Kisra", "wiki": "Kisra", "desc": "Thin fermented sorghum flatbread eaten with stews like mullah.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 (pre-war)", "when": "Lunch with stew"}, {"name": "Gurasa", "wiki": "Gurasa", "desc": "Thick wheat pancake served with stew or sugar.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 (pre-war)", "when": "Breakfast or lunch"}, {"name": "Karkade", "wiki": "Hibiscus tea", "desc": "Deep red hibiscus drink served cold in summer and hot in winter.", "price": "$0.30 to $0.50 (pre-war)", "when": "Any time, especially in summer"}, {"name": "Jebena coffee", "wiki": "Jebena", "desc": "Spiced coffee brewed in a clay jebena pot with ginger or cardamom, a social ritual.", "price": "$0.30 to $0.50 (pre-war)", "when": "Afternoons, from street tea ladies", "order": "Ask for it with ginger, zanjabil"}, {"name": "Asida", "wiki": "Asida", "desc": "A thick porridge made from sorghum or wheat, served with stew.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 (pre-war)", "when": "Breakfast or special occasions"}], "food_tip": "Before the war, street food like ful and tamiya (falafel) cost very little, and sharing a big bowl with strangers was common.", "culture": ["Sudanese hospitality is legendary, and before the war travellers were often invited into homes and fed generously.", "Sudan is mostly Muslim and conservative, so dress modestly and avoid alcohol, which is banned.", "Use your right hand for eating and greeting.", "Ask before photographing people, and a photo permit was required before the war.", "Be sensitive in conversations; nearly every Sudanese family has been affected by the conflict."], "safety": "Sudan is in active civil war with widespread violence, famine and displacement, and most governments advise against all travel. There is no realistic way to visit safely right now. If conditions change, check official advice and only travel with experienced local contacts.", "eat_beats": ["Sudan has been at war since April 2023, and most governments advise against all travel, so this describes life before the conflict.", "Lunch was the main meal, often eaten mid-afternoon, with a light dinner late in the evening.", "Tap water wasn't safe to drink, and bottled water was the norm.", "Vegetarian dishes like ful, lentils and okra stews were common and easy to find.", "Tipping wasn't expected, but small tips for good service were appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Aradeb", "desc": "A tangy tamarind juice served cold, especially during Ramadan. A glass cost about $0.30 before the war."}, {"name": "Laban", "desc": "Sweet tea with milk, often spiced with cardamom, cheap and drunk all day."}], "greet": "Greetings are long, with handshakes and questions about family. Men may not shake hands with women, so wait for them to offer.", "dress": "Both men and women dress conservatively, covering arms and legs. Women often wear a toub, a long wrap covering the body and hair.", "table": "Food is usually shared from a large tray, eaten with the right hand and bread. Try a bit of everything, as refusing can seem rude.", "faith": "Most Sudanese are Sunni Muslim, with Sufi orders important, plus Christian and traditional beliefs. Remove shoes and cover up at mosques and shrines.", "phrases": [["As-salaam alaykum (السلام عليكم)", "Peace be upon you, hello"], ["Shukran (شكرا)", "Thank you"], ["Bikam? (بكم؟)", "How much?"], ["Inshallah (إن شاء الله)", "God willing"], ["Tamam (تمام)", "Okay, fine"]]},
  "kenya": {"around": "The Madaraka Express train between Nairobi and Mombasa is cheap, comfortable and fun, but book ahead online, especially for the economy class. Matatus, the colourful shared minibuses, go nearly everywhere for very little, while buses like Modern Coast cover longer routes. Uber, Bolt and the local app Little work in Nairobi and Mombasa. Small planes to Lamu and the Maasai Mara save long road trips if your budget stretches.", "money": "The currency is the Kenyan shilling, and M-Pesa mobile money is how Kenya actually pays for things, from matatu fares to market stalls; get a Safaricom SIM and you can use it too. Cards work in malls, supermarkets and lodges, and ATMs are common in towns, though fees add up. Tip around 10 percent in restaurants and budget properly for safari guide and cook tips. Haggle at markets and with curio sellers, starting well below the first price.", "sleep": "Nairobi has several good hostels, especially in Westlands and Kilimani, and budget safaris in the Maasai Mara usually mean camping in tents outside the park. Diani, Watamu and Lamu have backpacker-friendly guesthouses and beach hostels, which fill up around Christmas and Easter.", "eat": ["Nyama choma: grilled goat or beef, shared by the kilo", "Ugali: firm maize porridge eaten with stews or greens", "Sukuma wiki: collard greens fried with onion and tomato", "Chai: milky spiced tea, served all day"], "data": "Safaricom has the best coverage, and its SIMs need passport registration at a shop, which also unlocks M-Pesa. Airtel is cheaper but patchier.", "heads_up": "Plastic carrier bags are banned in Kenya, and you can be fined, so pack reusable bags.", "food": [{"name": "Nyama choma", "wiki": "Nyama choma", "desc": "Grilled goat or beef, bought by the kilo and shared with kachumbari salad and ugali. A weekend ritual in roadside joints.", "price": "$5 to $10 per half kilo", "when": "Weekend afternoons at roadside grills", "order": "Order by weight, with kachumbari and ugali"}, {"name": "Ugali", "wiki": "Ugali", "desc": "Firm maize porridge you roll into balls with your fingers and use to scoop stews and greens. The cheapest filling meal in the country.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Lunch and dinner, with any stew", "order": "Roll a ball and use it to scoop"}, {"name": "Sukuma wiki", "wiki": "Sukuma wiki", "desc": "Collard greens fried with onion and tomato, the everyday side dish with ugali.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Daily side with ugali"}, {"name": "Chapati", "wiki": "Chapati", "desc": "Soft, flaky flatbread brought by the Indian community, sold everywhere and great with beans or stew.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50 each", "when": "Breakfast or with beans for lunch", "order": "Try it with ndengu, green gram stew"}, {"name": "Mandazi", "wiki": "Mandazi", "desc": "Slightly sweet fried dough triangles, eaten with chai for breakfast at roadside stalls for small change.", "price": "$0.10 to $0.30 each", "when": "Morning with chai", "order": "Coastal ones are made with coconut milk"}, {"name": "Chai", "wiki": "Masala chai", "desc": "Milky, sweet tea boiled with spices, drunk all day long across the country.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Breakfast and the afternoon break", "order": "Comes already sweet, so ask if you want less"}], "food_tip": "Local canteens called hotelis serve ugali, beans, stew and chapati for a couple of dollars. Supermarkets like Naivas and Carrefour are good for self-catering.", "culture": ["Greetings matter, so say jambo or habari before asking for directions or buying anything.", "Dress modestly away from the beach, especially in Lamu and on the Muslim coast.", "Ask before photographing people, especially Maasai, who may ask for payment.", "Kenyans love football, especially the English Premier League, which is a great conversation starter.", "Pole pole, meaning slowly slowly, sums up the pace of life, so be patient with delays."], "safety": "Kenya is generally safe for travellers, but Nairobi has a reputation for petty crime and phone snatching, so use Uber at night and keep your phone out of sight on the street. Malaria is present in many areas, so take prophylaxis and use repellent. Book safaris with registered operators, as cheap fake deals are common.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is usually chai and mandazi or chapati, lunch is around 1pm and dinner around 8pm.", "Don't drink tap water, and carry a filter bottle or buy big refill bottles.", "Vegetarians can eat well on beans, githeri (maize and beans), sukuma and chapati.", "On the coast, Swahili street food like viazi karai (spiced fried potatoes) and grilled cassava is sold from carts in the evening.", "Tipping around 10% in sit-down restaurants is appreciated, while small cafés don't expect it."], "drinks": [{"name": "Dawa", "desc": "The \"medicine\" cocktail of vodka, lime, honey and crushed ice, stirred with a honey stick. About $4 to $6 in a bar."}, {"name": "Madafu", "desc": "Young green coconuts chopped open on the coast, so you can drink the water then scoop out the soft flesh. About $0.50 to $1."}], "greet": "Shake hands and ask how the person is before anything else, often in Swahili with \"Habari yako?\". Elders may be greeted with \"Shikamoo\", which is a sign of respect.", "dress": "Casual clothes are fine in Nairobi, but on the Swahili coast cover shoulders and knees away from the beach. Neutral colours are best on safari.", "table": "Wash your hands before eating, as many meals are eaten by hand, and use your right hand only. Guests are often served first and encouraged to eat more.", "faith": "Most Kenyans are Christian, with a large Muslim community, especially on the coast. Ask before entering a mosque, dress modestly and remove shoes.", "phrases": [["Jambo", "Hello"], ["Asante sana", "Thank you very much"], ["Bei gani?", "How much?"], ["Sitaki pilipili", "I don't want chilli"], ["Hakuna shida", "No problem"]]},
  "tanzania": {"around": "Long-distance buses link most towns cheaply, and it's worth booking a day ahead with a well-reviewed company and avoiding the cheapest operators, as road safety varies a lot. Dala-dalas, the packed shared minibuses, cover short hops for almost nothing, and bajajis (tuk-tuks) and boda bodas fill the gaps. Uber and Bolt work in Dar es Salaam and Arusha. Fast ferries run several times a day between Dar es Salaam and Zanzibar, and buying tickets at the official port office avoids touts.", "money": "The currency is the Tanzanian shilling, but US dollars are widely used for safaris, Kilimanjaro climbs and tours, and older or tatty notes (anything printed before 2013, or with tears) often get refused, so bring crisp ones. National park fees are generally paid by card, while shillings in cash cover buses, street food and guesthouses; ATMs are easy to find in cities and on Zanzibar but scarce in the bush. Tipping safari guides, cooks and mountain crews is expected and adds up, so budget for it. Bargain at markets and with souvenir sellers, but keep it good humoured.", "sleep": "Arusha and Moshi have hostels and cheap guesthouses, handy as bases for safaris and Kilimanjaro. Budget safaris use public campsites inside or just outside the parks, which is how you keep a Serengeti trip affordable. On Zanzibar, Stone Town, Paje and Nungwi have backpacker guesthouses and hostels that fill up in the July to September and December to February high seasons.", "eat": ["Chipsi mayai: french fry omelet, sold at street stalls", "Mishkaki: grilled skewers of marinated beef or goat", "Urojo: tangy Zanzibar soup with fritters and potatoes", "Zanzibar pizza: stuffed and griddled dough from Forodhani Gardens"], "data": "Vodacom and Airtel SIMs are easy to get but need passport and fingerprint registration at an official shop, and coverage is good in towns and on Zanzibar. Expect little or no signal deep in the parks.", "heads_up": "Zanzibar is largely Muslim, so cover shoulders and knees away from the beach, especially in Stone Town.", "food": [{"name": "Chipsi mayai", "wiki": "Chipsi mayai", "desc": "Chips cooked into an omelette and served with a squirt of chilli sauce and kachumbari, sold at street stalls for a dollar or two. Tanzania's favourite cheap fill-up.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Evening street food in towns", "order": "Add kachumbari salad and chilli sauce"}, {"name": "Ugali", "wiki": "Ugali", "desc": "Stiff maize porridge rolled into balls by hand and used to scoop beans, greens or stew. The base of most local meals and very cheap.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch and dinner, with stew or greens", "order": "Eat it with mchicha (spinach) or fried fish"}, {"name": "Pilau", "wiki": "Pilaf", "desc": "Rice cooked with meat and warm spices like cardamom, cloves and cinnamon, a Swahili coast favourite served at weddings and local canteens.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Celebrations, and Zanzibar lunches", "order": "Ask for it with beef and kachumbari"}, {"name": "Nyama choma", "wiki": "Nyama choma", "desc": "Grilled goat or beef bought by weight and shared with friends over a cold Kilimanjaro or Safari lager.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Weekend afternoons with beer", "order": "Goat (mbuzi) is the classic, priced by weight"}, {"name": "Mandazi", "wiki": "Mandazi", "desc": "Lightly sweet fried dough, sometimes flavoured with coconut and cardamom, eaten with chai for breakfast at roadside stalls.", "price": "$0.10 to $0.30", "when": "Breakfast with chai"}, {"name": "Mtori", "wiki": "Mtori", "desc": "A smooth soup of green bananas and beef from the Kilimanjaro region, said to be good for recovering from a big night.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Breakfast around Kilimanjaro and Arusha"}], "food_tip": "Mama lishe stalls and local canteens serve rice, beans, ugali and stew for a couple of dollars. Forodhani Gardens night market in Stone Town is fun but touristy, so agree prices before you order.", "culture": ["Greetings come before business, so start with mambo (casual) or a respectful shikamoo for elders before asking anything.", "Zanzibar and the coast are mostly Muslim and fairly conservative, so cover shoulders and knees away from the beach and be extra respectful during Ramadan.", "Use your right hand for eating, shaking hands and passing money, as the left is seen as unclean.", "Ask before photographing people, including Maasai, and never photograph police, military sites or government buildings.", "Public displays of affection are frowned upon, and same-sex relationships are illegal, so be discreet."], "safety": "Tanzania is generally friendly and safe, but in Arusha and Moshi watch out for touts (flycatchers) selling cheap fake safaris and climbs, and book only with registered operators with solid reviews. Malaria is a real risk, so talk to a doctor about prophylaxis and use repellent. Avoid walking alone on beaches or in Dar es Salaam after dark, and take a taxi instead.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is around 1pm and dinner after 8pm, and many local places run out of food by mid evening.", "Don't drink tap water; bottled water is cheap and sold everywhere.", "Vegetarians can live on beans (maharage), rice, chapati and greens, which are standard at local stalls.", "Tipping isn't expected at local spots, but 5 to 10 percent is appreciated in tourist restaurants and safari lodges.", "Wash your hands before eating; many restaurants bring a jug and basin to the table."], "drinks": [{"name": "Chai", "desc": "Sweet, spiced milky tea drunk at breakfast and through the day. A cup costs $0.20 to $0.50."}, {"name": "Konyagi", "desc": "A local gin-like spirit, usually mixed with soda or tonic. A small bottle costs $2 to $4."}], "greet": "Greetings take time: ask 'Habari?' and expect 'Nzuri', then ask about family before anything else. Handshakes can be soft and long, sometimes held while you talk.", "dress": "Light, loose clothes suit the heat, but cover shoulders and knees in towns and on Zanzibar; a kanga makes a handy wrap. Wear neutral colours on safari and bring warm layers for Kilimanjaro and Ngorongoro.", "table": "Meals are often eaten with the right hand, rolling ugali into a ball to scoop up stew. Wait for elders to start, and avoid eating while walking in conservative areas.", "faith": "Tanzania is roughly split between Christians and Muslims, Zanzibar is almost entirely Muslim, and traditional beliefs carry on alongside both. Cover up and remove shoes at mosques, and ask before entering or taking photos.", "phrases": [["Jambo / Habari", "Hello"], ["Asante", "Thank you"], ["Bei gani?", "How much?"], ["Pole pole", "Slowly"], ["Hakuna shida", "No problem"]]},
  "uganda": {"around": "Big buses and minibus taxis connect towns cheaply, though matatu-style minibuses get crammed and the driving is wild. Boda bodas, motorbike taxis, handle short hops, and in Kampala SafeBoda, Uber and Bolt give you fixed prices and a helmet. Many travellers rent a 4x4 with a driver for the parks, which is easier to afford in a group. Gorilla permits for Bwindi and Mgahinga cost a lot and sell out, so book well ahead through the Uganda Wildlife Authority or a trusted agent.", "money": "The currency is the Ugandan shilling, and cash is what you'll use for almost everything outside Kampala's malls and the pricier lodges. ATMs are found in towns, but carry enough cash before heading to national parks, where they're rare. Mobile money (MTN MoMo and Airtel Money) is everywhere and handy once you have a local SIM. Haggle with boda drivers and at markets, and tip guides, porters and trackers on treks.", "sleep": "Kampala, Jinja and Fort Portal have hostels with dorms and camping, and Jinja in particular is a backpacker hub thanks to rafting on the Nile. Many parks have campsites or simple bandas (huts) for the budget crowd, and Lake Bunyonyi has some of the most relaxed cheap stays in East Africa. Cheap rooms near Bwindi are limited, so book ahead.", "eat": ["Rolex: omelet rolled in chapati, a street food favorite", "Matoke: steamed green plantain, often mashed with sauce", "Luwombo: meat or groundnut stew steamed in banana leaves", "Groundnut sauce: rich peanut sauce served with matoke or rice"], "data": "MTN and Airtel SIMs need passport registration at an official shop, and coverage is good across most of the country, though patchy in remote parks.", "heads_up": "Insist on a helmet when riding a boda boda, as road crashes are common.", "food": [{"name": "Rolex", "wiki": "Rolex (food)", "desc": "A chapati rolled around an omelette with cabbage and tomato, Uganda's beloved street snack. Costs about a dollar from roadside stalls.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Any time, from roadside stalls", "order": "Ask for extra tomato, cabbage and onions"}, {"name": "Matoke", "wiki": "Matoke", "desc": "Steamed and mashed green bananas, often wrapped in banana leaves and served with groundnut sauce or stew. The national staple.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Lunch and dinner, with sauce", "order": "Pair it with groundnut sauce"}, {"name": "Luwombo", "wiki": "Luwombo", "desc": "Chicken, beef or groundnut stew steamed in banana leaves, a Buganda dish served on special occasions.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Celebrations and Sunday lunch", "order": "Chicken luwombo is the most common"}, {"name": "Groundnut sauce", "wiki": "Peanut stew", "desc": "Rich, creamy peanut sauce eaten with matoke, rice or sweet potatoes.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Lunch, poured over matoke or rice"}, {"name": "Kikomando", "wiki": "Kikomando", "desc": "Chopped chapati mixed with beans, a cheap student and workers' meal.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Cheap lunch for students and workers", "order": "Ask for beans and chapati, chopped together"}, {"name": "Nsenene", "wiki": "Nsenene", "desc": "Fried grasshoppers sold seasonally, especially around November and May, as a crunchy snack.", "price": "$1 to $2 a cup", "when": "Rainy season, around November and April", "order": "Fried with onions and a little chilli"}], "food_tip": "Local restaurants serve heaped plates of matoke, rice, beans and stew for a couple of dollars, and rolex stalls are on every street. Fruit is cheap and amazing, especially pineapples and jackfruit.", "culture": ["Greetings are important and can be long, so take time to say hello and ask how someone is before getting to the point.", "Uganda is quite religious and conservative, so dress modestly, especially away from Kampala and tourist spots.", "Same-sex relations are criminalised under a harsh law passed in 2023, so LGBTQ+ travellers should be very discreet and read up before going.", "Ask before photographing people, and avoid photographing government buildings, police or soldiers.", "Ugandans are known as some of the friendliest people in Africa, so expect lots of chats and invitations."], "safety": "Uganda is generally safe for travellers. Road crashes are the biggest risk, especially on boda bodas, so insist on a helmet and avoid long road journeys at night. Malaria is widespread, so take prophylaxis and use repellent. Watch for pickpockets in busy parts of Kampala, and check advice on areas near the borders with South Sudan and Congo.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the biggest meal, often from 1pm to 3pm, and portions are generous.", "Don't drink tap water, so bring a filter bottle or buy big bottles.", "Vegetarians do well with beans, matoke, posho, greens and groundnut sauce.", "Local restaurants often have a set menu of starches, and you choose the sauce or meat to go with it.", "Tipping isn't expected at local places, but 5% to 10% in tourist restaurants is appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Waragi", "desc": "A local gin distilled from bananas, cassava or millet, usually mixed with tonic or soda. About $1 to $2 a shot in a bar."}, {"name": "Tonto", "desc": "A traditional banana beer brewed in villages, especially in Buganda. About $0.50 a cup."}], "greet": "Greetings are long, with a handshake and questions about health and family. Some Ugandans kneel to greet elders, and you'll be greeted with \"you are welcome\" everywhere.", "dress": "Women should cover their knees, and men wear long trousers in towns. Shorts and vests are fine on safari and at lodges.", "table": "Wash your hands before meals and eat with your right hand. Finish what you're served, as wasting food is frowned upon.", "faith": "Most Ugandans are Christian, with a significant Muslim minority. Dress modestly at churches and mosques, and the Uganda Martyrs' shrine at Namugongo draws huge crowds every 3 June.", "phrases": [["Oli otya?", "How are you? in Luganda"], ["Webale", "Thank you"], ["Sente mmeka?", "How much?"], ["Mpola mpola", "Slowly slowly"], ["Kale", "Okay"]]},
  "rwanda": {"around": "Buses leave from Kigali's Nyabugogo station for all major towns, and you can buy tickets on the spot. In Kigali, moto taxis are everywhere, come with helmets, and are cheap; public buses use a tap card. Roads are good and the country is small, but the drive to Nyungwe is long and winding. Gorilla permits for Volcanoes National Park are expensive and must be booked well ahead.", "money": "The currency is the Rwandan franc. Cards and mobile money (MTN MoMo) work widely in Kigali, while smaller towns need cash, and ATMs are found in towns. Prices are mostly fixed and haggling is light, though it's normal at markets and with moto drivers. Tipping 10 percent in restaurants and tipping guides and trackers is appreciated.", "sleep": "Kigali has several hostels and guesthouses, and towns on Lake Kivu like Gisenyi and Kibuye have simple lakeside lodges and camping. Musanze, near Volcanoes National Park, has a few budget guesthouses. Cheap options near Nyungwe are few.", "eat": ["Brochettes: grilled goat or beef skewers, often with fries", "Isombe: mashed cassava leaves cooked with peanut paste", "Ibihaza: pumpkin cooked with beans", "Akabenz: fried or grilled pork, a popular bar dish"], "data": "MTN and Airtel SIMs need your passport, and coverage is strong across most of this small country.", "heads_up": "On the last Saturday morning of each month, Umuganda community work pauses most traffic and businesses for a few hours.", "food": [{"name": "Brochettes", "wiki": "Skewer", "desc": "Grilled goat, beef or fish skewers, often served with fries or grilled plantain. Every bar sells them cheaply.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Evenings at bars, with beer", "order": "Goat is the classic; add grilled plantain"}, {"name": "Isombe", "wiki": "Isombe", "desc": "Pounded cassava leaves slow-cooked with peanut paste, onion and sometimes aubergine, served with rice or plantain at local buffets.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch buffets, with rice"}, {"name": "Ibitoke", "wiki": "Matoke", "desc": "Green cooking bananas stewed with tomato, onion and sometimes beef, a daily staple in homes and canteens.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "A side at most meals"}, {"name": "Ubugari", "wiki": "Ugali", "desc": "Stiff cassava or maize porridge eaten with your hands alongside isombe or bean stew. Filling and very cheap.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Lunch and dinner, with sauce"}, {"name": "Sambaza", "wiki": "Sambaza", "desc": "Tiny fried fish from Lake Kivu, eaten by the plateful as a snack with a cold Primus or Mützig beer in Gisenyi and Kibuye.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "By Lake Kivu, as a snack", "order": "Squeeze lime and add akabanga chilli oil"}, {"name": "Urwagwa", "wiki": "Banana beer", "desc": "Home-brewed banana beer, often shared at weddings and village celebrations. Sweeter and stronger than you'd expect.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Village celebrations and weddings"}], "food_tip": "Local buffets in Kigali serve plates of rice, beans, plantain and stew for a couple of dollars, and you pay a fixed price for one plate.", "culture": ["The 1994 genocide against the Tutsi still shapes the country; the Kigali Genocide Memorial is a moving and essential visit, but never ask people about their ethnicity, which is a taboo subject.", "April is the national mourning period (Kwibuka), when the mood is sombre and loud parties and celebrations are inappropriate.", "Rwanda takes cleanliness and order seriously: plastic bags are banned, littering is frowned on, and you may have your bags checked at the border.", "Greetings matter, so take a moment for a handshake and a muraho (hello) before getting down to business.", "Dress modestly in rural areas and churches, and ask before photographing people."], "safety": "Rwanda is one of the safest and most orderly countries in Africa, with low crime and well-kept roads, and Kigali is fine to walk around even in the evening. Malaria is present outside the higher, cooler areas, so take precautions. Check current advice before going near the border with the Democratic Republic of Congo around Gisenyi and Rusizi, where tensions flare from time to time.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch buffets run from about noon to 2pm, and dinner starts around 7pm.", "Don't drink tap water; bottled water is cheap.", "Vegetarians do well, as beans, isombe, plantain and rice are at every buffet.", "Tipping isn't expected at local places, but 5 to 10 percent is appreciated in restaurants.", "Markets sell fresh fruit cheaply; try tree tomatoes and passion fruit."], "drinks": [{"name": "Rwandan coffee", "desc": "Grown on the country's hillsides and served fresh in Kigali cafés for $1 to $3 a cup."}, {"name": "Ikivuguto", "desc": "Thick fermented milk, a traditional drink sold for under $1 a glass."}], "greet": "Say 'Muraho' with a handshake, often holding your right forearm with your left hand. Friends hug, touching shoulders a few times.", "dress": "Rwandans dress neatly, and shorts or revealing outfits stand out away from hotels. Bring warm layers for Volcanoes and Nyungwe, which are cool and wet.", "table": "Don't eat or drink while walking in town; sit down instead. Hosts often serve guests first, and it's polite to try everything.", "faith": "Most Rwandans are Christian, mainly Catholic and Protestant, with a small Muslim minority. At genocide memorials, dress modestly, keep silent and follow the photo rules.", "phrases": [["Muraho", "Hello"], ["Murakoze", "Thank you"], ["Ni angahe?", "How much?"], ["Amakuru?", "How are you?"], ["Mwaramutse", "Good morning"]]},
  "south-africa": {"around": "The Baz Bus is a hop-on hop-off minibus built for backpackers that drops you at hostel doors along the Garden Route and up to Durban. Intercape and other long-distance coaches link the major cities cheaply, and domestic flights on FlySafair and Airlink are often cheap enough to beat a 15-hour bus ride. Uber and Bolt work in all large cities and are the safest choice at night. For the Drakensberg, Kruger and the Wild Coast, renting a car is far easier, and they drive on the left.", "money": "The currency is the rand, and cards and contactless work almost everywhere, from supermarkets to hostels and most market stalls. ATMs are everywhere too, but use ones inside malls, banks or shops rather than on the street, and never accept help from a stranger at the machine. Tip 10 to 15 percent in restaurants, a few rand to petrol attendants and car guards, and something for safari guides. Haggling is fine at craft markets but not in shops.", "sleep": "South Africa has some of the best hostels on the continent, especially in Cape Town, along the Garden Route in places like Knysna and Plettenberg Bay, and on the Wild Coast. SANParks campsites and huts in Kruger and other parks are cheap but book out early, especially during South African school holidays. Many hostels offer camping too.", "eat": ["Bunny chow: hollowed-out bread loaf filled with curry, from Durban", "Bobotie: spiced minced meat baked with an egg topping", "Biltong: air-dried spiced meat, sold everywhere as a snack", "Braai: South African barbecue, usually with boerewors sausage"], "data": "Vodacom and MTN SIMs need RICA registration with your passport and an address, and coverage is good in cities and along main roads. Data is fairly pricey, so shop around for bundles.", "heads_up": "Avoid walking alone after dark in cities and keep phones out of sight, and use ride-hailing for evening trips.", "food": [{"name": "Bunny chow", "wiki": "Bunny chow", "desc": "A hollowed-out loaf of white bread filled with curry, Durban's iconic street food. A quarter bunny is a cheap and very filling lunch.", "price": "$3 to $6 for a quarter", "when": "Lunch in Durban", "order": "Ask for mutton or bean, mild or hot"}, {"name": "Bobotie", "wiki": "Bobotie", "desc": "Spiced minced meat baked with an egg custard topping, served with yellow rice and chutney. A Cape Malay classic.", "price": "$8 to $14", "when": "Family lunch and Cape Malay eateries"}, {"name": "Biltong", "wiki": "Biltong", "desc": "Air-dried spiced meat sold everywhere as a snack, from beef to kudu. Perfect for road trips.", "price": "$2 to $5 a bag", "when": "Road trips and sports matches", "order": "Ask for wet or dry, fatty or lean"}, {"name": "Boerewors", "wiki": "Boerewors", "desc": "A thick, coiled spiced sausage cooked on the braai, the South African barbecue that's practically a national religion.", "price": "$3 to $6 in a roll", "when": "Weekend braais and markets", "order": "Order a boerie roll with tomato relish"}, {"name": "Vetkoek", "wiki": "Vetkoek", "desc": "Deep-fried dough balls stuffed with mince or jam, cheap and filling street food.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch from street vendors", "order": "Savoury mince is the classic"}, {"name": "Koeksister", "wiki": "Koeksister", "desc": "Plaited fried dough soaked in cold syrup, crunchy and very sweet.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Afternoon tea or Sunday treat", "order": "Cape Malay koesisters are spiced and coconut-rolled"}], "food_tip": "Supermarkets like Pick n Pay, Checkers and Shoprite have great value braai packs and ready meals, and most hostels have braai areas. Cape Town's Gatsby, a massive sandwich loaded with chips and meat, feeds two or three people for little money.", "culture": ["Braais (barbecues) are a big social event, and being invited to one is a great way to meet locals; bring your own meat and drinks.", "South Africa has 12 official languages, and a few greetings like sawubona (Zulu) or howzit go a long way.", "The legacy of apartheid still shapes society, so be thoughtful when discussing race and politics.", "Ubuntu, the idea that we are who we are through others, sums up the warmth of many communities.", "Robots means traffic lights, and now now means some time soon, so learn the local slang."], "safety": "South Africa has high crime rates, but most travellers have trouble-free trips by being sensible: avoid walking alone after dark, keep phones out of sight on the street, use Uber at night and don't flash valuables. Be careful at ATMs and on quiet hiking trails like Table Mountain, where muggings occasionally happen. Ask your hostel which areas to avoid.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is from about noon and dinner from 6:30 to 8, with braais running late into the evening.", "Tap water is safe in the big cities, but Cape Town has had droughts, so use it carefully.", "Vegetarian food is easy in cities, and Indian and Cape Malay places have lots of veggie curries.", "Tip 10 to 15 percent in restaurants, and a few rand to car guards and petrol attendants who help you.", "Shisa nyama spots in townships let you pick your raw meat at a butcher counter, then they grill it for you."], "drinks": [{"name": "Rooibos tea", "desc": "Caffeine-free red bush tea from the Cederberg, drunk with milk or plain. A cup costs about $1 to $2."}, {"name": "Amarula", "desc": "Creamy liqueur made from the fruit of the marula tree, often over ice. A shot in a bar is $2 to $4."}], "greet": "A handshake and a howzit work almost everywhere. Take time to ask how someone is before getting to your question.", "dress": "Casual dress is normal and beachwear stays on the beach. Pack warm layers, as nights get cold even in summer inland.", "table": "At a braai, the person running the grill is in charge, so don't touch the meat without asking. Bring something to share when invited.", "faith": "Most people are Christian, often mixed with traditional beliefs, with Muslim, Hindu and Jewish communities too. Remove shoes in mosques and temples and cover up.", "phrases": [["Howzit", "Hello, how are you"], ["Sawubona", "Hello (Zulu)"], ["Lekker", "Great, nice (Afrikaans)"], ["Ngiyabonga", "Thank you (Zulu)"], ["Just now", "Sometime later, not right now"]]},
  "namibia": {"around": "Public transport is thin on the ground, so most visitors rent a car, often a 4x4 with a rooftop tent, and splitting it between three or four people makes it surprisingly affordable. Intercape coaches and shared minibus shuttles link Windhoek with Swakopmund, Walvis Bay and other bigger towns, but won't get you to the dunes or Etosha. Book rental cars and park campsites months ahead for the July to October dry season. If you'd rather not drive, budget camping tours out of Windhoek are a good alternative.", "money": "The Namibian dollar is pegged one to one with the South African rand, and rand notes are accepted everywhere in Namibia, but Namibian dollars aren't accepted in South Africa, so spend or swap them before you cross. Cards work in towns, supermarkets and most lodges, and ATMs are easy to find in towns but rare out in the bush, so carry cash for remote fuel stops and community campsites. Tip around 10 percent in restaurants, a few dollars to petrol attendants who wash your windscreen, and something for guides. Haggling is only really done at craft markets.", "sleep": "Camping is the main budget option, and Namibian campsites are some of the best in Africa, usually with clean ablutions, water and a braai pit at every pitch. Windhoek and Swakopmund have good backpacker hostels with dorms and camping. Camps near Sossusvlei and inside Etosha fill up early, so book as soon as your dates are fixed.", "eat": ["Kapana: grilled street beef strips, eaten with spicy salt", "Oshifima: stiff millet porridge from the north", "Potjiekos: slow-cooked stew made in a three-legged pot", "Game meat: oryx or kudu steak, common on menus"], "data": "MTC SIMs have the widest coverage and need your passport to register, while Paratus is an alternative in towns. Huge stretches of desert have no signal at all, so download offline maps and tell someone your route.", "heads_up": "Gravel roads cause many rollovers, so drive slowly and avoid driving after dark, when wildlife wanders onto the roads.", "food": [{"name": "Kapana", "wiki": "Kapana", "desc": "Strips of beef grilled over open fires at markets like Single Quarters in Windhoek's Katutura, dipped in a spicy salt mix and eaten with your hands. One of the cheapest and tastiest meals in the country.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch or evening, at markets", "order": "Dip in the spicy salt and add raw tomato salsa"}, {"name": "Oshifima", "wiki": "Ugali", "desc": "Stiff millet porridge from the north, rolled into balls and eaten with stew, spinach or dried fish.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch or dinner in the north", "order": "Eat with your hands, rolling it into balls"}, {"name": "Potjiekos", "wiki": "Potjiekos", "desc": "Meat and vegetables slow-cooked for hours in a three-legged cast-iron pot over coals, perfect for a campsite evening.", "price": "$6 to $12", "when": "Evening at camp, after a long drive"}, {"name": "Biltong", "wiki": "Biltong", "desc": "Air-dried spiced meat, including game like kudu and oryx, sold at butcheries and petrol stations. Ideal road trip fuel.", "price": "$3 to $6 per 100g", "when": "On road trips, from butcheries", "order": "Try kudu or oryx, and ask for droëwors too"}, {"name": "Vetkoek", "wiki": "Vetkoek", "desc": "Golden fried dough balls stuffed with mince or eaten with jam, sold cheaply in towns.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Breakfast or snack, in towns", "order": "Filled with curried mince is the classic"}, {"name": "Apfelstrudel", "wiki": "Apple strudel", "desc": "A leftover of German colonial history, bakeries and cafés in Windhoek and Swakopmund serve strudel, Black Forest cake and proper coffee.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Coffee break, in Swakopmund"}], "food_tip": "Stock up at Spar, Shoprite, Woermann Brock or Pick n Pay in towns before heading into the desert, as shops are few and far between. Supermarket braai packs and a cold Windhoek Lager around the fire are the classic cheap night in.", "culture": ["Take time to greet people properly before asking for directions or help, as diving straight in can seem rude.", "Always ask before photographing people, especially Himba and Herero communities, and visit villages through community-run tours rather than just turning up.", "Namibia is fairly conservative outside the tourist hubs, so dress modestly in towns and villages.", "Stay on established tracks, since tyre marks in the desert can last for decades and damage fragile lichen fields.", "The country's colonial history, including the genocide of the Herero and Nama peoples under German rule, is a sensitive subject worth reading up on."], "safety": "Namibia is one of the safest countries in Africa for travellers, though Windhoek has petty crime, so never leave valuables visible in your car and use registered taxis at night. The biggest danger is the roads: gravel causes many rollovers, so keep your speed down and don't drive after dark, when animals wander onto the road. Always carry extra water and fuel, and check entry rules before you go, as visa requirements for many nationalities changed in 2025.", "eat_beats": ["Namibians eat lunch around noon to 2pm, and dinner around 7pm, with a braai often lasting late.", "Tap water is safe in towns like Windhoek and Swakopmund, but bring plenty of bottled water for the desert.", "Vegetarian food is limited outside towns, as meat dominates, so self-cater or check menus ahead.", "Supermarkets in towns are well stocked, but shop alcohol sales are restricted on weekends, so stock up by Saturday morning.", "Tip about 10 percent in restaurants, and a small tip is normal for petrol station attendants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Windhoek Lager", "desc": "The national beer, brewed to German purity law, about $1.50 to $2.50 a bottle."}, {"name": "Rooibos tea", "desc": "Red bush tea, naturally caffeine free, served hot or iced for $1 to $2."}], "greet": "Handshakes are common and often involve a three-step grip. Greet with \"hello, how are you?\" before getting to business.", "dress": "Light, breathable clothes suit the heat, and warm layers for cold desert nights. Cover up in towns and villages.", "table": "Meals are often shared, and it's polite to wait for the host or eldest to start. At a braai, the host controls the fire, so don't take over.", "faith": "About 90 percent of Namibians are Christian, mainly Lutheran, often blended with traditional beliefs. Some sites are sacred to local communities, so follow guidance from guides.", "phrases": [["Hallo", "Hello (common)"], ["Dankie", "Thank you (Afrikaans)"], ["Wa lalapo", "Good morning (Oshiwambo)"], ["Lekker", "Great, nice (Afrikaans slang)"], ["Eish", "Expression of surprise"]]},
  "botswana": {"around": "Buses and minibuses link the main towns, including Gaborone, Maun, Nata and Kasane, cheaply, but they don't reach the parks. Most budget visitors join a mokoro (dugout canoe) trip into the Okavango Delta from Maun and a boat cruise or game drive in Chobe from Kasane, often shared with other travellers to cut costs. Self-drive 4x4 trips through Moremi or the Central Kalahari need serious planning, recovery gear and campsite bookings well ahead. Scenic flights over the Delta from Maun are pricey but a once-in-a-lifetime splurge.", "money": "The currency is the pula, and cards work in Maun, Kasane and Gaborone supermarkets and lodges, with ATMs in towns, but carry cash for small shops, minibuses, tips and remote campsites. Some lodges and safari operators price in US dollars. Tipping safari guides and mokoro polers is expected, so budget for it. Haggling isn't really done outside craft markets.", "sleep": "Camping is the cheapest way to stay in an expensive country, and lodges in Maun and Kasane often have campsites, dorms or simple rooms alongside their pricier chalets. Campsites inside national parks are limited, privately run in many cases, and book out months ahead. Botswana's tourism model is deliberately high cost, so this is a place where camping really earns its keep.", "eat": ["Seswaa: pounded slow-cooked beef, often served at celebrations", "Bogobe: stiff sorghum or maize porridge", "Magwinya: fried dough balls, a common breakfast", "Morogo: wild spinach cooked with onion and tomato"], "data": "Mascom, Orange and BTC SIMs need passport registration, and coverage is fine in towns and along main roads but very limited or absent in the Delta and the parks.", "heads_up": "Many camps are unfenced and wildlife walks through at night, so stay near your tent after dark and listen to staff.", "food": [{"name": "Seswaa", "wiki": "Seswaa", "desc": "Beef or goat boiled for hours with salt, then pounded until it shreds. The national dish, served at weddings and celebrations with pap.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Weddings, funerals and celebrations", "order": "Eat it with bogobe and morogo"}, {"name": "Bogobe", "wiki": "Ugali", "desc": "Stiff sorghum or maize porridge, the everyday base of most local meals, eaten with seswaa, stew or greens.", "price": "$1 to $2 as a side", "when": "Lunch and dinner, every day"}, {"name": "Magwinya", "wiki": "Vetkoek", "desc": "Golden fried dough balls, known as fat cakes, sold at roadside stalls for breakfast for a few pula.", "price": "$0.10 to $0.30 each", "when": "Breakfast, from roadside stalls", "order": "Eat them warm, with tea"}, {"name": "Morogo", "wiki": "Morogo", "desc": "Wild spinach cooked with onion and tomato, a common side with bogobe.", "price": "$1 to $3 as a side", "when": "Lunch and dinner"}, {"name": "Phane", "wiki": "Gonimbrasia belina", "desc": "Mopane worms, actually caterpillars, dried or fried and eaten as a crunchy, protein-rich snack. Give it a go if you see them at a market.", "price": "$1 to $3 a cup", "when": "Snack, especially after the rains", "order": "Try them fried with tomato and onion"}, {"name": "Dikgobe", "wiki": "Dikgobe", "desc": "A filling mix of beans and samp or sorghum, often served at family gatherings.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch, at family gatherings"}], "food_tip": "Supermarkets like Choppies, Spar and Shoprite in Maun and Kasane are the place to stock up before a trip into the bush, and local cafés serve big plates of pap and stew for very little.", "culture": ["Greetings come first, so say dumela (hello) with a handshake before asking anything; adding rra for a man or mma for a woman is a nice touch of respect.", "Batswana tend to be polite and softly spoken, so loud or pushy behaviour goes down badly.", "Ask before photographing people, especially in rural villages, and never photograph government buildings or military sites.", "Respect your guides' rules around wildlife at all times, especially on walking safaris and mokoro trips.", "Botswana is proud of its stable democracy and conservation record, and locals are happy to talk about both."], "safety": "Botswana is one of Africa's safest and most stable countries, with low crime and a relaxed vibe. The real risks are wildlife and the roads: elephants and cattle wander onto highways, so avoid driving at night, and always follow your guide's instructions near animals. Malaria is present in the north, including the Delta and Chobe, so take precautions.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is usually the main meal, often eaten around 1pm.", "Look for local takeaway shops and street stalls selling plates of meat, pap and salads.", "Tap water is generally safe in towns, but use bottled or filtered water in the bush.", "Vegetarians can find beans, morogo and pap, but meat features in most meals.", "Tip about 10% in restaurants, and tip your safari guides and camp staff."], "drinks": [{"name": "Bojalwa", "desc": "Traditional sorghum beer, thick and sour, usually homemade. A cup costs very little where available."}, {"name": "Ginger beer", "desc": "Homemade, fizzy ginger beer, sold at markets and roadside stalls. Expect $0.50 to $1 a bottle."}], "greet": "Greetings are important, so say dumela rra or dumela mma with a handshake. Many people hold their right forearm with their left hand as a sign of respect.", "dress": "Wear light, breathable clothes by day and warm layers for cold desert nights from May to August. Stick to khaki, green and brown on safari, and cover shoulders and knees in villages.", "table": "Wash your hands before eating, often with a bowl and jug brought round, and eat bogobe with your right hand when there's no cutlery. Elders and guests are usually served first, so wait until you're invited to start.", "faith": "Most Batswana are Christian, including many in independent churches like the Zion Christian Church, and traditional beliefs stay strong. Ask before photographing ceremonies, and treat sites like the Tsodilo Hills, sacred to the San, with respect.", "phrases": [["Dumela", "Hello"], ["Le kae?", "How are you?"], ["Ke teng", "I'm fine"], ["Ke a leboga", "Thank you"], ["Go siame", "OK, goodbye"]]},
  "zimbabwe": {"around": "Intercity coaches link Harare, Bulawayo, Masvingo and Victoria Falls and are cheap and fairly comfortable, so pick an established company and travel by day. Kombis (shared minibuses) cover shorter hops and leave when full, but they can be crammed and driven hard. Hwange and Matobo are tricky without your own wheels, so most backpackers join a tour or split a rental car. In peak season (roughly July to October) book rafting, Devil's Pool and other Vic Falls activities a few days ahead.", "money": "Zimbabwe runs on US dollars in practice, even though the official local currency is now the ZiG (Zimbabwe Gold), so bring a decent stash of clean, small USD notes from home. Change is a constant headache, and you will often get it back in ZiG, sweets or a promise, so ones and fives are gold. ATMs for foreign cards are unreliable, but Visa and Mastercard work at bigger hotels, lodges and tour operators in Victoria Falls and Harare, often with a surcharge. Tip guides and drivers a few dollars, and haggle politely at craft markets, especially the stalls around Vic Falls.", "sleep": "Victoria Falls is the backpacker hub, with a few proper hostels, dorms and campsites where a bed is affordable by southern African standards. Bulawayo and Harare have a handful of backpacker lodges and guesthouses, while Hwange, Matobo and Great Zimbabwe have national park campsites and simple chalets that are great value if you have a tent.", "eat": ["Sadza: stiff maize porridge, the daily staple", "Nyama: stewed or grilled meat served with sadza", "Matemba: small dried fish fried with tomato", "Mazoe: sweet orange cordial, a local classic"], "data": "Econet is the biggest network, with NetOne a cheaper second option, and both need your passport to register a SIM. Coverage is decent in towns and along main roads, and Wi-Fi in hostels can be slow during power cuts.", "heads_up": "Never photograph government buildings, police or soldiers, as it can get you into real trouble.", "food": [{"name": "Sadza", "wiki": "Ugali", "desc": "Stiff white maize porridge that sits at the centre of almost every Zimbabwean meal, rolled into a ball with your right hand and dunked in stew. A plate with relish and greens at a local canteen costs a couple of dollars.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch and dinner, every day", "order": "Order sadza ne nyama, with beef stew and greens"}, {"name": "Biltong", "wiki": "Biltong", "desc": "Air-dried, spiced strips of beef (or game) sold in supermarkets, butcheries and roadside stalls. It makes a brilliant protein snack for long bus rides.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Road trip snack", "order": "Choose wet (moist) or dry cuts"}, {"name": "Matemba (kapenta)", "wiki": "Kapenta", "desc": "Tiny dried fish from Lake Kariba, fried up with tomato and onion and eaten with sadza. Cheap, salty and very local.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Dinner, with sadza"}, {"name": "Madora (mopane worms)", "wiki": "Gonimbrasia belina", "desc": "Dried caterpillars fried until crunchy, a protein-packed snack you will spot in markets. Worth trying once for the story, and they taste better than you expect.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Snack, bought dried at markets"}, {"name": "Maheu", "wiki": "Mageu", "desc": "A thick, slightly sour fermented maize drink sold in cartons at every supermarket. It is filling enough to count as breakfast.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Any time, as a filling drink"}, {"name": "Chibuku", "wiki": "Chibuku", "desc": "Sorghum-based opaque beer sold in shakeable cartons, nicknamed shake shake, and drunk at local beer halls. Cheap, fizzy and an acquired taste.", "price": "$0.70 to $1.50", "when": "Weekend afternoons at beer halls"}], "food_tip": "Skip the tourist restaurants in Vic Falls town and eat where locals do: small takeaways and canteens serve sadza with stew for a fraction of the price. Supermarkets like OK and Spar are handy for self-catering if your hostel has a kitchen.", "culture": ["Greetings matter, so say hello and ask how someone is before launching into a question. In Shona, mhoro or mangwanani (good morning) goes down well.", "Use your right hand, or both hands, to give and receive things, as handing over money or food with only the left can come across as rude.", "Steer clear of criticising the government or the president in public, and do not photograph police, soldiers, government buildings or the State House.", "Dress is relaxed in towns, but cover up a bit more in rural areas and churches. Most Zimbabweans are Christian, and Sunday services are a big deal.", "Respect elders, who are often addressed with titles like sekuru (grandfather or uncle) or ambuya (grandmother). Letting older people go first is good manners."], "safety": "Zimbabwe is generally safe and friendly for travellers, though bag snatching and pickpocketing happen in Harare and Bulawayo, so keep valuables low-key after dark. Watch for touts offering black-market money exchange in Vic Falls, as counterfeit or old notes are a common trick. Malaria is a risk in low-lying areas like Hwange, Kariba and the Zambezi Valley, and police roadblocks are frequent, so carry your passport and stay patient.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, and canteens cook in the morning, so go early for the freshest food.", "Drink bottled or filtered water, as town supplies can be unreliable.", "Vegetarians can order sadza with beans, covo (greens) and dovi (peanut butter) sauce, though meat is the centrepiece of most plates.", "Prices are usually in US dollars, so carry small notes, as change is often short or given in local ZiG.", "Tipping around 10 percent is appreciated in restaurants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Zumbani tea", "desc": "Herbal tea from the fever tea bush, drunk for colds and comfort. A bag of dried leaves costs about $1 at markets."}, {"name": "Doro", "desc": "Traditional home-brewed sorghum and millet beer, made for ceremonies and shared from a clay pot. It's usually offered rather than sold."}], "greet": "Many people use a three-part handshake, palm then thumb grip then palm. Clapping your hands softly when greeting elders shows respect.", "dress": "Light layers work, but pack warm clothes for cold winter nights from May to August. Don't wear camouflage, which is illegal for civilians.", "table": "Wash your hands before eating, often from a bowl passed around, and eat sadza with your right hand. Elders are served first.", "faith": "Most Zimbabweans are Christian, often alongside traditional respect for ancestors, and Apostolic groups worship outdoors in white robes. Ask before photographing services, and treat Great Zimbabwe and the shrines of Matobo as sacred.", "phrases": [["Mhoro", "Hello"], ["Maita basa", "Thank you"], ["Imarii?", "How much is it?"], ["Makadii?", "How are you? (polite)"], ["Famba zvakanaka", "Go well"]]},
  "zambia": {"around": "Long-distance buses connect Lusaka with Livingstone, Chipata, Kitwe and the Copperbelt, and they are cheap but usually leave early and fill up. Book a day ahead at the bus station in busy periods, and favour the well-known companies. For South Luangwa, take a bus to Chipata and a shared car or minibus to Mfuwe, or fly if your budget stretches. The TAZARA train to Tanzania is an epic, slow and frequently delayed adventure, and Yango works for rides around Lusaka.", "money": "The Zambian kwacha is the only legal currency for day-to-day payments, so do not expect to pay in dollars outside some safari lodges. ATMs from big banks like Zanaco, Stanbic and FNB are easy to find in Lusaka, Livingstone and larger towns, and cards work at supermarkets and mid-range places. Carry cash for buses, markets and rural areas, and keep small notes as change can be short. Tipping guides and safari staff is expected, and friendly haggling is normal at curio markets.", "sleep": "Lusaka and Livingstone have solid backpacker hostels with dorms, pools and campsites, and Livingstone is the social hub for the falls. Around South Luangwa, several camps on the river let you pitch a tent or take a cheap chalet right next to the park, which is way cheaper than lodges inside.", "eat": ["Nshima: stiff maize porridge, eaten by hand", "Ifisashi: greens cooked in peanut sauce", "Kapenta: tiny dried fish, fried with tomato", "Chikanda: savory cake of orchid root and peanuts"], "data": "Airtel and MTN have the best coverage, with Zamtel as a cheaper option, and all need your passport to register. Expect good 4G in towns but little or nothing inside the national parks.", "heads_up": "Malaria is a risk across the whole country, so talk to a doctor about prevention before you go.", "food": [{"name": "Nshima", "wiki": "Ugali", "desc": "Thick maize porridge eaten at nearly every meal, shaped by hand and used to scoop up relishes. A plate with beans or greens at a local spot costs a dollar or two.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch and dinner, every day", "order": "Order with beans, greens or chicken"}, {"name": "Ifisashi", "wiki": "Ifisashi", "desc": "Greens like pumpkin leaves or spinach simmered in a peanut sauce, rich and comforting alongside nshima. A great option if you are vegetarian.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Lunch with nshima"}, {"name": "Kapenta", "wiki": "Kapenta", "desc": "Small sardine-like fish from Lake Kariba and Lake Tanganyika, dried and then fried with tomatoes and onions. You will see huge piles of it in every market.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch or dinner with nshima"}, {"name": "Chikanda", "wiki": "Chikanda", "desc": "Nicknamed African polony, it is a savoury cake made from wild orchid tubers and peanuts. Look for it sliced up at markets and roadside stalls.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 a slice", "when": "Snack from roadside stalls"}, {"name": "Munkoyo", "wiki": "Munkoyo", "desc": "A lightly fermented, sweet and sour drink made from maize and a special root, sold in bottles at markets. It is low in alcohol or none at all, depending on how long it has sat.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Hot afternoons, from markets"}], "food_tip": "Head to local restaurants and market canteens for nshima plates, which are cheap and filling. Shoprite and Pick n Pay are good for self-catering before a safari, since park food is pricey.", "culture": ["Greet people properly before asking anything, and a handshake with your left hand touching your right forearm shows extra respect.", "Zambia is officially a Christian nation and fairly conservative, so dress modestly away from tourist spots and avoid very short shorts in villages.", "Respect for elders runs deep, so let older people speak first and avoid contradicting them bluntly.", "Public displays of affection are frowned upon, and same-sex relationships are illegal, so LGBTQ+ travellers should be discreet.", "Ask before photographing people, especially in rural villages, and do not snap police, military or government buildings."], "safety": "Zambia is one of the more relaxed countries in the region, though petty theft happens at Lusaka's bus stations and markets, so keep your phone out of sight. Be cautious of anyone offering help with tickets or luggage at bus terminals, as some will try to charge you for it. Malaria is a risk countrywide, so take prevention seriously, and take wildlife warnings at camps very literally, especially hippos and elephants at night.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, served from noon, and dinner is around 7pm.", "Don't drink the tap water, and buy bottled or use a filter.", "Vegetarians do well with beans, greens and pumpkin leaves, but meat stock is common, so ask.", "Tip about 10 percent in restaurants if service isn't included, and tip safari guides separately.", "Wash your hands before eating, as nshima is eaten with the right hand."], "drinks": [{"name": "Maheu", "desc": "A thick, lightly sour drink made from fermented maize meal, sold in cartons and bottles for under $1."}, {"name": "Opaque beer", "desc": "Cloudy, sour sorghum and maize beer nicknamed shake shake, as you shake the carton first. About $1 a litre."}], "greet": "Shake hands and ask how someone is, using a gentle grip. Clapping softly or a slight bow shows respect to elders.", "dress": "Dress modestly, especially in villages, with long skirts or trousers. Neutral colours are best on safari.", "table": "Wash your hands before eating, and use your right hand to roll nshima into a ball. Wait for elders to start.", "faith": "Most Zambians are Christian, with traditional beliefs still important. Dress modestly in churches, and ask before attending or photographing ceremonies.", "phrases": [["Muli shani (Bemba)", "How are you?"], ["Natotela (Bemba)", "Thank you"], ["Mwapoleni", "Welcome, hello (Bemba)"], ["Zikomo (Nyanja)", "Thank you, excuse me"], ["Ni zingati? (Nyanja)", "How much?"]]},
  "mozambique": {"around": "Chapas (shared minibuses) go almost everywhere for very little, and longer-distance buses often leave before dawn from a town's main bus stop. Distances are big and roads are slow, so break up the Maputo to Tofo and Vilanculos trip rather than doing it in one brutal day. Dhow trips and island boat runs, like those to the Bazaruto archipelago, are arranged locally once you arrive. Domestic flights with LAM can save days if you are heading north, but book early for decent fares.", "money": "Bring meticais in cash, as cards only really work at bigger lodges, supermarkets and dive shops in the tourist towns. ATMs from Millennium BIM, BCI and Standard Bank are in most towns but can run out of cash around weekends and holidays, so withdraw when you can. Small notes help with chapas and markets. Haggling is normal at markets and for boat trips, and tipping 10 percent at sit-down restaurants is appreciated.", "sleep": "Tofo and Vilanculos are the backpacker beach towns, with chilled hostels, dorms and camping just back from the sand. Maputo has a few hostels and guesthouses, while Ilha de Moçambique has simple guesthouses in old colonial buildings. Book ahead around Christmas, New Year and Easter, when South African holidaymakers flood the south.", "eat": ["Matapa: cassava leaves stewed with peanuts and coconut milk", "Frango piri-piri: grilled chicken with hot chili sauce", "Prawns: grilled garlic prawns, a coastal specialty", "Pao: small crusty bread rolls sold fresh each morning"], "data": "Vodacom has the best coverage, with Movitel and Tmcel as alternatives, and you will need your passport to register a SIM. Signal is good in towns and along the coast but patchy inland.", "heads_up": "Police checks are common, so always keep your passport or a certified copy on you.", "food": [{"name": "Matapa", "wiki": "Matapa", "desc": "Cassava leaves pounded and stewed with ground peanuts and coconut milk, often with crab or prawns on the coast. Usually served with rice or xima.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch at home and local eateries", "order": "Comes with rice or xima"}, {"name": "Frango à piri-piri", "wiki": "Piri piri", "desc": "Chicken grilled over coals and slathered in fiery piri-piri chilli sauce. Look for roadside grills and local spots where half a chicken is cheap.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Evening grills, especially on weekends", "order": "Ask for extra piri-piri sauce on the side"}, {"name": "Rissóis de camarão", "wiki": "Rissole", "desc": "Crumbed, fried half-moon pastries stuffed with creamy prawn filling, sold at cafés and snack bars. The cheapest way to get your fix of Mozambique's famous prawns.", "price": "$0.30 to $0.70 each", "when": "Snack from bakeries and cafés"}, {"name": "Chamuças", "wiki": "Samosa", "desc": "Triangular fried pastries stuffed with spiced meat or veg, a legacy of the country's Indian community. Perfect bus-station snacks for a few coins.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50 each", "when": "Snack, especially in the north"}, {"name": "Cashews", "wiki": "Cashew", "desc": "Mozambique is a big cashew producer, and you will find bags of roasted nuts sold along the highways for much less than at home. Try them salted or with piri-piri.", "price": "$4 to $8 a kilo", "when": "Bought from roadside sellers", "order": "Roasted and salted is best for snacking"}], "food_tip": "Buy fresh pão rolls from bakeries in the morning and fill them with whatever is grilling nearby for a meal that costs almost nothing. Markets sell seafood straight off the boats if you can cook at your hostel.", "culture": ["Portuguese is the official language, and a few words like bom dia (good morning) and obrigado or obrigada (thank you) go a long way.", "Greet people before getting down to business, and handshakes are common, sometimes with a thumb clasp in the middle.", "The north, including Ilha de Moçambique, is largely Muslim, so dress more modestly there, covering shoulders and knees around mosques and in town.", "Ask before taking photos of people, and avoid photographing police, government buildings or bridges.", "Mozambicans tend to be relaxed about time, so expect things to run late and go with the flow."], "safety": "Police checks are frequent, and some officers may hunt for small fines, so carry your passport or a certified copy and stay calm and polite. Petty theft happens in Maputo, so avoid walking alone at night and keep valuables tucked away on the beach. The far north province of Cabo Delgado has an ongoing insurgency and most governments advise against travel there. Malaria is present across the country, so use repellent and a net.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, usually from 12:30pm to 2pm, and dinner is around 7pm.", "Don't drink tap water, and buy bottled water or bring a filter.", "Vegetarians can live on matapa, beans, rice, coconut curries and bread, though it's limited in rural areas.", "Seafood markets in Maputo let you buy fresh fish and prawns and have them cooked at stalls nearby.", "Tipping around 10% in restaurants is appreciated, as wages are very low."], "drinks": [{"name": "Nipa", "desc": "A strong spirit distilled from cashew apples in the south, especially around Inhambane during the summer harvest. A shot costs well under $1."}, {"name": "Sumo de baobá", "desc": "A tangy baobab fruit juice sold in markets and cafés. About $0.50 to $1."}], "greet": "Shake hands with everyone, sometimes holding your right wrist with your left hand as a sign of respect. Always ask \"tudo bem?\" before asking for anything.", "dress": "Cover shoulders and knees in towns and especially in the Muslim north. Beachwear is fine at resorts, but not in villages.", "table": "Wash your hands before eating, and use your right hand for passing food. Guests are often served first.", "faith": "Mozambique is mainly Christian, with a large Muslim population in the north and traditional beliefs everywhere. Dress modestly at mosques and churches, and ask before photographing ceremonies.", "phrases": [["Bom dia", "Good morning"], ["Obrigado / obrigada", "Thank you (male / female speaker)"], ["Quanto custa?", "How much?"], ["Sem piri-piri", "Without chilli"], ["Tudo bem?", "How are you?"]]},
  "ghana": {"around": "Tro-tros (shared minibuses) go almost everywhere for very little, and the mate will shout destinations from the window. For longer journeys, STC and VIP coaches are comfortable and affordable, and booking a day ahead helps on popular routes. Uber, Bolt and Yango work well in Accra and Kumasi, which saves you haggling with taxi drivers. Domestic flights to Tamale or Kumasi are reasonably priced if you are short on time.", "money": "The Ghanaian cedi is the currency, and cash is king for daily spending at chop bars, markets and tro-tros. ATMs are common in cities and bigger towns, and Visa tends to be more reliable than Mastercard. Mobile money (MoMo) is used everywhere, so ask a local to help you set it up if you are staying a while. Haggling is expected at markets, and tipping is appreciated but not mandatory.", "sleep": "Accra has a growing number of hostels and guesthouses, especially in Osu and Labone. Along the coast, beach lodges around Cape Coast, Kokrobite and Busua offer cheap rooms, dorms or camping. In the Volta region and the north, simple guesthouses are the norm and rarely need booking.", "eat": ["Jollof rice: spicy tomato rice, a source of friendly rivalry", "Waakye: rice and beans cooked together, with many toppings", "Kelewele: spicy fried plantain cubes, a street snack", "Banku: fermented corn dough, served with grilled tilapia"], "data": "MTN has the widest coverage, with Telecel and AT as alternatives, and SIM registration needs your passport. Data is cheap and 4G is good in cities and along major roads.", "heads_up": "Use your right hand to eat, give and receive, since the left is considered impolite.", "food": [{"name": "Jollof rice", "wiki": "Jollof rice", "desc": "Spicy tomato rice cooked with peppers and spices, served everywhere from street stands to weddings. Ghanaians will happily tell you theirs beats Nigeria's.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Parties, Sunday lunch and chop bars", "order": "Ask for it with chicken and shito"}, {"name": "Waakye", "wiki": "Waakye", "desc": "Rice and beans cooked together and piled up with toppings like spaghetti, egg, gari, fried plantain and shito chilli sauce. A morning plate from a street vendor is cheap and huge.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Breakfast from roadside sellers", "order": "Add gari, spaghetti, egg and shito"}, {"name": "Kelewele", "wiki": "Fried plantain", "desc": "Cubes of ripe plantain spiced with ginger and chilli, then deep-fried. Look for it at night street stalls, often with roasted peanuts.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Night snack from street stalls", "order": "Pairs well with roasted groundnuts"}, {"name": "Banku and tilapia", "wiki": "Banku (dish)", "desc": "Sour fermented corn and cassava dough served with whole grilled tilapia and hot pepper sauce. A beach-bar classic, especially in Accra.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Evenings at drinking spots", "order": "Comes with pepper sauce; eat with your right hand"}, {"name": "Fufu with light soup", "wiki": "Fufu", "desc": "Pounded cassava and plantain dough swallowed with spicy tomato-based soup and meat or fish. Eaten with your right hand at local chop bars.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch, especially on Sundays", "order": "Choose goat or fish; swallow fufu rather than chew"}, {"name": "Kenkey", "wiki": "Kenkey", "desc": "Fermented corn dough steamed in leaves, eaten with fried fish and fiery pepper sauce. A cheap, filling staple along the coast.", "price": "$0.50 to $2", "when": "Coastal lunches, with fried fish", "order": "Ga kenkey is in husks; Fante kenkey in plantain leaves"}], "food_tip": "Chop bars, small local eateries, serve big plates for little money, and street food at night is cheap and fresh. Buy water in sachets for pennies rather than bottles.", "culture": ["Always greet people before asking questions, and a quick how are you goes a long way. Ghanaians are famously warm and sociable.", "Use your right hand to eat, give and receive things, as the left hand is seen as unclean.", "Respect elders and chiefs, and if you visit a village, it is polite to greet the chief and sometimes bring a small gift.", "Dress modestly in villages and religious places, and remove shoes when entering a mosque.", "Same-sex relationships are illegal and attitudes are conservative, so LGBTQ+ travellers should be discreet."], "safety": "Ghana is one of the easiest and safest countries in West Africa for backpackers, but pickpocketing happens in busy markets like Makola and Kejetia. Watch out for scammers posing as friendly students or guides, and be cautious with online romance or business offers. Malaria is a real risk, so take prevention and use repellent, and swim only where locals do, as coastal currents can be strong.", "eat_beats": ["Many people eat their main meal at midday, and street food stalls get busy again after dark.", "Most local food is spicy, so ask for 'small pepper' if you can't take the heat.", "Vegetarians can find red red (beans with fried plantain) and kelewele, but many soups use fish or meat stock.", "Tipping isn't expected at chop bars, but 10 percent is appreciated in restaurants.", "Tap water isn't safe to drink."], "drinks": [{"name": "Sobolo", "desc": "Chilled hibiscus drink with ginger and spices, sold in bags or bottles for $0.30 to $1."}, {"name": "Palm wine", "desc": "Freshly tapped, slightly fizzy palm sap, best drunk in the morning. A cup costs under $1."}], "greet": "Shake hands with everyone, moving from right to left, and friends finish with a finger snap. 'Akwaaba' means welcome.", "dress": "Ghanaians dress smartly, and many wear bold African print on Fridays; a shirt from a local tailor makes a great souvenir. Cover up in villages and churches.", "table": "A bowl of water is brought for washing hands before fufu or banku. Eat with your right hand only, and if you're invited, try a little of everything.", "faith": "Around 70 percent of Ghanaians are Christian and about a fifth Muslim, mostly in the north, and traditional beliefs remain strong. At shrines and sacred groves, go with a guide and ask before taking photos.", "phrases": [["Akwaaba", "Welcome"], ["Medaase", "Thank you"], ["Ɛyɛ sɛn?", "How much is it?"], ["Wo ho te sɛn?", "How are you?"], ["Ɛyɛ", "It's fine"]]},
  "senegal": {"around": "Sept-place taxis (old seven-seat estate cars) and minibuses leave from the big gare routière in each town once full, and they are the classic way between Dakar, Saint-Louis and the Sine-Saloum. In Dakar, Yango and Heetch work well, the newer BRT buses and TER train help with traffic, and the Gorée ferry runs often from the port. If you are heading to Casamance, the overnight Dakar to Ziguinchor ferry is worth booking a few days ahead.", "money": "Senegal uses the West African CFA franc, and cash rules outside hotels and supermarkets. ATMs in towns generally take Visa more reliably than Mastercard, and mobile money apps like Wave and Orange Money are everywhere. Haggle at markets and agree taxi fares before you get in. Small tips for good service are appreciated but not expected.", "sleep": "Expect small guesthouses, auberges and campements (simple lodges), with a handful of hostels in Dakar and Saint-Louis. Campements in the Sine-Saloum delta often include meals, which helps the budget. Surf camps around Yoff and Ngor in Dakar are popular with backpackers.", "eat": ["Thieboudienne: fish, rice and vegetables cooked in tomato sauce", "Yassa poulet: chicken in a tangy onion and lemon sauce", "Mafé: meat in a rich peanut stew over rice", "Bissap: chilled sweet hibiscus juice sold everywhere"], "data": "Orange, Free and Expresso sell cheap SIMs that need a passport to register, and 4G coverage is good in towns and along the main roads.", "heads_up": "Self-appointed guides on Gorée and around Dakar's markets can be pushy, so politely decline unless you have agreed a price up front.", "food": [{"name": "Thieboudienne", "wiki": "Thieboudienne", "desc": "The national dish: fish, rice and vegetables cooked in a rich tomato sauce, shared from a big communal platter. Lunchtime is the time to find it at local eateries.", "price": "$1.50 to $4", "when": "Lunch, shared from a big bowl", "order": "\"Thieb bou dienn\" is fish; \"thieb yapp\" is meat"}, {"name": "Yassa poulet", "wiki": "Yassa (food)", "desc": "Chicken marinated in lemon and slowly cooked with heaps of onions, served over rice. Tangy, sweet and very moreish.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch or dinner, at local restaurants", "order": "Yassa poisson, with fish, is just as good"}, {"name": "Mafé", "wiki": "Peanut stew", "desc": "Meat simmered in a thick peanut and tomato sauce and served with rice. Hearty and cheap at local restaurants.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Lunch, at small local eateries"}, {"name": "Bissap", "wiki": "Hibiscus tea", "desc": "Chilled hibiscus juice, sweet and tart, sold in little bags or bottles everywhere. Perfect in the heat.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50", "when": "All day, from street sellers", "order": "Ask for it with mint for extra freshness"}, {"name": "Café Touba", "wiki": "Café Touba", "desc": "Coffee spiced with grains of Selim and sometimes clove, sold sweet from street carts for a few coins. A Senegalese morning ritual.", "price": "$0.10 to $0.30", "when": "Morning, from street carts"}, {"name": "Fataya", "wiki": "Fatayer", "desc": "Fried pastries filled with fish or meat and served with a spicy onion sauce, a popular street snack inspired by Lebanese pies.", "price": "$0.10 to $0.30 each", "when": "Afternoon snack, from street sellers", "order": "Ask for extra spicy onion sauce"}], "food_tip": "Eat lunch at small local restaurants where a plate of thieb or yassa is cheap, and grab street snacks like fataya and bissap for a few hundred CFA. Dinner tends to be lighter and later.", "culture": ["Teranga, Senegalese hospitality, is a big deal, so expect to be invited to share food or tea and accept graciously if you can.", "Greetings are long and important, so take time to say hello and ask after the person and their family. Salaam aleekum works almost everywhere.", "Senegal is mostly Muslim, so dress modestly away from the beach, covering shoulders and knees, especially around mosques and in rural areas.", "Use your right hand to eat from the shared bowl and only eat from the section in front of you.", "Ataya, strong sweet mint tea served in three rounds, is a social ritual, so if you are offered it, settle in and enjoy all three glasses."], "safety": "Senegal is one of West Africa's more stable and welcoming countries. Pickpockets and bag snatchers work busy areas of Dakar, and self-appointed guides on Gorée and around the markets can be persistent, so a polite but firm no is best. Malaria is present, especially in the rainy season and in Casamance, so take precautions. The Casamance region is calm these days, but check current advice before heading to its more remote borders.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch, around 1:30pm to 3pm, is the big meal, while dinner is lighter and often after 8pm.", "Don't drink tap water; bottled water or sachets of filtered water are cheap everywhere.", "Vegetarian options are limited, as most dishes include fish or meat, but rice, beans and salads are available.", "Local eateries (gargotes) cook a few dishes daily, so the menu often depends on what's ready.", "Tipping isn't expected in local spots, but 5 to 10 percent is welcome in tourist restaurants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Ataya", "desc": "Strong green tea with mint and sugar, served in three rounds, usually free when offered or under $0.20 a glass at stalls."}, {"name": "Bouye", "desc": "A thick, sweet drink made from baobab fruit, creamy and tangy, sold in bags or bottles for $0.30 to $1."}], "greet": "Greetings are long, starting with \"salaam aleekum\" and a handshake, followed by questions about family and health. Rushing them is rude.", "dress": "Wear loose clothes that cover shoulders and knees in towns and villages. Beachwear is fine at the beach, but cover up when you leave.", "table": "Meals are shared from a big bowl on the floor, eaten with your right hand or a spoon, and you eat only from the section in front of you. The host often pushes the best bits your way.", "faith": "About 95 percent of Senegalese are Muslim, mostly in Sufi brotherhoods like the Mourides, with a Christian minority. Remove shoes at mosques, dress modestly, and ask before photographing at religious sites.", "phrases": [["Na nga def?", "How are you? (Wolof)"], ["Jërëjëf", "Thank you"], ["Ñaata la?", "How much is it?"], ["Mangi fi rek", "I'm fine (reply)"], ["Ba beneen yoon", "See you next time"]]},
  "nigeria": {"around": "In Lagos and Abuja, Uber and Bolt are usually easier than flagging down cars, and Lagos also has BRT buses and the new Blue and Red Line metro trains. For longer trips, domestic flights are the safest and quickest way to cover the big distances, while intercity bus companies serve the southwest. Avoid road travel after dark, and check current advice before going anywhere outside the main southern cities.", "money": "The naira is the currency, and day-to-day life runs on cash and instant bank transfers. Foreign cards often fail at ATMs and shops, so plan how you will get naira before you arrive, whether through a trusted contact, a licensed exchange office or a travel money app. Haggling is normal in markets and with taxi drivers, and small tips for drivers, porters and helpers are common.", "sleep": "There are very few hostels, so most travellers use guesthouses, simple hotels or short-let apartments, especially in Lagos. Stick to safer, well-known areas like Lekki, Victoria Island or Ikoyi in Lagos, and check reviews for power backup and water supply.", "eat": ["Jollof rice: smoky tomato rice, usually served at every party", "Suya: spicy grilled beef skewers with ground peanut spice", "Pounded yam and egusi: yam dough with melon-seed and greens soup", "Zobo: chilled hibiscus drink often spiced with ginger"], "data": "MTN, Airtel and Glo SIMs are widely sold, but registration needs a passport and can take time, so an eSIM can be simpler for a short stay. Coverage is good in cities.", "heads_up": "Police and security checkpoints are frequent, so keep your passport or a copy handy and stay polite and patient.", "food": [{"name": "Jollof rice", "wiki": "Jollof rice", "desc": "Smoky tomato rice cooked with peppers, essential at every party and argued over endlessly with Ghana. Party jollof cooked over firewood is the gold standard.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Lunch, and every party", "order": "Ask for it with fried plantain (dodo)"}, {"name": "Suya", "wiki": "Suya", "desc": "Thin beef skewers rubbed with spicy peanut seasoning and grilled over open fires. Buy from evening street stands, served with sliced onion and tomato.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Evenings, from street grills", "order": "Ask for it with sliced onions and tomatoes"}, {"name": "Pounded yam and egusi", "wiki": "Egusi", "desc": "Smooth yam dough eaten with egusi soup, a thick stew of ground melon seeds, greens and meat. Found at buka canteens across the country.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch, at bukas", "order": "Swallow small balls, don't chew"}, {"name": "Akara", "wiki": "Akara", "desc": "Crispy fried bean fritters, a cheap breakfast street food often paired with pap or bread.", "price": "$0.20 to $1", "when": "Breakfast, from street vendors", "order": "Eat it with pap or bread"}, {"name": "Pepper soup", "wiki": "Peppersoup", "desc": "Spicy, light broth with goat, fish or chicken, loaded with local spices. Great with a cold beer at a local spot.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Evenings, at bars", "order": "Try catfish pepper soup"}, {"name": "Chapman", "wiki": "Chapman (drink)", "desc": "Fruity, red mocktail with Angostura bitters, cucumber and citrus, a Nigerian favourite at bars and parties.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Parties and lounges"}], "food_tip": "Bukas (canteens) and mama put stalls serve filling plates for little money, and street vendors grill suya into the night. Lagos restaurants can be pricey, so eat local for the best value.", "culture": ["Greetings are important, and you should greet elders first, often with a slight bow. In Yoruba areas, younger people may even kneel or prostrate to show respect.", "Use your right hand to give, receive and eat, as the left hand is considered impolite.", "Nigeria is roughly split between a mostly Muslim north and a mostly Christian south, so dress modestly in the north and around religious sites.", "Nigerians can be loud, direct and very social, and banter is part of the culture, so do not take a lively conversation as aggression.", "Same-sex relationships are illegal and attitudes are conservative, so LGBTQ+ travellers should be very discreet."], "safety": "Kidnapping and violent crime are real risks in parts of the country, and many governments advise against travel to the northeast, the northwest and parts of the Niger Delta, so check current advice and stick to well-known areas. In Lagos, avoid flashing valuables, use ride apps rather than street taxis and do not travel at night. Be wary of internet romance and business scams. Malaria is widespread, so take prevention seriously.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is often akara or bread and tea, lunch is the main meal, and dinner is usually light.", "Bukas and mama put stalls serve plates of rice, stews and soups, and you pay before or after eating.", "Don't drink tap water, and buy bottled water rather than the sachets (pure water) unless the brand is trusted.", "Vegetarian food is hard to find, but beans, rice and plantain are good options if you ask for no meat.", "Tip about 10% in restaurants, and give small tips for good service at hotels."], "drinks": [{"name": "Zobo", "desc": "Chilled hibiscus drink, often spiced with ginger and pineapple. A bottle costs about $0.30 to $1."}, {"name": "Palm wine", "desc": "Sweet, slightly fizzy sap from palm trees, which ferments and grows stronger through the day. Expect $1 to $2 a bottle."}], "greet": "Greet people properly before anything else, with good morning or good afternoon and a handshake. Greet elders first, and a slight bow or both hands on theirs shows respect.", "dress": "Lagos is fashion-conscious, so smart casual goes a long way. In the mostly Muslim north, cover arms and legs, and women should carry a scarf.", "table": "Wash your hands before and after eating, and use your right hand to pinch off swallow like pounded yam and dip it in soup. Accept food and drink offered, even just a little.", "faith": "Nigeria is roughly half Muslim and half Christian, and traditional beliefs are also common. Dress modestly, remove shoes at mosques and ask before taking photos at religious sites.", "phrases": [["How far?", "Hi, how are you? (Pidgin)"], ["I dey kampe", "I'm fine (Pidgin)"], ["Abeg", "Please (Pidgin)"], ["How much?", "How much is it?"], ["E se", "Thank you (Yoruba)"]]},
  "cameroon": {"around": "Agency buses and minibuses run between Douala, Yaoundé, Kribi and other towns, so buy tickets at the company office and go with a well-known operator. The Camrail train links Douala and Yaoundé and heads north to Ngaoundéré, which is a classic overnight trip. In towns, shared yellow taxis and moto-taxis are cheap, so state your destination and agree the price before getting in. Limbe and Mount Cameroon sit in the Southwest region, which is covered by travel advisories, so check current advice before planning that leg.", "money": "Cameroon uses the Central African CFA franc, and it is mostly a cash economy. ATMs in Douala and Yaoundé generally take Visa, with fewer options in smaller towns, so stock up before heading out. Mobile money from MTN and Orange is widely used. Haggle at markets, and tipping is modest and not expected at street food stalls.", "sleep": "Budget beds are mostly simple hotels, auberges and mission guesthouses, with very few hostels. Kribi has cheap beach bungalows, and Mount Cameroon treks use basic huts or camping arranged through local guides.", "eat": ["Ndolé: bitterleaf and peanut stew, often with shrimp or beef", "Poulet DG: chicken fried with plantains and vegetables", "Soya: spicy grilled beef skewers sold at night", "Puff-puff: fried sweet dough balls, a common snack"], "data": "MTN and Orange sell cheap SIMs that need passport registration, and coverage is decent in cities but patchy in rural and mountain areas.", "heads_up": "Checkpoints are common on intercity roads, so always carry your passport and keep things calm and polite.", "food": [{"name": "Ndolé", "wiki": "Ndolé", "desc": "The national dish: bitterleaf greens stewed with peanuts and beef or shrimp, served with plantains or bobolo. Rich and slightly bitter in a good way.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Sunday lunch and celebrations", "order": "Ndolé with shrimp (crevettes) is the treat version"}, {"name": "Achu soup", "wiki": "Achu (soup)", "desc": "Pounded cocoyam served with a bright yellow palm oil and spice soup, a specialty of the western highlands. Traditionally eaten with your fingers.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch in the Northwest, and at weddings", "order": "Eat with your fingers, dipping achu into the soup"}, {"name": "Koki", "wiki": "Koki (food)", "desc": "Black-eyed pea paste mixed with palm oil and steamed in banana leaves. Often sold with boiled plantain or cassava.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast or lunch, with plantain"}, {"name": "Soya", "wiki": "Suya", "desc": "Spicy grilled beef skewers sold at night by street vendors, seasoned with chilli and peanut spice. A cheap evening snack.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Evenings, from roadside grills", "order": "Ask for the chilli on the side"}, {"name": "Puff-puff", "wiki": "Puff-puff", "desc": "Fried sweet dough balls, often eaten with beans for a cheap breakfast. Grab them hot from street stalls.", "price": "$0.10 to $0.50", "when": "Breakfast, with beans and porridge", "order": "BHB means beignets, haricots and bouillie"}, {"name": "Bobolo", "wiki": "Chikwangue", "desc": "Fermented cassava wrapped in leaves and steamed into chewy sticks. It goes with almost any sauce or grilled fish.", "price": "$0.20 to $1", "when": "With fish or ndolé, any meal"}], "food_tip": "Roadside chop houses and street grills offer the cheapest meals, and grilled fish with plantain on the beach at Kribi is a bargain. Buy fruit at markets for a healthy, cheap snack.", "culture": ["Cameroon is bilingual, with French dominant in most regions and English in the northwest and southwest, plus Pidgin widely spoken. A bonjour or hello before anything else is essential.", "Greet elders and officials respectfully, often with a handshake and a slight bow, and wait to be invited to sit.", "The north is largely Muslim, so dress modestly there and around mosques, covering shoulders and knees.", "Ask permission before photographing people, especially in villages and at markets, and avoid photographing police, soldiers or government buildings.", "Same-sex relationships are illegal and attitudes are conservative, so LGBTQ+ travellers should be discreet."], "safety": "The Northwest and Southwest (anglophone) regions and the Far North are affected by conflict and most governments advise against travel there, so check current advice and stick to the safer central and coastal routes like Douala, Yaoundé and Kribi. Petty theft happens in Douala, especially at bus stations and markets, so keep valuables hidden. Malaria is present, so use prevention and sleep under a net.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, and chop houses and tourne-dos (street eateries) serve set plates from midday.", "Drink bottled water and skip ice from street stalls.", "Vegetarians can find beans, koki, plantain and some vegetable sauces, but ask, as many are cooked with meat or dried fish.", "Grilled fish at night markets is priced by size, so agree the price before it goes on the grill.", "Tipping isn't expected at stalls, but 5 to 10 percent is welcome in restaurants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Foléré", "desc": "A tangy hibiscus juice sold chilled in bottles or bags for around 20 to 50 cents."}, {"name": "Matango", "desc": "Palm wine tapped daily in villages and sold by the jug for under $1."}], "greet": "Shake hands with everyone, and ask about family and health before getting to business. Use bonjour in French areas or How na? in Pidgin.", "dress": "Douala and Yaoundé are stylish, with colourful kaba dresses and sharp suits, so neat clothes help. In the Muslim north, cover shoulders and legs.", "table": "Wash your hands before and after eating, and eat with your right hand when there's no cutlery. Elders and guests are often served first.", "faith": "Roughly 70 percent of Cameroonians are Christian and a quarter Muslim, and traditional chiefdoms still matter in the west. Ask before entering palaces or sacred forests, and take shoes off in mosques.", "phrases": [["How na?", "How are you? (Pidgin)"], ["Bonjour", "Hello (French)"], ["Merci", "Thank you (French)"], ["C'est combien?", "How much is it? (French)"], ["Na so", "That's how it is (Pidgin)"]]},
  "benin": {"around": "Zemidjans, moto-taxis with drivers in yellow shirts, are the everyday way around town, and the Gozem app lets you book motos and cars in Cotonou. Shared taxis and buses run between Cotonou, Ouidah, Abomey and Porto-Novo, and Ganvié is reached by pirogue from the Abomey-Calavi jetty. Crossing to Lomé in Togo by shared taxi or bus takes a few hours.", "money": "Benin uses the West African CFA franc, and cash in small notes is essential, since cards work mainly in bigger hotels. ATMs are found in towns and generally take Visa. Agree prices with moto drivers and at markets before you start, and tipping is optional but welcome.", "sleep": "Cheap options are auberges, small guesthouses and church-run accommodation, with a few hostels in Cotonou. Ouidah and Abomey have simple family-run places that are easy to find on arrival, and Grand-Popo has budget beach bungalows.", "eat": ["Amiwo: red maize dough cooked in tomato and spices", "Wagasi: soft fried Fulani cheese, often in sauce", "Akassa: fermented maize paste served with fish or sauce", "Sodabi: strong local spirit distilled from palm wine"], "data": "SIMs from MTN or Moov are cheap and require a passport, and 4G works well around Cotonou and the main southern towns.", "heads_up": "Vodun is a living religion here, so ask before photographing shrines, ceremonies or people.", "food": [{"name": "Amiwo", "wiki": "Amiwo", "desc": "Red maize dough cooked in tomato, onion and spices, usually served with fried chicken or fish. A festive dish but easy to find at local eateries.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Celebrations and lunch at maquis", "order": "Try it with fried fish or chicken"}, {"name": "Wagasi", "wiki": "Wagasi", "desc": "Soft, mild cheese made by Fulani herders, sold fried or in sauces. It often has a red rind from sorghum leaves.", "price": "$0.50 to $2", "when": "Lunch or snack", "order": "Try it fried or in tomato sauce"}, {"name": "Akara (ata)", "wiki": "Akara", "desc": "Crispy fried bean fritters sold hot on street corners, a cheap breakfast or snack.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50", "when": "Breakfast from street vendors", "order": "Dip in spicy pepper sauce"}, {"name": "Kuli-kuli", "wiki": "Kuli-kuli", "desc": "Crunchy fried peanut snack, spiced and sold in bags, ideal for bus rides.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50 a bag", "when": "Snack on bus rides"}, {"name": "Gari", "wiki": "Garri", "desc": "Toasted fermented cassava granules, eaten soaked in water with sugar and peanuts or cooked into a dough to go with sauces.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50", "when": "Snack or lunch", "order": "Ask for it with sugar and peanuts"}, {"name": "Sodabi", "wiki": "Sodabi", "desc": "A potent spirit distilled from palm wine, sometimes infused with herbs and roots. Sip carefully, it packs a punch.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 a shot", "when": "Evenings and ceremonies"}], "food_tip": "Street food and small maquis (local eateries) are where you will eat cheapest, especially at lunchtime. Fresh fruit from roadside stands is cheap and plentiful.", "culture": ["Vodun (voodoo) is a living, recognised religion here, so ask before photographing shrines, ceremonies or people, and expect a small fee at some sites.", "Greetings matter, so always say bonjour and ask how people are before asking for anything. French is the main language, with Fon and Yoruba widely spoken.", "Use your right hand to eat, give and receive, as the left hand is considered impolite.", "Dress modestly in villages and at religious sites, covering shoulders and knees.", "If you attend a Vodun ceremony, follow your guide's lead on where to stand and what to do, and avoid touching sacred objects."], "safety": "The south, including Cotonou, Ouidah and Abomey, is generally calm, but petty theft happens in busy markets like Dantokpa, so keep valuables hidden. The far north near the borders with Burkina Faso and Niger, including the national parks, has seen militant attacks and most governments advise against travel there. Wear a helmet when riding motos, and the ocean has strong currents, so swim with care. Malaria is present year-round.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, served from noon, and many maquis close by mid-afternoon.", "Don't drink tap water, and buy sealed bottled water or sachets.", "Vegetarians can find bean dishes, fritters and fruit, but meat and fish stock are common in sauces.", "Tipping isn't expected in local places, but rounding up or leaving 5 to 10 percent is welcome."], "drinks": [{"name": "Tchoukoutou", "desc": "A cloudy, slightly sour sorghum beer brewed in the north, sold in calabashes for about $0.20 to $0.50."}, {"name": "Bissap", "desc": "A sweet chilled hibiscus drink, often with ginger or mint, for about $0.20 a bag."}], "greet": "Shake hands and ask about family before anything else. A lighter handshake with the right hand is common.", "dress": "Dress modestly, especially in villages, and wear long trousers or skirts at religious sites.", "table": "Eat with your right hand and wash your hands first. Wait for the oldest person to start eating.", "faith": "Benin is split between Christianity, Islam and Vodun, and many people follow more than one. At shrines, follow your guide, ask before photos, and don't touch sacred objects.", "phrases": [["Bonjour", "Hello (French)"], ["Merci", "Thank you (French)"], ["C'est combien?", "How much? (French)"], ["A fon ganji a? (Fon)", "Good morning, did you wake well?"], ["Awanu (Fon)", "Thank you"]]},
  "togo": {"around": "Zémidjans (moto-taxis) are the cheapest way around Lomé, and the Gozem app works there for motos and cars. Bush taxis and minibuses run to Kpalimé and up the main road north toward Kara, which is the base for visiting Koutammakou. A shared or private car is the easiest way to reach Koutammakou itself.", "money": "Togo uses the West African CFA franc, and cash is essential outside Lomé. ATMs in larger towns usually accept Visa, and mobile money is widely used. Haggle at markets and agree moto fares before you hop on, and tips are welcome but modest.", "sleep": "Look for auberges, small guesthouses and simple hotels, plus a few hostel-style places in Lomé. Kpalimé has friendly family-run guesthouses that can arrange hiking guides in the hills.", "eat": ["Fufu: pounded yam dough served with spicy soup", "Akoumé: maize dough eaten with okra or tomato sauce", "Ablo: slightly sweet steamed maize and rice cakes", "Tchoukoutou: sour millet beer brewed in the north"], "data": "Togocom and Moov SIMs are easy to buy with a passport, and coverage is good in Lomé and along the main roads.", "heads_up": "Lomé's beach has strong currents and undertow, so swimming there is risky.", "food": [{"name": "Fufu", "wiki": "Fufu", "desc": "Pounded yam dough served with spicy soups like peanut or palm nut soup. You will hear the rhythmic pounding at chop houses all over the country.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Lunch and dinner, with soup", "order": "Pinch a piece and use it to scoop"}, {"name": "Palm nut soup (sauce graine)", "wiki": "Palm nut soup", "desc": "Rich red soup made from palm fruit, often with fish or meat, eaten with fufu or rice.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch, with fufu or rice", "order": "Ask for it with fish or chicken"}, {"name": "Kom (kenkey)", "wiki": "Kenkey", "desc": "Fermented maize dough wrapped in leaves, eaten with fried fish and hot pepper sauce. A cheap, filling southern staple.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Lunch with fried fish and pepper"}, {"name": "Ayimolou", "wiki": "Waakye", "desc": "Rice and beans cooked together and served with pepper sauce, a popular breakfast or lunch from street vendors.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Breakfast or lunch at street stalls", "order": "Comes with spicy pepper sauce and egg"}, {"name": "Tchoukoutou", "wiki": "Pito (beer)", "desc": "Sour millet beer brewed in the north and served in calabashes at local bars. Cheap and very local.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50 a calabash", "when": "Afternoons in the north, at markets"}, {"name": "Sodabi", "wiki": "Sodabi", "desc": "Strong palm spirit sold in bottles of all sizes, often infused with herbs. Go easy.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 a shot", "when": "Celebrations and village bars"}], "food_tip": "Eat at small local eateries and street stalls, where you can get a full plate for very little. The Grand Marché in Lomé is great for snacks and fresh fruit.", "culture": ["French is the official language, and Ewe is widely spoken in the south, so a bonjour or woezon (welcome) goes a long way.", "Greet people properly before asking anything, and take the time for small talk.", "Vodun is practised alongside Christianity and Islam, so be respectful at shrines and fetish markets and ask before taking photos.", "Use your right hand for eating and handing over money or gifts.", "Dress modestly in villages and religious sites, covering shoulders and knees."], "safety": "The south and centre are generally calm, but the far north (Savanes region) near Burkina Faso has seen militant attacks, so check current advice before heading up there. Petty theft happens in Lomé's markets and on the beach, especially at night. Wear a helmet on motos where you can, and malaria is present year-round.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, around noon to 2pm, and dinner is lighter.", "Don't drink tap water, and bottled or sachet water is sold everywhere.", "Vegetarians can eat beans, rice, yams and plantains, but most sauces use fish or meat stock.", "Street food stalls sell grilled corn, fried yams and akara (bean fritters) for very little.", "Tipping isn't expected at local places, but leaving small change in restaurants is appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Bissap", "desc": "A chilled hibiscus drink, sweet and tangy, sold in bags on the street. About $0.20 to $0.50."}, {"name": "Palm wine", "desc": "A mild, milky wine tapped from palm trees, best drunk fresh in the morning. About $0.30 to $1 a cup."}], "greet": "Shake hands, often with a finger snap at the end, and ask about family and health. Use \"bonjour\" in French or \"woezon\" in Ewe.", "dress": "Cover shoulders and knees in towns, villages and at religious sites. Locals dress well, so tidy clothes earn respect.", "table": "Wash your hands before meals and eat with your right hand. Wait for the eldest to start.", "faith": "Togo mixes Christianity, Islam and Vodun, with many people practising more than one. Ask permission before entering shrines, and don't touch fetish objects or photograph ceremonies without permission.", "phrases": [["Bonjour", "Hello, in French"], ["Akpe", "Thank you, in Ewe"], ["C'est combien?", "How much?"], ["Pas de piment", "No chilli"], ["Woezon", "Welcome, in Ewe"]]},
  "ivory-coast": {"around": "In Abidjan, Yango and Heetch work well for rides, and woro-woro shared taxis, gbaka minibuses and lagoon water buses are very cheap. Comfortable intercity buses from companies like UTB run to Yamoussoukro, Bouaké and beyond, and shared minibuses head to Grand-Bassam and Assinie. Buses fill fast on holiday weekends, so buy tickets at the depot a day ahead.", "money": "Côte d'Ivoire uses the West African CFA franc, and cash is still king for most things. ATMs are common in Abidjan, and mobile money apps like Wave and Orange Money are everywhere. Bargain at markets and with unmetered taxis, and rounding up or a small tip is appreciated in restaurants.", "sleep": "Budget stays are simple hotels, residences and short-let apartments, with only a few hostels in Abidjan. Grand-Bassam and Assinie have beach bungalows that get busy on weekends, so book ahead for Friday and Saturday nights.", "eat": ["Attiéké poisson: fermented cassava couscous with grilled fish", "Alloco: fried ripe plantain with chili and onion", "Garba: attiéké with fried tuna, a cheap street staple", "Kedjenou: chicken slow-cooked with vegetables in a sealed pot"], "data": "Orange, MTN and Moov SIMs are cheap and require passport registration, and 4G coverage is strong in the south and main cities.", "heads_up": "The Atlantic surf at Grand-Bassam and Assinie has dangerous rip currents, so swim only where locals do and in calm conditions.", "food": [{"name": "Attiéké poisson", "wiki": "Acheke", "desc": "Fermented cassava couscous with grilled fish, onions and chilli. Its cheaper cousin, garba, comes with fried tuna and is a street-food institution.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch and dinner at maquis", "order": "Ask for grilled fish (poisson braisé) with onions"}, {"name": "Alloco", "wiki": "Fried plantain", "desc": "Fried ripe plantain served with chilli and onion, often with grilled fish or egg. Sold at evening street stalls.", "price": "$0.50 to $2", "when": "Evening street snack", "order": "Add chilli and a fried egg"}, {"name": "Kedjenou", "wiki": "Kedjenou", "desc": "Chicken slow-cooked with vegetables in a sealed pot, so it stews in its own juices. Usually served with attiéké or rice.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Weekend meals at maquis", "order": "Comes with attiéké or rice"}, {"name": "Foutou", "wiki": "Fufu", "desc": "Pounded plantain or yam dough, eaten with sauces like sauce graine (palm nut) or peanut sauce.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch, served with sauce", "order": "Choose plantain (banane) or yam (igname)"}, {"name": "Sauce graine", "wiki": "Palm nut soup", "desc": "Palm nut sauce with meat or fish, a staple at maquis restaurants.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch at home and at maquis", "order": "Eat it with rice or foutou"}, {"name": "Gnamakoudji", "wiki": "Gnamakoudji", "desc": "Fiery ginger juice sold in bottles on the street, sweet with a real kick.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Cold, sold in bags on the street"}], "food_tip": "Maquis, open-air local restaurants, are the best place for cheap grilled chicken and fish, especially in the evening. Street garba stands are the ultimate budget meal.", "culture": ["French is the official language, and greeting people with bonjour and a handshake is expected before getting into anything.", "Ivorians are proud of their music, fashion and food, and evenings at maquis are social affairs, so join in.", "Dress smartly in the cities, as Ivorians generally take pride in their appearance, and dress modestly at mosques and churches.", "Use your right hand to give and receive things and to eat.", "Ask before photographing people and avoid photographing police, military or government buildings."], "safety": "Abidjan is generally safe for travellers, but petty theft and phone snatching happen, especially in busy areas like Adjamé and around bus stations. Some northern border areas near Burkina Faso and Mali carry advisories, so check current advice before heading up there. Malaria is present, so take prevention.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, from about 12 to 2pm, while maquis get busiest after 7pm.", "Drink bottled or sachet water rather than tap.", "Vegetarian options are limited, so ask for alloco, rice and peanut or aubergine sauces made without meat.", "Tipping isn't expected at maquis, but 5 to 10 percent is welcome in restaurants.", "Fruit stands sell mango, pineapple and papaya cheaply, especially in mango season in spring."], "drinks": [{"name": "Bissap", "desc": "Hibiscus juice served cold, sweet and tart, for $0.30 to $1."}, {"name": "Bangui", "desc": "Fresh palm wine, sweet and fizzy, sold in villages and markets for under $1."}], "greet": "Greetings take time, so ask about health and family before getting to the point. Greet elders first, and friends may end a handshake with a finger snap.", "dress": "Lightweight, breathable clothes suit the humid heat, and colourful wax print outfits are everywhere. Cover shoulders and knees at mosques, especially in the north.", "table": "Wash your hands before eating, eat with your right hand from shared dishes, and wait for the eldest to start. Finishing everything may just mean more food arrives.", "faith": "Ivory Coast is split between Islam, mostly in the north, and Christianity, mostly in the south, with traditional beliefs everywhere. Sacred forests and mask ceremonies have strict rules, so ask before photographing, and dress modestly at the basilica in Yamoussoukro.", "phrases": [["Bonjour", "Hello"], ["Merci", "Thank you"], ["C'est combien ?", "How much is it?"], ["On dit quoi ?", "What's up? (Nouchi slang)"], ["Ça va un peu", "I'm okay (local style)"]]},
  "sierra-leone": {"around": "Poda-podas (minibuses), shared taxis, kekes (tuk-tuks) and okadas (motorbikes) cover most trips, and fares are cheap if you ask locals first. From Lungi airport, a water taxi across the estuary to Freetown is the quickest option. Boats to the Banana Islands leave from Kent, and Tiwai Island is easiest with a hired driver or an arranged trip.", "money": "Sierra Leone uses the leone, which was redenominated in 2022 (three zeros knocked off), so double-check which notes you are handed. Bring cash, ideally US dollars or euros to change, since ATMs are limited and many reject foreign cards. Orange Money and Africell mobile money are widely used. Haggling is normal, and small tips are welcome.", "sleep": "Expect guesthouses in Freetown and beach huts or eco-lodges along the peninsula at places like River Number 2, Bureh and Tokeh. The Banana Islands and Tiwai have basic community-run lodges and camping.", "eat": ["Cassava leaves: stewed greens with palm oil and meat or fish", "Groundnut soup: peanut stew served over rice", "Jollof rice: spiced tomato rice, a party favorite", "Poyo: fresh palm wine sold in villages"], "data": "Orange and Africell SIMs are cheap and need ID to register, and coverage is good around Freetown but thin in rural areas.", "heads_up": "Malaria is common year-round, so use prevention and sleep under a net.", "food": [{"name": "Cassava leaves (plasas)", "wiki": "Palaver sauce", "desc": "Pounded cassava or potato leaves stewed with palm oil, fish or meat and served over rice. The heart of Sierra Leonean cooking.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch, at cookshops", "order": "Ask for extra pepper sauce if you like heat"}, {"name": "Groundnut soup", "wiki": "Peanut soup", "desc": "Thick peanut stew with chicken or fish, served over rice. Comforting and filling.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Lunch, at cookshops"}, {"name": "Jollof rice", "wiki": "Jollof rice", "desc": "Spiced tomato rice, a party favourite and always on the menu at local cookshops.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Parties, celebrations and Sunday lunch"}, {"name": "Akara", "wiki": "Akara", "desc": "Fried bean or rice-flour fritters, sold hot by street vendors for breakfast.", "price": "$0.10 to $0.30 each", "when": "Breakfast, from street sellers", "order": "Eat with pap, a light maize porridge"}, {"name": "Ginger beer", "wiki": "Ginger beer", "desc": "Homemade, fiery and sweet, sold in recycled bottles on the street. Nothing like the canned stuff.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "All day, from street sellers"}, {"name": "Poyo", "wiki": "Palm wine", "desc": "Fresh palm wine tapped in villages, sweet when fresh and sour as it ferments through the day.", "price": "$0.20 to $1", "when": "Afternoons in villages, fresh from the tree", "order": "Drink it in the morning for the sweetest taste"}], "food_tip": "Cookshops serve rice with plasas for very little, and fresh fish at beach villages is cheap if you buy straight from the fishermen.", "culture": ["Krio is the common language, and a friendly how di bodi? (how are you?) will get smiles everywhere.", "Greetings matter, so say hello and ask after people before getting to business.", "Sierra Leone is famous for religious tolerance between Muslims and Christians, and families often include both, so be respectful at mosques and churches and dress modestly.", "Use your right hand for eating, giving and receiving.", "Ask before photographing people, especially in villages, and be sensitive when discussing the civil war or Ebola."], "safety": "Sierra Leone is generally friendly and relaxed, but petty theft happens in Freetown, so keep valuables hidden and avoid walking alone at night. Okadas are cheap but accidents are common, so wear a helmet and avoid them after dark. The sea along the peninsula can have strong currents. Malaria is common year-round, so use prevention and sleep under a net.", "eat_beats": ["People eat a big lunch, often around 1pm to 3pm, and rice is the centre of most meals.", "Don't drink tap water; sealed water sachets and bottled water are sold everywhere.", "Vegetarian options are limited, but you can ask for plasas without meat or fish.", "Cookshops often run out by mid-afternoon, so eat lunch early for the best choice.", "Tipping isn't expected in local places, but leaving a little is appreciated in restaurants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Star beer", "desc": "The local lager, brewed in Freetown, about $1 to $2 a bottle."}, {"name": "Baobab juice", "desc": "A tangy, creamy juice from baobab fruit, sold in recycled bottles for $0.30 to $1."}], "greet": "Greetings are friendly and long, with a handshake that often ends with a finger snap. Ask \"how di bodi?\" and expect \"bodi fine\".", "dress": "Light, modest clothes are best, covering shoulders and knees in towns and villages. Locals dress up for church and events.", "table": "Meals are often shared from a common bowl, eaten with the right hand. Wash your hands before eating, and wait for elders to start.", "faith": "About 75 percent of people are Muslim and most of the rest Christian, and interfaith families are common. Remove shoes at mosques and dress modestly in churches.", "phrases": [["Kusheh", "Hello (Krio)"], ["Tenki", "Thank you"], ["Aw mos?", "How much?"], ["Wetin na yu nem?", "What's your name?"], ["A de go", "I'm leaving"]]},
  "gambia": {"around": "Shared yellow taxis and minibuses (gele-geles) run along the coast cheaply, while green tourist taxis cost much more. Heading upriver, buses and bush taxis use the south bank highway to Janjanbureh, and the Banjul to Barra ferry links the north bank, though it can be slow and crowded. Ask locals for the going fare before you set off.", "money": "The Gambian dalasi is the currency, and cash is easiest for most things. ATMs in the coastal resorts usually take Visa, and money changers are common. Haggle at craft markets and agree taxi fares first, and tipping is common in tourist areas.", "sleep": "The coast has guesthouses, eco-lodges and budget hotels, especially around Kololi, Kotu and further south at Sanyang and Kartong. Janjanbureh has simple riverside camps. The coast is busiest in the dry season from November to April, when it pays to book ahead.", "eat": ["Domoda: peanut stew with meat, served over rice", "Benachin: one-pot rice cooked with fish or meat", "Yassa: chicken or fish in lemon and onion sauce", "Wonjo: chilled sweet hibiscus juice"], "data": "Africell and QCell SIMs are easy to buy with a passport, and coverage is good near the coast but weaker upriver.", "heads_up": "Friendly young men offering help on beaches, known as bumsters, are common, so a polite but firm no works best.", "food": [{"name": "Domoda", "wiki": "Domoda", "desc": "Peanut stew with meat or fish, served over rice, often considered the national dish.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Lunch, the main meal", "order": "Ask for it with fish or chicken"}, {"name": "Benachin", "wiki": "Jollof rice", "desc": "One-pot rice cooked with tomato, fish or meat and vegetables, the Gambian take on jollof.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Lunch, especially at weekends", "order": "Ask for the crispy rice from the pot"}, {"name": "Yassa", "wiki": "Yassa (food)", "desc": "Chicken or fish marinated in lemon and onion and slow-cooked, served with rice.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch and dinner", "order": "Try it with fish, it's very popular"}, {"name": "Chakery", "wiki": "Chakery", "desc": "Sweet millet couscous mixed with yoghurt or sour milk, often spiced with nutmeg. A popular dessert or snack.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Snack or dessert", "order": "Eat it chilled"}, {"name": "Wonjo", "wiki": "Hibiscus tea", "desc": "Chilled sweet hibiscus juice, sold in bags or bottles on the street.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50", "when": "Any time, from street vendors"}, {"name": "Attaya", "wiki": "Senegalese tea culture", "desc": "Strong, sweet green tea with mint, brewed and poured from a height over three rounds. A social ritual shared everywhere.", "price": "Free when offered", "when": "Afternoon and evening, with friends", "order": "Stay for all three rounds"}], "food_tip": "Eat at small local chop shops and street stalls, where a plate of domoda or benachin costs very little. Beach bars aimed at tourists charge much more.", "culture": ["Greetings are important, so take time to say hello and ask after family. Salaam aleekum is understood everywhere.", "The Gambia is mostly Muslim, so dress modestly away from the beach, covering shoulders and knees.", "Attaya is a social ritual, and sharing it is a sign of friendship, so accept it if offered and stay for all three rounds.", "Use your right hand to eat from the shared bowl, and only eat from the section in front of you.", "Ask before photographing people, and be respectful at religious sites and during Ramadan."], "safety": "The Gambia is generally safe and laid-back, though petty theft happens at beaches and in busy markets. Romance scams and dodgy business offers aimed at tourists are common, so be cautious about getting financially involved. Malaria is present, especially in the rainy season.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, eaten around 2pm, and dinner is usually light.", "Street food stalls and chop shops sell cheap snacks like fried fish, akara and sandwiches.", "Don't drink tap water, and buy bottled or sachet water.", "Vegetarian options are limited, but you can find dishes with beans, vegetables and rice.", "Tipping isn't expected in local eateries, but 10% is appreciated in tourist restaurants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Bouye", "desc": "A thick, sweet drink made from baobab fruit, often mixed with milk. A bag or bottle costs about $0.30 to $1."}, {"name": "Café Touba", "desc": "Spiced coffee with cloves and grains of Selim, sold from street stalls. Expect $0.20 to $0.50 a cup."}], "greet": "Greetings are long, so say salaam aleekum and ask about family and health before anything else. Men usually shake hands, and greet elders first.", "dress": "Cover shoulders and knees, especially in villages and religious sites. Beachwear is fine on the beach, but cover up elsewhere.", "table": "Food is often shared from a big bowl, and you eat with your right hand. Only eat from the section in front of you.", "faith": "About 95% of Gambians are Muslim, alongside a small Christian community, and many follow Sufi brotherhoods. Ask before entering a mosque, dress modestly, remove shoes and avoid Friday midday prayers.", "phrases": [["Nanga def?", "How are you? (Wolof)"], ["Mangi fi rek", "I'm fine (Wolof)"], ["Jerejef", "Thank you (Wolof)"], ["Ñaata la?", "How much? (Wolof)"], ["Abaraka", "Thank you (Mandinka)"]]},
  "mali": {"around": "Intercity buses and shared minibuses link the main towns, river pinasses (wooden boats) have long run on the Niger, and taxis and moto-taxis cover Bamako. However, roads to Djenné, Dogon Country, Mopti and Timbuktu pass through areas affected by conflict, and jihadist blockades have hit fuel supplies and road travel even around Bamako. Check current government advice before any plans.", "money": "Mali uses the West African CFA franc and is very much cash-based. ATMs in Bamako generally take Visa only, and supply has been disrupted by fuel shortages and security issues. Mobile money is widely used. Haggling is expected in markets, and tips are small.", "sleep": "Traditional budget options are campements, simple guesthouses and mission houses, and in Dogon Country, nights were often spent on village rooftops under the stars. Today, most visitors stay in Bamako hotels.", "eat": ["Tô: millet or maize porridge with leaf or okra sauce", "Riz gras: rice cooked in a rich tomato and meat sauce", "Capitaine: grilled Nile perch from the Niger River", "Jus de gingembre: strong, sweet ginger juice"], "data": "Orange and Moov SIMs need passport registration, and coverage is good in Bamako but limited in remote areas.", "heads_up": "Even in Bamako, many governments advise against travel, so register with your embassy and follow local news closely.", "food": [{"name": "Riz gras", "wiki": "Riz gras", "desc": "Rice cooked in a rich tomato and meat sauce, a festive staple found at local eateries.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Lunch at local eateries"}, {"name": "Tigadèguèna (mafé)", "wiki": "Peanut stew", "desc": "Hearty peanut butter stew with meat or vegetables, served with rice.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Lunch, served over rice"}, {"name": "Capitaine", "wiki": "Nile perch", "desc": "Nile perch from the Niger River, grilled or fried and served with rice or chips.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Dinner by the Niger in Bamako"}, {"name": "Dégué", "wiki": "Chakery", "desc": "Sweet millet couscous mixed with yoghurt, a popular snack or dessert.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Afternoon snack, sold in small cups"}, {"name": "Fonio", "wiki": "Fonio", "desc": "Ancient West African grain, cooked like couscous and served with sauce.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch, often with peanut sauce"}, {"name": "Jus de gingembre", "wiki": "Gnamakoudji", "desc": "Strong, sweet ginger juice with a real kick, sold everywhere in bottles or bags.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50", "when": "Any time, sold chilled"}], "food_tip": "Street food and small local eateries are the cheapest options, and markets have fresh fruit and snacks.", "culture": ["Greetings are long and important, so take your time with salutations and ask after family. French is the official language, but Bambara is widely spoken.", "Mali is mostly Muslim, so dress modestly, with women covering shoulders and knees, and be respectful during prayer times and Ramadan.", "Use your right hand for eating, giving and receiving.", "Malian music and griot traditions are a huge source of pride, so if you get a chance to hear live music, take it.", "Ask before photographing people, and avoid photographing military or government buildings."], "safety": "Most Western governments advise against all travel to Mali because of ongoing conflict, terrorism and kidnapping risk, and many travel insurers will not cover you. If you do go, keep a very low profile, travel only with trusted local contacts, and follow the news closely. Malaria and heat are serious health risks too.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the biggest meal, and dinner usually comes around 8pm, after the evening prayer.", "Drink only bottled or filtered water, and skip ice and street juices unless you trust the source.", "Vegetarians can find bean dishes, peanut sauces and tô (millet porridge), but most sauces include meat or fish.", "Markets sell fruit, bread and snacks cheaply, and polite bargaining is expected.", "Tipping isn't expected at small eateries, but 5 to 10 percent is appreciated in restaurants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Bissap", "desc": "Chilled hibiscus juice, deep red and sweet, sold in bags or bottles for about 20 to 50 cents."}, {"name": "Green tea", "desc": "Strong green tea with mint and lots of sugar, brewed slowly in three rounds and shared. When you're invited it's free; at a stall a glass costs a few cents."}], "greet": "Shake hands and ask about family, health and work before anything else. Use i ni ce in Bambara, or bonjour in French.", "dress": "Long, loose clothes suit both the heat and local norms. Women should cover shoulders and knees, and men avoid shorts in towns.", "table": "Meals are shared from one bowl, so wash your hands and eat from the section in front of you with your right hand. Let your host hand you pieces of meat rather than reaching for them.", "faith": "About 95 percent of Malians are Muslim, with Christian and traditional minorities. Non-Muslims can't enter some mosques, including the Great Mosque of Djenné, so admire them from outside.", "phrases": [["I ni sogoma", "Good morning"], ["I ni ce", "Thank you"], ["Joli?", "How much?"], ["Ayiwa", "Okay"]]},
  "gabon": {"around": "The Transgabonais railway runs from Owendo near Libreville into the interior and stops at Lopé, making it the cheapest way to reach the park, so book seats ahead at the station or online. In Libreville, shared taxis are cheap if you call out your destination. Loango is usually reached by boat or a lodge transfer via Port-Gentil or Omboué, which is where costs climb.", "money": "Gabon uses the Central African CFA franc and is one of Africa's pricier countries, so budget accordingly. Cash is needed almost everywhere outside big Libreville hotels, and ATMs in Libreville and Port-Gentil take Visa. Tipping is modest, and haggling is light.", "sleep": "There are no real hostels, so budget beds are simple hotels and church-run guesthouses. In the parks, lodges dominate, though Lopé has some cheaper rooms and camping.", "eat": ["Poulet nyembwe: chicken in a palm nut sauce", "Bâton de manioc: fermented cassava sticks wrapped in leaves", "Atanga: butterfruit (safou), often boiled or roasted", "Régab: the local lager, cheap and everywhere"], "data": "Airtel and Moov Africa SIMs need passport registration, and coverage is solid in towns but disappears in the parks.", "heads_up": "Avoid photographing government buildings, police or the presidential palace.", "food": [{"name": "Poulet nyembwe", "wiki": "Moambe", "desc": "Chicken cooked in a rich palm nut sauce, the national dish, served with rice or cassava.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Lunch or dinner on Sundays", "order": "Ask for it with rice or bâton"}, {"name": "Bâton de manioc", "wiki": "Chikwangue", "desc": "Fermented cassava sticks wrapped in leaves, eaten with almost everything.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "With every meal"}, {"name": "Atanga", "wiki": "Dacryodes edulis", "desc": "Butterfruit (safou), boiled or roasted, buttery and seasonal.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Snack in the rainy season"}, {"name": "Odika sauce", "wiki": "Irvingia gabonensis", "desc": "Smoky sauce made from wild mango seeds, nicknamed Gabonese chocolate, often served with fish or meat.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Lunch with fish or meat"}, {"name": "Palm wine", "wiki": "Palm wine", "desc": "Fresh palm wine tapped from palms in villages, sweet at first and sourer as the day goes on.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 a glass", "when": "Village gatherings", "order": "Drink it fresh in the morning"}], "food_tip": "Street grills and local eateries are the cheapest options, and markets are great for fresh fruit and snacks. Supermarkets in Libreville are handy for self-catering, but imported goods are expensive.", "culture": ["French is the official language, so a bonjour and a handshake are expected before any conversation.", "Gabon is mostly Christian, with traditional beliefs like Bwiti still important, so be respectful if you encounter ceremonies.", "Dress smartly in Libreville, and modestly in villages and religious sites.", "Use your right hand for eating, giving and receiving.", "Ask before photographing people, and avoid photographing government buildings, police or the presidential palace."], "safety": "Gabon is relatively stable and calm, but petty theft happens in Libreville, so keep valuables out of sight. Malaria is present countrywide, so take prevention seriously. Wildlife in the parks, including elephants and gorillas, should be respected, so always go with a guide.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, and many places close in the afternoon.", "Don't drink tap water, and buy bottled water.", "Vegetarians will find it hard, but cassava, plantains and vegetables are always available.", "Tip about 10 percent in restaurants if service isn't included.", "Gabon is expensive for Central Africa, so markets and street grills are key for keeping costs down."], "drinks": [{"name": "Foléré", "desc": "Chilled hibiscus juice, sweet and tart, sold in bags and bottles at markets for under $1."}, {"name": "Ginger juice", "desc": "Fiery homemade ginger drink sold cold at markets and street stalls for about $0.50 to $1."}], "greet": "Shake hands with everyone when you enter a room, and say bonjour. Close friends may kiss on the cheek.", "dress": "Dress neatly in Libreville, and cover up in villages. Light, long clothes help with sun and insects.", "table": "Wait for the host to serve you and eat with your right hand. It's polite to try everything offered.", "faith": "Most people are Christian, with Bwiti, a spiritual practice, still important. Ask before attending or photographing ceremonies.", "phrases": [["Bonjour", "Hello"], ["Merci", "Thank you"], ["C'est combien?", "How much?"], ["Mbolo (Fang)", "Hello"], ["Akiba (Fang)", "Thank you"]]},
  "dr-congo": {"around": "Distances are huge and roads are poor, so domestic flights are often the only practical way to cover long distances. In Kinshasa, taxis, shared minibuses (fula-fula) and moto-taxis are cheap but chaotic. The east, including Goma, Bukavu and Virunga, is affected by conflict, with armed groups controlling large areas since 2025, so check current advice before any planning.", "money": "The Congolese franc is the official currency, but US dollars are widely used for bigger purchases. Notes must be recent, crisp and undamaged or they will be refused, and change often comes in francs. Cards work only in some upscale places, so carry cash, and haggling is normal.", "sleep": "Cheap rooms are mostly guesthouses and church-run hostels in the cities. Kinshasa has few budget options, so expect to pay more than elsewhere in the region.", "eat": ["Fufu: cassava or maize dough eaten with stews", "Pondu: stewed cassava leaves with palm oil and fish", "Moambe chicken: chicken in palm nut sauce", "Liboke: fish steamed in banana leaves with spices"], "data": "Vodacom, Airtel, Orange and Africell sell SIMs that need passport registration, and coverage is fair in cities but patchy elsewhere.", "heads_up": "Taking photos in public, especially of officials or infrastructure, can lead to trouble, so ask first or keep the camera away.", "food": [{"name": "Fufu", "wiki": "Fufu", "desc": "Cassava or maize dough eaten with stews and sauces, the national staple.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Lunch and dinner, with sauce", "order": "Ask for it with pondu, cassava leaves"}, {"name": "Moambe chicken", "wiki": "Moambe chicken", "desc": "Chicken in rich palm nut sauce, often considered the national dish.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Family meals and celebrations", "order": "Comes with rice or fufu"}, {"name": "Makemba", "wiki": "Fried plantain", "desc": "Fried or grilled plantains sold by street vendors all over Kinshasa, often eaten with grilled fish, beans or a spicy pili-pili sauce. A cheap and filling snack.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Snack or side, from street sellers"}, {"name": "Chikwangue (kwanga)", "wiki": "Chikwangue", "desc": "Fermented cassava wrapped in leaves and steamed, sold in markets and eaten with stews and grilled fish.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Side with grilled fish or meat"}, {"name": "Lotoko and malafu", "wiki": "Palm wine", "desc": "Palm wine (malafu) is sold in villages and markets, and strong homebrews are everywhere, so go easy.", "price": "$0.50 to $2", "when": "Evenings in local bars", "order": "Drink palm wine fresh, lotoko is much stronger"}], "food_tip": "Street food and local eateries (malewa) serve cheap plates of fufu, beans and grilled fish. Restaurants aimed at expats in Kinshasa are very expensive.", "culture": ["French and Lingala are widely spoken in Kinshasa, with Swahili common in the east, so learn a few greetings like mbote (hello in Lingala).", "Congolese music, especially rumba and ndombolo, is a source of huge national pride, and nightlife in Kinshasa is legendary.", "Dress smartly in Kinshasa, where people take great pride in their appearance, and modestly at churches.", "Use your right hand for giving, receiving and eating.", "Ask before photographing people, and avoid photographing officials, police, soldiers or infrastructure."], "safety": "Much of the east, including Goma, Bukavu and Virunga, is affected by ongoing conflict, and most governments advise against travel there. Kinshasa has street crime and occasional unrest, so avoid walking at night and keep a low profile. Disease outbreaks such as Ebola and mpox occur, so check health advice before travel, and malaria is widespread.", "eat_beats": ["Meals are often just two a day, with a big lunch and dinner after work.", "Don't drink tap water, and buy bottled or sachet water.", "Vegetarians can eat pondu, beans, plantains and rice, but sauces often include fish or meat.", "Street grills sell brochettes (meat skewers), grilled fish and chikwangue in the evenings.", "Tipping 5% to 10% in restaurants is common, and small change for helpful staff is appreciated."], "drinks": [{"name": "Kivu coffee", "desc": "Arabica grown on the hills around Lake Kivu, served strong in Goma and Bukavu. About $1 to $2 a cup."}, {"name": "Tangawisi", "desc": "A homemade ginger drink, spicy and sweet. About $0.50 a bottle."}], "greet": "Shake hands and ask about family before anything else. Say \"mbote\" in Lingala or \"jambo\" in Swahili in the east.", "dress": "Congolese dress smartly, so neat clothes are appreciated in Kinshasa. Cover shoulders and knees at churches and in villages.", "table": "Wash your hands before meals and eat with your right hand. Guests are often served first.", "faith": "Most people are Christian, mainly Catholic and Protestant, alongside Kimbanguists and traditional beliefs. Dress modestly at churches and ask before photographing services.", "phrases": [["Mbote", "Hello, in Lingala"], ["Matondo", "Thank you, in Lingala"], ["Ntalo boni?", "How much?"], ["Bonjour", "Hello, in French"], ["Merci", "Thank you, in French"]]},
  "angola": {"around": "In Luanda, ride-hailing apps such as Yango and Heetch are easier than the crowded blue-and-white candongueiro minibuses. Intercity buses run on the main roads to Malanje (for Kalandula Falls), Benguela and Lubango, and domestic flights save long days on the road. Book buses a day ahead at the company office.", "money": "Angola uses the kwanza, and it is pricey and largely cash-based. Foreign cards work only at some ATMs and hotels, so bring backup cash in US dollars or euros to change at banks or licensed exchange offices. Haggle at markets, and tips are welcome but not expected at street stalls.", "sleep": "Budget beds are scarce in Luanda, where simple guesthouses or apartments are the best value. Outside the capital, small hotels and pensions are cheaper and rarely need booking.", "eat": ["Muamba de galinha: chicken in palm oil sauce with okra", "Funge: stiff cassava porridge, the everyday staple", "Calulu: dried fish stewed with vegetables and palm oil", "Cuca: the national lager"], "data": "Unitel and Africell SIMs need a passport to register, and coverage is good in cities but thin in the countryside.", "heads_up": "Portuguese is the main language, and English is rare outside hotels, so a translation app helps a lot.", "food": [{"name": "Muamba de galinha", "wiki": "Moambe chicken", "desc": "Chicken in palm oil sauce with okra and garlic, the national dish, served with funge.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Family lunches and weekends", "order": "Eat it with funge to soak up the sauce"}, {"name": "Funge", "wiki": "Funge", "desc": "Stiff cassava porridge, the everyday staple, eaten with stews and sauces.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "A side for most lunches", "order": "Cassava funge is northern; maize funge is southern"}, {"name": "Calulu", "wiki": "Calulu", "desc": "Dried fish or meat stewed with vegetables and palm oil, often served with funge.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Lunch, often on weekends", "order": "Dried fish calulu is the classic"}, {"name": "Cocada amarela", "wiki": "Cocada", "desc": "Sweet coconut dessert made with egg yolks and sugar, a Portuguese-inspired treat.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Dessert at celebrations"}, {"name": "Cuca", "wiki": "Cuca (beer)", "desc": "The national lager, cheap and everywhere, best ice-cold at a beach bar.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Weekends at beach bars and parties", "order": "Drink it ice cold"}], "food_tip": "Street food and local eateries are cheaper than restaurants, and markets have fresh produce. Luanda is expensive, so self-catering helps.", "culture": ["Portuguese is the main language and English is rare outside hotels, so learn the basics and use a translation app.", "Greet people with a handshake and a bom dia, and take time for small talk.", "Dress smartly in Luanda, where people take pride in their appearance, and modestly in churches.", "Ask before photographing people, and avoid photographing government buildings, police or soldiers.", "Angolans are proud of their music, especially kizomba and semba, so if you get a chance to dance, go for it."], "safety": "Luanda has street crime, including phone snatching and muggings, so avoid walking at night and keep valuables hidden. Police checks are common, so carry your passport. Landmines remain in some rural areas, so stick to roads and marked paths. Malaria is present, so take prevention.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, from about 1 to 3pm, and dinner starts late.", "Don't drink tap water; buy bottled.", "Vegetarian options are few, but beans in palm oil and grilled vegetables help.", "Tipping about 10 percent is normal in restaurants.", "Card payments can be unreliable, so carry kwanza for street food and markets."], "drinks": [{"name": "Kissângua", "desc": "A fermented maize drink, slightly sour and refreshing, sold in markets for under $1."}, {"name": "Maboque juice", "desc": "Tart, tangy juice from the maboque fruit, about $1 to $2 a glass."}], "greet": "A handshake with 'Bom dia' and a 'Tudo bem?' is standard, and friends kiss on the cheek. Take time for small talk before business.", "dress": "Light clothing works on the coast, but bring a jacket for the cooler highlands around Huambo and Lubango. Shorts are fine at the beach, but people dress up in the city.", "table": "Wait for the host to invite you to start, and expect generous portions; refusing seconds can seem rude. Meals are long and social, often followed by music.", "faith": "Most Angolans are Christian, mainly Catholic and Protestant, and traditional beliefs are still part of daily life. Pilgrims flock to the church at Muxima, so dress modestly and stay quiet there.", "phrases": [["Bom dia", "Good morning"], ["Obrigado / Obrigada", "Thank you"], ["Quanto custa?", "How much is it?"], ["Tudo bem?", "All good?"], ["Kota", "Elder (respectful slang)"]]},
  "eswatini": {"around": "Minibus taxis (kombis) link Mbabane, Manzini, Ezulwini and the main towns cheaply and leave when full from each town's taxi rank. Public transport usually drops you on the main road outside the reserves, so reaching Mlilwane, Hlane and Malolotja is far easier with a rental car, often picked up in Johannesburg. Check that your rental agreement allows border crossings, and that the border posts you plan to use are open when you need them, since some smaller ones close early.", "money": "The lilangeni is pegged one to one with the South African rand, and rand notes are accepted everywhere, so there is no need to change money if you are coming from South Africa. Cards work in supermarkets, lodges and most places in Mbabane, Manzini and Ezulwini, and ATMs are easy to find in towns. Carry some cash for kombis, markets and craft stalls. Tip around 10 percent in restaurants and a little extra for good guides, and gentle haggling is fine at craft markets.", "sleep": "Hostel-style lodges, camping and beehive huts are the cheap options, and Mlilwane's rest camp has dorms, huts and campsites right among the zebras and warthogs. The Ezulwini Valley has a few backpacker lodges with a social vibe. Weekends and South African school holidays fill up fast, so book ahead then.", "eat": ["Sishwala: stiff maize porridge served with meat or stew", "Sidvudvu: pumpkin and maize porridge", "Emasi: thick soured milk, often eaten with maize", "Umcombotsi: home-brewed sorghum beer"], "data": "MTN and Eswatini Mobile sell SIMs that need passport registration, and coverage is good across most of this small country. If you already have a South African SIM, check roaming costs, as they can be steep.", "heads_up": "Lilangeni notes are not accepted in South Africa, so spend them before you leave and keep rand for the trip back.", "food": [{"name": "Sishwala (pap)", "wiki": "Ugali", "desc": "Stiff maize porridge that is the base of most Swazi meals, served with meat, stew or greens. A full plate at a local eatery is very cheap.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch and dinner, every day", "order": "Ask for it with stew or chakalaka"}, {"name": "Emasi", "wiki": "Amasi", "desc": "Thick fermented milk, tangy like a runny yoghurt, often eaten mixed with crumbly maize porridge. You will find it in cartons in supermarkets.", "price": "$1 to $2 a carton", "when": "Breakfast or with porridge"}, {"name": "Emahewu", "wiki": "Mageu", "desc": "A lightly fermented maize drink, slightly sour and very filling, sold in cartons or made at home.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Snack or drink, any time"}, {"name": "Fat cakes", "wiki": "Vetkoek", "desc": "Deep-fried dough balls sold at bus ranks and roadside stalls, sometimes stuffed with mince. Cheap fuel for long kombi rides.", "price": "$0.10 to $0.50 each", "when": "Morning, at bus ranks", "order": "Ask for one with mince inside"}, {"name": "Umcombotsi", "wiki": "Umqombothi", "desc": "Home-brewed sorghum beer, thick, sour and low in alcohol, shared at ceremonies and gatherings. If someone offers you a sip, it is a friendly gesture.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 a cup", "when": "Ceremonies and gatherings"}], "food_tip": "Self-catering is easy with big supermarkets in Mbabane, Manzini and Ezulwini, and many lodges have braai (barbecue) areas. For eating out, local takeaways near the bus ranks serve pap and stew for very little.", "culture": ["Eswatini is Africa's last absolute monarchy and the king is treated with great respect, so avoid criticising the royal family in public.", "Greet people before anything else, as sawubona (hello) followed by a handshake goes down really well.", "Umhlanga (the Reed Dance, around August or September) and Incwala (around December or January) are huge cultural events that visitors can sometimes watch, but follow local guidance on where to stand and what you can photograph.", "Dress modestly in rural areas and at ceremonies, and save the very short shorts for the lodge pool.", "Respect elders, wait to be invited into homesteads, and use both hands or your right hand to give and receive."], "safety": "Eswatini is generally relaxed and friendly, but petty crime and bag snatching happen in Mbabane and Manzini, so keep valuables out of sight. Avoid driving after dark, as roads have livestock, pedestrians and poor lighting. HIV prevalence is among the highest in the world, so take precautions. Malaria is a low, seasonal risk in the lowveld near Hlane and the Mozambique border.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal around 1pm, and dinner is around 7pm.", "Tap water is safe in larger towns, but use bottled or filtered water in rural areas.", "Vegetarians can find beans, greens, pap and pumpkin, but meat is central to most meals.", "Markets in Manzini and Mbabane sell fresh fruit and cooked food cheaply.", "Tip 10 percent in restaurants, and small tips for car guards and petrol attendants are normal."], "drinks": [{"name": "Sibebe beer", "desc": "A local lager named after Sibebe Rock, about $1 to $2 a bottle."}, {"name": "Marula drinks", "desc": "Fruit from marula trees is fermented into buganu, a seasonal brew, while bottled marula liqueur sells for around $10 to $15."}], "greet": "\"Sawubona\" (I see you) is the greeting, answered with \"yebo\". A handshake, often supporting your right arm with your left hand, shows respect.", "dress": "Wear modest clothes in towns and rural areas, and women should avoid very short skirts. Traditional dress is worn at ceremonies.", "table": "Wash your hands before eating, and use your right hand. Accept food offered, as refusing can seem rude.", "faith": "Most Swazis are Christian, often mixed with traditional beliefs and ancestor reverence. At royal and ceremonial sites, follow local guidance and ask before taking photos.", "phrases": [["Sawubona", "Hello"], ["Ngiyabonga", "Thank you"], ["Malini?", "How much?"], ["Unjani?", "How are you?"], ["Sala kahle", "Goodbye (to someone staying)"]]},
  "lesotho": {"around": "Minibus taxis and buses run from Maseru to most towns, including Semonkong and Malealea, but they are slow on steep, winding mountain roads, so start early. Sani Pass is usually done on a 4x4 tour from Underberg in South Africa or in your own high-clearance vehicle, and the South African side of the border closes in the late afternoon. Pony trekking from Malealea or Semonkong is the classic way to see the highlands and visit villages that have no road access at all.", "money": "The loti is pegged one to one with the rand, and rand is accepted everywhere. ATMs are in Maseru and the bigger towns, but many rural lodges, village shops and pony-trek operators are cash only, so stock up before heading into the mountains. Tipping guides and pony handlers is customary and appreciated, and haggling is fine at craft stalls.", "sleep": "Mountain lodges at Sani, Semonkong and Malealea offer dorms, rondavels and camping at budget prices, and some do homestay-style village nights on pony treks. Maseru has a few guesthouses but most travellers just pass through. Winter nights are freezing, so check that rooms have heating or extra blankets.", "eat": ["Papa: stiff maize porridge, the daily staple", "Moroho: cooked greens like spinach or cabbage", "Joala: home-brewed sorghum beer, flagged by a white flag", "Maluti: the local lager, named after the mountains"], "data": "Vodacom and Econet Telecom Lesotho sell SIMs that need passport registration, and coverage is good in towns but patchy or absent in the high mountains.", "heads_up": "Weather in the highlands changes fast, and snow is common from June to August, so pack warm layers even if the lowlands feel mild.", "food": [{"name": "Papa with moroho", "wiki": "Ugali", "desc": "Stiff maize porridge, eaten daily with moroho (cooked greens like spinach or cabbage) or a meat stew. The cheapest filling meal you will find.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch and dinner, every day", "order": "Eat it with your right hand"}, {"name": "Likhobe (samp)", "wiki": "Samp", "desc": "Dried, pounded maize kernels, often slow-cooked with beans into a hearty winter dish.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Winter lunches and family meals"}, {"name": "Makoenya", "wiki": "Vetkoek", "desc": "Fried dough fat cakes sold from roadside stalls and taxi ranks for a few coins each. Perfect with a cup of sweet tea.", "price": "$0.10 to $0.30 each", "when": "Breakfast, from roadside stalls", "order": "Eat them warm"}, {"name": "Leting", "wiki": "Mageu", "desc": "A lightly fermented sorghum or maize drink, slightly sour and refreshing, usually homemade.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Afternoon, at home"}, {"name": "Joala", "wiki": "Umqombothi", "desc": "Home-brewed sorghum beer, sold from houses that fly a little white flag outside. Cheap, thick and very local.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Weekends and celebrations", "order": "Look for the white flag outside"}], "food_tip": "Self-catering is the way to go, so stock up at supermarkets in Maseru or South African border towns before heading into the mountains. Lodges often offer good-value set dinners, which is handy when the nearest shop is hours away.", "culture": ["The Basotho blanket and the conical mokorotlo hat are national symbols, and you will see shepherds wrapped in blankets on horseback all over the highlands.", "Greet people with lumela (hello), and add ntate for a man or 'me for a woman as a sign of respect.", "Respect elders and chiefs, and ask permission before wandering into villages or homesteads, ideally with a local guide.", "Dress modestly in villages, and cover up for church, which is central to community life.", "Ask before photographing people, especially herders, and do not hand out sweets or money to kids, as it encourages begging."], "safety": "Lesotho is generally safe, but petty theft and the odd mugging happen in Maseru, so keep valuables hidden and avoid walking at night. Mountain roads are steep and winding, and weather changes fast, so drive carefully and never travel at night. On treks, take a local guide, as paths are unmarked and dogs at isolated homesteads can be aggressive. HIV prevalence is very high, so take precautions.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, often eaten around 1pm, and dinner is usually simple.", "Small shops (cafés) in towns sell plates of papa, meat and salads cheaply.", "Boil or treat tap water, especially in rural areas, and carry a filter bottle for mountain hikes.", "Vegetarian food is easy to find, as papa and moroho are everyday staples.", "Tip about 10% in restaurants and lodges."], "drinks": [{"name": "Local lager", "desc": "Lesotho brews its own lager in Maseru, using the clean mountain water the country is known for. A bottle costs about $1 to $2 in bars."}, {"name": "Ginger beer", "desc": "Homemade ginger beer, fizzy and sharp, sold at markets and served at gatherings. Expect $0.50 to $1 a bottle."}], "greet": "Greet people with lumela, adding ntate for a man or 'me for a woman, and shake hands. Respect elders by greeting them first.", "dress": "Bring warm layers year round, since the highlands can drop below freezing, even in summer nights. Dress modestly in villages and for Sunday church, and avoid short shorts.", "table": "Wash your hands before and after eating, and use your right hand to roll papa into a ball and scoop up moroho. Guests are usually served first, and it's polite to accept what's offered.", "faith": "Around 90% of Basotho are Christian, mainly Catholic and Protestant, and Sunday church is central to village life. Dress modestly for services, sit quietly and ask before photographing.", "phrases": [["Lumela", "Hello"], ["U phela joang?", "How are you?"], ["Ke phela hantle", "I'm fine"], ["Kea leboha", "Thank you"], ["Sala hantle", "Goodbye (stay well)"]]},
  "mauritius": {"around": "Public buses reach almost every village and cost very little, though they can be slow, stop early in the evening and get crowded. The Metro Express light rail runs from Port Louis to Curepipe and is quick and cheap. For the southwest, Le Morne and Chamarel, many travellers rent a car or scooter for a few days, and driving is on the left. Taxis are not metered, so agree the fare before getting in.", "money": "The Mauritian rupee is the currency, and cards are widely accepted in shops, restaurants and hotels, with ATMs easy to find in towns and malls. Carry some cash for buses, street food and markets. Tipping is optional and not expected at street stalls, though leaving 5 to 10 percent at a sit-down restaurant is appreciated. Haggling is normal only at markets and craft stalls, such as the ones in Port Louis.", "sleep": "Hostels are rare, so budget picks are guesthouses, self-catering apartments and small family-run hotels, especially around Grand Baie, Flic en Flac and Mahébourg. Airbnb-style apartments work well for groups. Book ahead for the December to January holidays.", "eat": ["Dholl puri: thin split-pea flatbread with curry and chutney", "Mine frit: fried noodles from Sino-Mauritian kitchens", "Gâteaux piments: crispy chili split-pea fritters", "Alouda: sweet milk drink with basil seeds and jelly"], "data": "SIMs from my.t or Emtel are easy to buy at the airport with a passport, and coverage is good across the island. Many guesthouses have decent Wi-Fi too.", "heads_up": "Wear reef shoes in shallow lagoons, since sea urchins and stonefish are common.", "food": [{"name": "Dholl puri", "wiki": "Dal puri", "desc": "Thin, soft flatbread made with ground split peas, wrapped around bean curry, rougaille and chutney. Sold from street carts for pocket change, it is the island's favourite snack.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Breakfast or lunch, from street stalls", "order": "Buy them in pairs, with extra chutney"}, {"name": "Farata", "wiki": "Roti", "desc": "Flaky, buttery flatbread served with curries, sold in pairs at street stands and markets.", "price": "$0.70 to $1.50", "when": "Lunch on the go, at markets"}, {"name": "Briyani", "wiki": "Biryani", "desc": "Spiced rice with meat, potatoes and herbs, a Mauritian favourite at weddings and festivals and served at many small canteens.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Weddings, festivals and Sunday lunch"}, {"name": "Rougaille", "wiki": "Rougaille", "desc": "Tomato-based Creole sauce cooked with fish, sausage or meat, served with rice. Homely and cheap.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Lunch at home and snack bars", "order": "Rougaille saucisse, with sausage, is a cheap favourite"}, {"name": "Mine frit", "wiki": "Chow mein", "desc": "Fried noodles from Sino-Mauritian kitchens, often with chicken or shrimp. A cheap, quick meal.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Quick lunch or dinner", "order": "Mine bouilli is the boiled noodle version"}, {"name": "Alouda", "wiki": "Falooda", "desc": "Sweet milk drink with basil seeds and agar jelly, sold at markets. Perfect on a hot day.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Hot afternoons at markets"}], "food_tip": "Street food is where Mauritius really shines, and dholl puri, gâteaux piments (chilli fritters) and farata cost very little. Port Louis's central market and the food stalls along beaches on weekends are great for cheap eats.", "culture": ["Mauritius is proudly multicultural, with Hindu, Muslim, Creole, Chinese and Franco-Mauritian communities, and religious festivals from all of them are public holidays.", "Remove shoes and dress modestly when visiting temples and mosques, covering shoulders and knees.", "Kreol is the everyday language, with French and English widely understood, so a bonzour goes down well.", "Topless sunbathing is frowned upon on public beaches, and away from the sand, cover up when walking through towns.", "If you are around for Maha Shivaratri, thousands of pilgrims walk to the sacred lake at Grand Bassin, so be respectful and patient with road closures."], "safety": "Mauritius is one of the safest places in Africa, but petty theft happens on beaches and in Port Louis, so do not leave bags unattended. Be cautious when swimming outside the lagoons, as currents can be strong. Sun protection is essential, and mosquitoes can carry dengue, so use repellent.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is often a quick street snack, dinner is around 7pm, and many small eateries close by 6pm.", "Tap water is treated, but most visitors drink bottled or filtered water, especially after heavy rain.", "Vegetarians eat very well thanks to the Hindu community, with dholl puri, curries and vegetable briyani everywhere.", "Many snack bars and market stalls are cash only, so carry small rupee notes.", "Tipping isn't required, but 5 to 10 percent in restaurants is welcome."], "drinks": [{"name": "Rhum arrangé", "desc": "Local rum infused with fruit, vanilla or spices. A glass costs $2 to $4, and bottles are sold at markets."}, {"name": "Vanilla tea", "desc": "Black tea flavoured with vanilla, grown on the island's southern plantations. A cup costs about $1."}], "greet": "Bonzour with a handshake is the usual hello, and friends kiss on the cheek. Some Hindu families greet elders with namaste.", "dress": "Light clothes are fine, but pack a layer for the cooler highlands in winter, June to September.", "table": "At family meals wait to be served, and accept what's offered. In some Hindu and Muslim homes people eat with the right hand, so follow your hosts.", "faith": "Hindus make up about half the population, alongside Christians (mostly Catholic), Muslims and Buddhists. At Grand Bassin and Hindu temples remove shoes and leather, and don't bring meat or alcohol.", "phrases": [["Bonzour", "Hello"], ["Mersi", "Thank you"], ["Komie sa?", "How much is it?"], ["Ki manier?", "How are you?"], ["Pa tro pima", "Not too spicy"]]},
  "seychelles": {"around": "Cheap public buses cover Mahé and Praslin and are a bargain compared to taxis, though they stop early in the evening. On La Digue, bikes are the main way around and can be rented for a few hundred rupees a day. Inter-island ferries link Mahé, Praslin and La Digue several times a day and are worth booking ahead in busy months. Car rental is useful on Mahé, but driving is on the left on narrow, winding roads.", "money": "The Seychelles rupee is the currency, and cards are widely accepted, with ATMs on Mahé, Praslin and La Digue. Seychelles is expensive, so budget carefully and bring a bit of cash for buses and takeaways. Haggling is not common, and many restaurants add a service charge, so check the bill before tipping.", "sleep": "There are no hostels and camping is not allowed, so budget travellers use self-catering guesthouses and small family-run places, which also let you cook and save on food. Book early for the December to January and Easter peaks.", "eat": ["Kari zourit: octopus curry with coconut milk", "Ladob: sweet plantain or breadfruit cooked in coconut milk", "Grilled fish: fresh catch with Creole sauces", "SeyBrew: the local lager"], "data": "Airtel and Cable & Wireless SIMs need a passport, and coverage is good on the inhabited islands. Many guesthouses offer Wi-Fi.", "heads_up": "Takeaway counters selling Creole plate lunches are the cheapest way to eat, much cheaper than resort restaurants.", "food": [{"name": "Kari zourit", "wiki": "Octopus as food", "desc": "Octopus curry simmered in coconut milk with spices, a Creole classic found at takeaway counters.", "price": "$6 to $10", "when": "Lunch from takeaway counters", "order": "Ask for it with rice and lentils"}, {"name": "Ladob", "wiki": "Ladob", "desc": "Plantain, breadfruit or sweet potato cooked in coconut milk with nutmeg and vanilla, often eaten as dessert.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Dessert after Sunday lunch"}, {"name": "Shark chutney", "wiki": "Shark chutney", "desc": "Boiled and mashed shark meat cooked with bilimbi fruit, onion and spices, served with rice.", "price": "$6 to $10", "when": "Lunch plates at takeaways", "order": "Squeeze on lime and add chilli"}, {"name": "Breadfruit", "wiki": "Breadfruit", "desc": "A starchy staple boiled, fried as chips or cooked into curries. Local legend says if you eat it, you will come back to Seychelles.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Side dish or roadside snack", "order": "Try it fried as crispy chips"}, {"name": "Kari sousouri", "wiki": "Seychelles fruit bat", "desc": "Fruit bat curry, a Creole delicacy for the curious eater. Rich and gamey.", "price": "$10 to $15", "when": "Special dinners at Creole places"}], "food_tip": "Takeaway counters selling Creole plate lunches are the cheapest way to eat, much cheaper than resort restaurants. Supermarkets on Mahé and Praslin are handy if your guesthouse has a kitchen.", "culture": ["Seychellois Creole is the everyday language, alongside English and French, and a friendly bonzour will get smiles.", "Seychelles is mostly Catholic, so dress modestly when visiting churches and cover up away from the beach.", "Nudity is illegal on public beaches, and topless sunbathing is frowned upon outside resorts.", "Island time is real, so expect a slower pace and do not rush people.", "Respect the environment, as many islands are protected areas, and never take shells or coral."], "safety": "Seychelles is generally very safe, but petty theft happens on beaches, so do not leave valuables unattended. Currents can be strong at some beaches, especially during the southeast monsoon from roughly May to October, so check conditions before swimming.", "eat_beats": ["Takeaway counters are busiest from noon to 1:30pm and many sell out or close by mid-afternoon.", "Tap water on Mahé and Praslin is treated and generally safe, though many visitors stick to bottled.", "Vegetarians can live on lentil curry, vegetable curry, breadfruit and tropical fruit from Victoria's market.", "A 10 percent service charge is often already on the bill at sit-down restaurants, so extra tipping is optional.", "Fish is cheapest and freshest at the main market in Victoria, early in the morning."], "drinks": [{"name": "Calou", "desc": "Palm sap left to ferment, sweet in the morning and sour and boozy by evening. A glass costs about $1 to $2 in villages."}, {"name": "Bacca", "desc": "A rough local rum made from fermented sugarcane, drunk at festivals and parties for a few dollars a glass."}], "greet": "Say bonzour with a smile and a handshake, and take a moment to chat before asking for anything.", "dress": "Swimwear is for the beach only, so throw on a shirt and shorts in Victoria and shops. Cover shoulders in churches.", "table": "Creole meals are relaxed and shared, with dishes set out for everyone. It's polite to try the chilli sauces your host has made.", "faith": "Most Seychellois are Catholic, with Anglican, Hindu and Muslim communities. Dress modestly in churches and remove shoes at the Hindu temple in Victoria.", "phrases": [["Bonzour", "Hello"], ["Mersi", "Thank you"], ["Konbyen?", "How much?"], ["Ki manyer?", "How are you?"], ["Bon apeti", "Enjoy your meal"]]},
  "cape-verde": {"around": "Aluguers (shared minivans and pickups) are the cheap way around each island, leaving from the main square or market once they fill up, and they are easiest to catch in the morning. Ferries link São Vicente and Santo Antão a couple of times daily and are the classic hop for hikers. For longer island hops, domestic flights are faster and more reliable than boats, so book them early when fares are lowest. Ferry schedules between the other islands are irregular and weather-dependent, so leave slack in your plans.", "money": "The Cape Verdean escudo is pegged to the euro, and some tourist businesses quote prices in euros too, though you usually get a better deal paying in escudos. ATMs (look for the Vinti4 network) work with Visa in towns, but Mastercard is hit and miss and smaller islands can run dry, so withdraw before heading to rural spots. Many guesthouses, aluguer drivers and local cafés take cash only. Tipping is modest, around rounding up or 5 to 10 percent at sit-down restaurants, and haggling is not really a thing outside craft stalls.", "sleep": "Pensões, residenciais and small guesthouses are the budget options, plus a few hostels in Praia and Mindelo where dorm beds are reasonably priced. Santo Antão has rural guesthouses in the valleys that suit hikers and often include dinner. Sal and Boa Vista are dominated by all-inclusive resorts, so look for guesthouses in Santa Maria or Sal Rei town instead.", "eat": ["Cachupa: hearty stew of corn, beans and meat or fish", "Pastel com diabo dentro: fried pastry with spicy tuna filling", "Queijo de cabra: fresh goat's cheese from the islands", "Grogue: strong sugarcane rum"], "data": "CVMóvel and Unitel T+ sell cheap SIMs with your passport, and coverage is good in towns but drops in deep valleys on Santo Antão and Fogo.", "heads_up": "Hiking trails on Santo Antão are steep and exposed, so carry plenty of water and start early before the heat kicks in.", "food": [{"name": "Cachupa", "wiki": "Cachupa", "desc": "Slow-cooked stew of hominy corn, beans, veg and meat or fish, the national dish. Order cachupa refogada, the fried leftovers topped with an egg, for a cheap and massive breakfast.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch, and fried leftovers for breakfast", "order": "Cachupa guisada is fried with eggs and sausage"}, {"name": "Canja de galinha", "wiki": "Canja de galinha", "desc": "Chicken and rice soup, comforting and often served at festivals, after a big night out or when someone is unwell.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Light lunch or after a big night"}, {"name": "Caldeirada", "wiki": "Caldeirada", "desc": "Hearty fish stew with potatoes and vegetables, found at local eateries near the ports. Fresh tuna and wahoo are common catches.", "price": "$6 to $12", "when": "Sunday lunch on the coast", "order": "Pick fish or a mix with octopus"}, {"name": "Queijo de cabra", "wiki": "Goat cheese", "desc": "Fresh, mild goat's cheese made on the islands, often eaten with bread or papaya jam. Fogo's is especially good.", "price": "$2 to $5 a cheese", "when": "Breakfast, with bread or papaya", "order": "Fogo cheese is the most famous"}, {"name": "Grogue", "wiki": "Grogue", "desc": "Fiery sugarcane rum, mostly distilled on Santo Antão, where you can visit small traditional distilleries. If it is too strong, try ponche, a sweeter mix with molasses.", "price": "$1 to $2 a shot", "when": "Evenings, music nights and festivals", "order": "Ask for it aged, it is smoother"}], "food_tip": "Look for a prato do dia (dish of the day) at local cafés, which is the best-value meal on the islands. Self-catering from markets and minimercados helps a lot on pricier islands like Sal.", "culture": ["Portuguese is the official language, but Kriolu is what you hear on the street, and a bom dia and obrigado or obrigada go a long way.", "Morabeza, the famous laid-back Cape Verdean hospitality, is a real thing, so expect warmth and a slower pace, and match it rather than rushing people.", "Music is everything here, from the soulful morna made famous by Cesária Évora to fast funaná, and live music nights in Mindelo are a highlight.", "Dress modestly away from the beach, especially in rural villages and churches, as most people are Catholic.", "Greetings matter, so say hello when you walk into a shop or get into an aluguer."], "safety": "Cape Verde is one of the safer countries in Africa, but petty theft and bag snatching happen in Praia and Mindelo, especially at night, so keep your phone out of sight. Sea currents can be strong, particularly on the windward beaches of Sal and Boa Vista. Dengue outbreaks occur in the rainy season, so use repellent.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is around 1pm and dinner is late, often 8pm or after on weekends.", "Don't drink tap water, and bottled water costs more on small islands, so stock up in towns.", "Vegetarians will find it tough, as cachupa often has meat, so ask for a vegetarian version or eat salads, beans and cheese.", "Fish markets on each island sell tuna, wahoo and lobster at low prices, often cooked at stalls nearby.", "Tipping 5% to 10% in restaurants is appreciated but not expected."], "drinks": [{"name": "Fogo wine", "desc": "Wine made from grapes grown in volcanic soil inside Fogo's crater. A bottle costs about $8 to $15."}, {"name": "Ponche", "desc": "Grogue sweetened with sugarcane molasses or honey, often with lime or passion fruit. About $1 to $2 a glass."}], "greet": "Shake hands or kiss on both cheeks with friends, and always say \"bom dia\" when entering a shop. In Kriolu say \"modi ki bu sta?\" (how are you?).", "dress": "Beachwear is fine at resorts, but cover up in towns. Bring a jacket for the windy islands and mountain hikes.", "table": "Meals are slow and social, and hosts will offer seconds. Say \"bom apetite\" before eating.", "faith": "Most Cape Verdeans are Catholic, and church festivals for patron saints are big events. Dress modestly in churches and stay quiet during Mass.", "phrases": [["Bom dia", "Good morning"], ["Obrigado / obrigada", "Thank you (male / female speaker)"], ["Kantu ki kusta?", "How much? in Kriolu"], ["Modi ki bu sta?", "How are you? in Kriolu"], ["Sabi", "Nice, tasty, in Kriolu"]]},
  "djibouti": {"around": "Minibuses cover Djibouti City cheaply, and shared taxis link the main towns. Lake Assal, Lac Abbé and the Day Forest need a 4x4 tour or a rented vehicle with a driver, as roads are rough and help is far away. Joining other travellers on a tour is the best way to split costs. The Djibouti to Addis Ababa railway is a good way to reach Ethiopia. Whale-shark trips in the Gulf of Tadjoura run mostly from November to January.", "money": "Djibouti is expensive for a backpacker, with prices closer to Europe than to neighbouring Ethiopia. The Djiboutian franc is pegged to the US dollar, and dollars and euros are widely accepted. ATMs exist in the capital and work with Visa and Mastercard, but cards are rarely accepted outside hotels, so carry cash. Tipping is modest, and haggling is fine at markets.", "sleep": "Budget beds are limited to simple hotels in the capital, which are pricey for what you get. At Lac Abbé and in the Goda Mountains, basic encampments with huts and shared meals are the usual stay, and they are some of the most memorable nights you will have.", "eat": ["Skoudehkaris: spiced rice cooked with lamb or goat", "Fah-fah: spicy goat soup with flatbread", "Laxoox: spongy flatbread, often eaten at breakfast", "Shaah: sweet spiced tea with milk"], "data": "Djibouti Telecom is the only operator, and SIMs need a passport. Data is relatively pricey, and coverage is limited outside the capital and main roads.", "heads_up": "Heat is extreme from May to September, so plan trips for the cooler months and carry plenty of water.", "food": [{"name": "Skoudehkaris", "wiki": "Bariis iskukaris", "desc": "Spiced rice cooked with lamb or goat and tomato, the national dish. Find it at local canteens at lunchtime.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch, as the national dish"}, {"name": "Fah-fah", "wiki": "Fah-fah", "desc": "Spicy goat soup with vegetables and chillies, eaten with flatbread.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch, with bread to dip", "order": "Usually goat or camel; ask for extra chilli"}, {"name": "Laxoox", "wiki": "Lahoh", "desc": "Spongy, sourdough flatbread, often eaten at breakfast with honey and sugar or with stew.", "price": "$0.50 to $2", "when": "Breakfast with tea", "order": "Try it with honey and sesame oil"}, {"name": "Sabaayad", "wiki": "Sabaayad", "desc": "Flaky, layered flatbread, perfect for scooping up stews and sauces.", "price": "$0.50 to $2", "when": "Breakfast, or with stews"}, {"name": "Sambusa", "wiki": "Samosa", "desc": "Fried pastries filled with spiced meat or lentils, especially popular during Ramadan.", "price": "$0.20 to $0.50", "when": "Ramadan iftar and snacks"}, {"name": "Xalwo", "wiki": "Halva", "desc": "Sweet, sticky, jelly-like dessert spiced with cardamom, served at weddings and celebrations.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Weddings, Eid and with coffee"}], "food_tip": "Eat at small Somali and Yemeni canteens in the capital, where a plate of rice and meat is far cheaper than at expat cafés. Fresh fish grilled at the waterfront is a good-value treat.", "culture": ["Djibouti is overwhelmingly Muslim, so dress modestly in town, with long trousers for men and covered shoulders and knees for women.", "Greet people with salaam aleikum, and French is widely spoken alongside Somali, Afar and Arabic.", "Chewing khat, a mildly stimulant leaf, is a huge afternoon ritual, and the city slows down when the daily shipment arrives from Ethiopia.", "During Ramadan, avoid eating, drinking or smoking in public in daylight.", "Ask before photographing people, and do not photograph military sites, the port or government buildings, as several foreign militaries are based here."], "safety": "Djibouti City is generally safe, but petty theft happens, so keep your phone and wallet out of sight. Do not travel near the Eritrean border, and check advice for the northern regions. The heat is the real danger, so drink plenty of water and avoid exertion in the middle of the day.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, around 12 to 2pm, before the city slows for the afternoon.", "Drink bottled water; tap water is not recommended.", "Vegetarians can find lentils, rice and salads, but stews nearly always have meat.", "Tipping isn't expected at canteens, but rounding up or 10 percent in restaurants is appreciated.", "From June to September the heat is intense, and many people eat late and stay indoors in the afternoon."], "drinks": [{"name": "Shaah", "desc": "Sweet, milky tea spiced with cardamom and cloves, for $0.30 to $0.50 a cup."}, {"name": "Fresh juice", "desc": "Mango, papaya and lemon juices from small cafés, about $1 to $2 a glass."}], "greet": "People greet with 'Salaam alaikum' and a handshake between men, while women often place a hand on their heart. 'Bonjour' works too.", "dress": "It's very hot, so wear light, loose cotton that covers arms and legs. Swimwear is fine at beaches and resorts but not in town.", "table": "Eat with your right hand from shared platters of rice and meat, and accept tea when it's offered. Leaving a little food signals you're satisfied.", "faith": "About 94 percent of Djiboutians are Sunni Muslim, with a small Christian community. Remove shoes and dress modestly at mosques, and don't visit during Friday prayers.", "phrases": [["Nabad", "Hello (Somali)"], ["Mahadsanid", "Thank you"], ["Waa imisa?", "How much is it?"], ["Iska warran?", "How are you?"], ["Haa / Maya", "Yes / No"]]},
  "eritrea": {"around": "Buses run from Asmara to Massawa and Keren along spectacular mountain roads, and they are cheap but basic. Foreigners need travel permits to leave Asmara, which you get from the tourism office, so plan ahead. In Asmara, shared taxis and buses are cheap, and the city is compact and walkable. The old Asmara to Massawa railway sometimes runs charter trips for groups.", "money": "Eritrea is strictly cash, foreign cards do not work, and there are no ATMs for visitors, so bring enough US dollars or euros for your whole trip. Change money only at official exchange offices or banks, as black-market trading is illegal and risky. Keep your exchange receipts, as you may need them to change money back. Tipping is modest, and haggling is light.", "sleep": "Budget stays are pensions and simple hotels in Asmara, many in classic Italian-era buildings. Massawa has a few modest hotels, but prices can be steep for what you get.", "eat": ["Zigni: spicy meat stew served on injera", "Shiro: thick chickpea stew, a cheap staple", "Ful: mashed fava beans with bread", "Macchiato: strong Italian-style coffee"], "data": "Mobile data is very limited, roaming is rare, and you should expect internet only through slow Wi-Fi cafés and some hotels.", "heads_up": "Avoid photographing military sites or infrastructure.", "food": [{"name": "Zigni", "wiki": "Zigni", "desc": "Spicy beef or lamb stew cooked with berbere spice, served on injera. The dish you will see on every local menu.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch or dinner, on injera", "order": "Ask for less berbere if spicy food worries you"}, {"name": "Shiro", "wiki": "Shiro (food)", "desc": "Thick chickpea stew, cheap and filling, especially on fasting days.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Lunch, especially Wednesdays and Fridays"}, {"name": "Injera", "wiki": "Injera", "desc": "Spongy sourdough flatbread, the base of most meals.", "price": "Included with meals", "when": "With every meal"}, {"name": "Ful", "wiki": "Ful medames", "desc": "Mashed fava beans with chilli, onion and oil, eaten for breakfast with bread.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast, at local cafes", "order": "Topped with yoghurt, chilli and an egg"}, {"name": "Fit-fit", "wiki": "Fit-fit", "desc": "Shredded flatbread mixed with spices and butter, a filling breakfast.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Breakfast, at home and cafes"}, {"name": "Macchiato", "wiki": "Caffè macchiato", "desc": "Strong Italian-style coffee served at Asmara's art deco cafés, a legacy of the colonial era.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Morning and evening passeggiata"}], "food_tip": "Local eateries in Asmara serve cheap plates of zigni and shiro, and the old cafés along Harnet Avenue are perfect for a cheap macchiato and a pastry. On Wednesdays and Fridays many Orthodox Christians fast, so vegan dishes are everywhere.", "culture": ["Eritrea is a mix of Orthodox Christian and Muslim communities, so dress modestly and remove shoes when entering mosques.", "Coffee ceremonies are a social ritual, and being invited is an honour, so accept if you can.", "Greetings are warm and often involve a handshake with a shoulder bump between friends, and selam (hello) is a good start. Asmara's evening passeggiata along Harnet Avenue is a great way to see local life.", "Avoid discussing politics, the government or military service, as it can put people in a difficult position.", "Ask before photographing people, and never photograph military sites or infrastructure."], "safety": "Eritrea has low crime, but travel restrictions and photography rules are strictly enforced, so carry your permits and passport. Avoid border areas with Ethiopia, Djibouti and Sudan. Health facilities are basic, so bring a good first aid kit and travel insurance.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is around 1pm, and dinner after 7pm, with cafes busy in late afternoon.", "Don't drink tap water; bottled water is widely available in Asmara.", "Vegetarians do well on fasting days, when shiro, lentils and vegetable stews are everywhere.", "Italian influence means pasta and pizza are common in Asmara.", "Tipping around 10 percent is appreciated in restaurants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Suwa", "desc": "Home-brewed barley and sorghum beer, cloudy and slightly sour, served at gatherings for under $1."}, {"name": "Asmara beer", "desc": "The local lager, brewed in Asmara since the colonial era, about $1 to $2 a bottle."}], "greet": "Greetings are warm and often include a handshake with a shoulder bump. Saying \"selam\" (hello) and asking about the person's family is polite.", "dress": "Dress conservatively, covering shoulders and knees, especially at religious sites. Asmara is fairly stylish, and locals dress neatly.", "table": "Eat with your right hand, tearing injera to scoop food. A host may feed you a mouthful by hand (gursha), a sign of respect, so accept it.", "faith": "Eritrea is roughly half Christian, mostly Orthodox, and half Muslim. Remove shoes at mosques and churches, and dress modestly.", "phrases": [["Selam (ሰላም)", "Hello"], ["Yekenyeley (የቐንየለይ)", "Thank you (Tigrinya)"], ["Kendey? (ክንደይ)", "How much?"], ["Tsibuk (ጽቡቕ)", "Good"], ["Dehan kun (ደሓን ኩን)", "Goodbye"]]},
  "somalia": {"around": "In Somaliland, travel outside Hargeisa, including to the Laas Geel cave paintings and Berbera, has generally required an armed police escort, so most visitors book a local guide and driver. Within Hargeisa, taxis are cheap. Check current advice before any plans, and be aware that most of the rest of Somalia is off-limits for independent travel.", "money": "In Somaliland, the Somaliland shilling is used for small purchases, but US dollars and mobile money like ZAAD are king. Money changers sit on the street with huge stacks of shillings, which is quite a sight. Bring clean, newer dollar notes, since ATMs for foreign cards are rare. Tipping is not expected, and haggling is normal at markets.", "sleep": "There are no hostels, so visitors stay in hotels in Hargeisa, many arranged through guides, with prices that are reasonable by regional standards.", "eat": ["Canjeero: spongy flatbread eaten at breakfast", "Bariis iskukaris: spiced rice with meat", "Camel milk: fresh or slightly soured, a local favorite", "Shaah: sweet spiced tea"], "data": "Telesom SIMs are cheap in Somaliland with a passport, and coverage is good in towns.", "heads_up": "Dress modestly and respect local customs, especially around prayer times.", "food": [{"name": "Canjeero", "wiki": "Lahoh", "desc": "Spongy flatbread eaten at breakfast with honey, sugar or stew.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Breakfast, with tea", "order": "Try it with suqaar or with honey and ghee"}, {"name": "Bariis iskukaris", "wiki": "Bariis iskukaris", "desc": "Spiced rice with meat, usually goat or camel, often served with a banana on the side.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch, the main meal", "order": "Eat the banana with the rice, like locals"}, {"name": "Suqaar", "wiki": "Suqaar", "desc": "Small pieces of meat cooked with vegetables and spices, eaten with flatbread or rice.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Breakfast or dinner", "order": "Ask for camel or goat"}, {"name": "Malawax", "wiki": "Malawah", "desc": "Sweet, crepe-like flatbread, a popular breakfast.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Breakfast, with tea"}, {"name": "Camel milk", "wiki": "Camel milk", "desc": "Fresh or slightly soured, sold at markets and a source of national pride.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 a glass", "when": "Any time, from market sellers", "order": "Try it fresh or slightly soured"}, {"name": "Xalwo", "wiki": "Halva", "desc": "Sweet, sticky, jelly-like dessert, often served at weddings and Eid.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Weddings, Eid and with coffee"}], "food_tip": "Local canteens serve big plates of rice or pasta with meat for very little, and the tea shops are where everyone hangs out. Hargeisa's markets are good for fruit, bread and snacks.", "culture": ["Somaliland is Muslim and conservative, so dress modestly, with women covering their hair and wearing loose, long clothing.", "Greetings are important, so say asalaamu calaykum and take time for small talk and tea before getting down to business.", "Alcohol is banned, so do not bring any in and do not go looking for it.", "During Ramadan, avoid eating, drinking or smoking in public in daylight.", "Ask before photographing people, especially women, and avoid political discussions.", "Somaliland is a self-declared state that most countries do not formally recognise, and people feel strongly about it, so be thoughtful when talking about Somalia and Somaliland."], "safety": "Most governments advise against all travel to Somalia because of terrorism, kidnapping and armed conflict, and that includes Mogadishu. Somaliland has been much calmer for years and sees a trickle of visitors, but check current advice, use a reputable local guide and follow escort rules outside Hargeisa. Petty crime is low, but the heat is brutal, so carry plenty of water, and make sure your insurance actually covers you.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch, eaten around 1pm, is the big meal, and Somali restaurants serve rice or pasta with meat and a banana.", "In Hargeisa, local canteens are cheap and busy, but women may eat in a separate family section.", "Don't drink tap water; bottled water is easy to find in towns.", "Vegetarian food is very limited, so ask for beans (digir) or vegetables and expect rice, bread and fruit.", "Tipping isn't expected at local places, but tipping your driver and guard is customary."], "drinks": [{"name": "Shaah", "desc": "Sweet, spiced black tea with cardamom, cinnamon and cloves, often with milk. A glass costs about $0.20 to $0.50."}, {"name": "Fresh fruit juice", "desc": "Mango, papaya, guava and lemon juices are blended fresh at juice bars. Expect $0.50 to $1.50 a glass."}], "greet": "Say asalaamu calaykum, and men shake hands with men. Don't offer your hand to women unless they offer first.", "dress": "Dress conservatively: men in long trousers, and women in loose, long clothing with a headscarf. Many Somali men wear a macawiis, a wrap-around sarong, in daily life.", "table": "Eat with your right hand, and accept tea when offered, as hospitality is deeply valued. In more traditional settings, men and women often eat separately.", "faith": "Somalia and Somaliland are almost entirely Sunni Muslim. Non-Muslims are rarely invited into mosques, so ask first, dress modestly and remove shoes.", "phrases": [["Iska warran?", "How are you?"], ["Mahadsanid", "Thank you"], ["Waa imisa?", "How much is it?"], ["Haa / Maya", "Yes / No"], ["Nabad gelyo", "Goodbye (go in peace)"]]},
  "sao-tome": {"around": "Shared yellow taxis and minibuses run along the main roads from the capital, leaving when full, and they are cheap but slow. For the wild south coast and Obô park, hiring a driver for the day saves a lot of time, especially if you split it with others. Flights to Príncipe are the only reliable link, as boats are irregular, so book them well ahead. Hitching is common and generally friendly here.", "money": "Bring cash in euros, since cards are rarely accepted outside a few hotels and ATMs that take foreign cards are few and unreliable. The dobra is pegged to the euro, and many guesthouses and drivers quote prices in euros anyway, so carry small notes. Tipping is modest, and haggling is gentle and good-humoured at markets.", "sleep": "Guesthouses, simple hotels and converted roças (old cocoa plantation estates) are the budget options, and staying at a roça is a great way to see the island's history. Príncipe has fewer cheap beds, so book early.", "eat": ["Calulu: smoked fish stew with greens and palm oil", "Grilled fish: fresh catch with banana or breadfruit", "Chocolate: local cocoa, sold as bars at roças", "Vinho de palma: fresh palm wine"], "data": "CST and Unitel SIMs are cheap with a passport, and coverage is fair in towns but limited in the south and on Príncipe.", "heads_up": "Malaria is present, so use prevention and repellent, especially in the rainy season.", "food": [{"name": "Calulu", "wiki": "Calulu", "desc": "Smoked fish or chicken stewed with greens, herbs and palm oil, usually served with rice or banana. The national dish, often cooked for hours.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Sunday lunch and celebrations", "order": "It takes hours, so order a day ahead"}, {"name": "Banana chips", "wiki": "Banana chips", "desc": "Crispy fried banana or breadfruit slices sold in bags by street vendors, perfect for road trips.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Snack, from street sellers"}, {"name": "Fruta-pão", "wiki": "Breadfruit", "desc": "Breadfruit roasted, boiled or fried, a cheap staple found everywhere.", "price": "$0.50 to $2", "when": "With grilled fish, any meal"}, {"name": "Chocolate", "wiki": "Chocolate", "desc": "São Tomé grows some of the world's finest cocoa, and you can buy bars made from it at roças and local chocolate makers.", "price": "$3 to $8", "when": "Bought at plantations and in town", "order": "Try a high-cocoa bar made on the island"}, {"name": "Vinho de palma", "wiki": "Palm wine", "desc": "Fresh palm wine, tapped from palms in villages, sweet in the morning and sourer by evening.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "Afternoons in villages"}], "food_tip": "Eat at local eateries and markets, where grilled fish with banana or breadfruit costs very little. Fruit is everywhere and cheap, so stock up.", "culture": ["Leve-leve (take it easy) is the national motto, so slow down and enjoy the relaxed pace.", "Portuguese is the official language, with Forro and other creoles widely spoken, so learn a few basics like bom dia and obrigado.", "Greetings matter, so say hello when entering shops and taxis.", "Most people are Catholic, so dress modestly at churches and in villages.", "Ask before photographing people, especially in fishing villages."], "safety": "São Tomé is one of the safest countries in Africa, with very little crime, though petty theft can happen in the capital. Roads are narrow and winding, so be careful driving, especially at night. Sea turtles nest on the beaches from roughly November to March, so follow local guides' rules when watching them.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch around midday is the main meal, and outside the capital many places close early in the evening.", "Drink bottled water only.", "Vegetarians can live on fruit, breadfruit, banana and rice, but most cooked dishes include fish.", "Grilled fish is the go-to meal at beaches and markets, so agree on the price before it's cooked.", "Tipping isn't expected, but rounding up or leaving 10 percent in restaurants is welcome."], "drinks": [{"name": "Cacharamba", "desc": "A strong, cheap local cane spirit, sold by the glass for under $1."}, {"name": "São Tomé coffee", "desc": "The islands grow arabica and robusta on old plantations, and a strong cup costs about $1 in cafés."}], "greet": "Shake hands and say bom dia, boa tarde or boa noite depending on the time. Friends kiss on both cheeks.", "dress": "Light, casual clothes are fine, but cover up away from the beach. Bring rain gear for the forested south.", "table": "Wait for your host to start, and accept seconds if offered. Saying bom apetite to others is common.", "faith": "Most São Tomeans are Catholic, with growing evangelical churches. Stay quiet during mass and dress modestly in churches.", "phrases": [["Bom dia", "Good morning"], ["Obrigado / Obrigada", "Thank you (said by men / women)"], ["Quanto custa?", "How much is it?"], ["Leve-leve", "Take it easy"], ["Bom apetite", "Enjoy your meal"]]},
  "usa": {"around": "In big cities like New York, Chicago and San Francisco, public transport is cheap and good, and Uber and Lyft work almost everywhere. Between cities, Greyhound, FlixBus and Megabus are the cheapest options, and Amtrak trains are scenic but slower and pricier unless you book well ahead. For national parks and the Southwest, renting a car is by far the most practical option, and splitting it with others keeps costs down. Book campsites at popular parks months in advance, and check whether a park needs a timed-entry reservation.", "money": "Cards and phone payments work almost everywhere, though a little cash helps for small diners, tips and the odd cash-only spot. Listed prices usually exclude sales tax, which varies by state and city and gets added at the till. Tipping is part of the wage system, so leave 18 to 20 percent at sit-down restaurants, a dollar or two per drink at bars, and something for tour guides and hostel housekeepers. Use a no-fee card to avoid foreign transaction charges, and haggling is not a thing outside flea markets.", "sleep": "Hostels exist in most big cities and some gateway towns, with dorm beds that are cheap by American standards but pricier than most of the world. On the road, budget motels and campgrounds are the cheapest options, and free dispersed camping is allowed on much public land in the West. Airbnb and couchsurfing can also save money in pricey cities.", "eat": ["New York pizza: thin, foldable slice sold by the piece", "Bagel: boiled then baked bread ring, often with cream cheese", "Breakfast burrito: tortilla with eggs, potatoes, chili and cheese", "Navajo frybread: puffy fried dough, served sweet or as a taco"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from T-Mobile, AT&T or Verizon need no registration, and eSIMs are easy to set up. Expect no signal in many canyons and remote parks.", "heads_up": "Medical care is extremely expensive, so travel insurance is essential, and carry plenty of water when hiking in desert heat.", "food": [{"name": "Burger", "wiki": "Hamburger", "desc": "The classic American meal, from fast-food chains to legendary local diners. A good burger with fries at a diner is filling and relatively cheap.", "price": "$8 to $15", "when": "Lunch, diners and backyard cookouts", "order": "You'll be asked how you want it cooked"}, {"name": "Barbecue", "wiki": "Barbecue in the United States", "desc": "Slow-smoked brisket, ribs and pulled pork, with regional styles in Texas, Kansas City, Memphis and the Carolinas. Worth the queue at a proper joint.", "price": "$15 to $25", "when": "Weekends, best in Texas and the South", "order": "Brisket in Texas, pulled pork in Carolina, ribs in Memphis"}, {"name": "Bagel", "wiki": "Bagel", "desc": "Chewy boiled-then-baked bread ring, best in New York with cream cheese or lox. A cheap, filling breakfast.", "price": "$3 to $8", "when": "Breakfast, especially in New York", "order": "Get an everything bagel with cream cheese, called a schmear"}, {"name": "Breakfast burrito", "wiki": "Breakfast burrito", "desc": "Tortilla stuffed with eggs, potatoes, chilli and cheese, a Southwest specialty that keeps you going all morning.", "price": "$7 to $12", "when": "Breakfast in the Southwest and California", "order": "In New Mexico, choose red or green chile, or 'Christmas'"}, {"name": "Buffalo wings", "wiki": "Buffalo wing", "desc": "Spicy fried chicken wings tossed in hot sauce, served with blue cheese dip. A sports-bar staple, often cheap on wing nights.", "price": "$12 to $18", "when": "Game days at sports bars", "order": "Comes with celery and blue cheese dip"}, {"name": "Hot dog", "wiki": "Hot dog", "desc": "Sold from street carts, ballparks and gas stations, with regional styles like Chicago's loaded dog. One of the cheapest meals going.", "price": "$3 to $7", "when": "Ballgames and street carts", "order": "Chicago style never has ketchup"}], "food_tip": "Grocery stores like Trader Joe's, Aldi and Walmart are your budget friends, and hostel kitchens save a fortune. Happy hours offer cheap food and drinks, and food trucks are great value in cities.", "culture": ["Tipping is not optional in the US, as service workers rely on it for their income, so factor it into your budget.", "Americans tend to be friendly and chatty, so expect small talk from strangers, and a simple how are you is usually just a greeting.", "The legal drinking age is 21, and you will often be asked for ID, so carry your passport or a photo ID when going out.", "Open containers of alcohol in public are illegal in most places, so keep it in bars, restaurants or your accommodation.", "Personal space is valued, so do not stand too close, and queue patiently."], "safety": "Most of the US is safe for travellers, but some city neighbourhoods have higher crime, so ask locals or your hostel for advice and avoid walking alone late at night. Medical care is extremely expensive, so travel insurance is essential. In national parks, follow wildlife rules, carry plenty of water and tell someone your plans before hiking.", "eat_beats": ["Meals run early, with lunch around noon and dinner from 6pm, though big cities stay open late.", "Tip 18 to 20 percent at sit-down restaurants and $1 to $2 per drink at bars; tips at counters are optional.", "Prices on menus and shelves usually exclude sales tax, which adds a few percent at the till.", "Tap water is safe and served free with ice in restaurants.", "Portions are big, so sharing or taking leftovers home in a box is completely normal."], "drinks": [{"name": "Sweet tea", "desc": "Iced black tea loaded with sugar, the default drink across the South. Usually $2 to $3, often with free refills."}, {"name": "Craft beer", "desc": "Almost every town has a local brewery, and hoppy IPAs are the American favourite. A pint costs $7 to $10 in a bar."}], "greet": "A casual 'Hi' or 'Hey, how's it going?' works almost everywhere, with a firm handshake in formal settings. Friends often hug.", "dress": "Casual clothes are fine almost everywhere, but bring a layer for strong air conditioning. Shoes and shirts are required in most shops and restaurants.", "table": "Many Americans cut food with the knife, then switch the fork to the right hand to eat. Hands are fine for burgers, pizza, ribs and wings, and servers bring the check when you ask.", "faith": "Christianity is the largest faith, but a growing share of people have no religion, and every major world religion has communities here. At churches, mosques, temples and Native American sacred sites, follow posted rules and don't photograph ceremonies without permission.", "phrases": [["Y'all", "You all (the South)"], ["How's it going?", "Hi, how are you?"], ["To go", "Takeaway"], ["The check", "The bill"], ["Restroom", "Toilet"]]},
  "canada": {"around": "Distances are vast, so most people fly between regions, with budget airlines like Flair and Porter offering cheap fares if you book ahead. In the Rockies, renting a car gives you the most freedom, but buses connect Vancouver, Banff and Jasper. VIA Rail links Toronto, Montreal and Quebec City, and the cross-country train is an epic trip if you have the time. Book Banff and Jasper beds and Parks Canada campsites early for summer, and use the park shuttle to reach Moraine Lake.", "money": "Cards and tap payments are accepted almost everywhere, so you can get by with very little cash. Prices exclude sales taxes, which are added at the till and vary by province, so expect to pay 5 to 15 percent more than the sticker. Tipping 15 to 20 percent in restaurants and bars is expected, and haggling is not a thing.", "sleep": "HI hostels and independent dorms cover the main cities and Rockies towns, and Parks Canada campgrounds are the cheapest option in summer. Book well ahead for summer in Banff, Jasper and Tofino, as everything fills up.", "eat": ["Poutine: fries topped with cheese curds and gravy", "Montreal smoked meat: spiced brisket piled high on rye bread", "Butter tart: small pastry with a sweet, gooey filling", "Nanaimo bar: no-bake layered bar of crumb, custard and chocolate"], "data": "Canadian mobile plans are pricey, so an eSIM is often the simplest choice, and coverage is good in towns but absent on many mountain highways.", "heads_up": "Bears live throughout the Rockies, so make noise on trails, carry bear spray, and never leave food out.", "food": [{"name": "Poutine", "wiki": "Poutine", "desc": "Fries topped with cheese curds and gravy, the ultimate Canadian comfort food. Best in Quebec, especially late at night.", "price": "$7 to $14", "when": "Late night, at casse-croûtes", "order": "Fresh, squeaky curds are the sign of a good one"}, {"name": "Montreal smoked meat", "wiki": "Montreal-style smoked meat", "desc": "Spiced brisket piled high on rye bread with mustard, a Montreal institution.", "price": "$10 to $16", "when": "Lunch, at Montreal delis", "order": "Order it \"medium\" for the best fat to meat ratio"}, {"name": "Montreal bagel", "wiki": "Montreal-style bagel", "desc": "Smaller, sweeter and denser than New York bagels, boiled in honey water and baked in wood-fired ovens.", "price": "$1 to $2 each", "when": "Breakfast, fresh from the oven", "order": "Sesame or poppy seed are the classics"}, {"name": "Butter tart", "wiki": "Butter tart", "desc": "Small pastry with a sweet, gooey filling of butter, sugar and eggs, sold at bakeries across Ontario.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Coffee break, at bakeries", "order": "Raisins or no raisins is a heated debate"}, {"name": "Nanaimo bar", "wiki": "Nanaimo bar", "desc": "No-bake layered bar of crumb, custard and chocolate, found in cafés across the country.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Coffee break, at cafes"}, {"name": "Caesar", "wiki": "Caesar (cocktail)", "desc": "Canada's national cocktail, made with vodka, Clamato juice and spices, served with a celery stick.", "price": "$8 to $14", "when": "Weekend brunch or bar nights", "order": "Ask for extra spicy, often with a pickled bean"}], "food_tip": "Supermarkets like No Frills and Superstore are the cheapest for groceries, and hostel kitchens save money. Tim Hortons is the go-to for cheap coffee and doughnuts.", "culture": ["Canadians are famously polite, so say please, thank you and sorry, and queue patiently.", "In Quebec, French is the main language, so greet people with bonjour before switching to English.", "Indigenous peoples (First Nations, Inuit and Métis) are central to Canada's story, so be respectful, learn whose land you are on, and buy art from Indigenous-owned businesses rather than knock-offs.", "Do not mistake Canadians for Americans, as it can be a sore point.", "Canadians love the outdoors and take Leave No Trace seriously, so pack out your rubbish, stay on trails and never feed wildlife."], "safety": "Canada is very safe, but wildlife, extreme weather and long distances are the main risks. In summer, wildfire smoke can affect air quality and close roads or parks, so check updates. In winter, roads can be dangerous, so drive carefully and carry supplies.", "eat_beats": ["Canadians eat early, with lunch around noon and dinner around 6pm.", "Tap water is safe and free, and restaurants bring it without being asked.", "Vegetarian and vegan food is easy to find in cities, and Toronto and Vancouver have some of the best Asian food outside Asia.", "Listed prices exclude sales tax, so expect 5 to 15 percent more at the till.", "Tipping 15 to 20 percent at restaurants is expected, as servers rely on it."], "drinks": [{"name": "Ice wine", "desc": "A sweet dessert wine made from grapes frozen on the vine, mainly in Ontario's Niagara region. A tasting glass costs $5 to $15."}, {"name": "Double-double", "desc": "Coffee with two creams and two sugars, the classic Canadian order at donut shops, about $2."}], "greet": "A handshake and a smile is normal, and friends hug. In Quebec, friends often kiss on both cheeks.", "dress": "Dress is casual and outdoorsy, but winter needs a proper parka, hat and gloves. Layers are key, as weather changes fast.", "table": "Table manners are relaxed and similar to the US. Bringing something to a dinner invite, like wine or dessert, is appreciated.", "faith": "Canada is diverse, with Christians the largest group and many people not religious, plus big Muslim, Sikh, Hindu and Jewish communities. Some Indigenous sites are sacred, so follow posted guidance.", "phrases": [["Eh?", "Right? or Isn't it?"], ["Toque", "Woolly winter hat"], ["Loonie / Toonie", "$1 / $2 coin"], ["Bonjour, hi", "Bilingual greeting in Montreal"], ["Merci", "Thank you (French)"]]},
  "belize": {"around": "Old repurposed school buses run the main highways out of Belize City, and they're cheap, loud and surprisingly frequent, with the run west to San Ignacio costing just a few dollars. Water taxis go from Belize City to Caye Caulker and San Pedro several times a day, and on the cayes you walk, cycle or hop in a golf cart. There's no Uber or similar, so use licensed taxis with green plates and agree the fare before you get in. Short Maya Island Air and Tropic Air hops are handy for the far south but cost a lot more than the bus.", "money": "The Belize dollar is pegged at 2 to 1 with the US dollar, and US cash is taken pretty much everywhere, though you'll usually get change in Belize dollars, so always check which currency a price is in. Cards work at bigger hotels, dive shops and tour operators, but small shops, street food and buses are cash only. ATMs at Belize Bank and Atlantic Bank take foreign cards, and Caye Caulker has a couple, but they can run dry on busy weekends. Tipping 10 to 15% is normal in tourist spots and for guides, and haggling isn't really a thing outside the odd craft stall.", "sleep": "Belize is pricier than its neighbours, so expect dorms to cost noticeably more than in Guatemala or Mexico. Caye Caulker and San Ignacio have the best spread of hostels and simple guesthouses, and Hopkins and Placencia have a few cheap cabanas. Island beds fill up fast around Easter, Christmas and long weekends, so book ahead then.", "eat": ["Stew chicken with rice and beans: the national everyday plate", "Fry jacks: puffy fried dough eaten for breakfast with beans", "Garnaches: crisp tortillas topped with beans, cabbage and cheese", "Hudut: Garifuna fish in coconut broth with mashed plantain"], "data": "Digi (Belize Telemedia) and Smart SIMs are cheap and easy to grab in town with your passport, and both work well on the cayes and main highways. Signal drops out in the jungle around caves and ruins, and hostel wifi is usually fine for messaging but slow for anything heavy.", "heads_up": "Caves like Actun Tunichil Muknal can only be visited with a licensed guide, so book a proper tour rather than trying to go it alone.", "food": [{"name": "Stew chicken with rice and beans", "wiki": "Rice and beans", "desc": "The everyday national plate: chicken stewed dark with recado spice, served with coconut-cooked rice and beans and usually potato salad. Look for it at lunch counters around town, often under US$7.", "price": "$5 to $8", "when": "Lunch at local cafes", "order": "Add fried plantain on the side"}, {"name": "Fry jacks", "wiki": "Fry jack", "desc": "Puffy squares or triangles of fried dough eaten at breakfast with refried beans, eggs and cheese, or drizzled with honey. Cheap and very filling before a day out on the water.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Breakfast", "order": "Order them with beans, eggs and cheese"}, {"name": "Garnaches", "wiki": "Garnacha (food)", "desc": "Crispy fried tortillas piled with refried beans, shredded cabbage and crumbly cheese. Street stalls sell them a few for a couple of Belize dollars.", "price": "$1 to $2 for three", "when": "Evening snack from street stalls", "order": "Add habanero onion relish"}, {"name": "Salbutes", "wiki": "Salbute", "desc": "Soft, puffed fried tortillas topped with shredded chicken, cabbage, tomato and onion. A Maya-influenced snack that's dirt cheap from markets and roadside stands.", "price": "$1 to $2 for three", "when": "Snack from markets and roadside stands"}, {"name": "Ceviche", "wiki": "Ceviche", "desc": "Fresh fish, shrimp or conch tossed with lime, onion, tomato and cilantro, best eaten on the cayes during conch season, roughly October to June. Served with tortilla chips and ideally a cold Belikin.", "price": "$6 to $12", "when": "Lunch on the cayes", "order": "Conch is only served in season, October to June"}], "food_tip": "Lunch is the big meal, so hunt down the set rice and beans plates at local cafes and market stalls rather than tourist restaurants. On Caye Caulker, the evening street grills are cheaper than sit-down seafood places.", "culture": ["English is the official language, but you'll hear Kriol, Spanish, Garifuna and Maya languages everywhere, and Belizeans are proud of that mix. A friendly \"good morning\" before diving into a question goes a long way.", "Life on the cayes runs on a \"go slow\" attitude, so don't expect anything to happen quickly and don't get stroppy about it.", "Beachwear is fine on the beach, but cover up a bit when walking around town or popping into shops, and topless sunbathing isn't accepted.", "Garifuna Settlement Day on November 19 is a big celebration in Dangriga and Hopkins, with drumming and dancing, and it's a great time to visit if you respect that it's a cultural holiday, not a party put on for tourists.", "Ask before photographing people, especially in Maya and Mennonite communities, where many prefer not to be photographed at all.", "Tip guides and boat crews directly if they've looked after you, as it makes up a real part of their income."], "safety": "Most travellers pass through Belize City quickly; it's fine in the daytime around the water taxi terminal, but take a taxi after dark rather than walking. On the cayes, petty theft from dorms and beaches is the main issue, so use lockers. Dengue is around, so pack repellent, and only drink filtered or bottled water outside the main towns. Sand flies on the beaches are relentless at dawn and dusk, so cover up.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is early, lunch from noon is the big meal, and many local cafes close by late afternoon.", "Tap water in Belize City and big towns is treated, but bottled or filtered water is safer in rural areas.", "Vegetarians can eat rice and beans, fry jacks and salads, but beans are often cooked with lard, so ask.", "Many restaurants add a service charge, and if not, tip 10 to 15 percent.", "Prices are often in Belize dollars, which are fixed at two to one US dollar, so check which currency you're being quoted."], "drinks": [{"name": "Seaweed shake", "desc": "A thick blend of dried seaweed, condensed milk, cinnamon and nutmeg, sold at stalls for about $2 to $4."}, {"name": "Cashew wine", "desc": "A sweet homemade wine from cashew fruit, a speciality of Crooked Tree village, for $5 to $10 a bottle."}], "greet": "A friendly good morning or good afternoon and a handshake is the norm. In Kriol, people often say \"wah gwaan\".", "dress": "Beachwear is fine on the sand, but cover up in towns, shops and churches. Bring long sleeves for jungle trips and insects.", "table": "Meals are relaxed, with hot sauce on every table. Wait to be invited to sit and eat in someone's home.", "faith": "Most Belizeans are Christian, mainly Catholic and Protestant, with Mennonite communities and Garifuna and Maya spiritual traditions. At Maya sites and sacred caves, stay on paths and never touch artefacts.", "phrases": [["Wah gwaan?", "What's up? (Kriol)"], ["Tanks", "Thanks (Kriol)"], ["Hoh mohch?", "How much? (Kriol)"], ["Go slow", "Take it easy"], ["Hola, buenas", "Hello (Spanish, widely spoken)"]]},
  "el-salvador": {"around": "Chicken buses are crazy cheap, usually well under a dollar a ride, but slow and packed, while tourist shuttles link San Salvador, El Tunco, the Ruta de las Flores and Santa Ana for more money and less hassle. Uber and inDrive work well in the capital and are the easiest way around San Salvador. The country is tiny, so most places are within a few hours of each other. Book shuttles a day ahead in surf season and on holiday weekends.", "money": "El Salvador uses the US dollar, so there's nothing to change if you're coming with greenbacks. Bitcoin is still accepted at a handful of places but you won't need it. Cards work at bigger restaurants and hotels in San Salvador and El Tunco, but carry small bills for buses, pupuserías and beach towns, as nobody wants to break a 20. ATMs are easy to find, though most charge a few dollars per withdrawal, and tipping around 10% is common in sit-down restaurants, sometimes already added to the bill.", "sleep": "El Tunco and El Zonte are packed with surf hostels and cheap guesthouses, and dorms are generally good value compared with Costa Rica. On the Ruta de las Flores, Juayúa and Ataco have small, chilled hostels, and San Salvador has a few options in safe neighbourhoods like San Benito and Escalón.", "eat": ["Pupusas: thick corn tortillas stuffed with cheese, beans or pork", "Yuca frita: fried cassava with pork crackling and pickled cabbage", "Panes con pollo: sandwich of chicken stewed in tomato sauce", "Horchata: sweet rice and morro seed drink with cinnamon"], "data": "Claro and Tigo SIMs are cheap and easy to buy at the airport or any mall with your passport, and coverage is solid across this small country, including the surf beaches.", "heads_up": "Rip currents are strong along much of the Pacific coast, so only swim where locals do and check conditions with surfers first.", "food": [{"name": "Pupusas", "wiki": "Pupusa", "desc": "Thick corn or rice-flour tortillas stuffed with cheese, beans, pork or loroco flowers, griddled and served with curtido and tomato salsa. Usually under a dollar each at any pupusería, and Sunday nights are pupusa night.", "price": "$0.50 to $1 each", "when": "Evenings, and especially Sunday nights", "order": "Revuelta mixes pork, beans and cheese"}, {"name": "Curtido", "wiki": "Curtido", "desc": "Tangy fermented cabbage slaw with carrot, onion and oregano that comes heaped on the side of every pupusa plate. It's free, so pile it on.", "price": "Free with pupusas", "when": "On every pupusería table"}, {"name": "Sopa de pata", "wiki": "Sopa de pata", "desc": "A rich, filling soup of cow's feet, tripe, yuca, corn and plantain. It's a local hangover cure, sold at markets and comedores for a few dollars.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Weekend mornings, a hangover cure"}, {"name": "Tamales", "wiki": "Tamale", "desc": "Salvadoran tamales come wrapped in banana leaves, filled with chicken or pork, or sweet corn versions called tamales de elote. Look for them at markets and from women selling door to door in the morning.", "price": "$0.75 to $1.50 each", "when": "Breakfast or with coffee, especially weekends", "order": "Ask for tamales de pollo"}, {"name": "Horchata", "wiki": "Horchata", "desc": "The Salvadoran version uses ground morro seeds, rice and cinnamon, giving it a nuttier taste than the Mexican kind. Served ice cold in a bag with a straw from street vendors for under a dollar.", "price": "$0.50 to $1.50", "when": "With lunch, or from street vendors", "order": "Salvadoran horchata uses morro seeds"}, {"name": "Atol de elote", "wiki": "Atole", "desc": "A thick, warm, sweet corn drink that's perfect on cooler evenings in the highlands. Sold by street vendors and at markets for pocket change.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Afternoons when corn is in season"}], "food_tip": "Pupusas are your budget best friend: three or four with curtido make a full meal for a couple of dollars. Comedores serving set lunches (comida a la vista) are the other cheap bet.", "culture": ["Salvadorans are famously friendly and polite, so greet people with a \"buenos días\" or \"buenas\" before asking for anything, and you'll find people genuinely happy to help.", "The country is mostly Catholic with a growing evangelical population, so dress modestly in churches, with shoulders and knees covered.", "Security has changed hugely in recent years and there's a heavy police and military presence. Be respectful at checkpoints, carry a copy of your passport, and don't photograph soldiers or police.", "Large or face tattoos can draw extra attention from police because of past gang associations, so it's worth keeping them covered in some situations.", "Eat pupusas with your hands, it's the done thing, and tearing them up with a fork marks you out as a newbie.", "Prices in markets are mostly fair and fixed, so heavy haggling isn't expected, though a little friendly bargaining for crafts is fine."], "safety": "Crime has dropped dramatically and most travellers now feel comfortable, but use normal big city sense in San Salvador and take Uber at night. Rip currents on the Pacific beaches are seriously strong, so swim where locals and surfers point you. Don't hike volcanoes like Santa Ana without the official guided group, and drink bottled or filtered water.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is often eggs, beans and plantains, lunch from noon to 2pm, and dinner around 7pm.", "Don't drink tap water, and buy big bottles or bags of purified water.", "Vegetarians can order pupusas de queso, de frijol or de loroco, a local edible flower.", "Markets in towns sell cheap fruit, and street vendors sell cut mango with lime, salt and chilli.", "A 10% tip is often added to the bill in sit-down restaurants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Ensalada", "desc": "Not a salad but a fruit punch with finely chopped pineapple, apple and mango floating in juice. About $0.50 to $1 a glass."}, {"name": "Salvadoran coffee", "desc": "El Salvador grows excellent coffee, served strong in cafés around the Ruta de las Flores. About $1 to $2 a cup."}], "greet": "Shake hands, and women often greet with a cheek kiss. Always greet with \"buenos días\" or \"buenas tardes\" before asking anything.", "dress": "Light clothes are fine on the coast, but people dress smarter in San Salvador. Cover shoulders and knees in churches.", "table": "Eat pupusas with your hands, and say \"buen provecho\" when you start or leave the table. Keep your hands visible.", "faith": "Most Salvadorans are Catholic or evangelical Christian. Dress modestly in churches, and be respectful at the tomb of Saint Óscar Romero in San Salvador's cathedral.", "phrases": [["¿Qué onda?", "What's up?"], ["Gracias", "Thank you"], ["¿Cuánto cuesta?", "How much?"], ["Sin chile", "Without chilli"], ["Chivo", "Cool, great"]]},
  "costa-rica": {"around": "Public buses are cheap and reach almost everywhere, but San José has several different terminals depending on where you're going, so check which one before you head out. Tourist shuttles between San José, La Fortuna, Monteverde and the beaches cost several times more but save hours. Uber and DiDi work in the Central Valley and some beach towns. In the December to April dry season, book shuttles and popular park tickets (like Manuel Antonio) a day or more ahead.", "money": "Costa Rica runs on colones, but US dollars are accepted almost everywhere in tourist areas, often at a slightly worse rate, so paying in colones usually works out better. Cards are widely accepted, even in small sodas, and ATMs from BCR and Banco Nacional are common, though many dispense dollars as well as colones and some charge a fee. Restaurant bills already include a 10% service charge plus 13% tax, so extra tipping is optional, but tipping good guides is normal. It's the priciest country in Central America, so budget accordingly.", "sleep": "Every tourist town has hostel dorms and cheap cabinas, which are simple rooms often run by local families. Expect to pay more than elsewhere in Central America for a dorm bed. Beds around Manuel Antonio, Santa Teresa and Puerto Viejo go fast in high season and over Easter.", "eat": ["Casado: rice, beans, salad, plantain and a choice of meat", "Gallo pinto: rice and black beans fried together for breakfast", "Chifrijo: bowl of rice, beans, fried pork and pico de gallo", "Olla de carne: hearty beef soup with root vegetables and corn"], "data": "Kölbi (ICE) has the widest coverage, with Claro and Liberty as alternatives, and SIMs are easy to pick up with your passport. Signal fades in deep cloud forest, the Osa Peninsula and some remote beaches.", "heads_up": "Never leave bags in a parked car at beaches or trailheads, as break-ins are common.", "food": [{"name": "Casado", "wiki": "Casado", "desc": "The classic lunch plate: rice, beans, salad, fried plantain and your choice of chicken, fish or beef. Sodas (small local diners) serve it for a fraction of what tourist restaurants charge.", "price": "$6 to $9", "when": "Lunch, at sodas", "order": "Pick pollo, res, cerdo or pescado as your protein"}, {"name": "Gallo pinto", "wiki": "Gallo pinto", "desc": "Rice and black beans fried together with onion, peppers and Salsa Lizano, eaten for breakfast with eggs and tortillas. Most hostels and sodas do it cheaply.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Breakfast, with eggs and coffee", "order": "Order pinto con huevo with natilla (sour cream)"}, {"name": "Arroz con pollo", "wiki": "Arroz con pollo", "desc": "Yellow rice cooked with shredded chicken and vegetables, usually with fries or salad on the side. A soda and party staple that fills you up for little money.", "price": "$5 to $8", "when": "Lunch, and at family parties"}, {"name": "Tamales", "wiki": "Tamale", "desc": "Costa Rican tamales are wrapped in banana leaves and filled with corn dough, pork and rice, and they're mostly a Christmas thing. If you're there in December, buy a pair from a family selling them.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Christmas season, bought by the dozen", "order": "A piña is two tamales tied together"}, {"name": "Tres leches", "wiki": "Tres leches cake", "desc": "Sponge cake soaked in three kinds of milk, sold by the slice in bakeries and sodas all over the country.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Dessert or the afternoon coffee break"}, {"name": "Costa Rican coffee", "wiki": "Coffee production in Costa Rica", "desc": "Grown in the highlands and taken very seriously. Do a farm tour in Monteverde or Naranjo, or just grab a cheap cup brewed through a cloth chorreador at a soda.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "The mid-afternoon cafecito", "order": "Try it brewed in a chorreador, a cloth filter"}], "food_tip": "Eat at sodas, not tourist restaurants, and a filling casado will cost a fraction of the price. Supermarkets like Palí and Maxi Palí are the cheapest places to stock up if your hostel has a kitchen.", "culture": ["\"Pura vida\" is a greeting, a goodbye, a thank you and a whole outlook. Use it and people will smile.", "Ticos are polite and avoid confrontation, so a \"maybe\" can mean no, and raising your voice gets you nowhere.", "Costa Rica is mostly Catholic, so cover shoulders and knees when visiting churches, and dress a bit more smartly in San José than at the beach.", "Don't feed or touch wildlife, including the monkeys and coatis that hang around beaches. It's illegal in national parks and makes animals aggressive.", "Directions are often given using landmarks, like \"200 metres north of the old church\", rather than street names, so learn to ask locals.", "Locals greet with a single cheek kiss between women or a man and a woman, while men usually shake hands."], "safety": "Petty theft is the main issue, especially bags left on beaches and anything visible in parked cars. Rip currents kill people every year, so swim at lifeguarded beaches or ask locals. Use registered red taxis or apps rather than pirate taxis at night, and be careful around Limón city and parts of San José after dark.", "eat_beats": ["Ticos eat lunch around noon, and many sodas in small towns close by 6 or 7pm.", "Tap water is safe in most of the country, including San José, but stick to bottled in remote areas and some beach towns.", "Vegetarians can ask for a casado vegetariano, with extra beans, eggs or cheese instead of meat.", "Restaurant bills already include a 10 percent service charge and 13 percent tax, so any extra tip is optional.", "Weekend ferias del agricultor (farmers' markets) in most towns are the cheapest place for fruit and snacks."], "drinks": [{"name": "Guaro", "desc": "A clear sugarcane spirit, often mixed with lime and soda as a guaro sour. A drink costs around $2 to $4."}, {"name": "Fresco natural", "desc": "Fresh fruit blended with water or milk, from mango to mora (blackberry) to cas. Sodas charge about $1.50 to $3."}], "greet": "Say buenos días or buenas tardes when you walk into a shop or onto a bus. Use usted with strangers and elders.", "dress": "Casual clothes work almost everywhere, though San José dresses a bit sharper than the coast. Bring a rain jacket in the green season, May to November.", "table": "Wait until everyone has been served before you start, and keep both hands visible on the table. Saying buen provecho to people eating nearby is polite.", "faith": "Most Costa Ricans are Catholic, with a fast-growing evangelical community. Speak quietly in churches, and expect huge crowds on 2 August, when pilgrims walk to the basilica in Cartago.", "phrases": [["Pura vida", "Great, all good"], ["Mae", "Dude, mate"], ["Tuanis", "Cool"], ["¿Cuánto cuesta?", "How much is it?"], ["Con mucho gusto", "You're welcome"]]},
  "panama": {"around": "Long-distance buses from Albrook terminal in Panama City are comfortable and cheap, with overnight runs to David, then minivans up to Boquete. Bocas del Toro is reached by bus to Almirante followed by a water taxi. Panama City has a clean, cheap Metro that needs a rechargeable card, and Uber, inDrive and DiDi are widely used. San Blas trips need a 4x4 transfer and a boat, which you book through a hostel or operator a day or two ahead.", "money": "Panama uses the US dollar for notes, with Panamanian balboa coins mixed in as change, and they're the same value. Cards work well in Panama City, but you'll need cash in Bocas del Toro, Boquete's small spots and especially San Blas, where there are no ATMs. ATMs usually charge a few dollars per withdrawal. Tipping around 10% in restaurants is normal, and haggling is mostly limited to taxis and craft stalls, where a little bargaining is expected.", "sleep": "Panama City's Casco Viejo has a bunch of backpacker hostels in a great setting, and Boquete and Bocas have plenty of dorms and party hostels. In San Blas you'll sleep in simple Guna-run cabins or hammocks, usually as part of a package with food and boat trips. Prices are mid-range for the region.", "eat": ["Sancocho: chicken soup with yam, corn and culantro", "Patacones: twice-fried green plantain slices", "Carimañolas: fried cassava rolls filled with spiced meat", "Hojaldras: fried dough eaten for breakfast"], "data": "+Móvil (Cable & Wireless), Tigo and Digicel SIMs are easy to buy with a passport, and coverage is good on the mainland. Most San Blas islands have little or no signal, so treat it as a digital detox.", "heads_up": "San Blas is run by the Guna people, who charge small entry and island fees, and you should ask before photographing anyone.", "food": [{"name": "Sancocho", "wiki": "Sancocho", "desc": "Panama's national soup: chicken, ñame (yam), corn and plenty of culantro. Locals swear by it as a hangover cure, and it's cheap at fondas.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Sunday lunch and as a hangover cure", "order": "Add rice and a dash of culantro"}, {"name": "Patacones", "wiki": "Tostones", "desc": "Green plantain slices smashed and fried twice until crispy, served with almost every meal. Great as a cheap side or snack with salt and hot sauce.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "A side with fish or meat"}, {"name": "Carimañolas", "wiki": "Carimañola", "desc": "Torpedo-shaped fried cassava rolls stuffed with seasoned meat, often eaten for breakfast. A couple from a street stall or bakery costs very little.", "price": "$0.75 to $1.50", "when": "Breakfast at fondas", "order": "Usually filled with beef, sometimes cheese"}, {"name": "Ceviche", "wiki": "Ceviche", "desc": "Raw corvina or shrimp cured in lime with onion and peppers. The stalls at Mercado de Mariscos in Panama City sell cups of it for a few dollars.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Snack at the fish market", "order": "Corvina is the classic; eat it with crackers"}, {"name": "Chicheme", "wiki": "Chicheme", "desc": "A sweet, thick corn drink with milk, cinnamon and vanilla, especially popular in the Azuero region. Cheap and filling on a hot day.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Hot afternoons, especially in La Chorrera"}, {"name": "Raspao", "wiki": "Shaved ice", "desc": "Shaved ice drowned in fruit syrup and condensed milk, sold from carts everywhere. A cheap way to cool down in Panama City heat.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Hot afternoons, from street carts", "order": "Ask for condensed milk on top"}], "food_tip": "Look for fondas, small local diners serving set lunch plates of rice, beans, meat and salad for a few dollars. Supermarkets like Super 99 and El Rey are handy if your hostel has a kitchen.", "culture": ["Panamanians are relaxed and friendly, but dress matters more than you'd think in the city. Walking around shirtless in towns, even in Bocas, can get you a warning or a fine.", "San Blas (Guna Yala) is an autonomous region run by the Guna people, who set their own fees and rules. Always ask before taking photos, and expect to pay a small fee if they say yes.", "Churches and cathedrals expect covered shoulders and knees, as the country is mostly Catholic.", "Panamanian time is relaxed, so plans and buses may not run to the minute, especially outside the capital.", "A friendly \"buenos días\" before any request is expected, and skipping it comes across as rude."], "safety": "Panama City is fine in tourist areas like Casco Viejo and the Cinta Costera, but avoid wandering into neighbouring El Chorrillo and parts of Colón, especially at night. Keep an eye on your phone in crowded spots and on night buses. The Darién region is off-limits for travellers. Rip currents and boat safety on San Blas trips are worth checking before you go.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, around 12 to 2pm, and fondas often close by mid afternoon.", "Tap water is safe in Panama City and most towns, but not in Bocas del Toro or San Blas.", "Restaurants often add a 10 percent service charge, so check before tipping more.", "Vegetarians can build plates from rice, beans, patacones and salad, but ask about meat in stews.", "Panama uses the US dollar, so prices are easy to read."], "drinks": [{"name": "Seco", "desc": "Panama's sugarcane spirit, mixed with milk or soda. A drink costs $2 to $4."}, {"name": "Chicha", "desc": "Here chicha means fresh fruit juice with water and sugar, like papaya or tamarind, about $1 a glass."}], "greet": "A handshake with 'buenos días' is standard, and women often greet friends with a cheek kiss.", "dress": "Wear light clothing for the heat and humidity, plus a rain jacket in the wet season. Bring long trousers for government offices and a warm layer for the highlands around Boquete.", "table": "Say 'buen provecho' to people who are eating. Meals are casual, and sharing platters is normal.", "faith": "Most Panamanians are Catholic, with a large evangelical community. On 21 October, thousands walk to Portobelo to honour the Black Christ, so dress modestly and stay respectful if you go.", "phrases": [["Hola", "Hello"], ["Gracias", "Thank you"], ["¿Cuánto cuesta?", "How much?"], ["¿Qué xopá?", "What's up? (Panamanian slang)"], ["Chuleta", "Wow (slang)"]]},
  "cuba": {"around": "Viazul tourist buses link Havana, Viñales, Trinidad, Cienfuegos and Santa Clara, but tickets sell out and fuel shortages can cancel departures, so confirm the day before. Shared colectivo taxis often cost about the same as the bus and are much quicker, and your casa host can book one for you. In Havana, classic car taxis, coco taxis and bici taxis are fun but agree the fare before you get in, as there's no international ride-hailing. Domestic flights exist but are unreliable.", "money": "Cuba is a cash country for travellers: bring euros or US dollars in clean, undamaged notes, because cards issued by US banks don't work at all and other foreign cards are hit and miss. There's a big gap between the official exchange rate and the informal rate, so most travellers change money with their casa host or trusted locals rather than at banks, and many places now price things in dollars or euros directly. ATMs are unreliable and often empty, so never count on them. Tipping is widely expected in a tight economy, from waiters to musicians to your casa host.", "sleep": "Casas particulares, licensed rooms in family homes marked with a blue anchor sign, are the classic Cuban budget bed, and a private room usually costs about what a hostel dorm would elsewhere. Hosts often offer huge breakfasts and dinners for a fee, and they're your best source for transport, money and tips. A handful of hostels exist in Havana, Viñales and Trinidad if you want a dorm.", "eat": ["Ropa vieja: shredded beef stewed with peppers and tomato", "Moros y cristianos: black beans and rice cooked together", "Tostones: flattened, twice-fried green plantain", "Café cubano: short, strong espresso sweetened while brewing"], "data": "ETECSA sells tourist SIMs at the airport and its offices, and data works but is slow and gets patchy during power cuts. Many international eSIMs don't cover Cuba, so check before you go, and a VPN helps as some sites and apps are blocked.", "heads_up": "Power and water cuts are common, so bring a power bank, a torch and any medicines you need, as pharmacies are often short.", "food": [{"name": "Ropa vieja", "wiki": "Ropa vieja", "desc": "Shredded beef stewed slowly with tomatoes, peppers and onions, served with rice and beans. A staple at paladares (private restaurants) and casa dinners.", "price": "$6 to $12", "when": "Lunch or dinner at paladares", "order": "Comes with rice, beans and salad"}, {"name": "Moros y cristianos", "wiki": "Moros y Cristianos", "desc": "Black beans and rice cooked together in one pot, the backbone of most Cuban meals. It comes free on the side of almost everything.", "price": "Free to $2", "when": "With every lunch and dinner"}, {"name": "Tostones", "wiki": "Tostones", "desc": "Green plantain slices flattened and fried twice, served as a side or snack, often with a garlicky mojo sauce.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Side dish or snack with drinks", "order": "Ask for mojo on the side"}, {"name": "Café cubano", "wiki": "Cuban espresso", "desc": "A tiny, very strong espresso sweetened with sugar as it brews. Grab one from a peso window stall for pocket change.", "price": "$0.20 to $1", "when": "Morning and after every meal", "order": "A colada is a big shot meant for sharing"}, {"name": "Pastelitos", "wiki": "Cuban pastry", "desc": "Flaky puff pastries filled with guava, cheese or meat, sold cheaply from bakery windows and street carts.", "price": "$0.20 to $1", "when": "Breakfast or snack on the go", "order": "Guayaba y queso is the classic combo"}, {"name": "Mojito", "wiki": "Mojito", "desc": "Rum, lime, mint, sugar and soda, invented here and sold everywhere. Bars in Havana and Trinidad charge a few dollars, much less away from the tourist strips.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Afternoons and evenings in bars"}], "food_tip": "Peso stalls and windows selling pizza, sandwiches and juice are by far the cheapest food, so carry some Cuban pesos for them. Casa breakfasts are usually huge and good value, so fill up in the morning.", "culture": ["Cubans are warm, chatty and resourceful, and the economy is genuinely hard right now, so be patient with shortages and generous with tips where you can.", "Avoid loudly criticising the government or getting into politics in public, as it can put locals in an awkward spot.", "Don't photograph police, military or government buildings, and ask before taking photos of people, as some will expect a tip.", "Greetings are friendly, usually a handshake or a cheek kiss, and \"mi amor\" or \"mi vida\" is everyday chat, not flirting.", "Many items like medicine, toiletries and clothes are hard to get, so small useful gifts left with your host are often appreciated more than cash."], "safety": "Violent crime against travellers is rare, but expect hustlers (jineteros) with scams like fake cigars, a \"salsa festival\" bar where you pay for everyone's drinks, or casa owners steering you elsewhere for commission. Check your change carefully and agree prices first. Mosquito-borne illnesses like dengue and chikungunya have hit hard recently, so use repellent, and drink bottled or boiled water.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal from noon, and many paladares serve dinner from 7 to 10pm.", "Don't drink tap water, so buy bottled or use a filter, as shortages can make bottled water hard to find.", "Vegetarians can live on rice, beans, plantains, eggs and salads, but options are basic, so tell your casa host early.", "Tip about 10 percent in restaurants, and leave tips for musicians and bathroom attendants, as wages are very low.", "Power cuts and shortages are common, so menus change daily and some items may be out."], "drinks": [{"name": "Guarapo", "desc": "Fresh sugarcane juice pressed while you wait, sweet and cold, for under $0.50 from street stands."}, {"name": "Cuba libre", "desc": "Rum, cola and lime, a simple Cuban classic. Expect $2 to $5 in bars."}], "greet": "A handshake or a cheek kiss is normal, and people call each other mi amor or chico as friendly chat. Saying buenos días when entering a shop is polite.", "dress": "Casual summer clothes are fine, but Cubans dress neatly in public. Cover shoulders in churches and save swimwear for the beach.", "table": "Meals are relaxed and generous, and your host may keep serving until you stop them. Finish what you take, as food is precious.", "faith": "Many Cubans are Catholic, and Santería, an Afro-Cuban faith, is widely practised alongside it. Don't photograph ceremonies or people dressed in white without asking.", "phrases": [["Hola, ¿qué bolá?", "Hi, what's up? (Cuban slang)"], ["Gracias", "Thank you"], ["¿Cuánto cuesta?", "How much is it?"], ["Asere", "Mate, buddy"], ["La cuenta, por favor", "The bill, please"]]},
  "dominican-republic": {"around": "Caribe Tours and Metro Tours run comfortable, air-conditioned buses between the big towns, and cheap guaguas (minibuses) cover shorter routes, stopping anywhere you flag them down. Santo Domingo has a Metro and a cable car, and Uber and inDrive work in the capital, Santiago and Punta Cana. Motoconchos (motorbike taxis) are everywhere in smaller towns like Las Terrenas and Cabarete, but agree the fare first and hold on tight.", "money": "The Dominican peso is the currency, and while US dollars are accepted in tourist zones, you'll get better value paying in pesos. Cards work in cities and resorts, but carry cash for guaguas, colmados (corner shops) and small eateries. Use ATMs inside banks or malls to avoid card skimming, and watch for withdrawal fees. Restaurant bills include 18% tax and a 10% legal service charge, and leaving an extra 10% for good service is customary.", "sleep": "Santo Domingo's Zona Colonial is the backpacker hub, with hostels and guesthouses in old colonial buildings. Cabarete has surf and kite hostels, and Jarabacoa and Las Terrenas have small family-run hotels and cabañas. Dorms are reasonably priced, and Punta Cana is all resorts, so budget travellers generally skip it.", "eat": ["La bandera: rice, red beans and stewed meat, the lunch staple", "Mangú: mashed green plantain with pickled onions, eaten at breakfast", "Sancocho: thick stew of several meats and root vegetables", "Morir soñando: orange juice mixed with milk and sugar"], "data": "Claro and Altice SIMs are easy to buy with a passport at the airport or any mall, and coverage is good in towns and along main roads, though it can drop in the mountains.", "heads_up": "Don't drink tap water, and stick to bottled or filtered water, including for ice in small places.", "food": [{"name": "Mangú", "wiki": "Mangú", "desc": "Mashed green plantain topped with pickled red onions and served with fried salami, cheese and eggs, a combo called los tres golpes. The classic Dominican breakfast.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Breakfast, at comedores and homes", "order": "Ask for \"los tres golpes\" for the full combo"}, {"name": "Sancocho", "wiki": "Sancocho", "desc": "A thick, hearty stew of several meats with yuca, plantain and corn, eaten at weekends and celebrations. Big bowls at comedores are cheap and filling.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Weekends and celebrations", "order": "Eat with white rice and avocado"}, {"name": "Chimi", "wiki": "Chimichurri burger", "desc": "The Dominican street burger: seasoned pork or beef patty with cabbage, onion and a pink sauce in soft bread. Late-night food trucks sell them for a few dollars.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Late night, from food trucks", "order": "Add extra sauce and a cold Presidente"}, {"name": "Habichuelas con dulce", "wiki": "Habichuelas con dulce", "desc": "A sweet, creamy bean dessert with coconut milk, cinnamon and sweet potato, mostly made around Easter.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Easter and Lent"}, {"name": "Mamajuana", "wiki": "Mama Juana", "desc": "Rum, red wine and honey soaked into a bottle of tree bark and herbs. Locals claim it cures everything, and you'll be offered a shot sooner or later.", "price": "$1 to $3 a shot", "when": "Nights out and with locals", "order": "Try a small shot before buying a bottle"}, {"name": "Presidente", "wiki": "Presidente (beer)", "desc": "The national beer, served ice cold in big bottles to share, often wearing a little paper jacket to stop it warming up.", "price": "$2 to $4 a big bottle", "when": "Evenings, at colmados and beaches", "order": "Ask for \"vestida de novia\" (frosted bottle)"}], "food_tip": "Look for comedores serving la bandera dominicana, rice, red beans, stewed meat and salad, at lunchtime for a few dollars. Colmados are also cheap for snacks, cold beer and a chat with locals.", "culture": ["Dominicans are loud, warm and sociable, and music (merengue and bachata) plays everywhere, so don't be shy to dance.", "Greet people before asking anything, and use \"buenos días\" or \"buenas\" with everyone, from bus drivers to shopkeepers.", "Churches like the Catedral Primada in Santo Domingo require covered shoulders and knees, and beachwear isn't appropriate in town.", "Catcalling is fairly common, so women travelling alone may get a lot of comments, which are usually best ignored.", "Baseball is close to a religion, and catching a winter league game is a great cheap night out.", "Haggling is normal at markets and with motoconchos, but keep it friendly."], "safety": "Petty theft and bag snatching from passing motorbikes are the main risks, so keep your phone away from the roadside. Use official taxis or Uber at night, and watch out for money changers offering amazing rates on the street. Don't drink the tap water, and use repellent for dengue.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch, the main meal, runs from around noon to 2pm, and dinner is lighter and often late.", "Don't drink tap water; buy bottled water or big refill jugs (botellones).", "Vegetarians can find rice, beans, plantains and salads, but beans may be cooked with meat.", "Street vendors sell fresh fruit, juices and fried snacks, so pick busy stalls.", "Restaurants add a 10 percent service charge plus tax, and leaving an extra 10 percent is common."], "drinks": [{"name": "Morir soñando", "desc": "A creamy drink of orange juice and milk with ice, sold at juice stands for $1 to $2."}, {"name": "Brugal rum", "desc": "Dark Dominican rum, often drunk with Coke or ginger ale, about $1 to $3 a glass at colmados."}], "greet": "A handshake or a single cheek kiss is normal, and greeting everyone in a room is polite. Saying \"buenas\" with a smile works anywhere.", "dress": "Locals dress smartly in towns, and beachwear is only for the beach. Long trousers are often required for men in government offices and some clubs.", "table": "Meals are relaxed and shared, and it's polite to wait for the host to invite you to eat. Saying \"buen provecho\" to people eating is common.", "faith": "Most Dominicans are Catholic, with a growing evangelical community. Cover shoulders and knees in churches, and keep quiet during services.", "phrases": [["¿Qué lo que?", "What's up? (slang)"], ["Gracias", "Thank you"], ["¿Cuánto cuesta?", "How much does it cost?"], ["Tato", "OK, cool"], ["Vaina", "Thing, stuff (used for everything)"]]},
  "jamaica": {"around": "Knutsford Express coaches link Kingston, Ocho Rios, Montego Bay, Negril and other towns comfortably and reliably, with online booking. Route taxis (shared cars) and minibuses are much cheaper and run local routes, but they're crowded and leave when full. Only use licensed taxis with red PP plates, and for the Blue Mountains or Port Antonio, guesthouses can arrange shared transfers.", "money": "Both Jamaican and US dollars are used in tourist areas, but you'll get better value paying in Jamaican dollars, since prices in US dollars tend to be rounded up. Cards work in larger shops, hotels and restaurants, but carry local cash for route taxis, jerk stands and markets. ATMs are easy to find in towns, and using ones inside banks is safer. Tipping 10 to 15% is common if there's no service charge, and haggling is expected with craft vendors and unofficial guides.", "sleep": "Guesthouses and a few hostels cover Kingston, Port Antonio, Treasure Beach and Negril, and the Blue Mountains have simple lodges and coffee farm stays. Hurricane Melissa did heavy damage to the west of the island in late 2025, so check your place is open and back to normal before booking there.", "eat": ["Jerk chicken: spicy smoked chicken cooked over pimento wood", "Ackee and saltfish: national dish of ackee fruit with salt cod", "Beef patty: flaky golden pastry filled with spiced beef", "Curry goat: slow-cooked goat in a rich curry sauce"], "data": "Digicel and Flow SIMs are cheap and easy to buy with a passport, and coverage is good on the coast but gets patchy high in the Blue Mountains.", "heads_up": "Persistent touts are common around beaches and markets, so a polite but firm \"no thanks\" works best.", "food": [{"name": "Jerk chicken", "wiki": "Jerk (cooking)", "desc": "Chicken or pork rubbed in scotch bonnet, allspice and thyme and smoked over pimento wood. The best comes from roadside oil-drum grills, sold by the quarter or half.", "price": "$6 to $12", "when": "Evenings, from roadside drum grills", "order": "Ask for festival or bread on the side"}, {"name": "Ackee and saltfish", "wiki": "Ackee and saltfish", "desc": "The national dish: ackee fruit cooked with salt cod, onions and peppers, eaten at breakfast with fried dumplings or breadfruit.", "price": "$8 to $14", "when": "Breakfast, especially on Sundays", "order": "Eat it with fried dumplings or boiled green banana"}, {"name": "Beef patty", "wiki": "Jamaican patty", "desc": "Flaky yellow pastry filled with spicy minced beef, sold at chains like Juici and Tastee for a couple of dollars. Have it in coco bread for the full experience.", "price": "$2 to $3.50", "when": "Lunch or snack, from patty shops", "order": "Ask for it in coco bread"}, {"name": "Curry goat", "wiki": "Goat curry", "desc": "Goat slow-cooked in a rich curry with potatoes and served with rice and peas. A favourite at cookshops and parties.", "price": "$7 to $12", "when": "Lunch, at cookshops", "order": "Comes with rice and peas"}, {"name": "Festival", "wiki": "Festival (food)", "desc": "Slightly sweet fried dumplings that come with jerk and fried fish. Cheap, filling and great for soaking up the spice.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "With jerk or fried fish"}, {"name": "Bammy", "wiki": "Bammy", "desc": "A flat cassava bread, soaked in milk and fried, usually served with escovitch fish at beachside spots.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "With fried fish, at beaches", "order": "Try it with escovitch fish"}], "food_tip": "Cookshops serving set lunches of rice and peas with a meat are much cheaper than tourist restaurants. Patty shops are perfect for a cheap, quick bite.", "culture": ["Always greet people with \"good morning\" or \"good afternoon\" before asking a question; jumping straight in comes off as rude.", "Jamaicans are direct and funny, and conversations can be loud and full of banter, so don't take it the wrong way.", "Small amounts of cannabis are decriminalised, but smoking in public is still illegal, and you shouldn't buy from beach dealers.", "Dress modestly away from the beach, as swimwear in town or at shops isn't appreciated, and churches expect smart clothes.", "Attitudes to LGBTQ travellers can be conservative, so many couples choose to be discreet in public, particularly outside resort areas.", "Ask before taking photos of people, as some may expect a tip or prefer not to be photographed."], "safety": "Persistent touts and hustlers are common around beaches and markets, and a polite but firm \"no thanks\" is the way to deal with them. Avoid walking alone after dark in parts of Kingston and Montego Bay, and use licensed taxis. Sunburn, mosquitoes and dodgy unofficial tour guides are the more likely annoyances than serious crime.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is often big, lunch is around noon, and Sunday dinner is the main family meal.", "Jerk pans and cookshops sell takeaway plates, and you order at the counter and pay first.", "Tap water is generally safe in towns, but drink bottled water in rural areas.", "Ital food from Rastafarian cooks is fully plant-based, so vegetarians eat well.", "Many restaurants add 10% to 15% service, so check the bill; otherwise tip about 10% to 15%."], "drinks": [{"name": "Sorrel", "desc": "Chilled hibiscus drink spiced with ginger and sometimes rum, made around Christmas. A glass costs about $2 to $4."}, {"name": "Irish moss", "desc": "A thick, creamy drink made from seaweed, milk and spices, said to boost energy. Expect $3 to $5 a bottle."}], "greet": "Say good morning or good afternoon before anything else, and shake hands. Friends often fist bump.", "dress": "Light, casual clothes work, but put a shirt on away from the beach, as swimwear in town isn't welcome. Dress smartly for church, and some nightclubs have dress codes.", "table": "Meals are relaxed, and Sunday dinner with rice and peas is a big family event. Compliment the cook, and pass dishes rather than reaching across the table.", "faith": "Jamaica has more churches per square mile than almost anywhere, and most people are Christian, with a small but well-known Rastafari community. Dress smartly for church services, and ask before photographing Rastafari people.", "phrases": [["Wah gwaan?", "What's up?"], ["Mi deh yah", "I'm here, I'm fine"], ["Irie", "All good, nice"], ["Soon come", "I'll be there eventually"], ["Big up", "Respect, well done"]]},
  "haiti": {"around": "Tap-taps, colourful shared pickups and minibuses, are the main public transport, but road travel between regions has been heavily disrupted by armed groups and isn't considered safe. Commercial flights into Port-au-Prince have been suspended for long periods, and the situation changes constantly. Any visit relies on current security advice and reliable local contacts arranging transport. Ride-hailing apps aren't a practical option.", "money": "Cash is essential, with US dollars used widely alongside Haitian gourdes, and card payments and ATMs are limited to a few hotels and banks. Prices are often quoted in Haitian dollars, an informal unit worth five gourdes, so always confirm which currency someone means. Bring small, clean US notes, as damaged bills are often refused. Tipping isn't strictly expected but is appreciated, and haggling is normal at markets.", "sleep": "Accommodation is mostly small hotels and guesthouses, and the few people visiting at the moment rely on places with good security and local hosts who follow the situation daily. The north, around Cap-Haïtien, has historically been calmer, but check current advice before going anywhere.", "eat": ["Griot: marinated pork chunks, fried until crisp", "Diri ak djon djon: rice cooked black with dried mushrooms", "Soup joumou: squash soup eaten on Independence Day, January 1", "Pikliz: spicy pickled cabbage and pepper relish"], "data": "Digicel and Natcom are the two carriers, and SIMs need ID registration. Network outages are common during unrest, so have backup ways to contact people.", "heads_up": "Drink only bottled or treated water, as cholera has returned in recent years.", "food": [{"name": "Griot", "wiki": "Griot (food)", "desc": "Pork chunks marinated in citrus and spices, then fried until crisp, served with fried plantain and pikliz. Haiti's favourite celebration food.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Weekend lunches and parties", "order": "Comes with bannann peze, flattened fried plantain"}, {"name": "Diri ak djon djon", "wiki": "Diri djondjon", "desc": "Rice cooked black with dried local mushrooms, giving it an earthy flavour. Usually served with meat or seafood at celebrations.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Sunday lunch and special occasions"}, {"name": "Soup joumou", "wiki": "Soup joumou", "desc": "A pumpkin soup with beef, pasta and vegetables, eaten every January 1 to celebrate Haiti's independence, when it was once forbidden to enslaved people.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "January 1, and Sunday mornings"}, {"name": "Pikliz", "wiki": "Pikliz", "desc": "A fiery relish of pickled cabbage, carrots and scotch bonnet peppers, served on the side of almost everything.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "On the side of almost every plate", "order": "Buy a jar to take home"}, {"name": "Rhum Barbancourt", "wiki": "Rhum Barbancourt", "desc": "Haiti's famous rum, made from sugar cane juice. Cheap by international standards and a point of national pride.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Celebrations, or as a gift", "order": "The aged five star version is the one to try"}, {"name": "Prestige", "wiki": "Prestige (beer)", "desc": "The local lager, light and very cold, and the drink of choice with griot.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Evenings, with fried food"}], "food_tip": "Street food like fried plantain and griot is cheap, but stick to busy, freshly cooked stalls. Fruit you peel yourself is the safest snack.", "culture": ["Haitians are proud of being the first independent Black republic, and the country has a rich culture of art, music and Vodou, so show respect and curiosity rather than judgement.", "Greet people properly with \"bonjou\" in the morning or \"bonswa\" later in the day, as greetings matter a lot.", "Ask before photographing people, especially at markets and religious ceremonies.", "Dress modestly, particularly in rural areas and churches, with shoulders and knees covered.", "Haitian Creole (Kreyòl) is the everyday language, and a few words go a long way, even if French is also official."], "safety": "Most governments advise against all travel to Haiti because of widespread gang violence, kidnapping and civil unrest, especially in and around Port-au-Prince. If you do go, it should be with organisations or contacts who have strong local security knowledge. Drink only bottled or treated water, as cholera has returned in recent years, and protect yourself from mosquitoes.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, usually noon to 2pm, and many street cooks sell out early.", "Don't drink tap water; buy sealed bottles or bags of treated water.", "Vegetarians can eat diri kole (rice and beans), fried plantain, salads and legim, a vegetable stew, though it sometimes has meat in it.", "Prices are sometimes quoted in Haitian dollars, an informal unit worth five gourdes, so ask which one is meant.", "Tip around 10 percent in sit-down restaurants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Kremas", "desc": "A creamy, spiced coconut and rum drink made for the holidays. A bottle costs about $5 to $10."}, {"name": "Clairin", "desc": "Raw, unaged cane spirit distilled in small village stills, poured for around $1 a glass."}], "greet": "Shake hands with everyone when you arrive, and friends kiss on the cheek. Always greet people before asking anything.", "dress": "Haitians dress neatly, especially for church on Sundays, so avoid scruffy clothes in town.", "table": "Wait to be invited to eat, and accept food when offered, as refusing can seem rude. Finishing your plate shows you enjoyed it.", "faith": "Most Haitians are Catholic or Protestant, and many also practise Vodou, a recognised religion. Treat ceremonies and peristyles (temples) with respect, and only attend with an invitation.", "phrases": [["Bonjou", "Good morning"], ["Mèsi", "Thank you"], ["Konbyen?", "How much?"], ["Sak pase?", "What's up?"], ["N ap boule", "All good (the reply)"]]},
  "trinidad-tobago": {"around": "Maxi taxis, shared minibuses with coloured bands showing their route, and route taxis (shared cars with H on the plates) are the cheap way to get around Trinidad. PTSC public buses cover main roads but are less frequent. The fast ferry and short flights between Trinidad and Tobago sell out around Carnival and holidays, so book early. On Tobago, route taxis or a rental car are the usual ways to explore.", "money": "The TT dollar is the currency, and cards are accepted in most shops and restaurants, with ATMs easy to find. Keep TT cash for maxi taxis, doubles stands and markets. Tipping 10 to 15% is normal if no service charge is added, and haggling isn't common outside some craft stalls.", "sleep": "Budget beds are mostly guesthouses and small hotels, with only a few hostels. In Tobago, Crown Point and Castara have cheap guesthouses near the beach. Carnival rooms in Port of Spain sell out months ahead and prices spike.", "eat": ["Doubles: fried flatbread with curried chickpeas and chutneys", "Bake and shark: fried shark in fried bread with toppings", "Roti: flatbread wrapped around curried meat or vegetables", "Pelau: one-pot rice with chicken, pigeon peas and caramelized sugar"], "data": "bmobile and Digicel SIMs are easy to buy with a passport, and coverage is good on both islands.", "heads_up": "Avoid walking alone at night in parts of Port of Spain, and keep valuables to a minimum in Carnival crowds.", "food": [{"name": "Doubles", "wiki": "Doubles (food)", "desc": "Two soft fried flatbreads topped with curried chickpeas and chutneys, sold from street carts in the morning for just a few TT dollars. Ask for \"slight pepper\" if you're unsure about the heat.", "price": "$1 to $1.50", "when": "Breakfast from street carts", "order": "Ask for slight pepper, or heavy if brave"}, {"name": "Bake and shark", "wiki": "Bake and shark", "desc": "Fried shark in fried bread, loaded at a topping bar with sauces and salad. The stalls at Maracas Bay are famous.", "price": "$6 to $9", "when": "Lunch at the beach", "order": "Load up on chadon beni and tamarind sauce"}, {"name": "Roti", "wiki": "Dal puri", "desc": "Soft, split-pea-filled flatbread wrapped around curried chicken, goat or vegetables. A big, cheap lunch from roti shops everywhere.", "price": "$5 to $8", "when": "Lunch from roti shops", "order": "Try dhalpuri or buss up shut"}, {"name": "Pelau", "wiki": "Pelau", "desc": "A one-pot dish of rice, pigeon peas and chicken, browned in caramelised sugar for a deep flavour. Common at beach limes and family gatherings.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Lunch and beach limes"}, {"name": "Callaloo", "wiki": "Callaloo", "desc": "A thick soup of dasheen leaves, okra and coconut milk, often served with Sunday lunch.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Sunday lunch"}, {"name": "Pholourie", "wiki": "Pholourie", "desc": "Spiced split-pea dough balls fried and dunked in tamarind or mango chutney. A cheap snack sold in bags at roadside stands.", "price": "$1 to $2 a bag", "when": "Snack from roadside stands", "order": "Dunk in tamarind chutney"}], "food_tip": "Street food is the way to eat cheap and well here, with doubles in the morning, roti for lunch and corn soup or bake and shark at night. Avoid hotel restaurants.", "culture": ["Trinis love to \"lime\", meaning hang out and chat, and you'll be welcomed into a lime with a drink in your hand.", "Greet people with \"good morning\" or \"good afternoon\" before anything else, as it's considered basic politeness.", "The country is a mix of African, Indian, Chinese and other heritage, and holidays like Carnival, Diwali and Eid are all big deals.", "Wearing camouflage clothing is illegal for civilians, so leave the camo shorts at home.", "Carnival is a huge, joyful party, but locals take mas and steelpan seriously, so respect the bands and performers."], "safety": "Trinidad has a high crime rate in some areas, so avoid walking alone at night in parts of Port of Spain and keep valuables minimal during Carnival crowds. Use recommended taxis at night. Tobago is calmer, but still don't leave things unattended on beaches. Sun and mosquitoes are the other things to watch.", "eat_beats": ["Doubles carts work from dawn and often sell out by mid-morning, so go early.", "Tap water is treated, but supplies can be patchy, so many people drink bottled or filtered water.", "Vegetarians do well thanks to Indo-Trinidadian food like doubles, roti, dhal and curried channa.", "A 10 percent service charge is often added in restaurants, so tip extra only for great service."], "drinks": [{"name": "Mauby", "desc": "A bittersweet drink made from tree bark and spices, an acquired taste. A glass or bottle costs $1 to $2."}, {"name": "Sorrel", "desc": "Hibiscus steeped with ginger, cloves and cinnamon, drunk at Christmas, about $2 to $3 a glass."}], "greet": "Say good morning or good afternoon before anything else, followed by a handshake or a fist bump.", "dress": "Dress is casual and colourful, but cover up in towns and places of worship. Camouflage clothing is illegal.", "table": "Meals are relaxed and shared, especially at Sunday lunch and limes. Pepper sauce is on every table, so try a small dab first.", "faith": "Christianity, Hinduism and Islam are all major faiths, and Diwali, Eid and Easter are public holidays. Remove shoes and dress modestly in temples and mosques.", "phrases": [["Wah de scene?", "What's up?"], ["Liming", "Hanging out"], ["Tanks", "Thanks"], ["Doh worry", "Don't worry"], ["Fete", "Party"]]},
  "chile": {"around": "Chile's long-distance buses are some of the best in South America, with semi-cama and cama reclining seats on overnight runs, and companies like Turbus and Pullman cover the whole country. Budget airlines JetSMART and Sky are often the cheapest way to reach San Pedro de Atacama (via Calama) and Patagonia, especially if you book early. Uber, DiDi and Cabify work in cities, and Santiago's Metro is clean and fast. Book Torres del Paine refugios or campsites months ahead for summer.", "money": "The Chilean peso is the currency, and cards and contactless are accepted almost everywhere, even in small shops. Many ATMs charge a hefty fixed fee per withdrawal, so take out larger amounts less often, and some banks' ATMs are cheaper than others, so compare. Restaurants usually suggest a 10% tip on the bill, called the propina, which is voluntary but expected. Prices are fixed, and haggling isn't really done outside some craft markets.", "sleep": "Hostel dorms are common in Santiago, Valparaíso, Pucón and San Pedro, and they're pricier than in Bolivia or Peru but usually good quality. In Patagonia, beds are expensive, and Torres del Paine refugios and campsites must be booked in advance. Camping is a popular budget option in the south in summer.", "eat": ["Empanada de pino: baked pastry with beef, onion, egg and olive", "Completo: hot dog piled with avocado, tomato and mayonnaise", "Pastel de choclo: baked corn pie over a meat filling", "Mote con huesillo: cold peach nectar with wheat and dried peaches"], "data": "Entel, Movistar, Claro and WOM SIMs are easy to buy, and coverage is good in towns. Signal gets thin in the Atacama and much of Patagonia, including large parts of Torres del Paine.", "heads_up": "Phone snatching is common in central Santiago and Valparaíso, so keep your phone away when you're walking.", "food": [{"name": "Empanada de pino", "wiki": "Empanada", "desc": "Baked pastry stuffed with minced beef, onion, egg and an olive, especially popular around the September 18 national holidays. Bakeries sell them cheaply all year.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch or snack, Sundays and September", "order": "Ask for pino, or queso for cheese"}, {"name": "Completo", "wiki": "Completo", "desc": "A hot dog buried under avocado, tomato and mayonnaise (the \"italiano\" version). Chileans eat them everywhere, and they're cheap at fuentes de soda.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch or late night", "order": "The italiano is classic, the dinámico adds everything"}, {"name": "Pastel de choclo", "wiki": "Pastel de choclo", "desc": "A baked dish of sweet corn paste over minced beef, chicken, olives and egg, topped with sugar. Best in summer when corn is in season.", "price": "$8 to $13", "when": "Lunch in summer"}, {"name": "Chorrillana", "wiki": "Chorrillana", "desc": "A giant pile of fries topped with strips of beef, fried onions and eggs, made for sharing with a few beers. Especially popular in Valparaíso.", "price": "$15 to $25 to share", "when": "Evening with friends and beer", "order": "Order one for every three people"}, {"name": "Mote con huesillo", "wiki": "Mote con huesillo", "desc": "A cold, sweet drink of peach nectar with cooked wheat and dried peaches. Sold by street vendors in summer for a small price.", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Hot summer afternoons, from street stalls"}, {"name": "Pisco sour", "wiki": "Pisco sour", "desc": "Pisco, lemon juice and sugar, shaken with egg white. Chile and Peru both claim it, so don't pick sides in public.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Evenings and celebrations"}], "food_tip": "Look for menú del día set lunches at local restaurants, which include a starter, main and drink for a reasonable price. Supermarkets like Líder and Unimarc are good for stocking up if you're cooking.", "culture": ["Chileans greet with a single cheek kiss, even when meeting for the first time, while men usually shake hands with each other.", "Chilean Spanish is fast and full of slang, like \"po\" and \"cachai\", so don't worry if you struggle at first.", "Afternoon \"once\", a light evening meal of tea, bread and snacks, is a daily ritual in many homes.", "The Mapuche are Chile's largest Indigenous group, and you'll see their culture in the south, so be respectful when visiting communities.", "Avoid talking up Peru or Argentina in comparison with Chile, as friendly rivalries run deep."], "safety": "Phone snatching and pickpocketing are common in central Santiago and Valparaíso, so keep your phone out of sight on the street. Chile is earthquake country, so take note of tsunami evacuation signs on the coast. In the Atacama and Patagonia, weather can change quickly, so bring proper gear and plenty of water.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the big meal from 1 to 3pm, and dinner rarely starts before 9pm.", "Tap water is safe in most cities, though it tastes heavy in the north.", "Vegetarian food is easy in Santiago and Valparaíso, but in rural areas expect cheese empanadas and salads.", "A 10 percent tip is normal and is often suggested on the bill, so just say yes when asked."], "drinks": [{"name": "Terremoto", "desc": "Sweet white wine with pineapple ice cream and grenadine, whose name means earthquake. A glass costs $4 to $7."}, {"name": "Carmenère", "desc": "Chile's signature red wine grape, rich and spicy. A good bottle is $6 to $10 at supermarkets."}], "greet": "One cheek kiss is the norm between women and between men and women, and men shake hands. Say hola and add a ¿cómo estás?", "dress": "Chileans dress fairly smart in Santiago, and people rarely wear shorts in the city. Layers are a must for cold nights.", "table": "Keep hands on the table and wait for the host to start. Don't leave before the sobremesa, the chat after the meal.", "faith": "Most Chileans are Catholic, with a growing evangelical and non-religious population. Dress modestly in churches and respect Mapuche ceremonial sites.", "phrases": [["Hola, ¿cómo estái?", "Hi, how are you? (Chilean)"], ["Gracias", "Thank you"], ["¿Cuánto vale?", "How much is it?"], ["Bacán", "Cool, great"], ["Al tiro", "Right away"]]},
  "brazil": {"around": "Long-distance buses leave from each city's rodoviária and can be booked online through apps and sites like ClickBus. Brazil is huge, so for big hops like Rio to Iguaçu or Salvador, budget flights on Azul, GOL or LATAM are often worth it if booked early. Uber and 99 work in all major cities. Ilha Grande is reached by ferry or speedboat from Conceição de Jacareí or Angra dos Reis.", "money": "Brazil runs on the real, and cards are used for almost everything, while locals pay with Pix, an instant bank transfer system that a few travel apps now let foreigners use too. You'll need little cash, but carry some for beach vendors and small towns. Use ATMs inside banks, malls or airports rather than on the street, and be aware some limit withdrawals at night. Many restaurants add a 10% service charge, and haggling is limited to markets and beach sellers.", "sleep": "Hostels and pousadas (small guesthouses) are everywhere, from party hostels in Rio and Florianópolis to chilled places in Paraty and Ilha Grande. Dorm prices are reasonable but jump massively during Carnival and New Year, when you need to book months ahead. Many hostels include breakfast, which helps the budget.", "eat": ["Feijoada: black bean and pork stew served with rice and farofa", "Pão de queijo: chewy cheese bread rolls made with cassava flour", "Coxinha: teardrop-shaped fried snack filled with shredded chicken", "Açaí na tigela: frozen açaí berry bowl topped with granola and banana"], "data": "Claro, Vivo and TIM SIMs can be registered with a passport, but the process can be fiddly, so an eSIM is often simpler. Coverage is good in cities and along the coast.", "heads_up": "Phone theft is common in big cities, so avoid using your phone near the street edge and carry a cheap spare if you can.", "food": [{"name": "Feijoada", "wiki": "Feijoada", "desc": "Black bean and pork stew served with rice, farofa, collard greens and orange slices. Traditionally eaten on Wednesdays and Saturdays, often as an all-you-can-eat lunch.", "price": "$6 to $12", "when": "Wednesday and Saturday lunch", "order": "Comes with rice, farofa, kale and orange slices"}, {"name": "Pão de queijo", "wiki": "Pão de queijo", "desc": "Chewy cheese bread balls made with cassava flour, eaten for breakfast or as a snack. Bakeries sell them warm for very little.", "price": "$0.30 to $1 each", "when": "Breakfast or afternoon snack", "order": "Best warm from a bakery"}, {"name": "Coxinha", "wiki": "Coxinha", "desc": "Teardrop-shaped fried dough filled with shredded chicken and creamy cheese. A cheap snack bar staple.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Snack from a lanchonete", "order": "Ask for it with catupiry cheese inside"}, {"name": "Açaí", "wiki": "Açai bowl", "desc": "Frozen açaí berry pulp blended thick and topped with granola and banana. Cheap and great after a day at the beach.", "price": "$3 to $6 a bowl", "when": "After the beach or gym", "order": "Choose your toppings, granola and banana are classic"}, {"name": "Acarajé", "wiki": "Acarajé", "desc": "Black-eyed pea fritters fried in palm oil and stuffed with shrimp and spicy paste. Sold by Baianas in white dresses on the streets of Salvador.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Afternoon street snack in Salvador", "order": "Say quente for hot pepper, frio for none"}, {"name": "Caipirinha", "wiki": "Caipirinha", "desc": "Cachaça, lime and sugar over ice. Beach vendors and bars sell them for a few reais, and they're stronger than they taste.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Beach evenings and weekends", "order": "Swap cachaça for vodka and it's a caipiroska"}], "food_tip": "Look for comida por quilo restaurants, where you pay by weight, or the set lunch called prato feito (PF). Lanchonetes (snack bars) are cheap too.", "culture": ["Brazilians greet with one, two or even three cheek kisses depending on the region, and are warm and touchy, so don't be surprised by hugs.", "Portuguese is spoken, not Spanish, and making an effort with a few words is appreciated much more than defaulting to Spanish.", "The thumbs-up is used constantly to mean OK or thanks, but avoid the OK hand sign, which is rude in Brazil.", "Beach culture is relaxed, but don't bring valuables, and walking around in swimwear away from the beach is frowned upon in some places.", "Time is flexible, so don't be surprised if social plans start late."], "safety": "Phone and bag theft is common in big cities, especially on beaches and busy streets, so keep your phone out of sight near the road and carry a cheap spare if you can. Only visit favelas with a reputable local-run tour. Use Uber at night rather than walking. Dengue is widespread, so use repellent, and check if you need a yellow fever vaccine for the areas you're visiting.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the big meal, from noon to 2pm, and dinner is late, often after 8pm.", "Don't drink tap water, though it's fine for brushing teeth, and filtered water is often free in hostels.", "Vegetarians can build plates at por quilo buffets, and rice, beans and salads are everywhere.", "Weekly street markets called feiras sell fruit, and stalls fry pastel, a crispy filled pastry, to eat with sugarcane juice.", "A 10% service charge is added to most restaurant bills, so you don't need to tip more."], "drinks": [{"name": "Guaraná", "desc": "A fizzy soft drink made from an Amazon berry, more popular than cola. About $1 a can."}, {"name": "Caldo de cana", "desc": "Fresh sugarcane juice pressed at markets, often with lime or pineapple. About $1 to $2 a cup."}], "greet": "Expect a hug and cheek kisses between women and between men and women, while men shake hands or back-slap. Say \"tudo bem?\" (all good?) and expect it back.", "dress": "Casual and light, with flip-flops worn everywhere. Cities like São Paulo are a bit smarter, and churches want shoulders covered.", "table": "Use a knife and fork even for pizza and burgers, and wipe your hands with a napkin rather than licking fingers. Wait to be told where to sit.", "faith": "Brazil has the world's largest Catholic population, plus fast-growing evangelical churches and Afro-Brazilian faiths like Candomblé. Be respectful at terreiros and churches, and ask before photographing ceremonies.", "phrases": [["Oi, tudo bem?", "Hi, how are you?"], ["Obrigado / obrigada", "Thank you (male / female speaker)"], ["Quanto custa?", "How much?"], ["Sem pimenta", "Without chilli"], ["Valeu", "Thanks, cheers"]]},
  "uruguay": {"around": "Buquebus, Colonia Express and Seacat ferries cross from Buenos Aires to Colonia and Montevideo, and buses from Tres Cruces terminal in Montevideo connect the whole country comfortably. Uber works in Montevideo. Cabo Polonio has no road, so you take a 4x4 truck from the entrance on Route 10.", "money": "The Uruguayan peso is the currency, and cards are accepted almost everywhere. Uruguay is one of the more expensive countries in South America, so expect higher costs than in its neighbours. Paying by foreign card in restaurants has often earned you a VAT discount, so it's worth checking if that's still running. Tipping about 10% in restaurants is normal, and haggling isn't done.", "sleep": "Hostels are common in Montevideo, Colonia and Punta del Diablo, and beach towns get busy in January and February, so book ahead then. Cabo Polonio has simple hostels without mains electricity, which fill up fast in summer.", "eat": ["Chivito: steak sandwich loaded with ham, cheese, egg and more", "Asado: beef and sausages slow-grilled over wood embers", "Mate: herbal tea sipped from a gourd, often carried with a thermos", "Torta frita: fried dough, traditionally eaten on rainy days"], "data": "Antel, Claro and Movistar SIMs are easy to buy, and coverage is good in towns. Signal is weak in Cabo Polonio and some rural spots.", "heads_up": "Cabo Polonio has no ATM and few card machines, so bring enough cash for your stay.", "food": [{"name": "Chivito", "wiki": "Chivito (sandwich)", "desc": "A massive steak sandwich loaded with ham, cheese, egg, bacon and salad, often served with fries. Uruguay's national sandwich and big enough to share.", "price": "$10 to $16", "when": "Lunch or dinner, very filling", "order": "Chivito al plato comes without bread"}, {"name": "Asado", "wiki": "Asado", "desc": "Beef, sausages and offal slow-grilled over wood embers. Visit a parrilla in Montevideo's Mercado del Puerto or join a Sunday asado if you're invited.", "price": "$15 to $25", "when": "Sunday lunch, cooked by the family", "order": "Try a parrillada, a mixed grill"}, {"name": "Mate", "wiki": "Mate (drink)", "desc": "Bitter herbal tea sipped from a gourd through a metal straw. Uruguayans carry a thermos everywhere and drink it all day.", "price": "Free when shared, $3 to $5 a bag of yerba", "when": "All day, from a thermos"}, {"name": "Fainá", "wiki": "Farinata", "desc": "A thin chickpea flatbread, often eaten on top of a pizza slice at pizzerias. A cheap, filling snack.", "price": "$1 to $2 a slice", "when": "With pizza, especially in Montevideo", "order": "Order a slice on top of your pizza"}, {"name": "Dulce de leche", "wiki": "Dulce de leche", "desc": "Thick caramel made from sweetened milk, spread on everything from toast to pastries. Supermarkets sell it in big tubs for not much.", "price": "$2 to $4 a jar", "when": "Breakfast, desserts and snacks"}, {"name": "Choripán", "wiki": "Choripán", "desc": "Grilled chorizo in a crusty roll with chimichurri, sold from street grills and at football matches.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Snacks at football matches and markets", "order": "Add chimichurri on top"}], "food_tip": "Look for menú ejecutivo set lunches at local restaurants, which are much cheaper than ordering à la carte. Cooking at your hostel saves a lot given the high restaurant prices.", "culture": ["Mate is a social ritual: if someone offers you a sip, drink it all and pass it back, and don't move the straw.", "Uruguayans are relaxed, polite and progressive, and the pace of life is slower than in Buenos Aires.", "Greetings are usually a single cheek kiss, even between men who know each other well.", "Dinner happens late, often after 9pm, and restaurants may be empty earlier.", "Carnival here is the longest in the world, with weeks of candombe drumming and murga performances, so catch some if you're there in February."], "safety": "Uruguay is one of the safest countries in South America, but petty theft happens in Montevideo's Ciudad Vieja, especially at night. Keep an eye on your bag at beaches and on buses, and watch out for strong winds and cold water on the coast.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is light, lunch from 1pm to 3pm, and dinner rarely starts before 9pm.", "Tap water is safe to drink throughout Uruguay.", "Vegetarians can find pasta, pizza, empanadas and salads, though the culture centres on meat.", "Montevideo's markets and weekend ferias sell cheap fruit, vegetables and street food.", "A 10% tip is normal in restaurants, and service is often not included."], "drinks": [{"name": "Medio y medio", "desc": "A mix of sparkling and white wine, served as an aperitif. About $6 to $10 a glass."}, {"name": "Tannat", "desc": "Uruguay's signature red wine, bold and tannic. A glass costs about $5 to $8."}], "greet": "One cheek kiss is common for everyone, even men who know each other well. Shake hands in formal situations.", "dress": "Uruguayans dress casually, but neater in Montevideo. Beachwear is fine on the coast.", "table": "Wait for everyone to be served before eating, and keep your hands on the table. Meals are long and social.", "faith": "Uruguay is one of the most secular countries in Latin America, though many people are Catholic. Be quiet in churches and dress respectfully.", "phrases": [["¿Qué tal?", "How's it going?"], ["Gracias", "Thank you"], ["¿Cuánto sale?", "How much?"], ["Ta", "Okay, in Uruguayan slang"], ["Bo", "Hey, mate"]]},
  "paraguay": {"around": "Long-distance buses from Asunción's main terminal are cheap and reach Encarnación in about six hours. Bolt and Uber work well in Asunción and are cheaper than taxis. Local buses are cheap but can be confusing, so ask locals for help. The Jesuit missions near Encarnación are reachable by local bus, though a taxi makes combining Trinidad and Jesús easier.", "money": "Paraguay uses the guaraní, and it's one of the cheapest countries in South America. Cards work in cities, but carry cash for buses, markets and small places. ATM fees per withdrawal can be high, so take out larger amounts at once. Tipping about 10% is normal in restaurants, and haggling is fine at markets.", "sleep": "Asunción and Encarnación have a few hostels and many cheap hotels, and booking ahead is rarely needed. Dorm beds are some of the cheapest in South America, and simple private rooms don't cost much more.", "eat": ["Chipa: chewy cheese and cassava bread ring", "Sopa paraguaya: dense cornbread with cheese and onion, despite the name", "Tereré: cold yerba mate infusion with herbs, drunk everywhere", "Mbejú: cassava starch and cheese pancake"], "data": "Tigo, Personal and Claro SIMs are cheap and need passport registration, and coverage is good in towns.", "heads_up": "Summer heat often goes over 35°C, so plan outdoor visits for early morning and carry water.", "food": [{"name": "Chipa", "wiki": "Chipa", "desc": "Chewy cheese bread rings made with cassava starch, sold by vendors on buses and street corners for very little.", "price": "$0.30 to $1", "when": "Breakfast and bus snacks", "order": "Buy it warm from basket sellers"}, {"name": "Sopa paraguaya", "wiki": "Sopa paraguaya", "desc": "Despite the name, it's a dense cornbread with cheese and onion, not a soup. Usually served with grilled meat.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "A side dish with asado"}, {"name": "Chipa guasu", "wiki": "Chipa guasu", "desc": "A soft, moist corn and cheese bake, often served alongside sopa paraguaya at asados.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "With grilled meat on weekends"}, {"name": "Mbejú", "wiki": "Mbeju", "desc": "A thick pancake made from cassava starch and cheese, usually eaten for breakfast or with cocido (sweetened mate).", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast or afternoons with cocido"}, {"name": "Vori vori", "wiki": "Vori vori", "desc": "A hearty soup with cornmeal and cheese balls and chicken, perfect on cooler days.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch on cold days"}, {"name": "Tereré", "wiki": "Tereré", "desc": "Yerba mate brewed with ice-cold water and often fresh herbs, drunk all day everywhere. Locals share it in a circle and offer you a sip.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "All day, shared from a guampa", "order": "Ask herb sellers for yuyos to add"}], "food_tip": "Comedores and market stalls serve cheap set lunches, and chipa from street vendors makes a filling snack for almost nothing.", "culture": ["Most Paraguayans speak both Spanish and Guaraní, and using a word or two of Guaraní, like \"mba'éichapa\" (how are you), will get big smiles.", "Tereré is a social ritual: when offered, drink it all and hand it back, and say thank you only when you've had enough.", "Siesta is common, especially in smaller towns, so many shops close in the early afternoon.", "Paraguayans are generally reserved but friendly, and greet each other with a handshake or cheek kiss.", "Dress modestly in churches and rural areas, as the country is quite conservative and mostly Catholic."], "safety": "Paraguay is generally safe for travellers, but watch for pickpockets in Asunción and on buses. Avoid border areas known for smuggling, such as around Pedro Juan Caballero. Summer heat is intense, and dengue is a risk, so use repellent.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal around noon, followed by a siesta, and dinner runs from 8 or 9pm.", "Tap water in Asunción is generally safe, but many people prefer bottled.", "Vegetarians can live on chipa, sopa paraguaya, mbejú and salads, though stews often contain meat.", "Tipping 10 percent is normal in restaurants.", "Sunday asado is a social event, and if you're invited, bring drinks or a side."], "drinks": [{"name": "Cocido", "desc": "Toasted yerba mate boiled with sugar and milk, a breakfast staple for about $0.50 to $1."}, {"name": "Mosto", "desc": "Fresh sugarcane juice sold from carts, sweet and cold, for $0.50 to $1."}], "greet": "Greet everyone when you arrive, and answer 'mba'éichapa' with 'iporã' (fine).", "dress": "It's hot most of the year, so light clothes work, but winter evenings from June to August can be chilly. Cover up in churches.", "table": "Wait for the host to start, and say 'buen provecho' to people eating. Asado is eaten slowly over hours, so don't rush.", "faith": "Around 90 percent of Paraguayans are Catholic, with Mennonite communities in the Chaco. On 8 December, huge crowds walk to Caacupé for the Virgin's feast day, so expect busy roads.", "phrases": [["Mba'éichapa", "How are you? (Guaraní)"], ["Aguyje", "Thank you (Guaraní)"], ["¿Cuánto cuesta?", "How much is it? (Spanish)"], ["Iporã", "Good, fine (Guaraní)"], ["Gracias", "Thank you (Spanish)"]]},
  "venezuela": {"around": "Canaima, Angel Falls and the Gran Sabana are mostly reached by small domestic flights or long road trips, usually arranged as packages through established operators. Many governments advise against travel to some states and border areas, and road travel at night is a bad idea. Local apps like Ridery and Yummy are used in Caracas instead of international ride-hailing apps. Police and military checkpoints are common on the roads, so keep your passport handy.", "money": "US dollars in small, clean notes are used for most everyday payments, and prices are often quoted in dollars even when you could pay in bolívares. Foreign cards often don't work, and ATMs are unreliable, so bring all the cash you'll need. Change for big notes is hard to get, so carry plenty of ones, fives and tens. Tipping is appreciated, especially for guides on multi-day treks.", "sleep": "Lodging is mostly posadas (small guesthouses) and tour-run camps, especially in Canaima and around Roraima. Supply is limited, so most travellers book accommodation as part of a tour.", "eat": ["Arepa: corn cake split and stuffed with fillings", "Pabellón criollo: shredded beef, black beans, rice and fried plantain", "Cachapa: sweet corn pancake folded around soft cheese", "Tequeños: fried dough sticks wrapped around white cheese"], "data": "Movistar, Digitel and Movilnet SIMs need ID registration, and coverage is weak or absent in Canaima and the Gran Sabana. Power cuts can also take out networks.", "heads_up": "Power, water and fuel outages are frequent, and the June 2026 earthquakes damaged some infrastructure, so check conditions with any operator before you go.", "food": [{"name": "Arepa", "wiki": "Arepa", "desc": "A grilled corn cake split and stuffed with anything from cheese and shredded beef to black beans. Areperas sell them all day for a few dollars.", "price": "$1.50 to $4", "when": "Breakfast or dinner, at areperas", "order": "Try a \"reina pepiada\", chicken and avocado"}, {"name": "Pabellón criollo", "wiki": "Pabellón criollo", "desc": "Shredded beef, black beans, rice and fried plantain, Venezuela's national dish and a big, filling plate.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Lunch, at local restaurants", "order": "Ask for \"a caballo\" for a fried egg on top"}, {"name": "Cachapa", "wiki": "Cachapa", "desc": "A sweet corn pancake folded around soft white cheese, sold at roadside stands.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Snack, from roadside stands", "order": "Ask for extra queso de mano"}, {"name": "Tequeños", "wiki": "Tequeño", "desc": "Fried dough sticks wrapped around white cheese, served at every party and in bakeries.", "price": "$1 to $3 per portion", "when": "Parties and bakeries", "order": "Dip in garlic or guasacaca sauce"}, {"name": "Empanadas", "wiki": "Empanada", "desc": "Venezuelan empanadas use crispy corn dough and come filled with cheese, beef, chicken or fish. A cheap breakfast from street stalls.", "price": "$0.70 to $1.50", "when": "Breakfast, from street stalls", "order": "Cheese, beef or cazón (shark) fillings are popular"}, {"name": "Hallaca", "wiki": "Hallaca", "desc": "Corn dough filled with a stew of meats, raisins and olives, wrapped in banana leaves and eaten at Christmas.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Christmas season, at home"}], "food_tip": "Areperas and street stalls are the cheapest way to eat well. Ask your posada or guide where locals eat.", "culture": ["Venezuelans are warm, social and proud of their food, music and baseball, and are often delighted to meet travellers.", "Politics is a deeply sensitive topic, so avoid raising it unless your host brings it up.", "Greetings usually involve a handshake or a single cheek kiss, and calling people \"chamo\" or \"pana\" is friendly slang.", "Dress modestly in churches and smaller towns, and take your cue from locals on how formal to be."], "safety": "Many governments advise against or warn strongly about travel to Venezuela because of crime, political instability and wrongful detention risks, and the situation has shifted a lot during 2026, so check the latest advice. Express kidnappings and armed robbery are real risks in Caracas, so use trusted drivers and don't flash valuables. Malaria is present in some areas, and drinking water should be bottled or treated.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch, the main meal, runs from about noon to 2pm, and dinner is lighter, often an arepa.", "Don't drink tap water; use bottled or filtered water.", "US dollars are widely used alongside bolívars, and small notes are useful.", "Vegetarians can order arepas with cheese, beans or avocado.", "Tipping 10 percent is normal in restaurants, and some add it to the bill."], "drinks": [{"name": "Malta", "desc": "A sweet, non-alcoholic malt drink, popular with breakfast, about $1."}, {"name": "Venezuelan rum", "desc": "Some of the region's best rum, aged for years, about $2 to $4 a glass."}], "greet": "A handshake or a single cheek kiss is normal, and greetings are warm and often loud. \"¿Qué más?\" means \"What's up?\"", "dress": "Venezuelans dress stylishly, and looking neat matters. Cover shoulders and knees in churches and small towns.", "table": "Meals are relaxed and sociable, and wait for the host to start. Saying \"buen provecho\" before eating is polite.", "faith": "Most Venezuelans are Catholic, with a growing evangelical community. Be quiet in churches, and dress modestly.", "phrases": [["¿Qué más?", "What's up?"], ["Chévere", "Cool, great"], ["¿Cuánto cuesta?", "How much does it cost?"], ["Pana", "Friend, buddy"], ["Gracias", "Thank you"]]},
  "guyana": {"around": "Numbered minibuses run around Georgetown and along the coast, and they're cheap but drive fast. The Rupununi is reached by small plane or a long, rough road trip to Lethem, and Kaieteur Falls is usually done as a day flight from Georgetown, which only goes when enough seats fill. Use taxis recommended by your accommodation rather than flagging one down.", "money": "Carry Guyanese dollars in cash, especially if you're heading into the interior, as cards work mainly at Georgetown hotels and supermarkets. US dollars can be changed at cambios in the capital, which usually give a better rate than banks. ATMs exist in Georgetown but not all take foreign cards. Tipping 10% is common in restaurants, and haggling is fine at markets like Stabroek.", "sleep": "Georgetown has guesthouses and small hotels, but no real hostel scene. In the Rupununi, eco-lodges and ranches are the main options, and they cost more than elsewhere in the region.", "eat": ["Pepperpot: dark meat stew flavored with cassareep, a cassava sauce", "Cook-up rice: rice cooked with beans, coconut milk and meat", "Roti and curry: flatbread with curried chicken, goat or duck", "Metemgee: hearty stew of root vegetables and dumplings in coconut milk"], "data": "GTT and Digicel SIMs are easy to buy in Georgetown with a passport, but coverage drops sharply once you leave the coast.", "heads_up": "Malaria is present in the interior, so talk to a doctor about prevention and use insect repellent.", "food": [{"name": "Pepperpot", "wiki": "Guyanese pepperpot", "desc": "A dark, rich stew of meat flavoured with cassareep, a sauce made from cassava. Traditionally eaten at Christmas with bread.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Christmas morning and family gatherings", "order": "Ask for plait bread to mop up the sauce"}, {"name": "Roti and curry", "wiki": "Roti", "desc": "Soft flatbread with curried chicken, goat or duck, sold cheaply at roti shops and markets.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch, at roti shops", "order": "Ask for dhal puri roti"}, {"name": "Pholourie", "wiki": "Pholourie", "desc": "Fried split-pea dough balls served with sour mango chutney, a cheap street snack.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Snack, from street vendors", "order": "Ask for extra sour"}, {"name": "Black cake", "wiki": "Rum cake", "desc": "Dense, rum-soaked fruit cake made at Christmas, often with El Dorado rum.", "price": "$3 to $5 a slice", "when": "Christmas and weddings"}, {"name": "El Dorado rum", "wiki": "Demerara Distillers", "desc": "Guyana's famous Demerara rum, cheap locally and great with coconut water.", "price": "$3 to $5 a drink", "when": "Evenings, at rum shops", "order": "Try it with coconut water"}, {"name": "Mauby", "wiki": "Mauby", "desc": "A bittersweet drink made from tree bark and spices, sold cold at snack shops.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Any time, from snackettes"}], "food_tip": "Snackettes and market stalls serve filling plates of cook-up rice, roti and curry at local prices. Stabroek Market is good for fruit and street food, but watch your bag there.", "culture": ["Guyana is English-speaking with strong Caribbean culture and a mix of Indo-, Afro-, Amerindian and other communities.", "Holidays like Mashramani, Phagwah and Diwali are big events, and travellers are often welcome to join in.", "Amerindian villages may require permission to visit, so travel with a guide or check with village councils.", "Greet people before diving into questions, and expect conversations to be friendly and relaxed."], "safety": "Georgetown has a reputation for petty crime and robbery, so don't walk around at night or flash valuables, and use trusted taxis. Malaria is present in the interior, so ask a doctor about prevention. Interior travel needs good planning and local guides.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, and Sunday cook-up rice is a family tradition.", "Snackettes sell cheap snacks like cheese rolls, pine tarts and patties.", "Don't drink tap water; use bottled or filtered water.", "Vegetarian food is easy thanks to the large Indo-Guyanese community, with dhal, curried vegetables and roti everywhere.", "Tip about 10% in restaurants, and check if a service charge is already added."], "drinks": [{"name": "Coconut water", "desc": "Fresh from the green nut, chopped open by vendors along the sea wall. About $1 to $1.50 each."}, {"name": "Swank", "desc": "A simple lime and sugar drink, Guyana's homemade lemonade. Expect $0.50 to $1 a glass."}], "greet": "Say good morning or good afternoon, and shake hands. People are friendly and chatty, so a bit of small talk before business is normal.", "dress": "Wear light clothes for the heat and humidity, and cover up for churches, mosques and mandirs.", "table": "Meals are relaxed and often shared, and roti is eaten with your right hand, tearing pieces to scoop curry. At Hindu homes, you may eat off a lotus or banana leaf at religious events.", "faith": "Guyana is mostly Christian, with large Hindu and Muslim communities. Remove shoes at mandirs and mosques, and dress modestly.", "phrases": [["Wuh happenin?", "What's up?"], ["Lime", "Hang out with friends"], ["Gyaff", "Chat, banter"], ["Bai", "Mate, buddy"], ["Me deh", "I'm here, I'm fine"]]},
  "suriname": {"around": "Cheap minibuses run from Paramaribo's central stops on fixed routes, and they drive on the left here. Trips into the interior are usually tours that combine a road transfer and a dugout boat ride, so book a few days ahead. Taxis don't use meters, so agree the price first.", "money": "Carry Surinamese dollars in cash, changed at licensed cambios, and bring euros or US dollars as backup, as cards mainly work at larger businesses. The exchange rate can move around, so check it when you arrive. Tipping isn't strongly expected but is welcome, and haggling is fine at markets.", "sleep": "Paramaribo has a handful of guesthouses and hostel-style places, many in old wooden colonial buildings. Interior lodges usually include meals and offer hammocks or simple huts.", "eat": ["Roti: flatbread with curried chicken, potatoes and long beans", "Pom: baked casserole of pomtajer root and chicken", "Moksi alesi: mixed rice with beans and salted meat or fish", "Saoto soup: Javanese chicken soup with bean sprouts and egg"], "data": "Digicel and Telesur SIMs are easy to buy in Paramaribo, but coverage fades quickly once you head upriver.", "heads_up": "Use insect repellent and cover up at dusk, as dengue and other mosquito-borne illnesses occur.", "food": [{"name": "Pom", "wiki": "Pom (dish)", "desc": "A baked casserole of grated pomtajer root and chicken, often eaten on a sandwich called broodje pom.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Birthdays, and in a sandwich any day", "order": "Order a broodje pom at sandwich shops"}, {"name": "Roti", "wiki": "Roti", "desc": "Flatbread with curried chicken, potatoes and long beans, sold cheaply at roti shops.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Lunch at roti shops", "order": "Kip (chicken) roti is the classic"}, {"name": "Saoto soup", "wiki": "Soto (food)", "desc": "Javanese chicken soup with bean sprouts, egg and fried potato sticks, a cheap and popular meal.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Lunch or late night, at warungs", "order": "Add sambal and sweet soy to taste"}, {"name": "Bami", "wiki": "Mie goreng", "desc": "Javanese-style fried noodles, sold at warungs around Paramaribo.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch at warungs"}, {"name": "Nasi goreng", "wiki": "Nasi goreng", "desc": "Fried rice with vegetables and chicken or shrimp, another Javanese favourite.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch at warungs"}, {"name": "Pindasoep", "wiki": "Peanut soup", "desc": "A rich peanut soup often served with tom-tom (plantain dumplings).", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Weekends and Creole celebrations"}], "food_tip": "Warungs in Paramaribo are the cheapest places to eat, especially in the Blauwgrond area. Look for lunchtime specials.", "culture": ["Suriname is one of the most multicultural places in South America, with Indian, Javanese, Maroon, Creole, Chinese and Indigenous communities.", "Dutch is the official language, but most people speak Sranan Tongo, and many also speak English.", "In Maroon and Indigenous villages, follow your guide's lead, ask before photographing people, and respect local customs.", "Keti Koti, July 1, marks the end of slavery and is an important day of celebration and remembrance."], "safety": "Paramaribo is mostly safe, but watch for petty theft and avoid walking in quiet areas at night. Dengue and other mosquito-borne illnesses occur, so use repellent, especially at dusk. Interior travel should be with experienced guides.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is the main meal, and many warungs open from late morning until mid afternoon.", "Tap water in Paramaribo is generally safe, but drink bottled in the interior.", "Vegetarians do well with vegetable roti, bara (fried lentil dough) and Javanese tempeh dishes.", "Paramaribo's central market is the cheapest place for fruit and snacks, and it's busiest early.", "Tipping isn't expected at warungs, but 10 percent is appreciated in restaurants."], "drinks": [{"name": "Dawet", "desc": "A sweet pink Javanese drink of coconut milk, lemongrass syrup and jelly. A glass is about $1 to $2."}, {"name": "Gemberbier", "desc": "Homemade ginger beer, very popular around holidays. A bottle costs around $1."}], "greet": "Shake hands with everyone when you arrive, and friends kiss on the cheek. Fa waka? is the Sranan Tongo way to ask how it's going.", "dress": "Light, airy clothes suit the heat, but cover up for mosques and Hindu temples and when visiting villages in the interior.", "table": "Wait to be served, and follow your host on whether to eat with hands or cutlery, as it varies by community.", "faith": "Christians, Hindus and Muslims are the biggest groups, alongside Winti, an Afro-Surinamese religion. In Paramaribo a mosque and synagogue stand side by side; remove shoes in mosques and temples.", "phrases": [["Fa waka?", "How's it going?"], ["Mi de bun", "I'm fine"], ["Grantangi", "Thank you"], ["Omeni a de?", "How much is it?"]]},
  "australia": {"around": "Greyhound buses cover the east coast with hop-on hop-off passes, and campervan relocation deals can be very cheap if your dates are flexible. Domestic flights on Jetstar, Virgin Australia and Qantas save days on long hops like Cairns to Uluru or Sydney to Perth, especially if you book in sales. Uber and DiDi operate in cities, and public transport in major cities increasingly takes contactless cards. Book Uluru tours and Whitsunday sailing trips ahead in peak season.", "money": "Australia uses the Australian dollar, and contactless cards and phone payments work almost everywhere, even at markets and tiny cafes. Watch for card surcharges at some places, often 1 to 2%. ATMs from the big four banks (CommBank, Westpac, ANZ, NAB) are usually cheaper than standalone machines, which can charge a few dollars a pop. Tipping isn't expected, though rounding up for great service is nice, and haggling isn't done.", "sleep": "Hostel dorms are everywhere along the east coast and in cities, though prices are high compared with Asia. Campgrounds and free rest areas suit people with a campervan, and many backpackers on working holiday visas do work-for-accommodation. Book ahead during school holidays and around Christmas.", "eat": ["Meat pie: hand-sized pastry filled with minced beef and gravy", "Vegemite toast: buttered toast with a thin layer of yeast spread", "Flat white: espresso with velvety steamed milk", "Barramundi: mild white fish, often grilled or battered"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Telstra, Optus or Vodafone are sold everywhere and need ID verification. Telstra has the best coverage in the outback, but expect long stretches with no signal.", "heads_up": "The sun is strong, so wear sunscreen and a hat, and only swim between the red and yellow flags on patrolled beaches.", "food": [{"name": "Meat pie", "wiki": "Meat pie (Australia and New Zealand)", "desc": "A hand-sized pastry filled with minced beef and gravy, ideally with tomato sauce on top. Bakeries and servos sell them for a few dollars.", "price": "$4 to $7", "when": "Lunch on the go, or at footy", "order": "Try a pie floater in Adelaide"}, {"name": "Sausage sizzle", "wiki": "Sausage sizzle", "desc": "A sausage on a slice of white bread with onions and sauce, sold at Bunnings and community fundraisers for a couple of dollars.", "price": "$2 to $3", "when": "Weekend mornings, at fundraisers", "order": "Ask for onions under the sausage"}, {"name": "Vegemite on toast", "wiki": "Vegemite", "desc": "A salty yeast spread on buttered toast. Spread it thin or you'll regret it.", "price": "$2 to $5 at cafes", "when": "Breakfast at home or cafes", "order": "Add avocado or cheese"}, {"name": "Flat white", "wiki": "Flat white", "desc": "Espresso with velvety steamed milk, and Australians take their coffee very seriously, especially in Melbourne.", "price": "$4.50 to $6", "when": "Morning, at cafes"}, {"name": "Chicken parma", "wiki": "Chicken parmesan", "desc": "Breaded chicken topped with tomato sauce and cheese, a pub classic often on cheap weeknight specials.", "price": "$15 to $25", "when": "Pub dinner, often on parma nights", "order": "Ask for it with chips and salad"}, {"name": "Tim Tams", "wiki": "Tim Tam", "desc": "Chocolate biscuits, ideally eaten with a \"Tim Tam slam\" through a cup of hot coffee or tea.", "price": "$3 to $5 a pack", "when": "Snack, from supermarkets", "order": "Bite off opposite corners before slamming"}], "food_tip": "Cook at your hostel and shop at Aldi, Coles or Woolworths to save big. Pub schnitzel and parma nights are the cheapest way to eat out.", "culture": ["Australians are relaxed and informal, and \"no worries\" really is the default response.", "Buying a \"shout\", a round of drinks, is a social expectation, so take your turn.", "Show respect for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander cultures, including not climbing Uluru, which has been banned since 2019, and following signs at sacred sites.", "Swearing is common and often friendly, but read the room before joining in.", "Queueing and fairness matter, so don't jump lines."], "safety": "Australia is safe overall, and the biggest dangers are environmental: strong sun, rip currents, heat in the outback and wildlife like jellyfish and crocodiles in the north. Only swim between the red and yellow flags on patrolled beaches. Be careful of job scams and exploitation in farm work, and check employers with other backpackers.", "eat_beats": ["Cafes are busiest at breakfast and brunch, and many close by 3pm, while dinner is usually 6pm to 8pm.", "Farmers' markets and Asian food courts in cities are the cheapest ways to eat out well.", "Tap water is safe everywhere, and you can ask for free tap water at any restaurant or bar.", "Vegetarian and vegan food is everywhere in cities, and menus often mark V, VG and GF.", "Tipping isn't expected because wages are high, though some people leave 10% for great service."], "drinks": [{"name": "Schooner", "desc": "The standard pub glass of beer in most states, about 425ml. Expect $9 to $13 in a city pub."}, {"name": "Lemon, lime and bitters", "desc": "A pub classic of lemonade, lime cordial and Angostura bitters, popular with drivers. Expect $5 to $7."}], "greet": "A casual g'day or how ya going is standard, often with a quick handshake. People use first names right away.", "dress": "Australians dress casually, and thongs (flip-flops) are fine almost everywhere. Some pubs and clubs have dress codes at night, and you should cover up away from the beach.", "table": "Meals are relaxed, and BYO restaurants let you bring your own wine for a small corkage fee. At barbecues, bring something to share, often meat or drinks.", "faith": "Australia is diverse and increasingly non-religious, with Christianity the largest faith. Aboriginal sacred sites like parts of Uluru have signs asking you not to photograph or enter, so follow them.", "phrases": [["G'day", "Hello"], ["How ya going?", "How are you?"], ["Arvo", "Afternoon"], ["Servo", "Petrol station"], ["Heaps good", "Really good"]]},
  "new-zealand": {"around": "InterCity buses link most towns, and campervan rentals or relocation deals are a popular way to travel, especially on the South Island. The Interislander and Bluebridge ferries cross between Wellington and Picton. Uber operates in bigger cities. Great Walk huts and campsites sell out months ahead for summer, so book early.", "money": "New Zealand uses the New Zealand dollar, and cards and contactless payments work almost everywhere, though some places add a small surcharge. ATMs are easy to find. Tipping isn't expected, but you can tip for great service, and prices are fixed, so no haggling.", "sleep": "Hostels with dorms are found in every tourist town, and Department of Conservation campsites are a cheap option. Freedom camping is restricted, and many spots now need a certified self-contained vehicle. Book ahead in Queenstown and Wānaka in summer and ski season.", "eat": ["Mince and cheese pie: bakery pie with beef and melted cheese", "Hāngī: Māori feast of meat and vegetables cooked underground", "Hokey pokey ice cream: vanilla ice cream with honeycomb toffee pieces", "Pavlova: meringue dessert topped with cream and fruit"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from One NZ, Spark or 2degrees are easy to buy at the airport with little paperwork, and coverage is good in towns but patchy in the mountains and national parks.", "heads_up": "Declare all food and outdoor gear when you arrive, as biosecurity rules are strict and undeclared items earn instant fines.", "food": [{"name": "Mince and cheese pie", "wiki": "Meat pie (Australia and New Zealand)", "desc": "A bakery pie filled with beef mince and melted cheese, a cheap and filling lunch.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch, from bakeries and petrol stations", "order": "Steak and cheese is the other classic"}, {"name": "Hāngī", "wiki": "Hāngī", "desc": "Māori meal of meat and vegetables cooked underground on hot stones. Try it at a cultural experience in Rotorua.", "price": "$12 to $20", "when": "Weekend markets, fundraisers and marae events", "order": "Hāngī packs at markets cost far less than tourist shows"}, {"name": "Fish and chips", "wiki": "Fish and chips", "desc": "Kiwi fish and chips are usually made with local fish like tarakihi or blue cod, and a cheap feed by the beach.", "price": "$6 to $12", "when": "Friday night, eaten on the beach", "order": "One scoop of chips often feeds two"}, {"name": "Hokey pokey ice cream", "wiki": "Hokey pokey (ice cream)", "desc": "Vanilla ice cream with crunchy honeycomb toffee pieces.", "price": "$3 to $5", "when": "Summer afternoons, from the dairy"}, {"name": "Pavlova", "wiki": "Pavlova", "desc": "Meringue dessert topped with cream and fruit, and yes, Kiwis say it's theirs.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Christmas and summer barbecues", "order": "Supermarkets sell ready-made bases cheaply"}, {"name": "L&P", "wiki": "Lemon & Paeroa", "desc": "A sweet lemon soft drink originally from Paeroa, sold in every dairy (corner shop).", "price": "$1.50 to $3", "when": "Any time, with fish and chips"}], "food_tip": "Supermarkets like Pak'nSave are the cheapest for self-catering. Bakeries offer cheap pies and sandwiches.", "culture": ["Māori culture is central to New Zealand, so learn a few words like \"kia ora\" (hello) and respect local customs.", "At a marae or cultural event, follow your hosts' lead, as there may be a pōwhiri welcome and a hongi (pressing noses).", "Don't sit on tables or touch people's heads, as both are considered disrespectful in Māori culture.", "Kiwis are friendly and down to earth, and humility goes over well.", "Many homes ask you to take shoes off at the door."], "safety": "New Zealand is very safe, but weather in the mountains changes fast, so always check forecasts and tell someone your plans. Car break-ins happen at trailheads, so don't leave valuables in vans. Sandflies are a nuisance, so bring repellent.", "eat_beats": ["Kiwis eat dinner early, often 6 to 7pm, and kitchens in small towns can close by 8:30pm.", "Tap water is safe almost everywhere, though some campsites and huts ask you to boil or treat it.", "Vegetarian and vegan options are common in cities, and most cafés label vegan and gluten free dishes.", "Tipping isn't expected, since staff earn at least minimum wage, but people sometimes round up for great service.", "Weekend farmers' markets are good for cheap fruit, and roadside honesty boxes sell produce in the countryside."], "drinks": [{"name": "Flat white", "desc": "Espresso with silky steamed milk, claimed by both Kiwis and Australians as their invention. It costs about $3 to $4 at any café."}, {"name": "Sauvignon blanc", "desc": "Marlborough's crisp white wine is famous worldwide. A decent supermarket bottle is $8 to $15."}], "greet": "A kia ora with a smile or a handshake works anywhere, and Kiwis switch to first names fast. Gidday and how's it going are the casual hellos.", "dress": "Dress is casual and practical, with layers and a rain jacket since the weather changes quickly. Bare feet are normal in some small towns, but cover up beyond swimwear away from the beach.", "table": "If invited to a barbecue, bring a plate (a dish to share) and your own drinks. On a marae, food is eaten in the dining hall, never in the meeting house.", "faith": "About half of Kiwis say they have no religion, Christianity is the largest faith, and Māori beliefs shape many places. Some sites are tapu (sacred), so follow signs at places like Cape Reinga and keep off areas you're asked to avoid.", "phrases": [["Sweet as", "Great, no worries"], ["Chur", "Thanks, cheers"], ["Jandals", "Flip-flops"], ["Chilly bin", "Cool box"], ["Dairy", "Corner shop"]]},
  "fiji": {"around": "Cheap local buses circle Viti Levu, and minibuses and taxis fill the gaps around Nadi. To reach the Yasawas, the Yasawa Flyer catamaran stops at island resorts daily, and an island-hopping pass can save money. Book ahead in the busy June to September season.", "money": "Fiji uses the Fijian dollar, and cards work at bigger hotels and shops in Nadi and Suva. There are no ATMs in the Yasawas, so take out enough cash before leaving. Tipping isn't expected, though many resorts have a staff Christmas fund box. Haggling is normal only at handicraft markets.", "sleep": "Budget island resorts in the Yasawas offer dorms and simple bungalows, usually with a required meal plan. In Nadi, hostels near Wailoaloa Beach are a cheap base.", "eat": ["Kokoda: raw fish cured in lime juice and coconut cream", "Lovo: meat and root vegetables cooked in an earth oven", "Kava: earthy root drink shared in a ceremonial bowl", "Roti and curry: Indo-Fijian curry wrapped or served with flatbread"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Vodafone Fiji or Digicel are sold at Nadi airport with your passport, with good coverage on the main islands and patchy signal on outer islands.", "heads_up": "When visiting a village, bring a gift of kava root for the sevusevu welcome, cover your shoulders and knees, and take your hat off.", "food": [{"name": "Kokoda", "wiki": "'Ota 'ika", "desc": "Raw fish cured in lime juice and coconut cream, served in a coconut shell at local spots.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Lunch or a starter", "order": "Ask for it with fresh chilli"}, {"name": "Lovo", "wiki": "Earth oven", "desc": "Meat and root vegetables cooked in an underground oven, often at village feasts or resort lovo nights.", "price": "$15 to $30", "when": "Sunday feasts and village events"}, {"name": "Kava", "wiki": "Kava", "desc": "An earthy, slightly numbing root drink shared in a ceremonial bowl. You'll be offered it in villages.", "price": "$5 to $10 for a sevusevu bundle", "when": "Evenings in villages", "order": "Clap once, say bula, drink in one go"}, {"name": "Rourou", "wiki": "Lūʻau (food)", "desc": "Taro leaves cooked in coconut milk, sometimes with fish or meat.", "price": "$2 to $4", "when": "Lunch or dinner"}, {"name": "Roti and curry", "wiki": "Roti", "desc": "Indo-Fijian curry wrapped in or served with flatbread, cheap and filling.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Lunch at curry houses", "order": "Ask for it with dhal"}], "food_tip": "Eat at local curry houses in Nadi and Suva for cheap meals. Markets are great for fruit.", "culture": ["\"Bula\" is the friendly greeting you'll hear everywhere, so say it back.", "When visiting a village, bring kava root for the sevusevu welcome, cover your shoulders and knees, and remove your hat.", "Touching someone's head is considered rude.", "Sunday is a day of rest and church, so many businesses close.", "\"Fiji time\" is real, so relax and don't expect things to run to schedule."], "safety": "Fiji is generally safe, but watch out for \"sword sellers\" who carve your name on a souvenir and then demand payment. Use reef shoes and be careful of strong currents. Dengue occurs, so use repellent.", "eat_beats": ["Breakfast is early, lunch is the main meal from noon, and many local places close in the early evening.", "Tap water is generally safe in Nadi and Suva, but bottled or boiled water is safer elsewhere.", "Vegetarians do well thanks to Indo-Fijian curries, dhal and roti.", "Tipping isn't expected, but rounding up or 5 to 10 percent is welcome.", "Markets in Suva and Nadi sell cheap fruit, coconuts and roadside snacks."], "drinks": [{"name": "Coconut water", "desc": "Fresh green coconuts sold at markets and roadside stalls for about $1."}, {"name": "Lemon leaf tea", "desc": "Tea brewed with fresh lemon leaves, often served at villages and homes, for about $0.50 a cup."}], "greet": "Say bula with a big smile, and a handshake is normal. In villages, people also say yadra in the morning.", "dress": "Cover shoulders and knees in villages, and remove hats and sunglasses. Bring a sulu (sarong) to wear.", "table": "Wait to be invited to eat, and sit cross-legged on mats in villages. Guests are served first, but don't start until a prayer is said.", "faith": "Most Fijians are Christian, with large Hindu and Muslim Indo-Fijian communities. Dress modestly in churches, temples and mosques, and remove shoes where asked.", "phrases": [["Bula", "Hello"], ["Vinaka", "Thank you"], ["E vica?", "How much?"], ["Moce", "Goodbye"], ["Io", "Yes"]]},
  "papua-new-guinea": {"around": "Domestic flights on Air Niugini and PNG Air are the main way between regions, since no road links Port Moresby with the Highlands, and fares are steep, so book early and expect delays. PMVs, the local minibuses, run in towns and on the Highlands Highway, but in Port Moresby and Lae it's safer to use transport arranged by your accommodation. Book the Kokoda Track with a licensed operator well ahead, and arrange Sepik River trips through a trusted local guide.", "money": "The kina is the currency, and you'll need cash for almost everything outside hotels and bigger supermarkets. ATMs from BSP and Kina Bank are found in larger towns only, and they can run out, so withdraw plenty before heading anywhere remote and carry small notes. PNG is not a cheap country, mostly because of flights and accommodation. Tipping isn't customary, and haggling isn't common at markets, where prices are fixed and fair.", "sleep": "Hotels in Port Moresby are expensive even at the budget end, so many travellers just pass through. Elsewhere, guesthouses, mission stays and village homestays are cheaper and far more memorable. On the Sepik and in the Highlands, simple village guesthouses with shared facilities are the norm.", "eat": ["Mumu: pork and vegetables cooked on hot stones underground", "Kaukau: sweet potato, the Highlands staple food", "Sago: starch from the sago palm, a staple along the Sepik", "Aibika: leafy greens often cooked in coconut milk"], "data": "Digicel has the widest network, with Vodafone as the other main option, and SIMs need registration with your passport. Coverage is fine in towns but limited or absent in remote areas and on trails.", "heads_up": "Avoid walking around Port Moresby and Lae after dark, and use transport arranged by your hotel or guide.", "food": [{"name": "Mumu", "wiki": "Earth oven", "desc": "Pork, chicken, sweet potato and greens wrapped in banana leaves and cooked on hot stones underground. Usually made for celebrations and village gatherings.", "price": "$5 to $15 per person", "when": "Feasts, weddings and community events"}, {"name": "Kaukau", "wiki": "Sweet potato", "desc": "Sweet potato is the Highlands staple, roasted in the fire or boiled, and sold in heaps at every market for a few kina.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Daily staple, roasted or boiled", "order": "Try it roasted on coals at markets"}, {"name": "Sago", "wiki": "Sago", "desc": "A starch pounded from the sago palm and cooked into pancakes or jelly-like lumps, the staple along the Sepik River.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Daily staple in the Sepik and lowlands", "order": "Eat it with fish or coconut"}, {"name": "Aibika", "wiki": "Abelmoschus manihot", "desc": "Leafy greens often cooked in coconut milk with other vegetables. A common side at local meals.", "price": "$0.30 to $1 a bunch", "when": "Daily side, cooked in coconut milk"}, {"name": "Kulau", "wiki": "Coconut water", "desc": "Fresh green coconuts, opened with a bush knife and sold cheaply at markets and roadsides. The best thirst-quencher in the heat.", "price": "$0.50 to $1", "when": "Any time, from markets"}], "food_tip": "Local markets are the cheapest and most fun places to eat, with fresh fruit, roasted kaukau and smoked fish for small change. Kai bars (cheap takeaway shops) sell rice and stew plates in towns.", "culture": ["PNG has more than 800 languages, but Tok Pisin is the common tongue, and a few words like \"moning\" (good morning) and \"tenkyu\" (thank you) go a long way.", "Almost all land is customary land owned by clans, so always ask before entering, swimming or taking photos, and expect to pay small fees.", "Dress modestly, with women covering their thighs, as PNG is quite conservative and churchgoing.", "Chewing buai (betel nut) is everywhere, but spitting the red juice in public is banned in some towns, so be aware if you try it.", "The wantok system, where people support relatives and those from their language group, shapes daily life, so expect hospitality but also obligations."], "safety": "Crime is a real concern in Port Moresby, Lae and Mount Hagen, so avoid walking around after dark and use transport arranged by your hotel or guide. Tribal fighting flares up in parts of the Highlands, so follow local advice. Malaria is present in lowland areas, so take precautions and talk to a doctor before you go.", "eat_beats": ["Meals are simple and often eaten twice a day, with lunch and dinner the main ones.", "Don't drink tap water, so carry purification tablets or a filter.", "Vegetarians do well on kaukau, greens, fruit and coconut, which are everyday staples.", "Markets close early, so go in the morning for the best fruit and smoked fish.", "Tipping isn't customary, and offering money for help can create obligations, so ask first."], "drinks": [{"name": "PNG coffee", "desc": "Highland arabica from Goroka and Mount Hagen, among the best in the Pacific. About $1 to $2 a cup in towns."}, {"name": "Lemon grass tea", "desc": "Fresh lemongrass boiled with water and sugar, served in villages. Usually free when offered."}], "greet": "Shake hands, and say \"moning\" or \"apinun\" (good afternoon) in Tok Pisin. A long, light handshake is common.", "dress": "Women should cover thighs and knees, and men wear shirts in towns. Swimwear is for the beach only.", "table": "Food is often shared, so accept what's offered and use your right hand. Feasts are communal and long.", "faith": "Over 95% of Papua New Guineans are Christian, with traditional beliefs still important. Ask before entering spirit houses (haus tambaran), and don't touch sacred objects.", "phrases": [["Moning", "Good morning"], ["Tenkyu", "Thank you"], ["Hamas?", "How much?"], ["Gutpela", "Good"], ["Lukim yu", "See you"]]},
  "samoa": {"around": "Colourful wooden buses run from Apia around Upolu, playing loud music and running on their own schedule, and they stop early in the afternoon and barely run on Sundays. Taxis are cheap if you agree the fare first. The car ferry from Mulifanua wharf crosses to Salelologa on Savai'i several times a day. Renting a car gives the most freedom, but you'll need a temporary local licence, and they drive on the left.", "money": "The Samoan tālā is the currency, and you'll need cash for beach fales, buses and village entry fees. ATMs are only in Apia and Salelologa, so stock up before heading out. Cards work at bigger hotels and supermarkets in Apia. Tipping isn't expected, and prices aren't haggled.", "sleep": "Beach fales, open-sided huts on the sand, usually include breakfast and dinner and are the classic budget stay, with Lalomanu on Upolu and Manase on Savai'i among the best known. In Apia, a few guesthouses and budget hotels have dorms or cheap rooms.", "eat": ["Oka: raw fish marinated in lime and coconut cream", "Palusami: taro leaves baked with coconut cream", "Koko Samoa: rich drink made from local roasted cocoa", "Panipopo: soft buns baked in sweet coconut sauce"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Digicel or Vodafone Samoa are easy to buy at the airport or in Apia, with good coverage on Upolu and patchier signal on Savai'i.", "heads_up": "Sunday is a day of rest, so plan for little transport, and stay quiet in villages during the evening prayer time.", "food": [{"name": "Oka", "wiki": "'Ota 'ika", "desc": "Raw fish marinated in lime and coconut cream, served at markets and fale dinners.", "price": "$4 to $8", "when": "Lunch, or as part of a feast", "order": "Best made with fresh tuna and coconut cream"}, {"name": "Palusami", "wiki": "Lūʻau (food)", "desc": "Taro leaves wrapped around coconut cream and baked until rich and creamy. A must at Sunday to'ona'i lunch.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Sunday lunch, from the umu"}, {"name": "Umu", "wiki": "Earth oven", "desc": "Samoan Sunday feasts are cooked in an umu, an above-ground oven of hot stones, with pork, taro, breadfruit and fish.", "price": "$5 to $10", "when": "Sunday mornings, after church"}, {"name": "Sapasui", "wiki": "Chop suey", "desc": "Samoa's version of chop suey with glass noodles, soy sauce and meat, served at every feast and very cheap at markets.", "price": "$3 to $6", "when": "Feasts, weddings and takeaway plates"}, {"name": "Koko Samoa", "wiki": "Koko Samoa", "desc": "A rich drink made from roasted and ground local cocoa beans, served hot or cold.", "price": "$1 to $2", "when": "Breakfast and afternoons", "order": "Try koko alaisa, a cocoa rice pudding"}], "food_tip": "Your fale usually includes meals, which saves a lot. In Apia, the Fugalei market and the fish market have cheap food stalls.", "culture": ["Fa'a Samoa, the Samoan way, centres on family, respect for elders and church, and visitors are expected to fit in with it.", "Sunday is for church and family, so expect little transport, and avoid swimming near villages or making noise.", "In the evening, many villages hold sa, a prayer time of about 15 minutes, when you should stop, stay quiet, and not walk through the village.", "Dress modestly in villages and towns, with shoulders and knees covered, and wear a lavalava (sarong) over swimwear.", "When visiting a home, sit before speaking and don't point your feet at people.", "Many beaches and pools belong to villages, so pay the small custom fee and ask before swimming."], "safety": "Samoa is very safe, but watch out for stray dogs, sunburn and strong currents near reefs. Dengue outbreaks happen, so use repellent. Cyclone season runs from November to April, so check forecasts.", "eat_beats": ["Sunday to'ona'i, a big lunch after church, is the main family meal of the week.", "Tap water isn't reliably safe outside Apia, so drink bottled or boiled water.", "Vegetarians can enjoy taro, breadfruit, palusami and fruit, but many dishes contain corned beef or fish.", "Tipping isn't customary in Samoa.", "Most shops close on Sundays, so buy food on Saturday."], "drinks": [{"name": "'Ava", "desc": "Kava, a mildly numbing drink made from pepper plant root, served at ceremonies and sold at markets for about $1 a cup."}, {"name": "Coconut water", "desc": "Drunk straight from a green coconut, sold at roadside stalls for about $1."}], "greet": "Say 'Talofa' with a handshake, and expect warm smiles and raised eyebrows as a casual hello.", "dress": "Wear a lavalava or long skirt in villages and keep shoulders covered. On Sundays, many people dress in white for church.", "table": "Wait for grace before eating, and let elders and guests eat first. Sit cross-legged on mats and tuck your legs away from others.", "faith": "Nearly all Samoans are Christian, across Congregational, Catholic, Methodist and Latter-day Saint churches. Visitors are welcome at services if they dress smartly, but avoid photos during worship.", "phrases": [["Talofa", "Hello"], ["Fa'afetai", "Thank you"], ["E fia?", "How much?"], ["Manuia!", "Cheers, good luck"], ["Tōfā", "Goodbye"]]},
  "vanuatu": {"around": "In Port Vila, minibuses with a B on the number plate act as shared taxis and charge a flat fare for most trips around town. Domestic flights connect Efate with Tanna and Espiritu Santo, but schedules have been unreliable since Air Vanuatu's problems in 2024, so build in spare days and book ahead. On Tanna, trucks and arranged 4x4 transfers are the way to reach Mount Yasur.", "money": "The vatu is the currency, and you'll need cash outside Port Vila and Luganville, as cards are rarely accepted and ATMs are scarce on outer islands. Withdraw enough before flying out. Tipping and haggling aren't part of the culture, and people may be confused if you try.", "sleep": "Locally run bungalows and guesthouses are the usual budget stay, often with meals included. On Tanna, simple bungalows and treehouses near Mount Yasur are popular. Port Vila has a few backpacker hostels.", "eat": ["Lap lap: grated root vegetable pudding baked with coconut cream", "Simboro: grated cassava wrapped in island cabbage leaves", "Tuluk: cassava parcel with a pork filling, a cheap snack", "Kava: strong, earthy root drink served in nakamals at dusk"], "data": "Prepaid SIMs from Digicel or Vodafone are sold in Port Vila with your passport, with decent coverage in towns and limited signal on outer islands.", "heads_up": "Many sites charge a custom fee to the landowners, so ask before entering land, swimming at a beach or taking photos.", "food": [{"name": "Laplap", "wiki": "Laplap", "desc": "Grated yam, taro or banana baked into a pudding with coconut cream, often with meat or fish on top. Vanuatu's national dish and sold cheaply at markets.", "price": "$2 to $5", "when": "Lunch, at markets and gatherings", "order": "Ask what's on top, as fillings vary"}, {"name": "Kava", "wiki": "Kava", "desc": "Vanuatu's kava is famously strong, served in nakamals at dusk for a small price. Expect numb lips and a very chill evening.", "price": "$1 to $2 a shell", "when": "Dusk, at nakamals", "order": "Order a half shell first if you're new"}, {"name": "Island cabbage", "wiki": "Abelmoschus manihot", "desc": "Leafy greens cooked in coconut milk or used to wrap simboro, grated cassava parcels.", "price": "$1 to $3", "when": "Lunch, with rice or laplap"}, {"name": "Coconut crab", "wiki": "Coconut crab", "desc": "A giant land crab served as a delicacy, but it's overharvested, so many travellers skip it.", "price": "$20 to $50", "when": "Special occasions, at tourist restaurants", "order": "Many skip it to protect the species"}, {"name": "Nangai nuts", "wiki": "Canarium indicum", "desc": "Rich, buttery nuts sold in small bags at markets, a great cheap snack.", "price": "$1 to $2 a bag", "when": "Snack, at markets"}], "food_tip": "The Port Vila market and food stalls offer cheap, filling plates. On outer islands, meals at your bungalow are usually the best value.", "culture": ["Many sites charge a custom fee to the landowners, so ask before entering land, swimming at a beach or taking photos.", "In nakamals, keep quiet, don't take photos, and follow the locals' lead, as these are traditional spaces.", "Dress modestly in villages and towns, especially women, with shoulders and knees covered.", "Bislama is the common language, and a few words like \"tank yu tumas\" (thank you very much) are appreciated.", "Vanuatu runs on island time, so be patient with schedules."], "safety": "Vanuatu is generally safe, but cyclones hit between November and April, and the December 2024 earthquake damaged parts of Port Vila. Check alert levels at Mount Yasur before visiting. Malaria is present on some outer islands, so take precautions.", "eat_beats": ["Lunch is usually the main meal around noon, and dinner is early, before dark.", "Tap water is safe in Port Vila and Luganville, but use bottled or rainwater elsewhere.", "Vegetarians can find root crops, greens and fruit, but meat is common.", "Market stalls sell fresh fruit, vegetables and cooked food cheaply.", "Tipping isn't expected and can cause awkwardness, so a sincere thank you is enough."], "drinks": [{"name": "Tusker beer", "desc": "The local lager, brewed in Port Vila, about $3 to $5 a bottle."}, {"name": "Coconut water", "desc": "Fresh green coconuts opened on request, sold for $0.50 to $1."}], "greet": "Handshakes are common, and greetings are friendly and relaxed. Saying \"halo\" or \"gud moning\" goes a long way.", "dress": "Cover shoulders and knees in villages and towns, especially women. Swimwear is for the beach or resort only.", "table": "Food is often shared, and it's polite to accept what's offered. Wait for the host to invite you to eat.", "faith": "Most ni-Vanuatu are Christian, often blended with kastom beliefs. Sundays are quiet, and some sites are sacred, so ask before entering or photographing.", "phrases": [["Halo", "Hello"], ["Tank yu", "Thank you"], ["Hamas?", "How much?"], ["Wanem nem blong yu?", "What's your name?"], ["Lukim yu", "See you"]]}
};
